ADDART({id:"sahel",t:"The Sahel",c:"Geography",i:"🌾",w:"Sahel",r:["sahara","desert","mali","senegalcountry","nigeriacountry","climchange"],
p:[["The shore of the desert","The Sahel is a belt of dry grassland along the southern edge of the Sahara."],["Ancient empires","Great empires such as Ghana, Mali and Songhai rose in the Sahel."],["A green wall","African countries are working to restore land across the region."]],
l:"The Sahel is a semi-arid belt of land stretching about 5,000 kilometers across Africa, from the Atlantic Ocean in Senegal and Mauritania to the Red Sea in Sudan and Eritrea, between the Sahara Desert to the north and the wetter savannas to the south. Its name comes from the Arabic word for shore, as it was seen as the shore of the great sea of sand. The Sahel was home to some of Africa’s greatest empires and trading cities, such as Timbuktu. Today its fast-growing population faces droughts, land degradation, conflict and the effects of climate change, but also inspiring efforts to regreen the land.",
s:[["Land and climate","The Sahel receives about 200 to 600 millimeters of rain a year, falling mostly in a short rainy season from June to September. The rest of the year is hot and dry. Grasses, thorny acacia trees and baobabs grow there. Rainfall varies greatly from year to year, making life uncertain for farmers and herders."],["Countries","The Sahel crosses parts of Senegal, Mauritania, Mali, Burkina Faso, Niger, Nigeria, Chad, Sudan and Eritrea, and touches Cameroon and others. Great rivers, such as the Niger and the Senegal, and Lake Chad have long supported farming and fishing."],["Peoples and ways of life","Many peoples live in the Sahel, including the Fulani, Tuareg, Hausa, Wolof, Songhai and Kanuri. Some are farmers growing millet, sorghum and peanuts; others are herders moving their cattle, camels, sheep and goats with the seasons in search of pasture and water."],["Empires and trade","From about the 700s, empires grew rich on trans-Saharan trade in gold, salt and other goods. The Ghana Empire was followed by the Mali Empire, whose ruler Mansa Musa made a famous pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324, and then the Songhai Empire. Cities such as Timbuktu, Gao and Djenné became centers of trade and Islamic learning."]],
k:[["Length","About 5,000 km"],["Rainfall","About 200–600 mm a year"],["Name means","Shore (Arabic)"],["Main crops","Millet, sorghum, peanuts"],["Major restoration effort","The Great Green Wall"]],
d:[["700s","The Ghana Empire grows rich on trade"],["1324","Mansa Musa of Mali makes his pilgrimage to Mecca"],["1591","The Songhai Empire falls to a Moroccan army"],["1960","Many Sahel countries become independent"],["1968–1974","A severe drought and famine strike the Sahel"],["2007","The Great Green Wall initiative is launched"],["2012","Conflict breaks out in northern Mali"]],
s2:[["Droughts","From the late 1960s to the 1980s, the Sahel suffered severe droughts. Crops failed and herds died, and famine killed many thousands of people and animals. The disasters brought international attention, aid and research into the causes, which include natural variations in ocean temperatures as well as human pressures on the land."],["Lake Chad","Lake Chad, shared by Chad, Cameroon, Niger and Nigeria, shrank dramatically from the 1960s to the 1980s because of drought and increased water use, losing most of its surface area. It supports millions of people through fishing, farming and herding, and its decline has added to hardship and conflict in the region."],["The Great Green Wall","In 2007 the African Union launched the Great Green Wall, a plan to restore degraded land across the Sahel, originally imagined as a wall of trees from Senegal to Djibouti. It has grown into a broader program of planting, protecting natural regrowth and improving farming. Progress has been uneven, but some areas have become greener."],["Farmers who regreened the land","In Niger and Burkina Faso, farmers have restored millions of hectares by protecting tree shoots that sprout from old roots, a method called farmer-managed natural regeneration, and by digging small pits and stone lines to catch rainwater. These low-cost methods have increased crop yields and tree cover."],["Conflict and politics","Since 2012, armed groups, including some linked to international jihadist movements, have spread violence in Mali, Burkina Faso, Niger and neighboring countries, killing thousands and forcing millions from their homes. Military coups took place in Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger between 2020 and 2023. These governments formed the Alliance of Sahel States and left the West African regional bloc ECOWAS in 2025. Views differ widely on the causes and solutions."],["Mud architecture","Sahel builders have long made beautiful buildings from mud brick. The Great Mosque of Djenné in Mali, rebuilt in 1907, is the largest mud-brick building in the world. Every year, the townspeople gather to replaster it with fresh mud in a joyful festival."]],
sp:["Replastering the Great Mosque of Djenné","Each year, before the rainy season, thousands of people in the town of Djenné in Mali take part in the Crepissage, a festival to repair their great mud mosque. For days beforehand, mud is mixed in pits and left to ferment. Then, on the chosen day, men and boys race up ladders and wooden beams that stick out from the walls, while women and girls carry water and mud. Working together, to music and cheers, they smooth a fresh layer of mud over the whole building, protecting it for another year."],
g:[["Semi-arid","Having little rain, but more than a desert"],["Savanna","A grassland with scattered trees"],["Herder","A person who looks after grazing animals"],["Land degradation","The decline of land’s ability to support plants and farming"],["Trans-Saharan trade","Trade carried across the Sahara Desert by camel caravans"]],
f:["Sahel means shore in Arabic.","The Great Mosque of Djenné is the largest mud-brick building.","The Great Green Wall began in 2007."],
fun:"Each year, the people of Djenné replaster their huge mud mosque together in a festival.",
tl:"Ang Sahel ay tuyong lupain sa timog ng Disyerto ng Sahara na umaabot mula Atlantiko hanggang Dagat Pula. Dito umusbong ang mga imperyo ng Ghana, Mali at Songhai. Ngayon, humaharap ito sa tagtuyot at kaguluhan, ngunit may mga pagsisikap na muling pagtamnan ang lupain."});

ADDART({id:"patagonia",t:"Patagonia",c:"Geography",i:"🏔️",w:"Patagonia",r:["andes","argentinacountry","chilecountry","penguin","darwin","iceage"],
p:[["The end of the world","Patagonia is the wild southern tip of South America."],["Ice and wind","It has huge glaciers, jagged peaks and fierce winds."],["Giant dinosaurs","Some of the largest dinosaurs ever found lived in Patagonia."]],
l:"Patagonia is a vast region at the southern end of South America, shared by Argentina and Chile. It includes the southern Andes, with glaciers, ice fields and jagged granite peaks, the windswept steppes of the Argentine plains and the islands of Tierra del Fuego. Sparsely populated, Patagonia is home to Indigenous peoples such as the Mapuche and Tehuelche, descendants of European settlers, including a Welsh-speaking community, and wildlife such as guanacos, condors and penguins. It is famous for its national parks and for fossils of giant dinosaurs.",
s:[["Land","Patagonia is usually said to begin around the Colorado River in Argentina and the region of the Biobío in Chile and extends south to Cape Horn. The Chilean side is wet, with forests, fjords and glaciers, while the Argentine side is dry, flat steppe swept by strong westerly winds. Together they cover about a million square kilometers."],["Ice fields","The Southern Patagonian Ice Field is the largest expanse of ice in the Southern Hemisphere outside Antarctica. Its glaciers include the Perito Moreno Glacier in Argentina, which regularly advances across a lake and then collapses spectacularly, unlike most glaciers, which are retreating."],["Wildlife","Guanacos, wild relatives of the llama, roam the plains with rheas, large flightless birds. Andean condors soar over the mountains, pumas hunt in the parks and colonies of Magellanic penguins nest on the Atlantic coast, while southern right whales and orcas visit the shores."],["First peoples","People have lived in Patagonia for at least 12,000 years. The Cueva de las Manos in Argentina has hundreds of painted handprints, some about 9,000 years old. Peoples such as the Tehuelche and Mapuche of the mainland and the Selk’nam and Yaghan of Tierra del Fuego adapted to its harsh climate."]],
k:[["Countries","Argentina and Chile"],["Area","About 1 million km²"],["Famous parks","Torres del Paine, Los Glaciares"],["Southernmost city","Ushuaia, Argentina"],["Name","From the Patagones, a name given by Magellan’s crew"]],
d:[["c. 7300 BCE","The oldest handprints at Cueva de las Manos are painted"],["1520","Ferdinand Magellan’s expedition reaches Patagonia"],["1834","Charles Darwin explores Patagonia on HMS Beagle"],["1865","Welsh settlers arrive in Chubut"],["1878","Argentina’s Conquest of the Desert campaign begins"],["1959","Torres del Paine National Park is created"],["2017","Patagotitan, a giant dinosaur, is named"]],
s2:[["Magellan and the giants","In 1520, sailors on Ferdinand Magellan’s expedition met tall Tehuelche people on the coast and called them Patagones, possibly after a giant in a Spanish romance novel, giving the region its name. European stories of a land of giants persisted for centuries, although the Tehuelche were simply tall."],["The Welsh in Patagonia","In 1865 about 150 Welsh settlers arrived on the ship Mimosa, seeking a place where they could keep their language and culture. They founded towns in the Chubut valley, such as Trelew and Gaiman. Today some of their descendants still speak Welsh and serve Welsh tea and cakes."],["Conquest and loss","In the late 1800s, Argentina and Chile extended their control over Patagonia by force. Argentina’s Conquest of the Desert campaign from 1878 killed and displaced many Indigenous people, and in Tierra del Fuego the Selk’nam were nearly wiped out by violence and disease. Indigenous communities continue to seek recognition and land rights."],["Dinosaur land","Patagonia’s rocks have revealed some of the largest dinosaurs ever discovered, such as Argentinosaurus and Patagotitan, giant long-necked plant-eaters weighing as much as 60 tonnes or more, and the huge meat-eater Giganotosaurus."],["Torres del Paine","Torres del Paine National Park in Chile is famous for its granite towers, turquoise lakes, glaciers and guanacos. With Los Glaciares National Park and Mount Fitz Roy in Argentina, it attracts hikers and climbers from around the world."],["Tierra del Fuego","At Patagonia’s southern tip lies Tierra del Fuego, the Land of Fire, named by Magellan’s crew after the fires of Indigenous people they saw on shore. The Argentine city of Ushuaia, often called the southernmost city in the world, is a starting point for ships to Antarctica."]],
sp:["Darwin in Patagonia","When Charles Darwin explored Patagonia in 1833 and 1834 during the voyage of HMS Beagle, he found fossils of giant extinct mammals, such as ground sloths and glyptodonts, huge armored relatives of armadillos. He noticed that they resembled smaller living animals of the same region. This puzzle, along with his observations of rheas and of the Galápagos Islands, helped lead him to his theory of evolution. He also described the vast, empty plains of Patagonia as haunting his memory."],
g:[["Steppe","A large, dry, treeless grassland"],["Ice field","A large area of connected glaciers"],["Guanaco","A wild South American relative of the llama"],["Rhea","A large, flightless South American bird"],["Glacier","A slowly moving mass of ice"]],
f:["Patagonia is shared by Argentina and Chile.","Welsh is still spoken by some Patagonians.","Giant dinosaurs such as Patagotitan were found there."],
fun:"Some people in Patagonia still speak Welsh, brought by settlers in 1865.",
tl:"Ang Patagonia ay malawak na rehiyon sa timog na dulo ng Timog Amerika na kahati ng Argentina at Chile. Kilala ito sa malalaking glacier, malakas na hangin at mga fossil ng higanteng dinosaur."});

ADDART({id:"polynesia",t:"Polynesia",c:"Geography",i:"🛶",w:"Polynesia",r:["pacific","hawaii","nzcountry","samoacountry","tongacountry","explor"],
p:[["Many islands","Polynesia is a huge triangle of islands in the Pacific Ocean."],["Master navigators","Polynesians crossed thousands of kilometers of ocean using stars, waves and birds."],["Shared roots","Polynesian peoples share related languages and cultures."]],
l:"Polynesia, meaning many islands, is a vast region of the central and southern Pacific Ocean, forming a triangle with Hawai‘i, New Zealand and Rapa Nui, or Easter Island, at its corners. It includes more than a thousand islands, among them Samoa, Tonga, Tahiti, Tuvalu and the Cook Islands. Polynesians are descendants of seafarers who, without maps or instruments, explored and settled the largest ocean on Earth, reaching New Zealand around 1250 to 1300 CE. Their related languages and cultures, including the haka, tattooing and the ideas of mana and tapu, are known worldwide.",
s:[["The Polynesian Triangle","Polynesia covers an area larger than Russia, mostly ocean. It includes independent countries such as Samoa, Tonga and Tuvalu, self-governing islands such as the Cook Islands and Niue, territories such as French Polynesia, American Samoa and Wallis and Futuna, the US state of Hawai‘i, Chile’s Rapa Nui and New Zealand."],["Origins","Polynesians descend from Austronesian-speaking peoples whose ancestors spread from Taiwan through the Philippines and Indonesia thousands of years ago. The Lapita people reached Tonga and Samoa about 2,800 years ago, and Polynesian culture developed there."],["The great voyages","After a long pause, Polynesians sailed east from about 1000 CE, settling the Society Islands, the Marquesas, Hawai‘i and Rapa Nui, and finally New Zealand around 1250 to 1300 CE. They traveled in large double-hulled canoes carrying families, plants and animals such as taro, breadfruit, pigs and chickens."],["Wayfinding","Polynesian navigators found their way without instruments, reading the rising and setting points of stars, the patterns of ocean swells, the flight of birds, clouds and the color of the water. This knowledge was passed down through generations."]],
k:[["Name means","Many islands"],["Corners","Hawai‘i, New Zealand, Rapa Nui"],["Settlement of New Zealand","About 1250–1300 CE"],["Language family","Austronesian"],["Famous canoe revival","Hōkūle‘a (1976)"]],
d:[["c. 800 BCE","Lapita settlers reach Tonga and Samoa"],["c. 1000 CE","Polynesians begin settling eastern Polynesia"],["c. 1200 CE","Polynesians and Native Americans make contact"],["c. 1250–1300","Polynesians settle New Zealand"],["1768–1779","James Cook’s voyages through Polynesia"],["1976","The canoe Hōkūle‘a sails from Hawai‘i to Tahiti"],["2023","Tuvalu and Australia agree on a treaty including climate migration"]],
s2:[["Languages","Polynesian languages, such as Māori, Hawaiian, Samoan, Tongan and Tahitian, are closely related and belong to the Austronesian family, which also includes Filipino, Malay and Malagasy. Words such as taboo, from tapu, and tattoo, from tatau, came into English from Polynesian languages."],["Mana and tapu","Mana is spiritual power, authority and prestige, while tapu marks something as sacred or forbidden. These ideas shaped Polynesian societies, guiding the roles of chiefs, priests and ordinary people and how people treated land, sea and each other."],["Moai of Rapa Nui","On Rapa Nui, the easternmost corner of the triangle, people carved nearly a thousand giant stone statues called moai, representing ancestors, between about 1250 and 1500 CE. How they were moved has long fascinated people; experiments suggest they were walked upright by teams rocking them with ropes."],["Contact with the Americas","Sweet potatoes, native to South America, were grown in Polynesia long before Europeans arrived, suggesting contact. A 2020 genetic study found evidence that Polynesians and Native Americans met around 1200 CE."],["Hōkūle‘a","In 1976, the Hawaiian voyaging canoe Hōkūle‘a sailed about 4,000 kilometers from Hawai‘i to Tahiti, guided by the Micronesian master navigator Mau Piailug without instruments. The voyage proved that Polynesians could have settled the Pacific by deliberate navigation and sparked a revival of traditional voyaging and pride across the Pacific."],["Colonial rule and today","From the 1700s, European explorers, missionaries, traders and colonial powers transformed Polynesia. Tonga was the only Polynesian kingdom never formally colonized, though it was a British protected state. France tested nuclear weapons in French Polynesia from 1966 to 1996. Today low-lying nations such as Tuvalu face rising seas from climate change."]],
sp:["Reading the ocean","Polynesian navigators were trained from childhood to read the ocean. They memorized the points on the horizon where dozens of stars rise and set, creating a star compass. They could feel different swells passing under the canoe, even at night, and notice how islands far over the horizon bend these swells. Clouds that gather over islands, the greenish reflection of lagoons on clouds and the flight paths of seabirds returning to land at dusk all pointed the way. With this knowledge, they found tiny islands in the vastness of the Pacific."],
g:[["Polynesia","The many islands of the central and southern Pacific"],["Wayfinding","Traditional navigation without instruments"],["Mana","Spiritual power and prestige"],["Tapu","Sacred or forbidden; the origin of the word taboo"],["Moai","Giant stone statues of Rapa Nui"]],
f:["Polynesia means many islands.","New Zealand was settled around 1250–1300 CE.","The words taboo and tattoo come from Polynesian languages."],
fun:"The English words taboo and tattoo come from Polynesian languages.",
tl:"Ang Polynesia ay malawak na rehiyon ng mga pulo sa Pasipiko, mula Hawai‘i hanggang New Zealand at Rapa Nui. Ang mga Polynesian ay mahuhusay na manlalayag na gumamit ng mga bituin at alon. Kamag-anak ng mga wikang Polynesian ang Filipino."});

ADDART({id:"centralasia",t:"Central Asia",c:"Geography",i:"🐎",w:"Central Asia",r:["silk","mongol","ibnsina","islam","russiacountry","chinacountry"],
p:[["The heart of Asia","Central Asia lies between China, Russia, Iran and Afghanistan."],["The Silk Road","Great Silk Road cities such as Samarkand and Bukhara grew rich on trade."],["Nomads and horses","Nomadic herders on horseback have lived on its steppes for thousands of years."]],
l:"Central Asia is a region in the heart of the Asian continent, usually defined as the five countries of Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan, Turkmenistan and Uzbekistan, with about 80 million people. It stretches from the Caspian Sea to China, with vast steppes and deserts in the west and north and high mountains, such as the Tian Shan and the Pamirs, in the southeast. For centuries the Silk Road ran through its oasis cities, such as Samarkand and Bukhara, connecting China with Persia and Europe. Ruled by many empires and later by Russia and the Soviet Union, the five countries became independent in 1991.",
s:[["Land","Central Asia is far from any ocean. The Kazakh Steppe, a vast grassland, covers the north, while the Karakum and Kyzylkum deserts stretch across Turkmenistan and Uzbekistan. In the southeast, the Pamirs, called the roof of the world, and the Tian Shan rise above 7,000 meters. Rivers such as the Amu Darya and Syr Darya once flowed into the Aral Sea."],["Nomads and cities","For thousands of years, nomadic peoples herded horses, sheep and camels on the steppes, living in portable felt tents called yurts. Settled peoples farmed in river valleys and oases, building cities that became centers of trade and learning."],["Empires","Central Asia was part of the Persian Empire and conquered by Alexander the Great. Arab armies brought Islam in the 700s. In the 1200s the Mongols under Genghis Khan devastated the region. In the late 1300s, Timur, also known as Tamerlane, built an empire with Samarkand as its glittering capital."],["Russian and Soviet rule","Russia conquered Central Asia in the 1800s. Under the Soviet Union, the region was divided into five republics. The Soviets built schools and industry but also forced nomads onto collective farms, causing a terrible famine in Kazakhstan in the early 1930s. The five republics became independent when the Soviet Union collapsed in 1991."]],
k:[["Countries","Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan, Turkmenistan, Uzbekistan"],["Population","About 80 million"],["Famous cities","Samarkand, Bukhara, Khiva, Almaty, Tashkent"],["Main languages","Kazakh, Kyrgyz, Uzbek, Turkmen, Tajik, Russian"],["Independence","1991"]],
d:[["329 BCE","Alexander the Great reaches Samarkand"],["700s","Arab armies bring Islam to the region"],["1220","Genghis Khan’s Mongols conquer the region"],["1370","Timur makes Samarkand his capital"],["1961","Yuri Gagarin is launched into space from Baikonur, Kazakhstan"],["1991","The five countries become independent"],["2005","A dam helps the North Aral Sea begin to recover"]],
s2:[["Scholars of the golden age","Central Asia produced some of the greatest scholars of the Islamic golden age. Al-Khwarizmi, from Khwarazm, gave his name to the word algorithm, and his book gave us algebra. Ibn Sina, born near Bukhara, wrote a medical encyclopedia used for centuries, and al-Biruni measured the Earth with remarkable accuracy."],["Samarkand","Samarkand, in Uzbekistan, is one of the oldest cities in Central Asia. Its Registan square, framed by three huge madrasas covered in blue tiles, is one of the most famous sights in the Islamic world. In the 1420s, Timur’s grandson Ulugh Beg built an observatory there and made a precise catalog of stars."],["The Aral Sea","The Aral Sea, once the fourth-largest lake in the world, shrank dramatically after Soviet planners diverted its rivers in the 1960s to irrigate cotton fields. Fishing towns were left far from the water, and dust storms carried salt and chemicals. Since a dam was built in 2005, the northern part in Kazakhstan has partly recovered."],["Space and nuclear tests","The Baikonur Cosmodrome in Kazakhstan, from which Sputnik and the first human in space were launched, is still used by Russia. The Soviet Union carried out hundreds of nuclear tests at Semipalatinsk in Kazakhstan from 1949 to 1989, harming the health of local people. The site was closed in 1991."],["Nomadic traditions","Central Asian peoples keep traditions such as hunting with golden eagles, horse games like kok-boru, in which riders compete for a goat carcass, and living in yurts in summer pastures. The Kyrgyz Epic of Manas is one of the longest epic poems in the world."],["Today","The five countries have different paths: Kazakhstan is rich in oil and uranium, Uzbekistan is the most populous, Turkmenistan has huge gas reserves, and Kyrgyzstan and Tajikistan are mountainous, relying on hydropower and on money sent home by workers abroad. Most people are Muslim, and Russian remains widely spoken."]],
sp:["The Door to Hell","In the Karakum Desert of Turkmenistan lies the Darvaza gas crater, a pit about 70 meters wide that has been burning for decades. According to the most common account, in 1971 Soviet geologists drilling for gas accidentally caused the ground to collapse, and the crater began leaking methane gas. To stop the gas from spreading, they set it on fire, expecting it to burn out in a few weeks. It is still burning, glowing orange at night, and is nicknamed the Door to Hell. Turkmenistan’s government has discussed putting it out."],
g:[["Steppe","A vast, dry, treeless grassland"],["Yurt","A round, portable felt tent used by nomads"],["Oasis","A fertile place with water in a desert"],["Madrasa","An Islamic school or college"],["Nomad","A person who moves from place to place with herds"]],
f:["Central Asia includes five countries ending in -stan.","The word algorithm comes from al-Khwarizmi.","The first human in space was launched from Kazakhstan."],
fun:"A gas crater in Turkmenistan has been burning since about 1971.",
tl:"Ang Gitnang Asya ay binubuo ng Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan, Turkmenistan at Uzbekistan. Dumaan dito ang Silk Road, kasama ang mga lungsod ng Samarkand at Bukhara. Naging malaya ang limang bansa noong 1991."});

ADDART({id:"lagos",t:"Lagos",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏙️",w:"Lagos",r:["nigeriacountry","cinema","urban","slavetrade","brazilcountry","africacolon"],
p:[["Africa’s megacity","Lagos is one of the largest and fastest-growing cities in Africa."],["City of lagoons","It is built on islands and the mainland around a large lagoon."],["Culture capital","Lagos is a center of Nollywood films and Afrobeats music."]],
l:"Lagos is the largest city in Nigeria and one of the largest cities in Africa, home to an estimated 15 million or more people in its metropolitan area. Built around the Lagos Lagoon on islands and the mainland along the Atlantic coast, it was Nigeria’s capital until 1991 and remains its business, financial and cultural center. Lagos is famous for its energy, traffic, markets, music, films and technology start-ups, and also for the challenges of rapid growth, such as housing, flooding and transport.",
s:[["Origins","The area was settled by the Awori, a Yoruba people, and the city is called Èkó in Yoruba. It became a kingdom ruled by an oba, a traditional king, influenced by the Kingdom of Benin. Portuguese traders named it Lagos, meaning lakes, after a town in Portugal."],["Colonial era","Lagos became a major port in the Atlantic slave trade in the 1700s and 1800s. Britain annexed Lagos in 1861, partly to end the slave trade, and it became the capital of the colony and later of Nigeria, which became independent in 1960. In 1991 the capital moved to Abuja."],["The city today","Lagos is divided between the islands, Lagos Island, Ikoyi and Victoria Island, where many banks, offices and wealthy neighborhoods are, and the vast mainland, where most people live. Long bridges, such as the Third Mainland Bridge, connect them."],["Economy","Lagos is Nigeria’s commercial heart, with its main port, airport, stock exchange and many company headquarters. Markets such as Balogun are packed with traders. The Lekki area has a free trade zone, a deep-sea port and one of the world’s largest oil refineries."]],
k:[["Country","Nigeria"],["Metropolitan population","An estimated 15 million or more"],["Yoruba name","Èkó"],["Capital of Nigeria until","1991"],["Known for","Business, Nollywood, Afrobeats"]],
d:[["1400s","The Awori Yoruba settle the area"],["1472","Portuguese sailors arrive"],["1861","Britain annexes Lagos"],["1960","Lagos becomes capital of independent Nigeria"],["1977","Lagos hosts the FESTAC ’77 arts festival"],["1991","The capital moves to Abuja"],["2023","The first line of the Lagos light rail opens"]],
s2:[["Music","Lagos is the birthplace of Afrobeat, created in the 1970s by Fela Kuti, who blended jazz, funk and Yoruba music with political lyrics at his club, the Shrine. Today Lagos is the center of Afrobeats, a newer pop style whose stars, such as Burna Boy, Wizkid and Tems, have huge global audiences."],["Nollywood","Nigeria’s film industry, Nollywood, centered in Lagos, produces more films each year than almost any other country. It began in the 1990s with low-budget movies sold on video and now includes cinema releases and streaming hits watched across Africa and the world."],["Technology","Lagos has become a leading African technology hub, with start-ups in online payments, banking and delivery. The area around Yaba is nicknamed Yabacon Valley. Companies founded in Lagos have attracted large international investment."],["Traffic and transport","Lagos is famous for its traffic jams, called go-slows. Yellow minibuses called danfos and motorcycle taxis carry millions. New bus rapid transit lines, light rail lines and ferries on the lagoon aim to ease congestion."],["Challenges","Lagos has grown extremely fast, and many residents live in informal settlements without reliable water, electricity or sanitation. Makoko, a community of homes on stilts over the lagoon, has become a symbol of both hardship and resourcefulness. Rising seas and heavy rains cause frequent flooding."],["The Brazilian Quarter","In the 1800s, formerly enslaved Africans and their descendants returned from Brazil and Cuba to Lagos. They settled in an area called the Brazilian Quarter, building houses and churches in Afro-Brazilian style and bringing foods, festivals and Portuguese surnames that survive today."]],
sp:["Eko Atlantic","Along Lagos’s Atlantic shore, a new city district called Eko Atlantic is being built on land reclaimed from the sea. Since the late 2000s, millions of tonnes of sand have been dredged from the ocean floor and protected by a sea wall made of thousands of concrete blocks, called the Great Wall of Lagos. Supporters see it as a modern business district protecting the coast from erosion; critics argue it serves mainly the wealthy and may worsen flooding elsewhere. It shows both the ambition and the inequality of the growing megacity."],
g:[["Megacity","A city with more than 10 million people"],["Lagoon","A shallow body of water separated from the sea"],["Oba","A traditional Yoruba king"],["Afrobeats","A popular West African music style"],["Informal settlement","An area of unplanned housing, often without services"]],
f:["Lagos was Nigeria’s capital until 1991.","Its Yoruba name is Èkó.","Fela Kuti created Afrobeat in Lagos."],
fun:"Lagos is being extended into the sea with a new district built on reclaimed land.",
tl:"Ang Lagos ang pinakamalaking lungsod ng Nigeria at isa sa pinakamalalaki sa Aprika. Ito ang sentro ng negosyo, ng pelikulang Nollywood at ng musikang Afrobeats."});

ADDART({id:"saopaulo",t:"São Paulo",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌆",w:"São Paulo",r:["brazilcountry","rio","coffee","japancountry","soccer","urban"],
p:[["Giant of the south","São Paulo is the largest city in South America."],["City of immigrants","People from Italy, Japan, Lebanon and many other countries made it their home."],["Brazil’s engine","It is Brazil’s richest city and business center."]],
l:"São Paulo is the largest city in Brazil and in South America, with about 11.5 million people in the city and more than 20 million in its metropolitan area. Founded by Jesuit missionaries in 1554, it grew rich from coffee in the 1800s and attracted millions of immigrants, including the largest Japanese community outside Japan. Today São Paulo is Brazil’s financial and industrial center, a huge city of skyscrapers, museums, restaurants and cultural life, but also of great inequality.",
s:[["Founding","São Paulo was founded on 25 January 1554 by Jesuit priests, including José de Anchieta and Manuel da Nóbrega, as a mission to convert Indigenous people. For centuries it was a small town. From there, bands of explorers called bandeirantes set out to search for gold and to capture Indigenous people as slaves, extending Portuguese control inland."],["Coffee and immigrants","In the 1800s, coffee plantations made São Paulo state rich. After slavery was abolished in Brazil in 1888, the government encouraged immigration. Italians, Portuguese, Spaniards, Japanese, Germans, Lebanese, Syrians and others arrived in large numbers. The city grew rapidly into an industrial center."],["A world city","São Paulo is home to Brazil’s stock exchange, banks and many company headquarters. Avenida Paulista, lined with skyscrapers, is its symbolic heart, closed to cars on Sundays for walking and cycling. The city has dozens of universities, theaters and some of the best restaurants in Latin America."],["Inequality","Wealthy neighborhoods with luxury apartments stand near favelas, informal communities where many families lack secure housing and services. The contrast between rich and poor is one of the city’s biggest challenges."]],
k:[["Country","Brazil"],["City population","About 11.5 million (2022 census)"],["Metropolitan population","More than 20 million"],["Founded","25 January 1554"],["Known for","Business, immigration, food, culture"]],
d:[["1554","Jesuit priests found São Paulo"],["1888","Slavery is abolished in Brazil, and immigration grows"],["1908","The first Japanese immigrants arrive"],["1922","The Week of Modern Art launches Brazilian modernism"],["1951","The first São Paulo Art Biennial is held"],["1968","The São Paulo Museum of Art building opens"],["2007","The Clean City Law bans outdoor billboards"]],
s2:[["Little Tokyo","In 1908 the ship Kasato Maru brought the first Japanese immigrants to Brazil to work on coffee farms. Today Brazil has the largest population of Japanese descent outside Japan, and the São Paulo district of Liberdade, with its red lanterns, shops and festivals, is a center of Japanese and East Asian culture."],["Art and architecture","The São Paulo Museum of Art, or MASP, designed by the Italian-Brazilian architect Lina Bo Bardi, seems to float above the street on four red pillars. Ibirapuera Park, with buildings by Oscar Niemeyer, hosts the São Paulo Art Biennial, one of the oldest and most important art exhibitions in the world."],["Food","Thanks to its immigrants, São Paulo has an enormous variety of food. It is famous for pizza, brought by Italians, and its own pizza day; for Japanese restaurants; and for Lebanese dishes such as esfihas and kibbeh, alongside Brazilian favorites like feijoada and pastel, a fried pastry sold at street markets."],["Sport","Football is a passion in São Paulo, with rival clubs such as Corinthians, Palmeiras and São Paulo FC, and Santos, Pelé’s club, nearby. The Interlagos circuit hosts Formula One races, and the city was home to the racing driver Ayrton Senna."],["The Clean City Law","In 2007 São Paulo banned outdoor billboards, large shop signs and advertising on buses with its Clean City Law. Thousands of billboards came down, revealing buildings and, critics noted, also exposing run-down areas and hidden favelas. Many residents came to love the cleaner skyline."],["Getting around","With heavy traffic, São Paulo relies on its metro, opened in 1974, trains and buses. Wealthy residents and businesspeople famously use helicopters, and the city has one of the largest numbers of helicopters of any city in the world."]],
sp:["The Week of Modern Art","In February 1922, a group of young artists, writers and musicians held a Week of Modern Art at São Paulo’s Municipal Theatre. They presented bold paintings, poems and music that broke with European traditions and celebrated Brazil’s mixed Indigenous, African and European roots. Some audiences booed and threw things. Afterwards the writer Oswald de Andrade proposed that Brazilian culture should devour foreign influences and transform them into something new. The event launched Brazilian modernism and shaped Brazilian art for decades."],
g:[["Metropolitan area","A city together with its surrounding suburbs and towns"],["Favela","An informal neighborhood in a Brazilian city"],["Bandeirante","A colonial explorer from São Paulo"],["Biennial","An event held every two years"],["Immigrant","A person who moves to live in another country"]],
f:["São Paulo is the largest city in South America.","It has the largest Japanese community outside Japan.","São Paulo banned billboards in 2007."],
fun:"São Paulo banned outdoor billboards in 2007.",
tl:"Ang São Paulo ang pinakamalaking lungsod sa Brazil at sa buong Timog Amerika. Lungsod ito ng mga imigrante, kabilang ang pinakamalaking komunidad ng mga Hapones sa labas ng Japan."});

ADDART({id:"mexicocity",t:"Mexico City",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌮",w:"Mexico City",r:["mexicocountry","aztec","kahlo","quake","olymp","soccer"],
p:[["City on a lake","Mexico City was built on the ruins of the Aztec capital, Tenochtitlan, on a lake."],["High and huge","It is one of the largest cities in the world, at over 2,200 meters above sea level."],["Sinking city","Parts of the city sink many centimeters each year."]],
l:"Mexico City is the capital and largest city of Mexico, with more than 20 million people in its metropolitan area, making it one of the largest cities in the world and the largest Spanish-speaking city. It lies in a high valley about 2,240 meters above sea level, on the site of Tenochtitlan, the capital of the Aztec Empire, founded on an island in Lake Texcoco in 1325. After the Spanish conquest in 1521, it became the capital of New Spain. Today it is famous for its history, museums, murals, food and lively culture, and also for earthquakes, sinking ground and once-severe air pollution.",
s:[["Tenochtitlan","According to legend, the Mexica, or Aztecs, founded Tenochtitlan in 1325 where they saw an eagle perched on a cactus eating a snake, an image now on Mexico’s flag. The city grew into one of the largest in the world, with causeways, canals, floating gardens and great temples."],["Conquest and New Spain","In 1521 Spanish conquistadors led by Hernán Cortés, with many Indigenous allies, conquered Tenochtitlan after a long siege. The Spanish built Mexico City on its ruins, draining much of the lake. It became the capital of New Spain, a vast colony that also governed the Philippines for over two centuries."],["Modern capital","After independence in 1821, Mexico City remained the capital. It grew enormously in the 1900s as people moved from the countryside, spreading across the valley. It hosted the 1968 Summer Olympics and the 1970 and 1986 football World Cups, and matches of the 2026 World Cup."],["The historic center","The Zócalo, one of the largest public squares in the world, is surrounded by the Metropolitan Cathedral, the National Palace with Diego Rivera’s murals and the ruins of the Templo Mayor, the great Aztec temple. The historic center is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."]],
k:[["Country","Mexico"],["Metropolitan population","More than 20 million"],["Elevation","About 2,240 m"],["Founded","1325, as Tenochtitlan"],["Known for","Aztec history, museums, murals, food"]],
d:[["1325","The Mexica found Tenochtitlan"],["1521","Tenochtitlan falls to the Spanish and their allies"],["1821","Mexico becomes independent"],["1968","Mexico City hosts the Summer Olympics"],["1978","Workers discover the Templo Mayor ruins"],["1985","A powerful earthquake strikes"],["2026","The city hosts the opening match of the World Cup"]],
s2:[["The sinking city","Because the city was built on soft lake clay and pumps huge amounts of water from underground, parts of Mexico City are sinking, in some places by tens of centimeters a year. Buildings tilt, pipes break and the cathedral has been carefully reinforced. Ironically, the city also faces water shortages and floods."],["Earthquakes","Mexico City’s soft lakebed soil amplifies earthquake shaking. On 19 September 1985, an earthquake killed thousands of people and destroyed many buildings. On the same date in 2017, another strong quake killed hundreds. The city now has strict building codes and an earthquake warning system with sirens."],["Xochimilco","In the south of the city, canals and chinampas, artificial islands used for farming since Aztec times, survive at Xochimilco. Families and visitors ride colorful boats called trajineras, accompanied by mariachi musicians. Xochimilco is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["Art and museums","Mexico City has one of the largest numbers of museums of any city in the world. The National Museum of Anthropology holds the famous Aztec Sun Stone. Murals by Diego Rivera, José Clemente Orozco and David Alfaro Siqueiros decorate public buildings, and Frida Kahlo’s Blue House in Coyoacán is a beloved museum."],["Food","Mexico City is a paradise for street food: tacos al pastor, tamales, quesadillas, tortas and elotes, corn on the cob. Mexican cuisine is recognized by UNESCO as intangible cultural heritage. Markets such as La Merced sell chilies, fruits and spices from across the country."],["Cleaner air","In the 1990s, Mexico City was considered one of the most polluted cities in the world, as smog trapped in the mountain valley. Policies such as restrictions on driving, cleaner fuels, moving factories and expanding public transport, including the metro and Metrobús, greatly improved air quality, though pollution remains a concern."]],
sp:["The stone of the moon goddess","On 21 February 1978, electrical workers digging in central Mexico City struck a huge carved stone. It turned out to be a giant disk showing Coyolxauhqui, the Aztec moon goddess, dismembered by her brother, the war god Huitzilopochtli. The discovery led archaeologists to excavate the Templo Mayor, the great twin temple at the heart of Tenochtitlan, which had been buried under colonial buildings for more than 450 years. The site and its museum now reveal thousands of Aztec offerings and treasures."],
g:[["Tenochtitlan","The capital city of the Aztec Empire"],["Chinampa","An artificial island used for farming"],["New Spain","The Spanish colony that included Mexico and governed the Philippines"],["Mural","A large painting on a wall"],["Subsidence","The gradual sinking of land"]],
f:["Mexico City was built on the Aztec capital Tenochtitlan.","It lies about 2,240 meters above sea level.","Parts of the city sink many centimeters each year."],
fun:"Workers digging for an electric cable discovered the Aztecs’ great temple in 1978.",
tl:"Ang Mexico City ang kabisera ng Mexico at isa sa pinakamalalaking lungsod sa mundo. Itinayo ito sa guho ng Tenochtitlan, ang kabisera ng mga Aztec. Mula rito pinamahalaan ng Espanya ang Pilipinas noong panahon ng New Spain."});

;
/* data/everyday.js */
// Everyday science and health: the atmosphere, recycling, weather forecasting, disaster preparedness, food safety and screens and health.
ADDART({id:"atmosphere",t:"The Atmosphere",c:"Geography",i:"🌤️",w:"Atmosphere of Earth",r:["climate","climchange","storms","wcycle","volc"],
p:[["A blanket of air","The atmosphere is the layer of gases that surrounds the Earth."],["Layers in the sky","It has several layers, from the troposphere where weather happens to the edge of space."],["Shield and blanket","It protects life from harmful rays and keeps the planet warm."]],
l:"The atmosphere is the layer of gases surrounding the Earth, held in place by gravity. It is made mostly of nitrogen and oxygen, with small amounts of argon, carbon dioxide, water vapor and other gases. The atmosphere provides the air we breathe, shields life from harmful ultraviolet rays and meteors, carries water around the planet as clouds and rain, and keeps the Earth warm enough for life through the greenhouse effect. It becomes thinner with height and is divided into layers, from the troposphere, where weather happens, to the exosphere, which fades into space.",
s:[["What air is made of","Dry air is about 78 percent nitrogen, 21 percent oxygen and almost 1 percent argon. Carbon dioxide makes up only about 0.04 percent but is very important for plants and for warming the planet. Air also contains varying amounts of water vapor, dust and other particles."],["The troposphere","The lowest layer, the troposphere, reaches up to about 8 kilometers at the poles and about 16 kilometers at the equator. It contains most of the air and almost all clouds and weather. The higher you go in the troposphere, the colder it gets, which is why mountaintops are cold."],["The stratosphere and ozone","Above the troposphere, up to about 50 kilometers, is the stratosphere, where large jets sometimes fly to avoid turbulence. It contains the ozone layer, which absorbs most of the Sun’s harmful ultraviolet rays. Temperatures rise with height in the stratosphere because ozone absorbs this energy."],["The upper layers","In the mesosphere, up to about 85 kilometers, most meteors burn up as shooting stars. The thermosphere, where the International Space Station orbits and the northern and southern lights glow, can reach very high temperatures. The exosphere gradually fades into space."]],
k:[["Main gases","Nitrogen (78%) and oxygen (21%)"],["Layers","Troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere, thermosphere, exosphere"],["Where weather happens","The troposphere"],["Ozone layer","In the stratosphere"],["Edge of space","Often set at 100 km (the Kármán line)"]],
d:[["1643","Evangelista Torricelli invents the barometer"],["1859","John Tyndall shows that some gases trap heat"],["1902","The stratosphere is discovered"],["1985","Scientists report the ozone hole over Antarctica"],["1987","The Montreal Protocol to protect the ozone layer is agreed"],["1991","Mount Pinatubo’s eruption cools the planet"]],
s2:[["Air pressure","The weight of the air above pushes on everything, a force called air pressure. At sea level it is about 1 kilogram on every square centimeter. Pressure falls with height: at the top of Mount Everest, it is about a third of that at sea level, so each breath contains far less oxygen, and climbers often use bottled oxygen."],["The greenhouse effect","Gases such as water vapor, carbon dioxide and methane let sunlight through but trap some of the heat the Earth gives off, like a blanket. Without this natural greenhouse effect, the Earth’s average temperature would be well below freezing. Adding more of these gases by burning fossil fuels is strengthening the effect and warming the planet."],["The ozone hole","In 1985 British scientists reported a large thinning of the ozone layer over Antarctica each spring, caused by chemicals called CFCs, once used in refrigerators and spray cans. In 1987 countries agreed the Montreal Protocol to phase them out. The ozone layer is slowly healing and is expected to recover over the coming decades, a major success of international cooperation."],["Why the sky is blue","Sunlight contains all colors. As it passes through the air, tiny gas molecules scatter shorter blue wavelengths much more than longer red ones, so blue light reaches our eyes from all directions. At sunrise and sunset, sunlight travels through more air, scattering away the blue and leaving the sky red and orange."],["Volcanoes and the air","Large volcanic eruptions can send gases and ash high into the stratosphere. After Mount Pinatubo in the Philippines erupted in 1991, a haze of tiny sulfur droplets spread around the world and cooled the Earth’s average temperature by about half a degree Celsius for a year or two."],["How the air formed","The Earth’s early atmosphere had almost no oxygen. Around 2.4 billion years ago, tiny ocean organisms called cyanobacteria, which make oxygen by photosynthesis, began filling the air with it, an event called the Great Oxidation Event. This made complex life, including animals and humans, possible."]],
sp:["Torricelli’s tube of mercury","In 1643 the Italian scientist Evangelista Torricelli filled a long glass tube with mercury, covered its open end and turned it upside down in a bowl of mercury. The mercury in the tube dropped, but only until it was about 76 centimeters high, leaving an empty space at the top. Torricelli realized that the weight of the air pressing on the mercury in the bowl was holding up the column. He had invented the barometer and shown that air has weight. He wrote that we live at the bottom of an ocean of air."],
g:[["Atmosphere","The layer of gases surrounding a planet"],["Ozone layer","A layer of the atmosphere that absorbs ultraviolet rays"],["Air pressure","The force of the air pressing on a surface"],["Greenhouse effect","The warming of a planet by gases that trap heat"]],
f:["Air is about 78 percent nitrogen.","Weather happens in the troposphere.","The ozone layer is slowly recovering."],
fun:"At the top of Mount Everest, the air pressure is only about a third of that at sea level.",
tl:"Ang atmospera ay ang patong ng mga gas na bumabalot sa Daigdig. Binubuo ito karamihan ng nitrogen at oxygen. Pinoprotektahan nito ang buhay mula sa mapanganib na sinag ng araw at pinananatiling mainit ang planeta."});

ADDART({id:"recycling",t:"Recycling and Waste",c:"Geography",i:"♻️",w:"Recycling",r:["plasticwaste","pollution","conservation","metals","paper","foodwaste","ewaste"],
p:[["Less waste first","The best way to deal with waste is to make less of it."],["New from old","Recycling turns used materials into new products."],["A big challenge","Only a small share of all plastic ever made has been recycled."]],
l:"Recycling is the process of collecting used materials, such as paper, glass, metal and some plastics, and turning them into new products instead of throwing them away. It saves raw materials and energy, reduces pollution and keeps waste out of landfills and the oceans. Recycling is one part of a wider approach often summed up as reduce, reuse, recycle, in which making less waste comes first. The world produces billions of tonnes of waste each year, and managing it is a major challenge, especially in fast-growing cities.",
s:[["Reduce, reuse, recycle","Experts rank ways of handling waste in order. Best is to reduce waste, for example by buying less packaging. Next is to reuse items, such as bottles, bags and containers. Then comes recycling, followed by recovering energy from waste. Sending waste to landfills or burning it in the open is the least desirable."],["How recycling works","Recyclable materials are collected, sorted by type, cleaned and processed. Paper is pulped and made into new paper; glass is crushed and melted; metals are melted and recast; and some plastics are shredded, melted and made into pellets for new products."],["Metals and glass","Aluminum can be recycled again and again without losing quality, and recycling it uses about 95 percent less energy than making new aluminum from ore. A recycled can may be back on a store shelf within about two months. Glass can also be recycled endlessly."],["Paper and composting","Paper can be recycled several times before its fibers become too short. Food scraps and garden waste can be composted, turned by microbes into rich soil, instead of rotting in landfills, where they release methane, a powerful greenhouse gas."]],
k:[["Waste hierarchy","Reduce, reuse, recycle"],["Energy saved recycling aluminum","About 95%"],["Recycling symbol designed","1970"],["Plastic ever recycled","Only about 9% (2017 estimate)"],["Philippine waste law","Ecological Solid Waste Management Act (2000)"]],
d:[["1970","The recycling symbol is designed for the first Earth Day"],["1988","Number codes for types of plastic are introduced"],["2000","The Payatas dumpsite collapse leads to a new Philippine waste law"],["2003","Kamikatsu in Japan sets a goal of zero waste"],["2017","A study finds only about 9 percent of plastic ever made has been recycled"],["2018","China stops accepting most imported plastic waste"]],
s2:[["The plastic problem","Plastic is cheap and useful but hard to recycle, because there are many types, often mixed or dirty. A 2017 study estimated that only about 9 percent of all plastic ever made had been recycled; most ended up in landfills or the environment. Many countries are banning single-use plastics and making companies responsible for their packaging."],["The recycling symbol","The familiar symbol of three chasing arrows was designed in 1970 by Gary Anderson, a university student in the United States, for a contest linked to the first Earth Day. Numbers from 1 to 7 inside the symbol on plastic items show the type of plastic, but they do not mean the item will actually be recycled."],["Waste in the Philippines","In July 2000, a mountain of garbage at the Payatas dumpsite in Quezon City collapsed after heavy rains, burying homes and killing more than 200 people. The disaster led to the Ecological Solid Waste Management Act of 2000, which requires waste segregation, recycling and composting. In 2022 a law made large companies responsible for recovering their plastic packaging."],["Waste pickers","In many countries, millions of informal waste pickers collect and sell recyclable materials, recovering much of what is recycled. They often work in dangerous conditions with low pay. Organizations work to improve their safety, income and recognition."],["Electronic waste","Old phones, computers and appliances form one of the fastest-growing kinds of waste. They contain valuable metals such as gold and copper, but also toxic substances. Only a minority of e-waste is properly collected and recycled worldwide."],["Zero-waste communities","The town of Kamikatsu in Japan sorts its waste into more than 40 categories and recycles most of it. In the Philippines, some local governments, such as San Fernando in Pampanga, have been praised for zero-waste programs with door-to-door collection and composting."]],
sp:["The sachet economy","Across Southeast Asia, including the Philippines, many everyday products, such as shampoo, coffee, toothpaste and seasoning, are sold in small single-use plastic sachets. They make products affordable for families who buy only what they can afford each day. But billions of sachets are used every year, and because they are made of layers of plastic and foil, they are almost impossible to recycle. Many end up clogging drains, worsening floods or washing into the sea. Companies and communities are testing refill stations and reusable containers as alternatives."],
g:[["Recycling","Turning used materials into new products"],["Landfill","A site where waste is buried"],["Compost","Rotted plant and food waste used to enrich soil"],["Segregation","Separating waste into different types"]],
f:["Recycling aluminum saves about 95 percent of the energy.","The recycling symbol was designed in 1970.","The Philippines passed a solid waste law in 2000."],
fun:"A recycled aluminum can may be back on a shelf in about two months.",
tl:"Ang recycling ay ang paggawa ng bagong produkto mula sa mga gamit na materyales gaya ng papel, bote, lata at ilang plastik. Mas mabuti pa ring bawasan at muling gamitin ang mga bagay. Sa Pilipinas, ipinag-uutos ng batas ang paghihiwalay ng basura."});

ADDART({id:"weatherforecast",t:"Weather Forecasting",c:"Geography",i:"📡",w:"Weather forecasting",r:["storms","phtyph","climate","monsoon","sat"],
p:[["Predicting the weather","Weather forecasters use science to predict rain, wind, heat and storms."],["Supercomputers","Forecasts are made by powerful computers solving the equations of the atmosphere."],["Saving lives","Typhoon warnings give people time to prepare and evacuate."]],
l:"Weather forecasting is the science of predicting the state of the atmosphere at a future time and place. Forecasters gather millions of observations from weather stations, balloons, ships, aircraft, radar and satellites, then use supercomputers to calculate how the atmosphere will change. Forecasts help people plan their days, farmers plant crops, pilots fly safely and governments warn of storms, floods and heat waves. Today’s five-day forecasts are about as accurate as one-day forecasts were in 1980, and warnings save many lives.",
s:[["Observations","Every day, thousands of weather stations measure temperature, humidity, air pressure, wind and rain. Twice a day, at the same time worldwide, weather balloons carry instruments high into the atmosphere. Satellites watch clouds and storms from space, and radar detects rain and wind inside storms."],["Computer models","The atmosphere follows the laws of physics. Computer models divide the atmosphere into a three-dimensional grid of boxes and calculate how temperature, wind, pressure and moisture will change in each one, step by step into the future. This is called numerical weather prediction."],["The forecaster’s job","Forecasters compare several computer models, check them against observations and their own experience and turn the results into forecasts and warnings people can understand, such as the chance of rain or a typhoon’s expected path."],["Limits of prediction","The atmosphere is chaotic: tiny differences at the start grow into large differences later. This means detailed forecasts become unreliable beyond about one to two weeks. To deal with uncertainty, forecasters run a model many times with slightly different starting conditions, called an ensemble."]],
k:[["Main tools","Weather stations, balloons, satellites, radar, supercomputers"],["Word forecast first used","By Robert FitzRoy, 1860s"],["First computer forecast","1950"],["Reliable range","About one to two weeks"],["Philippine weather agency","PAGASA"]],
d:[["1861","Robert FitzRoy publishes the first public weather forecasts"],["1865","The Manila Observatory is founded"],["1879","Federico Faura issues one of the first typhoon warnings in Asia"],["1922","Lewis Fry Richardson describes forecasting with mathematics"],["1950","The first computer weather forecast is made"],["1960","The first weather satellite, TIROS-1, is launched"]],
s2:[["The first forecasts","Robert FitzRoy, the British naval officer who had captained Charles Darwin’s ship, the Beagle, set up a weather service using telegraph reports and began publishing daily forecasts in a newspaper in 1861, coining the word forecast. His storm warnings saved sailors, but inaccurate forecasts brought ridicule."],["Weather in the Philippines","Jesuit priests founded the Manila Observatory in 1865. In 1879 its director, Father Federico Faura, issued a warning of an approaching typhoon, one of the first in Asia, and his barometer became famous among sailors. Today the Philippine Atmospheric, Geophysical and Astronomical Services Administration, PAGASA, issues forecasts and typhoon signals."],["Typhoon warnings","The Philippines is hit by many tropical cyclones each year. PAGASA names those entering its area and raises Tropical Cyclone Wind Signals from 1 to 5 for affected places, telling people how strong the winds will be. Accurate track forecasts allow evacuations before storms strike."],["Computers and the weather","In 1922 the British scientist Lewis Fry Richardson tried to forecast the weather by solving equations by hand, which took him weeks. In 1950 scientists using ENIAC, one of the first computers, made the first successful computer forecast. Today some of the world’s most powerful supercomputers are used for weather."],["Artificial intelligence","Since about 2023, weather models using artificial intelligence, trained on decades of past weather, have matched or beaten traditional models on many measures while running much faster. Weather agencies have begun using them alongside physics-based models."],["Folk forecasting","Before science, people forecast the weather by watching skies, animals and plants. Sayings such as red sky at night, sailor’s delight contain some truth in places where weather moves from west to east. Farmers in the Philippines and elsewhere have long read signs such as the behavior of ants and birds."]],
sp:["The butterfly effect","In 1961 the American meteorologist Edward Lorenz was running a simple weather simulation on a computer. To save time, he restarted it from the middle, typing in numbers rounded to three decimal places instead of six. To his surprise, the new forecast soon became completely different from the first. Tiny differences had grown enormously. Lorenz realized that long-range weather forecasting might be impossible. He later gave a talk asking whether the flap of a butterfly’s wings in Brazil could set off a tornado in Texas, giving the name butterfly effect to this idea of chaos."],
g:[["Forecast","A prediction of future weather"],["Radar","A system that uses radio waves to detect objects such as rain"],["Ensemble","A set of forecasts with slightly different starting conditions"],["Chaos","Behavior in which small changes lead to large differences"]],
f:["Robert FitzRoy coined the word forecast.","The first computer forecast was made in 1950.","The Manila Observatory was founded in 1865."],
fun:"The idea that a butterfly’s wings could cause a tornado comes from weather science.",
tl:"Ang weather forecasting ay ang paghula sa panahon gamit ang agham. Gumagamit ang mga forecaster ng mga satellite, radar at supercomputer. Sa Pilipinas, ang PAGASA ang naglalabas ng mga babala sa bagyo."});

ADDART({id:"disasterprep",t:"Disaster Preparedness",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🎒",w:"Emergency management",r:["quake","tsunami","phtyph","firstaid","volc"],
p:[["Be ready","Preparing before a disaster can save lives."],["Know the signs","Knowing the warning signs of earthquakes, tsunamis and typhoons helps people act fast."],["Plan together","Families and communities should plan where to go and what to do."]],
l:"Disaster preparedness means planning and getting ready before natural hazards such as earthquakes, typhoons, floods, tsunamis, volcanic eruptions and fires strike, so that people can stay safe and recover more quickly. A hazard becomes a disaster when it harms people who are unprepared or vulnerable. Simple steps, such as having an emergency kit, a family plan and knowledge of warning signs, together with early warning systems, safe buildings and evacuation centers, have greatly reduced deaths from disasters in many countries.",
s:[["Hazards and disasters","A hazard is a natural event, such as a strong earthquake or typhoon, that could cause harm. Whether it becomes a disaster depends on how many people are exposed and how prepared they are. Strong buildings, warnings and planning can turn a potential disaster into a manageable event."],["An emergency kit","Experts advise keeping a go bag with supplies for at least three days: drinking water, ready-to-eat food, a flashlight, a battery-powered or hand-crank radio, spare batteries, a first aid kit, medicines, a whistle, copies of important documents, some cash and a phone charger or power bank."],["A family plan","Families should agree on how to contact each other, where to meet if separated and where the nearest evacuation center is. Children should learn emergency phone numbers, such as 911 in the Philippines, and how to get help."],["Early warnings","Warning systems, such as typhoon signals, tsunami sirens, flood alerts and text messages to phones, give people precious time to act. The United Nations aims for everyone on Earth to be covered by early warning systems by 2027."]],
k:[["Kit should last","At least three days"],["Earthquake rule","Drop, Cover and Hold On"],["Tsunami rule","Go to high ground immediately"],["Philippine emergency number","911"],["Global framework","Sendai Framework (2015–2030)"]],
d:[["1923","The Great Kantō earthquake leads Japan to create a Disaster Prevention Day"],["1970","The Bhola cyclone kills hundreds of thousands in Bangladesh"],["2004","The Indian Ocean tsunami strikes"],["2010","The Philippines passes its disaster risk reduction law"],["2013","Typhoon Haiyan (Yolanda) devastates the central Philippines"],["2015","The Sendai Framework for disaster risk reduction is adopted"]],
s2:[["Earthquakes","During an earthquake, Drop to the ground, take Cover under a sturdy table and Hold On until the shaking stops. Stay away from windows and heavy objects. If outdoors, move to an open area away from buildings, trees and power lines. In the Philippines, where the same steps are taught as Duck, Cover and Hold, schools practice them in nationwide earthquake drills."],["Tsunamis","If you are near the coast and feel a strong or long earthquake, or see the sea suddenly drawing back, move immediately to high ground or far inland. Do not wait for an official warning, and do not go to look at the water. Tsunamis can arrive as a series of waves over hours."],["Typhoons and floods","Before a typhoon, secure loose objects, stock supplies, charge phones and follow official warnings. Evacuate early if told to. During floods, never walk or drive through moving water: just 15 centimeters of fast water can knock a person down, and about 60 centimeters can sweep away a car."],["Fires","Install smoke alarms and plan two ways out of every room. If there is smoke, stay low, where the air is cleaner. If your clothes catch fire, stop, drop and roll. Never go back into a burning building."],["Lessons from Haiyan","In November 2013, Typhoon Haiyan, known in the Philippines as Yolanda, one of the strongest storms ever to make landfall, struck the central Philippines. Its storm surge, a wall of seawater several meters high, killed more than 6,000 people, many in Tacloban. Many residents had not understood the term storm surge, and warnings now describe it in plain language, such as a tsunami-like wave."],["Saving lives in Bangladesh","In 1970 the Bhola cyclone killed hundreds of thousands of people in what is now Bangladesh. Since then, Bangladesh has built thousands of cyclone shelters and trained tens of thousands of volunteers to spread warnings. Later cyclones of similar strength have killed far fewer people, showing how preparation saves lives."]],
sp:["Tilly’s geography lesson","On 26 December 2004, ten-year-old Tilly Smith from England was on a beach in Phuket, Thailand, with her family when she noticed the sea bubbling and rapidly pulling back from the shore. Just two weeks earlier, her geography teacher had taught her class about tsunamis. Tilly warned her parents that a tsunami was coming. They alerted others, and the beach was cleared moments before giant waves struck. About 100 people were saved. Tilly’s story shows how knowing the warning signs can save lives."],
g:[["Hazard","A natural event that could cause harm"],["Evacuation","Moving people away from danger"],["Storm surge","A rise in sea level pushed ashore by a storm"],["Early warning","An alert given before a hazard strikes"]],
f:["An emergency kit should last at least three days.","During an earthquake, Drop, Cover and Hold On.","Never walk through moving floodwater."],
fun:"A ten-year-old girl saved about 100 people in 2004 because she remembered a geography lesson about tsunamis.",
tl:"Ang paghahanda sa sakuna ay pagpaplano bago dumating ang lindol, bagyo, baha o tsunami. Mahalaga ang go bag, plano ng pamilya at pag-alam sa mga babala. Sa lindol, tandaan ang Duck, Cover and Hold."});

ADDART({id:"foodsafety",t:"Food Safety",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🍽️",w:"Food safety",r:["hygiene","nutri","microbe","pasteur","refrigeration"],
p:[["Safe to eat","Food safety means handling, cooking and storing food to prevent illness."],["Germs in food","Bacteria, viruses and parasites in food can make people sick."],["Five keys","Keep clean, separate raw and cooked, cook well, keep food at safe temperatures and use safe water."]],
l:"Food safety is the practice of handling, preparing, cooking and storing food in ways that prevent foodborne illness, often called food poisoning. Food can carry harmful bacteria, viruses, parasites and chemicals. The World Health Organization estimates that around 600 million people fall ill and hundreds of thousands die each year from unsafe food, with children under five most at risk. Simple habits, such as washing hands, cooking food thoroughly and keeping it at safe temperatures, prevent most cases, while laws and inspections help keep food safe from farm to table.",
s:[["What causes food poisoning","Common causes include bacteria such as Salmonella, Escherichia coli, Campylobacter and Listeria, viruses such as norovirus and hepatitis A, and parasites. Symptoms can include stomach pain, vomiting, diarrhea and fever, usually starting hours to days after eating."],["Keep clean","Wash your hands with soap for at least 20 seconds before handling food, after using the toilet and after touching raw meat. Keep kitchen surfaces, utensils and dishcloths clean, and keep insects, pests and pets away from food."],["Separate raw and cooked","Raw meat, poultry and seafood can carry germs that spread to other foods. Use separate cutting boards and knives, store raw meat below other foods in the refrigerator and never put cooked food on a plate that held raw meat."],["Cook thoroughly","Cooking kills most harmful germs. Meat, poultry, eggs and seafood should be cooked until steaming hot all the way through, with no pink juices in chicken. Reheat leftovers until they are steaming hot."]],
k:[["People sickened each year","About 600 million (WHO estimate)"],["Danger zone","About 5 °C to 60 °C"],["Handwashing time","At least 20 seconds"],["World Food Safety Day","7 June"],["Philippine law","Food Safety Act of 2013"]],
d:[["1810","Nicolas Appert publishes his method of preserving food in sealed containers"],["1864","Louis Pasteur develops pasteurization"],["1906","The United States passes its first major food safety law"],["1907","Mary Mallon, called Typhoid Mary, is identified as a carrier of typhoid"],["1963","The Codex Alimentarius international food standards are created"],["2019","The first World Food Safety Day is held"]],
s2:[["Safe temperatures","Bacteria multiply quickly between about 5 °C and 60 °C, called the danger zone. Hot food should be kept hot and cold food cold. Leftovers should be cooled and refrigerated within about two hours, or sooner in hot climates, and not left out at room temperature."],["Rice and leftovers","Cooked rice can carry spores of a bacterium called Bacillus cereus, which survive cooking. If rice is left warm for hours, the bacteria grow and make toxins that reheating does not destroy. Cooling rice quickly and refrigerating it helps prevent illness, sometimes called fried rice syndrome."],["Safe water and food","Use safe drinking water for cooking, washing produce and making ice. Choose fresh, undamaged food, wash fruits and vegetables and check expiry dates. A best before date refers to quality, while a use by or expiry date refers to safety."],["Street food","Street food is loved around the world, from Filipino isaw and fishballs to tacos and samosas. To eat safely, choose busy stalls where food is cooked fresh and hot in front of you, with clean water and utensils, and avoid food left standing at warm temperatures."],["Preserving food","People have preserved food for thousands of years by drying, salting, smoking, fermenting and pickling, such as the Filipino tuyo, daing and atchara. Canning, invented by the French cook Nicolas Appert, pasteurization and refrigeration later made food safer and longer-lasting."],["Food safety laws","After the American writer Upton Sinclair described filthy meatpacking plants in his 1906 novel The Jungle, the United States passed food safety laws. Today the Codex Alimentarius sets international food standards, and countries such as the Philippines, under the Food Safety Act of 2013, inspect food producers and markets."]],
sp:["Typhoid Mary","In the early 1900s, a cook named Mary Mallon worked for wealthy families in New York. Wherever she worked, people fell ill with typhoid fever, a serious disease. In 1907 investigators discovered that she carried typhoid bacteria in her body without feeling sick herself, and that she spread it through the food she prepared, especially a dessert of ice cream with raw peaches. Mallon refused to believe she was the cause and was forced into isolation for many years. Her case showed that healthy-looking people can spread disease through food and made handwashing for cooks a priority."],
g:[["Foodborne illness","Sickness caused by eating contaminated food"],["Bacteria","Tiny single-celled living things, some of which cause disease"],["Pasteurization","Heating food or drink to kill harmful germs"],["Cross-contamination","The spread of germs from one food or surface to another"]],
f:["About 600 million people get sick from unsafe food each year.","Bacteria grow fastest between about 5 °C and 60 °C.","World Food Safety Day is 7 June."],
fun:"Typhoid Mary spread disease through her cooking without ever feeling sick.",
tl:"Ang kaligtasan sa pagkain ay ang wastong paghawak, pagluluto at pag-iimbak ng pagkain upang maiwasan ang sakit. Maghugas ng kamay, ihiwalay ang hilaw sa luto, lutuing mabuti at panatilihin ang pagkain sa ligtas na temperatura."});

ADDART({id:"screentime",t:"Screens and Health",c:"Health and the Body",i:"📲",w:"Screen time",r:["sleep","eye","mental","smartphone","socialmedia"],
p:[["Screens everywhere","Phones, tablets, computers and televisions are part of daily life."],["Balance matters","Too much screen time can affect sleep, eyes, posture and activity."],["Content counts","What people do on screens matters as much as how long they use them."]],
l:"Screen time is the amount of time spent using devices with screens, such as smartphones, tablets, computers, televisions and game consoles. Screens bring great benefits for learning, work, creativity and staying in touch, but spending many hours on them can affect sleep, eyesight, posture, physical activity and, according to some research, mental health, especially in children and teenagers. Health experts recommend balancing screen use with sleep, exercise, outdoor play and time with family and friends, and paying attention to what is watched or done, not just for how long.",
s:[["Sleep","Using screens late in the evening can delay sleep. Bright light, especially blue light from screens, signals the brain to stay awake, and exciting games, videos and messages make it hard to relax. Experts suggest putting screens away an hour before bed and keeping phones out of bedrooms."],["Eyes","Staring at screens for long periods can cause digital eye strain, with tired, dry eyes, blurred vision and headaches, because people blink less. The 20-20-20 rule helps: every 20 minutes, look at something about 20 feet, or 6 meters, away for 20 seconds."],["Nearsightedness","Myopia, or nearsightedness, is rising sharply worldwide, especially in East and Southeast Asia, where most young people in some cities are now nearsighted. Research suggests that spending more time outdoors in daylight helps protect children’s eyes, while long hours of close-up work, including screens, may add to the risk."],["Body and activity","Long hours sitting with screens can mean less physical activity and poor posture, such as bending the neck over a phone, sometimes called text neck. Health guidelines recommend that children and teenagers get at least an hour of physical activity a day."]],
k:[["WHO advice for under-2s","No sedentary screen time"],["WHO advice for ages 2–4","No more than 1 hour a day"],["Eye-care tip","The 20-20-20 rule"],["Bedtime tip","No screens an hour before sleep"],["Gaming disorder","Recognized by WHO in 2019"]],
d:[["1950s","Television spreads into homes"],["2007","Smartphones with touchscreens become popular"],["2018","France bans mobile phones in schools for younger students"],["2019","WHO publishes screen time guidelines for young children"],["2023","A UNESCO report urges caution about phones in classrooms"],["2025","Australia’s minimum age of 16 for social media accounts takes effect"]],
s2:[["Young children","The World Health Organization’s 2019 guidelines advise no sedentary screen time for babies under two and no more than one hour a day for children aged two to four, with more time for active play, reading and sleep. Talking, playing and reading with adults are vital for young children’s development."],["Mental health","Researchers disagree about how much screens and social media affect young people’s mental health. Some, such as the American psychologist Jonathan Haidt, argue that smartphones and social media have contributed to rising anxiety and depression among teenagers. Others say the evidence is weak or mixed and that effects depend on the person and how screens are used. Most agree that cyberbullying, lack of sleep and replacing in-person activities can be harmful."],["Phones in schools","Many countries and schools have restricted smartphones in classrooms to reduce distraction and bullying, including France, the Netherlands, China and parts of the Philippines. A 2023 UNESCO report recommended that phones be used in schools only when they help learning."],["Gaming","Video games can build problem-solving skills, creativity and friendships. But a small number of people develop gaming disorder, in which gaming takes over their lives and causes serious problems; the World Health Organization recognized it in 2019."],["Social media age limits","Several countries are debating minimum ages for social media. Australia passed a law setting a minimum age of 16 for many social media accounts, which took effect in December 2025. Supporters say it protects children; critics question whether it can be enforced and worry about privacy."],["Healthy habits","Tips for healthy screen use include setting screen-free times, such as during meals; turning off unnecessary notifications; using built-in tools to track screen time; choosing quality content; taking breaks to move; and talking with family about online safety. Adults can set a good example."]],
sp:["The 20-20-20 rule","When people read or look at screens up close, the muscles that focus their eyes stay tense, and they blink up to half as often as usual, drying their eyes. Eye doctors recommend a simple habit called the 20-20-20 rule: every 20 minutes, look at something about 20 feet, or 6 meters, away for at least 20 seconds. This lets the focusing muscles relax and reminds people to blink. Some people set timers or apps to remind them. It is an easy way to reduce tired eyes for students and office workers alike."],
g:[["Screen time","Time spent using devices with screens"],["Myopia","Nearsightedness; difficulty seeing distant things clearly"],["Sedentary","Involving sitting with little physical activity"],["Digital eye strain","Eye discomfort from long screen use"]],
f:["WHO advises no screen time for babies under two.","Time outdoors helps protect children’s eyesight.","Australia set a minimum age of 16 for social media."],
fun:"People blink up to half as often as usual while looking at screens.",
tl:"Ang screen time ay oras na ginugugol sa cellphone, tablet, computer at telebisyon. Kapaki-pakinabang ang mga screen ngunit ang labis na paggamit ay maaaring makaapekto sa tulog, mata at kalusugan. Mahalaga ang balanse at paglabas sa labas."});

;
/* data/modern.js */
// Technology and society articles: commercial spaceflight, misinformation, streaming, cloud computing, online shopping and digital payments.
ADDART({id:"commspace",t:"Commercial Spaceflight",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🛰️",w:"Private spaceflight",r:["rocket","spaceagencies","iss","sat","spacejunk"],
p:[["Companies in space","Private companies now build rockets and carry satellites and astronauts into space."],["Fly, land, repeat","Rockets that land and fly again have made reaching space much cheaper."],["Paying passengers","A few paying passengers have flown into space."]],
l:"Commercial spaceflight is space travel carried out by private companies rather than only by government space agencies. Since the early 2000s, companies such as SpaceX, Blue Origin, Rocket Lab and others have developed rockets and spacecraft that launch satellites, carry cargo and astronauts to the International Space Station, take tourists to the edge of space and even land on the Moon. Reusable rockets have greatly lowered the cost of reaching orbit. Commercial spaceflight has opened space to more countries and companies but also raises concerns about space junk, light pollution in the night sky and the power of a few firms.",
s:[["From agencies to companies","For decades, only governments, such as the United States and the Soviet Union, could afford to launch rockets. Private companies built parts but did not run missions. In the 2000s, governments, especially NASA, began buying launch services from companies instead of owning the rockets themselves."],["SpaceX","SpaceX, founded by Elon Musk in 2002, reached orbit with its Falcon 1 rocket in 2008, the first privately developed liquid-fueled rocket to do so. In 2012 its Dragon capsule became the first commercial spacecraft to deliver cargo to the International Space Station, and in 2020 Crew Dragon carried NASA astronauts there."],["Reusable rockets","In December 2015, a Falcon 9 first stage landed upright back on Earth after launching satellites, and boosters have since flown many times. Reusing rockets, rather than throwing them away after each launch, has sharply reduced costs and increased the number of launches worldwide."],["Space tourism","In 2001 the American businessman Dennis Tito paid to fly to the International Space Station on a Russian Soyuz. In July 2021, Virgin Galactic and Blue Origin flew their founders and passengers on short trips to the edge of space. In September 2021, Inspiration4 became the first orbital mission with an all-civilian crew."]],
k:[["Major companies","SpaceX, Blue Origin, Rocket Lab, Virgin Galactic"],["First commercial cargo to the ISS","2012"],["First orbital booster landing","2015"],["First commercial crew to the ISS","2020"],["First commercial Moon landing","2024"]],
d:[["2001","Dennis Tito becomes the first paying space tourist"],["2004","SpaceShipOne wins the Ansari X Prize"],["2008","SpaceX’s Falcon 1 reaches orbit"],["2015","A Falcon 9 booster lands after reaching space"],["2020","Crew Dragon carries astronauts to the International Space Station"],["2024","Intuitive Machines makes the first commercial Moon landing"]],
s2:[["The X Prize","In 2004, SpaceShipOne, a small rocket plane built by Scaled Composites and funded by Paul Allen, flew to space twice within two weeks, winning the $10 million Ansari X Prize. The prize inspired investment in private spaceflight, much as prizes for aviation had done a century earlier."],["Satellite constellations","Companies are launching thousands of small satellites into low orbit to provide internet service around the world, including to remote areas, ships and disaster zones. SpaceX’s Starlink has become the largest. Astronomers worry that the bright satellites streak across telescope images and spoil views of the night sky."],["Commercial Moon landings","In February 2024, Intuitive Machines’ Odysseus lander touched down near the Moon’s south pole, the first commercial spacecraft to land on the Moon, though it tipped over. In March 2025, Firefly Aerospace’s Blue Ghost made a fully successful landing. These missions carry instruments for NASA and other customers."],["A global industry","Commercial space companies now operate in many countries, including Rocket Lab, which launches from New Zealand, private rocket makers in China and India, and satellite companies across Europe, Asia and Africa. Smaller countries can now buy launches for their own satellites."],["Space and the Philippines","The Philippines built its first microsatellite, Diwata-1, with Japanese partners; it was released from the International Space Station in 2016. In 2019 the country created the Philippine Space Agency. Commercial launches have made it easier for Southeast Asian countries to put satellites into orbit for weather, mapping and disaster monitoring."],["Concerns","The rapid growth of launches adds to space debris, which threatens satellites and astronauts. Critics also raise concerns about the environmental effects of rocket launches, the concentration of power in a few companies and billionaires, and whether space tourism for the wealthy is a good use of resources. Supporters argue that commercial space lowers costs and speeds up exploration and science."]],
sp:["Landing a rocket on its tail","For more than 50 years, rocket boosters fell into the ocean after launch and were lost. On 21 December 2015, after several failed attempts, the first stage of a SpaceX Falcon 9 rocket, about 40 meters tall, separated from its upper stage, flipped around, relit its engines and descended back to Cape Canaveral, Florida. As crowds watched, it slowed, unfolded four legs and landed upright on a concrete pad. Engineers compared it to balancing a pencil on your hand in a storm. Today booster landings, on land and on ships at sea, have become routine."],
g:[["Commercial","Done by businesses to make money"],["Reusable rocket","A rocket that can be flown more than once"],["Space tourism","Travel into space for recreation"],["Satellite constellation","A large group of satellites working together"]],
f:["SpaceX reached orbit in 2008.","The first booster landing after reaching space was in 2015.","The first commercial Moon landing was in 2024."],
fun:"Engineers compared landing a rocket to balancing a pencil on your hand in a storm.",
tl:"Ang commercial spaceflight ay paglalakbay sa kalawakan na isinasagawa ng mga pribadong kompanya. Pinababa ng mga muling nagagamit na rocket ang gastos. Nakapagdala na sila ng mga astronaut sa International Space Station at nakalapag sa Buwan."});

ADDART({id:"misinformation",t:"Misinformation and Fact-Checking",c:"Technology",i:"🔎",w:"Misinformation",r:["socialmedia","journalism","internet","ai","democ"],
p:[["Fake or real?","Misinformation is false or misleading information, spread by mistake or on purpose."],["Fast and far","False stories can spread faster online than true ones."],["Check before sharing","Simple habits help people tell reliable information from falsehoods."]],
l:"Misinformation is false or misleading information. When it is spread deliberately to deceive people, it is often called disinformation. False claims, rumors and propaganda are as old as human communication, but the internet and social media allow them to spread to millions of people within hours, sometimes with serious effects on health, elections and public safety. Fact-checkers, journalists, researchers and platforms work to identify falsehoods, while media literacy teaches people to evaluate what they see. Deciding how to fight misinformation while protecting free expression is widely debated.",
s:[["Kinds of false information","Misinformation is shared by people who believe it is true. Disinformation is created to deceive, for example for political or financial gain. Other problems include misleading headlines, real photos used out of context, edited videos, satire mistaken for news and conspiracy theories."],["Why it spreads","False stories are often surprising, emotional or confirm what people already believe, which makes them more likely to be shared. A 2018 study of Twitter found that false news stories spread farther and faster than true ones, largely because people, not automated accounts, shared them."],["Fact-checking","Fact-checkers research claims made by politicians, viral posts and news reports, then publish their findings with evidence. Many belong to the International Fact-Checking Network, founded in 2015, which sets standards such as nonpartisanship and transparency. Fact-checking organizations operate in many countries, including the Philippines."],["Media literacy","Media literacy is the ability to find, evaluate and create information responsibly. Many schools teach it; in the Philippines, Media and Information Literacy is a subject in senior high school. It helps people recognize reliable sources, understand how news and advertising work and avoid being misled."]],
k:[["Misinformation","False information shared by mistake"],["Disinformation","False information spread on purpose"],["Fact-checking network founded","2015"],["International Fact-Checking Day","2 April"],["Key habit","Check before you share"]],
d:[["1835","A New York newspaper publishes the Great Moon Hoax"],["1938","A radio drama about a Martian invasion alarms some listeners"],["2015","The International Fact-Checking Network is founded"],["2017","The term deepfake comes into use"],["2018","A major study shows false news spreads faster online"],["2020","The WHO warns of an infodemic during the COVID-19 pandemic"]],
s2:[["How to check","Experts recommend simple steps: stop before sharing; check who is behind the information; search for the same claim in trusted news sources; look for the original source of a quote, photo or video; and check the date. Professional fact-checkers read laterally, opening new tabs to see what other sources say about a website, rather than judging it by how it looks."],["Images and deepfakes","Photos can be edited or shown out of context, and artificial intelligence can now create realistic fake images, voices and videos, called deepfakes. A reverse image search can show where a photo first appeared. Signs of AI-generated images include strange hands, text or backgrounds, though these are becoming harder to spot."],["Health misinformation","During the COVID-19 pandemic, false claims about cures, vaccines and the virus spread widely, which the World Health Organization called an infodemic. Health misinformation can lead people to avoid vaccines or use dangerous treatments, so health agencies work to share accurate information quickly."],["Misinformation and politics","False information has been used to influence elections and public opinion in many countries, including through networks of fake accounts and paid online campaigners. Researchers have studied such campaigns in the Philippines, the United States, Brazil, India and elsewhere. Different groups often accuse each other of spreading falsehoods, which is why independent, evidence-based checking matters."],["Free expression","Governments and platforms have tried to limit misinformation by labeling, removing or reducing the spread of false posts, and some countries have passed laws against fake news. Critics warn that such measures can be misused to silence journalists, critics and minority views. Many experts favor transparency, fact-checking and education over censorship."],["Journalists under pressure","Journalists who investigate disinformation sometimes face harassment. In 2021 the Nobel Peace Prize was awarded to the Filipino journalist Maria Ressa and the Russian journalist Dmitry Muratov for their efforts to safeguard freedom of expression, a decision that was praised by press freedom groups and criticized by their governments’ supporters."]],
sp:["The Great Moon Hoax","In August 1835, the New York newspaper The Sun published a series of articles claiming that the famous astronomer Sir John Herschel had used a powerful new telescope to discover life on the Moon: forests, oceans, bison-like animals, beavers that walked upright and winged, bat-like humans. The detailed stories, presented as reprinted from a scientific journal that no longer existed, caused a sensation, and the newspaper’s sales soared. Many readers believed them for weeks before the hoax was revealed. Herschel, who was in South Africa, knew nothing about it."],
g:[["Misinformation","False information shared without intent to deceive"],["Disinformation","False information spread deliberately"],["Fact-checking","Checking whether claims are true using evidence"],["Deepfake","A realistic fake image, video or audio made with AI"]],
f:["False news can spread faster online than true news.","International Fact-Checking Day is 2 April.","Media and Information Literacy is taught in Philippine senior high schools."],
fun:"In 1835 a newspaper convinced many readers that there were bat-people living on the Moon.",
tl:"Ang misinformation ay maling impormasyon na ikinakalat nang hindi sinasadya, habang ang disinformation ay sinadyang panlilinlang. Bago magbahagi, suriin ang pinagmulan, maghanap sa mapagkakatiwalaang balita at tingnan ang petsa."});

ADDART({id:"streaming",t:"Streaming and Digital Media",c:"Technology",i:"▶️",w:"Streaming media",r:["internet","tv","cinema","kpop","opm"],
p:[["Watch and listen anytime","Streaming lets people play music, videos and shows over the internet without downloading them first."],["A new way to share","Video platforms let anyone publish to a worldwide audience."],["Changing industries","Streaming transformed the music, film and television industries."]],
l:"Streaming is the delivery of music, video and other media over the internet so that people can watch or listen almost instantly, without first downloading whole files. Services such as YouTube, Spotify, Netflix and many others have changed how billions of people enjoy entertainment, letting them choose what to watch or hear, when and where. Streaming has made music and films from around the world, such as K-pop, Korean dramas and Philippine series, easy to find globally, but it has also raised debates about artists’ pay, screen time and the power of recommendation algorithms.",
s:[["How streaming works","Media files are broken into small pieces and sent over the internet. The device plays the first pieces while the rest are still arriving, storing a little ahead in a buffer. Compression makes the files smaller, and services adjust the quality to the speed of each viewer’s connection."],["Music","After file sharing and digital downloads in the 1990s and 2000s, music streaming services, such as Spotify, launched in 2008, offered huge catalogs for a monthly fee or free with ads. Streaming now provides most of the recorded music industry’s income worldwide."],["Video","YouTube, founded in 2005, allowed anyone to upload and share videos, creating new kinds of stars and content. Netflix, which began by renting DVDs by mail, started streaming in 2007 and later produced its own shows and films, followed by many other services."],["Live streaming","Live streaming lets people broadcast events as they happen, from concerts and sports to video games, classes and religious services. Platforms such as Twitch, YouTube, Facebook and TikTok host millions of live streams, and live selling has become popular in the Philippines and elsewhere."]],
k:[["Meaning","Playing media over the internet as it arrives"],["YouTube founded","2005"],["Netflix streaming began","2007"],["Spotify launched","2008"],["Share of internet traffic","Video is the largest share"]],
d:[["1995","Early internet radio and audio streaming begins"],["1999","Napster popularizes music file sharing"],["2005","YouTube is founded"],["2007","Netflix begins streaming"],["2008","Spotify launches"],["2021","Squid Game becomes one of the most-watched streaming series ever"]],
s2:[["A global stage","Streaming has helped music and shows from outside the English-speaking world find worldwide audiences. K-pop groups and Korean dramas such as Squid Game, Indian films, Spanish-language series, Nigerian Nollywood movies and Filipino music and series reach fans across continents."],["Paying artists","Streaming services pay artists and rights holders a tiny amount for each play, usually a fraction of a cent. Popular artists can earn a lot, but many musicians say streaming pays too little to make a living. The debate over fair pay continues."],["Algorithms","Streaming platforms use recommendation algorithms to suggest what to watch or hear next, based on users’ habits. This helps people discover new content but can also encourage binge-watching and narrow the range of what people see."],["Creators","Anyone with a phone can become a content creator, building audiences on YouTube, TikTok or other platforms and earning money from ads, sponsors and fans. Filipino vloggers and creators have large followings at home and among Filipinos abroad."],["Piracy","Before streaming, illegal downloading of music and films was widespread. Affordable, convenient streaming services reduced piracy in many places, though illegal streaming sites still exist."],["The cost to the planet","Streaming uses electricity in data centers, networks and devices. Video makes up the largest share of internet traffic. Experts debate the size of its environmental footprint, which is reduced by more efficient technology and renewable energy."]],
sp:["Me at the zoo","On 23 April 2005, Jawed Karim, one of the three founders of a new website called YouTube, uploaded its very first video. Titled Me at the zoo, it lasts just 19 seconds and shows Karim standing in front of the elephants at the San Diego Zoo, remarking that they have really, really long trunks. The video is still on YouTube and has been viewed hundreds of millions of times. Within a year and a half, YouTube was so popular that Google bought it for $1.65 billion. Today hundreds of hours of video are uploaded to it every minute."],
g:[["Streaming","Playing media over the internet as it arrives"],["Buffer","A small store of data kept ahead to keep playback smooth"],["Compression","Making files smaller for faster sending"],["Content creator","A person who makes videos, music or posts for an online audience"]],
f:["YouTube was founded in 2005.","Streaming provides most recorded music income.","Video is the largest share of internet traffic."],
fun:"The first YouTube video, uploaded in 2005, is 19 seconds long and shows elephants at a zoo.",
tl:"Ang streaming ay ang pagpapatugtog ng musika at video sa internet nang hindi muna ito dina-download. Binago nito ang industriya ng musika, pelikula at telebisyon at pinadali ang pagkalat ng K-pop, K-drama at OPM sa buong mundo."});

ADDART({id:"cloud",t:"Cloud Computing",c:"Technology",i:"☁️",w:"Cloud computing",r:["internet","comp","cybersecurity","ai","elec"],
p:[["Computers you rent","Cloud computing means using computers, storage and software over the internet."],["Buildings full of computers","The cloud is really huge buildings full of computers."],["Everywhere","Email, photo backups, streaming and AI all run in the cloud."]],
l:"Cloud computing is the delivery of computing services, such as storage, processing power, databases and software, over the internet from large data centers, instead of from a person’s own computer or a company’s own servers. When people save photos online, use web email, stream videos, collaborate on shared documents or use AI chatbots, they are using the cloud. Cloud computing lets businesses and individuals rent as much computing as they need, but it also concentrates huge amounts of data and power in a few companies and uses large amounts of electricity and water.",
s:[["What the cloud is","The cloud is not in the sky. It is made up of data centers, buildings packed with thousands of servers, connected by high-speed networks. Users reach these computers through the internet and pay for what they use, as they would for electricity or water."],["Kinds of cloud services","Software as a service provides ready-to-use programs, such as email or office tools, through a web browser. Platform as a service gives developers tools to build apps. Infrastructure as a service rents out raw computing power and storage."],["Beginnings","In 1961 the computer scientist John McCarthy suggested that computing might one day be sold like a public utility. Web email services and online software grew in the 1990s and 2000s. In 2006 Amazon Web Services began renting storage and computing power to anyone, and the term cloud computing became popular."],["Why people use it","The cloud lets organizations start quickly without buying expensive equipment, add capacity when demand rises and access their data from anywhere. Files saved in the cloud are backed up if a device is lost. Teams can work on the same documents at the same time."]],
k:[["Meaning","Computing services delivered over the internet"],["Physical form","Data centers full of servers"],["Service types","SaaS, PaaS, IaaS"],["Big providers","Amazon, Microsoft, Google, Alibaba"],["Key concerns","Outages, privacy, energy use"]],
d:[["1961","John McCarthy imagines computing as a utility"],["1999","Business software delivered over the web begins to spread"],["2004","Web email with large free storage appears"],["2006","Amazon Web Services begins renting computing power"],["2010","Microsoft launches its cloud platform"],["2020s","Artificial intelligence drives rapid growth of data centers"]],
s2:[["Data centers","Data centers can be as large as several football fields, filled with racks of servers that run day and night. They need enormous amounts of electricity and cooling, often using water. Companies build them near cheap power, cool climates or major internet connections, including increasingly in Southeast Asia."],["Energy and water","Data centers use a growing share of the world’s electricity, and demand is rising quickly because of artificial intelligence, which needs vast computing power. Companies are investing in renewable energy and more efficient cooling, but local communities sometimes worry about the strain on power grids and water supplies."],["Outages","Because so many services depend on a few cloud providers, a single failure can disrupt websites, apps, banks and airlines around the world at once. Major outages have shown the risks of relying heavily on a small number of companies."],["Privacy and security","Storing personal and business data in the cloud raises questions about who can access it, including companies, hackers and governments. Providers use encryption and security measures, and laws such as data privacy acts set rules on how data may be stored and used."],["Data sovereignty","Many countries require that certain data, such as government or health records, be stored within their borders. This has led cloud companies to build data centers in more countries, including the Philippines, which adopted a cloud-first policy for government in 2017."],["The cloud and everyday life","Much of modern life runs in the cloud: messaging apps, online games, streaming services, maps, online banking, school learning platforms and AI assistants. When a phone backs up photos overnight, it is sending them to a data center that may be on another continent."]],
sp:["There is no cloud","A popular sticker among computer engineers reads: There is no cloud, it’s just someone else’s computer. The joke makes an important point. The word cloud comes from diagrams in which engineers drew the internet as a fluffy cloud, because the details inside did not matter. But when people store files in the cloud, their data is sitting on real hard drives in real buildings, owned by real companies, in real countries with their own laws. Understanding this helps people think about privacy, security and what happens if a service shuts down."],
g:[["Cloud computing","Using computing services over the internet"],["Server","A computer that provides data or services to other computers"],["Data center","A building full of servers"],["Data sovereignty","The idea that data is subject to the laws of the country where it is stored"]],
f:["The cloud runs in data centers full of servers.","Amazon Web Services began in 2006.","AI is driving rapid growth in data centers."],
fun:"Engineers joke that the cloud is just someone else’s computer.",
tl:"Ang cloud computing ay ang paggamit ng storage, software at lakas ng computer sa pamamagitan ng internet mula sa malalaking data center. Nasa cloud ang email, mga backup ng litrato, streaming at AI."});

ADDART({id:"ecommerce",t:"Online Shopping and E-commerce",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🛒",w:"E-commerce",r:["internet","trade","money","smartphone","plasticwaste"],
p:[["Shopping from home","E-commerce means buying and selling goods and services over the internet."],["Giant marketplaces","Online marketplaces connect millions of sellers and buyers."],["Shopping festivals","Big online sales, such as 11.11, have become shopping events."]],
l:"E-commerce, or electronic commerce, is the buying and selling of goods and services over the internet, through websites, apps and social media. It ranges from huge online marketplaces such as Amazon, Alibaba, Shopee, Lazada, MercadoLibre and Jumia to small businesses selling through social media and live streams. Online shopping lets people compare prices and buy almost anything from home, and it gives small sellers access to customers far away. It has also raised concerns about scams, fake products, packaging waste, delivery workers’ conditions and the effect on local shops.",
s:[["Beginnings","Some say the first secure online purchase took place on 11 August 1994, when a man in the United States bought a music CD over the internet. Amazon, founded in 1994, began selling books online in 1995, the same year eBay started as an online auction site."],["Online marketplaces","Marketplaces let many sellers offer products on one website or app. Alibaba, founded in China in 1999, connects businesses, and its Taobao site serves consumers. In Southeast Asia, Shopee and Lazada are among the most popular, while MercadoLibre leads in Latin America and Jumia operates in Africa."],["How online shopping works","Shoppers browse products, read reviews, add items to a cart and pay online or on delivery. Warehouses pack the orders, and couriers deliver them, often by motorcycle in Asian cities. Returns and customer service are handled online."],["Mobile shopping","Most online shopping in many countries now happens on smartphones. Apps send notifications about sales, and payments can be made with e-wallets. Social media platforms have added shopping features."]],
k:[["Meaning","Buying and selling over the internet"],["Early online sale","1994"],["Big marketplaces","Amazon, Alibaba, Shopee, Lazada, MercadoLibre, Jumia"],["Biggest sale event","Singles’ Day, 11 November"],["Philippine law","Internet Transactions Act (2023)"]],
d:[["1994","An early secure online purchase is made"],["1995","Amazon and eBay begin selling online"],["1999","Alibaba is founded in China"],["2009","Singles’ Day becomes an online shopping festival"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic causes a surge in online shopping"],["2023","The Philippines passes the Internet Transactions Act"]],
s2:[["Sale days","Singles’ Day on 11 November, promoted by Alibaba from 2009, became the world’s largest shopping event. In the Philippines and Southeast Asia, double-date sales such as 9.9, 10.10, 11.11 and 12.12 bring huge discounts and millions of orders in a single day."],["Live selling","Live selling, in which sellers show products and answer questions in live video streams, is popular in China, the Philippines and elsewhere. Small businesses, including home-based sellers, use social media live streams to reach customers directly."],["Cash on delivery","In many countries where fewer people have bank cards, shoppers pay cash when their orders arrive. Cash on delivery has been very common in the Philippines and Southeast Asia, though digital payments are growing quickly."],["Scams and fakes","Online shopping has risks, such as fake sellers, counterfeit products, phishing links and items that never arrive. Shoppers are advised to use trusted platforms, check seller ratings, avoid deals that seem too good to be true and never share passwords or one-time codes. The Philippines’ Internet Transactions Act of 2023 created rules to protect online consumers."],["Effects on shops and workers","E-commerce has hurt some traditional stores but also helped many small businesses grow. The growth of delivery work has created jobs, though couriers and warehouse workers often face long hours, low pay and little job security, prompting calls for better protections."],["Packaging waste","Online orders use large amounts of boxes, plastic bags and bubble wrap. Governments and companies are working on recyclable packaging, reusable bags and fewer layers of wrapping."]],
sp:["A broken laser pointer","In September 1995, a computer programmer named Pierre Omidyar started a website called AuctionWeb, where people could sell items to the highest bidder. One of the first items sold was a broken laser pointer, which went for $14.83. Puzzled, Omidyar contacted the buyer to make sure he understood it did not work. The buyer explained that he collected broken laser pointers. Omidyar realized that online, almost anything could find a buyer. AuctionWeb was soon renamed eBay and became one of the first giants of online shopping."],
g:[["E-commerce","Buying and selling over the internet"],["Marketplace","A platform where many sellers offer goods"],["Courier","A person or company that delivers packages"],["Counterfeit","A fake copy made to look genuine"]],
f:["Amazon and eBay began in 1995.","Singles’ Day on 11 November is the biggest shopping event.","Cash on delivery is popular in Southeast Asia."],
fun:"One of the first things sold on eBay was a broken laser pointer.",
tl:"Ang e-commerce ay ang pagbili at pagbebenta sa internet. Kilala sa Pilipinas ang Shopee at Lazada, pati na ang live selling at mga sale gaya ng 11.11. Mag-ingat sa mga scam at pekeng produkto."});

ADDART({id:"digitalpay",t:"Digital Payments and E-wallets",c:"Economics and Society",i:"💳",w:"Digital payment",r:["money","smartphone","crypto","ofw","cybersecurity"],
p:[["Paying with phones","Digital payments let people pay and send money using phones, cards and computers."],["Mobile money","Services such as M-Pesa and GCash have brought financial services to millions."],["Protect your account","Never share passwords or one-time codes with anyone."]],
l:"Digital payments are ways of paying and transferring money electronically instead of with cash, using bank cards, online banking, mobile phones and e-wallets. In many countries, people now pay for groceries, rides, bills and online purchases by scanning QR codes or tapping phones. Mobile money services such as M-Pesa in Kenya, UPI in India, Pix in Brazil and GCash and Maya in the Philippines have brought financial services to millions who never had bank accounts. Digital payments are fast and convenient, but they also bring risks of scams, privacy loss and exclusion for people without phones or internet.",
s:[["From cards to phones","Credit cards appeared in the 1950s, and the first cash machine, or ATM, opened in London in 1967. Online payment services such as PayPal grew with internet shopping from the late 1990s. Smartphones then made it possible to pay with a phone anywhere."],["E-wallets","An e-wallet is an app that stores money or links to bank accounts and cards, allowing users to pay, send money, buy mobile load and pay bills. Users can add cash at stores or transfer from banks. Popular e-wallets include Alipay and WeChat Pay in China and GCash and Maya in the Philippines."],["QR codes","Many digital payments use QR codes, square barcodes that a phone camera can scan. Even small market stalls and tricycle drivers can display a code to receive payments. The Philippines adopted a national QR standard, QR Ph, allowing payments between different banks and e-wallets."],["Instant transfers","Many countries have built systems that move money between banks instantly, at any hour, such as UPI in India, launched in 2016, Pix in Brazil, launched in 2020, and InstaPay in the Philippines, launched in 2018."]],
k:[["First ATM","London, 1967"],["M-Pesa launched","2007, Kenya"],["UPI launched","2016, India"],["Philippine instant transfer system","InstaPay (2018)"],["Golden rule","Never share your one-time PIN"]],
d:[["1950","The first general-purpose charge card appears"],["1967","The first ATM opens in London"],["1999","PayPal begins online payments"],["2007","M-Pesa launches in Kenya"],["2016","India launches the Unified Payments Interface"],["2020","Brazil launches Pix"]],
s2:[["M-Pesa","In 2007 the Kenyan mobile company Safaricom launched M-Pesa, which let people send and store money using basic mobile phones and local agents, without a bank account. It spread rapidly across Kenya and other countries. A study found that access to M-Pesa helped lift many Kenyan households out of poverty, especially those headed by women."],["Digital payments in the Philippines","GCash and Maya are used by tens of millions of Filipinos to pay bills, shop, send money and receive salaries and government aid. Overseas Filipino workers increasingly send money home through apps. The central bank, the Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas, has promoted digital payments to include more people in the financial system."],["Scams and safety","Digital payments have brought new scams, such as fake messages asking for one-time PINs, phishing links, fake sellers and hacked accounts. Safety tips include never sharing passwords or one-time codes, checking links carefully, using official apps and enabling security features. Many countries have passed laws against financial scams."],["Who gets left behind","Not everyone has a smartphone, reliable internet or the skills to use digital payments, especially older people, the poor and those in remote areas. Some places have kept protections for cash so that no one is excluded."],["Going cashless","In Sweden, China and some other countries, cash has become rare, and many shops accept only digital payments. Supporters say this reduces costs, theft and corruption; critics warn about privacy, power outages, system failures and the loss of a payment method that works for everyone."],["Digital currencies","Cryptocurrencies such as Bitcoin are digital money not issued by governments, though their prices can swing wildly. Some central banks have created or are testing digital versions of their national currencies; the Bahamas launched one of the first, the Sand Dollar, in 2020, and China has tested a digital yuan."]],
sp:["The first cash machine","On 27 June 1967, the world’s first cash machine opened outside a Barclays bank branch in Enfield, north London. Its inventor, John Shepherd-Barron, was said to have had the idea in his bath after arriving at his bank too late to withdraw money. Plastic cards had not yet been developed for it, so customers used paper vouchers impregnated with a slightly radioactive substance, which the machine read. They then typed a PIN. Shepherd-Barron wanted a six-digit PIN, but his wife said she could remember only four, which became the standard."],
g:[["E-wallet","An app for storing and spending money"],["QR code","A square barcode read by a phone camera"],["One-time PIN","A temporary code used to confirm a transaction"],["Mobile money","Financial services offered through mobile phones"]],
f:["The first ATM opened in London in 1967.","M-Pesa launched in Kenya in 2007.","Never share your one-time PIN with anyone."],
fun:"The four-digit PIN was chosen because the ATM inventor’s wife could remember only four numbers.",
tl:"Ang digital payments ay pagbabayad gamit ang cellphone, card at internet sa halip na pera. Ginagamit ng milyun-milyong Pilipino ang GCash at Maya. Huwag kailanman ibahagi ang iyong password o one-time PIN."});

;
/* data/modern2.js */
// The Modern World, part 1: global challenges and how societies are changing — migration, hunger, water, the energy transition, the Sustainable Development Goals, inequality, population, megacities, ageing and fast fashion.
ADDART({id:"migration",t:"Migration and Refugees",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🧳",w:"Human migration",r:["ofw","un","hrights","climchange","inequality"],
p:[["People on the move","Hundreds of millions of people live outside the country where they were born."],["Why people move","People migrate for work, study, family and safety."],["Refugees","Refugees are people forced to flee war or persecution."]],
l:"Migration is the movement of people from one place to another to live, either within their own country or across borders. Today more than 300 million people, about one in every 30, live outside their country of birth. Most move for work, study or family, but tens of millions have been forced from their homes by war, persecution, violence or disasters, including refugees who have crossed borders to seek safety. Migrants send money home, fill jobs and enrich cultures, while migration also raises debates about borders, integration and rights. The Philippines is one of the world’s great migrant-sending countries.",
s:[["Why people migrate","People are pushed to leave by poverty, unemployment, conflict, persecution or disasters, and pulled toward places with jobs, safety, education or family. Most migrants move to nearby countries, and many more move within their own countries, especially from the countryside to cities."],["Refugees and asylum","A refugee is a person who has fled their country because of a well-founded fear of persecution or because of war. The 1951 Refugee Convention protects refugees from being sent back to danger. People who apply for protection are called asylum seekers, and the UN Refugee Agency, UNHCR, helps refugees worldwide."],["Forced displacement","More than 100 million people worldwide have been forced from their homes. Most are internally displaced people, who remain inside their own countries. Large displacement crises in recent years have affected people from Syria, Afghanistan, Ukraine, Venezuela, Sudan, Myanmar and elsewhere."],["Remittances","Migrants send money home to their families, called remittances, totaling hundreds of billions of US dollars a year to low- and middle-income countries, more than foreign aid. India, Mexico, China and the Philippines are among the largest recipients."]],
k:[["International migrants","More than 300 million"],["Refugee Convention","1951"],["UN Refugee Agency","UNHCR"],["Forcibly displaced people","More than 100 million"],["International Migrants Day","18 December"]],
d:[["1950","The UN Refugee Agency is created"],["1951","The Refugee Convention is adopted"],["1974","The Philippines begins its overseas employment program"],["1990","A UN convention on the rights of migrant workers is adopted"],["2015","More than a million migrants and refugees reach Europe by sea"],["2018","The Global Compact for Migration is adopted"]],
s2:[["Filipinos around the world","Millions of Filipinos live and work abroad as nurses, caregivers, seafarers, engineers, domestic workers and in many other jobs, in the Middle East, Asia, North America and Europe. Their remittances support families and make up a significant part of the Philippine economy. Their sacrifices and the long separations from families are part of modern Filipino life."],["Who hosts refugees","Contrary to common belief, most refugees live in low- and middle-income countries near their homelands. Countries such as Iran, Türkiye, Colombia, Germany, Uganda and Pakistan have hosted some of the largest refugee populations."],["Climate and migration","Rising seas, droughts, floods and storms are increasingly forcing people to move. Most climate-related displacement happens within countries. Low-lying Pacific nations such as Tuvalu and Kiribati are planning for a future in which some citizens may need to relocate."],["Debates about migration","Supporters say migrants fill labor shortages, pay taxes, start businesses and bring new ideas and cultures. Critics worry about pressure on wages, housing and public services, illegal border crossings and social cohesion. Governments try to balance border control, economic needs and humanitarian duties, and views differ widely."],["Dangerous journeys","Migrants without legal routes often make dangerous journeys across deserts, jungles and seas, sometimes at the mercy of smugglers and traffickers. Thousands die each year crossing the Mediterranean Sea and other routes."],["Brain drain and brain gain","When skilled workers such as doctors and nurses leave, their home countries can lose talent, called brain drain. But returning migrants and diaspora communities can bring back skills, investment and connections, sometimes called brain gain."]],
sp:["The Global Compact for Migration","In December 2018, more than 150 countries met in Marrakesh, Morocco, and adopted the Global Compact for Safe, Orderly and Regular Migration, the first international agreement covering all aspects of migration. It is not legally binding but sets out shared goals, such as saving lives on migration routes, fighting human trafficking, reducing the costs of sending remittances and helping migrants integrate. Some countries refused to join, saying it threatened their sovereignty, while supporters saw it as a step toward cooperation on an issue no country can manage alone."],
g:[["Migrant","A person who moves to live in another place"],["Refugee","A person forced to flee their country because of persecution or war"],["Asylum","Protection given by a country to a refugee"],["Remittance","Money sent home by a migrant worker"]],
f:["More than 300 million people live outside their country of birth.","The Refugee Convention was adopted in 1951.","The Philippines is one of the largest receivers of remittances."],
fun:"Remittances to developing countries are larger than all foreign aid combined.",
tl:"Ang migrasyon ay paglipat ng tao sa ibang lugar o bansa upang manirahan. Mahigit 300 milyong tao ang nakatira sa labas ng bansang sinilangan nila. Ang mga refugee ay napilitang tumakas dahil sa digmaan o pag-uusig. Milyun-milyong Pilipino ang nagtatrabaho sa ibang bansa."});

ADDART({id:"foodsecurity",t:"Hunger and Food Security",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🥣",w:"Food security",r:["agri","rice","greenrev","climchange","inequality","foodwaste"],
p:[["Enough food for all","Food security means everyone always has enough safe, nutritious food."],["Hunger today","Hundreds of millions of people still go hungry."],["Causes","Conflict, climate shocks and poverty are the main drivers of hunger."]],
l:"Food security exists when all people, at all times, can get enough safe and nutritious food for an active and healthy life. Although the world produces enough food to feed everyone, about 670 million people, around one in twelve, faced hunger in 2024, while many more could not afford a healthy diet. Hunger is driven mainly by conflict, climate shocks and economic crises, and it harms children’s growth and learning most. Ending hunger is the second of the United Nations’ Sustainable Development Goals.",
s:[["Hunger and malnutrition","Hunger is not having enough food. Malnutrition includes undernutrition, such as stunting, when children are too short for their age, and wasting, when they are too thin, as well as deficiencies in vitamins and minerals. At the same time, overweight and obesity are rising in many countries, a double burden."],["Why people go hungry","Most hungry people live in places affected by war, where farms, markets and roads are destroyed. Droughts, floods and storms ruin harvests. Rising food prices and poverty mean families cannot afford enough food even when it is available."],["Famine","Famine is the most extreme form of hunger, when many people die from starvation and disease. Experts use a scale called the Integrated Food Security Phase Classification to measure food crises. In recent years famine conditions have been confirmed in parts of Sudan and Gaza, caused by conflict and blocked aid."],["Food waste","Roughly a fifth to a third of food is lost or wasted, from farms to homes, while people go hungry. Reducing waste, improving storage and helping smallholder farmers could feed many more people."]],
k:[["People facing hunger","About 670 million (2024)"],["UN goal","Zero Hunger (SDG 2)"],["Main causes","Conflict, climate shocks, economic crises"],["World Food Day","16 October"],["Nobel Peace Prize","World Food Programme (2020)"]],
d:[["1945","The Food and Agriculture Organization is founded"],["1961","The World Food Programme is created"],["1966","IR8, a high-yielding rice from Los Baños, helps launch the Green Revolution in Asia"],["1996","The World Food Summit defines food security"],["2015","Zero Hunger becomes a global goal"],["2020","The World Food Programme wins the Nobel Peace Prize"]],
s2:[["Children","Children suffer most from hunger. Undernutrition in the first 1,000 days, from pregnancy to age two, can cause lasting damage to growth and brain development. Stunting affects about one in five children under five worldwide, and school feeding programs help keep children healthy and in school."],["The Philippines","Many Filipino families face food insecurity, especially during typhoons and price spikes. Stunting has long affected a large share of young children. The Philippines is one of the world’s largest rice importers, so world rice prices strongly affect Filipino households. Feeding programs, rice subsidies and support for farmers aim to improve food security."],["Climate change","Rising temperatures, changing rainfall and more extreme weather threaten crops, livestock and fisheries. Scientists are breeding crops that tolerate drought, flooding and salt, such as flood-tolerant rice developed at the International Rice Research Institute in Los Baños."],["Feeding the future","The world’s population is still growing. Ideas for producing more food sustainably include improving farm productivity, reducing waste, restoring soils, eating more plant-based foods and developing new foods such as cultivated meat, though each approach has supporters and critics."],["Food as a weapon","Blocking food supplies to civilians has been used as a weapon of war throughout history. International humanitarian law forbids starving civilians as a method of warfare, and in 2018 the UN Security Council condemned it."],["Food banks and community kitchens","Communities fight hunger through food banks, soup kitchens and sharing. In the Philippines in 2021, community pantries spread across the country, where people left food for neighbors in need, following the idea of giving according to one’s ability and taking according to one’s need."]],
sp:["The community pantry","In April 2021, during the COVID-19 lockdowns, a young woman named Ana Patricia Non set up a small bamboo cart of vegetables, rice and canned goods on Maginhawa Street in Quezon City, with a sign inviting people to give what they could and take only what they needed. Photos spread online, and within days hundreds of community pantries had appeared across the Philippines, run by neighbors, farmers, churches and students. The movement showed how ordinary people could respond to hunger with solidarity, and similar pantries appeared in other countries."],
g:[["Food security","Reliable access to enough safe, nutritious food"],["Malnutrition","Poor nutrition from too little, too much or the wrong food"],["Stunting","Being too short for one’s age because of poor nutrition"],["Famine","Extreme food shortage causing widespread deaths"]],
f:["About 670 million people faced hunger in 2024.","World Food Day is 16 October.","The World Food Programme won the 2020 Nobel Peace Prize."],
fun:"The Philippine community pantry movement began with one bamboo cart in 2021.",
tl:"Ang food security ay ang pagkakaroon ng sapat, ligtas at masustansiyang pagkain ng lahat sa lahat ng oras. Mga 670 milyong tao pa rin ang nagugutom. Ang digmaan, pagbabago ng klima at kahirapan ang pangunahing sanhi."});

ADDART({id:"waterscarcity",t:"The Water Crisis",c:"Geography",i:"🚰",w:"Water scarcity",r:["sanitation","wcycle","climchange","agri","water"],
p:[["Not enough water","Billions of people face water shortages for part of the year."],["Safe to drink","Around two billion people lack safely managed drinking water."],["Using water wisely","Farming uses most of the world’s fresh water."]],
l:"Water scarcity is a lack of enough fresh water to meet people’s needs. Although water covers most of the Earth, only a small fraction is fresh water that people can easily use, and it is unevenly spread. Around two billion people lack safely managed drinking water, and billions experience severe water shortages for at least part of the year. Growing populations, farming, industry, pollution and climate change are putting increasing pressure on rivers, lakes and underground water. Solutions include saving water, fixing leaks, recycling wastewater, protecting watersheds and, in some places, removing salt from seawater.",
s:[["How much fresh water?","About 97 percent of the Earth’s water is salty seawater. Of the remaining fresh water, most is frozen in ice caps and glaciers or hidden deep underground. Only a tiny share is in lakes, rivers and shallow groundwater that people can easily use."],["Where water goes","Farming uses about 70 percent of the fresh water taken from rivers, lakes and aquifers, mostly to irrigate crops. Industry and energy production use about a fifth, and homes about a tenth."],["Kinds of scarcity","Physical scarcity happens in dry places where there is simply not enough water. Economic scarcity happens where water exists but people lack pipes, wells or money to access it. Pollution can make available water unsafe."],["Groundwater","Underground water stored in rock layers called aquifers supplies drinking water for billions of people and much irrigation. In many places it is being pumped faster than rain can refill it, causing wells to dry up and land to sink."]],
k:[["Share of Earth’s water that is fresh","About 3%"],["Fresh water used by farming","About 70%"],["People without safely managed drinking water","Around 2 billion"],["World Water Day","22 March"],["Metro Manila’s main water source","Angat Dam"]],
d:[["1977","The first UN water conference is held"],["1993","The first World Water Day is celebrated"],["2010","The UN recognizes the human right to water and sanitation"],["2018","Cape Town narrowly avoids Day Zero"],["2019","Metro Manila suffers a water shortage"],["2023","The second UN water conference is held"]],
s2:[["Day Zero","In 2018, after three years of drought, the city of Cape Town in South Africa warned of Day Zero, when taps would be turned off and people would collect water at public points. Residents cut their water use by about half, limiting showers and reusing water, and the crisis was avoided when rains returned."],["Water in Metro Manila","Most of Metro Manila’s water comes from Angat Dam in Bulacan. In March 2019, low water levels at La Mesa Dam and rising demand left many households in the east of the metropolis without water for days. The crisis led to plans for new water sources, such as new dams, and debates over how water services should be run."],["Sinking cities","Pumping too much groundwater makes land sink. Parts of Jakarta, Mexico City and coastal areas of the Philippines, such as parts of Bulacan and Pampanga, are sinking, increasing flooding. Indonesia is building a new capital, Nusantara, partly because of Jakarta’s problems."],["Desalination","Desalination removes salt from seawater to make drinking water. It supplies much of the water used in countries such as Israel, Saudi Arabia and the United Arab Emirates. It is reliable but uses a lot of energy and produces salty waste that must be managed."],["Hidden water","Producing food and goods uses large amounts of water, called virtual water. Producing a kilogram of beef can take thousands of liters, mostly to grow animal feed, while a cotton T-shirt can take more than 2,000 liters. Choices about food and clothing affect water use around the world."],["Shared rivers","Many rivers cross borders, such as the Nile, the Mekong and the Indus, and countries sometimes disagree over dams and water sharing. Ethiopia’s Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam on the Nile, completed in 2025, caused years of tension with Egypt and Sudan, showing why cooperation on shared water is vital."]],
sp:["Saving water at home","Simple habits can save large amounts of water. Turning off the tap while brushing teeth can save several liters each time. Fixing a dripping faucet can save thousands of liters a year. Taking shorter showers, using a dipper and pail instead of a long shower, collecting rainwater for plants and washing full loads of clothes all help. In many Filipino homes, the tabo and timba, the dipper and pail, have long been an efficient way of bathing that uses far less water than a shower."],
g:[["Water scarcity","Not having enough fresh water to meet needs"],["Aquifer","An underground layer of rock that holds water"],["Desalination","Removing salt from seawater"],["Virtual water","Water used to produce food and goods"]],
f:["Only about 3 percent of Earth’s water is fresh.","Farming uses about 70 percent of fresh water.","World Water Day is 22 March."],
fun:"Cape Town residents halved their water use in 2018 to avoid Day Zero.",
tl:"Ang kakulangan sa tubig ay kawalan ng sapat na tubig-tabang. Halos 2 bilyong tao ang walang ligtas na inuming tubig. Agrikultura ang gumagamit ng karamihan ng tubig-tabang. Sa Metro Manila, nagkaroon ng krisis sa tubig noong 2019."});

ADDART({id:"energytransition",t:"The Energy Transition",c:"Technology",i:"🔋",w:"Energy transition",r:["renew","solarpower","ev","fossilfuels","climchange"],
p:[["From fossil fuels to clean energy","Countries are shifting from coal, oil and gas to cleaner energy sources."],["Solar on the rise","Solar and wind power have become the cheapest electricity in many places."],["A big challenge","The change raises questions about cost, jobs and reliability."]],
l:"The energy transition is the worldwide shift from fossil fuels such as coal, oil and gas toward low-carbon energy sources such as solar, wind, hydropower, geothermal and nuclear power, together with electric vehicles, better batteries and more efficient use of energy. Its main aim is to cut the greenhouse gas emissions that cause climate change, while also reducing air pollution and dependence on imported fuels. Solar and wind power have grown rapidly as their costs have fallen, but fossil fuels still supply most of the world’s energy, and the pace, cost and fairness of the transition are widely debated.",
s:[["Why change?","Burning fossil fuels releases carbon dioxide, the main cause of global warming, and pollutes the air, contributing to millions of deaths each year. Under the 2015 Paris Agreement, countries pledged to limit warming to well below 2 °C and to try for 1.5 °C, which requires cutting emissions sharply."],["Cheaper clean power","The price of solar panels has fallen by around 90 percent since 2010, and wind power has also become much cheaper. In many countries, new solar and wind farms are now the cheapest way to generate electricity. China installs more solar and wind power than any other country."],["Electrifying everything","Much of the transition involves using electricity from clean sources for things that once used fossil fuels: electric cars, buses and motorcycles, heat pumps for heating and cooling, and electric cooking. Sales of electric cars have grown to a large share of new cars in China, Europe and elsewhere."],["Storing energy","Because the sun does not always shine and the wind does not always blow, energy storage is essential. Large batteries, pumped hydropower and stronger power grids help balance supply and demand."]],
k:[["Goal","Cut greenhouse gas emissions"],["Key agreement","Paris Agreement (2015)"],["Fastest-growing source","Solar power"],["Solar panel cost fall since 2010","About 90%"],["COP28 pledge","Triple renewable capacity by 2030"]],
d:[["1954","The first practical silicon solar cell is made"],["1997","The Kyoto Protocol is agreed"],["2015","The Paris Agreement is adopted"],["2020","The Philippines stops approving new coal power plants"],["2023","Countries agree at COP28 to transition away from fossil fuels"],["2025","Renewables overtake coal in global electricity generation in the first half of the year"]],
s2:[["The Philippines","The Philippines relies heavily on imported coal and oil for electricity, but it has long used geothermal and hydropower. In 2020 it stopped approving new coal power plants, and it aims to raise the share of renewable energy in its electricity to 35 percent by 2030 and 50 percent by 2040. Large solar farms, offshore wind projects and rooftop solar are expanding."],["Critical minerals","Batteries, solar panels and wind turbines need minerals such as lithium, cobalt, nickel, copper and rare earth elements. The Democratic Republic of the Congo produces most of the world’s cobalt, and Indonesia and the Philippines are leading nickel producers. Mining these minerals raises concerns about environmental damage, workers’ rights and local communities."],["A just transition","Coal mining and power plants employ many workers, and some communities depend on them. A just transition means helping these workers and regions find new jobs and opportunities, so that the costs of change are shared fairly."],["Debates","Supporters argue the transition will create jobs, clean the air, lower energy costs in the long run and protect the climate. Critics raise concerns about the cost, the reliability of power supplies, the speed of change and the burden on poorer countries and households. Developing countries argue that rich countries, which caused most past emissions, should help pay."],["Nuclear power","Nuclear power produces large amounts of electricity without carbon emissions. Some countries are building new reactors or extending old ones, while others are phasing them out because of concerns about accidents, waste and cost. Small modular reactors are being developed."],["Energy efficiency","Using less energy for the same result, through efficient appliances, LED lights, insulated buildings and public transport, is often called the first fuel of the transition, because the cleanest energy is the energy not used."]],
sp:["The first silicon solar cell","In 1954, three scientists at Bell Laboratories in the United States, Daryl Chapin, Calvin Fuller and Gerald Pearson, created the first practical silicon solar cell, which turned sunlight directly into electricity. At a press demonstration, it powered a toy Ferris wheel and a small radio transmitter. Newspapers predicted it could one day harness the limitless energy of the sun. At first, solar cells were so expensive that they were used mainly on satellites, starting with Vanguard 1 in 1958. Today, after decades of improvement and mass production, solar power is one of the cheapest sources of electricity on Earth."],
g:[["Energy transition","The shift from fossil fuels to low-carbon energy"],["Renewable energy","Energy from sources that are naturally replenished"],["Grid","The network that delivers electricity"],["Just transition","A fair shift that supports affected workers and communities"]],
f:["Solar panel prices have fallen about 90 percent since 2010.","The Paris Agreement was adopted in 2015.","The Philippines stopped approving new coal plants in 2020."],
fun:"The first silicon solar cell powered a toy Ferris wheel in 1954.",
tl:"Ang energy transition ay ang paglipat mula sa karbon, langis at gas tungo sa malinis na enerhiya gaya ng araw, hangin at geothermal. Layunin nitong bawasan ang mga gas na nagdudulot ng pagbabago ng klima."});

ADDART({id:"sdgs",t:"The Sustainable Development Goals",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🎯",w:"Sustainable Development Goals",r:["un","foodsecurity","inequality","climchange","education"],
p:[["Seventeen goals","The Sustainable Development Goals are 17 global goals for a better world by 2030."],["For everyone","They cover poverty, hunger, health, education, equality, climate and more."],["Mixed progress","Progress has been uneven, and many targets are off track."]],
l:"The Sustainable Development Goals, or SDGs, are 17 goals adopted by all member states of the United Nations in September 2015 as a shared plan for peace and prosperity for people and the planet by 2030. They aim to end poverty and hunger, ensure health and education for all, achieve gender equality, provide clean water and energy, create decent work, reduce inequality, build sustainable cities, protect the climate, oceans and land, and promote peace and justice. The goals have 169 targets. Progress has been mixed, slowed by the COVID-19 pandemic, conflicts and economic crises.",
s:[["Where the goals came from","The SDGs followed the Millennium Development Goals, eight goals set in 2000 for 2015, which helped reduce extreme poverty and child deaths. Unlike the earlier goals, which focused on poorer countries, the SDGs apply to all countries, rich and poor."],["The 17 goals","The goals are: no poverty; zero hunger; good health and well-being; quality education; gender equality; clean water and sanitation; affordable and clean energy; decent work and economic growth; industry, innovation and infrastructure; reduced inequalities; sustainable cities and communities; responsible consumption and production; climate action; life below water; life on land; peace, justice and strong institutions; and partnerships for the goals."],["Leave no one behind","A central promise of the SDGs is to leave no one behind, reaching the poorest and most marginalized people first, including women and girls, Indigenous peoples, people with disabilities, migrants and those in remote areas."],["Measuring progress","Countries track hundreds of indicators and report their progress to the United Nations through Voluntary National Reviews. The Philippines has presented several such reviews, linking the goals to its national development plans."]],
k:[["Adopted","25 September 2015"],["Number of goals","17"],["Number of targets","169"],["Deadline","2030"],["Predecessor","Millennium Development Goals (2000–2015)"]],
d:[["2000","The Millennium Development Goals are adopted"],["2012","The Rio+20 conference calls for new sustainable development goals"],["2015","The SDGs are adopted at the United Nations"],["2016","The SDGs take effect"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic sets back progress"],["2030","The deadline for the goals"]],
s2:[["Progress so far","Before 2020, the world made progress on goals such as reducing child deaths, expanding electricity access and getting more children into school. But the COVID-19 pandemic, wars and rising food and energy prices pushed millions back into poverty and hunger. UN reports in the mid-2020s found that only about a third of the targets were on track or moving moderately in the right direction."],["Criticism","Critics argue that 17 goals and 169 targets are too many to focus on, that some are vague or hard to measure, and that the goals are not legally binding. Some economists suggest concentrating resources on fewer, high-impact goals."],["Support","Supporters say the SDGs give governments, businesses, schools and citizens a common language and roadmap, encourage data collection and remind leaders of promises to the world’s poorest people. Many companies and cities now align their plans with the goals."],["The goals and young people","Schools around the world teach the SDGs, and young people campaign on issues such as climate action, education and equality. The goals’ colorful icons have become widely recognized."],["Financing the goals","Reaching the goals requires trillions of dollars of investment in poorer countries each year. Debates focus on aid, debt relief, fair taxation of multinational companies and private investment."],["After 2030","As 2030 approaches, the United Nations and countries have begun discussing what should come after the SDGs, building on what worked and what did not."]],
sp:["Everyone had a say","Unlike most international agreements, written mainly by diplomats, the Sustainable Development Goals were shaped with input from millions of people. From 2013 to 2015, the United Nations held consultations in nearly 100 countries and ran a global online survey called MY World, in which more than seven million people, many of them young people in developing countries, voted on the issues most important to them. A good education, better health care and an honest and responsive government came out on top. Their votes influenced the final list of 17 goals."],
g:[["Sustainable development","Meeting today’s needs without harming future generations"],["Target","A specific aim within a goal"],["Indicator","A measurement used to track progress"],["Marginalized","Pushed to the edges of society"]],
f:["The SDGs were adopted in 2015.","There are 17 goals and 169 targets.","The deadline for the goals is 2030."],
fun:"Millions of people voted online on which issues the global goals should include.",
tl:"Ang Sustainable Development Goals ay 17 layunin ng United Nations para sa mas mabuting mundo pagsapit ng 2030. Kabilang dito ang pagwawakas ng kahirapan at gutom, kalusugan, edukasyon, pagkakapantay-pantay at pangangalaga sa klima."});

ADDART({id:"inequality",t:"Poverty and Inequality",c:"Economics and Society",i:"⚖️",w:"Economic inequality",r:["gdp","labor","taxes","sdgs","phecon"],
p:[["Rich and poor","Inequality is the gap between those who have more and those who have less."],["Extreme poverty","Hundreds of millions of people live on just a few dollars a day."],["Progress and gaps","Extreme poverty has fallen greatly, but large gaps remain."]],
l:"Poverty is the lack of enough money or resources to meet basic needs such as food, shelter, health care and education. Inequality refers to how unevenly income, wealth and opportunities are shared within and between countries. Over the past few decades, the share of the world’s people living in extreme poverty has fallen dramatically, especially in Asia, yet hundreds of millions still live on just a few dollars a day, most of them in sub-Saharan Africa. At the same time, wealth has become highly concentrated at the top. How to reduce poverty and inequality is one of the central debates in economics and politics.",
s:[["Measuring poverty","The World Bank measures extreme poverty using an international poverty line, updated over time; in 2025 it was set at about 3 US dollars a day, adjusted for differences in prices between countries. Countries also set their own national poverty lines. Poverty can also be measured by lack of education, health care, clean water and electricity."],["A great decline","In the early 1990s, more than a third of the world’s people lived in extreme poverty. Rapid economic growth, especially in China, India and Southeast Asia, lifted more than a billion people out of it. The COVID-19 pandemic briefly reversed the trend, and progress has since slowed."],["Measuring inequality","Economists often use the Gini coefficient, developed by the Italian statistician Corrado Gini, which ranges from 0, when everyone has the same income, to 1, when one person has everything. South Africa and several Latin American countries have some of the highest levels of income inequality."],["Income and wealth","Income is what people earn; wealth is what they own, such as land, houses, savings and shares. Wealth is usually far more unequal than income. Studies estimate that the richest 1 percent of people own a large share of the world’s wealth."]],
k:[["International poverty line","About $3 a day (2025)"],["Inequality measure","Gini coefficient"],["Most extreme poverty","Sub-Saharan Africa"],["UN goals","No Poverty (SDG 1), Reduced Inequalities (SDG 10)"],["Philippine cash-transfer program","Pantawid Pamilyang Pilipino Program (4Ps)"]],
d:[["1912","Corrado Gini publishes his measure of inequality"],["1990","The World Bank introduces a global poverty line"],["2003","Brazil launches the Bolsa Família cash-transfer program"],["2008","The Philippines launches the 4Ps program"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic pushes millions into poverty"],["2025","The World Bank updates the international poverty line"]],
s2:[["Poverty in the Philippines","The share of Filipinos living below the national poverty line has fallen over recent decades but remains significant, especially in rural areas and parts of Mindanao. Farmers, fisherfolk and Indigenous peoples are among the poorest. Remittances, economic growth and social programs have helped reduce poverty."],["Cash transfers","Many countries give regular cash payments to poor families, often on condition that children attend school and get health checkups. Brazil’s Bolsa Família and Mexico’s programs inspired others, including the Philippines’ Pantawid Pamilyang Pilipino Program, or 4Ps, launched in 2008 and made law in 2019. Studies show such programs can improve school attendance and health."],["Causes","Poverty and inequality have many causes, including lack of education and jobs, discrimination, conflict, corruption, unequal access to land and credit, and history, such as colonialism. Economists also debate the roles of technology, globalization and tax policies in rising inequality within many countries."],["Debates","Some emphasize economic growth, free markets and entrepreneurship as the best way to reduce poverty. Others emphasize redistribution through progressive taxes, public services, minimum wages and stronger workers’ rights. Most countries combine both approaches in different ways, and views differ on the right balance."],["Inequality between countries","The average income in the richest countries is many times higher than in the poorest. Where a person is born remains one of the biggest factors in how much they earn. Gaps between countries narrowed as Asian economies grew, but many African countries have yet to catch up."],["Beyond money","Inequality also includes gaps in health, education, life expectancy, digital access and political power, and differences between men and women, cities and the countryside, and ethnic groups."]],
sp:["The elephant curve","In 2013 the economists Christoph Lakner and Branko Milanović drew a graph showing how incomes around the world grew between 1988 and 2008. It looked like an elephant. The rising back showed large gains for the middle of the global distribution, mostly people in Asian countries such as China, India and Indonesia. The dip at the base of the trunk showed little growth for the working and middle classes of rich countries. The raised tip of the trunk showed huge gains for the world’s richest 1 percent. The elephant curve became a famous picture of how globalization shaped who gained and who did not."],
g:[["Poverty","Lacking enough resources to meet basic needs"],["Inequality","Uneven sharing of income, wealth or opportunities"],["Gini coefficient","A number measuring income inequality"],["Cash transfer","Money given directly to people, often by a government"]],
f:["Extreme poverty has fallen sharply since 1990.","The Gini coefficient measures inequality.","The Philippines’ 4Ps program began in 2008."],
fun:"A famous graph of global income growth is called the elephant curve because of its shape.",
tl:"Ang kahirapan ay kakulangan sa pangunahing pangangailangan, habang ang inequality ay ang hindi pantay na hatian ng yaman. Malaki na ang ibinaba ng matinding kahirapan sa mundo, ngunit malaki pa rin ang agwat ng mayaman at mahirap."});

ADDART({id:"worldpop",t:"World Population",c:"Geography",i:"👨‍👩‍👧",w:"World population",r:["urban","megacities","agingworld","migration","indiacountry"],
p:[["Eight billion","The world’s population passed 8 billion in 2022."],["Slowing growth","Families are getting smaller in most countries."],["A peak ahead","The UN expects the population to peak later this century."]],
l:"The world’s population is the total number of people living on Earth, which passed 8 billion in November 2022. For most of history, the population grew slowly, reaching 1 billion only around 1800. Thanks to better food, medicine and sanitation, it then grew rapidly, especially in the 20th century. Today growth is slowing as families have fewer children, and the United Nations projects that the population will peak at around 10 billion in the 2080s. India became the most populous country in 2023, overtaking China, while Africa is the fastest-growing continent.",
s:[["Slow growth for millennia","For most of human history, high birth rates were balanced by high death rates from disease, famine and war. The world’s population may have been only a few million at the end of the last ice age and around 300 million in 1 CE."],["The population boom","From the 1800s, improvements in farming, medicine, vaccines and clean water allowed many more children to survive. The population reached 2 billion around 1927, 3 billion in 1960 and then added a billion roughly every 12 to 15 years."],["Smaller families","As countries become richer and more urban, and as girls’ education and family planning spread, families tend to have fewer children. The average number of children per woman worldwide has fallen from about 5 in the 1950s to around 2.2 today. In many countries it is now below 2.1, the level needed to replace each generation."],["Where people live","More than half of all people live in Asia. India and China each have over 1.4 billion people. Africa’s population is growing fastest and is expected to make up a growing share of humanity this century. More than half of the world’s people now live in cities."]],
k:[["World population","About 8.2 billion (2025)"],["Passed 8 billion","15 November 2022"],["Most populous country","India (since 2023)"],["Projected peak","About 10.3 billion in the 2080s"],["World Population Day","11 July"]],
d:[["c. 1804","The world population reaches 1 billion"],["1927","The population reaches 2 billion"],["1960","The population reaches 3 billion"],["2011","The population reaches 7 billion"],["2022","The population passes 8 billion"],["2023","India overtakes China as the most populous country"]],
s2:[["The 7 billionth baby","To mark the world reaching 7 billion people, the United Nations chose 31 October 2011 as the symbolic day. Danica May Camacho, born just before midnight in a hospital in Manila, was one of several babies around the world celebrated as symbolic 7 billionth babies."],["The Philippines","The Philippines had about 112 million people in 2024, making it one of the most populous countries in the world. Its population is young, but birth rates have fallen quickly: by 2022 the average number of children per woman had dropped below 2. A young workforce can boost economic growth, sometimes called a demographic dividend."],["Shrinking populations","Some countries’ populations are already falling, including Japan, South Korea, Italy, China and several Eastern European countries. South Korea has the lowest fertility rate in the world, well below one child per woman. Shrinking, ageing populations raise concerns about workforces, pensions and care for the elderly."],["Population policies","Some governments have tried to influence population growth. China’s one-child policy, from 1980 to 2015, limited most families to one child, and was criticized for coercion and abuses. Today many countries with low birth rates offer incentives such as child payments and parental leave to encourage larger families, with limited success."],["Population and the planet","More people need more food, water and energy, but how much people consume matters as much as how many there are. A person in a rich country often uses far more resources and causes far more emissions than a person in a poor country."],["Counting people","Countries count their populations through censuses, usually every ten years. The Philippines held censuses in 2015, 2020 and 2024. Census data help governments plan schools, hospitals and roads and divide seats in legislatures."]],
sp:["From one billion to eight","It took the whole of human history, hundreds of thousands of years, for the world’s population to reach 1 billion, around the year 1804. The second billion took about 123 years, reached in 1927. The third billion took only 33 years, in 1960. After that, a new billion was added every 12 to 15 years: 4 billion in 1974, 5 billion in 1987, 6 billion in 1999, 7 billion in 2011 and 8 billion in 2022. Because birth rates are falling, the next billion is expected to take longer, and the population may never reach 11 billion."],
g:[["Population","The number of people living in an area"],["Fertility rate","The average number of children born per woman"],["Census","An official count of a population"],["Demographic dividend","Economic growth from having many working-age people"]],
f:["The world population passed 8 billion in 2022.","India became the most populous country in 2023.","A Filipino baby was a symbolic 7 billionth baby."],
fun:"A baby born in Manila in 2011 was celebrated as one of the symbolic 7 billionth people.",
tl:"Lumampas sa 8 bilyon ang populasyon ng mundo noong 2022. Bumabagal na ang paglaki nito dahil mas kaunti na ang anak ng mga pamilya. Ang India na ang may pinakamaraming tao mula 2023."});

ADDART({id:"megacities",t:"Megacities",c:"Geography",i:"🌃",w:"Megacity",r:["urban","worldpop","manila","tokyo","lagos"],
p:[["Giant cities","A megacity is a city with more than 10 million people."],["Growing fast","Most megacities are in Asia, Africa and Latin America."],["Big challenges","Traffic, housing, floods and pollution are major problems."]],
l:"A megacity is a metropolitan area with more than 10 million people. In 1950 only New York and Tokyo were that large; today there are dozens, most of them in Asia, Africa and Latin America, including Jakarta, Dhaka, Tokyo, Delhi, Shanghai, Manila, Cairo, Lagos, São Paulo and Mexico City. Megacities are engines of economies, culture and innovation, drawing people with jobs and opportunities, but they also face enormous challenges of traffic, housing, flooding, pollution and inequality. How they are planned and managed will shape the lives of billions.",
s:[["Why cities grow","Cities grow as people move from the countryside in search of jobs, education and services, and as city families have children. More than half of the world’s people now live in urban areas, and the share keeps rising."],["The largest cities","Rankings depend on how city boundaries are drawn. In a 2025 United Nations study using a new, consistent method, Jakarta, Dhaka and Tokyo were the world’s three largest cities, each with tens of millions of people. Other giant cities include Delhi, Shanghai, Guangzhou, Cairo, Manila, Kolkata and Seoul."],["Metro Manila","Metro Manila, the National Capital Region of the Philippines, is made up of 16 cities and one municipality, with over 13 million residents and millions more in surrounding provinces. It is one of the most densely populated urban areas in the world and the country’s center of government, business and culture."],["Traffic and transport","Megacities often suffer from severe traffic jams. Metro and rail systems, bus rapid transit, safe walking and cycling paths and good planning can move many more people than private cars. Cities from Tokyo to Bogotá are known for their public transport."]],
k:[["Definition","More than 10 million people"],["Megacities in 1950","2 (New York and Tokyo)"],["Largest in 2025 UN study","Jakarta"],["Share of people in urban areas","More than half"],["Metro Manila","16 cities and 1 municipality"]],
d:[["1950","Only New York and Tokyo have more than 10 million people"],["1974","Curitiba, Brazil, opens a pioneering bus rapid transit system"],["1975","Metro Manila is created"],["2007","Half of the world’s people live in urban areas for the first time"],["2022","Indonesia passes a law to move its capital from Jakarta"],["2025","A UN study names Jakarta the world’s largest city"]],
s2:[["Informal settlements","Around a billion people live in slums or informal settlements, often without secure housing, clean water, sanitation or electricity. Many megacities include large informal communities, where residents build their own homes and economies. Upgrading these areas with services, rather than evicting residents, is increasingly seen as the best approach."],["Floods and heat","Many megacities are on coasts or river deltas, making them vulnerable to floods, storm surges and rising seas, made worse in some places by sinking land. Concrete and asphalt trap heat, making cities hotter than the countryside, an effect called the urban heat island. Parks, trees and green roofs help cool them."],["Pollution","Traffic, factories and burning waste make air pollution a serious health problem in many megacities. Delhi often has some of the worst air quality in the world in winter. Cleaner vehicles, public transport and stricter rules have improved air in cities such as Mexico City and Beijing."],["Moving a capital","Some countries have moved their capitals away from crowded megacities, such as Brazil, which built Brasília in 1960, and Nigeria, which moved its capital from Lagos to Abuja in 1991. Indonesia is building a new capital, Nusantara, on Borneo, though progress has been debated."],["Smart cities","Cities increasingly use technology, such as sensors, apps and data, to manage traffic, energy, water and services, an idea called smart cities. Critics warn about privacy and say technology cannot replace good planning and governance."],["Engines of opportunity","Despite their problems, megacities are centers of jobs, universities, hospitals, culture and new ideas. They often produce a large share of their countries’ wealth, and many people find in them opportunities unavailable elsewhere."]],
sp:["Tokyo, the city that works","Greater Tokyo has long been one of the largest urban areas on Earth, with tens of millions of people. Yet it is known for being remarkably clean, safe and efficient. Its rail network carries billions of passengers a year, with trains so punctual that delays of a minute or two can prompt apologies. At Shinjuku Station, one of the busiest in the world, millions of people pass through each day. Tokyo’s mix of dense neighborhoods, mixed land uses and excellent public transport is often studied by planners from other megacities."],
g:[["Megacity","A city with more than 10 million people"],["Metropolitan area","A city and its surrounding suburbs"],["Urban heat island","A city area hotter than its surroundings"],["Informal settlement","An area of unplanned housing, often without services"]],
f:["A megacity has more than 10 million people.","Only two cities had over 10 million people in 1950.","Metro Manila has 16 cities and one municipality."],
fun:"In 1950 only two cities in the world had more than 10 million people.",
tl:"Ang megacity ay lungsod na may mahigit 10 milyong tao. Karamihan sa mga ito ay nasa Asya, Aprika at Latin Amerika, kabilang ang Metro Manila. Malaki ang hamon ng trapiko, pabahay, baha at polusyon sa mga ito."});

ADDART({id:"agingworld",t:"Ageing Societies",c:"Economics and Society",i:"👵",w:"Population ageing",r:["aging","worldpop","japancountry","family","ofw"],
p:[["Living longer","People around the world are living longer than ever before."],["More older people","In many countries, older people make up a growing share of the population."],["New challenges","Ageing societies must plan for pensions, health care and care for the elderly."]],
l:"Population ageing is the rise in the share of older people in a population, caused by people living longer and families having fewer children. Worldwide, life expectancy has risen to over 70 years, and since 2018 there have been more people aged 65 and over than children under five. Japan, Italy, South Korea and many European countries are already super-aged societies, with more than a fifth of people aged 65 or older, and many other countries, including China, are ageing fast. Ageing reflects great successes in health, but it also raises challenges for pensions, health care, caregiving and the workforce.",
s:[["Why societies are ageing","Better nutrition, sanitation, vaccines and medicine mean that far more people survive childhood and live into old age. At the same time, falling birth rates mean fewer young people. Together, these changes raise the average age of the population."],["Super-aged societies","A society is often called aged when more than 14 percent of its people are 65 or older, and super-aged when more than 20 percent are. Japan became super-aged in the 2000s, and about three in ten Japanese people are now 65 or older. South Korea became super-aged in 2024."],["Working and retiring","As the share of older people grows, fewer workers support each retiree through taxes and pension contributions. Many countries are raising retirement ages, encouraging older people to keep working if they wish, and debating how to keep pensions affordable."],["Health and care","Older people are more likely to live with long-term conditions such as heart disease, diabetes and dementia, and many need help with daily activities. Ageing societies need more geriatric health care, home care and caregivers."]],
k:[["Global life expectancy","Over 70 years"],["Super-aged society","More than 20% aged 65+"],["Oldest country by age","Japan"],["Since 2018","More people aged 65+ than children under 5"],["UN Decade of Healthy Ageing","2021–2030"]],
d:[["1950","Global life expectancy is under 50 years"],["1982","The first World Assembly on Ageing is held"],["1991","The UN adopts Principles for Older Persons"],["2018","People aged 65+ outnumber children under five for the first time"],["2021","The UN Decade of Healthy Ageing begins"],["2024","South Korea becomes a super-aged society"]],
s2:[["Caregivers from the Philippines","Many Filipinos work abroad as caregivers and nurses for older people in Japan, Canada, Israel, Taiwan, Europe and elsewhere. Their skills and warmth are highly valued, while their own families at home often depend on the money they send. Ageing in rich countries has made caregiving a major source of jobs for migrants."],["Ageing in the Philippines","The Philippines has a young population, but the share of older Filipinos is rising. Filipino families traditionally care for elderly relatives at home. Laws such as the Expanded Senior Citizens Act give discounts and benefits, and the Centenarians Act gives a cash gift to Filipinos who reach 100, extended in 2024 to those reaching 80, 85, 90 and 95."],["Healthy ageing","Staying physically active, eating well, keeping socially connected, learning new things and getting regular health checks help people stay healthy and independent as they age. The World Health Organization promotes age-friendly cities with accessible transport, housing and public spaces."],["Technology and care","Japan and other countries are testing robots, sensors and apps to help care for older people, from robotic lifting aids to companion robots. Supporters see technology easing the shortage of caregivers; others stress that human contact cannot be replaced."],["Wisdom and contribution","Older people contribute to society as workers, volunteers, caregivers for grandchildren and keepers of knowledge and traditions. Many cultures, including Filipino culture, honor elders with gestures such as the mano, pressing an elder’s hand to one’s forehead."],["Living to 100","The number of people aged 100 or more, centenarians, is rising rapidly worldwide. Japan has the most centenarians relative to its population. Researchers study long-lived communities to understand the roles of genes, diet, activity and social connection."]],
sp:["The mano","In the Philippines, a young person greeting an older relative or elder often takes the elder’s right hand and gently presses its back to their own forehead, saying mano po. The gesture, called the mano or pagmamano, shows respect and asks for a blessing, and the elder may reply, God bless you. It is commonly done when arriving at or leaving a relative’s home and after church. The custom is a daily reminder of the respect many Filipino families give to older generations, a value that shapes how elders are cared for at home."],
g:[["Population ageing","A rise in the share of older people in a population"],["Life expectancy","The average number of years a person is expected to live"],["Pension","Regular payments to retired people"],["Centenarian","A person aged 100 or older"]],
f:["People aged 65+ outnumber children under five since 2018.","Japan is one of the oldest countries by age.","South Korea became super-aged in 2024."],
fun:"Since 2018, the world has had more people over 65 than children under five.",
tl:"Ang pagtanda ng populasyon ay ang pagdami ng matatanda dahil humahaba ang buhay at kaunti na ang anak. Ang Japan ay isa sa may pinakamaraming matatanda. Sa Pilipinas, iginagalang ang matatanda sa pamamagitan ng pagmamano."});

ADDART({id:"fastfashion",t:"Fast Fashion",c:"Economics and Society",i:"👕",w:"Fast fashion",r:["clothing","plasticwaste","labor","recycling","waterscarcity"],
p:[["Cheap and fast","Fast fashion is cheap, trendy clothing produced quickly and in huge amounts."],["Hidden costs","It has large effects on the environment and on garment workers."],["Buying better","Secondhand clothes, repairs and buying less are alternatives."]],
l:"Fast fashion is a business model in which clothing companies quickly copy the latest trends and produce large quantities of inexpensive clothes, offering new styles every week. Brands such as Zara, H&M and, more recently, online retailers such as Shein have made fashion cheaper and more accessible than ever. But the rapid production and disposal of clothes has serious costs: large amounts of water, energy and chemicals, carbon emissions, textile waste and microplastic pollution, as well as low wages and unsafe conditions for many garment workers. Consumers, governments and companies are looking for more sustainable alternatives.",
s:[["How fast fashion works","Fast fashion companies design clothes based on runway shows, celebrities and social media trends, produce them in factories mainly in Asia within weeks and sell them at low prices. Some online retailers add thousands of new items every day. Low prices encourage people to buy more and wear items fewer times."],["More clothes, worn less","Global clothing production roughly doubled between 2000 and 2014, while the number of times an item is worn before being thrown away has fallen. Many clothes are worn only a handful of times."],["Environmental impact","The fashion industry uses large amounts of water, especially for growing cotton and dyeing fabric. It produces a significant share of the world’s carbon emissions and pollutes rivers with dyes and chemicals. Synthetic fabrics such as polyester, made from oil, shed tiny plastic fibers when washed."],["Textile waste","Most discarded clothing is burned or sent to landfills, and only a tiny share is recycled into new clothes. Huge piles of unwanted clothing have accumulated in places such as the Atacama Desert in Chile and markets in Ghana, which receive secondhand clothes from rich countries."]],
k:[["Meaning","Cheap, trendy clothing produced quickly"],["Production growth","Roughly doubled, 2000–2014"],["Deadliest garment factory disaster","Rana Plaza, Bangladesh (2013)"],["Main synthetic fabric","Polyester"],["Filipino secondhand clothes","Ukay-ukay"]],
d:[["1975","Zara opens its first store in Spain"],["2000s","Fast fashion brands expand around the world"],["2008","The online retailer Shein is founded"],["2013","The Rana Plaza factory collapse kills more than 1,100 workers"],["2014","Fashion Revolution Week begins"],["2020s","Governments debate laws to limit fast fashion and textile waste"]],
s2:[["Rana Plaza","On 24 April 2013, the Rana Plaza building near Dhaka, Bangladesh, which housed several garment factories, collapsed, killing more than 1,100 workers and injuring thousands. Workers had been ordered back inside despite cracks in the walls the day before. The disaster led to an agreement between brands and unions to improve factory safety in Bangladesh and to the Fashion Revolution movement, which asks who made my clothes?"],["Garment workers","Tens of millions of people, most of them women, work in garment factories in countries such as China, Bangladesh, Vietnam, India, Cambodia and Indonesia. The industry provides vital jobs, but many workers earn low wages, work long hours and face unsafe conditions. Campaigns call for living wages and the right to organize."],["Ukay-ukay","In the Philippines, ukay-ukay shops selling secondhand clothes, many imported in bales, are popular for finding bargains and unique items. Buying secondhand extends the life of clothes, though the trade in imported used clothing also raises questions about waste and local industries."],["Slow fashion","Slow fashion encourages buying fewer, better-made clothes, choosing natural or recycled materials, repairing and mending, swapping and renting clothes, and supporting local makers. Traditional Filipino textiles, such as piña, abacá and handwoven inabel, are celebrated as sustainable alternatives."],["What companies and governments are doing","Some brands offer recycling programs, repairs and clothes made from recycled fibers, though critics warn of greenwashing, exaggerated environmental claims. The European Union and some countries are introducing rules making producers responsible for textile waste and limiting the destruction of unsold clothes."],["What you can do","Wear clothes longer, care for them well, repair them, buy secondhand, donate or swap unwanted items and think before buying. The most sustainable garment is often the one already in your closet."]],
sp:["Who made my clothes?","After the Rana Plaza disaster in 2013, the designers Carry Somers and Orsola de Castro founded Fashion Revolution, a movement to make the fashion industry more transparent and fair. Each year around the anniversary of the collapse, during Fashion Revolution Week, people around the world post photos of themselves wearing their clothes inside out, showing the labels, and asking brands on social media: who made my clothes? Garment workers, in turn, hold signs saying I made your clothes. The campaign has pushed many brands to publish lists of their factories."],
g:[["Fast fashion","Cheap, trendy clothing made and sold quickly"],["Garment worker","A person who makes clothes in a factory"],["Microplastics","Tiny pieces of plastic, including fibers from synthetic clothes"],["Greenwashing","Making misleading claims about being environmentally friendly"]],
f:["Clothing production roughly doubled from 2000 to 2014.","The Rana Plaza collapse in 2013 killed more than 1,100 people.","Ukay-ukay shops sell secondhand clothes in the Philippines."],
fun:"Each year people wear their clothes inside out to ask brands who made them.",
tl:"Ang fast fashion ay murang damit na mabilis na ginagawa at sumusunod sa uso. May malaking epekto ito sa kalikasan at sa mga manggagawa. Mga alternatibo ang ukay-ukay, pagkukumpuni ng damit at pagbili nang mas kaunti."});

;
/* data/modern3.js */
// The Modern World, part 2: work, digital life, health and science today, and global pop culture — the gig economy, remote work, data privacy, COVID-19, CRISPR, mRNA vaccines, anime, internet memes and esports.
ADDART({id:"gigeconomy",t:"The Gig Economy",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🛵",w:"Gig worker",r:["labor","smartphone","ecommerce","remotework","inequality"],
p:[["Work by the task","In the gig economy, people do short jobs, or gigs, often found through apps."],["Riders and freelancers","Delivery riders, ride-hailing drivers and online freelancers are gig workers."],["Flexible but uncertain","Gig work offers flexibility but often lacks job security and benefits."]],
l:"The gig economy is the part of the economy in which people work on short-term tasks or projects, called gigs, rather than in permanent jobs, often finding work through smartphone apps and websites. It includes ride-hailing drivers, food delivery riders, couriers, home cleaners and online freelancers such as writers, designers, programmers and virtual assistants. Gig work gives millions of people flexible ways to earn money, but many gig workers have unstable incomes and lack benefits such as health insurance, paid leave and pensions, leading to debates about their rights.",
s:[["How it works","Digital platforms connect customers with workers. A customer orders a ride, a meal or a design through an app; the platform assigns the job, sets or suggests the price, handles payment and takes a commission. Workers are usually treated as independent contractors rather than employees."],["Ride-hailing and delivery","Uber, founded in 2009, popularized ride-hailing through apps. In Southeast Asia, Grab, founded in 2012, offers rides, food delivery and payments. Food and parcel delivery apps employ huge numbers of riders on motorcycles and bicycles in cities around the world."],["Online freelancing","Websites such as Upwork and Fiverr let businesses hire freelancers anywhere in the world for tasks such as writing, design, translation, coding and customer support. The Philippines has become one of the world’s leading sources of online freelancers and virtual assistants."],["Why people do gig work","Gig work can be flexible, letting people choose their hours, earn extra income, work from home or start working quickly without formal qualifications. For some it is a side job; for others it is their main source of income."]],
k:[["Meaning","Short-term, task-based work, often through apps"],["Examples","Ride-hailing, delivery, online freelancing"],["Uber founded","2009"],["Grab founded","2012"],["Key debate","Are gig workers employees or contractors?"]],
d:[["2008","Online freelancing marketplaces begin to grow rapidly"],["2009","Uber is founded"],["2012","Grab is founded in Malaysia"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic boosts delivery work"],["2021","The UK Supreme Court rules that Uber drivers are workers"],["2024","The European Union adopts rules on platform work"]],
s2:[["Workers’ rights","Because most gig workers are classified as independent contractors, they often do not receive minimum wages, overtime pay, sick leave, health insurance or pensions. Courts and lawmakers in many countries have debated whether some gig workers should be treated as employees. In 2021 the UK Supreme Court ruled that Uber drivers were workers entitled to minimum wage and holiday pay."],["Managed by algorithms","Apps use algorithms to assign tasks, set prices, rate workers and sometimes deactivate accounts. Workers say these systems can be opaque and unfair, with little chance to appeal. New laws in the European Union require more transparency about how platforms manage workers."],["Safety","Delivery riders and drivers face road accidents, long hours, heat, rain and pressure to deliver quickly. Some cities and companies have introduced insurance, rest areas and safety training."],["Gig work in the Philippines","Many Filipinos work as delivery riders, ride-hailing drivers and motorcycle taxi riders, as well as online freelancers serving clients abroad in fields such as customer support, design and bookkeeping. Lawmakers have proposed measures to protect freelancers and gig workers’ pay and benefits."],["Debates","Supporters say the gig economy creates opportunities, flexibility and convenient services. Critics argue that it shifts risks from companies to workers and weakens labor protections. Many people seek a middle path that keeps flexibility while guaranteeing basic protections."],["Where the word comes from","The word gig was long used by musicians for a single performance or engagement. It came to describe short-term jobs more broadly, and the term gig economy became popular around 2009, after the global financial crisis, when many people turned to freelance work."]],
sp:["The virtual assistant capital","With strong English skills, a large young workforce and long experience in call centers, the Philippines has become one of the world’s leading sources of virtual assistants and online freelancers. From homes and coworking spaces in Manila, Cebu, Davao and small towns, Filipinos manage email and schedules, answer customers, edit videos, keep books and run social media for businesses in the United States, Australia, Europe and elsewhere. Surveys of freelance platforms have repeatedly ranked the Philippines among the fastest-growing freelance markets in the world."],
g:[["Gig","A short-term job or task"],["Platform","An app or website that connects customers and workers"],["Independent contractor","A self-employed worker paid per job"],["Algorithm","A set of rules a computer follows"]],
f:["Uber was founded in 2009 and Grab in 2012.","Many Filipinos work as online freelancers.","The EU adopted rules on platform work in 2024."],
fun:"The word gig originally meant a musician’s single performance.",
tl:"Ang gig economy ay ang paggawa ng maiikling trabaho o gig sa pamamagitan ng mga app, gaya ng pagde-deliver, pagmamaneho at online freelancing. Flexible ito ngunit kadalasan ay walang benepisyo at seguridad sa trabaho."});

ADDART({id:"remotework",t:"Remote Work",c:"Economics and Society",i:"💻",w:"Remote work",r:["internet","cloud","gigeconomy","covid19","urban"],
p:[["Working from anywhere","Remote work means doing a job away from the office, often from home."],["A pandemic shift","The COVID-19 pandemic made remote work common almost overnight."],["Home and office","Many workers now split their time between home and office."]],
l:"Remote work, also called telework or working from home, is doing a job from a place other than a central workplace, usually using computers, the internet and video calls. Long possible for some jobs, it became widespread almost overnight in 2020, when the COVID-19 pandemic closed offices around the world. Today many people work fully remotely or in hybrid arrangements, splitting their week between home and office. Remote work can save commuting time and widen job opportunities, but it also raises questions about productivity, isolation, fairness and the future of cities.",
s:[["Beginnings","In 1973 the American engineer Jack Nilles coined the word telecommuting, imagining that telecommunications could replace the daily commute. Over the following decades, personal computers, the internet, email and mobile phones made working from home increasingly practical for some jobs."],["The pandemic shift","When the COVID-19 pandemic struck in 2020, governments ordered lockdowns and companies sent office workers home. Video calling apps saw enormous growth: daily participants in Zoom meetings rose from about 10 million in December 2019 to about 300 million by April 2020."],["Hybrid work","After the pandemic, many companies adopted hybrid work, with employees working some days at home and others in the office. Others called workers back to offices full-time, while some stayed fully remote. The best arrangement is widely debated."],["Tools","Remote workers rely on laptops, fast internet, video calls, chat apps, shared online documents and cloud storage. Project management apps help teams coordinate across locations and time zones."]],
k:[["Also called","Telework, working from home"],["Word telecommuting coined","1973"],["Big shift","2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic"],["Philippine law","Telecommuting Act (2018)"],["Common today","Hybrid work"]],
d:[["1973","Jack Nilles coins the word telecommuting"],["1990s","Email and the internet make remote work easier"],["2018","The Philippines passes the Telecommuting Act"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic makes remote work widespread"],["2020","Estonia launches one of the first digital nomad visas"],["2025","The Philippines creates a digital nomad visa"]],
s2:[["Benefits","Remote work can save hours of commuting, reduce traffic and pollution, give workers more flexibility, help parents and caregivers and allow people with disabilities to work more easily. Companies can hire talent from anywhere and save on office space."],["Challenges","Remote workers may feel isolated, find it hard to separate work from home life or struggle with poor internet or crowded homes. Managers worry about communication, teamwork, training new staff and building company culture. Studies of productivity have found mixed results, depending on the job and the arrangement."],["Not for everyone","Many jobs, such as those of nurses, factory workers, farmers, drivers, cooks and shop staff, cannot be done remotely. The rise of remote work has highlighted differences between people who can work from home and those who cannot."],["Remote work in the Philippines","The Philippines passed the Telecommuting Act in 2018, giving employees who work from home the same rights as office workers. During the pandemic, many companies, including business process outsourcing firms, shifted to work-from-home arrangements. Heavy traffic in Metro Manila makes remote work especially attractive to many workers."],["Digital nomads","Digital nomads are remote workers who travel while working online, often living in different countries for months at a time. Many countries, including Estonia, Thailand, Indonesia and the Philippines, which created a digital nomad visa in 2025, now offer special visas to attract them."],["Cities after the pandemic","With fewer people commuting to offices every day, some city centers have seen empty office buildings and quieter streets, while suburbs and smaller towns have grown. Cities are rethinking how to use office space, sometimes converting it into homes."]],
sp:["The man who named telecommuting","In 1973, during a global oil crisis that made fuel scarce and expensive, Jack Nilles, a former NASA rocket engineer working at the University of Southern California, began studying whether people could work at home or in nearby offices using telecommunications instead of driving to work. He coined the words telecommuting and telework and led an experiment with an insurance company in which employees worked from satellite offices closer to home. Nilles predicted that computers would one day make remote work common, nearly half a century before the COVID-19 pandemic proved him right."],
g:[["Remote work","Working away from a central workplace"],["Hybrid work","Splitting work time between home and office"],["Telecommuting","Working from home using telecommunications"],["Digital nomad","A remote worker who travels while working"]],
f:["The word telecommuting was coined in 1973.","Remote work became widespread in 2020.","The Philippines passed a Telecommuting Act in 2018."],
fun:"Daily Zoom meeting participants grew about thirtyfold in four months in 2020.",
tl:"Ang remote work ay paggawa ng trabaho nang malayo sa opisina, kadalasan sa bahay. Naging karaniwan ito noong pandemya ng COVID-19 noong 2020. Marami ngayon ang hybrid, na nagtatrabaho sa bahay at sa opisina."});

ADDART({id:"dataprivacy",t:"Data Privacy",c:"Technology",i:"🔐",w:"Information privacy",r:["cybersecurity","internet","socialmedia","smartphone","hrights"],
p:[["Your personal data","Data privacy is about who can collect, use and share information about you."],["Everything leaves a trace","Apps, websites and devices collect large amounts of personal data."],["Laws and habits","Privacy laws and good online habits help protect personal information."]],
l:"Data privacy, also called information privacy, is the right of people to control how information about them is collected, used, stored and shared. Every day, websites, apps, phones, cameras and smart devices collect personal data such as names, locations, contacts, purchases, photos, health information and online behavior. This data powers useful services and advertising, but it can also be misused, sold, leaked or used for surveillance. Laws such as the European Union’s General Data Protection Regulation and the Philippines’ Data Privacy Act of 2012 set rules for how organizations handle personal data.",
s:[["What is personal data?","Personal data is any information that can identify a person, directly or indirectly, such as a name, address, phone number, ID number, photo, location, fingerprints or online account. Sensitive data includes health records, religion, political views, finances and information about children."],["Who collects data","Social media companies, search engines, online shops, banks, phone companies, governments, schools and employers all collect data. Many apps track location and activity. Data brokers buy and sell information about people, often without their knowledge."],["Why it matters","Leaked or misused data can lead to identity theft, scams, discrimination, embarrassment, stalking or political manipulation. Privacy is also linked to freedom: people may not speak or act freely if they feel constantly watched."],["Privacy laws","The EU’s General Data Protection Regulation, in force since 2018, gives people rights to see, correct and delete their data and allows large fines for violations. The Philippines’ Data Privacy Act of 2012 created the National Privacy Commission, which enforces rules on how personal data is processed."]],
k:[["Philippine law","Data Privacy Act of 2012"],["Philippine regulator","National Privacy Commission"],["EU law","General Data Protection Regulation (2018)"],["Data Privacy Day","28 January"],["Key habit","Think before sharing personal information"]],
d:[["1981","The Council of Europe adopts the first international data protection treaty"],["2012","The Philippines passes the Data Privacy Act"],["2013","Edward Snowden reveals mass surveillance programs"],["2014","A European court recognizes a right to be forgotten"],["2018","The Cambridge Analytica scandal breaks"],["2018","The EU’s General Data Protection Regulation takes effect"]],
s2:[["Cambridge Analytica","In 2018 it was revealed that the political consulting firm Cambridge Analytica had obtained data from up to 87 million Facebook users, many of whom never agreed to share it, through a personality quiz app. The Philippines had one of the largest numbers of affected users. The scandal led to investigations, fines and greater public concern about data privacy."],["Data breaches","Data breaches happen when hackers steal data or organizations accidentally expose it. In 2016, a breach of the Philippine Commission on Elections’ website exposed personal information of tens of millions of registered voters, one of the largest government data breaches in the world at the time."],["Surveillance","Governments collect data to fight crime and terrorism, but mass surveillance raises concerns about civil liberties. In 2013 the former US intelligence contractor Edward Snowden revealed secret programs collecting phone and internet data worldwide, sparking global debate."],["The right to be forgotten","In 2014 the Court of Justice of the European Union ruled that people can ask search engines to remove links to outdated or irrelevant personal information, known as the right to be forgotten. Supporters see it as protecting privacy; critics worry about limiting access to information."],["Protecting your privacy","Tips include using strong, unique passwords and two-factor authentication; checking app permissions; thinking before posting personal details, locations or photos; being cautious with quizzes and free Wi-Fi; reading privacy settings; and never sharing one-time codes. Children should ask a trusted adult before sharing personal information online."],["Privacy and artificial intelligence","AI systems are trained on huge amounts of data, sometimes including personal information and images collected from the internet. Face recognition and AI that can imitate voices raise new privacy concerns, and regulators are working on rules for AI and data."]],
sp:["If it’s free, you may be the product","Many online services, such as social media, search engines and free apps, cost users nothing. Instead, they earn money mainly by showing advertisements, which are more valuable when targeted to each user’s interests. To do this, they collect detailed information about what people search, watch, like, buy and where they go. A popular saying sums it up: if you are not paying for the product, you are the product. While this is a simplification, it reminds users that their attention and data have value, and that understanding how free services make money helps people make informed choices."],
g:[["Personal data","Information that can identify a person"],["Data breach","An incident in which data is stolen or exposed"],["Surveillance","Close watching of people, often by governments or companies"],["Two-factor authentication","A login that requires two forms of proof"]],
f:["The Philippines passed the Data Privacy Act in 2012.","The EU’s GDPR took effect in 2018.","Data Privacy Day is 28 January."],
fun:"The first international treaty on data protection was signed in 1981, long before smartphones.",
tl:"Ang data privacy ay ang karapatang kontrolin kung paano kinokolekta at ginagamit ang impormasyon tungkol sa iyo. Sa Pilipinas, pinoprotektahan ito ng Data Privacy Act of 2012. Gumamit ng matibay na password at mag-isip bago magbahagi online."});

ADDART({id:"covid19",t:"The COVID-19 Pandemic",c:"Health and the Body",i:"😷",w:"COVID-19 pandemic",r:["pandemic","vacc","mrna","immune","remotework"],
p:[["A global pandemic","COVID-19 spread around the world from 2020, changing daily life everywhere."],["Millions of deaths","Millions of people died, and many more fell ill."],["Vaccines in record time","Vaccines were developed and given to billions of people within about two years."]],
l:"The COVID-19 pandemic was a worldwide outbreak of coronavirus disease 2019, caused by the virus SARS-CoV-2, first identified in Wuhan, China, in December 2019. It spread to almost every country, and the World Health Organization declared it a pandemic on 11 March 2020. More than 7 million deaths were officially reported, and the WHO estimated that the true number of excess deaths in 2020 and 2021 alone was around 15 million. Lockdowns, school closures and travel bans transformed daily life, while vaccines developed in record time saved millions of lives. The WHO ended the global emergency in May 2023.",
s:[["The virus","SARS-CoV-2 is a coronavirus, a family of viruses named for the crown-like spikes on their surface. It spreads mainly through the air when infected people breathe, talk, cough or sneeze, especially indoors. Symptoms range from none or mild, like a cold, to severe pneumonia and death, with older people and those with certain health conditions at highest risk."],["Spread and response","After the first cases in Wuhan, the virus spread rapidly through travel. Countries responded with lockdowns, closures of schools and businesses, travel restrictions, testing, contact tracing, quarantine, mask wearing and physical distancing. Hospitals in many places were overwhelmed."],["Vaccines","Scientists developed vaccines in under a year, building on decades of research. The first vaccinations outside trials began in the United Kingdom in December 2020. Vaccines using mRNA, viral vectors and inactivated virus were given to billions of people, greatly reducing severe illness and deaths, though access was unequal between rich and poor countries."],["Variants","As the virus spread, it mutated into new variants, such as Delta in 2021 and Omicron, identified in November 2021, which spread more easily. Updated vaccines and earlier infections gave many people some immunity, and the disease became less deadly for most."]],
k:[["Cause","The SARS-CoV-2 virus"],["First identified","Wuhan, China, December 2019"],["Declared a pandemic","11 March 2020"],["Reported deaths","More than 7 million"],["Global emergency ended","5 May 2023"]],
d:[["2019","The first cases are identified in Wuhan, China"],["2020","The WHO declares a pandemic on 11 March"],["2020","The Philippines places Luzon under enhanced community quarantine"],["2020","The first COVID-19 vaccinations begin in the United Kingdom"],["2021","The Omicron variant is identified"],["2023","The WHO ends the COVID-19 global health emergency"]],
s2:[["The pandemic in the Philippines","The Philippines confirmed its first case in January 2020. In March 2020 the government placed Luzon under an enhanced community quarantine, one of the strictest and longest lockdowns in the world, followed by different levels of community quarantine for many months. Schools moved to distance learning for about two years. Vaccinations began in March 2021. Frontline health workers were hailed as heroes."],["Effects on daily life","Billions of people stayed home. Offices shifted to remote work, students to online and modular learning, and shopping, worship and celebrations moved online. Many people lost jobs and income, and the pandemic increased poverty, hunger, mental health problems and learning losses, especially for poorer families."],["Long COVID","Some people continue to have symptoms such as tiredness, breathlessness and difficulty concentrating for months after infection, a condition known as long COVID. Researchers are still studying its causes and treatments."],["Where did it come from?","The origin of SARS-CoV-2 is still debated. Many scientists think the virus most likely spread to humans from animals, possibly at a market in Wuhan, as earlier coronaviruses did. Others consider it possible that it escaped from a laboratory in Wuhan that studied coronaviruses. Investigations have not reached a definitive conclusion."],["Misinformation","False claims about the virus, cures and vaccines spread widely online, which the WHO called an infodemic. Health authorities worked to share accurate information, while debates over lockdowns, masks and vaccine mandates became politically divisive in many countries."],["Lessons for the future","The pandemic exposed weaknesses in health systems and global cooperation. In 2025 member states of the World Health Organization adopted a Pandemic Agreement aiming to improve preparedness and fairer access to vaccines and medicines in future pandemics."]],
sp:["The first vaccine shot","On 8 December 2020, at a hospital in Coventry, England, a 90-year-old grandmother named Margaret Keenan became the first person in the world to receive an approved COVID-19 vaccine outside a clinical trial. Wearing a blue Christmas T-shirt with a penguin, she received the Pfizer-BioNTech vaccine and said it was the best early birthday present she could wish for. The second person vaccinated that morning was a man named William Shakespeare, a coincidence that delighted the media. Within two years, billions of vaccine doses had been given around the world."],
g:[["Pandemic","An outbreak of disease that spreads across many countries"],["Coronavirus","A family of viruses with crown-like spikes"],["Variant","A version of a virus with changes in its genes"],["Quarantine","Keeping people apart to stop disease spreading"]],
f:["The WHO declared COVID-19 a pandemic on 11 March 2020.","More than 7 million deaths were officially reported.","The global emergency ended in May 2023."],
fun:"The second person to receive an approved COVID-19 vaccine was named William Shakespeare.",
tl:"Ang pandemya ng COVID-19 ay pandaigdigang pagkalat ng sakit na dulot ng virus na SARS-CoV-2 mula 2020. Milyun-milyon ang namatay. Nagkaroon ng mga lockdown, at nakagawa ng bakuna sa loob ng isang taon."});

ADDART({id:"crispr",t:"Gene Editing and CRISPR",c:"Science",i:"✂️",w:"CRISPR gene editing",r:["dna","genome","mendel","microbe","bloodtypes"],
p:[["Editing life’s code","CRISPR is a tool that lets scientists change DNA precisely."],["Borrowed from bacteria","It comes from a natural defense system bacteria use against viruses."],["Hope and caution","Gene editing offers new cures but raises serious ethical questions."]],
l:"Gene editing is the technology of making precise changes to the DNA of living things. The most famous tool, CRISPR-Cas9, adapted from a natural defense system in bacteria, acts like molecular scissors that can cut DNA at a chosen place, allowing genes to be disabled, repaired or changed. Developed in 2012 by Jennifer Doudna and Emmanuelle Charpentier, who won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 2020, CRISPR has transformed biology and medicine. The first CRISPR-based treatment, for sickle cell disease, was approved in 2023. Gene editing also raises serious ethical questions, especially about editing human embryos.",
s:[["Bacteria’s defense","Scientists noticed strange repeated patterns in bacterial DNA as early as 1987 and later named them CRISPR. They discovered that bacteria store pieces of viral DNA in these patterns as a memory of past infections and use enzymes such as Cas9 to recognize and cut the DNA of returning viruses."],["Turning it into a tool","In 2012 Jennifer Doudna and Emmanuelle Charpentier showed that CRISPR-Cas9 could be programmed with a guide molecule to cut any chosen DNA sequence. Soon after, scientists including Feng Zhang showed it worked in human and animal cells. The tool was cheaper, faster and easier than earlier gene-editing methods."],["How it works","A short guide RNA, designed to match a target DNA sequence, leads the Cas9 enzyme to the right spot, where it cuts both strands of DNA. When the cell repairs the cut, scientists can disable a gene or insert a new sequence. Newer methods, called base editing and prime editing, can change single DNA letters without cutting both strands."],["Uses in research","CRISPR lets scientists switch genes on and off to learn what they do, create models of diseases and study how cells work, speeding up research across biology."]],
k:[["Main tool","CRISPR-Cas9"],["Origin","A bacterial defense system"],["Developed as a tool","2012"],["Nobel Prize","Doudna and Charpentier (Chemistry, 2020)"],["First approved CRISPR therapy","2023, for sickle cell disease"]],
d:[["1987","Scientists notice repeated DNA patterns in bacteria"],["2002","The name CRISPR is coined"],["2012","Doudna and Charpentier show CRISPR can edit DNA"],["2018","A scientist announces the birth of gene-edited babies"],["2020","Doudna and Charpentier win the Nobel Prize in Chemistry"],["2023","The first CRISPR-based medicine is approved"]],
s2:[["Treating disease","In 2023 the United Kingdom and then the United States approved Casgevy, the first medicine using CRISPR, to treat sickle cell disease and beta thalassemia, inherited blood disorders. Doctors edit patients’ own blood stem cells and return them to the body. In 2025, doctors in the United States treated a baby with a rare inherited disease using a gene-editing therapy designed just for him."],["Crops and animals","Gene editing can produce crops that resist disease, tolerate drought or have better nutrition, sometimes faster than traditional breeding. Countries regulate gene-edited crops differently; the Philippines adopted rules in 2022 for evaluating gene-edited products, and scientists there are working on improved rice and other crops."],["The gene-edited babies","In November 2018, the Chinese scientist He Jiankui announced that twin girls had been born after he edited their genes as embryos to try to make them resistant to HIV. Scientists around the world condemned the experiment as unsafe and unethical, and he was sentenced to prison in China."],["Ethics","Editing genes in a patient’s body cells affects only that person, but editing embryos, eggs or sperm creates changes that could be passed to future generations. Many scientists and countries oppose such heritable editing, at least for now, because of safety risks and fears of designer babies and new inequalities."],["Fair access","Gene-editing treatments are currently extremely expensive, raising concerns that only the wealthy will benefit. Sickle cell disease is most common among people of African, Middle Eastern and South Asian descent, many in low-income countries, making affordable access an important issue."],["Gene drives","Scientists are exploring gene drives, which spread an edited gene through a whole population, for example to reduce mosquitoes that carry malaria. Because such changes could spread through wild populations, they are being studied very carefully."]],
sp:["The yogurt connection","Some of the key early discoveries about CRISPR came from an unexpected place: the yogurt and cheese industry. Dairy companies use bacteria to ferment milk, and viruses that attack these bacteria can ruin whole batches. In 2007, scientists working for the food company Danisco showed that bacteria used to make yogurt gained immunity to viruses by adding pieces of viral DNA to their CRISPR regions. Their work proved that CRISPR is a bacterial immune system and helped dairy companies breed hardier bacteria, paving the way for the gene-editing revolution."],
g:[["Gene editing","Making precise changes to DNA"],["CRISPR","A bacterial defense system used as a gene-editing tool"],["Cas9","An enzyme that cuts DNA"],["Heritable","Able to be passed on to offspring"]],
f:["CRISPR comes from a bacterial defense system.","Doudna and Charpentier won the 2020 Nobel Prize in Chemistry.","The first CRISPR medicine was approved in 2023."],
fun:"Early CRISPR discoveries came from scientists studying yogurt bacteria.",
tl:"Ang CRISPR ay kasangkapan na nagpapahintulot sa mga siyentipiko na baguhin nang eksakto ang DNA. Nagmula ito sa likas na depensa ng bakterya laban sa virus. Nakatutulong ito sa paggamot ng sakit ngunit may mga tanong sa etika."});

ADDART({id:"mrna",t:"mRNA Vaccines",c:"Health and the Body",i:"💉",w:"mRNA vaccine",r:["vacc","covid19","immune","dna","crispr"],
p:[["A new kind of vaccine","mRNA vaccines teach the body to make a harmless piece of a germ."],["Fast to make","They can be designed and produced much faster than many older vaccines."],["Decades of work","They were built on decades of research by scientists such as Katalin Karikó and Drew Weissman."]],
l:"An mRNA vaccine is a type of vaccine that uses messenger RNA, a molecule that carries genetic instructions, to teach cells to make a harmless piece of a virus, usually a protein from its surface. The immune system learns to recognize this protein and is prepared if the real virus attacks. The first mRNA vaccines, against COVID-19, were authorized in December 2020, less than a year after the virus was identified, and were given to hundreds of millions of people. They were made possible by decades of research, including discoveries by Katalin Karikó and Drew Weissman, who won the 2023 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.",
s:[["What mRNA does","In every cell, DNA stores genetic instructions, and messenger RNA carries copies of these instructions to the cell’s protein-making machinery. mRNA is temporary and breaks down naturally after a short time; it does not enter the cell nucleus or change a person’s DNA."],["How the vaccines work","An mRNA vaccine contains instructions for a viral protein, such as the spike protein of the COVID-19 virus, wrapped in tiny fat bubbles called lipid nanoparticles. After injection, cells read the instructions and make the protein, which the immune system recognizes as foreign, producing antibodies and immune cells."],["A long road","Scientists experimented with mRNA from the 1990s, but it caused harmful inflammation and broke down quickly. In 2005 Katalin Karikó and Drew Weissman showed that changing one of the building blocks of mRNA prevented the inflammatory reaction. Improvements in lipid nanoparticles helped deliver mRNA into cells."],["COVID-19 vaccines","When the genetic code of the COVID-19 virus was published in January 2020, companies such as BioNTech, working with Pfizer, and Moderna designed vaccines within days. After large trials, they were authorized in December 2020 and proved highly effective at preventing severe illness."]],
k:[["Uses","Messenger RNA instructions"],["First authorized","December 2020, for COVID-19"],["Key discovery","Karikó and Weissman (2005)"],["Nobel Prize","Physiology or Medicine, 2023"],["Delivery","Lipid nanoparticles"]],
d:[["1961","Messenger RNA is discovered"],["1990","Scientists show mRNA injected into mice can make proteins"],["2005","Karikó and Weissman publish their key discovery"],["2020","The first mRNA vaccines are authorized for COVID-19"],["2023","Karikó and Weissman win the Nobel Prize"],["2024","An mRNA vaccine for another disease, RSV, is approved"]],
s2:[["Katalin Karikó","The Hungarian-born biochemist Katalin Karikó spent decades working on mRNA, often with little funding and recognition. She was demoted at her university and repeatedly turned down for grants. Her persistence, and her partnership with the immunologist Drew Weissman, made mRNA vaccines possible, and in 2023 they shared the Nobel Prize."],["Speed and flexibility","Because mRNA vaccines are made from a genetic code rather than grown in eggs or cells, they can be designed quickly and updated when viruses change. COVID-19 vaccines were updated for new variants, and scientists hope the platform can respond quickly to future pandemics."],["Safety","mRNA vaccines went through large clinical trials and continued monitoring of billions of doses. Most side effects, such as a sore arm, tiredness or fever, are mild and short-lived. A rare side effect, inflammation of the heart muscle called myocarditis, was found mainly in young men, usually mild. Health agencies concluded that for most people the benefits outweighed the risks."],["Beyond COVID-19","Researchers are developing mRNA vaccines and treatments for influenza, RSV, which was approved in 2024, HIV, malaria, tuberculosis and cancer. Personalized cancer vaccines, designed for each patient’s tumor, are being tested in trials."],["Challenges","Early mRNA vaccines needed very cold storage, which made distribution difficult in hot countries with limited electricity. Newer versions are more stable. Unequal access to vaccines during the pandemic also led to efforts to build vaccine factories in Africa, Asia and Latin America."],["Hesitancy","Some people were hesitant to take mRNA vaccines because the technology was new, and misinformation spread online, including false claims that the vaccines change DNA. Health workers and scientists worked to explain how the vaccines work."]],
sp:["Designed in a weekend","On 10 January 2020, Chinese scientists published the genetic sequence of the new coronavirus online. At BioNTech in Germany, the husband-and-wife founders Uğur Şahin and Özlem Türeci, both children of Turkish immigrants, read the reports and quickly redirected their company, which had been working on cancer vaccines, to the new virus, designing vaccine candidates within days. At Moderna in the United States, scientists finalized the design of their vaccine just two days after the sequence was published. Less than a year later, both vaccines were being given to people around the world."],
g:[["mRNA","Messenger RNA, a molecule that carries genetic instructions"],["Spike protein","A protein on the surface of the COVID-19 virus"],["Lipid nanoparticle","A tiny fat bubble that carries mRNA into cells"],["Antibody","A protein that helps the body fight infection"]],
f:["The first mRNA vaccines were authorized in December 2020.","mRNA does not change a person’s DNA.","Karikó and Weissman won the 2023 Nobel Prize."],
fun:"Moderna’s COVID-19 vaccine was designed two days after the virus’s genetic code was published.",
tl:"Ang mRNA vaccine ay uri ng bakuna na nagtuturo sa mga selula na gumawa ng hindi mapanganib na bahagi ng virus upang matuto ang immune system. Ginamit ito laban sa COVID-19 mula Disyembre 2020."});

ADDART({id:"anime",t:"Anime and Manga",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎌",w:"Anime",r:["anim","comics","japancountry","hokusai","streaming","manga"],
p:[["Japanese animation","Anime is the distinctive animation style that comes from Japan."],["Comics first","Many anime are based on manga, Japanese comics."],["Loved worldwide","Anime has fans in almost every country, including the Philippines."]],
l:"Anime is animation from Japan, known for its distinctive art styles, expressive characters and wide range of stories, from adventure and science fiction to romance, sports, comedy and drama, made for children and adults alike. Many anime are adapted from manga, Japanese comics, which are read by people of all ages. Beginning with television series such as Astro Boy in 1963, anime grew into a global phenomenon, with films by Studio Ghibli, series such as Pokémon, Dragon Ball, One Piece and Demon Slayer and fans around the world. In the Philippines, anime shows such as Voltes V became part of popular culture.",
s:[["Manga","Manga are Japanese comics, usually printed in black and white and read from right to left. They cover every genre and are read by children and adults. Japan’s manga tradition draws on centuries of illustrated stories, and the artist Hokusai used the word manga for his sketchbooks in the 1800s."],["The god of manga","Osamu Tezuka, often called the god of manga, created Astro Boy, about a robot boy, first as a manga in the 1950s and then as Japan’s first popular weekly animated television series in 1963. His big-eyed characters and cinematic storytelling shaped the style of anime and manga."],["A global boom","From the 1970s and 1980s, anime spread abroad on television. Series such as Voltes V, Dragon Ball, Sailor Moon and Pokémon gained huge followings, and the film Akira, released in 1988, impressed audiences worldwide. Today streaming services make anime available almost everywhere."],["Studio Ghibli","Studio Ghibli, founded in 1985 by Hayao Miyazaki, Isao Takahata and Toshio Suzuki, is famous for beautifully hand-drawn films such as My Neighbor Totoro and Princess Mononoke. Spirited Away, released in 2001, won the Academy Award for Best Animated Feature in 2003, and The Boy and the Heron won the same award in 2024."]],
k:[["Origin","Japan"],["Based on","Often manga, Japanese comics"],["Early TV classic","Astro Boy (1963)"],["Famous studio","Studio Ghibli (founded 1985)"],["Oscar winners","Spirited Away, The Boy and the Heron"]],
d:[["1963","Astro Boy becomes Japan’s first popular weekly anime series"],["1977","Voltes V first airs in Japan"],["1985","Studio Ghibli is founded"],["1997","The One Piece manga and Pokémon anime begin"],["2003","Spirited Away wins an Academy Award"],["2020","Demon Slayer: Mugen Train becomes Japan’s highest-grossing film"]],
s2:[["Anime in the Philippines","Anime has been hugely popular in the Philippines since the late 1970s, when robot series such as Voltes V, Mazinger Z and Daimos captured young viewers. Later shows such as Dragon Ball, Ghost Fighter, Slam Dunk and Naruto, dubbed in Filipino, became favorites. In 2023 a Filipino live-action version, Voltes V: Legacy, was produced."],["The Voltes V story","When the Marcos government removed Voltes V and other robot anime from television in 1979, saying they were too violent, many fans were upset. Some later saw the show’s story of rebellion against a tyrannical empire as having political meaning. Voltes V returned to Philippine television after 1986, with a popular rerun in 1999, and its theme song remains famous."],["One Piece","One Piece, a manga by Eiichiro Oda about a young pirate named Luffy searching for treasure, began in 1997 and has sold more copies than any other comic series by a single author, according to Guinness World Records. Its anime has run for more than a thousand episodes."],["Fans and cosplay","Anime fans gather at conventions around the world, where many practice cosplay, dressing up as their favorite characters. Fan communities create art, music and videos and discuss their favorite series online. Enthusiastic fans are sometimes called otaku."],["Industry","The anime industry has grown to be worth trillions of yen, earning a growing share of its income from overseas. However, many animators in Japan work long hours for low pay, and the industry is debating how to improve working conditions."],["Influence","Anime and manga have influenced filmmakers, artists, fashion and video games worldwide. Their stories often explore friendship, perseverance, nature, technology and the struggle between good and evil, themes that resonate across cultures."]],
sp:["Astro Boy’s weekly miracle","When Osamu Tezuka decided to turn his manga Astro Boy into a weekly television series in 1963, experts said it was impossible: full animation took far too long and cost too much. Tezuka found shortcuts. His team reused drawings, moved only characters’ mouths during dialogue, held still images while the camera panned and used fewer drawings per second. These techniques, called limited animation, made weekly anime possible and became part of the style that defines anime today. Astro Boy was a hit, and it was soon broadcast in the United States and other countries."],
g:[["Anime","Animation from Japan"],["Manga","Japanese comics"],["Cosplay","Dressing up as a character from a story"],["Limited animation","Animation using fewer drawings to save time"]],
f:["Astro Boy began as a weekly anime in 1963.","Spirited Away won an Academy Award in 2003.","Voltes V is a beloved anime in the Philippines."],
fun:"Hokusai used the word manga for his sketchbooks almost 200 years ago.",
tl:"Ang anime ay animasyon mula sa Japan, at marami rito ay batay sa manga o komiks ng Hapon. Sikat ito sa buong mundo. Sa Pilipinas, minahal ng maraming henerasyon ang Voltes V, Dragon Ball at iba pa."});

ADDART({id:"memes",t:"Internet Memes",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"😂",w:"Internet meme",r:["socialmedia","internet","misinformation","comics","streaming"],
p:[["Jokes that spread","An internet meme is an image, video or phrase that spreads and changes online."],["Shared culture","Memes let people share jokes and ideas quickly across the world."],["More than jokes","Memes can raise money, spread news and also spread misinformation."]],
l:"An internet meme is an idea, image, video, phrase or style that spreads rapidly online, usually copied and changed by many people along the way. Memes often combine pictures with short captions, but they also include dances, challenges, catchphrases and viral videos. They are a form of shared humor and culture, letting people comment on everyday life, news and politics in a few seconds. The word meme was coined by the biologist Richard Dawkins in 1976. Memes can raise awareness and money for good causes, but they can also spread misinformation and mockery.",
s:[["Where the word comes from","In his 1976 book The Selfish Gene, the British biologist Richard Dawkins coined the word meme for a unit of culture, such as a tune, a catchphrase or a fashion, that spreads from person to person by imitation, the way genes spread by reproduction. The word later came to mean viral internet content."],["How memes work","A meme usually starts with an image, video or phrase. Others copy it, change the words or pictures and share it, creating countless variations. Popular formats are recognized instantly, so a new caption on a familiar image can make people laugh."],["Early internet memes","Among the first internet memes was the Dancing Baby, a 3D animation that spread by email in 1996. In the 2000s, image macros with bold white captions, cat pictures and prank links such as Rickrolling, which tricks people into watching a 1987 music video, became popular."],["Memes everywhere","Today memes spread on social media and messaging apps in every language, including Filipino. They are used in advertising, politics, education and everyday conversation, and businesses and governments sometimes use them to reach young people."]],
k:[["Word coined","1976, by Richard Dawkins"],["Early meme","The Dancing Baby (1996)"],["Famous challenge","Ice Bucket Challenge (2014)"],["Typical form","An image with a caption"],["Spreads by","Copying and changing"]],
d:[["1976","Richard Dawkins coins the word meme"],["1996","The Dancing Baby spreads online"],["2007","Rickrolling becomes a popular prank"],["2013","The Doge meme inspires a cryptocurrency"],["2014","The Ice Bucket Challenge raises money for ALS research"],["2017","The Distracted Boyfriend meme spreads worldwide"]],
s2:[["The Ice Bucket Challenge","In 2014 the Ice Bucket Challenge asked people to film themselves pouring a bucket of ice water over their heads and nominate friends to do the same, or donate to research on ALS, a nerve disease. Millions took part, including celebrities, and the ALS Association received over 100 million US dollars in a few weeks, which helped fund research that identified a new gene linked to the disease."],["Doge","A 2010 photo of Kabosu, a Shiba Inu dog in Japan, glancing sideways became the Doge meme, with captions in broken English such as such wow. It inspired a joke cryptocurrency, Dogecoin, in 2013, which later became worth billions of dollars. Kabosu died in 2024, mourned by fans around the world."],["Memes and misinformation","Because memes are short, funny and easily shared, they can spread false or misleading claims quickly, without sources. They are sometimes used in political campaigns and propaganda. Checking claims before sharing applies to memes too."],["Copyright and consent","Memes often use photos, film clips or images of real people without permission. Some people who became memes found fame and even income, while others suffered embarrassment or harassment. Questions about copyright and respect for people in memes are increasingly discussed."],["Filipino meme culture","Filipino internet users are known for their creativity and humor online, turning celebrities, teleserye scenes, politicians, everyday situations and wordplay in Filipino and English into memes. Memes are part of how many Filipinos react to news, from typhoon class suspensions to sports victories."],["Why memes matter","Researchers study memes as a form of folk culture, like jokes and sayings passed around in earlier times. They show what people care about, laugh at and worry about, and how ideas spread in the digital age."]],
sp:["The dancing baby","In 1996, software developers created a short 3D animation of a baby dancing the cha-cha to show off what their program could do. People began emailing the file to each other, adding music and new versions, and soon the Dancing Baby was everywhere, even appearing on the television series Ally McBeal. In an age before social media, when most people still used dial-up internet, it became one of the first viral internet memes, spreading worldwide simply because people found it funny and shared it."],
g:[["Meme","An idea or piece of content that spreads by imitation"],["Viral","Spreading rapidly from person to person online"],["Format","A recognizable template used for many memes"],["Challenge","An online trend inviting people to do and share an activity"]],
f:["Richard Dawkins coined the word meme in 1976.","The Ice Bucket Challenge raised over $100 million for ALS research.","The Dancing Baby was one of the first internet memes."],
fun:"A dog meme inspired a cryptocurrency that became worth billions.",
tl:"Ang internet meme ay larawan, video o pahayag na mabilis kumalat at binabago ng maraming tao online. Ginagamit ito sa biruan at pagbabahagi ng ideya, ngunit maaari rin itong magkalat ng maling impormasyon."});

ADDART({id:"esports",t:"Esports",c:"Sports",i:"🎮",w:"Esports",r:["videogames","olymp","streaming","phsport","chess"],
p:[["Competitive gaming","Esports are organized video game competitions."],["Huge audiences","Top tournaments fill arenas and are watched online by millions."],["Filipino champions","Philippine teams have won world titles in Mobile Legends."]],
l:"Esports, short for electronic sports, are organized competitions in which individual players or teams compete in video games, often for prizes and in front of large audiences, both in arenas and online. Popular esports games include Mobile Legends: Bang Bang, League of Legends, Dota 2, Counter-Strike, Valorant and fighting games. Professional players train for hours a day, belong to teams with coaches and sponsors and compete in leagues and world championships with millions of dollars in prize money. Esports have become medal events at the Southeast Asian Games and the Asian Games, and the Philippines has become a powerhouse in mobile esports.",
s:[["Early competitions","One of the first video game competitions was held at Stanford University in 1972, when students competed at the game Spacewar! for a year’s subscription to Rolling Stone magazine. In 1980 a Space Invaders championship in the United States attracted about 10,000 players."],["The rise of esports","In the late 1990s and 2000s, online games such as StarCraft became hugely popular in South Korea, where professional players became celebrities and matches were shown on television. The growth of internet streaming in the 2010s allowed millions to watch tournaments live online."],["Popular games","Esports include many types of games: multiplayer online battle arena games such as League of Legends, Dota 2 and Mobile Legends, where teams of five battle to destroy each other’s bases; shooting games such as Counter-Strike and Valorant; fighting games; sports games; and strategy games."],["Life as a pro","Professional esports players train long hours, studying strategies and practicing teamwork, often living together in gaming houses. Careers can be short, as reflexes peak young. Teams employ coaches, analysts, nutritionists and psychologists."]],
k:[["Meaning","Organized video game competitions"],["Early tournament","Spacewar!, Stanford, 1972"],["Popular in the Philippines","Mobile Legends: Bang Bang"],["SEA Games medal event","Since 2019"],["Asian Games medal event","Since 2022 (held in 2023)"]],
d:[["1972","Students hold a Spacewar! tournament at Stanford"],["1980","A Space Invaders championship draws about 10,000 players"],["2000s","StarCraft leagues make esports famous in South Korea"],["2011","Live streaming of games grows rapidly"],["2019","Esports become medal events at the SEA Games in the Philippines"],["2026","A Philippine team wins a sixth straight Mobile Legends world championship"]],
s2:[["The Philippines and Mobile Legends","Mobile Legends: Bang Bang, played on smartphones, is enormously popular in the Philippines and Indonesia. Philippine teams won six Mobile Legends world championships in a row, from the M2 championship in January 2021 to M7 in January 2026, and the national team, Sibol, has won international medals. Filipino players are among the most respected in mobile esports."],["The 2019 SEA Games","When the Philippines hosted the Southeast Asian Games in 2019, esports were among the medal events, one of the first times at a major multi-sport event, and Filipino players won several gold medals, including in Mobile Legends and Dota 2."],["Esports and the Olympics","Esports were a medal event at the 2022 Asian Games, held in Hangzhou, China, in 2023. The International Olympic Committee has explored creating Olympic Esports Games, though debates continue over which games should be included, since many involve combat and are owned by private companies."],["Big prizes","Some tournaments offer enormous prize money. The International, the world championship of Dota 2, has had prize pools of tens of millions of US dollars, funded partly by players buying items in the game. Top players can earn large incomes from prizes, salaries, sponsors and streaming."],["Health and balance","Long hours of gaming can cause eye strain, back and wrist problems and lack of sleep and exercise. Esports teams increasingly encourage fitness, healthy diets and mental health support. For most players, gaming is best enjoyed as a hobby balanced with school, work and other activities."],["Is it a sport?","People debate whether esports are true sports. Supporters point to the skill, strategy, teamwork, fast reactions and training they require, like chess, which is recognized as a sport by many bodies. Others argue that sports should involve physical exertion."]],
sp:["The first esports tournament","On 19 October 1972, students gathered at the Stanford Artificial Intelligence Laboratory in California for the Intergalactic Spacewar Olympics. They competed at Spacewar!, a game created at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology in 1962 in which two spaceships battle while being pulled by a star’s gravity. The grand prize was a year’s subscription to Rolling Stone magazine, whose reporter covered the event. It is often considered the first esports tournament, half a century before world championships filled stadiums and drew millions of online viewers."],
g:[["Esports","Organized competitive video gaming"],["MOBA","Multiplayer online battle arena, a team strategy game"],["Prize pool","The total prize money in a tournament"],["Streaming","Broadcasting live over the internet"]],
f:["The first esports tournament was held in 1972.","Esports were medal events at the 2019 SEA Games.","Philippine teams won six Mobile Legends world titles in a row."],
fun:"The prize at the first esports tournament was a magazine subscription.",
tl:"Ang esports ay organisadong paligsahan sa video game. Napakasikat ng Mobile Legends sa Pilipinas, at nanalo ang mga koponang Pilipino ng anim na sunod na kampeonato sa mundo. Naging medal event ang esports sa SEA Games 2019."});

;
/* data/modern4.js */
// The Modern World, part 3: health, science and pop culture today — young people’s mental health, obesity, AI in medicine, Korean dramas, short-video apps and superheroes.
ADDART({id:"mentalyouth",t:"Young People’s Mental Health",c:"Health and the Body",i:"💚",w:"Mental health of adolescents",r:["mental","screentime","emotions","cyberbullying","loneliness"],
p:[["Minds matter","Mental health is as important as physical health, especially for young people."],["Common and treatable","Many young people experience anxiety or depression, and help works."],["Talk to someone","Reaching out to a trusted adult or a helpline is a strong first step."]],
l:"Mental health is a person’s emotional, psychological and social well-being, affecting how they think, feel, act, handle stress and relate to others. Adolescence is a time of big changes, and the World Health Organization estimates that about one in seven young people aged 10 to 19 experiences a mental health condition, most often anxiety or depression. Half of all mental health conditions begin by age 14, but most go unrecognized and untreated. Concern about young people’s mental health has grown in many countries, and schools, families and governments are working to reduce stigma and make support easier to reach. Mental health conditions are common, and they can be treated.",
s:[["What affects mental health","Many things shape young people’s mental health: family life, friendships, school pressure, poverty, violence, bullying, discrimination, physical health, sleep and genes. Big events, such as the COVID-19 pandemic, disasters or losing a loved one, can also have strong effects."],["Common conditions","Anxiety involves excessive worry or fear that interferes with daily life. Depression involves long-lasting sadness, loss of interest, tiredness and changes in sleep or appetite. Other conditions include eating disorders, attention difficulties and the effects of trauma. Everyone feels sad or worried sometimes; it becomes a concern when feelings are intense, last a long time or make daily life hard."],["Stigma","Many young people do not seek help because they fear being judged, called weak or misunderstood. Talking openly about mental health, as about any health issue, helps reduce stigma and encourages people to get support."],["Getting help","Talking to a trusted adult, such as a parent, teacher, school counselor or health worker, is an important step. Counseling, therapy and, in some cases, medicine prescribed by a doctor can help. Many countries have free helplines staffed by trained listeners."]],
k:[["Young people with a mental health condition","About 1 in 7 (ages 10–19)"],["Age when half of conditions begin","By 14"],["Most common","Anxiety and depression"],["World Mental Health Day","10 October"],["Philippine law","Mental Health Act (2018)"]],
d:[["1992","The first World Mental Health Day is held"],["2013","The WHO adopts a global mental health action plan"],["2018","The Philippines passes the Mental Health Act"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic disrupts schools and daily life"],["2021","UNICEF devotes its flagship report to children’s mental health"],["2023","US health officials issue an advisory on social media and youth mental health"]],
s2:[["Mental health in the Philippines","The Philippine Mental Health Act of 2018 recognizes the right to mental health services and calls for mental health programs in schools, workplaces and communities. The National Center for Mental Health runs a crisis hotline, reachable by dialing 1553 from a landline or through mobile numbers listed on its website. Schools have been increasing counseling and guidance services."],["Social media and screens","Researchers debate how much social media affects young people’s mental health. Some evidence links heavy use with poorer sleep, cyberbullying and negative comparisons; other studies find small or mixed effects and benefits such as connection and support. Most experts agree that balance, good sleep and in-person relationships matter."],["Looking after your mind","Habits that support mental health include getting enough sleep, being physically active, spending time outdoors, eating regular meals, staying connected with friends and family, doing things you enjoy, limiting late-night screen use and talking about feelings. These habits help, but they do not replace professional care when it is needed."],["Helping a friend","If a friend seems to be struggling, listening without judging, letting them know you care and encouraging them to talk to a trusted adult can make a big difference. If someone talks about wanting to harm themselves, it is important to tell a responsible adult right away and seek urgent help."],["Schools","Schools can support mental health by teaching social and emotional skills, preventing bullying, training teachers to recognize warning signs and providing counselors. Some countries have introduced mental health lessons as part of the curriculum."],["Progress and gaps","Awareness of mental health has grown, and more young people now talk openly about it. But in many countries, mental health services are scarce, especially in rural areas, and there are few trained professionals for children and adolescents."]],
sp:["The semicolon","Some people mark their support for mental health with a small symbol: the semicolon. In writing, a semicolon is used where an author could have ended a sentence but chose to continue. In 2013 an American movement called Project Semicolon adopted it as a symbol that a person’s story is not over. Since then, people around the world have drawn or tattooed semicolons on their wrists to show solidarity with those facing mental health struggles and to encourage them to keep going and seek help. The message is simple: your story is not finished."],
g:[["Mental health","Emotional, psychological and social well-being"],["Anxiety","Excessive worry or fear"],["Depression","Long-lasting sadness and loss of interest"],["Stigma","Negative attitudes that make people feel ashamed"]],
f:["About 1 in 7 adolescents has a mental health condition.","Half of mental health conditions begin by age 14.","World Mental Health Day is 10 October."],
fun:"The semicolon has become a symbol of hope for mental health: the story is not over.",
tl:"Ang kalusugang pangkaisipan ay kasinghalaga ng kalusugan ng katawan. Halos isa sa bawat pito na kabataan ang may kondisyong pangkaisipan, gaya ng anxiety o depression. Makipag-usap sa mapagkakatiwalaang matanda o tumawag sa helpline, gaya ng 1553 ng National Center for Mental Health."});

ADDART({id:"obesity",t:"Obesity and Healthy Weight",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🥗",w:"Obesity",r:["nutri","exercise","diabetes","heartdisease","foodsecurity"],
p:[["A growing health issue","Obesity has become far more common around the world in recent decades."],["Many causes","Food environments, activity, sleep, stress and genes all play a part."],["Health, not shame","Experts focus on health and support, not blame."]],
l:"Obesity is a condition in which excess body fat builds up to a level that can harm health, raising the risk of type 2 diabetes, heart disease, some cancers and joint problems. The World Health Organization estimates that in 2022 about one in eight people worldwide were living with obesity, and adult obesity had more than doubled since 1990, while obesity among adolescents had quadrupled. At the same time, many countries still face undernutrition, a double burden. Obesity has many causes, from the foods available and affordable to people, to physical activity, sleep, stress, medicines and genes, and experts emphasize prevention, healthy environments and respectful, non-judgmental care.",
s:[["How it is measured","Doctors often use the body mass index, or BMI, calculated from weight and height, to screen for weight-related health risks in adults; for children, BMI is compared with charts for their age and sex. BMI is a rough guide, not a diagnosis, and doctors also consider waist size, fitness, diet and overall health."],["Why obesity has increased","Over recent decades, highly processed foods and sugary drinks have become cheaper and more widely available and heavily advertised, while many people move less because of cars, desk jobs and screens. Urban environments without safe places to walk or play make activity harder."],["Not just willpower","Body weight is influenced by genes, hormones, sleep, stress, medicines, income and the places where people live, work and study. Experts warn that blaming individuals is unfair and unhelpful, and that weight stigma can harm both mental and physical health."],["Health risks","Obesity increases the risk of type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, heart disease, stroke, some cancers, sleep apnea and joint problems. Even modest improvements in eating, activity and sleep can improve health, whatever a person’s size."]],
k:[["People living with obesity","About 1 in 8 (2022)"],["Adult obesity since 1990","More than doubled"],["Adolescent obesity since 1990","Quadrupled"],["Common screening tool","Body mass index (BMI)"],["World Obesity Day","4 March"]],
d:[["1835","Adolphe Quetelet describes the formula later called body mass index"],["1997","A WHO meeting describes obesity as a global epidemic"],["2014","Mexico introduces a tax on sugary drinks"],["2018","The Philippines introduces a tax on sweetened beverages"],["2021","A new class of weight-loss medicines is approved"],["2022","About one in eight people worldwide live with obesity"]],
s2:[["The double burden","Many countries, including the Philippines, face both undernutrition, such as stunting in young children, and rising overweight and obesity, sometimes within the same family. Both are linked to diets lacking fresh, nutritious food, which can be expensive or hard to find."],["Food environments","Governments use policies to make healthy choices easier, such as taxes on sugary drinks, clear front-of-package food labels, limits on junk-food advertising to children and healthier school meals. Mexico introduced a sugary drinks tax in 2014, and the Philippines included a tax on sweetened beverages in its 2018 tax reform law."],["Treatments","Treatment usually starts with support for healthier eating, more activity, better sleep and managing stress, ideally with health professionals. Some people are helped by medicines or surgery. New medicines called GLP-1 receptor agonists, first developed for diabetes, have shown large effects on weight and became widely used in the 2020s, though they are expensive and can have side effects."],["Children","Childhood obesity has risen in most countries. Experts recommend active play every day, plenty of fruits and vegetables, water instead of sugary drinks, enough sleep and limited recreational screen time, with families eating together when possible. Children should never be shamed about their bodies."],["Traditional diets","Many traditional diets, such as those based on vegetables, legumes, fish, rice and fruit in parts of Asia and the Mediterranean, are linked to good health. Filipino dishes such as sinigang, pinakbet and grilled fish fit healthy eating patterns, while fried foods and sweet drinks are best in moderation."],["Body image","Healthy bodies come in many shapes and sizes. Experts encourage focusing on healthy habits, strength and well-being rather than appearance or numbers on a scale, and warn against extreme diets, which can harm health."]],
sp:["The astronomer who invented BMI","The formula now called the body mass index was devised in the 1830s by Adolphe Quetelet, a Belgian astronomer and statistician, who wanted to describe the average man using statistics. He noticed that adult weight tended to rise with the square of height. He never intended it to measure individual health. More than a century later, in 1972, the American scientist Ancel Keys named it the body mass index and recommended it for studying populations. Today it is widely used as a quick screening tool, though doctors know it does not tell the whole story about a person’s health."],
g:[["Obesity","Excess body fat that may harm health"],["BMI","Body mass index, a screening measure based on weight and height"],["Double burden","Undernutrition and obesity existing together"],["Processed food","Food changed from its natural state, often with added sugar, salt or fat"]],
f:["About one in eight people lived with obesity in 2022.","BMI was devised by an astronomer in the 1830s.","The Philippines taxes sweetened beverages."],
fun:"The body mass index was invented by a Belgian astronomer.",
tl:"Ang obesity ay labis na taba sa katawan na maaaring makasama sa kalusugan. Dumami ito sa buong mundo dahil sa mga pagkaing processed, matatamis na inumin at kaunting paggalaw. Mahalaga ang suporta at malusog na gawi, hindi ang paninisi."});

ADDART({id:"aimedicine",t:"AI in Medicine",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🩺",w:"Artificial intelligence in healthcare",r:["ai","medimaging","mlearn","dataprivacy","crispr"],
p:[["Smarter health care","Artificial intelligence is helping doctors detect diseases and develop new medicines."],["Reading scans","AI can spot signs of disease in X-rays, eye photos and other images."],["Humans in charge","Experts say AI should support, not replace, doctors and nurses."]],
l:"Artificial intelligence, or AI, is increasingly used in medicine to help detect diseases, read medical images, predict which patients are at risk, discover new drugs and reduce paperwork for health workers. AI systems trained on large amounts of medical data can sometimes spot patterns that humans miss, such as early signs of eye disease or cancer. In 2024 the Nobel Prize in Chemistry honored, among others, the creators of AlphaFold, an AI system that predicts the shapes of proteins. AI in medicine promises faster, more accurate and more accessible care, but it also raises concerns about errors, bias, privacy and accountability.",
s:[["Reading medical images","AI systems can analyze X-rays, CT scans, mammograms, eye photographs and skin images. In 2018 the United States approved the first AI system that could diagnose diabetic eye disease without a specialist reading the images. In breast cancer screening, studies have found AI can help radiologists find cancers while reducing their workload."],["Predicting risk","By analyzing patient records, AI can help predict which patients are at risk of conditions such as sepsis, a life-threatening reaction to infection, or of being readmitted to hospital, so that doctors can act early."],["Discovering medicines","AI helps scientists search through millions of possible molecules to find new drugs faster. In 2020 researchers at MIT used AI to discover a new antibiotic, named halicin, that killed many drug-resistant bacteria in laboratory tests."],["Paperwork and communication","Doctors and nurses spend much of their time on records. AI tools can turn conversations into clinical notes, summarize records and help translate information for patients, giving health workers more time to care for people."]],
k:[["Main uses","Imaging, risk prediction, drug discovery, paperwork"],["First autonomous AI diagnosis approved","2018 (diabetic eye disease)"],["AlphaFold","Predicts protein structures"],["Nobel Prize for protein AI","Chemistry, 2024"],["Key concerns","Errors, bias, privacy, accountability"]],
d:[["1970s","Early expert systems are developed to suggest diagnoses"],["2018","An AI system is approved to diagnose diabetic eye disease"],["2020","AlphaFold makes a breakthrough in predicting protein shapes"],["2020","AI helps discover a new antibiotic"],["2021","The WHO publishes guidance on ethics of AI in health"],["2024","The Nobel Prize in Chemistry honors protein structure prediction"]],
s2:[["AlphaFold","Proteins are the molecular machines of life, and their shapes determine what they do. Working out a protein’s shape used to take years of laboratory work. In 2020 AlphaFold, developed by Google DeepMind, predicted protein shapes with remarkable accuracy, and its database now contains predictions for hundreds of millions of proteins. Demis Hassabis and John Jumper shared the 2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for it with David Baker, who designs new proteins."],["Reaching more people","In places with few specialists, such as rural areas of Asia and Africa, AI tools on phones or simple devices could help health workers screen for eye disease, tuberculosis or skin conditions. Telemedicine combined with AI may bring expert-level advice to remote communities, including island communities in the Philippines."],["Bias","AI systems learn from data, and if that data mostly comes from certain groups, the systems may work less well for others. For example, skin-image tools trained mostly on light skin may perform worse on darker skin. Researchers stress the need for diverse data and careful testing."],["Errors and responsibility","AI can make mistakes, and chatbots can give confident but wrong medical answers. Experts say AI should support trained professionals, who remain responsible for decisions, and that patients should check health advice with qualified health workers."],["Privacy","Medical data is highly sensitive. Using it to train AI requires strong protections, consent and compliance with privacy laws, such as the Data Privacy Act in the Philippines."],["The road ahead","Researchers are exploring AI that helps design personalized treatments, monitors patients at home through wearable devices and speeds up clinical trials. Regulators are developing rules to make sure medical AI is safe, effective and fair."]],
sp:["The antibiotic named after a fictional computer","In 2020 scientists at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology trained an AI system on thousands of molecules to learn which ones could kill bacteria. They then let it search a library of more than 100 million molecules. It picked out a compound that had been studied as a possible diabetes drug but had never been considered an antibiotic. In lab tests, it killed many dangerous drug-resistant bacteria. The researchers named it halicin, after HAL 9000, the artificial intelligence in the film 2001: A Space Odyssey, a playful nod to the AI that found it."],
g:[["Artificial intelligence","Computer systems that perform tasks needing human-like intelligence"],["Medical imaging","Pictures of the inside of the body, such as X-rays and scans"],["Protein","A large molecule that does work in living cells"],["Telemedicine","Health care provided at a distance using technology"]],
f:["AI can help read X-rays and eye photos.","AlphaFold predicts the shapes of proteins.","The 2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry honored protein AI."],
fun:"An antibiotic discovered by AI was named after the computer HAL 9000.",
tl:"Ginagamit ang artificial intelligence sa medisina upang tumulong sa pagtukoy ng sakit, pagbasa ng X-ray at pagtuklas ng bagong gamot. Dapat itong sumuporta sa mga doktor, hindi pumalit sa kanila, at may mga alalahanin sa pagkakamali at privacy."});

ADDART({id:"kdrama",t:"Korean Dramas",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"📺",w:"Korean drama",r:["kpop","tv","streaming","southkoreacountry","cinema"],
p:[["K-dramas","Korean television dramas are watched by fans all over the world."],["Hallyu","K-dramas are part of Hallyu, the global spread of South Korean culture."],["Loved in the Philippines","Koreanovelas have been hugely popular in the Philippines since the early 2000s."]],
l:"Korean dramas, often called K-dramas, are television series produced in South Korea, known for their romance, family stories, historical settings, thrillers, beautiful scenery and emotional storytelling, usually told in a single season. From the late 1990s, they spread across Asia and then the world as part of Hallyu, the Korean Wave, along with K-pop, films and food. Shows such as Winter Sonata, Jewel in the Palace, Crash Landing on You and Squid Game became international hits. In the Philippines, dubbed Koreanovelas have been popular since the early 2000s, and several have been remade with Filipino actors.",
s:[["What makes K-dramas popular","K-dramas are praised for strong writing, high production quality, attractive actors, memorable soundtracks and a mix of genres within a single show. Most tell a complete story in one season of about 12 to 20 episodes, rather than running for years."],["Genres","Popular genres include romantic comedy, melodrama, historical dramas called sageuk, crime thrillers, fantasy and medical and legal dramas. Many explore themes of family, social class, friendship, first love and perseverance."],["The Korean Wave","The term Hallyu, or Korean Wave, was popularized by media in China in the late 1990s to describe the sudden popularity of Korean dramas and music. The South Korean government and companies supported the spread of Korean culture abroad, which also boosted tourism, language learning and exports."],["Streaming","Global streaming services made K-dramas available with subtitles in many languages, greatly expanding their audiences in the Americas, Europe, Africa and the Middle East from the late 2010s."]],
k:[["Country of origin","South Korea"],["Part of","Hallyu, the Korean Wave"],["Typical length","One season of 12–20 episodes"],["Historical dramas","Sageuk"],["Global hit","Squid Game (2021)"]],
d:[["1997","A Korean drama becomes a hit in China, helping start the Korean Wave"],["2002","Winter Sonata becomes a sensation in Japan"],["2003","Jewel in the Palace airs and is later broadcast worldwide"],["2003","Dubbed Korean dramas become hits on Philippine television"],["2019","Crash Landing on You is released"],["2021","Squid Game becomes one of the most-watched streaming series ever"]],
s2:[["Koreanovelas in the Philippines","In 2003 Philippine networks began airing Korean dramas dubbed in Filipino, called Koreanovelas, and shows such as Endless Love and Lovers in Paris drew huge audiences. They inspired Filipino remakes of hits such as My Love from the Star and Start-Up, increased interest in learning Korean and visiting Korea and helped make Filipinos some of the most enthusiastic K-drama fans in the world."],["Squid Game","Squid Game, released in 2021, tells of debt-ridden people competing in deadly versions of children’s games for a huge cash prize, a story criticizing inequality. It became one of the most-watched streaming series ever, and its star, Lee Jung-jae, became the first actor to win an Emmy for lead actor in a drama for a non-English-language performance."],["Korean film","Korean cinema has also gained global acclaim. Bong Joon-ho’s film Parasite won the Academy Award for Best Picture in 2020, the first non-English-language film to do so."],["Food and fashion","K-dramas have spread interest in Korean food, such as ramyeon, kimchi, tteokbokki and Korean fried chicken, as well as fashion, skincare and filming locations, which become tourist attractions."],["Criticism","Some viewers criticize certain K-dramas for repeating clichés, unrealistic romance, product placement and narrow beauty standards. Workers in the industry have also raised concerns about long filming hours, leading to reforms."],["A two-way exchange","Korean dramas often feature Filipino and Southeast Asian fans, locations and stories, and Korean producers increasingly co-produce with other countries. The success of K-dramas has encouraged other Asian countries, including the Philippines, Thailand and Japan, to compete for global streaming audiences."]],
sp:["Winter Sonata and the Japanese fans","In 2003 and 2004, the Korean drama Winter Sonata, a romantic story of first love, snow and memory loss, became a sensation in Japan. Its star, Bae Yong-joon, gained a huge following, especially among middle-aged women, who called him Yon-sama, a very respectful title. Thousands of fans traveled to Korea to visit filming locations, such as Nami Island, and in 2004 crowds greeted the actor at Tokyo’s airport. Winter Sonata helped improve Japanese views of Korea and showed how a television drama could change relations between countries."],
g:[["K-drama","A television drama from South Korea"],["Hallyu","The Korean Wave, the global spread of Korean culture"],["Sageuk","A Korean historical drama"],["Koreanovela","A Korean drama dubbed in Filipino"]],
f:["Squid Game was released in 2021.","Koreanovelas began airing in the Philippines in 2003.","Parasite was the first non-English film to win Best Picture."],
fun:"Fans of Winter Sonata gave its star a title of deep respect: Yon-sama.",
tl:"Ang K-drama ay mga seryeng pantelebisyon mula sa South Korea na kilala sa romansa at magandang kuwento. Bahagi ito ng Hallyu o Korean Wave. Sikat ang mga Koreanovela sa Pilipinas mula 2003."});

ADDART({id:"shortvideo",t:"Short-Video Apps",c:"Technology",i:"📹",w:"Short-form content",r:["socialmedia","streaming","smartphone","screentime","misinformation"],
p:[["Quick videos","Short-video apps let people make and watch videos lasting seconds to a few minutes."],["The endless feed","Algorithms choose an endless stream of videos for each viewer."],["Big influence","Short videos shape music, trends, shopping and news."]],
l:"Short-video apps are social media platforms built around brief, vertical videos, usually from a few seconds to a few minutes long, watched on smartphones. TikTok, known in China as Douyin, made the format hugely popular, and Instagram Reels and YouTube Shorts followed. Their feeds are driven by algorithms that learn what each viewer likes. Short videos have launched new stars, songs, dances, recipes and businesses, and they are used for entertainment, learning and selling. They have also raised concerns about screen time, attention, privacy, misinformation and the safety of young users.",
s:[["Beginnings","Vine, launched in 2013, let users share six-second looping videos and created early internet stars before closing in 2017. Musical.ly, launched in 2014, let users lip-sync to songs. The Chinese company ByteDance launched Douyin in China in 2016 and TikTok internationally in 2017, merging it with Musical.ly in 2018."],["The For You feed","Short-video apps show an endless feed of videos chosen by algorithms based on what users watch, like, share and skip. Even new creators can reach huge audiences if the algorithm picks their videos, which made the format very different from apps based mainly on following friends."],["Creators and trends","Creators make dances, comedy sketches, tutorials, cooking videos, news explainers and more. Trends spread rapidly as people copy sounds and challenges. Songs that go viral on short-video apps often climb music charts."],["Competition","After TikTok’s success, Instagram launched Reels and YouTube launched Shorts in 2020, and many other apps added short videos. Short-form video became one of the most popular ways people spend time online."]],
k:[["Best-known app","TikTok (Douyin in China)"],["Owner of TikTok","ByteDance"],["Early pioneer","Vine (2013–2017)"],["Competitors","Instagram Reels, YouTube Shorts"],["Format","Short, vertical videos"]],
d:[["2013","Vine launches six-second videos"],["2014","Musical.ly launches"],["2016","Douyin launches in China"],["2018","TikTok merges with Musical.ly"],["2020","Instagram Reels and YouTube Shorts launch"],["2024","A US law requires TikTok’s owner to sell its US business or face a ban"]],
s2:[["Short videos in the Philippines","Filipinos are among the world’s most active users of short-video apps, sharing comedy, music, dance, food, travel and family videos. Many small businesses sell products through live streams and in-app shops, and creators have built large followings at home and among Filipinos abroad."],["Learning in short bursts","Teachers, scientists, doctors and museums use short videos to explain ideas quickly, and many people learn recipes, crafts and study tips from them. Critics note that complex topics can be oversimplified, and that it is important to check facts."],["Attention and screen time","Because feeds never end and each video is short, people can scroll for long periods without noticing. Some researchers worry about effects on attention, sleep and mood, especially for young people, though the evidence is still debated. Apps have added screen-time reminders and limits for teenagers."],["Misinformation and safety","False claims can spread quickly through short videos, and dangerous challenges have occasionally caused injuries. Platforms remove harmful content and label some information, but critics say moderation is inconsistent."],["Politics and security","Some governments have raised concerns about TikTok’s Chinese ownership and data security, which the company disputes. India banned TikTok in 2020, and in 2024 the United States passed a law requiring its owner to sell its American business, leading to long negotiations. Supporters of the app emphasize free expression and the livelihoods of creators."],["Music and culture","Short-video apps have changed the music industry, with older songs gaining new popularity and new artists discovered through viral clips. Dances, slang and fashion trends now often begin on these apps."]],
sp:["Six seconds of fame","When Vine launched in January 2013, users could post videos only six seconds long, looping endlessly. The tight limit pushed creators to be inventive, with quick jokes, magic tricks and stop-motion animation packed into a few seconds. Some Vine stars became famous musicians, comedians and actors. Although Vine closed in 2017, it pioneered the short, looping, vertical video format and the culture of internet creators that TikTok, Reels and Shorts would later bring to billions of people."],
g:[["Short-form video","A video lasting seconds to a few minutes"],["Algorithm","Rules a computer follows to choose content"],["Creator","A person who makes content for online audiences"],["Viral","Spreading rapidly online"]],
f:["Vine videos lasted six seconds.","TikTok merged with Musical.ly in 2018.","Instagram Reels and YouTube Shorts launched in 2020."],
fun:"Vine, an early short-video app, limited videos to just six seconds.",
tl:"Ang mga short-video app gaya ng TikTok ay nagpapakita ng maiikling video na pinipili ng algorithm. Ginagamit ang mga ito sa libangan, pag-aaral at pagbebenta, ngunit may mga alalahanin sa screen time at maling impormasyon."});

ADDART({id:"superhero",t:"Superheroes",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🦸",w:"Superhero",r:["comics","cinema","anime","phcinema","gmyth"],
p:[["Heroes with powers","Superheroes are fictional heroes with special powers or skills who fight evil."],["From comics to blockbusters","They began in comic books and became stars of the biggest films."],["Filipino heroes","Darna and other Filipino superheroes are beloved in the Philippines."]],
l:"Superheroes are fictional characters with extraordinary powers, skills or technology who use them to protect people and fight evil, usually wearing costumes and hiding secret identities. The modern superhero was born in American comic books with Superman in 1938, followed by Batman, Wonder Woman, Spider-Man and many others. Superheroes have since spread worldwide through comics, television, anime and films, which have become some of the highest-grossing movies of all time. Many countries have their own superheroes, such as Darna, the Filipino heroine created by Mars Ravelo.",
s:[["The first superheroes","Stories of heroes with superhuman powers go back to ancient myths, from Hercules to Hanuman. The modern superhero began with Superman, created by Jerry Siegel and Joe Shuster, in Action Comics in June 1938. Batman followed in 1939 and Wonder Woman in 1941, during the golden age of comics."],["Marvel’s heroes","In the 1960s, Marvel Comics introduced heroes with everyday problems, such as the Fantastic Four, Spider-Man, the X-Men and Black Panther, created by Stan Lee, Jack Kirby, Steve Ditko and others. Black Panther, introduced in 1966, was the first Black superhero in mainstream American comics."],["Why we love superheroes","Superhero stories explore courage, responsibility, justice, friendship and identity. Many heroes are outsiders or ordinary people who choose to help others, and their stories often reflect the hopes and fears of their times."],["Superheroes around the world","Japan has its own traditions of heroes, such as Ultraman, introduced in 1966, and the color-coded teams of Super Sentai, which inspired the Power Rangers. India, Nigeria, Brazil and many other countries have created superheroes rooted in their own cultures."]],
k:[["First modern superhero","Superman (1938)"],["Created by","Jerry Siegel and Joe Shuster"],["Filipino superheroine","Darna, by Mars Ravelo"],["Marvel film series began","2008"],["First Black mainstream superhero","Black Panther (1966)"]],
d:[["1938","Superman first appears"],["1950","Darna appears in Pilipino Komiks"],["1962","Spider-Man first appears"],["1966","Ultraman debuts in Japan"],["2008","Iron Man begins the Marvel Cinematic Universe"],["2019","Avengers: Endgame becomes one of the highest-grossing films ever"]],
s2:[["Darna","Darna was created by the Filipino writer Mars Ravelo and illustrated by Nestor Redondo, first appearing in Pilipino Komiks in 1950, based on an earlier character. Narda, a young girl from the provinces, swallows a magic stone from space and shouts Darna! to transform into a powerful heroine who fights villains such as the snake-haired Valentina. Darna has been played by many famous Filipino actresses in films and television series."],["Other Filipino heroes","Filipino komiks created many heroes, such as Captain Barbell, Lastikman and Panday, the blacksmith with a magic sword. More recently, the comic series Trese, about a detective who protects Manila from creatures of Philippine folklore, was adapted into an animated series in 2021."],["Superheroes on screen","Superheroes appeared in film serials and cartoons from the 1940s. The Marvel Cinematic Universe, beginning with Iron Man in 2008, linked dozens of films and series into one story, and Avengers: Endgame became one of the highest-grossing films of all time. Films such as Black Panther in 2018 drew praise for their representation."],["Diversity","Over time, superheroes have become more diverse, with heroes of different genders, ethnicities, religions and abilities, such as Ms. Marvel, a Pakistani American Muslim teenager. Fans and critics debate how well these characters are portrayed."],["Superhero fatigue","After years of superhero films, some critics and audiences began to speak of superhero fatigue, as some films earned less than expected in the 2020s. Others argue that audiences still enjoy superhero stories that are well told and fresh."],["Real-life heroes","Superhero stories often inspire people to see heroism in everyday life: health workers, rescuers, teachers and ordinary people who help others. During the COVID-19 pandemic, health workers were often celebrated as real-life superheroes."]],
sp:["Superman’s 130 dollars","In 1938, two young friends from Cleveland, Ohio, Jerry Siegel and Joe Shuster, sons of Jewish immigrants, finally sold their story about an alien hero from the planet Krypton to a comic book publisher, after years of rejections. They received 130 dollars, including the rights to the character. Superman, lifting a car on the cover of Action Comics number 1, became a sensation and launched the superhero genre. Siegel and Shuster later fought for decades for credit and payment, and in 1975 the publisher agreed to give them pensions and restore their names on every Superman story."],
g:[["Superhero","A fictional hero with extraordinary powers or skills"],["Secret identity","A hero’s hidden ordinary self"],["Komiks","Filipino comic books"],["Blockbuster","A very popular, high-earning film"]],
f:["Superman first appeared in 1938.","Darna was created by Mars Ravelo.","Black Panther was the first Black superhero in mainstream American comics."],
fun:"Superman’s creators sold the rights to the character for 130 dollars.",
tl:"Ang superhero ay kathang-isip na bayaning may pambihirang kapangyarihan. Nagsimula ang makabagong superhero kay Superman noong 1938. Sa Pilipinas, kilala si Darna na nilikha ni Mars Ravelo."});

;
/* data/modern5.js */
// The Modern World, part 4: work, money, digital life and changing societies — the cost of living, start-ups, automation, online learning, cyberbullying, the digital divide, smart homes, housing, loneliness and urban farming.
ADDART({id:"costliving",t:"The Cost of Living",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🧾",w:"Cost of living",r:["inflation","money","labor","foodsecurity","housing"],
p:[["Everyday prices","The cost of living is how much money people need for food, housing, transport and other basics."],["When prices jump","After 2021, prices rose quickly in many countries, causing a cost-of-living crisis."],["Making ends meet","Families, businesses and governments respond in different ways."]],
l:"The cost of living is the amount of money needed to pay for basic needs such as food, housing, transport, electricity, water, health care and education in a particular place and time. When prices rise faster than incomes, people can afford less, and families struggle to make ends meet. From 2021 to 2023, many countries suffered a cost-of-living crisis, as prices for food, fuel and housing surged after the COVID-19 pandemic and Russia’s invasion of Ukraine. Governments and central banks responded with interest rate rises, subsidies, wage increases and price controls, which remain subjects of debate.",
s:[["Measuring prices","Governments track the cost of living using a consumer price index, which measures the prices of a basket of common goods and services over time. The rate at which these prices rise is called inflation. In the Philippines, the Philippine Statistics Authority publishes inflation figures every month."],["The crisis of the early 2020s","As economies reopened after pandemic lockdowns, demand recovered while supply chains were disrupted. Russia’s invasion of Ukraine in 2022 pushed up prices of oil, gas, wheat and fertilizer. Global inflation reached its highest levels in decades, and many households saw their real incomes fall."],["Who is hit hardest","Rising prices hurt poorer households most, because they spend a larger share of their income on food, fuel and rent. People on fixed incomes, such as pensioners, also suffer when prices rise faster than their payments."],["Wages and prices","When prices rise, workers often ask for higher wages. In the Philippines, regional wage boards set minimum wages. Economists debate how to raise incomes without pushing prices even higher."]],
k:[["Measure of prices","Consumer price index"],["Rise in prices","Inflation"],["Recent crisis","2021–2023"],["Philippine inflation data","Philippine Statistics Authority"],["Main tool of central banks","Interest rates"]],
d:[["1970s","Oil price shocks cause high inflation worldwide"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic disrupts supply chains"],["2022","Russia’s invasion of Ukraine pushes up energy and food prices"],["2022","Onion prices soar in the Philippines"],["2023","Philippine inflation peaks at its highest level in over a decade"],["2023","The Philippines sets a temporary ceiling on rice prices"]],
s2:[["The onion crisis","In late 2022 and early 2023, onion prices in the Philippines soared because of poor harvests, limited imports and hoarding, at one point costing more per kilo than meat. Some restaurants removed onions from their menus, and the episode became a symbol of rising food prices. Authorities investigated price manipulation and allowed more imports."],["Rice prices","Rice is the staple food of most Filipinos, so its price strongly affects household budgets. When rice prices rose in 2023, the government briefly set price ceilings, and in 2024 it cut import tariffs on rice to lower prices. Such measures help consumers but can affect farmers’ incomes, a long-running debate."],["Central banks","To slow inflation, central banks, such as the Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas, raise interest rates, making borrowing more expensive and cooling spending. Higher rates can bring inflation down but may also slow economic growth and raise loan costs."],["Shrinkflation","When costs rise, some companies keep prices the same but put less product in each package, a practice nicknamed shrinkflation. Shoppers may notice smaller chocolate bars or snack packs, and some governments require clearer labeling."],["Coping with high prices","Families cope by budgeting, buying in bulk, comparing prices, cooking at home, growing vegetables and cutting unnecessary spending. Community programs, cash transfers and food assistance help the most vulnerable."],["Debates","Economists and politicians disagree about how much governments should intervene with subsidies, price controls or tax cuts, which can provide quick relief but may be costly or distort markets, versus letting central banks and markets adjust. Views often depend on how people weigh short-term relief against long-term effects."]],
sp:["The basket of goods","To measure inflation, statisticians imagine a basket filled with the things a typical household buys: rice, bread, fish, meat, vegetables, cooking oil, rent, electricity, transport fares, school supplies, haircuts and more. Every month, they check the prices of these items in markets and stores across the country. If the basket costs more than it did a year earlier, prices have risen. The contents are updated over time; mobile phone plans and internet subscriptions were added as they became part of everyday life, while items such as typewriters were removed."],
g:[["Cost of living","The money needed to pay for basic needs"],["Inflation","A general rise in prices over time"],["Consumer price index","A measure of the prices of a basket of goods and services"],["Shrinkflation","Reducing product size while keeping the price"]],
f:["Prices surged worldwide from 2021 to 2023.","Inflation is measured with a consumer price index.","Onions briefly cost more than meat in the Philippines."],
fun:"In early 2023, onions in the Philippines briefly cost more per kilo than meat.",
tl:"Ang cost of living ay ang halagang kailangan para sa pagkain, bahay, pamasahe at iba pang pangangailangan. Mula 2021 hanggang 2023, mabilis tumaas ang presyo sa maraming bansa, kabilang ang Pilipinas, gaya ng sibuyas at bigas."});

ADDART({id:"startups",t:"Start-ups and Entrepreneurs",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🚀",w:"Startup company",r:["money","stockm","internet","gigeconomy","digitalpay"],
p:[["New businesses","A start-up is a young company trying to grow quickly with a new idea."],["Taking risks","Entrepreneurs start businesses, taking risks in hope of success."],["Billion-dollar start-ups","Start-ups valued at a billion dollars are called unicorns."]],
l:"A start-up is a newly founded company that aims to grow rapidly by offering a new product, service or way of doing business, often using technology. Start-ups are founded by entrepreneurs, people willing to take risks to turn ideas into businesses. Famous companies such as Apple, Google, Amazon, Alibaba and Grab began as start-ups, some in garages or small apartments. Many start-ups are funded by investors called venture capitalists, and those valued at over a billion dollars are nicknamed unicorns. Most start-ups fail, but successful ones can create jobs, new industries and great wealth.",
s:[["What makes a start-up","Unlike a typical small business, such as a neighborhood store, a start-up aims to scale quickly, reaching many customers, often across countries. Start-ups usually begin with a small team, an idea and little money, and they experiment rapidly to find what customers want."],["Funding","Founders often start with their own savings, money from family and friends or small grants. Angel investors and venture capital firms then invest in exchange for a share of ownership, hoping a few big successes will make up for many failures. Some start-ups eventually sell shares on the stock market."],["Garage legends","Many famous companies began humbly. Hewlett-Packard started in a garage in California in 1939, Apple in a garage in 1976 and Amazon in a garage in 1994. Silicon Valley in California became the world’s best-known start-up hub."],["Unicorns","In 2013 the investor Aileen Lee coined the term unicorn for privately held start-ups valued at over a billion US dollars, because such companies were then rare. Today there are hundreds of unicorns around the world."]],
k:[["Meaning","A young company aiming to grow quickly"],["Founders","Entrepreneurs"],["Main investors","Venture capitalists"],["Unicorn","A start-up valued at over $1 billion"],["Philippine law","Innovative Startup Act (2019)"]],
d:[["1939","Hewlett-Packard is founded in a garage"],["1976","Apple is founded"],["1999","Alibaba is founded in China"],["2012","Grab is founded"],["2013","The term unicorn is coined for billion-dollar start-ups"],["2019","The Philippines passes the Innovative Startup Act"]],
s2:[["Start-ups in Southeast Asia","Southeast Asia has produced major tech companies, such as Grab, which began in Malaysia and is now based in Singapore, Gojek and Tokopedia in Indonesia and Sea Group, owner of Shopee. They grew by solving local problems, such as hailing safe rides, delivering food and making payments for people without bank cards."],["Start-ups in the Philippines","The Philippine start-up scene has grown, especially in digital payments, online lending, logistics and education. The company behind GCash became the country’s first unicorn in 2021. The Innovative Startup Act of 2019 provides support such as grants, training and easier business registration."],["Africa and Latin America","Start-ups in Africa, such as the payments companies Flutterwave and M-Pesa’s successors, and in Latin America, such as MercadoLibre and Nubank, have grown rapidly by serving large populations with mobile technology."],["Failure","Most start-ups fail, often because there is not enough demand for their product, they run out of money or face strong competition. Many entrepreneurs see failure as a learning experience, and some successful founders failed several times before succeeding."],["Social enterprises","Some entrepreneurs start businesses mainly to solve social or environmental problems, such as providing clean water, affordable lighting or jobs for marginalized people, while still earning money. These are called social enterprises."],["Criticism","Critics say start-up culture can glorify overwork, encourage risky growth at any cost and concentrate wealth among a few founders and investors. Some high-profile start-ups collapsed after misleading investors. Supporters point to the innovations, jobs and services that start-ups create."]],
sp:["The garage in Palo Alto","In 1939 two young Stanford University graduates, Bill Hewlett and David Packard, set up a business in a small rented garage behind a house at 367 Addison Avenue in Palo Alto, California, with about 538 dollars. Their first major product was an audio oscillator, an electronic instrument for testing sound equipment; one of their first customers was Walt Disney Studios, which used it for the film Fantasia. Hewlett-Packard grew into a giant electronics company, and the garage is now a historic landmark known as the birthplace of Silicon Valley."],
g:[["Start-up","A young company aiming for rapid growth"],["Entrepreneur","A person who starts a business, taking risks"],["Venture capital","Money invested in young companies in exchange for ownership"],["Unicorn","A start-up valued at over a billion dollars"]],
f:["Many famous companies began as start-ups in garages.","A unicorn is a start-up worth over $1 billion.","The Philippines passed an Innovative Startup Act in 2019."],
fun:"One of Hewlett-Packard’s first customers was Walt Disney Studios.",
tl:"Ang start-up ay bagong kompanyang naglalayong lumago nang mabilis gamit ang bagong ideya. Ang mga negosyanteng nagsisimula nito ay tinatawag na entrepreneur. Ang start-up na nagkakahalaga ng mahigit isang bilyong dolyar ay tinatawag na unicorn."});

ADDART({id:"automation",t:"Automation and the Future of Work",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🤖",w:"Technological unemployment",r:["robot","ai","labor","gigeconomy","education"],
p:[["Machines at work","Automation means machines and software doing tasks once done by people."],["Jobs lost and created","Technology removes some jobs but creates new ones."],["Learning for the future","New skills help workers adapt as work changes."]],
l:"Automation is the use of machines, robots, computers and artificial intelligence to perform tasks that were once done by people. Since the Industrial Revolution, automation has transformed farming, factories and offices, eliminating some jobs while creating new ones and raising productivity. Today, advances in robotics and AI are spreading automation to new kinds of work, from driving and customer service to writing and analysis. People debate how many jobs may be affected, who will benefit and how societies should help workers adapt, through education, training and social protections.",
s:[["A long history","Machines have replaced human work for centuries, from water mills and steam engines to tractors, assembly lines and computers. In the early 1800s, English textile workers called Luddites smashed machines they feared would destroy their livelihoods. Over time, new industries created new jobs, though the transition was painful for many."],["Robots in factories","The first industrial robot, Unimate, began working on a General Motors assembly line in the United States in 1961, handling hot metal parts. Today millions of industrial robots weld, paint, assemble and pack goods. South Korea, Singapore, China, Germany and Japan use the most robots per worker."],["AI and office work","Artificial intelligence can now handle tasks such as answering customer questions, translating, summarizing documents, writing code and analyzing data. This means automation increasingly affects office and professional jobs, not just manual ones."],["Jobs that change","Most jobs are not entirely replaced; instead, some tasks are automated while others remain, changing what workers do. Tasks needing creativity, complex judgment, empathy, care and physical dexterity in unpredictable settings are harder to automate."]],
k:[["First industrial robot","Unimate (1961)"],["Early protesters","The Luddites (1811–1816)"],["Most robots per worker","South Korea"],["Jobs exposed to AI","Many, according to IMF estimates"],["Key response","Education and retraining"]],
d:[["1811","Luddites begin smashing textile machines in England"],["1913","Henry Ford introduces the moving assembly line"],["1961","The first industrial robot starts work"],["1997","A computer defeats the world chess champion"],["2013","A widely cited study estimates many jobs could be automated"],["2022","AI chatbots bring automation to writing and office tasks"]],
s2:[["How many jobs?","Estimates vary widely. A famous 2013 Oxford study estimated that about 47 percent of US jobs were at high risk of automation, while later studies gave lower figures. In 2024 the International Monetary Fund estimated that around 40 percent of jobs worldwide are exposed to AI, with some likely to benefit and others at risk. Many economists expect new jobs to emerge, as in past technological changes."],["The Philippines","The Philippines’ large business process outsourcing industry, including call centers and back-office work, employs well over a million people. AI tools that handle customer service and data processing could change many of these jobs, while also creating new roles in managing, training and checking AI systems. Training workers in new skills is a priority."],["New jobs","Technology has created jobs that did not exist a generation ago, such as app developers, data scientists, social media managers, drone pilots, cybersecurity experts and electric vehicle technicians. Care work, such as nursing and elderly care, is also growing."],["Responses","Ideas for helping workers include lifelong learning and retraining, stronger social safety nets, shorter working weeks and, more controversially, a universal basic income, regular payments to all citizens regardless of work. Countries are experimenting with different approaches."],["Who benefits?","Automation can raise productivity and lower prices, but its benefits may go mainly to company owners and highly skilled workers unless policies spread them more widely. How to share the gains fairly is a central question."],["Humans and machines together","Many experts emphasize working alongside machines, using technology to take over dangerous, dull or repetitive tasks so that people can focus on creative, social and complex work."]],
sp:["The Luddites","Between 1811 and 1816, skilled textile workers in the north of England, facing falling wages and unemployment, broke into workshops at night and smashed new mechanized looms and knitting frames. They claimed to follow a mysterious leader called General Ned Ludd, probably a legendary figure. The government sent thousands of soldiers to stop them and made machine-breaking punishable by death. Today the word Luddite is used for anyone who opposes new technology, but historians point out that the original Luddites were not against machines as such; they were protesting how machines were used to cut wages and worsen their working conditions."],
g:[["Automation","Using machines to do work once done by people"],["Industrial robot","A programmable machine used in manufacturing"],["Retraining","Learning new skills for a different job"],["Universal basic income","Regular payments to all citizens regardless of work"]],
f:["The first industrial robot began work in 1961.","The Luddites protested against machines in 1811–1816.","South Korea uses the most robots per worker."],
fun:"The original Luddites claimed to follow a leader called General Ned Ludd, who probably never existed.",
tl:"Ang automation ay paggamit ng makina, robot at artificial intelligence sa mga gawaing dating ginagawa ng tao. Nawawala ang ilang trabaho ngunit may mga bagong nalilikha. Mahalaga ang pag-aaral ng bagong kasanayan."});

ADDART({id:"onlinelearning",t:"Online Learning",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🎓",w:"Online learning",r:["education","internet","digitaldivide","covid19","remotework"],
p:[["Learning online","Online learning uses the internet to teach and learn."],["Classes for everyone","Free online courses let anyone study subjects from top universities."],["A pandemic test","During COVID-19, schools around the world moved to distance learning."]],
l:"Online learning, also called e-learning or distance learning when done remotely, is education delivered through the internet using videos, live classes, interactive exercises and digital materials. It ranges from online courses at universities and free lessons on websites to language-learning apps and video tutorials. Online learning makes education more flexible and accessible, but it depends on devices, internet access and self-discipline. During the COVID-19 pandemic, about 1.6 billion students were affected by school closures, and many schools, including in the Philippines, turned to online and modular distance learning, revealing both its promise and its limits.",
s:[["Learning at a distance","Distance learning is older than the internet. In the 1840s, Isaac Pitman taught shorthand by post. Radio and television later carried lessons, and the United Kingdom’s Open University, founded in 1969, taught students at home through broadcasts and printed materials."],["The internet era","From the 1990s, universities began offering online courses. In 2008 Khan Academy began posting free video lessons, and in 2012, called the year of the MOOC, massive open online courses from platforms such as Coursera and edX let millions study university subjects for free."],["Kinds of online learning","Some online classes are live, with teachers and students meeting by video. Others are self-paced, with recorded lessons and exercises. Blended learning combines online and face-to-face teaching. Apps teach languages, mathematics and coding through short, game-like lessons."],["Benefits","Online learning lets people study anytime and anywhere, at their own pace, often for free or at low cost. It can reach learners in remote areas, working adults and people with disabilities, and gives access to courses not available locally."]],
k:[["Also called","E-learning, distance learning"],["Open University founded","1969"],["Year of the MOOC","2012"],["Students affected by pandemic closures","About 1.6 billion"],["Philippine pandemic approach","Modular distance learning"]],
d:[["1840","Isaac Pitman teaches shorthand by post"],["1969","The Open University is founded in the United Kingdom"],["2008","Khan Academy begins posting free lessons"],["2012","Massive open online courses become popular"],["2020","School closures affect about 1.6 billion students worldwide"],["2022","Philippine schools return to full in-person classes"]],
s2:[["The Philippines during the pandemic","When schools closed in 2020, the Department of Education adopted distance learning. Most students used printed self-learning modules, while others had online classes or lessons on television and radio. Parents and older siblings often helped as home teachers. Schools returned to full in-person classes in November 2022."],["Challenges","Online learning requires a device, a reliable internet connection and a quiet place to study, which many students lack. It can be harder to stay motivated without classmates and teachers nearby, and young children especially need in-person interaction. Studies found significant learning losses during long school closures worldwide."],["Teachers online","Teachers had to learn new skills quickly during the pandemic, creating digital lessons, using video platforms and finding ways to keep students engaged. Many continue to use online tools in their classrooms."],["Learning anytime","Millions of people learn new skills online through video platforms, podcasts and courses, from cooking and music to programming and languages. Many Filipino workers take online courses to improve their job prospects."],["AI and learning","Artificial intelligence tools can now explain concepts, give feedback and act as tutors. Supporters see them as a way to give every student personal help; critics worry about cheating, errors and students relying on AI instead of thinking for themselves. Schools are developing rules for their use."],["The future","Most experts expect a mix of in-person and online learning. The pandemic showed that schools are important not just for lessons but for friendship, health, safety and support, while online tools can extend learning beyond the classroom."]],
sp:["Khan Academy began with a cousin","In 2004, Salman Khan, a financial analyst in the United States, began tutoring his young cousin Nadia in mathematics over the phone and internet. Soon other relatives wanted help too, so he started recording short video lessons and posting them on YouTube, where anyone could watch. His cousins said they preferred him on video, because they could pause and replay him without feeling embarrassed. The videos became so popular that in 2008 he founded Khan Academy, a nonprofit offering free lessons. Today it has lessons in dozens of languages used by millions of students around the world."],
g:[["Online learning","Education delivered through the internet"],["MOOC","Massive open online course"],["Blended learning","A mix of online and in-person teaching"],["Module","A self-learning booklet or unit of study"]],
f:["The Open University was founded in 1969.","2012 was called the year of the MOOC.","About 1.6 billion students were affected by pandemic school closures."],
fun:"Khan Academy began when Salman Khan tutored his cousin online.",
tl:"Ang online learning ay pag-aaral gamit ang internet. Noong pandemya ng COVID-19, gumamit ang mga paaralan sa Pilipinas ng modular at online distance learning. Kapaki-pakinabang ito ngunit nangangailangan ng gadget at internet."});

ADDART({id:"cyberbullying",t:"Cyberbullying",c:"Technology",i:"🛑",w:"Cyberbullying",r:["socialmedia","mentalyouth","dataprivacy","cybersecurity","misinformation"],
p:[["Bullying online","Cyberbullying is using phones, games or the internet to hurt, threaten or embarrass someone."],["It can be stopped","Blocking, reporting and telling a trusted adult help stop it."],["Be an upstander","Friends and bystanders can make a big difference."]],
l:"Cyberbullying is bullying that takes place through digital devices, such as phones, computers and game consoles, on social media, messaging apps, online games and other platforms. It includes sending hurtful messages, spreading rumors, sharing embarrassing photos or videos, excluding people from groups, impersonating them or threatening them. Because online content can spread quickly, reach many people and be hard to remove, cyberbullying can deeply hurt those targeted. Many countries, including the Philippines, have laws and school policies against it, and experts encourage young people to block, report and tell a trusted adult.",
s:[["Forms of cyberbullying","Cyberbullying can include name-calling and insults, spreading lies, posting embarrassing images, creating fake accounts to mock someone, deliberately leaving someone out of group chats, threatening messages and sharing private information or images without permission."],["How it differs","Unlike face-to-face bullying, cyberbullying can happen at any time of day, follow people home and reach large audiences. Bullies may hide behind anonymous accounts, and hurtful posts can be copied and shared widely."],["Effects","People who are cyberbullied may feel sad, anxious, embarrassed, angry or alone, and may avoid school or lose interest in activities. Cyberbullying can affect sleep, grades and mental health. Support from family, friends and schools helps people recover."],["How common is it","Surveys around the world suggest that a significant share of young people have experienced cyberbullying. A 2019 UNICEF poll of young people in 30 countries found that about one in three said they had been bullied online."]],
k:[["Where it happens","Social media, messaging apps, games"],["UNICEF 2019 poll","About 1 in 3 young people bullied online"],["Philippine law","Anti-Bullying Act of 2013"],["First steps","Don’t reply, save evidence, block, report, tell an adult"],["Stop Cyberbullying Day","A Friday in June"]],
d:[["1990s","Online chat rooms bring new forms of harassment"],["2000s","Social media and mobile phones spread cyberbullying"],["2013","The Philippines passes the Anti-Bullying Act"],["2019","The Philippines passes the Safe Spaces Act, covering online harassment"],["2019","A UNICEF poll highlights online bullying of young people"],["2020s","Platforms add more tools to block and report bullying"]],
s2:[["What to do if you are cyberbullied","Experts advise: do not reply or retaliate; take screenshots to save evidence; block the person; report the content to the platform; and tell a trusted adult, such as a parent, teacher or school counselor. If there are threats of violence, the police can help."],["Laws in the Philippines","The Anti-Bullying Act of 2013 requires schools to adopt policies against bullying, including cyberbullying. The Safe Spaces Act of 2019 covers gender-based harassment online, and other laws address threats, identity theft and sharing intimate images without consent."],["Upstanders","Bystanders who see cyberbullying can help by not sharing or liking hurtful content, supporting the person targeted with a kind message, reporting the content and telling an adult. Being an upstander rather than a bystander can stop bullying from spreading."],["Think before you post","Before posting or sharing, it helps to ask: Is it true? Is it kind? Would I say it face to face? Would I want it said about me? Online posts can last a long time and be seen by many people."],["Platforms and schools","Social media companies provide tools to block, mute, restrict and report users and to filter offensive comments. Schools teach digital citizenship, helping students understand respectful behavior online and how to get help."],["Kindness online","Campaigns encourage people to use the internet to spread kindness, such as posting encouraging messages, standing up for others and calling out hate speech. A positive online community makes the internet safer for everyone."]],
sp:["Pink shirts against bullying","In 2007, in Nova Scotia, Canada, a ninth-grade boy was bullied for wearing a pink shirt to school. Two older students, David Shepherd and Travis Price, decided to take a stand. They bought dozens of pink shirts and handed them out, encouraging classmates to wear them. The next day, hundreds of students came to school dressed in pink, and the bullies backed down. Their simple act grew into Pink Shirt Day, an anti-bullying day now marked in many countries, showing how bystanders acting together can stop bullying."],
g:[["Cyberbullying","Bullying through digital devices and the internet"],["Bystander","A person who sees something happen"],["Upstander","A person who acts to help when they see wrongdoing"],["Digital citizenship","Responsible and respectful behavior online"]],
f:["About one in three young people in a UNICEF poll said they were bullied online.","The Philippines passed the Anti-Bullying Act in 2013.","Save evidence, block, report and tell a trusted adult."],
fun:"Pink Shirt Day began when two students handed out pink shirts to stand up to bullies.",
tl:"Ang cyberbullying ay pananakit, pananakot o pagpapahiya sa isang tao gamit ang cellphone, laro o internet. Huwag gumanti, mag-screenshot ng ebidensiya, mag-block, mag-report at magsabi sa mapagkakatiwalaang matanda."});

ADDART({id:"digitaldivide",t:"The Digital Divide",c:"Technology",i:"📶",w:"Digital divide",r:["internet","inequality","onlinelearning","smartphone","sat"],
p:[["Connected and unconnected","The digital divide is the gap between people who have internet access and those who do not."],["Billions still offline","More than 2 billion people have never used the internet or rarely do."],["Closing the gap","Cheaper devices, better networks and digital skills help."]],
l:"The digital divide is the gap between people who have access to computers, smartphones and the internet, and the skills to use them, and those who do not. Although most of the world’s people are now online, more than 2 billion remain offline, most of them in low-income countries, rural areas and among women, older people and the poor. Lack of access can limit education, jobs, health care, banking and participation in society. Governments, companies and communities are working to close the gap through better networks, affordable devices and data, public Wi-Fi and digital skills training.",
s:[["Access","The first part of the divide is access: whether there are mobile networks, fiber cables or satellites to connect people, and whether they have devices. Remote islands, mountains and rural areas are often the hardest and most expensive to connect."],["Affordability","Even where networks exist, many people cannot afford smartphones, computers or data plans. The United Nations has set a goal that basic internet should cost no more than about 2 percent of average monthly income."],["Skills and use","Having access is not enough; people also need digital skills to use the internet safely and productively, to find reliable information, protect their privacy and avoid scams. Older people and those with less education often have fewer digital skills."],["Gender and other gaps","In many low-income countries, women are less likely than men to own a mobile phone or use the internet. People with disabilities may face barriers if websites and apps are not designed to be accessible."]],
k:[["People offline","More than 2 billion"],["Most affected","Low-income countries, rural areas, women, older people"],["UN affordability target","Basic internet under about 2% of monthly income"],["Philippine free Wi-Fi law","2017"],["Key solutions","Networks, affordable devices, digital skills"]],
d:[["1990s","The term digital divide comes into use"],["2005","The One Laptop per Child project is launched"],["2016","The UN declares internet access important for human rights"],["2017","The Philippines passes a law for free Wi-Fi in public places"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic highlights the digital divide"],["2020s","Satellite internet reaches more remote areas"]],
s2:[["The Philippines","Internet use in the Philippines has grown rapidly, and Filipinos are among the world’s heaviest social media users. But many remote barangays, islands and mountain communities have weak or no signal, and data costs are a burden for poor families. The Free Internet Access in Public Places Act of 2017 aims to provide free Wi-Fi in public places such as schools, plazas and hospitals, and new cell towers and satellite services are expanding coverage."],["Learning during the pandemic","When schools closed during the COVID-19 pandemic, students without devices or internet fell behind. In the Philippines, many families shared one phone among several children, and students climbed hills or trees to find a signal, which made the digital divide visible to everyone."],["Mobile phones first","In much of Africa and Asia, many people first connected to the internet through mobile phones rather than computers. Mobile money, such as M-Pesa in Kenya, showed how phones can bring services to people without bank branches or computers."],["Satellites and new networks","Satellite internet services can reach remote islands and villages where cables are too expensive. Other approaches include community networks built by local residents and undersea cables linking islands and countries."],["Libraries and community centers","Public libraries, schools and community centers provide computers, internet access and training for people who lack them at home. In the Philippines, local governments and nonprofit groups run digital literacy programs."],["Why it matters","As more services move online, from government forms and job applications to banking, health care and learning, people without access risk being left further behind. Closing the digital divide is part of the Sustainable Development Goals."]],
sp:["Climbing for a signal","During the COVID-19 pandemic, stories and photos spread of students in the Philippines and other countries climbing hills, trees and rooftops, or walking long distances to roadsides, to find a mobile signal strong enough to submit assignments or join online classes. In some communities, residents built small bamboo huts on hilltops as study spots with better reception. These images showed the determination of students, but also how unequal access to the internet could decide who was able to keep learning."],
g:[["Digital divide","The gap between those with and without digital access"],["Connectivity","The ability to connect to the internet"],["Digital literacy","The skills to use digital technology well"],["Accessibility","Design that allows people with disabilities to use something"]],
f:["More than 2 billion people are offline.","The Philippines passed a free public Wi-Fi law in 2017.","Many people first go online through mobile phones."],
fun:"During the pandemic, some students built bamboo huts on hilltops to get a better phone signal.",
tl:"Ang digital divide ay agwat sa pagitan ng may access sa internet at wala. Mahigit 2 bilyong tao pa rin ang walang internet. Sa Pilipinas, maraming malalayong lugar ang mahina ang signal."});

ADDART({id:"smarthome",t:"Smart Homes and the Internet of Things",c:"Technology",i:"🏠",w:"Internet of things",r:["internet","ai","dataprivacy","cybersecurity","elec"],
p:[["Connected things","The Internet of Things connects everyday objects to the internet."],["Smart homes","Lights, speakers, cameras and appliances can be controlled by phone or voice."],["Convenience and risks","Smart devices are convenient but raise privacy and security concerns."]],
l:"The Internet of Things, or IoT, is the growing network of everyday objects, such as lights, thermostats, door locks, cameras, appliances, watches, cars, factory machines and farm sensors, that are connected to the internet and can collect data, communicate and be controlled remotely. A smart home uses such devices so that people can control lighting, appliances, security and entertainment with a phone app or voice commands. IoT devices can save energy, improve safety and help older people and people with disabilities live independently, but they also raise concerns about privacy, hacking and electronic waste.",
s:[["Origins","In 1982 students at Carnegie Mellon University connected a soda machine to their network so they could check whether drinks were cold before walking to it. In 1999 the British technologist Kevin Ashton coined the phrase Internet of Things. Cheap sensors, wireless networks and smartphones later made connected devices common."],["Smart home devices","Common smart home devices include voice-controlled speakers, smart lights and plugs, security cameras and video doorbells, smart locks, thermostats and air conditioners, robot vacuum cleaners and smart televisions. Many can be programmed to follow schedules or respond to sensors."],["Voice assistants","Smart speakers with voice assistants, such as Amazon’s Alexa, launched in 2014, and Google Assistant, let people play music, set timers, check the weather and control devices by speaking. They use artificial intelligence to understand speech."],["Beyond the home","The IoT is used in cities to manage traffic lights and streetlights, in farms to monitor soil moisture and livestock, in hospitals to track equipment and patients, in factories to predict machine failures and in disaster monitoring, such as flood and weather sensors."]],
k:[["Term coined","1999, by Kevin Ashton"],["Early connected device","A soda machine at Carnegie Mellon (1982)"],["Smart speaker with Alexa launched","2014"],["Common standard","Matter (2022)"],["Main concerns","Privacy, security, e-waste"]],
d:[["1982","Students connect a soda machine to a network"],["1991","A coffee pot webcam goes online at Cambridge"],["1999","The term Internet of Things is coined"],["2014","Amazon launches a smart speaker with Alexa"],["2016","A huge cyberattack uses hacked IoT devices"],["2022","The Matter standard aims to make smart devices work together"]],
s2:[["Energy saving","Smart thermostats, air conditioners, lights and plugs can turn devices off when they are not needed and schedule them for cheaper times. Smart electricity meters help households and utilities track power use."],["Helping people live independently","Smart devices can help older people and people with disabilities by allowing voice control of lights and doors, sending reminders to take medicine and alerting family members or caregivers if someone falls."],["Security risks","Many IoT devices have weak security, such as default passwords that are never changed. In 2016 hackers used a program called Mirai to take over hundreds of thousands of poorly secured cameras and routers, using them to launch a huge attack that disrupted major websites. Experts advise changing default passwords and updating devices."],["Privacy","Smart speakers, cameras and other devices collect data about people’s homes and habits. Users should check privacy settings, know when devices are listening or recording and be careful about cameras in private spaces."],["Smart devices in the Philippines","Smart home devices, such as Wi-Fi plugs, cameras and smart TVs, are increasingly common in Filipino homes, along with community uses such as CCTV networks in barangays and sensors that warn of rising river levels during typhoons."],["Electronic waste","As smart devices multiply, they add to electronic waste, especially when they stop receiving software updates or their companies shut down services. Repairable designs and longer support periods can help."]],
sp:["The coffee pot that made history","In 1991, researchers at the University of Cambridge in England grew tired of walking to the coffee pot in the Trojan Room only to find it empty. They pointed a camera at the pot and wrote software so they could see a small picture of it on their computer screens. In 1993 the image was put on the World Wide Web, making it one of the first webcams. People around the world logged on just to watch a coffee pot. When it was finally switched off in 2001, the pot was sold at auction for thousands of pounds."],
g:[["Internet of Things","Everyday objects connected to the internet"],["Smart home","A home with devices controlled remotely or automatically"],["Sensor","A device that detects changes such as heat, light or motion"],["Voice assistant","Software that responds to spoken commands"]],
f:["The term Internet of Things was coined in 1999.","A Cambridge coffee pot was one of the first webcams.","Weak passwords make smart devices vulnerable to hackers."],
fun:"One of the first webcams showed a university coffee pot.",
tl:"Ang Internet of Things ay ang pagkonekta ng mga pang-araw-araw na bagay sa internet, gaya ng ilaw, kamera at appliances. Sa smart home, nakokontrol ang mga ito gamit ang cellphone o boses. May mga alalahanin sa privacy at seguridad."});

ADDART({id:"housing",t:"Housing and Homes",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🏘️",w:"Affordable housing",r:["urban","megacities","inequality","costliving","architecture"],
p:[["A place to live","Everyone needs a safe, decent and affordable home."],["A global challenge","Billions of people live in inadequate or unaffordable housing."],["Different solutions","Countries try public housing, new building, subsidies and slum upgrading."]],
l:"Housing is a basic human need and is recognized as a human right in the Universal Declaration of Human Rights. Yet around the world, billions of people live in housing that is crowded, unsafe, insecure or too expensive, and around a billion live in slums or informal settlements. In many cities, house prices and rents have risen much faster than incomes, making homes unaffordable for young people and low-income families. Governments, builders, communities and charities try different solutions, from public housing and subsidies to slum upgrading and community-built homes, and the best approach is widely debated.",
s:[["What makes housing adequate","According to the United Nations, adequate housing means more than four walls and a roof: it includes security from eviction, access to water, sanitation and electricity, affordability, safety, enough space, access to jobs and schools and respect for culture."],["Why housing is expensive","House prices and rents rise when demand grows faster than supply, as when cities attract many workers. Land costs, building rules, slow approvals, interest rates, speculation and investors buying homes can all push prices up. Economists debate which factors matter most in each place."],["Informal settlements","In many fast-growing cities, people who cannot afford formal housing build homes on unused land, along rivers, railways or hillsides, often without secure ownership or services. These communities are vulnerable to floods, fires and evictions, but they also show great resourcefulness."],["Homelessness","Homelessness affects people in rich and poor countries alike. Causes include poverty, high rents, unemployment, family breakdown, disasters and mental illness. Approaches such as Housing First, which gives homeless people a stable home before addressing other problems, have shown success."]],
k:[["Human right","Recognized in the Universal Declaration of Human Rights"],["People in slums","Around 1 billion"],["Singapore public housing","About 80% of residents"],["Philippine housing program","Pambansang Pabahay para sa Pilipino (2023)"],["World Habitat Day","First Monday of October"]],
d:[["1948","The Universal Declaration of Human Rights recognizes the right to housing"],["1960","Singapore creates its Housing and Development Board"],["1976","Habitat for Humanity is founded"],["2003","Gawad Kalinga begins building communities in the Philippines"],["2016","The UN adopts the New Urban Agenda"],["2023","The Philippines launches a national housing program"]],
s2:[["Housing in the Philippines","The Philippines has a large housing backlog, estimated in the millions of units, and many families live in informal settlements, especially in Metro Manila. Government agencies, the Pag-IBIG Fund and private developers build housing, and the Pambansang Pabahay para sa Pilipino program, launched in 2023, aims to build large numbers of affordable homes. Critics have raised concerns about affordability and location."],["Singapore’s public housing","Since 1960, Singapore’s Housing and Development Board has built high-rise public housing estates where about 80 percent of residents live, most of them owning their flats on long leases. The system is admired for providing affordable, well-planned homes, though it relies on unique conditions such as state ownership of most land."],["Vienna’s social housing","In Vienna, Austria, a large share of residents live in social or city-owned housing, built since the 1920s, with good design and affordable rents, mixing people of different incomes. Many cities study Vienna’s model."],["Community-built homes","Organizations such as Habitat for Humanity, founded in 1976, and Gawad Kalinga in the Philippines, founded in 2003, help families build homes with volunteers, often requiring families to contribute their own labor, known as sweat equity, reflecting the Filipino tradition of bayanihan."],["Debates","Some argue that the main solution is to build many more homes by loosening building rules. Others emphasize rent controls, public and social housing, taxes on vacant homes and limits on speculation. Many experts suggest combining several approaches suited to local conditions."],["Building better","Homes in disaster-prone places, like the Philippines, need to withstand typhoons, floods and earthquakes. Architects are developing affordable, climate-resilient and energy-efficient designs, including homes built with bamboo and other local materials."]],
sp:["Bayanihan: moving a house together","In the traditional Filipino custom of bayanihan, when a family in a village needed to move, neighbors would gather to lift their entire nipa hut on bamboo poles and carry it on their shoulders to its new location, sometimes dozens of men walking together. In return, the family would prepare a meal for everyone. The image of villagers carrying a house became a symbol of community spirit in the Philippines, celebrated in art and the famous painting by Carlos Botong Francisco. Today the word bayanihan is used for any act of communities working together, including building homes for those in need."],
g:[["Adequate housing","Safe, secure, affordable housing with basic services"],["Informal settlement","An area of unplanned housing without secure ownership"],["Public housing","Homes built or owned by the government"],["Bayanihan","The Filipino spirit of community cooperation"]],
f:["Housing is recognized as a human right.","About 80 percent of Singapore residents live in public housing.","Bayanihan once meant carrying a neighbor’s house together."],
fun:"In traditional bayanihan, neighbors carried a whole nipa hut on their shoulders.",
tl:"Ang pabahay ay pangunahing pangangailangan at karapatang pantao. Bilyun-bilyong tao ang nakatira sa hindi sapat o mahal na tirahan. Sa Pilipinas, malaki ang kakulangan sa pabahay, at simbolo ng pagtutulungan ang bayanihan."});

ADDART({id:"loneliness",t:"Loneliness and Social Connection",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🤝",w:"Loneliness",r:["mental","family","agingworld","mentalyouth","socialmedia"],
p:[["Feeling alone","Loneliness is the painful feeling of having fewer close connections than you want."],["A public health issue","Health organizations now treat loneliness as a serious health concern."],["Connection helps","Friendships, family and community protect health and happiness."]],
l:"Loneliness is the distressing feeling that one’s social relationships are fewer or less close than one would like. It is different from being alone; people can feel lonely in a crowd or content in solitude. Research shows that long-lasting loneliness and social isolation are linked to higher risks of heart disease, stroke, depression, dementia and early death. In 2025 a World Health Organization commission estimated that about one in six people worldwide is affected by loneliness. Governments, health organizations and communities increasingly treat social connection as a public health priority.",
s:[["Loneliness and isolation","Social isolation means having few social contacts, while loneliness is the feeling of lacking connection. Both can harm health. Older people living alone, young people, people with disabilities, migrants and those who are poor or face discrimination may be especially at risk."],["Effects on health","Studies link chronic loneliness to higher blood pressure, weaker immunity, poorer sleep and greater risk of heart disease, depression and anxiety. Some researchers have compared the health risks of severe social disconnection with those of smoking."],["Causes","Loneliness can be caused by moving away, losing a loved one, illness, unemployment, long working hours, living alone, bullying or difficulties making friends. Changes in society, such as smaller families, urban living and less participation in community groups, may also contribute."],["Young people","Surprisingly, surveys in many countries find that young adults report loneliness as often as or more often than older people. The roles of social media and screens are debated: they can help people stay connected but may also replace face-to-face time."]],
k:[["People affected by loneliness","About 1 in 6 worldwide (WHO, 2025)"],["UK minister for loneliness","Appointed 2018"],["Japan minister for loneliness","Appointed 2021"],["WHO commission on social connection","Launched 2023"],["Best remedy","Meaningful relationships and community"]],
d:[["1938","A long-running Harvard study of adult life begins"],["2018","The United Kingdom appoints a minister for loneliness"],["2021","Japan appoints a minister for loneliness"],["2023","The WHO launches a commission on social connection"],["2023","US health officials call loneliness an epidemic"],["2025","A WHO report estimates loneliness affects one in six people"]],
s2:[["What helps","Ways to build connection include reaching out to friends and family regularly, joining clubs, sports teams, religious groups or volunteer activities, talking to neighbors, sharing meals and helping others. Even small interactions, such as greeting a shop owner, can lift mood."],["Communities in the Philippines","Filipino culture values close family ties, barkada friendships, fiestas and bayanihan, which can protect against loneliness. But many Filipinos experience long separations from loved ones, such as families of overseas workers. Video calls, community groups and shared celebrations help families stay close across distances."],["The Harvard study","Since 1938, a Harvard University study has followed hundreds of men, and later their families, throughout their lives. One of its main findings is that close, warm relationships are a strong predictor of long, healthy and happy lives, more than wealth or fame."],["Governments act","In 2018 the United Kingdom appointed a minister for loneliness, and Japan did the same in 2021. Some doctors now practice social prescribing, referring lonely patients to community activities such as gardening clubs, art classes or walking groups."],["Older people","Older people may become isolated after retirement, the death of a partner or loss of mobility. Programs that bring generations together, such as students visiting elderly neighbors or shared community centers, benefit both young and old."],["Being alone is not always lonely","Time alone can be restful and creative, and some people enjoy solitude. Loneliness becomes a problem when people feel unwanted, misunderstood or disconnected for long periods. Anyone who feels persistently lonely or low can talk to someone they trust or seek help from a health professional."]],
sp:["Chatty benches","In 2019, police in the town of Burnham-on-Sea in England put up signs on park benches reading: Happy to chat bench. Sit here if you don’t mind someone stopping to say hello. The idea was simple: anyone who sat on the bench was signaling that they would welcome a conversation with a stranger. Chat benches spread to many towns in the United Kingdom and other countries, from Australia to Canada, helping lonely people, especially older people, find someone to talk to. Some cafés followed with chatter tables where customers could join others for conversation."],
g:[["Loneliness","The painful feeling of lacking close connections"],["Social isolation","Having few social contacts"],["Social prescribing","Doctors referring patients to community activities"],["Barkada","A Filipino word for a close group of friends"]],
f:["About one in six people worldwide is affected by loneliness.","The UK appointed a minister for loneliness in 2018.","Close relationships predict long, healthy lives."],
fun:"Some towns have chat benches where sitting down means you are happy to talk.",
tl:"Ang kalungkutan o loneliness ay ang pakiramdam na kulang ang malapit na ugnayan. Maaari itong makasama sa kalusugan. Nakatutulong ang pamilya, barkada, komunidad at pakikipag-usap sa iba."});

ADDART({id:"urbanfarm",t:"Urban Farming",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🥬",w:"Urban agriculture",r:["agri","foodsecurity","urban","megacities","recycling"],
p:[["Farms in the city","Urban farming means growing food in and around cities."],["Rooftops and walls","Food grows on rooftops, balconies, vacant lots and even indoors."],["Fresh and local","City farms provide fresh food, green space and community."]],
l:"Urban farming, or urban agriculture, is the growing of food, and sometimes raising of animals, in and around cities, on rooftops, balconies, backyards, vacant lots, schoolyards, walls and even indoors. It ranges from family vegetable gardens and community gardens to high-tech vertical farms. Urban farming can provide fresh, nutritious food, income, green spaces, cooler neighborhoods and stronger communities, and it can make cities more resilient to food price shocks and disasters. In the Philippines, urban gardening grew rapidly during the COVID-19 pandemic. Challenges include limited space, soil contamination and costs.",
s:[["An old idea","People have always grown food near their homes. The Aztec capital Tenochtitlan fed its people with chinampas, floating gardens. During the World Wars, millions of people in Britain, the United States and elsewhere planted victory gardens in backyards and parks to grow food."],["Kinds of urban farms","Urban farms include home gardens, community gardens shared by neighbors, school gardens, rooftop farms, container gardens on balconies and indoor farms. Some raise chickens, fish, bees or mushrooms."],["Growing without soil","Hydroponics grows plants in nutrient-rich water instead of soil, and aquaponics combines fish tanks with plant beds, with fish waste fertilizing the plants. Vertical farms grow crops in stacked layers indoors under LED lights, using little water and space."],["Benefits","Urban farms provide fresh vegetables close to where people live, reduce transport, reuse food waste as compost, cool the air, absorb rainwater and create green spaces where neighbors meet. Gardening is also good exercise and can reduce stress."]],
k:[["Also called","Urban agriculture"],["Where food grows","Rooftops, balconies, vacant lots, indoors"],["Soil-free methods","Hydroponics, aquaponics"],["Historic example","Victory gardens in the World Wars"],["Philippine program","Plant, Plant, Plant (2020)"]],
d:[["1300s","The Aztecs farm chinampas in Tenochtitlan"],["1940s","Victory gardens spread during World War II"],["1990s","Havana turns to urban organic farms during an economic crisis"],["2010","Quezon City launches an urban farming program"],["2019","Singapore sets a goal to grow more of its own food"],["2020","The Philippines promotes home gardening during the pandemic"]],
s2:[["Havana’s organopónicos","When the Soviet Union collapsed in 1991, Cuba lost much of its imported food, fuel and fertilizer, causing severe shortages. In response, people in Havana turned vacant lots and rooftops into organic gardens called organopónicos, which produced large amounts of vegetables for the city. Havana became a famous example of urban farming."],["Urban gardening in the Philippines","During the COVID-19 pandemic, many Filipinos started growing vegetables at home in pots, recycled containers and small plots. The Department of Agriculture’s Plant, Plant, Plant program distributed seeds and seedlings, and local governments, such as Quezon City with its Joy of Urban Farming program, supported community gardens. Popular crops include pechay, kangkong, tomatoes, eggplant and okra."],["Singapore","Singapore, a small island city-state that imports most of its food, set a goal in 2019 to produce much more of its own food, encouraging rooftop farms, vertical farms and fish farms. Progress proved harder than expected, and the targets were later revised."],["Challenges","City soil may be contaminated by lead or other pollutants, so gardeners often test soil or use raised beds and containers. Space, water, sunlight, time and the cost of high-tech systems can also be challenges. Urban farms can supplement but not replace rural farming."],["Schools and gardens","School gardens teach children where food comes from, how plants grow and how to eat healthily. In the Philippines, the Gulayan sa Paaralan program encourages schools to grow vegetables, which can be used in school feeding programs."],["Start your own","Anyone can start small: herbs such as basil or tanglad in a pot, kangkong in a bucket of water, or vegetables in recycled plastic bottles. Compost from kitchen scraps can enrich the soil, turning waste into food."]],
sp:["Kangkong in a bottle","One of the easiest vegetables to grow in a small space is kangkong, water spinach, a favorite in Filipino dishes such as adobong kangkong and sinigang. Gardeners can take kangkong stems bought at the market, remove the leaves for cooking and place the stems in a container of water or moist soil near a sunny window. Within days, roots appear and new leaves sprout, ready to harvest within weeks. Many Filipino families grow kangkong in cut plastic bottles hung on walls or fences, turning trash into a small garden."],
g:[["Urban agriculture","Growing food in and around cities"],["Hydroponics","Growing plants in water with nutrients instead of soil"],["Vertical farm","An indoor farm with crops grown in stacked layers"],["Compost","Decayed organic matter used to enrich soil"]],
f:["Victory gardens fed people during the World Wars.","Hydroponics grows plants without soil.","Many Filipinos started home gardens during the pandemic."],
fun:"Kangkong can regrow from market stems placed in a bottle of water.",
tl:"Ang urban farming ay pagtatanim ng pagkain sa lungsod, gaya ng sa bubong, balkonahe at bakanteng lote. Lumaganap ito sa Pilipinas noong pandemya. Madaling magtanim ng kangkong, pechay at kamatis sa maliit na espasyo."});

;
/* data/lang2.js */
// Languages, part 2: Japanese, Korean, Hindi, French, Swahili and Malay–Indonesian.
ADDART({id:"japanese",t:"Japanese",c:"Languages",i:"🗾",w:"Japanese language",r:["japancountry","murasaki","haikuart","anime","mandarin"],
p:[["Three scripts","Japanese is written with three scripts used together: hiragana, katakana and kanji."],["Polite speech","Japanese has special forms for showing respect."],["Words for the world","Words such as tsunami, karaoke and emoji come from Japanese."]],
l:"Japanese is the language of Japan, spoken by about 125 million people, almost all in Japan, with communities abroad in places such as Brazil, the United States, Peru and the Philippines. It is unusual for using three scripts together: kanji, characters borrowed from Chinese that represent meanings, and two syllabic scripts, hiragana and katakana, that represent sounds. Japanese has a rich system of polite and honorific language and has given the world words such as tsunami, karaoke, sushi, anime and emoji. Its literature includes The Tale of Genji, often called the world’s first novel, and haiku poetry.",
s:[["Where it comes from","Japanese belongs to the small Japonic family, which also includes the Ryukyuan languages of Okinawa. Its origins are debated. Over centuries it borrowed many words from Chinese, much as English borrowed from Latin and French."],["Kanji","Kanji are characters adopted from Chinese writing, beginning around the 400s CE. Each represents a meaning and can have more than one pronunciation. Japanese students learn about 2,000 commonly used kanji by the end of high school."],["Hiragana and katakana","Hiragana and katakana each have about 46 basic characters, each standing for a syllable, such as ka, ki or ku. Hiragana is used for grammar endings and native words, while katakana is used mainly for foreign words, names and emphasis. Japanese can also be written in the Latin alphabet, called romaji."],["Grammar","Japanese usually places the verb at the end of a sentence, as in I sushi eat. It uses small words called particles to show the role of each word, has no plural endings for most nouns and often leaves out the subject when it is clear from context."]],
k:[["Speakers","About 125 million"],["Scripts","Kanji, hiragana, katakana"],["Family","Japonic"],["Oldest surviving book","Kojiki (712)"],["Words in English","Tsunami, karaoke, emoji, sushi"]],
d:[["400s","Chinese characters reach Japan"],["712","The Kojiki, Japan’s oldest surviving book, is completed"],["c. 900","Hiragana and katakana develop"],["c. 1010","Murasaki Shikibu writes The Tale of Genji"],["1543","Portuguese traders arrive, bringing new words"],["1946","Japan simplifies kanji and publishes an official list"]],
s2:[["Politeness","Japanese has several levels of polite speech, called keigo, used depending on who is speaking to whom. People use humble forms for themselves and respectful forms for others, such as customers, teachers and elders. Even simple words, such as eat or go, change in polite speech."],["Borrowed words","Japanese has borrowed many words from other languages. Pan, meaning bread, came from Portuguese in the 1500s. Today many words come from English, adapted to Japanese sounds, such as konpyūtā for computer and terebi for television."],["Japanese words in English","English has borrowed Japanese words such as tsunami, karaoke, sushi, origami, anime, manga, futon, tycoon and emoji. Emoji comes from e, meaning picture, and moji, meaning character, and is not related to the English word emotion."],["Japanese in the Philippines","Before World War II, a large Japanese community lived in Davao, working on abacá plantations. Many Filipinos study Japanese today for work, study and travel, and Japanese is one of the foreign languages offered in some Philippine high schools. Anime and manga have also inspired many Filipino learners."],["Writing vertically","Japanese can be written horizontally, left to right, or vertically, from top to bottom and right to left. Newspapers, novels and manga often use vertical writing, while websites and science books usually use horizontal writing."],["Learning Japanese","Japanese pronunciation is fairly simple, with few vowel sounds, and is relatively easy for Filipino speakers. The writing system, especially kanji, takes longer to learn. Many learners take the Japanese-Language Proficiency Test, offered around the world."]],
sp:["Emoji, a Japanese word","In 1999, Shigetaka Kurita, working for the Japanese mobile company NTT Docomo, designed a set of 176 tiny pictures, each just 12 by 12 pixels, so that phone users could express emotions and ideas quickly in short messages: hearts, faces, weather symbols and more. They were called emoji, from the Japanese words for picture and character. After emoji were added to international computer standards and smartphones in the 2010s, they spread worldwide. Kurita’s original set is now in the collection of the Museum of Modern Art in New York."],
g:[["Kanji","Chinese characters used in Japanese"],["Hiragana","A Japanese script for native words and grammar"],["Katakana","A Japanese script mainly for foreign words"],["Keigo","Polite and honorific Japanese"]],
f:["Japanese uses three scripts together.","The Kojiki of 712 is Japan’s oldest surviving book.","The word emoji comes from Japanese."],
fun:"The first emoji were just 12 by 12 pixels.",
tl:"Ang wikang Hapon ay sinasalita ng mga 125 milyong tao. Gumagamit ito ng tatlong sistema ng pagsulat: kanji, hiragana at katakana. Galing sa wikang Hapon ang mga salitang tsunami, karaoke at emoji."});

ADDART({id:"korean",t:"Korean",c:"Languages",i:"📝",w:"Korean language",r:["sejong","southkoreacountry","kpop","kdrama","alphabet"],
p:[["A scientific alphabet","Korean is written in Hangul, an alphabet invented in the 1400s."],["Two Koreas","Korean is spoken in South Korea, North Korea and by Koreans worldwide."],["Popular to learn","K-pop and K-dramas have made Korean popular with learners around the world."]],
l:"Korean is the language of South Korea and North Korea, spoken by about 80 million people, including Korean communities in China, the United States, Japan and elsewhere. It is written in Hangul, an alphabet created in 1443 under King Sejong the Great and admired by linguists for its logical design, in which letter shapes reflect how sounds are made. Korean has a complex system of speech levels for showing respect. The global popularity of K-pop and Korean dramas has made it one of the fastest-growing languages for learners, including in the Philippines.",
s:[["Where it comes from","Korean is usually considered a language isolate or part of the small Koreanic family, with no proven relatives, though some scholars have suggested links to other Asian languages. Over many centuries, it borrowed a large number of words from Chinese."],["Before Hangul","For centuries, Koreans wrote using Chinese characters, called hanja, which were difficult to learn and did not fit the Korean language well. Only the educated elite could read and write."],["The creation of Hangul","In 1443 King Sejong and his scholars created a new alphabet, announced in 1446 in a document called Hunminjeongeum, the correct sounds for the instruction of the people. Sejong wanted ordinary people to be able to read and write. Some scholars opposed it, and it took centuries to replace Chinese characters fully."],["How Hangul works","Hangul has 24 basic letters, 14 consonants and 10 vowels. Letters are grouped into square syllable blocks, so a word looks somewhat like Chinese characters but is made of alphabet letters. The shapes of consonants imitate the position of the mouth and tongue when making the sound."]],
k:[["Speakers","About 80 million"],["Alphabet","Hangul (created 1443)"],["Creator","King Sejong the Great"],["Basic letters","24"],["Hangul Day","9 October (South Korea)"]],
d:[["1443","King Sejong creates Hangul"],["1446","Hangul is officially announced"],["1504","A king bans the use of Hangul"],["1894","Hangul becomes official in government documents"],["1945","Korea is divided into North and South"],["2017","Korean is added to foreign language classes in Philippine public schools"]],
s2:[["Speech levels","Korean has several speech levels that change verb endings depending on the relationship between speakers, such as age and social position. Speaking to elders, teachers or strangers uses polite forms, while close friends may use casual speech. Learning when to use each level is an important part of Korean culture."],["North and South","After Korea was divided in 1945, the Korean spoken in North and South Korea gradually developed differences in vocabulary, spelling and pronunciation. South Korean Korean has borrowed many English words, while North Korea has tried to replace foreign words with native Korean ones."],["Hangul Day","South Korea celebrates Hangul Day on 9 October as a national holiday, and North Korea marks its own Korean Alphabet Day in January. Hangul is a source of great national pride."],["Korean in the Philippines","Interest in Korean has grown rapidly in the Philippines, thanks to K-pop, K-dramas and work and study opportunities. In 2017 Korean became one of the foreign languages offered in Philippine public high schools, and many Filipinos learn it online and in language centers."],["Korean words","Words now known around the world include kimchi, hallyu, taekwondo, chaebol and oppa, a word used by younger women for an older brother or male friend, popularized by K-pop and dramas."],["Easy to read","Because Hangul is so systematic, many learners can learn to read it in a few hours or days, though understanding the language takes much longer. King Sejong is said to have written that a wise person can learn it in a morning."]],
sp:["A wise person can learn it in a morning","When King Sejong announced his new alphabet in 1446, an accompanying text praised its simplicity, saying that a wise person could learn it in a morning, and even a less clever person within ten days. Sejong designed it so that farmers, women and servants, who had no time to master thousands of Chinese characters, could read and write and make their voices heard. Today South Korea has one of the highest literacy rates in the world, and UNESCO named its literacy prize after King Sejong."],
g:[["Hangul","The Korean alphabet"],["Hanja","Chinese characters used in Korean"],["Syllable block","A group of Hangul letters forming one syllable"],["Speech level","A form of speech showing respect or familiarity"]],
f:["Hangul was created in 1443 under King Sejong.","Hangul has 24 basic letters.","Hangul Day is 9 October in South Korea."],
fun:"Hangul’s creators said a wise person could learn it in a morning.",
tl:"Ang wikang Koreano ay sinasalita ng mga 80 milyong tao. Isinusulat ito sa Hangul, alpabetong nilikha noong 1443 sa ilalim ni Haring Sejong. Dumami ang nag-aaral nito sa Pilipinas dahil sa K-pop at K-drama."});

ADDART({id:"hindi",t:"Hindi",c:"Languages",i:"🕉️",w:"Hindi",r:["indiacountry","bollywood","indianphil","hindu","alphabet"],
p:[["One of the biggest languages","Hindi is one of the most spoken languages in the world."],["Devanagari script","It is written in Devanagari, with a line along the top of the letters."],["Words we share","English words such as shampoo and pajamas come from Hindi."]],
l:"Hindi is an Indo-Aryan language spoken mainly in northern and central India, where it is one of the two official languages of the national government, alongside English. With hundreds of millions of native speakers and many more who speak it as a second language, Hindi is among the most spoken languages in the world. It is written in the Devanagari script and is closely related to Urdu; together they are sometimes called Hindustani. Hindi is the language of most Bollywood films and songs, and it has given English words such as shampoo, jungle, bungalow and pajamas.",
s:[["Where it comes from","Hindi descends from Sanskrit, the ancient classical language of India, through medieval languages called Prakrits and Apabhramsha. It belongs to the Indo-European family, making it a distant relative of English, Persian, Spanish and Russian."],["Devanagari","Hindi is written left to right in Devanagari, an abugida in which each consonant carries a built-in vowel that can be changed with marks. Most letters hang from a horizontal line called the shirorekha. Devanagari is also used for Sanskrit, Marathi and Nepali."],["Hindi and Urdu","Everyday spoken Hindi and Urdu are very similar and largely understandable to each other. They differ in script, Urdu using a Persian-Arabic script, and in formal vocabulary, with Hindi drawing on Sanskrit and Urdu on Persian and Arabic. Urdu is the national language of Pakistan."],["Official language","India’s constitution, adopted in 1950, made Hindi in Devanagari the official language of the central government, with English also used. India recognizes 22 scheduled languages, and many Indians speak their regional language, Hindi and English."]],
k:[["Speakers","Hundreds of millions (one of the most spoken languages)"],["Script","Devanagari"],["Family","Indo-Aryan (Indo-European)"],["Close relative","Urdu"],["Hindi Day","14 September"]],
d:[["c. 1000","Early forms of Hindi emerge"],["1500s","Poets such as Kabir and Tulsidas write in early Hindi"],["1949","India’s Constituent Assembly adopts Hindi as an official language"],["1950","India’s constitution takes effect"],["1975","The first World Hindi Conference is held"],["2006","World Hindi Day is first celebrated on 10 January"]],
s2:[["Hindi in film and music","Most Bollywood films are in Hindi, often mixed with Urdu and English, and their songs are loved across South Asia, the Middle East, Africa and beyond. Films have helped spread Hindi to people whose first language is different."],["Words from Hindi in English","English borrowed many words from Hindi and related languages during British rule in India, including shampoo, from champo, to press or massage, jungle, bungalow, pajamas, loot, thug, cheetah, bandana and guru."],["Sanskrit words in Filipino","Long before the Spanish arrived, Sanskrit words reached the Philippines through trade with Malay and Indianized kingdoms of Southeast Asia. Filipino words such as guro, teacher, from guru, diwa, spirit, mukha, face, and budhi, conscience, share roots with Sanskrit words that are also found in Hindi."],["Languages of India","India has hundreds of languages from several families, including Indo-Aryan languages such as Hindi, Bengali and Marathi, and Dravidian languages such as Tamil, Telugu and Kannada. Debates over whether Hindi should be promoted as a national language have sometimes caused tension in non-Hindi-speaking states, especially in the south."],["Hindi around the world","Hindi and related languages are spoken by communities of Indian origin in Fiji, Mauritius, Suriname, Trinidad and Tobago, the Gulf countries, the United Kingdom and the United States, as well as by Indian communities in the Philippines."],["Greetings","Namaste, said with palms pressed together and a slight bow, is a common Hindi greeting meaning I bow to you. It has become widely known around the world, especially through yoga."]],
sp:["Shampoo, a gift from India","The word shampoo comes from the Hindi word champo, meaning to press or massage, related to a word for kneading the body. In the 1700s, British travelers in India encountered head massages with oils and herbs. In 1814 Sake Dean Mahomed, a businessman from India, opened a bathhouse in Brighton, England, offering shampooing, a therapeutic massage with steam and oils. It became fashionable, and he was even appointed shampooing surgeon to King George IV. Only later did the word shampoo come to mean washing hair with soap."],
g:[["Devanagari","The script used to write Hindi"],["Abugida","A writing system where consonants carry built-in vowels"],["Sanskrit","The ancient classical language of India"],["Hindustani","The shared everyday language of Hindi and Urdu"]],
f:["Hindi is written in Devanagari.","Hindi and Urdu are closely related.","The word shampoo comes from Hindi."],
fun:"An Indian businessman was once appointed shampooing surgeon to a British king.",
tl:"Ang Hindi ay isa sa pinakamalaganap na wika sa mundo at opisyal na wika ng India. Isinusulat ito sa Devanagari. May mga salitang Filipino na galing sa Sanskrit, kaugnay ng Hindi, gaya ng guro at diwa."});

ADDART({id:"french",t:"French",c:"Languages",i:"🥐",w:"French language",r:["francecountry","latin","english","spanish","africacolon"],
p:[["A world language","French is spoken on five continents."],["From Latin","French is a Romance language that grew from Latin."],["Africa’s French","Most French speakers today live in Africa."]],
l:"French is a Romance language that developed from the Latin spoken in Roman Gaul, now France. Today more than 300 million people speak it, and it is an official language in more than two dozen countries in Europe, Africa, the Americas and the Pacific, including France, Belgium, Switzerland, Canada, Haiti and many African countries. Most French speakers now live in Africa. French was long the language of diplomacy and remains an official language of the United Nations, the Olympic Games and many international organizations. It has strongly influenced English, which borrowed thousands of French words.",
s:[["From Latin to French","When the Roman Empire ruled Gaul, people adopted Latin. After Rome fell, the Latin spoken there changed over centuries, influenced by the Germanic Franks, who gave France its name. The Strasbourg Oaths of 842 are one of the earliest texts in an early form of French."],["Standard French","In 1539 King Francis I ordered that official documents be written in French instead of Latin. In 1635 Cardinal Richelieu founded the Académie française to protect and standardize the language, a role it still plays, issuing a dictionary and guidance."],["French around the world","Through colonization, French spread to North America, the Caribbean, Africa, the Middle East, Southeast Asia and the Pacific. After independence, many countries kept French as an official language, alongside local languages."],["Sounds and spelling","French is known for nasal vowels, a throaty r and silent letters at the ends of words. Its spelling is complex, with many letters not pronounced. It has accents, such as é and è, and nouns are either masculine or feminine."]],
k:[["Speakers","More than 300 million"],["Family","Romance (from Latin)"],["Most speakers live in","Africa"],["Académie française founded","1635"],["International Francophonie Day","20 March"]],
d:[["842","The Strasbourg Oaths are written in early French"],["1066","The Norman Conquest brings French to England"],["1539","French replaces Latin in official documents in France"],["1635","The Académie française is founded"],["1970","The organization of French-speaking countries is founded"],["2020s","Africa is home to most of the world’s French speakers"]],
s2:[["French in English","After the Norman Conquest of England in 1066, French became the language of the English court and law for centuries. English absorbed thousands of French words, such as government, justice, beef, pork, restaurant, fashion and ballet. By some estimates, a large share of English vocabulary comes from French or Latin through French."],["Africa","Countries such as the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Côte d’Ivoire, Cameroon, Senegal, Madagascar and Mali use French in government and schools, and Kinshasa is often described as the largest French-speaking city in the world. African French includes many local words and expressions."],["Canada","In Canada, French and English are both official languages. The province of Quebec, where most people speak French, has laws to protect the language, and Canadian French has its own accent and vocabulary."],["La Francophonie","The International Organisation of La Francophonie, founded in 1970, brings together countries and governments that share the French language, promoting culture, education and cooperation. International Francophonie Day is celebrated on 20 March."],["Language of diplomacy","From the 1600s to the early 1900s, French was the main language of European diplomacy and of educated elites. It remains an official language of the United Nations, the European Union, the African Union and the Olympic Games, where announcements are made in French first."],["French and the Philippines","French is one of the foreign languages taught in some Philippine schools and universities, and Filipino workers in Canada, Africa and Europe often learn it. A few Filipino words come from French through Spanish, and French cuisine has influenced Filipino baking."]],
sp:["Beef from the French, cows from the English","After the Normans conquered England in 1066, French-speaking nobles ruled over English-speaking peasants. This left a curious trace in English. The farmers who raised the animals used old English words: cow, pig, sheep and deer. But the nobles who ate the meat used French words: boeuf became beef, porc became pork, mouton became mutton and venaison became venison. Today English speakers still use English words for the animals in the field and French-derived words for the meat on the plate."],
g:[["Romance language","A language that developed from Latin"],["Francophone","French-speaking"],["Nasal vowel","A vowel pronounced with air passing through the nose"],["Académie française","The institution that guards the French language"]],
f:["Most French speakers live in Africa.","The Académie française was founded in 1635.","English borrowed thousands of French words."],
fun:"English uses English words for farm animals but French words for their meat.",
tl:"Ang Pranses ay wikang Romance na nagmula sa Latin. Mahigit 300 milyong tao ang nagsasalita nito sa limang kontinente, at karamihan ay nasa Aprika. Maraming salitang Ingles ang galing sa Pranses."});

ADDART({id:"swahili",t:"Swahili",c:"Languages",i:"🌍",w:"Swahili language",r:["tanzaniacountry","kenyacountry","ugandacountry","africacolon","arabic"],
p:[["East Africa’s language","Swahili is spoken by tens of millions of people across East and Central Africa."],["A language of trade","It grew on the coast through trade between Africans, Arabs and others."],["Hakuna matata","The phrase hakuna matata, meaning no worries, is Swahili."]],
l:"Swahili, known to its speakers as Kiswahili, is a Bantu language that is the most widely spoken African language, used by an estimated 200 million people as a first or second language across East and Central Africa, including Tanzania, Kenya, Uganda, Rwanda, Burundi, the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Mozambique. It developed over many centuries on the East African coast, where Bantu-speaking communities traded with Arab, Persian and Indian merchants, and it contains many Arabic loanwords. Swahili is an official language in several countries and a working language of the African Union, and words such as safari and hakuna matata are known worldwide.",
s:[["Origins","Swahili grew among the people of the East African coast, from Somalia to Mozambique, in trading towns such as Kilwa, Mombasa, Lamu and Zanzibar. Its grammar is Bantu, but it borrowed many words from Arabic, as well as from Persian, Portuguese, Hindi and English. The name comes from the Arabic word sawahil, meaning coasts."],["Spreading inland","Traders, caravans and later colonial governments and missionaries spread Swahili inland. After independence, Tanzania’s first president, Julius Nyerere, made Swahili the national language to unite more than a hundred ethnic groups, and he translated Shakespeare’s plays into Swahili."],["Writing","Swahili was first written in Arabic script; the oldest known manuscripts date to the 1700s. Since the colonial period, it has been written in the Latin alphabet. Swahili spelling is very regular, so words are pronounced as they are written."],["Grammar","Swahili nouns are grouped into many noun classes, marked by prefixes, which affect other words in the sentence. For example, mtoto means child and watoto means children. Verbs are built by adding prefixes for subject, tense and object to a root."]],
k:[["Speakers","About 200 million (first and second language)"],["Family","Bantu"],["Name means","Coasts (from Arabic)"],["Official in","Tanzania, Kenya, Uganda, Rwanda and others"],["World Kiswahili Language Day","7 July"]],
d:[["c. 800","Swahili-speaking trading towns grow on the East African coast"],["1728","The oldest known Swahili manuscript poem is written"],["1961","Tanganyika becomes independent and promotes Swahili"],["2010","Kenya’s new constitution makes Swahili an official language"],["2022","The African Union adopts Swahili as a working language"],["2022","The first World Kiswahili Language Day is celebrated"]],
s2:[["Swahili words","Common Swahili words include jambo, hello; asante, thank you; rafiki, friend; simba, lion; safari, journey; and hakuna matata, there are no worries. Safari comes from the Arabic word for travel and entered English through East Africa."],["A language of unity","In Tanzania, Swahili is used in government, primary schools, newspapers and parliament, helping create a strong national identity across many ethnic groups. Kenya adopted Swahili as an official language alongside English in its 2010 constitution."],["Swahili and the world","UNESCO declared 7 July World Kiswahili Language Day, first celebrated in 2022, the first African language to be honored with such a UN day. Swahili is taught in universities around the world and broadcast by international radio stations."],["Literature","Swahili has a rich tradition of poetry, including long epic poems called utendi, and modern novels, plays and songs. Shaaban Robert, a Tanzanian writer of the 20th century, is celebrated as a great Swahili poet and author."],["A distant cousin of Filipino?","Swahili and Filipino are not related, but both have absorbed words from trade languages, including Arabic. Interestingly, Swahili has borrowed words from many languages of the Indian Ocean world, reflecting centuries of seafaring between Africa, Arabia, India and Southeast Asia."],["Swahili in pop culture","The 1994 Disney film The Lion King made Swahili words famous, including Simba, lion, Rafiki, friend, and hakuna matata. Swahili songs by East African musicians, such as bongo flava from Tanzania, have large audiences."]],
sp:["Nyerere’s Shakespeare","Julius Nyerere, the first president of Tanzania, was a teacher before he was a politician; Tanzanians still call him Mwalimu, meaning teacher. He believed Swahili could express any idea as well as English and that it should unite his young nation. To prove it, he translated two of William Shakespeare’s plays, Julius Caesar and The Merchant of Venice, into Swahili in the 1960s. His translations showed that Swahili could carry great world literature and inspired pride in an African language as a language of learning and government."],
g:[["Kiswahili","The Swahili name for the Swahili language"],["Bantu","A large family of African languages"],["Noun class","A grammatical group of nouns marked by prefixes"],["Lingua franca","A common language used between people with different first languages"]],
f:["Swahili is the most widely spoken African language.","Its name comes from an Arabic word for coasts.","World Kiswahili Language Day is 7 July."],
fun:"Tanzania’s first president translated Shakespeare into Swahili.",
tl:"Ang Swahili o Kiswahili ay ang pinakamalaganap na wikang Aprikano, sinasalita ng humigit-kumulang 200 milyong tao sa Silangan at Gitnang Aprika. Galing dito ang mga salitang safari at hakuna matata."});

ADDART({id:"malay",t:"Malay and Indonesian",c:"Languages",i:"🌺",w:"Malay language",r:["malaysiacountry","indonesiacountry","phlang","seaempires","baybayin"],
p:[["A language of the islands","Malay is spoken across Malaysia, Indonesia, Brunei and Singapore."],["A national language","Indonesian is a standard form of Malay used by over 270 million people."],["A relative of Filipino","Malay and Filipino are related Austronesian languages with many shared words."]],
l:"Malay is an Austronesian language spoken across maritime Southeast Asia. Its standard forms include Malaysian, the national language of Malaysia, and Indonesian, the national language of Indonesia, as well as the Malay of Brunei and Singapore. Counting Indonesian, it is used by around 300 million people, making it one of the most widely spoken languages in the world, though most Indonesians speak it as a second language alongside their regional languages. For centuries Malay was the lingua franca of trade in the region, and it is closely related to Filipino, sharing many words such as mata, eye, anak, child, and langit, sky.",
s:[["Austronesian roots","Malay belongs to the Austronesian family, which spread from Taiwan through the Philippines, Indonesia and the Pacific to Madagascar. Its closest relatives are other languages of Indonesia and Malaysia, but it also shares many words with Philippine languages."],["Old Malay","The oldest Malay inscriptions date from the 600s CE in Sumatra, during the Srivijaya Empire. Malay became the language of trade in the ports of the region, used by merchants from India, China, Arabia and Europe. Ferdinand Magellan’s expedition recorded Malay words in the 1500s, and a Malay-speaking slave named Enrique served as an interpreter."],["Scripts","Malay was written in Indic scripts, then from the 1300s in Jawi, an Arabic-based script, and later in the Latin alphabet, called Rumi in Malaysia. Jawi is still used in some religious and cultural contexts in Malaysia and Brunei."],["Indonesian","At the Youth Pledge of 1928, Indonesian nationalists declared bahasa Indonesia, based on Malay, as the language of unity for a future nation of many peoples. It became the national language at independence in 1945 and is now used by nearly all Indonesians."]],
k:[["Speakers","Around 300 million (including Indonesian)"],["Family","Austronesian"],["Official in","Malaysia, Indonesia (as Indonesian), Brunei, Singapore"],["Scripts","Latin (Rumi), Jawi"],["Shared words with Filipino","Mata, anak, langit, lima"]],
d:[["683","One of the oldest Old Malay inscriptions is carved in Sumatra"],["c. 900","The Laguna Copperplate Inscription is written in a Malay-influenced language"],["1300s","Malay begins to be written in Jawi script"],["1521","Magellan’s expedition records Malay words"],["1928","The Youth Pledge declares Indonesian the language of unity"],["1945","Indonesian becomes the national language of independent Indonesia"]],
s2:[["Words shared with Filipino","Because Malay and Philippine languages are related, many basic words are similar or identical: mata, eye; anak, child; langit, sky; bulan, moon; lima, five; and ikan, fish, compared with Filipino isda. Other shared words came through trade, such as Filipino words borrowed from Malay, including some from Sanskrit and Arabic."],["The Laguna Copperplate Inscription","The oldest known written document from the Philippines, the Laguna Copperplate Inscription, dated to 900 CE, is written in a form of Old Malay mixed with Old Tagalog, Old Javanese and Sanskrit, using the Kawi script. It records the cancellation of a debt and shows the Philippines’ links with the Malay world long before the Spanish arrived."],["Malay in the Philippines","Malay was a trading language in the southern Philippines before Spanish rule, and Sulu and Maguindanao rulers corresponded in Malay written in Jawi. Today Malaysian and Indonesian are close neighbors of the Philippines, and their languages are taught in some Philippine universities."],["Malaysian and Indonesian","Malaysian and Indonesian are largely mutually understandable but differ in some vocabulary, spelling and pronunciation, partly due to British influence in Malaysia and Dutch influence in Indonesia. In 1972 the two countries agreed on a common spelling system."],["Malay words in English","English has borrowed Malay words such as amok, from amuk, gong, rattan, bamboo, orangutan, from orang hutan, meaning person of the forest, ketchup, possibly from a Chinese-Malay word, and compound."],["A simple grammar","Malay grammar is considered relatively easy to learn, with no verb conjugations for tense, no grammatical gender and plurals often formed by repeating the word, such as anak-anak for children, similar to Filipino doubling."]],
sp:["Person of the forest","The word orangutan comes from the Malay words orang, meaning person, and hutan, meaning forest: person of the forest. The great apes, found only in the rainforests of Borneo and Sumatra, earned their name from people who saw their humanlike faces, intelligence and gentle behavior. Interestingly, in Malay and Indonesian today, people usually call them orang utan, and the word orang alone means person, much as tao does in Filipino. Many other animal and plant names in English, such as cockatoo and durian, also come from Malay."],
g:[["Austronesian","A language family spread from Taiwan across the islands of Asia and the Pacific"],["Lingua franca","A shared language used for trade between peoples"],["Jawi","An Arabic-based script used to write Malay"],["Bahasa","The Malay word for language"]],
f:["Malay and Filipino are related Austronesian languages.","Indonesian is a standard form of Malay.","The word orangutan means person of the forest."],
fun:"The Philippines’ oldest known document, from 900 CE, is written partly in Old Malay.",
tl:"Ang Malay ay wikang Austronesian na sinasalita sa Malaysia, Indonesia, Brunei at Singapore. Kamag-anak ito ng Filipino at marami silang magkatulad na salita, gaya ng mata, anak at langit."});

;
/* data/science7.js */
// Science articles, part 7: great discoveries (X-rays, the double helix, insulin), the mind (dreams, habits, optical illusions) and how life works (symbiosis, metamorphosis).
ADDART({id:"xrays",t:"The Discovery of X-rays",c:"History of Science",i:"🦴",w:"X-ray",r:["medimaging","light","curie","radioact","scimeth"],
p:[["Seeing inside","X-rays let doctors see bones and other structures inside the body."],["A lucky discovery","Wilhelm Röntgen discovered X-rays by accident in 1895."],["Many uses","X-rays are used in medicine, airports, science and astronomy."]],
l:"X-rays are a form of invisible, high-energy light that can pass through soft materials such as skin and muscle but are blocked by denser materials such as bone and metal. They were discovered by accident in November 1895 by the German physicist Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen, who noticed a glowing screen near a covered tube. Within weeks, X-ray pictures of bones amazed the world, and doctors began using them to find fractures and swallowed objects. Röntgen received the first Nobel Prize in Physics in 1901. X-rays transformed medicine and science, though their dangers were learned at great cost.",
s:[["The discovery","On 8 November 1895, in his laboratory in Würzburg, Röntgen was experimenting with a cathode-ray tube covered in black cardboard. He noticed that a screen coated with a fluorescent chemical, a meter or so away, began to glow. Some unknown rays were passing through the cardboard. He called them X-rays, X standing for unknown."],["The first X-ray picture","Röntgen worked in secret for weeks. On 22 December 1895, he made an image of his wife Anna Bertha’s hand, showing her bones and wedding ring. When she saw it, she is said to have exclaimed that she had seen her own death. He published his findings at the end of December."],["A worldwide sensation","News spread rapidly in early 1896. Within months, doctors around the world were using X-rays to locate bullets, find broken bones and diagnose disease. Röntgen refused to patent his discovery, wanting it to benefit everyone."],["What X-rays are","X-rays are electromagnetic waves, like visible light and radio waves, but with much shorter wavelengths and higher energy. In 1912 scientists showed that X-rays are diffracted by crystals, proving they are waves and opening a new way to study the structure of matter."]],
k:[["Discovered by","Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen"],["Date","8 November 1895"],["First X-ray image of a hand","22 December 1895"],["Nobel Prize","First in Physics, 1901"],["What they are","High-energy electromagnetic waves"]],
d:[["1895","Röntgen discovers X-rays"],["1896","Henri Becquerel, inspired by X-rays, discovers radioactivity"],["1901","Röntgen receives the first Nobel Prize in Physics"],["1912","X-rays are shown to be diffracted by crystals"],["1971","The first CT scan of a patient is made"],["1999","The Chandra X-ray Observatory is launched"]],
s2:[["Dangers discovered","Early X-ray workers did not know the rays were harmful. Many suffered burns, hair loss and cancer. Clarence Dally, an assistant of the inventor Thomas Edison, died in 1904 after years of X-ray experiments. Scientists learned to use lead shielding and limit exposure, and modern X-rays use very low doses."],["Shoe shop X-rays","From the 1920s to the 1950s, many shoe shops had X-ray machines called fluoroscopes, so customers could see their foot bones inside new shoes. Children loved them, but the machines exposed people to unnecessary radiation and were banned once the dangers were understood."],["CT scans","In the early 1970s, the British engineer Godfrey Hounsfield built the first computed tomography, or CT, scanner, which takes many X-ray images from different angles and uses a computer to create detailed cross-sections of the body. He shared the 1979 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with Allan Cormack."],["Revealing molecules","X-ray crystallography, which shines X-rays through crystals to work out the arrangement of atoms, revealed the structures of salt, DNA, proteins and many medicines. Rosalind Franklin’s X-ray photographs were key to discovering the double helix of DNA."],["X-rays in daily life","Besides hospitals and dental clinics, X-rays are used in airport security to check bags, in factories to find hidden cracks in metal and by museums to look beneath the surfaces of paintings, revealing earlier sketches."],["X-ray astronomy","Hot objects in space, such as black holes and exploding stars, give off X-rays. Because Earth’s atmosphere blocks X-rays, telescopes such as NASA’s Chandra X-ray Observatory, launched in 1999, study them from orbit."]],
sp:["The hand with the ring","Just before Christmas in 1895, Wilhelm Röntgen asked his wife, Anna Bertha, to place her hand on a photographic plate in front of his mysterious tube. After about 15 minutes, the developed plate showed the ghostly bones of her fingers and the dark band of her wedding ring. It was the first X-ray photograph of a human body part. Röntgen sent copies to fellow scientists on 1 January 1896, and newspapers around the world printed it. The image made the invisible visible and became one of the most famous pictures in the history of science."],
g:[["X-ray","High-energy invisible light that can pass through soft tissue"],["Fluorescent","Glowing when struck by radiation"],["CT scan","A detailed X-ray image made with a computer"],["Crystallography","Studying the arrangement of atoms using X-rays"]],
f:["Röntgen discovered X-rays in 1895.","He received the first Nobel Prize in Physics.","The X in X-ray means unknown."],
fun:"The X in X-ray stands for unknown, because Röntgen did not know what the rays were.",
tl:"Ang X-ray ay di-nakikitang liwanag na tumatagos sa balat at kalamnan ngunit hinaharangan ng buto. Natuklasan ito ni Wilhelm Röntgen noong 1895. Ginagamit ito sa medisina, sa paliparan at sa agham."});

ADDART({id:"doublehelix",t:"The Discovery of the Double Helix",c:"History of Science",i:"🧬",w:"Molecular structure of Nucleic Acids",r:["dna","rfranklin","genome","xrays","mendel"],
p:[["The shape of life","In 1953 scientists discovered that DNA is shaped like a twisted ladder."],["A team effort","Watson, Crick, Franklin and Wilkins all contributed to the discovery."],["A key to biology","The double helix explained how genetic information is copied."]],
l:"The double helix is the shape of DNA, the molecule that carries genetic instructions in living things. In 1953 James Watson and Francis Crick, at the University of Cambridge, proposed that DNA consists of two strands twisted around each other like a spiral staircase, held together by pairs of chemical bases. Their model relied heavily on X-ray photographs and data from Rosalind Franklin and Maurice Wilkins at King’s College London. The discovery explained how DNA stores information and copies itself, launching modern molecular biology. Watson, Crick and Wilkins received the Nobel Prize in 1962; Franklin had died in 1958.",
s:[["Before 1953","In 1869 the Swiss chemist Friedrich Miescher discovered DNA in cell nuclei. In 1944 Oswald Avery and colleagues showed that DNA carries genetic information. Erwin Chargaff found that DNA always contains equal amounts of the bases adenine and thymine, and of guanine and cytosine. But no one knew DNA’s shape."],["Photo 51","At King’s College London, Rosalind Franklin, an expert in X-ray crystallography, and her student Raymond Gosling produced sharp X-ray images of DNA in 1952. One, called Photo 51, showed an X-shaped pattern revealing a helix. Maurice Wilkins showed it to Watson without Franklin’s knowledge."],["The model","Using Franklin’s images and data, Chargaff’s rules and cardboard and metal models, Watson and Crick built a model of DNA as a double helix, with sugar and phosphate backbones on the outside and pairs of bases, A with T and G with C, in the middle. They published it in the journal Nature on 25 April 1953, alongside papers by Wilkins’s and Franklin’s teams."],["How DNA copies itself","Watson and Crick noted that the base pairing immediately suggested how DNA could copy itself: the two strands separate, and each serves as a template for a new partner strand. This explained how genetic information passes from cell to cell and parent to child."]],
k:[["Shape","A double helix, like a twisted ladder"],["Published","25 April 1953, in Nature"],["Key scientists","Watson, Crick, Franklin, Wilkins"],["Base pairs","A with T, G with C"],["Nobel Prize","1962 (Watson, Crick, Wilkins)"]],
d:[["1869","Friedrich Miescher discovers DNA"],["1944","DNA is shown to carry genetic information"],["1952","Rosalind Franklin’s team takes Photo 51"],["1953","The double helix model is published"],["1962","Watson, Crick and Wilkins receive the Nobel Prize"],["2003","The Human Genome Project is completed"]],
s2:[["Rosalind Franklin","Rosalind Franklin’s careful X-ray work was essential to the discovery, but for many years she received little credit. She died of cancer in 1958, aged 37, and Nobel Prizes are not awarded after death. Today she is widely recognized as one of the key figures behind the double helix."],["Controversy","Watson’s 1968 memoir, The Double Helix, described the race to the discovery but portrayed Franklin unfairly, sparking criticism. Historians have since emphasized how Watson and Crick used her data without her knowledge and how she was close to solving the structure herself."],["What came next","The double helix led to discovering the genetic code, how cells make proteins and how to read and edit DNA. The Human Genome Project, completed in 2003, read the full human genetic sequence, and today DNA is used in medicine, forensics, agriculture and tracing ancestry."],["A famous announcement","According to Watson’s account, on 28 February 1953, Crick walked into the Eagle pub in Cambridge and announced that they had found the secret of life. A plaque on the pub now marks the event."],["The model’s beauty","The double helix is often called one of the most beautiful structures in science, and its spiral shape has become a symbol of biology and genetics, appearing in art, architecture and logos around the world."],["DNA in daily life","Understanding DNA has helped solve crimes, identify victims of disasters, diagnose inherited diseases, develop medicines and breed better crops. It also raises questions about privacy and the ethics of genetic testing and editing."]],
sp:["The secret of life at the Eagle pub","The Eagle, a pub near the Cavendish Laboratory in Cambridge, was where scientists often ate lunch. According to James Watson’s memoir, on 28 February 1953, after he and Francis Crick realized how the bases of DNA fit together, Crick strode in and told everyone within hearing that they had found the secret of life. Whether he said exactly those words is debated, but the story became legend. In 2003, on the 50th anniversary of the discovery, a blue plaque was unveiled at the pub, and visitors still come to see where the announcement was said to have been made."],
g:[["DNA","The molecule that carries genetic instructions"],["Double helix","Two strands twisted around each other"],["Base pair","Two chemical bases joined across the DNA ladder"],["X-ray crystallography","Using X-rays to find the arrangement of atoms"]],
f:["The double helix was published in 1953.","Rosalind Franklin took Photo 51.","A pairs with T and G with C."],
fun:"Crick is said to have announced the discovery of the secret of life in a pub.",
tl:"Ang double helix ay ang hugis ng DNA, na parang baluktot na hagdan. Inilathala ito nina Watson at Crick noong 1953 gamit ang mga larawang X-ray ni Rosalind Franklin. Ipinaliwanag nito kung paano kinokopya ang impormasyong genetiko."});

ADDART({id:"insulin",t:"The Discovery of Insulin",c:"History of Science",i:"💉",w:"History of insulin",r:["diabetes","hormone","penicil","vacc","antibiotics"],
p:[["A life-saving hormone","Insulin is a hormone that controls the amount of sugar in the blood."],["A medical miracle","Its discovery in 1921 turned a deadly disease into a manageable one."],["Sold for a dollar","Its discoverers sold the patent for just one dollar each."]],
l:"Insulin is a hormone made by the pancreas that allows the body’s cells to take in sugar from the blood for energy. Before 1922, people with type 1 diabetes, whose bodies cannot make insulin, usually died within months or a few years of diagnosis. In 1921, at the University of Toronto in Canada, Frederick Banting and Charles Best, working in the laboratory of John Macleod and later joined by the chemist James Collip, succeeded in extracting insulin. In January 1922 a 14-year-old boy, Leonard Thompson, became the first person treated with it, and his life was saved. The discovery won the Nobel Prize in 1923 and has since saved millions of lives.",
s:[["Diabetes before insulin","Diabetes had been known since ancient times; the Greek name means to pass through, referring to heavy urination, and doctors noticed the urine tasted sweet. Before insulin, the only treatment for type 1 diabetes was a near-starvation diet, which could extend life only briefly."],["The pancreas","In 1889 scientists found that removing a dog’s pancreas caused diabetes. Researchers suspected that small clusters of cells in the pancreas, the islets of Langerhans, produced a substance that controlled blood sugar, but extracting it proved very difficult."],["Toronto, 1921","Frederick Banting, a young surgeon, had an idea for isolating the substance. In the summer of 1921, with the student Charles Best, he experimented on dogs in John Macleod’s laboratory and succeeded in lowering blood sugar with extracts. James Collip then purified the extract so it could be used safely in people."],["The first patient","On 11 January 1922, Leonard Thompson, a 14-year-old boy dying of diabetes in Toronto General Hospital, received the first injection. It caused a reaction because it was impure, but a second injection of Collip’s purified insulin on 23 January dramatically lowered his blood sugar. He lived for 13 more years."]],
k:[["Made by","The pancreas"],["Discovered","1921, University of Toronto"],["Discoverers","Banting, Best, Macleod, Collip"],["First patient","Leonard Thompson (January 1922)"],["Nobel Prize","1923 (Banting and Macleod)"]],
d:[["1889","Removing the pancreas is shown to cause diabetes"],["1921","Banting and Best extract insulin in Toronto"],["1922","Leonard Thompson becomes the first patient treated with insulin"],["1923","Banting and Macleod receive the Nobel Prize"],["1955","Frederick Sanger works out the structure of insulin"],["1982","Human insulin made by genetically engineered bacteria is approved"]],
s2:[["Sharing the credit","The 1923 Nobel Prize went to Banting and Macleod. Banting was angry that Best was left out and shared his prize money with him, and Macleod shared his with Collip. The Romanian scientist Nicolae Paulescu had also extracted a pancreatic substance in 1921, and some argue his work deserved more recognition."],["A one-dollar patent","Banting, Best and Collip sold their patent rights for insulin to the University of Toronto for one dollar each, wanting it to be available to everyone. Banting reportedly said that insulin belonged to the world. Drug companies then worked with the university to mass-produce it."],["Engineered insulin","At first, insulin came from the pancreases of cows and pigs. In 1955 Frederick Sanger worked out the sequence of insulin, the first protein ever sequenced, winning a Nobel Prize. In 1978 scientists produced human insulin using genetically engineered bacteria, approved for use in 1982, one of the first products of biotechnology."],["Diabetes today","Hundreds of millions of people live with diabetes, most with type 2, which is linked to lifestyle and genes and may be treated with diet, exercise, tablets or insulin. People with type 1 diabetes need insulin every day, delivered by injections or pumps."],["Access and cost","More than a century after its discovery, insulin is still too expensive or hard to obtain for many people in low- and middle-income countries and even in some rich ones. The World Health Organization lists it as an essential medicine and campaigns for affordable access."],["World Diabetes Day","World Diabetes Day is celebrated on 14 November, the birthday of Frederick Banting, to raise awareness of diabetes and its prevention and care."]],
sp:["The ward of sleeping children","Before insulin, children with type 1 diabetes were often kept on starvation diets in hospital wards, where many fell into comas. According to a story often told about the early days of insulin, doctors went from bed to bed injecting the sleeping children with the new extract. Before they had reached the last bed, the first children were waking up from their comas, while families wept with joy. Whether every detail is exact or not, the story captures how insulin seemed like a miracle, turning a death sentence into a manageable condition."],
g:[["Insulin","A hormone that controls blood sugar"],["Pancreas","An organ that makes insulin and digestive juices"],["Diabetes","A disease in which blood sugar is too high"],["Biotechnology","Using living things, such as bacteria, to make products"]],
f:["Insulin was discovered in Toronto in 1921.","The first patient was 14-year-old Leonard Thompson.","World Diabetes Day is Banting’s birthday, 14 November."],
fun:"The discoverers of insulin sold their patent for one dollar each.",
tl:"Ang insulin ay hormone na kumokontrol sa asukal sa dugo. Natuklasan ito sa Toronto noong 1921 nina Banting at Best. Bago nito, karaniwang namamatay ang may type 1 diabetes. Nailigtas nito ang milyun-milyong buhay."});

ADDART({id:"dreams",t:"Dreams",c:"Health and the Body",i:"💭",w:"Dream",r:["sleep","brain","memory","emotions","psychology"],
p:[["Stories in sleep","Dreams are images, feelings and stories the mind experiences during sleep."],["Everyone dreams","Most people dream several times a night but forget most dreams."],["Why we dream","Scientists have several theories, but dreams are still a mystery."]],
l:"Dreams are the images, sounds, feelings and stories that people experience during sleep. Most vivid dreams happen during rapid eye movement, or REM, sleep, discovered in 1953, when the brain is very active and the eyes move quickly under closed lids. People typically have several periods of REM sleep a night and may dream for a total of about two hours, but they forget most dreams soon after waking. Scientists have proposed that dreams help process memories and emotions, rehearse responses to threats or simply result from brain activity during sleep, but why we dream is still not fully understood.",
s:[["REM sleep","In 1953 the American scientists Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman noticed that sleepers’ eyes darted around under their eyelids during certain periods, and that people woken then usually reported vivid dreams. They called this rapid eye movement sleep. During REM sleep, most muscles are temporarily paralyzed, which stops people acting out their dreams."],["What dreams are like","Dreams can be ordinary or strange, happy or frightening. They often mix recent events, worries and memories in odd ways, and the dreamer usually accepts strange events as normal. People dream in color, and many dream with all their senses."],["Why we forget","Most dreams are forgotten within minutes of waking, possibly because the brain chemistry during sleep does not favor storing new memories. Keeping a notebook by the bed and writing dreams down immediately can help people remember them."],["Nightmares","Nightmares are frightening dreams that often wake the dreamer. They are common in children and can be triggered by stress, fear, illness or frightening experiences. Talking about them, a calm bedtime routine and, when they are frequent, help from a health professional can make them less frequent."]],
k:[["Most vivid dreams","During REM sleep"],["REM sleep discovered","1953"],["Dream time per night","About two hours in total"],["Dreams remembered","Only a small fraction"],["Dreams while aware","Lucid dreams"]],
d:[["c. 2000 BCE","Ancient Mesopotamians record dreams and their meanings"],["1899","Sigmund Freud publishes The Interpretation of Dreams"],["1953","REM sleep is discovered"],["1977","A theory links dreams to random brain activity during sleep"],["2001","Studies suggest rats replay their day during sleep"],["2010s","Brain scans help scientists study dreaming"]],
s2:[["Theories of dreaming","Sigmund Freud thought dreams expressed hidden wishes. Later scientists suggested that dreams are the brain’s attempt to make sense of random signals during sleep. Others propose that dreams help sort memories, process emotions or rehearse responses to threats. Many researchers think dreaming may serve several purposes."],["Dreams and memory","Studies show that sleep helps people remember what they have learned. In experiments, rats that learned mazes showed similar patterns of brain activity during sleep, as if replaying their route. Some people dream about tasks they practiced, and their performance improves after sleep."],["Lucid dreams","In a lucid dream, the dreamer realizes they are dreaming and may even control what happens. Lucid dreaming has been confirmed in sleep laboratories, where dreamers signaled with eye movements while still asleep."],["Inspired by dreams","Some discoveries and creations have been linked to dreams. The chemist August Kekulé said the ring shape of the benzene molecule came to him in a dream of a snake biting its tail, and Paul McCartney said the melody of the song Yesterday came to him in a dream. Such stories are hard to verify but show how dreams can spark ideas."],["Dreams across cultures","Many cultures see dreams as messages, omens or visits from ancestors. In the Philippines, people traditionally interpret certain dreams as signs, and the word bangungot refers both to nightmares and to sudden unexplained death during sleep, which doctors now link to certain heart conditions."],["Animals dream too","Many mammals and birds have REM sleep and probably dream. Dogs and cats may twitch, move their paws or make sounds while asleep, as if chasing something in a dream."]],
sp:["The snake that bit its tail","In the 1860s, the German chemist August Kekulé was trying to work out the structure of benzene, a puzzling molecule with six carbon atoms. Years later, in 1890, he told a famous story: dozing by the fire, he saw atoms dancing before his eyes, forming chains that twisted like snakes. Then one snake seized its own tail, whirling in a ring. He woke with the idea that benzene’s atoms form a ring, which turned out to be correct. Some historians doubt the story, but it remains one of the most famous examples of a dream inspiring science."],
g:[["Dream","Images and stories experienced during sleep"],["REM sleep","A stage of sleep with rapid eye movements and vivid dreams"],["Lucid dream","A dream in which you know you are dreaming"],["Nightmare","A frightening dream"]],
f:["Most vivid dreams happen during REM sleep.","REM sleep was discovered in 1953.","Most dreams are forgotten within minutes."],
fun:"A chemist said the shape of a molecule came to him in a dream of a snake biting its tail.",
tl:"Ang panaginip ay mga larawan at kuwento na nararanasan habang natutulog, karaniwan sa REM sleep. Lahat ng tao ay nananaginip ngunit madalas makalimutan ito. Hindi pa lubos na alam kung bakit tayo nananaginip."});

ADDART({id:"habits",t:"How Habits Work",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🔁",w:"Habit",r:["brain","memory","psychology","sleep","exercise"],
p:[["Automatic actions","Habits are actions we do automatically, often without thinking."],["Cue and reward","Habits follow a pattern of cue, routine and reward."],["Changing habits","Good habits can be built and bad ones changed with practice."]],
l:"A habit is a behavior that has been repeated so often that it happens almost automatically in response to a familiar situation, such as brushing teeth before bed or reaching for a phone on waking. Habits save the brain effort, freeing attention for other things, and a large part of daily behavior is habitual. Scientists describe habits as a loop of cue, routine and reward, involving brain regions such as the basal ganglia. Understanding how habits form helps people build healthy habits, such as exercise and study routines, and change unhelpful ones.",
s:[["Why we have habits","The brain looks for ways to save energy. When an action is repeated in the same situation, the brain links the situation to the action, so it can be done with little conscious thought. This lets people perform complex tasks, like riding a bicycle or typing, automatically."],["The habit loop","Many habits follow a pattern: a cue, such as a time, place, feeling or other people, triggers a routine, the behavior itself, which leads to a reward, such as pleasure or relief. Over time, the brain begins to crave the reward as soon as it notices the cue."],["In the brain","Habits rely on the basal ganglia, a group of structures deep in the brain involved in movement and learning. As a behavior becomes a habit, activity shifts from the parts of the brain involved in decision-making to these deeper regions."],["How long it takes","A popular myth says it takes 21 days to form a habit. A 2009 study at University College London found that it took participants an average of 66 days for a new behavior to become automatic, with a range of 18 to 254 days, depending on the person and the habit."]],
k:[["Habit loop","Cue, routine, reward"],["Brain region","Basal ganglia"],["Average time to form a habit (2009 study)","About 66 days"],["Myth","Habits take 21 days"],["Early writer on habit","William James (1890)"]],
d:[["c. 350 BCE","Aristotle writes that we become virtuous by practicing virtue"],["1890","William James writes a famous chapter on habit"],["1890s","Ivan Pavlov studies conditioned reflexes in dogs"],["1938","B. F. Skinner describes learning through rewards"],["2009","A study finds habits take about 66 days on average to form"],["2010s","Books on habits become worldwide bestsellers"]],
s2:[["Building good habits","Tips for building habits include starting very small, such as one minute of exercise; linking the new habit to an existing one, such as reading after dinner; making it easy and visible; repeating it at the same time and place; and rewarding yourself. Missing a day occasionally does not ruin progress."],["Breaking bad habits","It is often easier to replace a habit than to simply stop it. Identifying the cue and reward helps: someone who snacks when bored might take a short walk instead. Changing the environment, such as keeping a phone out of the bedroom, removes cues."],["Habits and character","Long ago, the Greek philosopher Aristotle wrote that we become just by doing just acts and brave by doing brave acts, suggesting that character is built through habits. Many religious and cultural traditions emphasize daily practices."],["Habits in children","Routines, such as regular bedtimes, washing hands, brushing teeth and reading together, help children develop healthy habits that can last a lifetime. Children learn many habits by watching adults."],["Technology and habits","Apps and games are often designed to form habits, using notifications, streaks and rewards to keep people coming back. Being aware of these designs helps people decide how they want to use technology."],["Pavlov’s dogs","In the 1890s, the Russian scientist Ivan Pavlov noticed that dogs began to drool when they heard the footsteps of the assistant who fed them. He showed that animals can learn to link a signal with a reward, a process called conditioning, which helps explain how habits and associations form."]],
sp:["The myth of 21 days","Where does the popular idea that it takes 21 days to form a habit come from? It is often traced to a 1960 book, Psycho-Cybernetics, by the American plastic surgeon Maxwell Maltz, who noticed that his patients took about 21 days to get used to their new appearance. He wrote that it takes a minimum of about 21 days for old mental images to dissolve. Over time, the minimum was forgotten and 21 days became a rule. Research has since shown that habits often take much longer to form and that the time varies greatly from person to person."],
g:[["Habit","A behavior repeated until it becomes automatic"],["Cue","A trigger that starts a habit"],["Basal ganglia","Brain structures involved in movement and habits"],["Conditioning","Learning to link a signal with a response"]],
f:["Habits follow a cue, routine and reward loop.","A 2009 study found habits take about 66 days on average.","The 21-day rule is a myth."],
fun:"The idea that habits take 21 days to form came from a plastic surgeon’s observations.",
tl:"Ang ugali o habit ay gawaing naging awtomatiko dahil paulit-ulit na ginagawa. Sumusunod ito sa pattern na cue, routine at reward. Sa karaniwan, mga 66 na araw bago mabuo ang bagong ugali."});

ADDART({id:"illusions",t:"Optical Illusions",c:"Health and the Body",i:"👁️",w:"Optical illusion",r:["eye","brain","light","modernart","psychology"],
p:[["Tricking the eye","Optical illusions are images that make us see something different from reality."],["The brain fills in","Illusions show that the brain interprets what the eyes see."],["Art and science","Artists and scientists use illusions to explore how vision works."]],
l:"An optical illusion is an image or scene that makes us perceive something different from what is actually there, such as lines that look different lengths but are equal, colors that look different but are the same or still pictures that seem to move. Illusions happen because seeing is not just recording light: the brain constantly interprets signals from the eyes, using shortcuts and expectations to make sense of the world quickly. Usually these shortcuts help us, but illusions reveal how they work. Scientists study illusions to understand perception, and artists use them to create surprising works.",
s:[["How seeing works","Light enters the eyes and forms images on the retina, which sends signals to the brain. The brain must work out sizes, distances, colors and movement from this information, using context, past experience and assumptions, such as that light usually comes from above."],["Length and size illusions","In the Müller-Lyer illusion, created in 1889, two lines of equal length look different because of arrow-like fins at their ends. In the Ponzo illusion, two equal lines look different sizes when placed between converging lines, like railway tracks, because the brain interprets them as being at different distances."],["Color and brightness illusions","In the checker shadow illusion, created by Edward Adelson in 1995, two squares that are exactly the same shade of gray look very different because one appears to be in shadow. The brain adjusts for the shadow, as it does in real life."],["Ambiguous images","Some images can be seen in two ways. The duck-rabbit drawing, published in 1892, can look like a duck or a rabbit, and the Necker cube, described in 1832, seems to flip between two orientations. The brain switches between interpretations but cannot see both at once."]],
k:[["Cause","The brain’s interpretation of visual signals"],["Müller-Lyer illusion","1889"],["Duck-rabbit drawing","1892"],["Checker shadow illusion","1995"],["Famous viral illusion","The dress (2015)"]],
d:[["c. 350 BCE","Aristotle notes that visual effects can deceive the senses"],["1832","The Necker cube is described"],["1889","The Müller-Lyer illusion is created"],["1892","The duck-rabbit drawing is published"],["1995","The checker shadow illusion is created"],["2015","A photo of a dress divides viewers over its colors"]],
s2:[["The dress","In February 2015, a photo of a striped dress went viral as people argued whether it was blue and black or white and gold. The dress was actually blue and black. Scientists explained that viewers’ brains made different assumptions about the lighting, some assuming bluish shade and others warm light, leading them to see different colors."],["Motion illusions","Some still images seem to move. The rotating snakes illusion, created by the Japanese psychologist Akiyoshi Kitaoka in 2003, shows circles that appear to turn as the eyes move across them. Films and animation themselves rely on an illusion: a series of still pictures shown quickly looks like smooth motion."],["The moon illusion","The Moon often looks much larger near the horizon than high in the sky, though its size in the sky is almost the same. The cause is still debated, but most explanations involve how the brain judges distance and size using the landscape."],["Impossible objects","Some drawings show objects that could not exist, such as the Penrose triangle, a triangle that seems to twist impossibly. The Dutch artist M. C. Escher used such ideas in famous prints of endless staircases and waterfalls that flow uphill."],["Illusions in art and design","Artists have used illusions for centuries, from painted ceilings that seem to open to the sky to op art, which uses patterns to create movement and vibration. Designers use illusions to make rooms look larger or roads safer, such as painted lines that make drivers slow down."],["Not just the eyes","Illusions also affect other senses. In the McGurk effect, what we see a person’s lips doing changes the sound we think we hear. Such illusions show that the brain combines information from different senses."]],
sp:["The dress that divided the internet","On 26 February 2015, a Scottish woman posted a photograph of a dress that her daughter’s future mother-in-law planned to wear to a wedding. Friends could not agree on its colors. Within hours, millions of people worldwide were arguing: blue and black, or white and gold? Celebrities joined in, and news programs covered the debate. The dress was in fact blue and black. Scientists studied why people saw it so differently, and their research showed how much the brain’s guesses about lighting shape the colors we see."],
g:[["Optical illusion","An image that tricks visual perception"],["Perception","The brain’s interpretation of signals from the senses"],["Retina","The light-sensitive layer at the back of the eye"],["Ambiguous image","A picture that can be seen in two ways"]],
f:["Illusions show that the brain interprets what we see.","The duck-rabbit drawing was published in 1892.","The viral dress of 2015 was blue and black."],
fun:"Films work because of an illusion: still pictures shown quickly look like motion.",
tl:"Ang optical illusion ay larawang nagpapakita ng iba sa tunay na nandoon. Nangyayari ito dahil binibigyang-kahulugan ng utak ang nakikita ng mata. Ginagamit ito ng mga siyentipiko at artista."});

ADDART({id:"symbiosis",t:"Symbiosis",c:"Science",i:"🐠",w:"Symbiosis",r:["eco","microbiome","coral","fungi","evo"],
p:[["Living together","Symbiosis is a close, long-term relationship between different species."],["Helping each other","In mutualism, both partners benefit."],["Inside our cells","Even parts of our cells began as partnerships with bacteria."]],
l:"Symbiosis is a close and long-lasting relationship between two different species. The word comes from Greek, meaning living together. In mutualism, both partners benefit, as when bees pollinate flowers while collecting nectar, or when tiny algae live inside corals and share food. In commensalism, one partner benefits and the other is unaffected, and in parasitism, one benefits at the other’s expense. Symbiosis is everywhere in nature, from lichens and the fungi on plant roots to the trillions of microbes in the human gut, and it has shaped the evolution of life, including the origins of complex cells.",
s:[["Mutualism","In mutualism, both species gain. Clownfish live among the stinging tentacles of sea anemones, protected from predators, while cleaning the anemone and scaring off fish that eat it. Flowering plants provide nectar to bees, birds and bats, which carry pollen from flower to flower."],["Commensalism","In commensalism, one species benefits while the other is neither helped nor harmed. Remora fish attach to sharks and whales, getting a free ride and scraps of food. Some birds nest in trees without affecting them."],["Parasitism","Parasites, such as tapeworms, ticks, lice and the organisms that cause malaria, live on or in a host and take nutrients, often harming it. Parasites are extremely common, and many have complex life cycles involving several hosts."],["Lichens","A lichen is a partnership between a fungus and algae or bacteria that can photosynthesize. The fungus provides shelter and absorbs water and minerals, while its partner makes food from sunlight. Together they can live on bare rocks, tree bark and in harsh places such as deserts and the Arctic."]],
k:[["Word means","Living together (Greek)"],["Types","Mutualism, commensalism, parasitism"],["Coined in biology","1870s"],["Famous mutualists","Clownfish and sea anemones"],["Endosymbiotic theory","Lynn Margulis (1967)"]],
d:[["1877","Albert Frank uses the word symbiosis for lichens"],["1879","Anton de Bary defines symbiosis broadly"],["1885","Fungi that partner with plant roots are named mycorrhizae"],["1967","Lynn Margulis proposes that mitochondria came from bacteria"],["2007","Studies begin mapping the human microbiome in detail"],["2016","A study confirms birds and humans cooperate to find honey"]],
s2:[["Coral and algae","Reef-building corals contain tiny algae called zooxanthellae in their tissues. The algae photosynthesize and give the coral most of its food, while the coral provides shelter and nutrients. When water gets too hot, corals expel the algae and turn white, called coral bleaching, which can kill them."],["Fungi and roots","Most land plants form partnerships with fungi on their roots, called mycorrhizae. The fungi’s thread-like networks absorb water and minerals from the soil and pass them to the plant, receiving sugars in return. These networks link plants underground."],["Cells that were once bacteria","In 1967 the American biologist Lynn Margulis proposed that mitochondria, the energy factories of our cells, and chloroplasts in plants were once free-living bacteria that came to live inside other cells. Her theory, at first rejected, is now widely accepted, showing that symbiosis helped create complex life."],["Our microbes","The human body hosts trillions of microbes, especially in the gut, where bacteria help digest food, make vitamins and train the immune system. In return, they get a warm home and a supply of food."],["Ants and fungi","Leafcutter ants in the Americas cut leaves not to eat them but to feed a fungus that they farm in underground gardens. The ants eat the fungus and protect it from pests, a farming partnership that has lasted millions of years."],["Symbiosis in the Philippines","Philippine coral reefs, such as Tubbataha, depend on coral and algae partnerships, and clownfish and anemones are favorite sights for divers. In Philippine forests, figs and the tiny wasps that pollinate them depend entirely on each other."]],
sp:["The bird that leads people to honey","In parts of Africa, a small bird called the greater honeyguide has a remarkable partnership with people. The bird guides honey hunters to wild bees’ nests hidden in trees, flying ahead and calling. The hunters open the nest and take the honey, and the bird feeds on the leftover wax and larvae. In 2016 scientists working with Yao honey hunters in Mozambique showed that the birds respond to a special call, a trill followed by a grunt, used by the hunters to recruit them. It is one of the few known cases of wild animals and humans cooperating as partners."],
g:[["Symbiosis","A close, long-term relationship between species"],["Mutualism","A relationship in which both partners benefit"],["Parasite","An organism that lives on or in a host and harms it"],["Lichen","A partnership between a fungus and algae or bacteria"]],
f:["Symbiosis means living together.","Mitochondria were once free-living bacteria.","Most land plants partner with fungi on their roots."],
fun:"In Africa, a wild bird leads people to honey and shares the reward.",
tl:"Ang symbiosis ay malapit at pangmatagalang ugnayan ng dalawang magkaibang uri ng buhay. Sa mutualism, parehong nakikinabang, gaya ng clownfish at sea anemone, at ng coral at algae."});

ADDART({id:"metamorph",t:"Metamorphosis",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦋",w:"Metamorphosis",r:["butterfly","frog","insect","beetle","dragonfly"],
p:[["Changing shape","Metamorphosis is a dramatic change in body form as an animal grows."],["Caterpillar to butterfly","Butterflies transform completely inside a chrysalis."],["Tadpole to frog","Frogs change from swimming tadpoles into air-breathing adults."]],
l:"Metamorphosis is a dramatic change in body shape and structure that some animals undergo as they develop from young to adult. Butterflies, moths, beetles, flies and bees go through complete metamorphosis, with four stages: egg, larva, pupa and adult. Grasshoppers, dragonflies and cicadas go through incomplete metamorphosis, in which young nymphs gradually come to resemble adults. Frogs and other amphibians also undergo metamorphosis, changing from water-dwelling tadpoles into adults. Metamorphosis allows young and adults to live in different ways and eat different foods, reducing competition between them.",
s:[["Complete metamorphosis","In complete metamorphosis, an egg hatches into a larva, such as a caterpillar, grub or maggot, which eats and grows. The larva then becomes a pupa, often inside a cocoon or chrysalis, where its body is rebuilt. Finally, an adult emerges, usually with wings. Most insect species develop this way."],["Inside the chrysalis","Inside a chrysalis, much of the caterpillar’s body breaks down into a kind of cellular soup. Groups of cells called imaginal discs, present since the caterpillar hatched, then grow into the wings, legs, eyes and other adult parts. Remarkably, some studies suggest that moths can remember things learned as caterpillars."],["Incomplete metamorphosis","In incomplete metamorphosis, young insects called nymphs look like small, wingless versions of adults. They grow by molting, shedding their outer skin several times, and develop wings at the final molt. Dragonfly nymphs live underwater for months or years before climbing out to become flying adults."],["Frogs and toads","A frog’s egg hatches into a tadpole, which lives in water, breathes with gills and has a tail. Over weeks, it grows legs, its tail shrinks, its gills are replaced by lungs and its mouth and gut change for eating insects. This transformation is controlled by thyroid hormone."]],
k:[["Complete stages","Egg, larva, pupa, adult"],["Incomplete stages","Egg, nymph, adult"],["Examples of complete","Butterflies, beetles, flies, bees"],["Examples of incomplete","Grasshoppers, dragonflies, cicadas"],["Controls frog metamorphosis","Thyroid hormone"]],
d:[["c. 350 BCE","Aristotle describes insect development"],["1669","Jan Swammerdam shows that the adult butterfly forms inside the pupa"],["1705","Maria Sibylla Merian publishes her paintings of insect metamorphosis"],["1912","Thyroid hormone is shown to trigger frog metamorphosis"],["2008","A study suggests moths remember what they learned as caterpillars"],["2020s","Scientists map the genes that control metamorphosis"]],
s2:[["Why metamorphosis?","Having different young and adult forms lets each stage specialize: larvae are eating machines that grow, while adults focus on reproduction and spreading to new places. Because caterpillars eat leaves and butterflies drink nectar, they do not compete for food."],["Maria Sibylla Merian","In the 1600s, many Europeans believed insects arose spontaneously from mud or rotting matter. The German-born artist and naturalist Maria Sibylla Merian raised caterpillars and carefully painted every stage of their lives. In 1699 she traveled to Suriname in South America, and her 1705 book of insect metamorphosis there became a scientific and artistic masterpiece."],["Other animals","Many marine animals, such as sea stars, crabs and corals, have tiny larvae that drift in the ocean before transforming into adults. Flatfish, such as flounders, start life swimming upright, and then one eye moves across the head so they can lie flat on the seabed."],["The axolotl","Some salamanders, such as the axolotl of Mexico, can stay in their larval form throughout life, keeping their gills and living in water while becoming able to reproduce. This is called neoteny."],["Butterflies of the Philippines","The Philippines is home to hundreds of butterfly species, many found nowhere else, and butterfly gardens let visitors watch caterpillars, chrysalises and adults. Children often learn about metamorphosis by raising caterpillars at school."],["A symbol of change","Because of its dramatic transformation, the butterfly has become a symbol of change, growth and rebirth in many cultures, appearing in art, poetry and stories around the world."]],
sp:["Remembering life as a caterpillar","Can a butterfly or moth remember its life as a caterpillar, even though its body and brain are reorganized inside the pupa? In a 2008 experiment, scientists at Georgetown University in the United States trained tobacco hornworm caterpillars to avoid a particular smell by pairing it with a mild shock. After the caterpillars turned into adult moths, many of them still avoided the smell. The results suggested that some memories can survive the dramatic transformation of metamorphosis, a surprising discovery about how nervous systems change."],
g:[["Metamorphosis","A dramatic change in form during development"],["Larva","The young, wingless feeding stage of an insect"],["Pupa","The resting stage in which an insect transforms"],["Nymph","A young insect that resembles the adult"]],
f:["Complete metamorphosis has four stages.","Tadpoles change into frogs.","Some moths may remember their caterpillar days."],
fun:"Some moths seem to remember things they learned as caterpillars.",
tl:"Ang metamorphosis ay malaking pagbabago ng anyo ng hayop habang lumalaki. Ang uod ay nagiging paru-paro sa loob ng chrysalis, at ang butete ay nagiging palaka."});

;
