ADDART({id:"guatemalacountry",t:"Guatemala",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌋",w:"Guatemala",r:["maya","mexicocountry","volc","coffee","hrights","spanph"],
p:[["Heart of the Maya","Guatemala was a center of the ancient Maya civilization."],["Land of volcanoes","Volcanoes tower over its highlands and lakes."],["Living Maya cultures","Millions of Maya people keep their languages and traditions alive."]],
l:"Guatemala is a country in Central America, with about 18 million people, the most populous in the region. Its capital is Guatemala City. Guatemala was a heartland of the ancient Maya civilization, whose great cities, such as Tikal, rise from its rainforests. Today about two-fifths of Guatemalans are Indigenous Maya, speaking more than 20 Mayan languages and famous for their colorful woven textiles. Conquered by Spain in the 1500s, Guatemala became independent in 1821. In the 20th century it suffered a long civil war, from 1960 to 1996, in which some 200,000 people were killed or disappeared, most of them Maya.",
s:[["Land","Guatemala has Pacific lowlands, central highlands crowned by more than 30 volcanoes, and the northern lowland rainforests of Petén. Volcán Tajumulco is the highest peak in Central America. Lake Atitlán, surrounded by volcanoes and Maya villages, is famous for its beauty."],["The Maya","The Maya built cities with pyramids, palaces and ball courts, developed a writing system, a calendar and advanced mathematics, including the concept of zero. Tikal, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, was one of their greatest cities, flourishing between about 200 and 900 CE."],["Spanish conquest","Spanish forces led by Pedro de Alvarado conquered the region from 1524. Antigua Guatemala became the capital of a large colonial region until earthquakes in 1773 led to the founding of Guatemala City. The colonial city of Antigua is now a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["Independence and the 20th century","Guatemala declared independence from Spain on 15 September 1821. In 1954 a coup supported by the United States overthrew the elected president Jacobo Árbenz, whose land reforms had angered a powerful American fruit company. Political instability followed."]],
k:[["Capital","Guatemala City"],["Population","About 18 million (2024)"],["Area","About 108,900 km²"],["Highest point","Volcán Tajumulco, 4,220 m"],["Currency","Quetzal"]],
d:[["c. 200–900","Classic Maya cities such as Tikal flourish"],["1524","Spanish conquest begins"],["1773","Earthquakes destroy Antigua Guatemala"],["1821","Guatemala becomes independent from Spain"],["1954","A US-backed coup overthrows President Árbenz"],["1996","Peace accords end the civil war"],["2018","The Fuego volcano erupts, killing hundreds"]],
s2:[["The civil war","From 1960 to 1996, government forces fought leftist guerrillas. The army and paramilitary groups killed and disappeared tens of thousands of civilians, especially Maya villagers. A UN-backed truth commission found that state forces committed acts of genocide against Maya communities in the early 1980s. Peace accords were signed on 29 December 1996."],["Rigoberta Menchú","Rigoberta Menchú, a K’iche’ Maya woman whose family members were killed during the war, became an internationally known campaigner for Indigenous rights. She was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize in 1992, the 500th anniversary of Columbus’s arrival in the Americas."],["Maya cultures today","Maya communities speak languages such as K’iche’, Q’eqchi’, Kaqchikel and Mam, and many wear traditional clothing such as the huipil, a woven blouse whose patterns show a person’s community. Maya spiritual traditions, markets and festivals remain vibrant, though Indigenous people still face poverty and discrimination."],["The quetzal","The resplendent quetzal, a bird with brilliant green feathers and a long tail, is Guatemala’s national symbol and gives its name to the currency. It was sacred to the ancient Maya and appears on the national flag."],["Volcanoes and disasters","Guatemala’s volcanoes provide fertile soils for coffee but also danger. In June 2018, the Fuego volcano erupted with fast-moving flows of hot gas and ash, killing hundreds of people. Earthquakes, such as the one in 1976, have also caused great destruction."],["Economy and migration","Guatemala exports coffee, bananas, sugar and cardamom, of which it is one of the world’s largest producers. Many Guatemalans have migrated to the United States, and their remittances are important to the economy. In 2023, voters elected Bernardo Arévalo as president on a promise to fight corruption."]],
sp:["Tikal in the rainforest","Deep in the rainforest of northern Guatemala, the temples of Tikal rise above the treetops, some more than 60 meters tall. At its height, around 700 CE, Tikal may have been home to tens of thousands of people, with plazas, palaces, reservoirs and ball courts. After its decline, the jungle swallowed it for centuries. In recent years, scientists using lidar, laser scanning from aircraft, revealed thousands of previously hidden Maya structures, roads and farms beneath the forest around Tikal, showing that the Maya world was far larger and more connected than once thought."],
g:[["Maya","An Indigenous people and ancient civilization of Central America"],["Huipil","A traditional woven Maya blouse"],["Quetzal","A brilliantly colored bird and Guatemala’s currency"],["Peace accords","Agreements that end a war"],["Lidar","Laser scanning used to map land, even under forests"]],
f:["Guatemala was a heartland of the Maya.","Its civil war lasted from 1960 to 1996.","Rigoberta Menchú won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1992."],
fun:"Lasers from aircraft have revealed thousands of hidden Maya structures in Guatemala’s forests.",
tl:"Ang Guatemala ay bansa sa Gitnang Amerika at puso ng sinaunang sibilisasyong Maya. Mga dalawa sa bawat lima nitong mamamayan ay katutubong Maya. Dumaan ito sa mahabang digmaang sibil mula 1960 hanggang 1996."});

ADDART({id:"costaricacountry",t:"Costa Rica",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🦥",w:"Costa Rica",r:["conservation","renew","deforestation","coffee","panamacountry","tourism"],
p:[["A country without an army","Costa Rica abolished its army in 1948."],["Rich in nature","It holds a large share of the world’s species in a small area."],["Pura vida","Costa Ricans’ motto, pura vida, means pure life."]],
l:"Costa Rica is a small country in Central America, between the Caribbean Sea and the Pacific Ocean, with about 5 million people. Its capital is San José. Costa Rica is famous for abolishing its army in 1948 and investing instead in education and health, for its long-standing democracy and for its extraordinary biodiversity, protected in national parks covering a large part of its territory. It generates almost all its electricity from renewable sources and has reversed deforestation, becoming a world leader in ecotourism and conservation. Costa Ricans, called Ticos, often greet each other with pura vida, pure life.",
s:[["Land","Costa Rica has beaches on two oceans, volcanic mountains, cloud forests, rainforests and dry forests. Volcanoes such as Arenal and Poás attract visitors, and Cerro Chirripó is the highest peak. The Osa Peninsula and Corcovado National Park are among the most biodiverse places on Earth."],["History","Before the Spanish arrived, the region was home to Indigenous peoples, including those who made mysterious stone spheres in the south. Christopher Columbus reached its Caribbean coast in 1502. Costa Rica became independent from Spain in 1821 and grew rich from coffee in the 1800s."],["Abolishing the army","After a short civil war in 1948, President José Figueres Ferrer abolished the army, symbolically breaking down a wall of the army headquarters with a hammer. The constitution of 1949 banned a permanent army. Money once spent on the military went to schools, health care and social programs."],["Democracy","Costa Rica has one of the oldest and most stable democracies in Latin America, with free elections and peaceful transfers of power. In 1987 President Óscar Arias won the Nobel Peace Prize for his plan to end wars in Central America."]],
k:[["Capital","San José"],["Population","About 5.1 million (2024)"],["Area","About 51,100 km²"],["Highest point","Cerro Chirripó, 3,820 m"],["Currency","Costa Rican colón"]],
d:[["1502","Columbus reaches the Caribbean coast"],["1821","Costa Rica becomes independent from Spain"],["1869","Primary education becomes free and compulsory"],["1948","Costa Rica abolishes its army"],["1987","Óscar Arias wins the Nobel Peace Prize"],["1996","A forestry law begins paying landowners to protect forests"],["2021","Costa Rica wins an Earthshot Prize for restoring forests"]],
s2:[["Biodiversity","Though it covers only a tiny fraction of the Earth’s land, Costa Rica is home to about 5 percent of the world’s species, including sloths, toucans, quetzals, jaguars, poison dart frogs, hummingbirds and sea turtles that nest on its beaches. About a quarter of the country is protected in parks and reserves."],["Bringing back the forests","By the 1980s, cattle ranching and logging had cleared much of Costa Rica’s forest. The country then began paying landowners to protect and restore forests, banned clearing of mature forest and promoted ecotourism. Forest cover has grown back to more than half the country, and in 2021 Costa Rica won the Earthshot Prize for this work."],["Clean energy","Costa Rica generates almost all of its electricity from renewable sources, mainly hydropower, along with geothermal, wind and solar power. It has set goals to cut greenhouse gas emissions across its economy."],["Ecotourism","Visitors come to watch wildlife, hike in cloud forests such as Monteverde, surf and see volcanoes. Ecotourism is a major source of income and jobs, encouraging communities to protect nature."],["Long lives","The Nicoya Peninsula is often cited as one of the world’s regions where people live unusually long lives, linked to strong family and community ties, physical activity and traditional diets, though researchers debate the reasons."],["Economy","Costa Rica exports coffee, bananas and pineapples, of which it is the world’s largest exporter, as well as medical devices made by international companies. Challenges include inequality, rising crime linked to drug trafficking and the cost of living."]],
sp:["The hammer that ended an army","On 1 December 1948, after winning a brief civil war, José Figueres Ferrer, head of Costa Rica’s provisional government, stood at the Bellavista military barracks in San José and struck a wall with a sledgehammer, announcing the abolition of the army. He handed the keys of the barracks to the education minister. The building became the National Museum of Costa Rica. Since then, Costa Rica has had no army, relying on its police and international law for security, and 1 December is celebrated as the Day of the Abolition of the Army."],
g:[["Biodiversity","The variety of living things in an area"],["Ecotourism","Travel to natural areas that supports conservation"],["Cloud forest","A mountain forest often covered in mist"],["Ticos","A nickname for Costa Ricans"],["Pura vida","A Costa Rican saying meaning pure life"]],
f:["Costa Rica abolished its army in 1948.","It has about 5 percent of the world’s species.","Almost all its electricity is renewable."],
fun:"Costa Rica’s former army headquarters is now the National Museum.",
tl:"Ang Costa Rica ay maliit na bansa sa Gitnang Amerika na nag-alis ng hukbong sandatahan noong 1948. Mayaman ito sa likas na yaman at halos lahat ng kuryente nito ay mula sa renewable na enerhiya. Pura vida ang kanilang kasabihan."});

ADDART({id:"panamacountry",t:"Panama",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🚢",w:"Panama",r:["canals","colombiacountry","costaricacountry","usacountry","ships","trade"],
p:[["Between two oceans","The Panama Canal links the Atlantic and Pacific oceans."],["Joining the Americas","Panama connects North and South America."],["Crossroads of the world","Ships, goods and people from around the world pass through Panama."]],
l:"Panama is a country on the narrow isthmus that connects Central and South America, with about 4.5 million people. Its capital is Panama City. Panama is best known for the Panama Canal, which cuts across the isthmus to link the Atlantic and Pacific oceans, carrying a significant share of world trade. Once part of Colombia, Panama became independent in 1903 with American support, and the United States built and controlled the canal until handing it to Panama in 1999. Panama is a hub for shipping, banking and air travel, with rainforests, islands and Indigenous peoples such as the Guna.",
s:[["A land bridge","About three million years ago, the Isthmus of Panama rose from the sea, joining North and South America. Animals crossed in both directions, and the land bridge changed ocean currents and climates. Today Panama’s forests are rich in wildlife."],["Spanish era","In 1513 the Spanish explorer Vasco Núñez de Balboa crossed the isthmus and became the first European to see the Pacific Ocean from the Americas. Panama City, founded in 1519, was the first European city on the Pacific coast of the Americas. Gold and silver from Peru were carried across Panama to ships bound for Spain."],["Independence","Panama became independent from Spain in 1821 and joined Colombia. In 1903, when Colombia refused to approve an American canal treaty, Panama declared independence with US support, and the United States gained control of a strip of land, the Canal Zone."],["The modern country","Panama was ruled by military leaders for parts of the 20th century. In December 1989 the United States invaded Panama and removed the military ruler Manuel Noriega. Since then Panama has held democratic elections and grown into one of the fastest-growing economies in Latin America."]],
k:[["Capital","Panama City"],["Population","About 4.5 million (2024)"],["Area","About 75,000 km²"],["Highest point","Volcán Barú, 3,475 m"],["Currency","Balboa and US dollar"]],
d:[["1513","Balboa sees the Pacific Ocean"],["1519","Panama City is founded"],["1821","Panama becomes independent from Spain and joins Colombia"],["1903","Panama separates from Colombia"],["1914","The Panama Canal opens"],["1999","The canal is handed over to Panama"],["2016","The expanded canal opens"]],
s2:[["The Panama Canal","The Panama Canal, about 80 kilometers long, opened on 15 August 1914. Ships are raised and lowered by locks, water-filled chambers, to cross Gatun Lake, an artificial lake about 26 meters above sea level. A trip from New York to San Francisco through the canal saves about 13,000 kilometers compared with sailing around South America."],["Building the canal","France began building a canal in 1881 under Ferdinand de Lesseps, builder of the Suez Canal, but gave up after disease, landslides and financial scandal; more than 20,000 workers died. The United States took over in 1904, controlled yellow fever and malaria by fighting mosquitoes and completed the canal using tens of thousands of workers, many from the Caribbean."],["Returning the canal","The Torrijos–Carter Treaties of 1977 agreed to transfer the canal to Panama. On 31 December 1999, Panama took full control. Under Panamanian management, the canal was expanded with larger locks, opened in 2016, allowing much bigger ships to pass."],["Water and the canal","Each ship passing through the canal uses millions of liters of fresh water from Gatun Lake. In 2023 a severe drought lowered the lake, forcing the canal to limit the number and size of ships, showing how climate affects world trade."],["The Guna","The Guna people live in the autonomous region of Guna Yala, a chain of islands and coast on the Caribbean. They have their own government and are famous for molas, colorful layered textile panels sewn by women. In 1925 the Guna revolted to protect their culture and won autonomy."],["The Darién Gap","The Darién, a roadless region of rainforest and mountains on the border with Colombia, interrupts the Pan-American Highway. In recent years, hundreds of thousands of migrants seeking to reach North America have made dangerous journeys through it."]],
sp:["The ship that opened the canal","On 15 August 1914, the cargo and passenger ship SS Ancon made the first official passage through the Panama Canal, traveling from the Atlantic to the Pacific in about nine hours. The opening received less attention than planned because World War I had broken out in Europe just days earlier. Yet the canal transformed world shipping. Today, roughly 13,000 to 14,000 ships pass through it in a typical year, paying tolls based on their size. The highest tolls have been hundreds of thousands of dollars for large cruise ships, while the lowest ever was 36 cents, paid in 1928 by the adventurer Richard Halliburton, who swam the canal."],
g:[["Isthmus","A narrow strip of land connecting two larger areas"],["Lock","A water-filled chamber that raises or lowers ships"],["Canal Zone","The strip of land around the canal once controlled by the US"],["Mola","A colorful layered textile made by Guna women"],["Autonomy","Self-government"]],
f:["The Panama Canal opened in 1914.","Panama took control of the canal in 1999.","The isthmus joins North and South America."],
fun:"A man paid just 36 cents to swim through the Panama Canal in 1928.",
tl:"Ang Panama ay bansa sa makitid na lupang nag-uugnay sa Hilaga at Timog Amerika. Kilala ito sa Panama Canal na nag-uugnay sa Atlantiko at Pasipiko. Napasakamay ng Panama ang kanal noong 1999."});

ADDART({id:"haiticountry",t:"Haiti",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏰",w:"Haiti",r:["toussaint","slavetrade","frrev","cubacountry","quake","francecountry"],
p:[["The first free Black republic","Haiti won independence in 1804 after a revolution by enslaved people."],["A proud culture","Haiti is famous for its art, music, food and Creole language."],["Facing hardship","Haiti has faced disasters, poverty and political crises."]],
l:"Haiti is a country on the western part of the Caribbean island of Hispaniola, which it shares with the Dominican Republic, with about 12 million people. Its capital is Port-au-Prince. Once the French colony of Saint-Domingue, the richest colony in the Caribbean, built on the labor of enslaved Africans, Haiti became in 1804 the first independent nation founded by formerly enslaved people, after the only slave revolution to create a state. Haiti has a rich culture of art, music and the Haitian Creole language, but it has suffered foreign debts and interventions, dictatorship, a devastating earthquake in 2010 and, in recent years, severe insecurity caused by armed gangs.",
s:[["Land","Haiti is mountainous; its name comes from the Taíno word Ayiti, land of high mountains. Much of its forest has been cut down, making it vulnerable to floods and landslides during hurricanes. The island of La Gonâve and the Citadelle in the north are among its landmarks."],["Saint-Domingue","Christopher Columbus reached Hispaniola in 1492, and the Taíno population was devastated. In 1697 France gained the western part of the island. Saint-Domingue produced huge amounts of sugar and coffee, worked by hundreds of thousands of enslaved Africans under brutal conditions."],["The Haitian Revolution","In August 1791, enslaved people rose up in the north. Led by Toussaint Louverture and later Jean-Jacques Dessalines and Henri Christophe, they defeated French, Spanish and British forces, including an army sent by Napoleon. On 1 January 1804, Dessalines declared the independence of Haiti."],["A costly freedom","In 1825 France demanded a huge payment from Haiti, as compensation for former slave owners, in exchange for recognizing its independence, backed by warships. Haiti paid this debt and the loans taken to pay it for many decades, draining its economy. The United States occupied Haiti from 1915 to 1934."]],
k:[["Capital","Port-au-Prince"],["Population","About 12 million (2024)"],["Area","About 27,750 km²"],["Highest point","Pic la Selle, 2,680 m"],["Currency","Haitian gourde"]],
d:[["1492","Columbus reaches Hispaniola"],["1697","France gains the western part of Hispaniola"],["1791","The Haitian Revolution begins"],["1804","Haiti declares independence"],["1825","France demands a huge payment for recognizing Haiti"],["1915","The United States begins occupying Haiti"],["2010","A powerful earthquake strikes near Port-au-Prince"]],
s2:[["The 2010 earthquake","On 12 January 2010, a magnitude 7.0 earthquake struck near Port-au-Prince, destroying homes, schools, hospitals and government buildings. Estimates of the dead range from about 100,000 to more than 300,000. The world sent aid, but recovery was slow, and a cholera outbreak introduced by UN peacekeepers later killed thousands."],["Political crises","After the Duvalier family’s dictatorship from 1957 to 1986, Haiti struggled to build stable democracy, with coups, disputed elections and foreign interventions. In July 2021 President Jovenel Moïse was assassinated. Armed gangs have since taken control of much of Port-au-Prince, causing violence, displacement and hunger, and international security missions have been authorized to support Haitian police."],["The Citadelle","After independence, King Henri Christophe built the Citadelle Laferrière, a massive fortress on a mountaintop in the north, to defend against a French return. With the nearby Sans-Souci Palace, it is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and the largest fortress in the Americas."],["Language and faith","Haitian Creole, based on French with African and other influences, is spoken by nearly all Haitians, and French is also official. Most Haitians are Christian, and many also practice Vodou, a religion that blends African traditions with Catholicism."],["Art and food","Haiti is famous for its vibrant paintings, metal sculptures and music such as kompa. On Independence Day, 1 January, Haitians eat soup joumou, a pumpkin soup once forbidden to the enslaved, as a symbol of freedom; UNESCO recognized it in 2021."],["Haitians abroad","Large Haitian communities live in the United States, Canada, France, the Dominican Republic and elsewhere. Money sent home by Haitians abroad is a major part of the economy."]],
sp:["Soup joumou, the soup of freedom","In colonial Saint-Domingue, soup made with joumou, a kind of squash or pumpkin, was a delicacy enjoyed by French slave owners, and enslaved people who cooked it were forbidden to eat it. After Haiti won its independence on 1 January 1804, Haitians began eating soup joumou on New Year’s Day to celebrate their freedom. The tradition continues in Haitian homes around the world, with families sharing the soup, rich with beef, vegetables and pasta, every 1 January. In 2021 UNESCO added soup joumou to its list of intangible cultural heritage."],
g:[["Hispaniola","The Caribbean island shared by Haiti and the Dominican Republic"],["Haitian Creole","The language spoken by nearly all Haitians"],["Indemnity","A payment demanded as compensation"],["Vodou","A Haitian religion blending African and Catholic traditions"],["Kompa","A popular Haitian dance music"]],
f:["Haiti became independent in 1804.","It was founded by formerly enslaved people.","Soup joumou is eaten on Independence Day."],
fun:"Haitians eat pumpkin soup every 1 January to celebrate their freedom.",
tl:"Ang Haiti ay bansa sa isla ng Hispaniola sa Caribbean. Naging malaya ito noong 1804 matapos ang rebolusyon ng mga inaliping Aprikano, ang unang malayang republika ng mga dating alipin. Dumanas ito ng lindol at krisis pampulitika."});

ADDART({id:"uruguaycountry",t:"Uruguay",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🧉",w:"Uruguay",r:["argentinacountry","brazilcountry","soccer","democ","renew","wind"],
p:[["A small, stable country","Uruguay is known for its democracy, social policies and quality of life."],["World Cup history","Uruguay hosted and won the first football World Cup in 1930."],["Mate culture","Uruguayans drink more mate tea per person than almost anyone."]],
l:"Uruguay is a small country in southeastern South America, between Argentina and Brazil, with about 3.4 million people, nearly half of whom live in the capital, Montevideo. It is known for its strong democracy, low corruption, progressive social laws and high quality of life. Uruguay hosted and won the first football World Cup in 1930 and won again in 1950. It is a land of rolling grasslands and cattle, with more cows than people, and it generates most of its electricity from renewable sources, especially wind. Its people share with Argentina a love of tango, beef and mate tea.",
s:[["Land","Uruguay is mostly low rolling grassland, called pampas, ideal for cattle and sheep, with rivers, lagoons and Atlantic beaches. Its highest hill is only about 514 meters high. The Río de la Plata estuary separates it from Argentina."],["History","The Charrúa and other Indigenous peoples lived in the region. The Portuguese founded Colonia del Sacramento in 1680, and the Spanish founded Montevideo in the 1720s. After struggles between Spain, Portugal, Brazil and Argentina, Uruguay became independent, declaring it in 1825 and gaining recognition in 1828."],["A welfare state","In the early 1900s, President José Batlle y Ordóñez introduced reforms such as free public education, workers’ rights, pensions and the separation of church and state, making Uruguay one of the first welfare states in the Americas."],["Dictatorship and democracy","A military dictatorship ruled from 1973 to 1985, imprisoning and torturing thousands of opponents. Democracy returned in 1985, and Uruguay is now regularly ranked among the most democratic countries in the world."]],
k:[["Capital","Montevideo"],["Population","About 3.4 million (2024)"],["Area","About 176,000 km²"],["Highest point","Cerro Catedral, 514 m"],["Currency","Uruguayan peso"]],
d:[["1680","The Portuguese found Colonia del Sacramento"],["1726","Montevideo is founded"],["1825","Uruguay declares independence"],["1930","Uruguay hosts and wins the first World Cup"],["1973","A military dictatorship begins"],["2007","Uruguay begins giving a laptop to every schoolchild"],["2013","Uruguay legalizes the cannabis market and same-sex marriage"]],
s2:[["Football","In 1930 Uruguay hosted and won the first FIFA World Cup, beating Argentina in the final. In 1950 it shocked the world by beating host Brazil in the decisive match at the Maracanã Stadium in Rio de Janeiro, a victory called the Maracanazo. For a small country, it has produced many great players."],["Social laws","Uruguay has been a pioneer in social policies, legalizing divorce early in the 1900s and, in 2013, becoming the first country to create a legal, regulated market for cannabis and one of the first in Latin America to legalize same-sex marriage. These laws have supporters and critics."],["A laptop for every child","In 2007 Uruguay began Plan Ceibal, giving a free laptop to every child and teacher in public primary schools, one of the first countries to do so nationally, and connecting schools to the internet."],["Clean energy","Within about a decade, Uruguay transformed its electricity system, building wind farms and solar parks alongside hydropower. Renewable sources now provide most of its electricity, making it a model for clean energy."],["José Mujica","José Mujica, a former guerrilla imprisoned for years under the dictatorship, served as president from 2010 to 2015. He became internationally famous for his simple lifestyle, living on a small farm, driving an old car and giving most of his salary to charity, and was nicknamed the world’s poorest president. He died in 2025."],["Mate and asado","Uruguayans drink mate, a herbal tea sipped through a metal straw from a gourd, carrying thermos flasks of hot water everywhere. Asado, barbecued beef, is a beloved social tradition, and candombe drumming, of African origin, fills the streets during Carnival."]],
sp:["The Maracanazo","On 16 July 1950, almost 200,000 people packed the Maracanã Stadium in Rio de Janeiro for the final match of the World Cup between Brazil and Uruguay. Brazil needed only a draw to win, and newspapers had already printed headlines celebrating Brazil as champions. Brazil scored first, but Uruguay equalized, and with 11 minutes left Alcides Ghiggia scored again for Uruguay. The huge crowd fell silent. Uruguay won 2–1. The defeat became known in Brazil as the Maracanazo, and Ghiggia later said that only three people had ever silenced the Maracanã: Frank Sinatra, the Pope and himself."],
g:[["Pampas","Grassy plains of South America"],["Welfare state","A state that provides social services and protections"],["Mate","A herbal tea popular in South America"],["Candombe","An Afro-Uruguayan drumming tradition"],["Estuary","Where a river meets the sea"]],
f:["Uruguay won the first World Cup in 1930.","It has more cattle than people.","Most of its electricity comes from renewable sources."],
fun:"Uruguay silenced nearly 200,000 Brazilian fans by winning the 1950 World Cup decider.",
tl:"Ang Uruguay ay maliit na bansa sa Timog Amerika na kilala sa matatag na demokrasya at mataas na kalidad ng buhay. Ito ang nagwagi sa unang World Cup noong 1930. Karamihan ng kuryente nito ay mula sa renewable na enerhiya."});

ADDART({id:"irelandcountry",t:"Ireland",c:"Countries and Places",i:"☘️",w:"Republic of Ireland",r:["ukcountry","celts","christ","eu","poetry","potato"],
p:[["The Emerald Isle","Ireland is famous for its green landscapes, castles and coast."],["Great writers","Ireland has produced many celebrated writers and four Nobel laureates in literature."],["From famine to prosperity","Once devastated by famine, Ireland is now one of Europe’s richest countries."]],
l:"Ireland, officially the Republic of Ireland, occupies most of the island of Ireland in northwestern Europe, with about 5.4 million people; the northeast of the island, Northern Ireland, is part of the United Kingdom. Its capital is Dublin. Known as the Emerald Isle for its green countryside, Ireland has ancient monuments, a Celtic heritage and a rich tradition of literature, music and storytelling. It endured centuries of English and British rule and the Great Famine of the 1840s, which killed about a million people and caused mass emigration. Independent since 1922, Ireland joined the European Union and became one of Europe’s fastest-growing economies.",
s:[["Land","Ireland has a central lowland of farms, lakes and bogs, ringed by coastal hills and mountains. Its mild, rainy climate, shaped by the Atlantic Ocean, keeps the land green. The Cliffs of Moher and the Giant’s Causeway, in Northern Ireland, are famous sights."],["Ancient and Christian Ireland","Newgrange, a huge passage tomb built around 3200 BCE, is older than the pyramids of Giza. Celtic culture spread later, and St Patrick brought Christianity in the 400s. Irish monasteries preserved learning in the early Middle Ages, producing illuminated manuscripts such as the Book of Kells."],["English rule","Vikings founded Dublin in the 800s, and Anglo-Norman knights invaded in 1169, beginning centuries of English involvement. English and Scottish Protestants were settled on Irish land, and Catholics faced harsh laws. In 1801 Ireland was united with Great Britain."],["Independence","After the Easter Rising of 1916 and the War of Independence from 1919 to 1921, the Irish Free State was created in 1922, while Northern Ireland remained in the United Kingdom. A civil war followed over the treaty. Ireland declared itself a republic in 1949."]],
k:[["Capital","Dublin"],["Population","About 5.4 million (2024)"],["Area","About 70,300 km²"],["Highest point","Carrauntoohil, 1,038 m"],["Currency","Euro"]],
d:[["c. 3200 BCE","Newgrange is built"],["841","Vikings found Dublin"],["1845","The Great Famine begins"],["1916","The Easter Rising takes place in Dublin"],["1922","The Irish Free State is created"],["1973","Ireland joins the European Economic Community"],["1998","The Good Friday Agreement is signed"]],
s2:[["The Great Famine","From 1845 to 1852, a disease called potato blight destroyed the potato crop, the main food of millions of poor Irish people. About a million people died of hunger and disease, and more than a million emigrated, many to the United States, Canada, Britain and Australia. Food continued to be exported from Ireland during the famine, which many blame on British policies. Ireland’s population has never returned to its pre-famine level."],["The Irish around the world","Millions of people worldwide have Irish ancestry, especially in the United States, Britain, Australia and Canada. St Patrick’s Day, 17 March, is celebrated around the world with parades and the color green."],["Northern Ireland and peace","From the late 1960s, a conflict known as the Troubles between mainly Catholic nationalists seeking a united Ireland and mainly Protestant unionists wishing to remain in the United Kingdom killed more than 3,500 people. The Good Friday Agreement of 1998 brought a peace process and power-sharing government."],["Literature","Ireland has produced writers such as Jonathan Swift, Oscar Wilde, James Joyce, Samuel Beckett and four Nobel Prize winners in literature: W. B. Yeats, George Bernard Shaw, Beckett and Seamus Heaney. Irish storytelling, poetry and drama are celebrated worldwide."],["Modern Ireland","From the 1990s, Ireland’s economy grew rapidly in a boom called the Celtic Tiger, attracting technology and pharmaceutical companies. It suffered a severe crash in 2008 but recovered. Ireland has also changed socially, becoming in 2015 the first country to approve same-sex marriage by popular vote."],["Language and culture","Irish, or Gaelic, is the first official language and is taught in schools, though English is spoken by most people; Irish-speaking areas are called the Gaeltacht. Traditional music, dancing and sports such as hurling and Gaelic football are beloved. Many Filipino nurses and health workers live and work in Ireland."]],
sp:["The midwinter sunrise at Newgrange","Newgrange, a great mound of stone and earth in the Boyne Valley, was built around 3200 BCE by Stone Age farmers. Above its entrance is a small opening called a roof box. For a few days around the winter solstice, the shortest day of the year, the rising sun shines through the roof box and along a passage about 19 meters long, lighting up the inner chamber for about 17 minutes. The builders aligned it precisely more than 5,000 years ago. Today so many people want to witness the event that places inside are given out by lottery."],
g:[["Celtic","Relating to the Celts, an ancient people of Europe"],["Passage tomb","An ancient tomb with a stone passage leading to a chamber"],["Potato blight","A plant disease that destroyed potato crops"],["Troubles","The conflict in Northern Ireland from the late 1960s to 1998"],["Gaeltacht","Irish-speaking areas of Ireland"]],
f:["Newgrange is older than the pyramids of Giza.","The Great Famine killed about a million people.","Ireland approved same-sex marriage by popular vote in 2015."],
fun:"The midwinter sun lights up the inside of Newgrange, a tomb built over 5,000 years ago.",
tl:"Ang Ireland ay bansa sa hilagang-kanlurang Europa na tinatawag na Emerald Isle dahil sa luntiang lupain. Dumanas ito ng Malaking Taggutom noong 1840s. Naging malaya ito noong 1922 at kilala sa mga manunulat at musika."});

ADDART({id:"austriacountry",t:"Austria",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🎻",w:"Austria",r:["alps","mozart","beethoven","germanycountry","danube","eu"],
p:[["Land of music","Austria was home to Mozart, Haydn, Schubert and the Strauss waltzes."],["The Habsburg Empire","For centuries, Vienna was the capital of a great European empire."],["Alpine country","The Alps cover most of Austria."]],
l:"Austria is a landlocked country in central Europe, with about 9.2 million people. Its capital is Vienna. For more than six centuries, Austria was the heart of the Habsburg monarchy, one of Europe’s great powers, and Vienna became a center of music, art, science and philosophy, home to Mozart, Beethoven, Schubert, the Strauss family, Sigmund Freud and Gustav Klimt. After the empire collapsed in 1918, Austria became a republic, was annexed by Nazi Germany in 1938 and regained independence in 1955 as a neutral country. Today it is a prosperous member of the European Union, famous for the Alps, skiing, coffee houses and classical music.",
s:[["Land","About two-thirds of Austria is covered by the Alps, with peaks such as the Grossglockner, glaciers and lakes. The Danube River flows through the north, past Linz and Vienna. Eastern Austria has lowlands, vineyards and the shallow Lake Neusiedl."],["The Habsburgs","The Habsburg family ruled Austria from 1278 until 1918. Through marriages and wars, they built an empire including Hungary, Bohemia, parts of Italy and the Balkans and, for a time, Spain and its colonies, including the Philippines under Spain’s Habsburg kings. Vienna resisted Ottoman sieges in 1529 and 1683."],["The end of the empire","In 1867 the empire became Austria-Hungary. The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to the throne, in Sarajevo in 1914 triggered World War I. Defeat in 1918 ended the monarchy, and Austria became a small republic."],["Anschluss and occupation","In March 1938 Nazi Germany annexed Austria in the Anschluss, welcomed by many Austrians but opposed by others. Austrian Jews were persecuted, and many were murdered in the Holocaust. After World War II, Austria was occupied by the Allies until the State Treaty of 1955 restored its independence."]],
k:[["Capital","Vienna"],["Population","About 9.2 million (2024)"],["Area","About 83,900 km²"],["Highest point","Grossglockner, 3,798 m"],["Currency","Euro"]],
d:[["996","The name Ostarrîchi, an early form of Austria, first appears"],["1278","The Habsburgs begin ruling Austria"],["1683","Vienna resists an Ottoman siege"],["1815","The Congress of Vienna redraws the map of Europe"],["1918","Austria becomes a republic"],["1938","Nazi Germany annexes Austria"],["1955","Austria regains independence and declares neutrality"]],
s2:[["Music","Vienna is often called the city of music. Haydn, Mozart, Beethoven, Schubert, Brahms and Mahler lived and worked there. Johann Strauss II composed waltzes such as The Blue Danube, and the Vienna Philharmonic’s New Year’s Concert is watched by millions around the world. Salzburg, Mozart’s birthplace, hosts a famous music festival."],["Science and ideas","Austria produced influential thinkers and scientists, including the psychoanalyst Sigmund Freud, the physicist Erwin Schrödinger, the geneticist Gregor Mendel, who worked in Brno in the Austrian Empire, and the philosopher Ludwig Wittgenstein."],["Neutrality","When Austria regained independence in 1955, it declared permanent neutrality, celebrated on its National Day, 26 October. Vienna hosts many international organizations, including a United Nations headquarters, the International Atomic Energy Agency and OPEC."],["Coffee houses","Viennese coffee houses, with marble tables, newspapers and pastries such as Sachertorte and apple strudel, have been meeting places for writers, artists and thinkers for centuries. UNESCO recognized Viennese coffee house culture as intangible cultural heritage."],["Art and architecture","Vienna has grand palaces such as Schönbrunn, the Habsburg summer residence, and art from many periods, including the golden paintings of Gustav Klimt, such as The Kiss. Its social housing estates are admired around the world."],["The Alps and skiing","Austria is one of the world’s top destinations for skiing and mountain sports, and Austrian skiers are among the most successful in Winter Olympics and World Cup races. Mountain villages and Alpine traditions, such as yodeling, attract visitors year-round."]],
sp:["The Blue Danube","In 1866 Austria suffered a painful defeat in a war with Prussia, and the mood in Vienna was gloomy. The next year, the composer Johann Strauss II wrote a waltz for a choral society, originally with words, called On the Beautiful Blue Danube. At first it was only modestly successful, but in its instrumental version it soon became a worldwide hit and an unofficial anthem of Austria. Every New Year’s Day, the Vienna Philharmonic plays it as an encore at its famous concert. In the 1968 film 2001: A Space Odyssey, it accompanied spaceships gliding through space."],
g:[["Habsburg","The royal family that ruled Austria for centuries"],["Anschluss","The annexation of Austria by Nazi Germany in 1938"],["Neutrality","Not taking sides in wars"],["Waltz","A ballroom dance in three-four time"],["Alps","A mountain range in central Europe"]],
f:["The Habsburgs ruled Austria from 1278 to 1918.","Mozart was born in Salzburg.","Austria declared neutrality in 1955."],
fun:"The Blue Danube waltz accompanied spaceships in the film 2001: A Space Odyssey.",
tl:"Ang Austria ay bansa sa gitnang Europa na sakop ng Alps. Dati itong puso ng imperyong Habsburg, at sa Vienna nanirahan sina Mozart at Beethoven. Naging neutral na bansa ito mula 1955."});

ADDART({id:"finlandcountry",t:"Finland",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌲",w:"Finland",r:["swedencountry","norwaycountry","russiacountry","arctic","education","eu"],
p:[["Land of lakes","Finland has around 188,000 lakes and vast forests."],["Happiest country","Finland has repeatedly been ranked the happiest country in the world."],["Sauna nation","Finland has millions of saunas, a central part of its culture."]],
l:"Finland is a country in northern Europe, between Sweden, Norway and Russia, with about 5.6 million people. Its capital is Helsinki. Known as the land of a thousand lakes, it actually has around 188,000 lakes and is mostly covered by forest, with Lapland in the far north above the Arctic Circle. Finland was part of Sweden for centuries and then of the Russian Empire before becoming independent in 1917. It is famous for its education system, technology, design, saunas and the Sámi culture of Lapland, and it has repeatedly been ranked the happiest country in the world. In 2023 it joined NATO.",
s:[["Land","Finland is mostly low and flat, shaped by Ice Age glaciers that left thousands of lakes, islands and ridges. Forests of pine, spruce and birch cover about three-quarters of the land. In Lapland, the sun does not set for weeks in summer and barely rises in winter, when the northern lights glow."],["Swedish and Russian rule","From the Middle Ages, Finland was part of the Swedish kingdom, and Swedish remains an official language alongside Finnish. In 1809 Russia conquered Finland, which became an autonomous Grand Duchy within the Russian Empire, with its own parliament and laws."],["Independence","After the Russian Revolution, Finland declared independence on 6 December 1917. A brief civil war followed in 1918. During World War II, Finland fought the Soviet Union in the Winter War of 1939 to 1940 and the Continuation War, keeping its independence though losing territory."],["Women’s rights","In 1906 Finland became the first country in Europe to give women the right to vote and the first in the world to allow women to stand for parliament. In 1907, 19 women were elected to its parliament."]],
k:[["Capital","Helsinki"],["Population","About 5.6 million (2024)"],["Area","About 338,000 km²"],["Highest point","Halti, 1,324 m"],["Currency","Euro"]],
d:[["1809","Finland becomes part of the Russian Empire"],["1835","The national epic, the Kalevala, is published"],["1906","Finnish women gain the right to vote and stand for parliament"],["1917","Finland becomes independent"],["1939","The Winter War with the Soviet Union begins"],["1995","Finland joins the European Union"],["2023","Finland joins NATO"]],
s2:[["Education","Finland’s schools are admired worldwide. Children start formal school at seven, have short school days, little homework and few standardized tests, and teachers are highly trained and respected. Finnish students ranked near the top of international tests in the 2000s, attracting visitors from around the world to study its approach."],["Happiness","Finland has topped the World Happiness Report every year since 2018. Researchers point to trust in government and neighbors, low corruption, strong social services, work-life balance and closeness to nature, though Finns themselves often describe their happiness as quiet contentment."],["Saunas","Finland has around three million saunas for its 5.6 million people, in homes, apartment buildings, offices and even a Burger King restaurant. Finns relax in the hot steam and then cool off in a lake or snow. Finnish sauna culture is recognized by UNESCO."],["Technology and design","Finland is home to companies such as Nokia, once the world’s largest mobile phone maker, and gave the world the Linux operating system, created by Linus Torvalds in 1991, and the Angry Birds game. Finnish design, from Marimekko fabrics to Alvar Aalto’s furniture and buildings, is world-renowned."],["The Sámi","The Sámi are the Indigenous people of Lapland and the far north of Norway, Sweden and Russia, known for reindeer herding and joik singing. Finland has a Sámi Parliament, and efforts continue to protect Sámi languages and rights."],["Culture","The Kalevala, a collection of Finnish folk poetry compiled by Elias Lönnrot in 1835, inspired Finnish national identity and the composer Jean Sibelius, whose Finlandia became a patriotic anthem. Tove Jansson’s Moomins, created in 1945, are loved worldwide."]],
sp:["The Winter War","On 30 November 1939, the Soviet Union, with a far larger army, invaded Finland after the Finns refused to give up territory. Many expected Finland to fall within weeks. Instead, Finnish soldiers, dressed in white and moving swiftly on skis through snowy forests in temperatures as low as −40 °C, fought fiercely, defeating Soviet columns many times their size. Although Finland eventually had to give up about a tenth of its territory in the peace treaty of March 1940, it kept its independence. The Winter War became a symbol of Finnish sisu, a word meaning determination and courage in the face of hardship."],
g:[["Lapland","The far northern region of Finland"],["Grand Duchy","A territory ruled by a grand duke"],["Sauna","A small room or building for hot-air or steam bathing"],["Sisu","A Finnish word for determination and resilience"],["Kalevala","Finland’s national epic poem"]],
f:["Finland has around 188,000 lakes.","Finnish women were the first in the world allowed to stand for parliament.","Finland joined NATO in 2023."],
fun:"Finland has around three million saunas for 5.6 million people.",
tl:"Ang Finland ay bansa sa hilagang Europa na may humigit-kumulang 188,000 lawa. Naging malaya ito noong 1917. Kilala ito sa mahusay na sistema ng edukasyon, sauna, at sa pagiging pinakamasayang bansa sa mundo sa maraming taon."});

;
/* data/space8.js */
// Space, more articles: auroras, dwarf planets, the Andromeda Galaxy, quasars, the Artemis program and China's Tiangong space station.
ADDART({id:"aurora",t:"Auroras",c:"Astronomy",i:"🌌",w:"Aurora",r:["spaceweather","sun","magnet","atmosphere","jupiter"],
p:[["Lights in the sky","Auroras are glowing curtains of light that appear in the night sky near the poles."],["Made by the Sun","They happen when particles from the Sun strike gases high in Earth’s atmosphere."],["North and south","Near the North Pole they are called the northern lights; near the South Pole, the southern lights."]],
l:"An aurora is a natural display of colored light in the night sky, usually seen in regions close to the North and South Poles. Auroras are caused by electrically charged particles from the Sun, carried by the solar wind, which are guided by Earth’s magnetic field toward the polar regions. When these particles collide with oxygen and nitrogen in the upper atmosphere, roughly 100 to 300 kilometers up, the gases give off light, much like the glowing gas in a neon sign. The northern lights are called the aurora borealis and the southern lights the aurora australis. During strong solar storms, auroras can be seen much closer to the equator than usual.",
s:[["How auroras form","The Sun constantly sends out a stream of charged particles called the solar wind. Earth’s magnetic field shields the planet from most of them, but some are funneled along magnetic field lines toward the poles. There they crash into atoms and molecules in the upper atmosphere, giving them extra energy, which they release as light."],["The colors","The color of an aurora depends on which gas is struck and how high up. Oxygen produces the most common green glow at around 100 to 250 kilometers, and a rarer deep red higher up. Nitrogen produces blue and purple tints, often at the lower edges of the display."],["Where to see them","Auroras are seen most often inside two ring-shaped zones around the magnetic poles, covering places such as northern Norway, Finland, Iceland, Canada, Alaska and Antarctica. They are rarely seen in the tropics, including the Philippines, except during the most powerful solar storms."],["Solar storms","When the Sun releases huge clouds of charged gas, called coronal mass ejections, they can trigger geomagnetic storms on Earth. These make auroras brighter and push them toward the equator. Strong storms can also disturb satellites, radio communication, navigation systems and power grids."]],
k:[["Northern lights","Aurora borealis"],["Southern lights","Aurora australis"],["Height","About 100 to 300 km and higher"],["Most common color","Green, from oxygen"],["Cause","Charged particles from the Sun"]],
d:[["1619","Galileo Galilei uses the name aurora borealis"],["1741","Scientists in Sweden link auroras to changes in Earth’s magnetism"],["1859","The Carrington Event, a huge solar storm, brings auroras close to the tropics"],["1908","Kristian Birkeland publishes his theory that auroras are caused by particles from the Sun"],["1958","Explorer 1 discovers belts of charged particles around Earth"],["2024","The strongest solar storm in two decades makes auroras visible far from the poles"]],
s2:[["Names and legends","The word aurora comes from the Roman goddess of the dawn, and borealis from the Greek word for the north wind. Many peoples told stories about the lights. Some Sámi people of northern Europe believed they should be treated with respect and quiet, while some Inuit traditions linked them to the spirits of the dead playing in the sky."],["Unlocking the mystery","For centuries people did not know what caused auroras. In the early 1900s the Norwegian scientist Kristian Birkeland built a model Earth, a magnetized ball in a vacuum chamber, and fired electrons at it, producing glowing rings around its poles. His ideas were later confirmed by satellites that measured particles in space."],["The Carrington Event","In September 1859 an enormous solar storm struck Earth. Auroras were reported as far south as the Caribbean, and telegraph systems sparked and failed. Astronomer Richard Carrington had seen a bright flare on the Sun just before. Scientists study this event to understand how a similar storm could affect today’s technology."],["Auroras of 2024","In May 2024 a series of solar eruptions caused the strongest geomagnetic storm in about 20 years. Millions of people in places that rarely see auroras, including parts of southern Europe, the southern United States and northern India, photographed the sky glowing red and pink. Phone cameras often captured colors more clearly than the human eye could."],["Auroras on other worlds","Other planets with magnetic fields and atmospheres also have auroras. The Hubble and James Webb space telescopes have photographed glowing rings around the poles of Jupiter and Saturn, and auroras have been detected on Uranus, Neptune and even Mars, which has patchy auroras despite having no global magnetic field."],["Forecasting the lights","Space weather centers watch the Sun with satellites and warn when a solar storm is heading toward Earth, usually one to three days ahead. Aurora watchers use these forecasts, along with clear, dark skies away from city lights, to plan where and when to look."]],
sp:["Chasing the northern lights","Every winter, travelers head to places such as Tromsø in Norway, Rovaniemi in Finland and Fairbanks in Alaska hoping to see the northern lights. They need long, dark nights, clear skies and a little luck. Guides check space weather forecasts and drive away from clouds and city lights. When an aurora appears, it may start as a faint gray arc that slowly brightens into green curtains that ripple and dance across the sky. On the best nights, the whole sky can seem to pulse with light for a few minutes before fading away again."],
g:[["Aurora","A natural display of light in the sky near the poles"],["Solar wind","A stream of charged particles flowing from the Sun"],["Geomagnetic storm","A disturbance of Earth’s magnetic field caused by solar activity"],["Coronal mass ejection","A huge cloud of charged gas thrown out by the Sun"]],
f:["Auroras happen about 100 to 300 kilometers above the ground.","Green auroras are made by oxygen.","Jupiter has auroras bigger than Earth."],
fun:"Jupiter’s auroras are hundreds of times more energetic than Earth’s, and some are larger than our whole planet.",
tl:"Ang aurora ay makukulay na liwanag sa langit malapit sa North at South Pole. Nangyayari ito kapag tumatama ang mga partikulong mula sa Araw sa mga gas sa itaas na atmospera. Bihira itong makita sa Pilipinas, maliban sa napakalakas na solar storm."});

ADDART({id:"dwarfplanets",t:"Dwarf Planets",c:"Astronomy",i:"🪐",w:"Dwarf planet",r:["pluto","kuiper","asteroidbelt","solar","solarform"],
p:[["Small worlds","Dwarf planets are round worlds that orbit the Sun but are smaller than the eight planets."],["Five recognized","Astronomers officially recognize five: Ceres, Pluto, Eris, Haumea and Makemake."],["A new category","The category was created in 2006, when Pluto was reclassified."]],
l:"A dwarf planet is a body that orbits the Sun and is large enough for its own gravity to pull it into a nearly round shape, but which has not cleared other objects from the region around its orbit. The International Astronomical Union created the category in 2006, which led to Pluto being reclassified from a planet to a dwarf planet. Five bodies are officially recognized as dwarf planets: Ceres, in the asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter, and Pluto, Eris, Haumea and Makemake, which lie far beyond Neptune. Astronomers think there may be many more in the outer solar system.",
s:[["What makes a dwarf planet","Under the 2006 definition, a planet must orbit the Sun, be massive enough to be round, and have cleared its orbital neighborhood of other objects. A dwarf planet meets the first two conditions but not the third. Pluto, for example, shares its region with many other icy bodies in the Kuiper Belt."],["Ceres","Ceres is the smallest recognized dwarf planet and the largest object in the asteroid belt, about 940 kilometers across. It was discovered in 1801 by Giuseppe Piazzi and was called a planet, then an asteroid, before becoming a dwarf planet. NASA’s Dawn spacecraft orbited it from 2015 to 2018 and found bright salt deposits in its craters."],["Pluto","Pluto was discovered in 1930 by Clyde Tombaugh and was counted as the ninth planet for 76 years. It is about 2,380 kilometers across and has five known moons, the largest being Charon. In 2015 the New Horizons spacecraft flew past, revealing mountains of water ice and a huge heart-shaped plain of frozen nitrogen."],["Eris, Haumea and Makemake","Eris, found in 2005, is slightly smaller than Pluto but more massive, and its discovery led to the debate over what counts as a planet. Haumea spins so fast, once about every four hours, that it is stretched into an egg shape and has a ring. Makemake is a reddish icy world with a small moon."]],
k:[["Recognized dwarf planets","Ceres, Pluto, Eris, Haumea, Makemake"],["Category created","2006"],["Defined by","International Astronomical Union"],["Largest by size","Pluto"],["Most massive","Eris"]],
d:[["1801","Giuseppe Piazzi discovers Ceres"],["1930","Clyde Tombaugh discovers Pluto"],["2005","The discovery of Eris is announced"],["2006","The International Astronomical Union creates the dwarf planet category"],["2015","Dawn reaches Ceres and New Horizons flies past Pluto"],["2017","Astronomers discover a ring around Haumea"]],
s2:[["The great Pluto debate","The 2006 decision was controversial. Supporters said a clear definition was needed as more Pluto-sized objects were found, otherwise the solar system might soon have dozens of planets. Critics, including some planetary scientists, argued that the definition was unclear and that any round world should count as a planet. The debate continues, and some scientists still call Pluto a planet."],["Other candidates","Many other objects beyond Neptune may be dwarf planets, including Gonggong, Quaoar, Sedna and Orcus. Astronomers are not always sure whether they are round, because they are so far away and faint. Some estimates suggest there could be hundreds of dwarf planets in the outer solar system."],["Icy and active worlds","Dwarf planets were once expected to be dull, frozen balls. Instead, New Horizons found that Pluto has flowing glaciers of nitrogen ice, a hazy blue atmosphere and possibly an ocean beneath its surface. Dawn found signs that Ceres may still have pockets of salty water underground."],["Moons and rings","Most known dwarf planets have moons. Pluto has five, Haumea has two and Eris and Makemake have one each. In 2017 a ring was found around Haumea, and in 2023 astronomers reported rings around Quaoar, surprisingly far from the body itself."],["Naming dwarf planets","Dwarf planets far from the Sun are named after creation gods and figures from world mythology. Haumea is a Hawaiian goddess of childbirth, Makemake is a creator god of the Rapa Nui people of Easter Island, and Eris is the Greek goddess of strife, a fitting name for the object that sparked the debate."],["Dwarf planets and the Sun’s family","Dwarf planets help scientists understand how the solar system formed. Many are leftovers from the early disk of material around the young Sun that never grew into full planets. Studying their ice and rock gives clues to the raw ingredients from which Earth and the other planets were made."]],
sp:["The heart of Pluto","When New Horizons sent back its first close images of Pluto in July 2015, people around the world were delighted to see a huge, pale heart shape on its surface. Scientists named it Tombaugh Regio, after Pluto’s discoverer. The left side of the heart is a vast plain of frozen nitrogen, now called Sputnik Planitia, about 1,000 kilometers wide. Its surface is broken into cells that slowly churn like a pot of thick soup, as warmer ice rises from below. The heart became a symbol of public affection for the little world that had lost its planet status."],
g:[["Dwarf planet","A round body orbiting the Sun that has not cleared its orbit of other objects"],["Kuiper Belt","A region of icy bodies beyond Neptune"],["Trans-Neptunian object","Any body that orbits the Sun beyond Neptune"],["IAU","International Astronomical Union, which names and classifies objects in space"]],
f:["Ceres was the first dwarf planet discovered.","Pluto was called a planet for 76 years.","Haumea is shaped like an egg."],
fun:"Ceres has been called a planet, an asteroid and a dwarf planet at different times since its discovery in 1801.",
tl:"Ang dwarf planet ay bilog na mundo na umiikot sa Araw ngunit hindi nalinis ang paligid ng orbit nito. Lima ang opisyal na kinikilala: Ceres, Pluto, Eris, Haumea at Makemake. Nilikha ang kategoryang ito noong 2006, kaya naging dwarf planet si Pluto."});

ADDART({id:"andromeda",t:"The Andromeda Galaxy",c:"Astronomy",i:"🌀",w:"Andromeda Galaxy",r:["galaxies","milky","hubble","distances","stars"],
p:[["Our big neighbor","The Andromeda Galaxy is the nearest large galaxy to our own Milky Way."],["Seen with the eye","On dark nights it can be seen without a telescope as a faint, fuzzy patch."],["Coming closer","Andromeda is moving toward the Milky Way."]],
l:"The Andromeda Galaxy, also known as Messier 31 or M31, is a large spiral galaxy about 2.5 million light-years away, in the direction of the constellation Andromeda. It is the nearest major galaxy to the Milky Way and the largest member of the Local Group, the small cluster of galaxies to which we belong. Andromeda contains roughly a trillion stars and is one of the most distant objects that can be seen with the naked eye. In the 1920s, observations of Andromeda helped prove that the universe contains many galaxies beyond our own. The galaxy is moving toward us and may one day merge with the Milky Way.",
s:[["Size and shape","Andromeda is a spiral galaxy, a flat disk of stars, gas and dust with curving arms around a bright central bulge. Its disk is more than 150,000 light-years across, somewhat larger than the Milky Way. Its central bulge contains a supermassive black hole far heavier than the one at the center of our galaxy."],["Finding it in the sky","From a dark place, Andromeda appears as a faint oval smudge of light, best seen in the Northern Hemisphere autumn sky. From the Philippines it can be seen low in the northern sky on clear, moonless nights from about September to December. Binoculars show its bright core, while long-exposure photographs reveal its full size, wider than several full moons."],["Light from the past","Because Andromeda is about 2.5 million light-years away, the light we see left it about 2.5 million years ago, when early human ancestors were living in Africa. Looking at Andromeda is a way of looking back in time."],["The Local Group","Andromeda, the Milky Way and the smaller Triangulum Galaxy are the three largest members of the Local Group, along with dozens of small dwarf galaxies. Andromeda has its own family of satellite galaxies, including M32 and M110, which can be seen near it in telescope images."]],
k:[["Also called","Messier 31 (M31)"],["Distance","About 2.5 million light-years"],["Type","Spiral galaxy"],["Number of stars","Roughly a trillion"],["Group","Local Group"]],
d:[["964","Persian astronomer al-Sufi describes Andromeda as a little cloud"],["1764","Charles Messier lists it as object 31 in his catalog"],["1888","Isaac Roberts takes one of the first photographs showing its spiral shape"],["1923","Edwin Hubble finds a variable star in Andromeda"],["1925","Hubble’s results show Andromeda is a separate galaxy"],["2015","The Hubble Space Telescope releases a detailed panorama of Andromeda’s stars"]],
s2:[["A little cloud","The earliest known written description of Andromeda comes from the Persian astronomer Abd al-Rahman al-Sufi, who in his Book of Fixed Stars, written around 964, called it a little cloud. For centuries afterwards it was thought to be a nebula, a cloud of gas within our own galaxy."],["The great debate","In the early 1900s astronomers argued about whether spiral nebulae such as Andromeda were nearby clouds or distant island universes of stars. In 1920 this question was the subject of a famous discussion between Harlow Shapley and Heber Curtis. Shapley believed the Milky Way was essentially the whole universe, while Curtis argued that spiral nebulae were separate galaxies."],["Hubble’s discovery","In 1923 Edwin Hubble, using the large telescope at Mount Wilson in California, found a type of pulsating star called a Cepheid variable in Andromeda. Because the brightness of these stars reveals their distance, Hubble could show that Andromeda was far outside the Milky Way. The discovery showed that the universe is enormously larger than many had thought."],["Heading our way","Andromeda is moving toward the Milky Way at about 110 kilometers per second. For years astronomers predicted that the two galaxies would collide and merge in about 4.5 billion years. A 2025 study using newer measurements suggested the outcome is less certain, giving roughly even odds of a merger within the next 10 billion years."],["A gentle collision","Even if the galaxies do merge, individual stars would be very unlikely to collide, because the space between them is so vast. Instead, gravity would slowly reshape both spirals into one large galaxy, sometimes nicknamed Milkomeda. The Sun and its planets would most likely survive, flung into a new orbit."],["A history written in stars","Telescopes have found streams of stars around Andromeda, the remains of smaller galaxies it has pulled apart and swallowed. These streams show that large galaxies grow by absorbing smaller ones, a process that also shaped the Milky Way."]],
sp:["Seeing a galaxy with your own eyes","Most galaxies can only be seen with large telescopes, but Andromeda is a rare exception. On a clear, dark night far from city lights, observers can find it by first locating the bright W shape of the constellation Cassiopeia, or the great square of Pegasus, and then looking for a faint, elongated glow. It does not look like the colorful photographs, just a soft gray smudge, but the experience is unforgettable. The faint light reaching the eye has been traveling through space for about 2.5 million years, and comes from a trillion distant suns."],
g:[["Galaxy","A huge system of stars, gas, dust and dark matter held together by gravity"],["Spiral galaxy","A galaxy with a flat disk and curving arms"],["Light-year","The distance light travels in a year, about 9.5 trillion km"],["Cepheid variable","A pulsating star whose brightness reveals its distance"]],
f:["Andromeda is about 2.5 million light-years away.","It contains roughly a trillion stars.","It can be seen without a telescope on dark nights."],
fun:"The light from Andromeda that reaches your eyes tonight began its journey about 2.5 million years ago.",
tl:"Ang Andromeda Galaxy ang pinakamalapit na malaking galaxy sa ating Milky Way, mga 2.5 milyong light-year ang layo. Makikita ito nang walang teleskopyo sa madilim na gabi bilang malabong liwanag. Gumagalaw ito papalapit sa Milky Way."});

ADDART({id:"quasars",t:"Quasars",c:"Astronomy",i:"💫",w:"Quasar",r:["bh","galaxies","bigbang","distances","scope"],
p:[["Brightest objects","Quasars are among the brightest objects in the universe."],["Black hole power","They are powered by gas falling into giant black holes at the centers of galaxies."],["Far away","Most quasars are billions of light-years away, so we see them as they were long ago."]],
l:"A quasar is an extremely bright and distant object at the center of a galaxy, powered by a supermassive black hole that is swallowing large amounts of gas. As the gas spirals inward, it forms a swirling disk that heats up to enormous temperatures and shines more brightly than all the stars in its galaxy put together. Quasars were discovered in the early 1960s, when astronomers found star-like objects giving off strong radio waves at surprisingly great distances. The name is short for quasi-stellar radio source, meaning a source that looks like a star. Because they are so far away, quasars give astronomers a view of the early universe.",
s:[["What powers a quasar","At the heart of every quasar is a supermassive black hole, millions to billions of times the mass of the Sun. Gas, dust and stars drawn toward it form an accretion disk. Friction and gravity heat the disk to millions of degrees, releasing vast amounts of light, X-rays and other radiation before the material disappears into the black hole."],["How bright they are","A bright quasar can give off thousands of times more light than the entire Milky Way, all from a region not much larger than our solar system. Some quasars also shoot out narrow jets of particles at nearly the speed of light, stretching far beyond their galaxies."],["Discovery","In 1963 the Dutch-born astronomer Maarten Schmidt studied the light of a star-like radio source called 3C 273. He realized that its spectrum was strongly shifted toward red, meaning it was about two billion light-years away. To appear so bright from so far, it had to be incredibly powerful."],["Windows on the early universe","Light from distant quasars has traveled for billions of years, so astronomers see them as they were when the universe was young. Some known quasars existed less than a billion years after the Big Bang, which raises the question of how their black holes grew so large so quickly."]],
k:[["Full name","Quasi-stellar radio source"],["Power source","Supermassive black hole"],["Discovered","1963"],["First studied in detail","3C 273"],["Brightness","Can outshine whole galaxies"]],
d:[["1963","Maarten Schmidt shows that 3C 273 is extremely far away"],["1964","The term quasar is first used"],["1979","The first gravitationally lensed quasar is discovered"],["1990s","The Hubble Space Telescope shows quasars sit in the centers of galaxies"],["2021","A quasar from about 670 million years after the Big Bang is reported"],["2024","Astronomers report J0529-4351, the brightest quasar known"]],
s2:[["Active galaxies","Quasars belong to a larger family called active galactic nuclei, the bright centers of galaxies with feeding black holes. Others include Seyfert galaxies and blazars. Many astronomers think these may be similar objects seen from different angles; a blazar, for example, may be a quasar whose jet points almost straight at Earth."],["Quasars and galaxy growth","Quasars appear to have been most common about 10 billion years ago. The energy they released may have heated and pushed gas out of their galaxies, slowing the birth of new stars. This suggests black holes and their host galaxies grow together and influence each other."],["Cosmic flashlights","As quasar light travels across the universe, it passes through clouds of gas that absorb certain colors. By studying these absorption lines, astronomers can map invisible gas between galaxies and learn how the universe changed over time."],["Gravitational lenses","Sometimes a massive galaxy lies between Earth and a distant quasar. Its gravity bends the quasar’s light, producing two or more images of the same quasar, an effect predicted by Albert Einstein’s theory of general relativity. The first such lensed quasar, called the Twin Quasar, was found in 1979."],["The brightest of all","In 2024 astronomers reported that a quasar called J0529-4351 is the most luminous object known in the universe. Its black hole was estimated to be about 17 billion times the mass of the Sun and to be swallowing the equivalent of about one Sun every day."],["Our galaxy’s quiet black hole","The Milky Way has its own supermassive black hole, Sagittarius A*, but it is very quiet compared with a quasar because little gas is falling into it. Astronomers think that most large galaxies, including ours, may have gone through quasar-like phases in the distant past."]],
sp:["The puzzle of 3C 273","In the early 1960s astronomers using radio telescopes found many sources of radio waves in the sky, and some matched faint objects that looked like ordinary stars. Their light, however, showed strange lines that no one could identify. In 1963 Maarten Schmidt looked again at the spectrum of 3C 273 and suddenly recognized familiar lines of hydrogen, shifted far toward the red end of the spectrum. The shift meant the object was racing away from us as the universe expanded, and must be about two billion light-years away. Schmidt later recalled being stunned; the little star was one of the most powerful objects ever seen."],
g:[["Quasar","A very bright galaxy center powered by a feeding supermassive black hole"],["Accretion disk","A swirling disk of hot gas falling into a black hole"],["Redshift","The stretching of light toward red as an object moves away"],["Active galactic nucleus","A galaxy center giving off huge amounts of energy"]],
f:["The word quasar comes from quasi-stellar radio source.","A quasar can outshine thousands of galaxies like ours.","Quasars are powered by supermassive black holes."],
fun:"The brightest known quasar’s black hole is thought to swallow the mass of about one Sun every day.",
tl:"Ang quasar ay napakaliwanag na gitna ng malayong galaxy na pinapagana ng napakalaking black hole na lumalamon ng gas. Mas maliwanag ito kaysa sa lahat ng bituin sa galaxy nito. Natuklasan ang mga quasar noong dekada 1960."});

ADDART({id:"artemis",t:"The Artemis Program",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌙",w:"Artemis program",r:["apollo","moon","rocket","spaceagencies","astronauts"],
p:[["Back to the Moon","Artemis is NASA’s program to send astronauts back to the Moon."],["The first crew","In April 2026 four astronauts flew around the Moon, the first crewed lunar voyage since 1972."],["Partners","Space agencies and companies from several countries take part."]],
l:"The Artemis program is a human spaceflight program led by NASA, the United States space agency, to return astronauts to the Moon for the first time since the Apollo missions and to prepare for later journeys to Mars. It uses the Space Launch System rocket and the Orion spacecraft, with contributions from the European, Canadian and Japanese space agencies and from private companies. The uncrewed Artemis I mission orbited the Moon in 2022. In April 2026 Artemis II carried four astronauts around the Moon and back, the first people to travel to the Moon’s vicinity in more than 50 years. Later missions aim to land astronauts near the Moon’s south pole.",
s:[["Why go back","Supporters say returning to the Moon will help scientists study its history and resources, test technology for living in deep space, inspire young people and keep nations working together. Water ice found in shadowed craters near the Moon’s south pole could one day supply drinking water, air and rocket fuel."],["The hardware","The Space Launch System, or SLS, is a very powerful rocket built for NASA. The Orion capsule carries up to four astronauts, while its service module, built in Europe, provides power and propulsion. For landings, NASA has contracted private companies, SpaceX and Blue Origin, to develop lunar landers."],["Artemis I","On 16 November 2022 the first SLS rocket launched an uncrewed Orion on a 25-day test flight. The capsule traveled around the Moon, farther from Earth than any spacecraft built for humans had gone before, and splashed down in the Pacific Ocean on 11 December."],["Artemis II","On 1 April 2026 Artemis II launched from Florida carrying NASA astronauts Reid Wiseman, Victor Glover and Christina Koch and Canadian astronaut Jeremy Hansen. During a mission of about ten days they flew around the far side of the Moon and returned to splash down in the Pacific Ocean off California on 10 April."]],
k:[["Led by","NASA"],["Rocket","Space Launch System (SLS)"],["Spacecraft","Orion"],["First crewed flight","Artemis II, April 2026"],["Named after","Artemis, twin sister of Apollo in Greek myth"]],
d:[["2017","The United States directs NASA to return astronauts to the Moon"],["2019","The program is named Artemis"],["2020","The Artemis Accords on peaceful space cooperation are first signed"],["2022","Artemis I flies an uncrewed Orion around the Moon"],["2023","The crew of Artemis II is announced"],["2026","Artemis II carries four astronauts around the Moon"]],
s2:[["A crew of firsts","The Artemis II crew included Christina Koch, the first woman to travel to the Moon’s vicinity, Victor Glover, the first Black astronaut to do so, and Jeremy Hansen, the first non-American. Reid Wiseman commanded the mission. During their flyby they saw parts of the Moon’s far side with their own eyes and photographed Earth setting behind the lunar horizon."],["International partners","The European Space Agency builds Orion’s service module, Canada provides astronauts and a robotic arm for a planned lunar station, and Japan contributes technology and astronauts. Many countries have also signed the Artemis Accords, a set of principles for peaceful and transparent exploration of the Moon and beyond."],["The lunar Gateway","NASA and its partners have planned a small space station called the Gateway to orbit the Moon, serving as a meeting point between Orion and lunar landers. Plans for the Gateway and other parts of the program have been reviewed and changed several times as budgets and priorities shifted."],["Costs and criticism","Artemis has faced criticism for delays and high costs. The SLS rocket, in particular, is expensive and cannot be reused. Some experts argue that cheaper, reusable commercial rockets should play a bigger role, while supporters say SLS provides a proven, government-controlled way to launch astronauts. The balance between NASA and private companies remains debated."],["A new space race?","China plans to land its own astronauts on the Moon by 2030 and is building rockets, spacecraft and landers for that goal. Some observers describe a new space race between the United States and China, while others emphasize scientific cooperation and the growing number of countries sending robotic missions to the Moon, including India and Japan."],["Why the south pole","The planned landing area near the Moon’s south pole has craters that never see sunlight, where water ice has survived for billions of years. Nearby peaks receive almost constant sunlight, useful for solar power. No humans have visited the region before; all Apollo landings were near the Moon’s equator."]],
sp:["The view from Orion","For the crew of Artemis II, one of the most memorable moments came as Orion swung around the Moon. The gray, cratered surface filled their windows, including regions of the far side that no human had ever seen directly. Then, as the spacecraft continued, the blue and white Earth appeared to sink below the lunar horizon, recalling the famous Earthrise photograph taken by the Apollo 8 astronauts in 1968. Millions of people followed the mission online and on television, and many schools around the world held viewing events for the launch and splashdown."],
g:[["Artemis program","NASA’s program to return humans to the Moon"],["SLS","Space Launch System, a powerful rocket built for NASA"],["Orion","NASA’s spacecraft for carrying astronauts into deep space"],["Artemis Accords","Principles for peaceful exploration of the Moon signed by many countries"]],
f:["Artemis II launched on 1 April 2026.","Its crew of four flew around the Moon.","Artemis was the twin sister of Apollo in Greek myth."],
fun:"The program is named after Artemis, the Greek goddess of the Moon and twin sister of Apollo, whose name was given to the first Moon landing program.",
tl:"Ang Artemis ay programa ng NASA para muling magpadala ng mga astronaut sa Buwan. Noong Abril 2026, apat na astronaut ng Artemis II ang lumipad paikot sa Buwan, ang unang ganitong paglalakbay mula 1972. Layunin ng susunod na misyon na lumapag malapit sa south pole ng Buwan."});

ADDART({id:"tiangong",t:"Tiangong Space Station",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛰️",w:"Tiangong space station",r:["iss","spaceagencies","astronauts","rocket","china"],
p:[["China’s station","Tiangong is a space station built and operated by China."],["Heavenly palace","Its name means heavenly palace in Chinese."],["Crews of three","Chinese astronauts live and work on board in crews of three."]],
l:"Tiangong, meaning heavenly palace, is a space station in low Earth orbit built and operated by the China Manned Space Agency. Its first module, Tianhe, was launched in April 2021, and the station was completed at the end of 2022 with the addition of two laboratory modules, Wentian and Mengtian. Tiangong orbits about 400 kilometers above Earth and is permanently crewed by teams of three astronauts, often called taikonauts, who usually stay for about six months. The station is smaller than the International Space Station and is used for scientific experiments, technology tests and preparing for China’s plans to send astronauts to the Moon.",
s:[["Building the station","China built Tiangong in stages. The core module Tianhe, which contains living quarters and controls, was launched on 29 April 2021. The Wentian laboratory module followed in July 2022 and Mengtian in October 2022, forming a T-shaped station with a mass of roughly 100 tonnes, about a quarter that of the International Space Station."],["Getting there","Astronauts travel to Tiangong aboard Shenzhou spacecraft, launched by Long March rockets from the Jiuquan launch center in the Gobi Desert. Supplies, fuel and equipment are delivered by robotic Tianzhou cargo ships. Crews overlap briefly during handovers before the departing team returns to land in Inner Mongolia."],["Life on board","Taikonauts carry out experiments, exercise daily to protect their muscles and bones, maintain equipment and sometimes go on spacewalks. They have given science lessons broadcast from orbit to millions of schoolchildren in China, showing how water, flames and objects behave in weightlessness."],["Science in orbit","Tiangong hosts experiments in biology, materials, physics and medicine. Scientists have grown rice and other plants from seed to seed on board, tested new types of fuel and studied how living cells respond to space. Experiments from other countries have also been selected through the United Nations."]],
k:[["Operated by","China Manned Space Agency"],["Name meaning","Heavenly palace"],["First module launched","29 April 2021"],["Completed","2022"],["Crew","Usually three"]],
d:[["2003","Yang Liwei becomes China’s first astronaut"],["2011","The test station Tiangong-1 is launched"],["2016","Tiangong-2, a second test station, is launched"],["2021","The core module Tianhe is launched"],["2022","The Wentian and Mengtian modules complete the station"],["2023","Gui Haichao becomes China’s first civilian in space"]],
s2:[["China’s road to space","China’s human spaceflight program began in 1992. In 2003 Yang Liwei orbited Earth aboard Shenzhou 5, making China the third country, after the Soviet Union and the United States, to launch a person into space on its own. Later missions added spacewalks and docking with small test stations, Tiangong-1 and Tiangong-2."],["Why China built its own station","Chinese astronauts have not taken part in the International Space Station. A United States law passed in 2011 restricts NASA from working directly with China’s space program, citing security concerns. China responded by developing its own capabilities. Chinese officials have said Tiangong is open to cooperation with other countries."],["International cooperation","China has invited foreign scientists to send experiments to Tiangong and has trained with astronauts from other countries. It has announced plans to fly an astronaut from Pakistan to the station. Supporters of cooperation see space as a place for nations to work together, while others point to rivalry between China and the United States."],["Tiangong and the ISS","The International Space Station, a partnership of the United States, Russia, Europe, Japan and Canada, has been crewed continuously since 2000 but is expected to be retired around 2030. If that happens, Tiangong may for a time be one of the few space stations in orbit, alongside planned commercial stations."],["Space debris concerns","Tiangong has had to maneuver to avoid pieces of space debris and satellites. In 2021 China told the United Nations that the station had made avoidance maneuvers to steer clear of satellites. The incident highlighted the growing problem of crowded orbits."],["Toward the Moon","Tiangong supports China’s plan to land astronauts on the Moon by 2030. Astronauts on long missions help scientists learn how the human body copes with months in space, while engineers test life-support systems and spacecraft that could be used for lunar journeys."]],
sp:["A classroom in the sky","On several occasions, taikonauts aboard Tiangong have given live science lessons to students on Earth. Floating in front of the camera, they showed how a ball of water wobbles without breaking, how a tablet dropped into water fizzes into a glowing sphere, and how a candle flame becomes a round blue ball in weightlessness. Children in classrooms across China watched and asked questions over a live link. The lessons, called Tiangong Classroom, were designed to spark interest in science and space among the next generation."],
g:[["Space station","A large spacecraft where astronauts live and work for long periods"],["Taikonaut","A common nickname for a Chinese astronaut"],["Module","A section of a space station launched separately"],["Low Earth orbit","An orbit from about 160 to 2,000 km above Earth"]],
f:["Tiangong means heavenly palace.","Its first module was launched in 2021.","Its crews usually stay about six months."],
fun:"Astronauts aboard Tiangong have grown rice in space from seed all the way to new seeds.",
tl:"Ang Tiangong ay space station na itinayo at pinapatakbo ng China. Ang pangalan nito ay nangangahulugang palasyo sa langit. Tatlong astronaut ang karaniwang nakatira dito nang mga anim na buwan."});

;
/* data/body5.js */
// The Human Body, more articles: muscles, the lymphatic system, stress, rabies and measles.
ADDART({id:"muscles",t:"Muscles",c:"Human Body",i:"💪",w:"Muscle",r:["bones","heart","exercise","nervous","nutri"],
p:[["Moving the body","Muscles let us move, from running and lifting to blinking and smiling."],["Three kinds","The body has skeletal, smooth and cardiac muscle."],["Pulling power","Muscles can only pull, so they often work in pairs."]],
l:"Muscles are soft tissues that can contract, or shorten, to produce movement. The human body has more than 600 skeletal muscles, which are attached to bones and controlled by choice, as well as smooth muscle in the walls of organs such as the stomach and blood vessels, and cardiac muscle, which makes up the heart. Muscles make up a large share of body weight, about a third or more in many adults. Besides moving the body, they keep it upright, protect organs, move food through the gut and produce heat. Regular exercise and a healthy diet help keep muscles strong throughout life.",
s:[["Skeletal muscle","Skeletal muscles are attached to bones by tough cords called tendons. They are under voluntary control, meaning we decide when to move them. Under a microscope they look striped. They range from large muscles in the thighs and buttocks to tiny muscles that move the eyes."],["Smooth muscle","Smooth muscle lines hollow organs such as the stomach, intestines, bladder and blood vessels. It works automatically, without our thinking about it, squeezing food along the gut, controlling blood flow and adjusting the size of the pupils."],["Cardiac muscle","Cardiac muscle is found only in the heart. It contracts rhythmically about 60 to 100 times a minute at rest, every minute of a person’s life, without tiring. Its cells are linked so that electrical signals spread quickly and make the heart beat in a coordinated way."],["Working in pairs","A muscle can pull but cannot push. To bend and straighten a joint, muscles work in opposing pairs. When the biceps on the front of the upper arm contracts, the elbow bends; when the triceps on the back contracts, the arm straightens."]],
k:[["Skeletal muscles","More than 600"],["Types","Skeletal, smooth, cardiac"],["Largest muscle","Gluteus maximus (buttock)"],["Smallest muscle","Stapedius (in the ear)"],["Muscle proteins","Actin and myosin"]],
d:[["c. 170","The Greek physician Galen describes muscles and their actions"],["1543","Andreas Vesalius publishes detailed drawings of human muscles"],["1791","Luigi Galvani shows that electricity makes frog muscles twitch"],["1864","Wilhelm Kühne isolates the muscle protein myosin"],["1954","The sliding filament theory of muscle contraction is proposed"],["1986","Scientists identify the gene behind Duchenne muscular dystrophy"]],
s2:[["How a muscle contracts","Each muscle is made of bundles of long cells called muscle fibers. Inside them, thin strands of the proteins actin and myosin overlap. When a nerve signal arrives, myosin pulls on actin, sliding the strands past each other so the fiber shortens. This explanation, the sliding filament theory, was proposed in 1954 by two research teams in Britain."],["Fuel for muscles","Muscles run on a molecule called ATP, made from glucose and fat using oxygen. During short bursts of hard effort, muscles can make energy without enough oxygen, but this cannot last long. Fit muscles have more blood vessels and energy-producing mitochondria, so they tire less easily."],["Building strength","When muscles are worked harder than usual, as in lifting weights or climbing, tiny fibers are strained and then rebuilt thicker and stronger. Protein from food, such as fish, eggs, beans and meat, supplies the building blocks, while rest and sleep give muscles time to repair."],["Cramps and strains","A cramp is a sudden, painful tightening of a muscle that will not relax, often after exercise or in hot weather. A strain, or pulled muscle, is a tear in muscle fibers caused by overstretching. Gentle stretching, warming up before activity and drinking enough water help prevent both."],["Muscles and aging","After about the age of 30, people slowly lose muscle mass and strength unless they stay active. In older adults, severe muscle loss, called sarcopenia, can lead to falls and loss of independence. Strength exercises, even simple ones such as standing up from a chair, help older people stay strong."],["Muscle diseases","Muscular dystrophies are inherited diseases in which muscles gradually weaken. Duchenne muscular dystrophy, the most common severe form in children, mainly affects boys. Other conditions, such as myasthenia gravis, interfere with the signals between nerves and muscles. Researchers are testing gene therapies and new medicines for these diseases."]],
sp:["The muscles of a smile","Every expression on the human face is made by small muscles attached to the skin rather than to bones. A genuine smile uses muscles that lift the corners of the mouth and others that crinkle the skin around the eyes, while a polite, forced smile often moves only the mouth. The French doctor Guillaume Duchenne studied facial expressions in the 1800s by stimulating face muscles with electricity, and the true, eye-crinkling smile is still called the Duchenne smile. Facial muscles also help us chew, speak, blink and protect the eyes."],
g:[["Tendon","A tough cord that attaches muscle to bone"],["Muscle fiber","A long cell that makes up muscle"],["Contraction","The shortening of a muscle"],["Sarcopenia","Loss of muscle mass and strength with age"]],
f:["The body has more than 600 skeletal muscles.","The heart is made of cardiac muscle.","Muscles can only pull, never push."],
fun:"The smallest muscle in the body, the stapedius in the middle ear, is only about a millimeter long.",
tl:"Ang kalamnan ay tisyu na umiikli upang makagalaw ang katawan. Mayroon tayong mahigit 600 skeletal muscle, pati smooth muscle sa mga organo at cardiac muscle sa puso. Pinapalakas ang kalamnan ng ehersisyo at wastong pagkain."});

ADDART({id:"lymphatic",t:"The Lymphatic System",c:"Human Body",i:"🫧",w:"Lymphatic system",r:["immune","blood","heart","cancer","digest"],
p:[["The body’s drainage","The lymphatic system collects extra fluid from tissues and returns it to the blood."],["Fighting germs","Its lymph nodes filter out germs and help the immune system."],["Nodes and organs","It includes lymph vessels, lymph nodes, the spleen, the thymus and the tonsils."]],
l:"The lymphatic system is a network of thin vessels, small organs called lymph nodes, and other tissues that work alongside the blood circulation. Each day, fluid leaks out of tiny blood vessels into the body’s tissues. The lymphatic system collects this fluid, now called lymph, filters it through lymph nodes, where white blood cells trap and destroy germs, and returns it to the bloodstream near the heart. The system also absorbs fats from the intestines and is a key part of the immune system. Its main parts include lymph vessels, several hundred lymph nodes, the spleen, the thymus, the tonsils and patches of tissue in the gut.",
s:[["Lymph and lymph vessels","Lymph is a clear or milky fluid containing water, proteins, fats and white blood cells. It travels through lymph vessels that branch through almost every part of the body. Unlike blood, lymph has no heart to pump it; it is pushed along by the squeezing of nearby muscles and by breathing, while tiny valves keep it flowing in one direction."],["Lymph nodes","Lymph nodes are small, bean-shaped organs found in clusters in the neck, armpits, groin, chest and abdomen. They act like filters. White blood cells inside them recognize germs and abnormal cells and begin an immune response. When fighting an infection, nearby nodes may swell and feel tender, often called swollen glands."],["The spleen and thymus","The spleen, under the ribs on the left side, filters blood, removes old red blood cells and stores white blood cells. The thymus, in the chest behind the breastbone, is where a type of white blood cell called the T cell matures; it is largest in childhood and shrinks after puberty."],["Absorbing fats","In the lining of the small intestine are tiny lymph vessels called lacteals. They absorb fats from digested food, which turn the lymph milky white. This fatty lymph is then carried to the bloodstream."]],
k:[["Fluid","Lymph"],["Main organs","Lymph nodes, spleen, thymus, tonsils"],["Number of lymph nodes","Several hundred"],["Key cells","Lymphocytes (white blood cells)"],["Returns lymph to blood","Near the heart"]],
d:[["1622","Gaspare Aselli describes milky lymph vessels in the gut of a dog"],["1651","Jean Pecquet describes the main lymph duct"],["1653","Olof Rudbeck and Thomas Bartholin publish descriptions of the lymphatic system"],["1832","Thomas Hodgkin describes the lymph node cancer later named after him"],["1961","The role of the thymus in immunity is discovered"],["2015","Researchers report lymph vessels in the coverings of the brain"]],
s2:[["Discovering the system","For centuries doctors knew about blood vessels but not lymph vessels. In 1622 the Italian physician Gaspare Aselli noticed white, milky threads in the intestines of a dog that had recently eaten. Over the following decades scientists including Jean Pecquet, Olof Rudbeck and Thomas Bartholin traced the network and described its connection to the blood."],["Swollen nodes","Lymph nodes often swell during common infections such as colds, sore throats and ear infections, and return to normal when the illness passes. Nodes that stay swollen for weeks, feel hard or grow without explanation should be checked by a doctor, because they can occasionally be a sign of more serious illness."],["Lymphoma","Lymphoma is a cancer of lymphocytes, the white blood cells of the lymphatic system. The two main groups are Hodgkin lymphoma and non-Hodgkin lymphoma. Many types can be treated successfully with chemotherapy, radiation and newer immune-based therapies."],["Lymphatic filariasis","Lymphatic filariasis, sometimes called elephantiasis, is caused by tiny worms spread by mosquitoes. The worms damage lymph vessels, which can lead to severe swelling of the legs and other body parts. The Philippines and many other countries have run mass drug programs to eliminate the disease, and many provinces have been declared free of it."],["Lymphedema","When lymph vessels or nodes are damaged or removed, for example after cancer surgery, lymph can build up and cause long-lasting swelling called lymphedema. Treatment includes special massage, compression garments, exercise and careful skin care."],["Brain drainage","Scientists long believed the brain had no lymph vessels. In 2015 researchers reported finding lymph vessels in the meninges, the protective layers around the brain. The discovery has led to new research on how the brain clears waste and how this might relate to diseases such as Alzheimer’s."]],
sp:["A hidden river","Most people know that blood circulates through the body, but few think about the second, quieter system flowing beside it. Every day, a few liters of fluid seep out of the blood into the spaces between cells. If it were not drained away, the body would swell like a sponge. The lymphatic system acts as a hidden river, gathering this fluid drop by drop, cleaning it as it passes through hundreds of lymph nodes, and pouring it back into large veins near the neck. Without it, the body could not keep its fluids balanced or fight off infections effectively."],
g:[["Lymph","Clear fluid that flows through lymph vessels"],["Lymph node","A small organ that filters lymph and helps fight infection"],["Lymphocyte","A type of white blood cell that fights infection"],["Lacteal","A lymph vessel in the small intestine that absorbs fat"]],
f:["The body has several hundred lymph nodes.","Swollen glands are usually lymph nodes fighting infection.","Lymph has no heart to pump it."],
fun:"Lymph turns milky white after a fatty meal because of the fat it carries from the intestines.",
tl:"Ang lymphatic system ay network ng mga sisidlan at lymph node na nag-iipon ng sobrang likido sa katawan at ibinabalik ito sa dugo. Sinasala ng mga lymph node ang mikrobyo at tumutulong sa immune system. Kasama rito ang spleen, thymus at tonsil."});

ADDART({id:"stress",t:"Stress",c:"Health",i:"😮‍💨",w:"Stress (biology)",r:["mental","hormone","sleep","exercise","emotions"],
p:[["The body’s alarm","Stress is the body’s response to a challenge or threat."],["Helpful and harmful","Short bursts of stress can help us, but long-lasting stress can harm health."],["Coping","Sleep, exercise, talking to others and relaxing help people cope."]],
l:"Stress is the body’s natural response to pressure, danger or change. When a person faces a challenge, such as an exam, an argument or an emergency, the brain triggers the release of hormones such as adrenaline and cortisol. The heart beats faster, breathing quickens and muscles tense, preparing the body to act. This reaction, often called fight or flight, can be helpful in short bursts, sharpening attention and giving energy. When stress continues for a long time, however, it can affect sleep, mood, digestion, the immune system and the heart. Learning healthy ways to manage stress is an important part of physical and mental health.",
s:[["Fight or flight","When the brain senses a threat, a region called the hypothalamus signals the adrenal glands above the kidneys to release adrenaline. Within seconds the heart speeds up, blood pressure rises, breathing becomes faster and sugar is released into the blood for energy. The American physiologist Walter Cannon described this as the fight-or-flight response in 1915."],["Cortisol","If stress continues, the body releases cortisol, sometimes called the stress hormone. Cortisol helps keep energy available and controls inflammation. Over long periods, however, high cortisol levels are linked to poor sleep, weight gain, weaker immunity and difficulty concentrating."],["Good and bad stress","Not all stress is harmful. Moderate, short-term stress, sometimes called eustress, can motivate people to study, perform or meet a deadline. Stress becomes harmful when it is intense, constant or feels impossible to control, a condition called chronic stress."],["Signs of stress","Stress can show up as headaches, stomachaches, tight muscles, trouble sleeping, tiredness, irritability, worry, sadness or changes in appetite. Young people may notice difficulty concentrating in class or losing interest in activities they usually enjoy."]],
k:[["Main stress hormones","Adrenaline and cortisol"],["Fight-or-flight described","Walter Cannon, 1915"],["Stress research pioneer","Hans Selye"],["Helpful stress","Eustress"],["Long-lasting stress","Chronic stress"]],
d:[["1915","Walter Cannon describes the fight-or-flight response"],["1936","Hans Selye publishes his first work on the body’s response to stress"],["1967","The Holmes and Rahe scale ranks stressful life events"],["1980","Post-traumatic stress disorder is formally recognized"],["2018","The Philippines passes the Mental Health Act"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic raises stress levels worldwide"]],
s2:[["Hans Selye","The Hungarian-Canadian scientist Hans Selye spent decades studying how the body reacts to strain. In 1936 he described a pattern he later called the general adaptation syndrome, with stages of alarm, resistance and exhaustion. He helped make stress a common word in medicine and everyday language."],["Stress and the body","Long-term stress has been linked to high blood pressure, heart disease, stomach problems, headaches and a greater chance of catching infections. It can also make existing conditions, such as asthma or skin problems, worse. Researchers continue to study exactly how stress affects different organs."],["Stress and the mind","Ongoing stress can contribute to anxiety and depression. After very frightening events such as disasters, violence or accidents, some people develop post-traumatic stress disorder, with flashbacks, nightmares and strong fear. Support and treatment from trained professionals can help people recover."],["Healthy ways to cope","Helpful habits include getting enough sleep, being physically active, eating regular meals, spending time with friends and family, and taking breaks from screens. Slow breathing, prayer, meditation, music, hobbies and time outdoors can calm the body’s alarm system. Breaking big tasks into smaller steps can make them feel less overwhelming."],["Talking about it","Sharing worries with a trusted parent, teacher, friend, counselor or health worker can ease stress and help find solutions. In the Philippines, the Mental Health Act of 2018 aims to make mental health services more available, and the National Center for Mental Health runs a crisis hotline. Many schools also have guidance counselors."],["Stress in modern life","Surveys in many countries report high levels of stress linked to work, money, school, family responsibilities and constant digital connection. Long commutes, crowded cities and natural disasters add pressure in places such as Metro Manila. Some employers and schools now offer wellness programs, flexible hours or mental health days."]],
sp:["The five-minute calm-down","When stress builds up, simple techniques can help the body switch off its alarm. One popular method is box breathing: breathe in slowly for a count of four, hold for four, breathe out for four, and hold again for four, repeating several times. Another is the five senses exercise, in which a person names five things they can see, four they can touch, three they can hear, two they can smell and one they can taste. These techniques slow the heart rate and bring attention back to the present moment. They are used by students before exams, athletes before competitions and even soldiers and astronauts."],
g:[["Stress","The body’s response to pressure or threat"],["Adrenaline","A hormone that prepares the body for action"],["Cortisol","A hormone released during longer periods of stress"],["Chronic stress","Stress that lasts a long time"]],
f:["Adrenaline makes the heart beat faster.","Some stress can be helpful.","Talking to someone can reduce stress."],
fun:"The word stress was borrowed from engineering, where it describes the force acting on a material such as a metal beam.",
tl:"Ang stress ay natural na tugon ng katawan sa presyon o panganib. Nakatutulong ito kung panandalian, ngunit nakasasama kung matagal. Nakatutulong ang sapat na tulog, ehersisyo at pakikipag-usap sa ibang tao upang makayanan ito."});

ADDART({id:"rabies",t:"Rabies",c:"Disease and Medicine",i:"🐕",w:"Rabies",r:["vacc","pasteur","dogs","immune","firstaid"],
p:[["A deadly virus","Rabies is a viral disease that attacks the brain and is almost always fatal once symptoms begin."],["Spread by bites","Most human cases come from the bites of infected dogs."],["Preventable","Rabies can be prevented with prompt wound washing and vaccines."]],
l:"Rabies is a disease caused by a virus that infects the brain and nervous system of mammals, including humans. It is usually spread through the saliva of an infected animal, most often by a bite or scratch. In countries such as the Philippines, dogs cause almost all human cases. Once symptoms such as fever, confusion, fear of water and paralysis appear, rabies is almost always fatal. Yet it is entirely preventable: washing a wound immediately with soap and water and getting vaccines quickly after exposure can save a person’s life. Vaccinating dogs is the most effective way to stop the disease at its source.",
s:[["How rabies spreads","The rabies virus lives in the saliva of infected animals. It enters the body through a bite, a scratch or contact with broken skin or the eyes, nose or mouth. It then travels slowly along nerves to the brain, a journey that can take weeks or months. This delay gives time for vaccines to work if treatment starts early."],["Symptoms","Early signs include fever, headache and tingling or pain at the bite site. As the virus reaches the brain, people may become anxious, confused and agitated, have difficulty swallowing and develop a fear of water, called hydrophobia. Paralysis and coma follow. Infected animals may become unusually aggressive or unusually quiet and drool heavily."],["What to do after a bite","Anyone bitten or scratched by a dog, cat or other mammal should immediately wash the wound with soap and running water for about 15 minutes and go to a health center or animal bite treatment center. Doctors decide whether a series of rabies vaccines, and sometimes rabies antibodies, is needed. Home remedies such as applying garlic or having the wound sucked do not prevent rabies."],["A worldwide problem","The World Health Organization estimates that rabies kills tens of thousands of people every year, most of them in Asia and Africa and many of them children. Nearly all deaths could be prevented with vaccination of dogs and timely treatment of bite victims."]],
k:[["Cause","Rabies virus"],["Main source in humans","Dog bites"],["Fatality","Almost always fatal once symptoms appear"],["First vaccine","Louis Pasteur, 1885"],["World Rabies Day","28 September"]],
d:[["c. 1930 BC","The Laws of Eshnunna in Mesopotamia mention dogs that spread a deadly disease"],["1885","Louis Pasteur gives the first rabies vaccine to Joseph Meister"],["2004","A teenager in the United States survives rabies without vaccination"],["2007","The Philippines passes the Anti-Rabies Act"],["2007","The first World Rabies Day is held"],["2015","Global partners set a goal of ending dog-transmitted rabies deaths by 2030"]],
s2:[["Pasteur’s gamble","In July 1885 a nine-year-old boy named Joseph Meister was badly bitten by a rabid dog in France. His mother brought him to the scientist Louis Pasteur, who had been developing a rabies vaccine using dried spinal cord tissue from infected rabbits. Pasteur, who was not a medical doctor, gave the boy a series of injections. Meister survived, and the treatment soon spread around the world."],["Rabies in the Philippines","The Philippines is among the countries with the most rabies deaths, with a few hundred people dying each year. The Anti-Rabies Act of 2007 requires dog owners to have their pets vaccinated and registered and declares March as Rabies Awareness Month. Hundreds of animal bite treatment centers offer vaccines across the country."],["Vaccinating dogs","Experts agree that vaccinating at least 70 percent of dogs in a community can stop rabies from spreading. Countries in Latin America greatly reduced human rabies deaths through mass dog vaccination. Responsible pet ownership, including keeping dogs from roaming, also helps."],["Other animals","Besides dogs, rabies can be carried by cats, bats, monkeys, foxes, raccoons and other mammals. In the Americas, bats are a major source of human cases. Birds, reptiles and fish do not carry rabies."],["Surviving rabies","Only a handful of people are known to have survived rabies after symptoms began. In 2004 an American teenager named Jeanna Giese survived after doctors put her in an induced coma, a method called the Milwaukee protocol. Later attempts using the same method have rarely succeeded, so prevention remains essential."],["Myths and facts","Some people wait to see whether the biting dog stays healthy before seeking treatment, or rely on traditional healers called tandok or tawak. Health authorities warn that such delays can be fatal and urge immediate wound washing and medical care, whether or not the animal appears sick."]],
sp:["Joseph Meister’s story","Joseph Meister never forgot the man who saved his life. As an adult he worked as a caretaker at the Pasteur Institute in Paris, the research center founded in Louis Pasteur’s honor. According to a popular account, when German forces occupied Paris in 1940, Meister was still at his post. He died that year. Today World Rabies Day is held every 28 September, the anniversary of Pasteur’s death in 1895, to remind people that rabies is still a threat and that it can be prevented."],
g:[["Rabies","A deadly viral disease that attacks the nervous system"],["Post-exposure prophylaxis","Vaccines and treatment given after a possible exposure"],["Hydrophobia","Fear of water, a symptom of rabies"],["Saliva","The liquid in the mouth that can carry the rabies virus"]],
f:["Dogs cause most human rabies cases.","Rabies can be prevented with vaccines.","Wash any animal bite with soap and water."],
fun:"Rabies gets its name from the Latin word rabies, meaning madness or rage.",
tl:"Ang rabies ay nakamamatay na sakit na dulot ng virus na umaatake sa utak. Karaniwan itong naipapasa sa kagat ng asong may rabies. Maiiwasan ito sa agarang paghuhugas ng sugat gamit ang sabon at tubig at sa pagpapabakuna."});

ADDART({id:"measles",t:"Measles",c:"Disease and Medicine",i:"🤒",w:"Measles",r:["vacc","immune","pandemic","pregnancy","hygiene"],
p:[["Very contagious","Measles is one of the most contagious diseases known."],["Fever and rash","It causes high fever, cough, red eyes and a spreading rash."],["Prevented by vaccine","Two doses of measles vaccine give strong, long-lasting protection."]],
l:"Measles is a highly contagious disease caused by a virus that spreads through the air when an infected person coughs, sneezes or breathes. It causes high fever, cough, runny nose, red eyes and a red, blotchy rash that spreads from the face to the rest of the body. Most people recover, but measles can lead to pneumonia, brain swelling, blindness and death, especially in young children and malnourished people. A safe and effective vaccine has been available since 1963, and vaccination has saved tens of millions of lives. Outbreaks still occur where too few people are vaccinated, including in the Philippines in 2019.",
s:[["How it spreads","The measles virus can float in the air and remain infectious for up to two hours after an infected person leaves a room. About nine out of ten unvaccinated people exposed to it will catch the disease. People are contagious from about four days before the rash appears until about four days after."],["Symptoms","Symptoms begin about 10 to 14 days after infection, with fever, cough, runny nose and red, watery eyes. Tiny white spots, called Koplik spots, may appear inside the mouth. A few days later a rash breaks out on the face and spreads down the body."],["Complications","Measles can cause ear infections, diarrhea, pneumonia and swelling of the brain. It also weakens the immune system for months or even years afterwards, making people more likely to catch other infections. Children under five, pregnant women and people with weak immune systems face the highest risk."],["The vaccine","The measles vaccine is usually given in two doses, often combined with vaccines against mumps and rubella, called MMR, or with rubella alone. Two doses protect about 97 percent of people. In the Philippines, measles vaccines are given free as part of the routine immunization program for infants."]],
k:[["Cause","Measles virus"],["Filipino name","Tigdas"],["Spread","Through the air"],["Vaccine available since","1963"],["Protection from two doses","About 97 percent"]],
d:[["c. 900","Persian physician al-Razi describes measles and smallpox"],["1757","Francis Home shows measles is caused by something in the blood of patients"],["1954","John Enders and Thomas Peebles isolate the measles virus"],["1963","The first measles vaccine is licensed"],["2019","The Philippines declares a measles outbreak"],["2019","Worldwide measles cases reach their highest level in more than 20 years"]],
s2:[["A disease of history","Measles has afflicted humans for well over a thousand years. The Persian physician al-Razi, around the year 900, was among the first to describe it clearly and tell it apart from smallpox. When Europeans brought measles to the Americas and the Pacific islands, it killed huge numbers of people who had never been exposed. In 1875 an epidemic in Fiji killed about a quarter of the population."],["Making the vaccine","In 1954 the American scientists John Enders and Thomas Peebles collected blood from a sick boy named David Edmonston and grew the measles virus in the laboratory. Weakened forms of this virus were used to create the first vaccines, licensed in 1963. Later improvements made the vaccine safer and more effective."],["The 2019 Philippine outbreak","In February 2019 the Philippine Department of Health declared a measles outbreak in Metro Manila and several regions. There were tens of thousands of cases and hundreds of deaths, mostly among young children who had not been vaccinated. Health workers held catch-up vaccination drives in schools, homes and evacuation centers."],["Vaccine confidence","Health experts linked falling vaccination rates in the Philippines partly to public fears after the controversy over the dengue vaccine Dengvaxia in 2017, which shook trust in vaccines generally. Globally, a 1998 study that claimed a link between the MMR vaccine and autism was found to be fraudulent and retracted in 2010; large studies have found no such link."],["Measles today","Thanks to vaccination, measles deaths worldwide have fallen sharply since 2000. However, gaps in vaccination, worsened by disruptions during the COVID-19 pandemic, have allowed outbreaks to return in many countries, including some that had previously eliminated the disease."],["Herd immunity","Because measles is so contagious, about 95 percent of a community must be vaccinated to stop it from spreading. This protects babies too young to be vaccinated and people who cannot receive vaccines for medical reasons. This shared protection is known as herd immunity or community immunity."]],
sp:["The disease that makes the immune system forget","In 2019 scientists published studies showing that measles does more than cause a rash and fever. The virus destroys many of the memory cells the immune system uses to recognize germs it has met before. After recovering, a child may lose part of their protection against diseases they had already fought off, a condition researchers called immune amnesia. This helps explain why, in the years after a measles epidemic, children often suffer more illnesses of other kinds. It is another reason doctors say preventing measles with vaccines protects children’s health far beyond the illness itself."],
g:[["Measles","A highly contagious viral disease with fever and rash"],["Koplik spots","Small white spots inside the mouth, an early sign of measles"],["MMR","A combined vaccine against measles, mumps and rubella"],["Herd immunity","Protection of a community when most people are immune"]],
f:["Measles spreads through the air.","Two vaccine doses protect about 97 percent of people.","In Filipino, measles is called tigdas."],
fun:"The measles virus can stay infectious in the air of a room for up to two hours after an infected person has left.",
tl:"Ang tigdas ay lubhang nakahahawang sakit na dulot ng virus na kumakalat sa hangin. Nagdudulot ito ng mataas na lagnat, ubo, mapupulang mata at pantal. Naiiwasan ito ng dalawang dosis ng bakuna."});

;
/* data/tech8.js */
// Technology, more articles: elevators and escalators, high-speed rail, drones, the power grid and clocks.
ADDART({id:"elevators",t:"Elevators and Escalators",c:"Engineering",i:"🛗",w:"Elevator",r:["skyscrapers","machines","elec","bridges","rail"],
p:[["Going up","Elevators carry people and goods between the floors of buildings."],["Safety first","Elisha Otis’s safety brake made passenger elevators trusted in the 1850s."],["Moving stairs","Escalators are moving staircases that carry crowds smoothly."]],
l:"An elevator, called a lift in British English, is a platform or cabin that moves up and down inside a shaft to carry people or goods between floors. Simple hoists pulled by people, animals or water power have existed since ancient times, but modern passenger elevators became possible in the 1850s, when the American inventor Elisha Otis demonstrated a safety brake that stopped a cabin from falling if its rope broke. Together with steel frames, safe elevators made tall buildings and skyscrapers practical. Escalators, or moving staircases, followed in the late 1800s and now carry millions of people every day in malls, airports and train stations.",
s:[["How an elevator works","Most elevators in tall buildings hang from strong steel cables that pass over a wheel at the top of the shaft, driven by an electric motor. A heavy counterweight on the other side of the wheel balances the cabin, so the motor needs much less power. Shorter buildings often use hydraulic elevators, which are pushed up by a piston filled with oil."],["Safety systems","Modern elevators have several cables, each strong enough to hold the cabin alone. If the cabin moves too fast, a governor triggers safety brakes that grip the guide rails. Doors cannot open unless the cabin is level with a floor, and buffers at the bottom of the shaft cushion any fall. Elevators are among the safest forms of transport."],["Escalators","An escalator is a loop of linked steps that moves continuously, driven by a motor and chains, with a moving handrail kept at the same speed. The steps flatten at the top and bottom so riders can step on and off. Moving walkways work in a similar way on flat ground or gentle slopes, as in airports."],["Making cities taller","Before elevators, buildings rarely rose above five or six stories, because few people wanted to climb more stairs. Safe elevators made upper floors desirable and valuable, helping create the skylines of New York, Chicago, Hong Kong, Dubai and the business districts of Makati and Bonifacio Global City."]],
k:[["Safety brake demonstrated","Elisha Otis, 1854"],["First passenger safety elevator","New York, 1857"],["First electric elevator","Werner von Siemens, 1880"],["Escalator name","Trademarked by Otis, 1900"],["British name","Lift"]],
d:[["c. 236 BC","Archimedes is said to build a lifting device using ropes and pulleys"],["1743","A counterweighted personal lift is built for King Louis XV in Versailles"],["1854","Elisha Otis demonstrates his safety brake in New York"],["1857","The first passenger safety elevator is installed in New York"],["1880","Werner von Siemens builds the first electric elevator"],["1896","Jesse Reno’s inclined moving stairway carries riders at Coney Island"]],
s2:[["Otis’s famous show","At the 1854 world’s fair in New York, Elisha Otis stood on an open elevator platform raised high above a crowd. He then ordered the only rope holding it to be cut. The platform dropped only a few centimeters before his spring-loaded brake caught it. He is said to have called out, all safe, gentlemen. Orders for his elevators soon followed."],["Electric elevators","The German engineer Werner von Siemens built the first electric elevator in 1880. Electric motors were faster, quieter and cleaner than steam-powered machines. By the early 1900s electric elevators served most tall buildings, and push-button controls gradually replaced human elevator operators."],["The fastest elevators","Elevators in some of the world’s tallest buildings travel at more than 70 kilometers per hour. The Burj Khalifa in Dubai, the tallest building in the world, has elevators that climb more than 500 meters. Very tall buildings use sky lobbies, where passengers change from express elevators to local ones."],["Accessibility","Elevators, ramps and lifts make buildings and transport usable for wheelchair users, older people, parents with strollers and people carrying heavy loads. Many countries, including the Philippines through its Accessibility Law of 1982, require public buildings to provide accessible features."],["Escalator history","Several inventors designed moving stairs in the 1800s. In 1896 Jesse Reno installed an inclined moving belt at Coney Island in New York, while Charles Seeberger developed designs that the Otis company bought and sold under the name escalator, trademarked in 1900. The word later became generic."],["Elevators of the future","Engineers are developing lighter cables made of carbon fiber that could allow single elevator rides of a kilometer or more, and cabins that move sideways as well as up and down using magnetic systems. The idea of a space elevator, a cable stretching from Earth into orbit, first imagined by Konstantin Tsiolkovsky in 1895, remains beyond current materials."]],
sp:["Elevator etiquette","Riding an elevator comes with unwritten rules that many people follow around the world. Let passengers step out before stepping in, move to the back to make space, and hold the door for those hurrying to catch it if it is safe. In many Philippine malls and office towers, people stand quietly facing the doors, and in busy buildings the button for closing the door is pressed often during rush hour. On escalators in some cities, such as Tokyo or London, riders stand on one side to let others walk past on the other, though some transport authorities now ask everyone to stand still for safety."],
g:[["Elevator","A cabin that moves up and down between floors"],["Counterweight","A weight that balances the elevator cabin"],["Escalator","A moving staircase"],["Hydraulic","Powered by liquid pushed under pressure"]],
f:["Elisha Otis invented the elevator safety brake.","The first electric elevator was built in 1880.","Escalator was once a trademark."],
fun:"Escalator was originally a brand name owned by the Otis Elevator Company before it became an ordinary word.",
tl:"Ang elevator ay kahon na umaakyat at bumababa sa loob ng gusali upang magdala ng tao at bagay. Ginawang ligtas ito ni Elisha Otis noong dekada 1850 sa pamamagitan ng preno pangkaligtasan. Ang escalator naman ay hagdanang gumagalaw."});

ADDART({id:"highspeedrail",t:"High-Speed Rail",c:"Transport",i:"🚄",w:"High-speed rail",r:["rail","japan","china","energytransition","megacities"],
p:[["Fast trains","High-speed trains travel at 250 kilometers per hour or more on special tracks."],["Started in Japan","Japan opened the first high-speed line, the Shinkansen, in 1964."],["Growing networks","China now has by far the largest high-speed network."]],
l:"High-speed rail is a type of passenger train service that runs much faster than ordinary trains, usually at 250 kilometers per hour or more on new lines, using specially built tracks, powerful electric trains and advanced signaling. The first high-speed line, Japan’s Tokaido Shinkansen between Tokyo and Osaka, opened in 1964. High-speed networks have since spread across Europe and Asia, and China has built the largest network in the world. Supporters say high-speed rail is fast, safe and produces less pollution than flying or driving, while critics point to its very high construction costs.",
s:[["How the trains go so fast","High-speed trains are streamlined to cut air resistance and draw electricity from overhead wires to power motors spread along the train. The tracks have gentle curves, no level crossings with roads, and continuous welded rails for a smooth ride. Signals are displayed inside the cab, because drivers cannot read trackside signals at such speeds."],["The Shinkansen","Japan’s Shinkansen, often called the bullet train, opened on 1 October 1964, just before the Tokyo Olympics, with trains running at up to 210 kilometers per hour. Today Shinkansen lines connect most of Japan’s major cities, with trains reaching 320 kilometers per hour. The network is famous for its punctuality and safety record."],["Europe’s fast trains","France opened its first TGV line between Paris and Lyon in 1981, followed by high-speed lines in Germany, Italy, Spain and other countries. The Eurostar, through the Channel Tunnel, links London with Paris and Brussels. Spain has one of the longest high-speed networks in Europe."],["China’s network","China opened its first major high-speed line in 2008 and has since built a network of tens of thousands of kilometers, more than the rest of the world combined. Trains on some lines run at 350 kilometers per hour, carrying billions of passenger trips a year and linking cities from Beijing to Guangzhou."]],
k:[["Usual top speed","250 to 350 km/h"],["First line","Tokaido Shinkansen, Japan, 1964"],["Largest network","China"],["Wheeled speed record","574.8 km/h, France, 2007"],["Maglev speed record","603 km/h, Japan, 2015"]],
d:[["1964","Japan opens the Tokaido Shinkansen between Tokyo and Osaka"],["1981","France opens its first TGV line"],["1994","Eurostar services begin through the Channel Tunnel"],["2004","The Shanghai Maglev begins commercial service"],["2007","A French TGV sets the wheeled train speed record of 574.8 km/h"],["2015","A Japanese maglev train reaches 603 km/h"]],
s2:[["Maglev trains","Maglev, short for magnetic levitation, trains float above a guideway using powerful magnets, so there is no friction between wheels and rails. The Shanghai Maglev, opened in 2004, links the city to its airport at speeds of up to about 300 to 430 kilometers per hour. Japan is building a maglev line between Tokyo and Nagoya designed for speeds of 500 kilometers per hour."],["Safety","High-speed rail has an excellent safety record. Japan’s Shinkansen has carried billions of passengers since 1964 without a passenger death caused by a derailment or collision, thanks to dedicated tracks, automatic train control and earthquake detection systems that stop trains when tremors are sensed."],["Benefits and costs","Supporters say high-speed rail cuts travel times between cities, reduces road congestion and produces far less carbon dioxide per passenger than flying. Critics note that building lines costs billions, some lines carry fewer passengers than predicted, and money might be better spent on local transport. Debates over cost have slowed projects in countries such as the United States and the United Kingdom."],["Trains versus planes","For journeys of a few hundred kilometers, high-speed trains often compete well with air travel, because stations are in city centers and boarding is quick. After high-speed lines opened between Madrid and Barcelona and between Paris and Lyon, many travelers switched from planes to trains."],["Rail in the Philippines","The Philippines does not yet have high-speed rail. The North–South Commuter Railway under construction in Luzon is designed for faster electric trains linking Clark, Metro Manila and Calamba, though at speeds below true high-speed rail. A Mindanao railway has also been planned. Supporters see modern rail as key to easing traffic, while critics point to delays and costs."],["Rail around the world","High-speed lines also operate or are being built in South Korea, Taiwan, Turkey, Morocco, Saudi Arabia, Indonesia and India. Indonesia opened Southeast Asia’s first high-speed railway, between Jakarta and Bandung, in 2023, built with Chinese partners."]],
sp:["Seven minutes to clean a bullet train","At Tokyo Station, Shinkansen trains often have only a short turnaround before departing again. In about seven minutes, teams of cleaners sweep through every carriage, collecting rubbish, wiping tables, turning all the seats to face the new direction of travel and checking the toilets. When they finish, the team lines up on the platform and bows to the waiting passengers. Visitors film the routine, sometimes called the seven-minute miracle, as an example of Japanese efficiency and pride in work, and transport companies from other countries have studied it."],
g:[["High-speed rail","Passenger trains running at 250 km/h or more"],["Shinkansen","Japan’s high-speed rail network"],["Maglev","A train that floats above its track using magnets"],["Streamlined","Shaped to move smoothly through air"]],
f:["Japan’s Shinkansen opened in 1964.","China has the largest high-speed network.","A Japanese maglev reached 603 km/h."],
fun:"The long nose of some Shinkansen trains was designed partly to reduce a loud boom when they rush out of tunnels; one design was inspired by the beak of the kingfisher.",
tl:"Ang high-speed rail ay mga tren na tumatakbo nang 250 kilometro bawat oras o higit pa sa espesyal na riles. Nagsimula ito sa Shinkansen ng Japan noong 1964. Ang China ang may pinakamalaking network sa mundo."});

ADDART({id:"drones",t:"Drones",c:"Technology",i:"🛸",w:"Unmanned aerial vehicle",r:["flight","robot","gps","camera","aieveryday"],
p:[["Flying without a pilot","Drones are aircraft that fly without a pilot on board."],["Many uses","They take photos, map land, spray crops, deliver supplies and help in disasters."],["Rules and worries","Their use raises questions about safety, privacy and warfare."]],
l:"A drone, or unmanned aerial vehicle, is an aircraft that flies without a human pilot on board. It is controlled by a person on the ground using a remote control, or flies itself using computers, sensors and GPS. Drones range from tiny toys to large military aircraft with wingspans like those of airliners. Small camera drones with four rotors, called quadcopters, became popular in the 2010s for photography, filmmaking and hobby flying. Drones are now used in farming, mapping, construction, search and rescue, deliveries and disaster response. Their use in warfare and their effects on privacy and safety are widely debated.",
s:[["How a quadcopter flies","A quadcopter has four rotors that spin to create lift. By changing the speed of individual rotors, a flight computer tilts the drone to move forward, backward or sideways, or spins it around. Sensors such as gyroscopes, accelerometers and GPS keep it stable, and many drones can hover in place or return home automatically."],["Cameras in the sky","Camera drones let photographers and filmmakers capture aerial views that once required helicopters. They are used to film sports, weddings, tourism videos, news events and nature documentaries. Some carry thermal cameras that detect heat, useful for finding people or inspecting buildings."],["Drones at work","Farmers use drones to check the health of crops and spray fertilizer or pesticide. Engineers inspect bridges, power lines and wind turbines without climbing them. Surveyors make detailed maps and 3D models of land, and scientists use drones to count wildlife, study volcanoes and track forests."],["Drones in disasters","After typhoons, earthquakes and floods, drones can quickly survey damaged areas, find stranded people and help plan relief. In the Philippines, drones have been used to map damage after typhoons, monitor volcanoes such as Taal and Mayon and deliver supplies to areas cut off by landslides."]],
k:[["Also called","Unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV)"],["Common design","Quadcopter"],["Name origin","Queen Bee target aircraft, 1935"],["Philippine regulator","Civil Aviation Authority of the Philippines"],["Controlled by","Remote pilot or onboard computer"]],
d:[["1918","The Kettering Bug, an early pilotless flying bomb, is tested in the United States"],["1935","Britain flies the Queen Bee, a radio-controlled target plane"],["1995","The Predator drone first flies on military missions"],["2006","The drone company DJI is founded in China"],["2016","Drones begin delivering blood to hospitals in Rwanda"],["2022","Drones are used on a large scale in the war in Ukraine"]],
s2:[["Where the name came from","In the 1930s Britain built a radio-controlled target aircraft called the Queen Bee, used for gunnery practice. When the United States developed similar planes, they were nicknamed drones, after male bees. The name stuck and was later applied to all kinds of pilotless aircraft."],["Delivering medicine","In 2016 the company Zipline began using small fixed-wing drones to deliver blood supplies to hospitals in Rwanda, dropping packages by parachute. Similar services now deliver medicines, vaccines and lab samples in Ghana, Nigeria and other countries. Some companies also test drone delivery of food and parcels in cities."],["Drones in war","Militaries use drones for surveillance and attacks, and armed drones have played a major role in conflicts in the Middle East, Africa and Ukraine. Supporters say drones keep pilots out of danger and can strike precisely, while critics raise concerns about civilian casualties, accountability and the possibility of weapons that choose targets without human control. International discussions on rules for autonomous weapons continue."],["Safety and rules","Drones can be dangerous near airports, crowds and emergency operations. Many countries require drone owners to register their aircraft, keep them within sight, avoid flying near airports and stay below certain heights. In the Philippines, the Civil Aviation Authority of the Philippines sets rules and issues certificates for drone operators."],["Privacy","Because drones can carry cameras over homes and private property, they raise privacy concerns. Laws in many places limit filming people without permission, and some groups call for stronger rules. Drone hobbyists argue that responsible pilots should not be over-regulated."],["Light shows","Hundreds or thousands of drones carrying colored lights can be programmed to fly together, forming pictures, words and moving shapes in the night sky. Drone light shows have appeared at Olympic Games, New Year celebrations and festivals, including events in the Philippines, sometimes replacing fireworks because they are quieter and create no smoke."]],
sp:["Mapping after the storm","When a strong typhoon strikes, roads may be blocked, bridges washed away and phone lines down, so it is hard to know where help is needed most. Teams of volunteers and government workers now launch drones to fly over towns and photograph them from above. The pictures are stitched together into detailed maps showing destroyed houses, flooded fields and blocked roads. Relief agencies use these maps to decide where to send food, water and rescue teams. Some Filipino universities and local governments train students and staff to fly drones for disaster mapping."],
g:[["Drone","An aircraft that flies without a pilot on board"],["UAV","Unmanned aerial vehicle"],["Quadcopter","A drone with four rotors"],["Thermal camera","A camera that detects heat"]],
f:["Drones were named after male bees.","Quadcopters have four rotors.","Drones deliver medicine in parts of Africa."],
fun:"Drone light shows can use thousands of drones at once, each flying to its exact position in the sky.",
tl:"Ang drone ay sasakyang panghimpapawid na lumilipad nang walang piloto sa loob. Ginagamit ito sa pagkuha ng larawan, pagsasaka, paghahatid at pagtulong sa sakuna. Pinagtatalunan ang paggamit nito sa digmaan at ang epekto nito sa privacy."});

ADDART({id:"powergrid",t:"The Power Grid",c:"Energy",i:"🔌",w:"Electrical grid",r:["elec","phpower","renew","battery","nuclear"],
p:[["Electricity on the move","The power grid carries electricity from power plants to homes, schools and businesses."],["High voltage","Electricity travels long distances at very high voltage, then is stepped down for use."],["Keeping balance","Grid operators must match supply and demand every second."]],
l:"A power grid, or electrical grid, is the network that delivers electricity from where it is generated to where it is used. It includes power plants, high-voltage transmission lines, substations, transformers and the local distribution lines that bring power to homes and buildings. Because electricity is hard to store in large amounts, grid operators must balance the amount produced with the amount used at every moment. If the balance fails, blackouts can occur. Grids are changing quickly as more electricity comes from solar and wind power, which vary with the weather, and as batteries and smart technology help manage supply and demand.",
s:[["From plant to plug","Electricity is produced in power plants that burn coal, gas or oil, split atoms, or use water, wind, sunlight or underground heat. Step-up transformers raise its voltage to hundreds of thousands of volts for long-distance travel on transmission towers. At substations near towns, transformers lower the voltage, and distribution lines carry it along streets to homes, where it arrives at about 220 to 240 volts in the Philippines."],["Why high voltage","Sending electricity at high voltage means less current flows for the same amount of power, so less energy is wasted as heat in the wires. This is why transmission lines are kept high above the ground on tall towers and why substations are fenced off for safety."],["Balancing supply and demand","Demand for electricity rises and falls through the day, peaking on hot afternoons and evenings when air conditioners, lights and appliances are switched on. Control centers watch the grid constantly and tell power plants to increase or decrease output. The grid’s frequency, 60 hertz in the Philippines, must stay steady; if it drops, it signals that demand is outrunning supply."],["Blackouts","Blackouts happen when power plants fail, lines are damaged by storms or demand exceeds supply. To avoid a total collapse, operators sometimes cut power to some areas in turns, called rotational brownouts in the Philippines. Typhoons often topple power poles and lines, leaving communities without electricity for days."]],
k:[["Philippine grid frequency","60 hertz"],["Household voltage in the Philippines","About 220–230 volts"],["Philippine transmission operator","National Grid Corporation of the Philippines"],["Main grids","Luzon, Visayas, Mindanao"],["First central power station","Pearl Street, New York, 1882"]],
d:[["1882","Thomas Edison opens the Pearl Street power station in New York"],["1886","William Stanley demonstrates an AC power system with transformers"],["1895","The Niagara Falls power plant begins generating alternating current"],["1903","The Manila Electric Railway and Light Company, later Meralco, is founded"],["2003","A blackout leaves about 50 million people without power in North America"],["2025","A major blackout strikes Spain and Portugal"]],
s2:[["The war of the currents","In the 1880s Thomas Edison promoted direct current, or DC, which flows in one direction. George Westinghouse and Nikola Tesla promoted alternating current, or AC, which reverses direction many times a second and can easily be changed to higher or lower voltages with transformers. AC proved better for sending power over long distances and became the standard for grids worldwide."],["The Philippine grid","The Philippines has three main grids, serving Luzon, the Visayas and Mindanao, connected by undersea cables. The Mindanao–Visayas Interconnection Project, completed in the 2020s, linked Mindanao to the rest of the country for the first time. The National Grid Corporation of the Philippines operates the transmission network, while distribution companies such as Meralco and electric cooperatives deliver power to customers."],["Islands and microgrids","Many small islands in the Philippines are not connected to the main grids. They often rely on diesel generators, which are expensive and polluting. Solar panels, batteries and small local grids, called microgrids, are increasingly used to bring cheaper, cleaner and more reliable electricity to remote communities."],["Renewables on the grid","Solar and wind power produce electricity only when the Sun shines or the wind blows. Grid operators manage this by combining many sources across wide areas, using batteries and pumped hydropower to store energy, and building more transmission lines. Supporters say these tools allow very high shares of renewable power, while others warn that grids must be carefully planned to stay reliable."],["Smart grids","Smart grids use digital meters, sensors and communication networks to monitor electricity flows in real time. They can detect faults, reroute power, encourage people to use electricity when it is cheaper and connect electric vehicles and home solar panels. Smart meters also allow customers to track their own use."],["Grid security","Because modern life depends on electricity, power grids are considered critical infrastructure. Operators protect them against extreme weather, equipment failures and cyberattacks. In 2015 and 2016 hackers caused blackouts in parts of Ukraine, showing that grids can be targeted through computer networks."]],
sp:["The day Spain went dark","Shortly after midday on 28 April 2025, the power grid of Spain and Portugal suddenly collapsed. Within seconds, trains stopped in tunnels, traffic lights went dark, elevators halted between floors and mobile networks struggled. Millions of people walked home, gathered around battery radios and shared food in the streets. Engineers gradually restarted the grid, section by section, and most power returned by the next morning. The blackout led to investigations into its causes and wide debate across Europe about how to keep grids stable as they rely more on renewable energy."],
g:[["Power grid","The network that delivers electricity"],["Transmission line","A high-voltage line carrying power over long distances"],["Transformer","A device that raises or lowers voltage"],["Frequency","How many times per second alternating current changes direction"]],
f:["The Philippine grid runs at 60 hertz.","Electricity travels at high voltage over long distances.","Edison opened the first central power station in 1882."],
fun:"Electricity flowing through the grid must be produced at almost the exact moment it is used, because very little is stored along the way.",
tl:"Ang power grid ay network na naghahatid ng kuryente mula sa planta hanggang sa mga tahanan at gusali. Dumadaan ang kuryente sa mataas na boltahe sa malalayong lugar bago ibaba para magamit. Kailangang balansehin ang suplay at pangangailangan bawat segundo."});

ADDART({id:"clocks",t:"Clocks and Timekeeping",c:"Inventions",i:"🕰️",w:"Clock",r:["timephys","sun","machines","gps","compass"],
p:[["Measuring time","Clocks measure and show the passing of time."],["From shadows to atoms","People have used sundials, water clocks, pendulums, quartz crystals and atoms to keep time."],["Precise time","Modern atomic clocks are accurate to a tiny fraction of a second over millions of years."]],
l:"A clock is a device for measuring and showing time. People first told time by watching the Sun, Moon and stars, then built sundials and water clocks thousands of years ago. Mechanical clocks with gears appeared in medieval China and Europe, and the pendulum clock, invented in 1656, made timekeeping far more accurate. Quartz clocks, using vibrating crystals, became common in the 20th century, and atomic clocks, which count the vibrations of atoms, now define the second itself. Precise time is essential for navigation, satellites, the internet, power grids and banking, as well as for daily life.",
s:[["Sundials and water clocks","A sundial tells time by the shadow cast by a pointer as the Sun moves across the sky. The ancient Egyptians and Babylonians used shadow clocks over 3,000 years ago. Water clocks, which measured time by the steady flow of water into or out of a container, worked at night and on cloudy days and were used in Egypt, Greece, China and the Islamic world."],["Mechanical clocks","Mechanical clocks use a falling weight or a coiled spring to turn gears, controlled by a device called an escapement that releases the gears in small, regular steps. The first mechanical clocks in Europe appeared in church towers in the late 1200s and 1300s. They rang bells to mark the hours, which is where the word clock, from a word for bell, comes from."],["The pendulum","The Italian scientist Galileo Galilei noticed that a swinging pendulum keeps a steady rhythm. In 1656 the Dutch scientist Christiaan Huygens built the first pendulum clock, which was accurate to within about a minute a day, a huge improvement. Pendulum clocks remained the most accurate timekeepers for almost 300 years."],["Quartz and atomic clocks","A quartz crystal vibrates at a precise rate when electricity passes through it. The first quartz clock was built in 1927, and cheap quartz watches spread worldwide from the 1970s. Atomic clocks, first built in 1955, measure the natural vibrations of caesium or other atoms and are so accurate that they would lose less than a second in millions of years."]],
k:[["Pendulum clock invented","Christiaan Huygens, 1656"],["First quartz clock","1927"],["First caesium atomic clock","1955"],["The second defined by atoms","1967"],["Philippine time keeper","PAGASA"]],
d:[["c. 1500 BC","Egyptians use shadow clocks and water clocks"],["1092","Su Song completes a giant astronomical clock tower in China"],["1656","Christiaan Huygens builds the first pendulum clock"],["1761","John Harrison’s H4 marine timekeeper is tested at sea"],["1955","The first caesium atomic clock is built in England"],["2013","The Philippines passes the Philippine Standard Time Act"]],
s2:[["Su Song’s clock tower","In 1092 the Chinese scholar and engineer Su Song completed a huge water-driven astronomical clock tower in the capital, Kaifeng. More than ten meters tall, it turned a rotating globe of the stars and had figures that appeared to announce the hours. It used an early form of escapement, centuries before similar devices in Europe."],["Finding longitude","Sailors could find their latitude from the Sun and stars, but not their longitude, their east–west position, without knowing the exact time at home. The British government offered a prize for a solution. The carpenter and clockmaker John Harrison spent decades building sea clocks, and his H4 watch, tested in 1761, kept time accurately enough on a voyage to Jamaica to solve the problem."],["Time zones","Before railways, towns set their clocks by the local Sun, so noon differed from place to place. Railway timetables made this confusing, and in 1884 an international conference chose the line through Greenwich in England as the starting point for world time zones. The Philippines uses Philippine Standard Time, eight hours ahead of Greenwich."],["Juan Time","The Philippine Standard Time Act of 2013 requires government offices and broadcasters to synchronize their clocks with the official time kept by PAGASA. The campaign behind the law encouraged punctuality and challenged the habit, jokingly called Filipino time, of arriving late."],["Atomic time and GPS","GPS satellites carry atomic clocks and broadcast precise time signals. A receiver works out its position by comparing how long signals take to arrive from several satellites; an error of a millionth of a second would put the location off by about 300 meters. Atomic time also keeps mobile networks, the internet, stock markets and power grids in step."],["The leap second","Earth’s rotation is slightly irregular and gradually slowing, so atomic time and solar time drift apart. Since 1972 leap seconds have occasionally been added to keep them aligned, but they cause problems for computer systems. In 2022 international scientists agreed to stop adding leap seconds by 2035."]],
sp:["The clocks of old Manila","In Spanish colonial Manila, church bells and tower clocks marked the hours for city life, calling people to prayer, work and rest. Many old churches in the Philippines, such as those in Intramuros and in provincial towns, still have bell towers that once served as the community’s clock. In the 1930s the Manila City Hall was built with its famous clock tower, which became a landmark. Its four large faces still look out over the city, and its lights are sometimes changed to different colors to mark special occasions."],
g:[["Sundial","A device that tells time by a shadow cast by the Sun"],["Escapement","A mechanism that releases a clock’s gears in regular steps"],["Pendulum","A weight that swings back and forth at a steady rate"],["Atomic clock","A clock that keeps time using the vibrations of atoms"]],
f:["The pendulum clock was invented in 1656.","Atomic clocks define the second.","The word clock comes from a word for bell."],
fun:"Clocks run slightly faster on a mountaintop than at sea level because of gravity, an effect modern atomic clocks can measure.",
tl:"Ang orasan ay kagamitang sumusukat at nagpapakita ng oras. Gumamit ang tao ng sundial, orasang tubig, pendulum, quartz at atomo upang sukatin ang oras. Ang PAGASA ang nag-iingat ng opisyal na oras ng Pilipinas."});

;
/* data/science8.js */
// Science and Numbers, more articles: the periodic table, catalysts, neutrinos, the Higgs boson and the theory of evolution.
ADDART({id:"periodictable",t:"The Periodic Table",c:"Chemistry",i:"🧪",w:"Periodic table",r:["atom","metals","molecules","reactions","electron"],
p:[["Chart of the elements","The periodic table arranges all known chemical elements in order of atomic number."],["Rows and columns","Elements in the same column behave in similar ways."],["Mendeleev’s idea","Dmitri Mendeleev published the first widely used table in 1869."]],
l:"The periodic table is a chart that arranges the chemical elements, the basic substances from which all matter is made, in order of their atomic number, the number of protons in each atom’s nucleus. Elements are placed in rows, called periods, and columns, called groups, so that elements with similar chemical properties fall in the same column. The Russian chemist Dmitri Mendeleev published the first widely accepted version in 1869 and boldly left gaps for elements not yet discovered, correctly predicting their properties. Today the table contains 118 confirmed elements, from hydrogen, the lightest, to oganesson, the heaviest. It is one of the most important tools in science.",
s:[["Periods and groups","Each row, or period, begins a new layer of electrons around the nucleus. Each column, or group, contains elements with the same number of electrons in their outer layer, which largely decides how they react. For example, the alkali metals in group 1, such as sodium and potassium, are all soft and react strongly with water."],["Metals and nonmetals","Most elements are metals, found on the left and in the middle of the table. They are usually shiny, conduct electricity and can be bent or shaped. Nonmetals, such as carbon, oxygen and nitrogen, sit on the upper right. Between them lie metalloids, such as silicon, which have properties of both and are vital in electronics."],["Noble gases","The column on the far right holds the noble gases, including helium, neon and argon. Their outer electron layers are full, so they hardly react with other elements. Neon glows red-orange in signs, and helium, lighter than air, fills balloons."],["Reading a square","Each square usually shows an element’s symbol, such as H for hydrogen, Fe for iron from the Latin ferrum, or Au for gold from the Latin aurum. It also shows the atomic number and the atomic mass, the average weight of its atoms."]],
k:[["Confirmed elements","118"],["Arranged by","Atomic number"],["First widely used table","Dmitri Mendeleev, 1869"],["Lightest element","Hydrogen"],["Heaviest confirmed element","Oganesson"]],
d:[["1669","Hennig Brand discovers phosphorus, the first element found by a known person"],["1789","Antoine Lavoisier publishes a list of chemical elements"],["1869","Dmitri Mendeleev presents his periodic table"],["1875","Gallium, predicted by Mendeleev, is discovered"],["1913","Henry Moseley shows elements should be ordered by atomic number"],["2016","The names of the last four elements to complete the seventh row are approved"]],
s2:[["Mendeleev’s gaps","Mendeleev arranged the elements known in his time by atomic weight and grouped them by their properties. Where the pattern broke, he left gaps and predicted that undiscovered elements would fill them. He described their likely weights, densities and behavior. When gallium, scandium and germanium were found between 1875 and 1886, they matched his predictions remarkably well."],["Moseley and atomic number","In 1913 the young British physicist Henry Moseley used X-rays to show that each element has a unique number of positive charges in its nucleus, its atomic number. Ordering elements by this number fixed a few puzzling spots in Mendeleev’s table. Moseley was killed in the First World War in 1915, at the age of 27."],["Making new elements","Elements heavier than uranium, number 92, are very rare or absent in nature and have mostly been created in laboratories by smashing atoms together. Many exist for only fractions of a second. The four newest elements, nihonium, moscovium, tennessine and oganesson, were named in 2016, completing the seventh row of the table."],["Named after places and people","Element names come from many sources. Some honor scientists, such as einsteinium, curium and mendelevium. Others are named after places, such as americium, francium, polonium after Poland, and nihonium, from Nihon, a Japanese name for Japan. Some come from mythology, such as titanium, from the Titans of Greek myth."],["Elements in everyday life","Elements surround us. Carbon forms the backbone of living things, oxygen keeps us alive, silicon is used in computer chips, and lithium powers rechargeable batteries. The Philippines is a major producer of nickel, an element used in stainless steel and electric car batteries, and also mines copper and gold."],["A table that keeps growing","Scientists are trying to create element 119 and beyond, which would begin an eighth row. Some predict an island of stability, where certain very heavy elements might last longer than their neighbors. In 2019 the world celebrated the International Year of the Periodic Table, 150 years after Mendeleev’s work."]],
sp:["A dream of a table","A popular story says that Dmitri Mendeleev, after struggling for days to find a pattern in the elements, fell asleep at his desk and saw in a dream a table where all the elements fell into place. He woke and wrote it down. Historians doubt the dream story, noting that he had worked for years on the problem, writing the elements and their properties on cards and arranging them like a game of patience. Whatever the truth, the table he published in 1869 changed chemistry. Element 101, mendelevium, was named in his honor in 1955."],
g:[["Element","A pure substance made of only one kind of atom"],["Atomic number","The number of protons in an atom’s nucleus"],["Group","A column of the periodic table"],["Period","A row of the periodic table"]],
f:["The periodic table has 118 confirmed elements.","Mendeleev predicted elements before they were found.","Au, the symbol for gold, comes from Latin."],
fun:"Only two elements are liquid at room temperature: mercury and bromine.",
tl:"Ang periodic table ay tsart ng lahat ng kilalang elemento na inayos ayon sa atomic number. Magkahawig ang katangian ng mga elementong nasa iisang hanay. Si Dmitri Mendeleev ang naglathala ng unang malawakang ginamit na talahanayan noong 1869."});

ADDART({id:"catalysts",t:"Catalysts",c:"Chemistry",i:"⚗️",w:"Catalysis",r:["reactions","digest","cars","molecules","foodsecurity"],
p:[["Speeding up reactions","A catalyst is a substance that speeds up a chemical reaction without being used up."],["Catalysts of life","Living things use catalysts called enzymes."],["In industry","Catalysts help make fertilizer, fuels, plastics and medicines."]],
l:"A catalyst is a substance that makes a chemical reaction happen faster, or at a lower temperature, without itself being permanently changed or used up. It works by providing an easier pathway for the reaction, lowering the amount of energy needed to get it started. Catalysts are essential to life: the enzymes in our bodies are biological catalysts that digest food, copy DNA and release energy. They are also vital to modern industry, where they help make fertilizers, fuels, plastics, medicines and countless other products, and in cars, where catalytic converters clean exhaust gases. Most of the world’s chemical products are made using catalysts at some stage.",
s:[["How catalysts work","For a reaction to happen, molecules must collide with enough energy to break old bonds and form new ones. This energy barrier is called the activation energy. A catalyst offers a different route with a lower barrier, so more collisions succeed and the reaction speeds up. At the end of the reaction, the catalyst is left unchanged and can be used again."],["Enzymes","Enzymes are proteins that act as catalysts in living cells. Each enzyme fits only certain molecules, like a lock and key. Amylase in saliva begins breaking down starch in rice and bread, while enzymes in the stomach and intestines digest proteins and fats. Without enzymes, many reactions in the body would be far too slow to sustain life."],["Types of catalysts","Some catalysts are mixed in with the reacting substances, often in a liquid. Others are solids, such as metals or minerals, on whose surfaces gases or liquids react. Metals such as platinum, palladium, nickel and iron are common industrial catalysts."],["Catalytic converters","Since the 1970s many cars have been fitted with catalytic converters in their exhaust systems. Inside, a honeycomb coated with platinum, palladium and rhodium changes poisonous carbon monoxide, unburned fuel and nitrogen oxides into less harmful carbon dioxide, water and nitrogen."]],
k:[["Definition","Speeds up a reaction without being used up"],["Term catalysis coined","Jöns Jacob Berzelius, 1835"],["Biological catalysts","Enzymes"],["Common metal catalysts","Platinum, palladium, nickel, iron"],["Car exhaust cleaner","Catalytic converter"]],
d:[["1835","Jöns Jacob Berzelius coins the word catalysis"],["1909","Wilhelm Ostwald wins the Nobel Prize for work on catalysis"],["1913","The Haber–Bosch process begins making ammonia on an industrial scale"],["1926","James Sumner shows that an enzyme is a protein"],["1975","Catalytic converters are widely fitted to new cars in the United States"],["2021","The Nobel Prize in Chemistry honors new tools for making molecules with organic catalysts"]],
s2:[["Feeding the world","The Haber–Bosch process, developed by Fritz Haber and Carl Bosch in the early 1900s, uses an iron-based catalyst to combine nitrogen from the air with hydrogen to make ammonia, the main ingredient of most fertilizers. Fertilizer made this way helps grow food for a large share of the world’s population. However, the process uses large amounts of energy, and overuse of fertilizer can pollute rivers and seas."],["Fuels and plastics","Oil refineries use catalysts made from minerals called zeolites to break large hydrocarbon molecules into smaller ones for gasoline, diesel and jet fuel. Other catalysts link small molecules into long chains to make plastics such as polyethylene, used in bags and bottles."],["Catalysts in the kitchen and home","Enzymes are added to laundry detergents to break down stains from food, blood and grass. Yeast uses its own enzymes to ferment sugar, making bread rise and producing alcohol in drinks. Meat tenderizers contain enzymes from papaya or pineapple that break down tough proteins."],["Green chemistry","Chemists design new catalysts to make reactions cleaner, using less energy and producing less waste. In 2021 Benjamin List and David MacMillan received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for developing small organic molecules as catalysts, an approach that avoids some toxic metals and helps make medicines more efficiently."],["Catalysts and clean energy","Catalysts are central to many clean energy technologies. Fuel cells use platinum catalysts to combine hydrogen and oxygen into electricity and water, and electrolyzers use catalysts to split water into hydrogen. Researchers are searching for cheaper catalysts, because metals such as platinum are rare and expensive."],["Poisoning a catalyst","A catalyst can stop working if other substances stick to its surface and block it, a process called poisoning. For example, lead in old types of gasoline ruined catalytic converters, which is one reason leaded gasoline was phased out. The Philippines banned leaded gasoline in 2000."]],
sp:["The papaya that softens meat","For generations, cooks in the Philippines and other tropical countries have used unripe papaya to soften tough meat. In dishes such as tinola, green papaya is cooked with chicken, and some cooks wrap meat in papaya leaves or rub it with papaya juice before cooking. The secret is an enzyme called papain, found in the fruit’s milky sap. Papain breaks the long protein chains in meat into smaller pieces, making it more tender. Today papain is extracted and sold as commercial meat tenderizer, and is also used in medicines and cosmetics. It is a delicious example of a natural catalyst at work."],
g:[["Catalyst","A substance that speeds up a reaction without being used up"],["Enzyme","A protein that acts as a catalyst in living things"],["Activation energy","The energy needed to start a reaction"],["Catalytic converter","A device that cleans car exhaust gases"]],
f:["Enzymes are biological catalysts.","Catalysts are not used up in reactions.","Papaya contains a meat-softening enzyme."],
fun:"A single enzyme molecule can speed up millions of reactions every second.",
tl:"Ang catalyst ay sangkap na nagpapabilis ng reaksiyong kemikal nang hindi nauubos. Ang mga enzyme sa ating katawan ay mga catalyst na tumutunaw ng pagkain. Ginagamit din ang catalyst sa paggawa ng pataba, gasolina at gamot."});

ADDART({id:"neutrinos",t:"Neutrinos",c:"Physics",i:"👻",w:"Neutrino",r:["particles","standardmodel","sun","supernova","radioact"],
p:[["Ghost particles","Neutrinos are tiny particles that pass through almost everything."],["Everywhere","Trillions of neutrinos from the Sun pass through your body every second."],["Hard to catch","Scientists need huge underground detectors to see them."]],
l:"A neutrino is a tiny, electrically neutral particle with an extremely small mass. Neutrinos are among the most abundant particles in the universe, produced in huge numbers by nuclear reactions in the Sun and other stars, by exploding stars, by radioactive decay and by nuclear reactors. Because they barely interact with other matter, they can pass straight through people, buildings and even the whole Earth without stopping, earning them the nickname ghost particles. Neutrinos were proposed by Wolfgang Pauli in 1930 and first detected in 1956. The discovery that they change from one type to another showed that they have mass, a finding that earned the 2015 Nobel Prize in Physics.",
s:[["What a neutrino is","Neutrinos belong to a family of particles called leptons, which also includes the electron. They carry no electric charge and are not affected by the strong force that holds atomic nuclei together. They interact only through gravity and the weak nuclear force, which is why they rarely collide with anything."],["Three flavors","There are three types, or flavors, of neutrino: the electron neutrino, the muon neutrino and the tau neutrino. Each is linked to a different charged particle. Experiments have shown that neutrinos can switch between these flavors as they travel, a process called neutrino oscillation."],["Neutrinos from the Sun","Nuclear fusion in the Sun’s core produces enormous numbers of neutrinos. They stream out into space at nearly the speed of light, and tens of billions pass through every square centimeter of Earth every second, day and night, since they travel through the planet as easily as through empty space."],["Catching neutrinos","To detect neutrinos, scientists build huge detectors deep underground, under mountains or in mines, to shield them from other particles. The detectors contain vast amounts of water, ice or special liquids. On the very rare occasions when a neutrino hits an atom, it produces a faint flash of light that sensitive instruments can record."]],
k:[["Proposed by","Wolfgang Pauli, 1930"],["Named by","Enrico Fermi"],["First detected","1956"],["Types","Electron, muon and tau neutrinos"],["Nobel Prize for oscillation","2015"]],
d:[["1930","Wolfgang Pauli proposes the neutrino"],["1934","Enrico Fermi develops a theory of beta decay including the neutrino"],["1956","Clyde Cowan and Frederick Reines detect neutrinos from a nuclear reactor"],["1987","Detectors record neutrinos from a supernova in a nearby galaxy"],["1998","Super-Kamiokande in Japan finds evidence that neutrinos change type"],["2013","The IceCube detector in Antarctica reports neutrinos from beyond the solar system"]],
s2:[["Pauli’s desperate remedy","In the 1920s physicists were puzzled by beta decay, a type of radioactivity in which energy seemed to disappear. In 1930 Wolfgang Pauli suggested that an unseen, neutral particle was carrying away the missing energy. He called his idea a desperate remedy and worried that the particle could never be detected. Enrico Fermi later named it the neutrino, Italian for little neutral one."],["The first detection","In 1956 the American physicists Clyde Cowan and Frederick Reines placed tanks of water and detectors next to a nuclear reactor in South Carolina, which produced huge numbers of neutrinos. They recorded the telltale signals of neutrinos striking protons and sent Pauli a telegram announcing their success. Reines shared the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1995."],["The solar neutrino problem","From the 1960s, experiments counting neutrinos from the Sun found only about a third to a half of the number expected. Scientists wondered whether their models of the Sun were wrong. The answer came around 2000: many electron neutrinos change into other flavors on their way to Earth, so early detectors, which saw only one flavor, missed them."],["Neutrinos have mass","The standard theory of particle physics originally assumed neutrinos had no mass. The discovery of oscillation, by the Super-Kamiokande experiment in Japan in 1998 and the Sudbury Neutrino Observatory in Canada in 2001, proved they must have a tiny mass. Takaaki Kajita and Arthur McDonald shared the 2015 Nobel Prize for this work. Their exact masses are still being measured."],["Messages from exploding stars","In February 1987 a star exploded as a supernova in the Large Magellanic Cloud, a small galaxy near the Milky Way. A few hours before its light was seen, detectors in Japan and the United States recorded a burst of about two dozen neutrinos. It was the first time neutrinos from beyond the solar system had been detected, opening a new way of studying the universe."],["Neutrino telescopes","IceCube, buried in a cubic kilometer of ice at the South Pole, watches for flashes of light from neutrinos crossing the ice. In 2018 it traced a high-energy neutrino to a distant galaxy with a giant black hole. Other huge detectors are being built in Japan, the United States and China to learn more about these elusive particles."]],
sp:["A telegram to Pauli","On 14 June 1956, Clyde Cowan and Frederick Reines sent a telegram to Wolfgang Pauli, who was attending a conference in Switzerland. It said that they had definitely detected neutrinos from a nuclear reactor. More than 25 years after Pauli had predicted the particle, worrying that he had proposed something no one could ever find, his ghostly particle had been caught. Pauli reportedly interrupted the conference to read the message aloud, and later replied that everything comes to him who knows how to wait. He is also said to have celebrated with friends by sharing a case of champagne."],
g:[["Neutrino","A tiny neutral particle that rarely interacts with matter"],["Lepton","A family of particles that includes electrons and neutrinos"],["Oscillation","The changing of a neutrino from one type to another"],["Weak nuclear force","One of the four fundamental forces, involved in radioactive decay"]],
f:["Neutrinos can pass through the whole Earth.","Neutrinos were first detected in 1956.","There are three types of neutrino."],
fun:"About 100 trillion neutrinos from the Sun pass through your body every second, and you never feel a thing.",
tl:"Ang neutrino ay napakaliit na partikulong walang karga na dumaraan sa halos lahat ng bagay. Trilyon-trilyong neutrino mula sa Araw ang tumatagos sa ating katawan bawat segundo. Natuklasan ito noong 1956 gamit ang malalaking detector."});

ADDART({id:"higgs",t:"The Higgs Boson",c:"Physics",i:"✨",w:"Higgs boson",r:["standardmodel","accelerators","particles","quantum","atom"],
p:[["A missing piece","The Higgs boson is a particle linked to the field that gives other particles mass."],["Found in 2012","Scientists at CERN announced its discovery on 4 July 2012."],["A Nobel Prize","Peter Higgs and François Englert received the 2013 Nobel Prize in Physics."]],
l:"The Higgs boson is an elementary particle connected to the Higgs field, an invisible field thought to fill the whole universe and give mass to fundamental particles such as electrons and quarks. Without it, these particles would have no mass, and atoms, stars and planets could not form. The field and its particle were proposed in 1964 by several physicists, including Peter Higgs, François Englert and Robert Brout. Nearly 50 years later, on 4 July 2012, scientists working with the Large Hadron Collider at CERN, near Geneva, announced that they had found it. The discovery completed the Standard Model, the theory describing the known fundamental particles and forces.",
s:[["What the Higgs field does","Physicists describe the Higgs field as something like an invisible substance spread through space. Particles that interact strongly with it behave as if they are heavy, while particles that interact weakly are light, and particles such as photons, which do not interact with it, have no mass at all. The Higgs boson is a ripple, or excitation, in this field."],["Why it mattered","The Standard Model successfully explained many experiments, but it needed a way for particles to have mass without breaking its mathematics. The Higgs mechanism provided that. Finding the Higgs boson was the final test of the theory’s last missing piece."],["The Large Hadron Collider","The Large Hadron Collider, or LHC, is the world’s largest and most powerful particle accelerator, a ring 27 kilometers around buried beneath the border of France and Switzerland. It smashes protons together at nearly the speed of light, briefly creating new particles, including, very rarely, Higgs bosons, which decay almost instantly into other particles."],["How it was found","Because a Higgs boson lasts only a tiny fraction of a second, scientists cannot see it directly. Instead, giant detectors called ATLAS and CMS recorded the particles it decays into. By analyzing billions of collisions, both teams found a small bump in the data at a mass of about 125 billion electronvolts, the signature of a new particle."]],
k:[["Proposed","1964"],["Discovery announced","4 July 2012"],["Where","CERN, Large Hadron Collider"],["Mass","About 125 GeV"],["Nobel Prize","Peter Higgs and François Englert, 2013"]],
d:[["1964","Higgs, Englert and Brout, and other physicists propose the mechanism"],["1993","Physicist Leon Lederman’s book popularizes the nickname God particle"],["2008","The Large Hadron Collider starts operating"],["2012","CERN announces the discovery of a Higgs-like particle"],["2013","Peter Higgs and François Englert receive the Nobel Prize in Physics"],["2024","Peter Higgs dies at the age of 94"]],
s2:[["Many discoverers","In 1964 three separate groups published papers on how particles could gain mass: Robert Brout and François Englert in Belgium, Peter Higgs in Scotland, and Gerald Guralnik, Carl Hagen and Tom Kibble in London. Higgs pointed out that the theory predicted a new particle, which came to bear his name. Brout died in 2011, before the Nobel Prize was awarded."],["The God particle","The physicist Leon Lederman nicknamed the Higgs boson the God particle in a 1993 book. The name caught on in newspapers but was disliked by many scientists, including Peter Higgs, who was not religious and felt it was misleading. Lederman later joked that his publisher would not let him call it the goddamn particle, his original complaint about how hard it was to find."],["The announcement","On 4 July 2012, scientists packed an auditorium at CERN, while others watched online around the world, as the ATLAS and CMS teams presented their results. When the evidence was shown, the audience broke into applause. Peter Higgs, then 83, was seen wiping away tears. CERN’s director, Rolf-Dieter Heuer, said, I think we have it."],["A global effort","Thousands of scientists and engineers from more than 100 countries worked on the LHC and its detectors, including researchers from Asia, Africa and Latin America. Physicists from the Philippines have also taken part in CERN collaborations and training programs, and CERN welcomes students from many nations."],["What remains unknown","The Higgs boson completed the Standard Model, but the model does not explain everything. It does not include gravity, dark matter or dark energy, and it cannot explain why the universe contains more matter than antimatter. Scientists study the Higgs boson’s properties closely, hoping to find small differences from predictions that might point to new physics."],["The future of colliders","The LHC is being upgraded to produce many more collisions from around 2030. Physicists have proposed even larger colliders, including a ring of about 90 kilometers at CERN and a large collider in China, to study the Higgs boson in greater detail. Supporters say these machines could reveal new particles, while critics question their very high cost."]],
sp:["A shy man and a famous particle","Peter Higgs was a quiet, modest physicist who disliked fame. He developed his idea in the summer of 1964 at the University of Edinburgh, and his paper predicting the new particle was at first rejected by one journal. For decades he watched as experiments searched without success. When the discovery was announced in 2012, he was overwhelmed, saying he had never expected it to happen in his lifetime. On the day the Nobel Prize was announced in 2013, he famously avoided reporters by going out for lunch, and only learned the news when a former neighbor congratulated him on the street."],
g:[["Higgs boson","A particle linked to the field that gives other particles mass"],["Higgs field","An invisible field thought to fill all of space"],["Standard Model","The theory describing known particles and forces"],["CERN","The European laboratory for particle physics near Geneva"]],
f:["The Higgs boson was found in 2012.","The LHC is 27 kilometers around.","It is nicknamed the God particle."],
fun:"A Higgs boson exists for only about a tenth of a billionth of a trillionth of a second before decaying.",
tl:"Ang Higgs boson ay partikulong kaugnay ng larangang nagbibigay ng masa sa ibang partikulo. Inihayag ng CERN ang pagkatuklas nito noong 4 Hulyo 2012. Nakumpleto nito ang Standard Model ng pisika."});

ADDART({id:"evolution",t:"Discovering Evolution",c:"History of Science",i:"🦎",w:"History of evolutionary thought",r:["evo","darwin","fossils","mendel","genome"],
p:[["Life changes","Evolution is the process by which living things change over many generations."],["How it works","Charles Darwin and Alfred Russel Wallace explained how it works through natural selection."],["Shared ancestry","All living things are related through common ancestors."]],
l:"The discovery of evolution, the process by which populations of living things change over many generations through changes in their inherited traits, is one of the great stories of science. The modern theory began with Charles Darwin and Alfred Russel Wallace, who in 1858 independently proposed natural selection as its main cause: individuals with traits that help them survive and reproduce tend to leave more offspring, so those traits become more common over time. Darwin’s book On the Origin of Species, published in 1859, presented the evidence in detail. Later discoveries in genetics, fossils and DNA have strongly supported the theory, which is the foundation of modern biology. It explains how the great diversity of life on Earth arose from common ancestors over billions of years.",
s:[["Natural selection","Members of a species vary in size, color, speed and many other traits, and some of these differences are inherited. More young are born than can survive, so individuals with helpful traits are more likely to live and reproduce. Over many generations this process, natural selection, can change a population and eventually produce new species."],["Evidence from fossils","Fossils preserved in layers of rock show that life has changed over time, from simple single-celled organisms to fish, amphibians, reptiles, mammals and birds. Transitional fossils, such as Archaeopteryx, which had features of both dinosaurs and birds, and Tiktaalik, a fish with limb-like fins, show stages between major groups."],["Evidence from genes","DNA allows scientists to compare species directly. Humans share most of their DNA with chimpanzees, and a large share with mice, fish and even plants, reflecting common ancestry. Genetic comparisons let scientists build family trees of life that match those based on anatomy and fossils."],["Evolution we can see","Evolution is usually slow, but some changes happen fast enough to observe. Bacteria evolve resistance to antibiotics, insects become resistant to pesticides, and scientists studying finches on the Galápagos Islands have measured changes in beak size after droughts within just a few generations."]],
k:[["Main mechanism","Natural selection"],["Proposed by","Charles Darwin and Alfred Russel Wallace"],["Key book","On the Origin of Species (1859)"],["Age of life on Earth","About 3.5 billion years or more"],["Modern humans appeared","About 300,000 years ago"]],
d:[["1809","Jean-Baptiste Lamarck proposes an early theory of evolution"],["1835","Charles Darwin visits the Galápagos Islands aboard HMS Beagle"],["1858","Darwin and Wallace present natural selection in London"],["1859","Darwin publishes On the Origin of Species"],["1866","Gregor Mendel publishes his laws of inheritance"],["1953","The structure of DNA is discovered"]],
s2:[["Darwin’s voyage","From 1831 to 1836 the young naturalist Charles Darwin sailed around the world aboard the British survey ship HMS Beagle. He collected fossils, plants and animals in South America, the Galápagos Islands and elsewhere. He noticed that animals on different islands differed slightly, and over the next 20 years he developed his ideas about how species change."],["Wallace in Southeast Asia","Alfred Russel Wallace reached the same idea while collecting animals in the Malay Archipelago, in what are now Malaysia and Indonesia. He also noticed a sharp boundary between Asian and Australian animals, now called the Wallace Line. In 1858 he sent his ideas to Darwin, and their work was presented together at a meeting in London."],["Genetics joins the theory","Darwin did not know how traits were passed on. The work of Gregor Mendel on pea plants, rediscovered in 1900, showed that traits are inherited through units now called genes. In the 1930s and 1940s scientists combined genetics and natural selection into the modern synthesis, and the discovery of DNA’s structure in 1953 revealed how genes are copied and changed."],["Evolution in the Philippines","The Philippines is a natural laboratory of evolution. Because its islands were separated for long periods, many species evolved there that are found nowhere else, such as the Philippine eagle, the tarsier and many kinds of cloud rats and frogs. In 2019 scientists described Homo luzonensis, an extinct human species, from fossils found in Callao Cave in Cagayan."],["Human evolution","Fossils and DNA show that humans and chimpanzees shared a common ancestor several million years ago. Many extinct human species lived in the past, including Homo erectus and the Neanderthals. Our own species, Homo sapiens, appeared in Africa about 300,000 years ago and later spread around the world."],["Evolution and belief","Evolution is accepted by the overwhelming majority of scientists. Some religious groups reject it, holding that species were created separately as described in literal readings of scripture, a view called creationism. Many other religious communities, including the Catholic Church, teach that evolution can be compatible with faith in a creator. Debates have arisen in some countries over how evolution is taught in schools."]],
sp:["The finches that changed","Since 1973 the biologists Peter and Rosemary Grant have spent part of nearly every year on the tiny Galápagos island of Daphne Major, catching, measuring and tagging its finches. In 1977 a severe drought killed most of the plants with small, soft seeds, leaving mainly large, hard seeds. Finches with bigger, stronger beaks survived better, and the next generation had noticeably larger beaks. Years later, when wet weather brought back small seeds, the trend reversed. The Grants showed that natural selection, which Darwin thought too slow to watch, could be measured in real time."],
g:[["Evolution","Change in the inherited traits of living things over generations"],["Natural selection","The process by which helpful traits become more common"],["Species","A group of living things that can breed together"],["Common ancestor","An ancestor shared by two or more species"]],
f:["Darwin published On the Origin of Species in 1859.","Wallace developed the same idea in Southeast Asia.","Bacteria can evolve resistance to antibiotics."],
fun:"Darwin spent eight years studying barnacles before publishing his theory, partly to build his reputation as a careful scientist.",
tl:"Ang ebolusyon ay ang proseso kung saan nagbabago ang mga nilalang sa paglipas ng maraming henerasyon. Ipinaliwanag nina Charles Darwin at Alfred Russel Wallace na ito ay dulot ng natural selection. Sinusuportahan ito ng mga fossil at ng DNA."});

;
/* data/arts8.js */
// Arts and Culture, more articles: the sarswela, documentary film, comedy and street art.
ADDART({id:"zarzuela",t:"Sarswela",c:"Performing Arts",i:"🎭",w:"Sarsuwela",r:["theatre","opera","phcinema","opm","musicals"],
p:[["Musical theater","The sarswela is a form of musical theater with spoken dialogue, songs and dances."],["From Spain","It came from the Spanish zarzuela and became a beloved Filipino art form."],["A golden age","Tagalog sarswela flourished in the early 1900s."]],
l:"The sarswela, also spelled sarsuwela, is a form of musical theater that mixes spoken scenes with songs, dances and instrumental music. It developed from the zarzuela of Spain, a popular theater form that takes its name from a royal hunting lodge near Madrid where such entertainments were staged in the 1600s. Spanish troupes brought the zarzuela to Manila in the late 1800s, and Filipino writers and composers soon created their own in Tagalog and other Philippine languages, with local stories, characters and music such as the kundiman. Its golden age came in the first decades of the 1900s, before films and vaudeville drew audiences away. Today the sarswela is revived by theater companies and schools as part of the nation’s cultural heritage.",
s:[["The Spanish zarzuela","The zarzuela began in 17th-century Spain as court entertainment combining spoken drama with music. The playwright Pedro Calderón de la Barca wrote some of the earliest works. In the 1800s it became hugely popular with ordinary Spaniards, with comic, romantic stories set in everyday city and village life."],["Arrival in the Philippines","Spanish touring companies performed zarzuelas in Manila from the late 1870s. Filipino audiences loved the mix of drama, romance, comedy and music. Local artists began translating Spanish works and then writing their own, and by the end of the 1800s sarswela in Philippine languages was appearing on Manila stages."],["Stories and characters","Filipino sarswelas often told stories of love between young people from different social classes, family conflicts, greedy landlords, village fiestas and the problems of everyday life. Many included comic servants and lively crowd scenes, and they usually ended happily. Some carried messages about patriotism, freedom or social justice."],["Music of the sarswela","Composers wrote songs in styles audiences knew, including the kundiman, the slow and heartfelt Filipino love song, the danza and lively marches. Orchestras or small bands accompanied the singers. Many sarswela songs became popular hits that people sang at home and at gatherings long after the shows closed."]],
k:[["Also spelled","Sarsuwela"],["Origin","Spanish zarzuela"],["Golden age in the Philippines","About 1900 to 1930"],["Famous work","Walang Sugat (1902)"],["Famous star","Atang de la Rama"]],
d:[["1657","Calderón de la Barca writes one of the first Spanish zarzuelas"],["1878","Spanish zarzuela troupes begin performing regularly in Manila"],["1902","Walang Sugat by Severino Reyes premieres"],["1917","Dalagang Bukid by Hermogenes Ilagan and Leon Ignacio premieres"],["1919","A film version of Dalagang Bukid becomes the first Filipino-made feature film"],["1987","Atang de la Rama is named National Artist for Theater and Music"]],
s2:[["Walang Sugat","Walang Sugat, meaning not wounded, was written by Severino Reyes with music by Fulgencio Tolentino and first performed in 1902. Set during the revolution against Spain, it tells of Tenyong, a young man who joins the revolutionaries, and his beloved Julia, whose mother wants her to marry a rich man. It is one of the most famous and frequently revived Filipino sarswelas."],["Dalagang Bukid","Dalagang Bukid, meaning country maiden, by Hermogenes Ilagan with music by Leon Ignacio, premiered in 1917. It tells of a flower vendor whose parents push her to marry a wealthy old man. In 1919 José Nepomuceno turned it into a film, considered the first feature film made by a Filipino, starring Atang de la Rama."],["Theater and resistance","In the early years of American rule, some Filipino playwrights wrote dramas with hidden messages against colonial rule, using symbols such as the flag, which was then banned. Several writers were arrested for these so-called seditious plays. Sarswelas themselves were mostly romantic and comic, but some also expressed national pride."],["Atang de la Rama","Honorata de la Rama, known as Atang, began performing as a child and became the most celebrated sarswela star of her time, admired as the Queen of the Kundiman. She performed across the Philippines and abroad and later championed Filipino theater and music. In 1987 she was named a National Artist."],["Decline and revival","From the 1920s and 1930s, sarswela faced competition from movies, radio and bodabil, or vaudeville. Many theaters closed, and the sarswela became rare. From the 1970s onward, groups such as the Cultural Center of the Philippines, Tanghalang Pilipino, university theater companies and regional groups revived classic works and created new ones."],["Beyond Manila","Sarswela also thrived in other regions in local languages. In Iloilo and other parts of the Visayas, playwrights wrote sarswelas in Hiligaynon and Cebuano, and Ilocano and Pampango versions were performed in the north. These local traditions added regional music, humor and stories to the form."]],
sp:["An evening at the sarswela","In the early 1900s, a night at the sarswela was a major social event in Manila and provincial towns. Families dressed in their best clothes and filled wooden theaters such as the Teatro Zorrilla and the Manila Grand Opera House. Vendors sold snacks outside, and the audience cheered favorite singers, laughed at comic servants and wept during sad love songs. Popular performers became stars whose photographs were sold and whose songs were hummed in the streets. For many Filipinos it was the main form of entertainment before the coming of cinema."],
g:[["Sarswela","Filipino musical theater with spoken dialogue and songs"],["Zarzuela","The Spanish musical theater form from which the sarswela came"],["Kundiman","A slow, heartfelt Filipino love song"],["Bodabil","Filipino vaudeville, a variety show of songs, dances and comedy"]],
f:["The sarswela came from the Spanish zarzuela.","Walang Sugat premiered in 1902.","Atang de la Rama was named a National Artist."],
fun:"The word zarzuela comes from the Palacio de la Zarzuela, a royal lodge near Madrid whose name refers to the brambles, zarzas, that grew there.",
tl:"Ang sarswela ay dulang musikal na may diyalogo, awit at sayaw na nagmula sa zarzuela ng Espanya. Umunlad ito sa Pilipinas noong unang mga dekada ng 1900. Kabilang sa mga tanyag na sarswela ang Walang Sugat at Dalagang Bukid."});

ADDART({id:"documentary",t:"Documentary Film",c:"Film",i:"🎥",w:"Documentary film",r:["cinema","phcinema","tv","streaming","photography"],
p:[["Real life on screen","A documentary is a nonfiction film about real people, places and events."],["Many kinds","Documentaries cover nature, history, science, politics, music and everyday life."],["Truth and storytelling","Filmmakers balance telling a story with showing the truth."]],
l:"A documentary is a nonfiction film or program that records real people, places and events in order to inform, explain, persuade or preserve a record. Documentaries can explore almost any subject, from wildlife and science to history, politics, sport, music and ordinary lives. The word was first used in this sense in 1926 by the Scottish filmmaker John Grierson, who described the documentary as the creative treatment of actuality. Early landmarks include Robert Flaherty’s Nanook of the North in 1922. Today documentaries are made for cinemas, television and streaming services, and range from huge nature series to small films shot on phones. Questions about truth, fairness and the filmmaker’s point of view are central to the art form.",
s:[["Beginnings","The first films ever shown, by the Lumière brothers in France in 1895, were short scenes of real life, such as workers leaving a factory and a train arriving at a station. Newsreels showing current events became common in cinemas in the early 1900s, informing audiences before television existed."],["Nanook of the North","In 1922 the American filmmaker Robert Flaherty released Nanook of the North, about an Inuit family in Arctic Canada. It was a great success and is often called the first feature-length documentary. Later it was revealed that Flaherty staged some scenes and asked people to use traditional methods they no longer used, starting long debates about how much documentaries may shape reality."],["Styles of documentary","Some documentaries use a narrator who explains what is shown. Others, in the style called observational or cinéma vérité, follow people with a camera and let events unfold. Some mix interviews, archive footage, animation and reenactments. Each approach gives a different sense of what is real."],["Nature documentaries","Wildlife films have become one of the most popular kinds of documentary. The British naturalist David Attenborough has presented landmark series such as Life on Earth in 1979 and Planet Earth in 2006, using new camera technology to show animal behavior never filmed before. Such films have increased public interest in conservation."]],
k:[["Term coined by","John Grierson, 1926"],["Early landmark","Nanook of the North (1922)"],["First films of real life","Lumière brothers, 1895"],["Famous presenter","David Attenborough"],["Also called","Docu, doc"]],
d:[["1895","The Lumière brothers show short films of real life in Paris"],["1922","Robert Flaherty releases Nanook of the North"],["1926","John Grierson uses the word documentary for film"],["1929","Dziga Vertov makes Man with a Movie Camera"],["1979","David Attenborough’s Life on Earth is broadcast"],["2006","Planet Earth is broadcast"]],
s2:[["Propaganda films","Documentary techniques can be used to persuade rather than inform. During the 1930s and the Second World War, governments made films to spread their political messages. Triumph of the Will, made by Leni Riefenstahl in 1935 for the Nazi regime, is often studied as a powerful but deeply troubling example of how film can be used as propaganda."],["Documentaries that changed things","Some documentaries have influenced public debate and policy, on subjects such as the environment, health, food and justice. Films about wrongful convictions have led to cases being reopened, while environmental documentaries have raised awareness of climate change, plastic pollution and endangered species. Critics note that films with a strong message may leave out other viewpoints."],["Documentaries in the Philippines","Filipino filmmakers have made documentaries about history, culture, Indigenous communities, migrant workers, disasters and politics. Television networks have long produced documentary programs and investigative reports, and film festivals such as Cinemalaya and those of the Cultural Center of the Philippines have featured documentaries. Some Filipino documentaries have won awards at international festivals."],["The ethics of filming real people","Documentary makers must think carefully about the people they film. They ask for consent, try to represent people fairly, and consider how a film might affect those shown, especially children or vulnerable people. Viewers and critics debate whether reenactments, music and editing can make a film misleading even when its facts are accurate."],["The streaming boom","Streaming services have greatly increased the number of documentaries made and watched. True crime series, sports documentaries, celebrity biographies and nature programs attract large global audiences. Some critics worry that the pressure to entertain encourages sensational stories, while others welcome the chance for more voices and subjects to reach viewers."],["Making a documentary today","Light digital cameras, drones and phones have made documentary filmmaking more affordable. Students, community groups and citizen journalists can record important events and share them online. Skills such as research, interviewing, fact-checking and careful editing remain essential to making a documentary that viewers can trust."]],
sp:["Filming the deep","Making a big nature documentary can take years. For a single sequence, a camera crew may wait weeks in a hide in the rainforest or spend months at sea to capture an animal behavior that lasts only a few minutes. Crews have used cameras that work in near darkness, tiny cameras hidden inside fake rocks or animals, and deep-sea submersibles to film creatures thousands of meters below the surface. When the BBC series Blue Planet II showed the effects of plastic waste on ocean life in 2017, it sparked public calls to reduce single-use plastics in several countries."],
g:[["Documentary","A nonfiction film about real people and events"],["Newsreel","A short film of news shown in cinemas"],["Cinéma vérité","A documentary style that observes events directly"],["Reenactment","Acting out real past events for a film"]],
f:["John Grierson coined the term documentary in 1926.","Nanook of the North was released in 1922.","The first films ever shown were of real life."],
fun:"The very first films shown to a paying audience, in Paris in 1895, were short documentaries less than a minute long.",
tl:"Ang dokumentaryo ay pelikulang hindi kathang-isip na nagpapakita ng tunay na tao, lugar at pangyayari. Sinasaklaw nito ang kalikasan, kasaysayan, agham at pulitika. Mahalaga sa paggawa nito ang katotohanan at pagiging patas."});

ADDART({id:"comedy",t:"Comedy",c:"Performing Arts",i:"😂",w:"Comedy",r:["theatre","tv","cinema","phcinema","memes"],
p:[["Making people laugh","Comedy is entertainment meant to make people laugh."],["Ancient roots","Comedy began as a form of theater in ancient Greece."],["Many forms","Today it includes stand-up, sitcoms, comedy films, sketches and online videos."]],
l:"Comedy is a kind of entertainment, in theater, film, television, writing and live performance, that aims to amuse people and make them laugh. As a form of drama, it began in ancient Greece, where comic plays were performed at festivals in Athens. Over the centuries comedy took many forms, including the masked clowns of Italian commedia dell’arte, the plays of Shakespeare and Molière, silent film slapstick, radio and television sitcoms, stand-up routines and short online videos. Comedy can be gentle or sharp, silly or clever, and often comments on daily life, society and politics. In the Philippines, comedians have been among the country’s most beloved entertainers.",
s:[["Greek and Roman comedy","Ancient Greek comedy was performed at festivals in honor of the god Dionysus. Aristophanes, who wrote in the 400s BC, mocked politicians, philosophers and the war of his time. Later Greek and Roman writers, such as Menander, Plautus and Terence, wrote comedies about mistaken identities, clever servants and young lovers, plots still used today."],["Commedia dell’arte","In 16th-century Italy, traveling troupes performed commedia dell’arte, improvised comedies with stock characters wearing masks, such as the foolish old merchant Pantalone and the tricky servant Arlecchino, or Harlequin. Their physical humor and comic routines influenced theater across Europe."],["Shakespeare and Molière","William Shakespeare wrote comedies such as A Midsummer Night’s Dream and Twelfth Night, full of disguises, misunderstandings and happy endings. In France, Molière wrote sharp comedies mocking hypocrisy, greed and snobbery, such as Tartuffe and The Miser."],["Comedy on screen","Silent film comedians such as Charlie Chaplin and Buster Keaton became world stars with slapstick, comic chases and stunts. With radio and television came situation comedies, or sitcoms, built around the same characters in everyday settings, along with sketch shows, comedy variety programs and talk shows."]],
k:[["Origin","Ancient Greece"],["Famous Greek comic playwright","Aristophanes"],["Italian masked comedy","Commedia dell’arte"],["Silent film stars","Charlie Chaplin, Buster Keaton"],["Philippine King of Comedy","Dolphy"]],
d:[["c. 425 BC","Aristophanes’ comedies are performed in Athens"],["1550s","Commedia dell’arte troupes perform across Italy"],["1664","Molière’s Tartuffe is first performed"],["1914","Charlie Chaplin’s Little Tramp character first appears on film"],["1979","Eat Bulaga! premieres on Philippine television"],["2010","Dolphy receives the Grand Collar of the Order of the Golden Heart"]],
s2:[["Stand-up comedy","In stand-up comedy, a single performer speaks directly to an audience, telling jokes and stories, often about their own life and observations. It grew from music hall and vaudeville acts and became especially popular in the United States and Britain in the 20th century. Comedy clubs, television specials and online videos have spread it worldwide, including to comedy bars in Metro Manila."],["Comedy in the Philippines","Filipino comedy grew from bodabil, the variety shows of the early 1900s, and from radio programs. Comedians such as Dolphy, often called the King of Comedy, Panchito, Chiquito and Babalu starred in hugely popular films and television shows. Later stars included Vic Sotto, Joey de Leon, Tito Sotto, Vice Ganda and many others, whose humor often relies on wordplay, timing and playful teasing."],["Why we laugh","Scientists and philosophers have offered several explanations for laughter. Some say we laugh at surprise, when an expected pattern is suddenly broken; others say laughter releases tension or shows a sense of superiority. Laughter also bonds people together, which may be why we laugh much more when we are with others than when alone."],["Satire","Satire uses humor to criticize people, governments or society. It has a long history, from Aristophanes to the writer Jonathan Swift, and today appears in political cartoons, comedy news shows, parody songs and online memes. In many countries satire is valued as a form of free expression, while some governments have punished comedians for mocking leaders."],["Comedy and offense","Comedy often pushes boundaries, and audiences disagree about what is acceptable. Some argue that comedians should be free to joke about any subject, while others say jokes that rely on stereotypes about race, religion, gender, disability or appearance can hurt people. Debates about where to draw the line continue among comedians and audiences."],["Comedy goes online","Social media and video platforms allow anyone to share comic sketches, parodies, reaction videos and memes, reaching millions of viewers without a television network or film studio. Many Filipino comedians and content creators first became famous online before moving to television and film."]],
sp:["The longest-running noontime show","On 30 July 1979 a variety program called Eat Bulaga! premiered on Philippine television, hosted by the comic trio Tito Sotto, Vic Sotto and Joey de Leon. Mixing games, songs, comedy segments and giveaways, it became a lunchtime habit for millions of Filipino families and one of the longest-running television shows of its kind in the world. Its comic segments, catchphrases and segments featuring ordinary people became part of popular culture. In 2023 the hosts left the program’s original production company and continued on another channel, leading to a legal dispute over the show’s name."],
g:[["Comedy","Entertainment meant to make people laugh"],["Satire","Humor that criticizes people or society"],["Slapstick","Comedy based on physical action, such as falls and chases"],["Sitcom","A situation comedy, usually on television"]],
f:["Comedy began in ancient Greece.","Charlie Chaplin was a silent film star.","Dolphy is called the King of Comedy."],
fun:"People are about 30 times more likely to laugh when they are with others than when they are alone, according to some studies.",
tl:"Ang komedya ay libangang layuning magpatawa ng tao. Nagsimula ito bilang dula sa sinaunang Gresya. Sa Pilipinas, kabilang si Dolphy, ang tinaguriang Hari ng Komedya, sa pinakaminamahal na alagad ng sining."});

ADDART({id:"streetart",t:"Street Art",c:"Art",i:"🎨",w:"Street art",r:["modernart","jeepney","photography","architecture","caveart"],
p:[["Art in public","Street art is art made in public places such as walls, streets and bridges."],["Graffiti and murals","It includes graffiti, painted murals, stencils, stickers and installations."],["Art or vandalism","Whether it is art or vandalism depends on permission and point of view."]],
l:"Street art is visual art created in public spaces, such as walls, buildings, bridges, sidewalks and vehicles, where anyone can see it. It includes graffiti, large painted murals, stencil art, posters, stickers, mosaics and sculptures. People have written and drawn on public walls since ancient times, but modern street art grew from the graffiti that spread across New York City in the 1970s. Some street art is painted illegally, while much is now commissioned by cities, businesses and communities to brighten neighborhoods. Street artists such as Banksy have become world-famous, and their work sells for millions. Debates continue over when street art is a public gift and when it is vandalism.",
s:[["Ancient graffiti","The word graffiti comes from the Italian for scratched. Ancient Romans scratched messages, jokes, election slogans and drawings on walls, many of which survived at Pompeii, the city buried by the eruption of Mount Vesuvius in AD 79. Even earlier, people painted and carved images on cave walls and rocks."],["New York graffiti","In the late 1960s and 1970s, young people in Philadelphia and New York began writing their nicknames, called tags, on walls and subway trains. Writers competed to create bigger, more colorful and more stylish pieces. Graffiti became one of the four elements of hip-hop culture, along with rapping, DJing and breakdancing."],["Murals","Murals are large paintings made directly on walls. The Mexican muralists of the 1920s and 1930s, such as Diego Rivera, José Clemente Orozco and David Alfaro Siqueiros, painted huge public works about history and the lives of workers. Many modern street artists paint murals with permission, often several stories high."],["Stencils and stickers","Some street artists use stencils, cut-out designs that let them spray an image quickly and repeat it in many places. Others paste up posters or stick small printed stickers. The British artist Banksy is famous for stencil works with humorous or political messages, while the American artist Shepard Fairey became known for his sticker and poster campaigns."]],
k:[["Word graffiti from","Italian, meaning scratched"],["Modern graffiti began","Philadelphia and New York, late 1960s and 1970s"],["Famous street artist","Banksy"],["Mexican muralists","Rivera, Orozco, Siqueiros"],["Philippine example","Painted jeepneys"]],
d:[["AD 79","The eruption of Vesuvius preserves graffiti on the walls of Pompeii"],["1920s","Mexican muralists begin painting large public murals"],["1970s","Graffiti spreads across New York City’s subway trains"],["1990","Artists paint the East Side Gallery on a remaining stretch of the Berlin Wall"],["2018","A Banksy painting partly shreds itself just after being sold at auction"],["2021","The shredded Banksy work sells again for a record price"]],
s2:[["Art or vandalism?","Painting on property without the owner’s permission is illegal in most places, and cleaning graffiti costs cities large sums. Critics see unauthorized graffiti as vandalism that makes neighborhoods look neglected. Supporters argue that street art brings color, creativity and important messages to dull or forgotten spaces. Many cities now offer legal walls where artists may paint freely."],["Banksy","Banksy is a British street artist whose real identity has been kept secret. Since the 1990s, Banksy’s stencil works have appeared in Bristol, London, New York, Bethlehem and elsewhere, often commenting on war, money, power and consumerism. In 2018, moments after his painting Girl with Balloon sold at auction, a hidden shredder in the frame cut it partly into strips. Renamed Love is in the Bin, it later sold for a record price."],["The Berlin Wall","While the Berlin Wall divided the city during the Cold War, its western side became covered in graffiti and paintings. After the wall fell in 1989, artists from many countries painted a long remaining section, now called the East Side Gallery. It is one of the largest open-air galleries in the world."],["Street art in the Philippines","Street art in the Philippines ranges from the brightly painted jeepneys, decorated with pictures, slogans and chrome, to murals on walls, bridges and schools. Cities and communities have commissioned murals on subjects such as Philippine heroes, nature, local culture and Baybayin script. Art festivals and groups of muralists have painted large works in Manila, Cebu, Davao, Baguio and other cities."],["Murals that help communities","Mural projects often involve local residents, including children, in choosing subjects and painting together. Such projects can make neighborhoods more welcoming, attract visitors and support local businesses. Some murals honor community members or mark important events, while others share messages about health, the environment or disaster preparedness."],["Street art and the art world","Once seen only as rebellion, street art is now shown in galleries and museums, and leading artists sell works to collectors. Some fans worry that commercial success goes against the spirit of free public art, and that neighborhoods made popular by murals can become more expensive for the people who live there."]],
sp:["Art on wheels","The Philippine jeepney is sometimes called a moving work of street art. After the Second World War, Filipinos rebuilt leftover American military jeeps into longer passenger vehicles and decorated them to attract riders. Owners and painters added bright colors, chrome horses on the hood, religious images, landscapes, family names and witty sayings. Workshops such as those in Las Piñas became famous for their craftsmanship. As older jeepneys are replaced under a government modernization program, many artists and residents hope to preserve their decorations as part of the nation’s visual culture."],
g:[["Street art","Art created in public places"],["Graffiti","Writing or drawings on walls or other surfaces in public"],["Mural","A large painting made directly on a wall"],["Stencil","A cut-out design used to paint the same image many times"]],
f:["Graffiti means scratched in Italian.","Graffiti survives on the walls of ancient Pompeii.","Jeepneys are a form of Philippine street art."],
fun:"Graffiti scratched by ancient Romans in Pompeii includes jokes, love notes, insults and even election campaign slogans.",
tl:"Ang street art ay sining na ginagawa sa pampublikong lugar tulad ng pader, kalye at tulay. Kasama rito ang graffiti, mural at stencil. Itinuturing ding sining na panlansangan ang makukulay na dyipni ng Pilipinas."});

;
/* data/modern6.js */
// The Modern World, part 5: biodiversity loss, extreme heat, antimicrobial resistance, wearable technology, gene therapy, influencers and podcasts.
ADDART({id:"biodivloss",t:"Biodiversity Loss",c:"Environment",i:"🦋",w:"Biodiversity loss",r:["extinction","conservation","deforestation","eagle","climchange"],
p:[["Life in decline","Biodiversity loss is the decline in the variety of living things on Earth."],["Main causes","Habitat loss, overuse of wildlife, climate change, pollution and invasive species are the main causes."],["Protecting nature","Countries have agreed to protect 30 percent of land and sea by 2030."]],
l:"Biodiversity, short for biological diversity, is the variety of life on Earth, from genes and species to whole ecosystems such as forests, coral reefs and wetlands. Biodiversity loss is the reduction of this variety, through the extinction of species, the shrinking of wildlife populations and the destruction of habitats. Scientists report that wildlife populations have declined sharply since 1970 and that around a million species may be at risk of extinction. The main causes are the conversion of forests and other natural areas to farms and cities, overfishing and hunting, climate change, pollution and invasive species. In 2022 nearly 200 countries adopted a global agreement to halt and reverse the loss of nature by 2030.",
s:[["Why biodiversity matters","Living things provide food, clean water, medicines, building materials and fertile soil. Bees and other insects pollinate many crops, forests store carbon and protect watersheds, and mangroves and reefs shield coasts from storms. Many people also value nature for its beauty and for cultural and spiritual reasons, and believe other species have a right to exist."],["Measuring the decline","In 2019 an international panel of scientists, IPBES, estimated that around one million animal and plant species are threatened with extinction, many within decades. The Living Planet Index, published by WWF and the Zoological Society of London, reported in 2024 an average decline of about 73 percent in monitored wildlife populations since 1970."],["The main drivers","The biggest cause of biodiversity loss is changes in how land and sea are used, especially clearing forests and draining wetlands for agriculture. Other major causes are the direct harvesting of plants and animals, climate change, pollution from plastics, chemicals and fertilizers, and invasive species that outcompete native ones."],["A sixth mass extinction?","Earth has experienced five great mass extinctions, including the one that ended the age of dinosaurs. Some scientists warn that human activities may be causing a sixth. Others argue that current losses, though serious and rapid, have not yet reached the scale of past mass extinctions, and that there is still time to prevent one."]],
k:[["Species at risk","Around 1 million (IPBES, 2019)"],["Wildlife decline since 1970","About 73 percent on average (Living Planet Index, 2024)"],["Global agreement","Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, 2022"],["Key target","Protect 30 percent of land and sea by 2030"],["Philippine status","Megadiverse country"]],
d:[["1948","The International Union for Conservation of Nature is founded"],["1964","The first Red List of threatened species is started"],["1992","The Convention on Biological Diversity is signed at the Rio Earth Summit"],["2019","IPBES warns that a million species face extinction"],["2022","Countries adopt the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework"],["2024","The Living Planet Index reports a 73 percent average decline in wildlife populations"]],
s2:[["The Philippines, a megadiverse country","The Philippines is one of about 17 megadiverse countries, which together hold most of the world’s species. A large share of its mammals, birds, reptiles, amphibians and plants are found nowhere else. It is also considered a biodiversity hotspot, meaning that its rich wildlife is under serious threat, especially from the loss of forests."],["Threatened Philippine species","The Philippine eagle, the national bird, is critically endangered, with only a few hundred pairs estimated to remain in the wild. Other threatened species include the tamaraw of Mindoro, the Philippine cockatoo, the Visayan warty pig and many frogs, corals and plants. Conservation groups and government agencies run breeding programs and protect key forests."],["30 by 30","The Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, agreed in December 2022, set targets including protecting at least 30 percent of the world’s land and oceans by 2030, restoring degraded ecosystems and reducing harmful subsidies. Supporters call it a landmark deal for nature, while some Indigenous groups and experts stress that protected areas must respect the rights of local communities."],["Indigenous peoples and nature","Lands managed by Indigenous peoples often contain high levels of biodiversity. In the Philippines, Indigenous communities such as the Ifugao, the Lumad of Mindanao and the Palaw’an have long traditions of caring for forests and rice terraces. Many experts argue that recognizing Indigenous land rights is an effective way to protect nature."],["Balancing people and nature","Protecting biodiversity can conflict with other needs, such as growing food, building roads and creating jobs. Debates continue over how to share the costs and benefits, especially between rich and poor countries. Approaches include paying communities to protect forests, sustainable fishing rules, wildlife-friendly farming and ecotourism."],["What people can do","Individuals can help by supporting protected areas, planting native trees, reducing waste and plastic use, avoiding products from endangered species, choosing sustainably caught seafood and joining citizen science projects that count birds, butterflies and other wildlife."]],
sp:["The return of the mangroves","Mangrove forests once lined much of the Philippine coastline, but large areas were cut down for fishponds, firewood and coastal development during the 20th century. Mangroves shelter young fish and crabs, filter water and protect villages from storm surges. After Typhoon Haiyan struck in 2013, studies found that places with healthy mangroves suffered less damage. Communities, schools and conservation groups have since planted mangrove seedlings along many coasts. Scientists stress that the right species must be planted in the right places, because poorly planned planting can fail, but well-managed projects have helped bring back fish, birds and protection from the sea."],
g:[["Biodiversity","The variety of life on Earth"],["Extinction","The disappearance of a species forever"],["Habitat","The natural home of a plant or animal"],["Invasive species","A species introduced to a new area where it causes harm"]],
f:["Around a million species may be at risk of extinction.","The Philippines is a megadiverse country.","The Philippine eagle is critically endangered."],
fun:"Scientists have described only a fraction of the species thought to live on Earth; many insects, fungi and deep-sea animals have yet to be named.",
tl:"Ang pagkawala ng biodiversity ay ang pagbaba ng iba’t ibang uri ng buhay sa mundo. Pangunahing sanhi nito ang pagkasira ng tirahan, labis na pangangaso at pangingisda, pagbabago ng klima at polusyon. Isa ang Pilipinas sa mga bansang pinakamayaman sa biodiversity."});

ADDART({id:"extremeheat",t:"Extreme Heat",c:"Environment",i:"🥵",w:"Heat wave",r:["climchange","climate","water","megacities","firstaid"],
p:[["Dangerous temperatures","Extreme heat is a period of unusually and dangerously hot weather."],["Getting hotter","Heat waves are becoming more frequent and intense as the climate warms."],["Staying safe","Drinking water, staying in the shade and checking on others save lives."]],
l:"Extreme heat refers to periods of unusually high temperatures, often combined with high humidity, that can be dangerous to people, animals, crops and infrastructure. Long stretches of such weather are called heat waves. Extreme heat is one of the deadliest weather hazards, causing heat exhaustion, heatstroke and the worsening of heart and lung diseases, especially among older people, young children, outdoor workers and the poor. Scientists have found that heat waves are becoming more frequent, longer and more intense as Earth’s climate warms. The years 2023 and 2024 were the hottest on record worldwide. Cities, schools and workplaces are adapting with warning systems, cooling centers, shade and new building designs.",
s:[["What makes heat dangerous","The human body cools itself mainly by sweating. When the air is very hot and humid, sweat evaporates slowly, and the body struggles to lose heat. Weather services use measures such as the heat index, which combines temperature and humidity to show how hot it actually feels, to warn the public."],["Heat illness","Heat exhaustion causes heavy sweating, weakness, dizziness, headache and nausea. If the body keeps heating up, it can lead to heatstroke, a medical emergency in which body temperature rises above about 40°C and a person may become confused or collapse. Heatstroke requires immediate cooling and medical help."],["A warming world","As greenhouse gases warm the planet, hot days have become hotter and more common. Studies by climate scientists have found that many recent heat waves would have been extremely unlikely without climate change. The World Meteorological Organization confirmed that 2024 was the warmest year on record, about 1.5°C above preindustrial levels."],["Heat in cities","Cities are often several degrees hotter than the surrounding countryside, an effect called the urban heat island. Concrete, asphalt and buildings absorb heat during the day and release it at night, while traffic and air conditioners add more. Trees, parks, water features and light-colored roofs can help cool urban areas."]],
k:[["Measure used in the Philippines","Heat index"],["Danger level in the Philippines","Heat index of 42 to 51°C"],["Hottest year on record","2024"],["Most serious heat illness","Heatstroke"],["City effect","Urban heat island"]],
d:[["2003","A heat wave in Europe causes tens of thousands of deaths"],["2010","A severe heat wave and wildfires strike Russia"],["2021","A heat dome brings record temperatures to western Canada and the United States"],["2023","The year becomes the hottest on record at the time"],["2024","Extreme heat leads to class suspensions across the Philippines"],["2025","The Philippines returns the start of the school year to June, partly because of the heat"]],
s2:[["Heat in the Philippines","The hottest months in the Philippines are usually April and May, before the rainy season. In 2024 the heat index reached dangerous levels in many places, and schools across the country suspended in-person classes or shifted to online learning. PAGASA, the national weather agency, issues daily heat index forecasts and warnings."],["Changing the school calendar","For several years the Philippine school year began in August or later, meaning classes continued through the hot months of April and May. After widespread concern about students and teachers suffering in hot classrooms, the Department of Education moved the opening of classes back to June beginning in 2025, so that the hottest period would fall during the school break."],["Workers and heat","Construction workers, farmers, street vendors, traffic enforcers and delivery riders are especially exposed to extreme heat. Some countries require rest breaks, shade and water for outdoor workers, or ban heavy outdoor work during the hottest hours. Labor groups in many places have called for stronger heat protections."],["Who is most at risk","Older people, babies and young children, pregnant women, people with chronic illnesses and those living alone or without cooling are most at risk. Poorer households often live in crowded, poorly ventilated homes with metal roofs that trap heat, making them more vulnerable."],["Keeping cool safely","Health authorities advise drinking plenty of water, avoiding the midday sun, wearing light, loose clothing and a hat, resting in shade or cool rooms and never leaving children or pets in parked cars. Checking on elderly neighbors and relatives can save lives. Anyone showing signs of heatstroke should be moved to a cool place, cooled with water and taken to a doctor."],["Adapting to a hotter future","Cities and countries are adapting by planting trees, creating cooling centers, designing buildings with better ventilation and shade, and setting up heat warning systems. Air conditioning saves lives but uses large amounts of electricity, so engineers are working on more efficient cooling and passive designs that keep buildings cool naturally."]],
sp:["The traditional cool house","Long before air conditioning, Filipinos built homes designed for a hot, wet climate. The bahay kubo, a small house of bamboo and nipa palm, stands on stilts that let air flow underneath, with woven walls that breathe and a steep thatched roof that sheds rain and keeps out the Sun’s heat. The larger bahay na bato of the Spanish era had thick stone lower walls and an upper floor of wood with wide sliding windows made of capiz shells and ventanillas, small openings below the windows, to let breezes through. Architects today study these designs for ideas on keeping modern buildings cool without using so much energy."],
g:[["Heat wave","A long period of unusually hot weather"],["Heat index","How hot it feels when humidity is combined with temperature"],["Heatstroke","A dangerous rise in body temperature"],["Urban heat island","A city that is warmer than the surrounding countryside"]],
f:["2024 was the hottest year on record.","Cities are hotter than the countryside.","Heatstroke is a medical emergency."],
fun:"A city can be several degrees warmer than the nearby countryside at night because buildings and roads release heat stored during the day.",
tl:"Ang matinding init ay panahon ng di-pangkaraniwan at mapanganib na init ng panahon. Dumadalas at tumitindi ang heat wave habang umiinit ang klima. Nakatutulong ang pag-inom ng tubig, pananatili sa lilim at pagbabantay sa iba upang maging ligtas."});

ADDART({id:"amr",t:"Antimicrobial Resistance",c:"Health",i:"🦠",w:"Antimicrobial resistance",r:["antibiotics","penicil","evolution","pandemic","hygiene"],
p:[["When medicines stop working","Antimicrobial resistance happens when germs evolve to survive the medicines meant to kill them."],["Hard-to-treat germs","Germs resistant to many medicines are called superbugs."],["A global threat","Resistant infections kill more than a million people each year."]],
l:"Antimicrobial resistance, often shortened to AMR, happens when bacteria, viruses, fungi and parasites change over time so that medicines such as antibiotics no longer kill them or stop their growth. Infections then become harder or impossible to treat, raising the risk of severe illness, spread of disease and death. Germs that resist many medicines are often called superbugs. Resistance develops naturally through evolution, but it is greatly sped up by the overuse and misuse of antibiotics in people and animals. Researchers estimate that resistant bacterial infections directly cause more than a million deaths a year worldwide, and health organizations rank AMR among the greatest threats to global health.",
s:[["How resistance develops","When antibiotics are used, they kill most of the bacteria causing an infection, but a few may survive because of random genetic changes. These survivors multiply and pass on their resistance. Bacteria can also share resistance genes with each other, spreading resistance quickly between different kinds of germs."],["Misuse of antibiotics","Taking antibiotics for illnesses they cannot cure, such as colds and flu, which are caused by viruses, gives bacteria more chances to become resistant. So does stopping a course of antibiotics early, sharing leftover medicine or buying antibiotics without a prescription. In many countries antibiotics are also used in large amounts in livestock and fish farming."],["Superbugs","Some bacteria have become resistant to several antibiotics at once. MRSA, a resistant form of Staphylococcus aureus, can cause serious skin, blood and lung infections. Drug-resistant forms of tuberculosis, gonorrhea and bacteria found in hospitals are especially worrying because few medicines remain that can treat them."],["The scale of the problem","A major study published in 2024 estimated that resistant bacterial infections directly caused about 1.14 million deaths in 2021 and played a part in nearly 5 million. Without action, deaths could rise substantially by 2050. The burden is heaviest in low- and middle-income countries."]],
k:[["Short name","AMR"],["Nickname for resistant germs","Superbugs"],["Deaths directly caused (2021)","About 1.14 million"],["First warning","Alexander Fleming, 1945"],["Main driver","Overuse and misuse of antimicrobials"]],
d:[["1928","Alexander Fleming discovers penicillin"],["1945","Fleming warns that misuse of penicillin could make bacteria resistant"],["1961","MRSA is first reported in Britain"],["2015","The World Health Organization adopts a global action plan on AMR"],["2016","The United Nations holds its first high-level meeting on AMR"],["2024","World leaders agree a new declaration on AMR at the United Nations"]],
s2:[["Fleming’s warning","When Alexander Fleming received the Nobel Prize in 1945 for discovering penicillin, he warned in his speech that people who took too little of the drug, or took it carelessly, could make bacteria resistant. Within a few years, resistant strains of bacteria were indeed found in hospitals. His warning is often quoted today."],["The empty pipeline","Few new types of antibiotics have been discovered since the 1980s. Developing them is expensive, and because new antibiotics are kept in reserve for resistant infections, companies earn little from them. Governments and charities are testing new ways to reward companies for developing antibiotics."],["New discoveries","Scientists are searching for new antibiotics in soil, oceans and even the human nose. In 2020 researchers used artificial intelligence to identify a new antibiotic compound, named halicin. Other approaches include viruses that attack bacteria, called phages, and vaccines that prevent infections in the first place."],["One Health","Resistance spreads among people, animals and the environment, so experts call for a One Health approach that links human medicine, veterinary care, farming and environmental protection. Many countries have restricted the use of antibiotics to make farm animals grow faster."],["AMR in the Philippines","The Philippines has a national action plan on antimicrobial resistance and a surveillance program that tracks resistant bacteria in hospitals. Health authorities remind the public that antibiotics should be bought only with a doctor’s prescription and taken exactly as directed, and campaigns during World AMR Awareness Week each November spread the message."],["What everyone can do","People can help slow resistance by using antibiotics only when prescribed, completing the full course as directed, never sharing or saving leftover antibiotics, washing hands, keeping vaccinations up to date and preparing food safely to prevent infections."]],
sp:["The pill that cannot cure a cold","Many people believe antibiotics will help them recover from a cold, cough or flu, and some ask doctors for them or buy them from a pharmacy. But colds and flu are caused by viruses, and antibiotics work only against bacteria. Taking them for a viral illness does not speed recovery; it may cause side effects such as diarrhea and allergic reactions, and it helps resistant bacteria spread. Doctors usually recommend rest, fluids and simple remedies for colds. Public health campaigns in many countries now use slogans reminding people that antibiotics are not for every illness."],
g:[["Antimicrobial","A medicine that kills or stops the growth of microbes"],["Antibiotic","A medicine that fights bacteria"],["Resistance","The ability of a germ to survive a medicine"],["Superbug","A germ resistant to several medicines"]],
f:["Antibiotics do not work on viruses.","MRSA is a well-known superbug.","Fleming warned about resistance in 1945."],
fun:"Some bacteria can share resistance genes with completely different kinds of bacteria, passing them around like recipes.",
tl:"Ang antimicrobial resistance ay nangyayari kapag ang mikrobyo ay nagbabago upang hindi na tablan ng gamot. Pinabibilis ito ng maling paggamit ng antibiotic. Uminom lamang ng antibiotic kapag inireseta ng doktor at tapusin ito ayon sa tagubilin."});

ADDART({id:"wearables",t:"Wearable Technology",c:"Technology",i:"⌚",w:"Wearable technology",r:["smartphone","exercise","dataprivacy","aimedicine","sleep"],
p:[["Tech you wear","Wearable technology includes smartwatches, fitness trackers and other devices worn on the body."],["Tracking health","Wearables count steps, measure heart rate and track sleep."],["Benefits and worries","They can encourage healthy habits but raise questions about accuracy and privacy."]],
l:"Wearable technology, or wearables, means electronic devices worn on the body, such as smartwatches, fitness bands, smart rings, wireless earbuds and smart glasses. Most contain sensors that measure movement, heart rate, sleep and other body signals, and connect to smartphones to show the data in apps. Fitness trackers became popular in the late 2000s and smartwatches in the 2010s, and hundreds of millions of people now use them. Medical wearables, such as continuous glucose monitors for people with diabetes and devices that check heart rhythms, can help manage health conditions. Supporters say wearables encourage exercise and early detection of problems, while critics raise concerns about accuracy, privacy and constant self-monitoring.",
s:[["How wearables work","Wearables use tiny sensors. Accelerometers and gyroscopes detect movement to count steps and recognize activities. Optical sensors shine green or red light into the skin and measure the reflected light to estimate heart rate and blood oxygen. Data is sent by Bluetooth to a phone, where apps turn it into charts, goals and reminders."],["Fitness trackers","Clip-on and wristband trackers, such as those made by Fitbit from 2009, made counting steps popular. Many people set a goal of 10,000 steps a day, a number that came from a Japanese pedometer marketed in the 1960s. Research suggests that health benefits increase with more daily steps, with large gains even at around 7,000 steps."],["Smartwatches","Smartwatches combine fitness tracking with features of a phone, such as notifications, messages, maps and payments. The Apple Watch, launched in 2015, became the best-selling smartwatch, and companies such as Samsung, Garmin, Huawei and Xiaomi make many others. Some watches can record an electrocardiogram or detect a fall and call for help."],["Medical wearables","Continuous glucose monitors attach a tiny sensor under the skin to measure blood sugar every few minutes, helping people with diabetes avoid dangerous highs and lows. Other medical wearables monitor heart rhythm, blood pressure or breathing, allowing doctors to follow patients at home."]],
k:[["Examples","Smartwatches, fitness bands, smart rings"],["Early fitness tracker","Fitbit, 2009"],["Apple Watch launched","2015"],["Origin of 10,000 steps","A 1960s Japanese pedometer"],["Key sensors","Accelerometer, optical heart sensor"]],
d:[["1965","A Japanese pedometer called Manpo-kei, meaning 10,000 steps meter, goes on sale"],["2009","The first Fitbit tracker is released"],["2013","Pebble’s smartwatch raises millions through crowdfunding"],["2015","The Apple Watch is launched"],["2018","A smartwatch electrocardiogram feature is cleared by United States regulators"],["2020s","Smart rings and medical wearables grow in popularity"]],
s2:[["Do they make people healthier?","Studies show that wearables can motivate some people to walk more and sit less, at least for a time. Others lose interest after a few months, and the devices work best when combined with personal goals, social support or advice from health workers. Researchers continue to study long-term effects."],["Accuracy","Wearables are good at counting steps and measuring heart rate during rest, but less accurate for calories burned, sleep stages and some activities. Readings can be affected by skin tone, tattoos, movement and how tightly the device is worn. Experts advise treating the numbers as rough guides rather than medical measurements."],["Saving lives","Some smartwatch users have been alerted to irregular heart rhythms, such as atrial fibrillation, that they did not know they had, leading them to see a doctor. Fall detection has helped older people get help after accidents. Doctors note that such alerts can also cause false alarms and unnecessary worry."],["Privacy and data","Wearables collect personal information about health, location and daily routines. This data may be stored by companies, shared with partners or used for advertising and research. Privacy advocates warn about how such data could be used by employers, insurers or governments, and many countries have data protection laws, including the Philippines’ Data Privacy Act of 2012."],["Wearables at work and school","Some companies give workers wearables to track safety or productivity, and some schools use them in physical education. Supporters say this can prevent injuries and encourage fitness, while critics argue it can feel like surveillance and put pressure on people to meet targets."],["The future of wearables","Companies are developing smart glasses that show information in front of the eyes, clothing with built-in sensors, patches that analyze sweat, and devices that could measure blood pressure or blood sugar without needles. Artificial intelligence is being used to spot patterns in wearable data that may warn of illness early."]],
sp:["Where 10,000 steps came from","Many fitness apps suggest a goal of 10,000 steps a day, but the number did not come from a scientific study. In 1965, ahead of a wave of interest in fitness after the 1964 Tokyo Olympics, a Japanese company sold a pedometer called the Manpo-kei, which means 10,000 steps meter. The round number was easy to remember and became popular around the world. Decades later, scientists studied the link between daily steps and health. They found that walking more is linked to lower risks of heart disease and early death, with benefits beginning well below 10,000 steps, so every extra walk counts."],
g:[["Wearable","An electronic device worn on the body"],["Accelerometer","A sensor that detects movement"],["Smartwatch","A wristwatch with computer and phone features"],["Glucose monitor","A device that measures blood sugar"]],
f:["The 10,000-step goal began with a 1965 pedometer.","Smartwatches can record heart rhythms.","Wearables use light to measure heart rate."],
fun:"The green lights on the back of many smartwatches measure your pulse by detecting how much light is absorbed by blood flowing under your skin.",
tl:"Ang wearable technology ay mga elektronikong kagamitang isinusuot sa katawan tulad ng smartwatch at fitness tracker. Sinusukat nito ang hakbang, tibok ng puso at tulog. Nakatutulong ito sa kalusugan ngunit may tanong tungkol sa katumpakan at privacy."});

ADDART({id:"genetherapy",t:"Gene Therapy",c:"Medicine",i:"🧬",w:"Gene therapy",r:["crispr","dna","genome","mrna","cancer"],
p:[["Fixing genes","Gene therapy treats disease by adding, replacing or editing genes inside a patient’s cells."],["New treatments","It has been approved for some inherited diseases, blindness and cancers."],["High costs and hard questions","Gene therapies can be very expensive and raise ethical debates."]],
l:"Gene therapy is a medical approach that treats or prevents disease by changing the genes inside a person’s cells. It may add a working copy of a faulty gene, switch off a harmful gene, or edit DNA directly using tools such as CRISPR. Genes are usually delivered using modified, harmless viruses that carry them into cells, or cells are taken from the patient, changed in a laboratory and put back. After decades of research and setbacks, gene therapies have been approved since the 2010s for certain inherited diseases, a form of inherited blindness, spinal muscular atrophy, sickle cell disease and some blood cancers. These treatments can transform lives but often cost millions of dollars, and editing genes raises important ethical questions.",
s:[["How gene therapy works","Many diseases are caused by a faulty gene that fails to make a needed protein. Gene therapy aims to fix the problem at its source. Scientists often use viruses that have been emptied of their harmful parts as delivery vehicles, called vectors, to carry a healthy gene into cells, where it begins producing the missing protein."],["Two main approaches","In one approach, the gene is delivered directly into the body, for example by an injection into the eye, the blood or the spinal fluid. In the other, doctors collect a patient’s own cells, such as blood stem cells or immune cells, change them in a laboratory, and return them to the patient."],["Gene editing","Newer techniques such as CRISPR let scientists cut and change DNA at precise locations. In 2023 regulators in Britain and the United States approved Casgevy, the first CRISPR-based treatment, for sickle cell disease and a related blood disorder. Newer methods called base editing and prime editing can change single letters of DNA."],["Cancer therapies","CAR-T therapy is a kind of gene therapy in which a patient’s immune cells, called T cells, are given a new gene that helps them recognize and attack cancer cells. Since its first approval in 2017, it has helped some patients with leukemia and lymphoma who had run out of other options."]],
k:[["First approved trial","1990, United States"],["First CRISPR therapy approved","Casgevy, 2023"],["Delivery method","Modified viruses (vectors)"],["Cancer gene therapy","CAR-T"],["Main concerns","Cost, safety, ethics"]],
d:[["1990","Ashanti DeSilva becomes the first person treated in an approved gene therapy trial"],["1999","The death of Jesse Gelsinger in a trial sets back the field"],["2012","The first gene therapy is approved in Europe"],["2017","A gene therapy for inherited blindness and the first CAR-T therapy are approved in the United States"],["2023","Casgevy becomes the first approved CRISPR therapy"],["2025","A baby receives the first personalized gene-editing treatment"]],
s2:[["The first patient","In September 1990 a four-year-old girl named Ashanti DeSilva, who had an inherited disease that left her almost without an immune system, became the first person treated in an approved gene therapy trial in the United States. Doctors added working copies of the missing gene to her white blood cells. Her condition improved, though she also continued other treatment."],["Setbacks","In 1999 an 18-year-old volunteer, Jesse Gelsinger, died from a severe immune reaction to the virus used in a gene therapy trial. In the early 2000s some children treated for an immune disorder developed leukemia. These tragedies led to stricter safety rules and slowed the field for years, before safer vectors brought new success."],["Restoring sight and strength","In 2017 United States regulators approved Luxturna, which treats a rare form of inherited blindness by delivering a gene to cells in the retina. Zolgensma, approved in 2019, treats spinal muscular atrophy, a disease that weakens the muscles of babies, with a single infusion; children treated early have learned to sit, crawl and walk."],["A personalized treatment","In 2025 doctors in the United States reported treating a baby named KJ Muldoon, born with a rare and dangerous liver disorder, using a base-editing therapy designed specifically for his genetic mutation. It was developed in a matter of months. Scientists hope such personalized treatments may one day help other patients with very rare diseases."],["The cost problem","Gene therapies are among the most expensive medicines ever sold, some costing two million dollars or more for a single treatment. Companies say prices reflect high development costs and the value of a one-time cure, while health systems and patient groups worry that few people, especially in poorer countries, can afford them. Ways to lower costs and share access are widely debated."],["Ethics and editing embryos","Most gene therapies change only the cells of the patient being treated, so changes are not passed to future children. Editing embryos, sperm or eggs would create changes that could be inherited. In 2018 the Chinese scientist He Jiankui announced he had edited the genes of twin babies, sparking worldwide condemnation; he was later jailed. Most countries ban or strictly limit inheritable gene editing."]],
sp:["Walking after Zolgensma","Spinal muscular atrophy, or SMA, is caused by a missing or faulty gene needed by the nerve cells that control muscles. In its most severe form, babies lose the ability to move, swallow and breathe, and many once died before the age of two. Zolgensma delivers a working copy of the gene with a single infusion into the bloodstream. When given early, many treated babies have reached milestones that would once have been impossible, such as sitting up, crawling and even walking. Some countries now test newborns for SMA so treatment can begin before symptoms appear, though the treatment’s very high price remains a challenge."],
g:[["Gene therapy","Treating disease by changing genes in a patient’s cells"],["Vector","A harmless virus or other carrier used to deliver genes"],["CAR-T","A therapy that changes immune cells to fight cancer"],["Germline editing","Gene changes that can be passed to future generations"]],
f:["The first approved gene therapy trial was in 1990.","Casgevy was the first approved CRISPR therapy.","Some gene therapies cost millions of dollars."],
fun:"Scientists turn viruses, usually known for causing illness, into tiny delivery trucks that carry healing genes into cells.",
tl:"Ang gene therapy ay paraan ng paggamot sa sakit sa pamamagitan ng pagbabago ng mga gene sa selula ng pasyente. Naaprubahan na ito para sa ilang minanang sakit, pagkabulag at kanser. Napakamahal nito at may mga tanong na etikal."});

ADDART({id:"influencers",t:"Social Media Influencers",c:"Media and Culture",i:"🤳",w:"Influencer marketing",r:["socialmedia","shortvideo","memes","misinformation","ecommerce"],
p:[["Online personalities","Influencers are people with large followings on social media."],["A new kind of job","Many earn money from advertising, brand deals and sponsorships."],["Influence and responsibility","Their power raises questions about honesty, misinformation and young audiences."]],
l:"A social media influencer is a person who has built a large following on platforms such as YouTube, Instagram, TikTok and Facebook, and who can shape the opinions and buying decisions of their audience. Influencers create content about topics such as beauty, fashion, food, travel, gaming, fitness, comedy, parenting, finance and everyday life. Many earn income from advertising revenue, sponsored posts, brand partnerships, live-stream gifts and selling their own products, making up a large part of what is called the creator economy. Influencers have become important in marketing, entertainment and politics. Their rise has also prompted debate about honesty in advertising, the spread of misinformation, pressure on young people and the wellbeing of creators themselves.",
s:[["From bloggers to influencers","In the 2000s, bloggers and early YouTubers gained followings by sharing opinions, tutorials and daily life online. As smartphones and platforms such as Instagram, launched in 2010, and TikTok, which spread internationally from 2017, grew, ordinary people could reach millions of viewers. Brands began paying popular creators to promote products."],["Types of influencers","Influencers are often grouped by audience size, from mega-influencers with millions of followers to micro- and nano-influencers with smaller but closely engaged communities. Some are celebrities who became influential online; others became famous only through social media. Virtual influencers, computer-generated characters run by companies, also exist."],["How influencers earn","Influencers may earn money from platform payments for views, sponsored posts, affiliate links that pay a share of sales, merchandise, paid subscriptions, live-stream gifts and appearances at events. Income varies hugely; a small number earn millions, while most creators earn little or nothing."],["Influencers in the Philippines","Filipinos are among the world’s heaviest users of social media, and the country has a large and lively creator scene. Filipino vloggers, content creators and live sellers promote food, travel, beauty and products, often mixing English and Filipino. Brands, businesses and even government agencies work with influencers to reach audiences."]],
k:[["Main platforms","YouTube, Instagram, TikTok, Facebook"],["Industry name","Creator economy"],["Smaller influencers","Micro- and nano-influencers"],["Common income","Brand deals and ad revenue"],["Disclosure tag","#ad or Paid partnership"]],
d:[["2005","YouTube is launched"],["2010","Instagram is launched"],["2017","TikTok begins spreading worldwide"],["2019","The United States Federal Trade Commission issues a guide for influencers on disclosure"],["2022","Influencers play a visible role in Philippine election campaigns"],["2024","MrBeast becomes the most-subscribed YouTube channel"]],
s2:[["Honest advertising","When influencers are paid to promote products, many countries require them to say so clearly, using labels such as #ad or paid partnership. Regulators worry that audiences, especially young people, may not realize they are watching advertising. Some influencers have been fined or criticized for promoting harmful or misleading products, including risky investments and unproven health remedies."],["Influencers and politics","Political campaigns around the world, including in the Philippines, have worked with influencers and vloggers to reach voters, especially young people. Supporters say this brings politics closer to ordinary citizens. Critics and researchers have raised concerns about undisclosed paid political content and the spread of false or misleading information, and have called for clearer rules."],["Effects on young people","Influencers can inspire young people to learn new skills, discover interests and feel part of a community. But constant exposure to edited images and seemingly perfect lives may affect self-esteem and body image for some. Many young people say they want to become influencers, and parents and teachers encourage them to think critically about what they see online."],["Kidfluencers","Some influencers are children, whose parents film and share their lives online. These channels can earn large sums, raising questions about children’s privacy, consent and earnings. Some places have passed laws requiring that part of a child influencer’s income be set aside for them."],["Behind the screen","Creating content constantly, responding to followers and keeping up with changing algorithms can be stressful. Many influencers have spoken about burnout, online harassment and the pressure to share their private lives. Some have taken breaks or left social media to protect their mental health."],["Live selling","Live selling, in which creators sell products during live video streams, is popular in the Philippines and other parts of Asia. Sellers demonstrate clothes, gadgets or food, chat with viewers and announce discounts in real time, and buyers comment to claim items. It has become an important source of income for many small businesses and individuals."]],
sp:["A day in the life of a creator","Behind a few minutes of polished video, an influencer may spend many hours planning ideas, filming, editing, writing captions, answering messages and studying which posts perform best. Some work alone with a phone and a ring light in a bedroom, while successful creators may hire editors, managers and camera operators. Many creators juggle content creation with school or another job. Because platform algorithms decide which posts are shown widely, income can rise and fall suddenly. For many young Filipinos, becoming a content creator has become a dream career, though only a few make it a full-time living."],
g:[["Influencer","A person who can affect the opinions of many followers online"],["Creator economy","The businesses and income built around online content creators"],["Sponsored post","Content that a brand pays a creator to make"],["Algorithm","The rules a platform uses to decide what content people see"]],
f:["Instagram launched in 2010.","Paid posts should be labeled as advertising.","Live selling is popular in the Philippines."],
fun:"Some popular influencers are not real people at all but computer-generated characters managed by companies.",
tl:"Ang influencer ay taong may malaking bilang ng tagasubaybay sa social media at nakaiimpluwensiya sa kanilang opinyon at pagbili. Kumikita sila mula sa patalastas at pakikipagtulungan sa mga brand. Pinagtatalunan ang katapatan sa patalastas, maling impormasyon at epekto sa kabataan."});

ADDART({id:"podcasts",t:"Podcasts",c:"Media and Culture",i:"🎙️",w:"Podcast",r:["radio","streaming","smartphone","internet","shortvideo"],
p:[["Shows on demand","A podcast is a series of audio or video episodes that people can listen to whenever they like."],["Anyone can make one","With a microphone and an internet connection, almost anyone can start a podcast."],["Huge variety","There are podcasts about news, true crime, comedy, history, science, sports and much more."]],
l:"A podcast is a series of spoken audio episodes, and sometimes video episodes, made available online so that listeners can download or stream them whenever they want. The word combines iPod, Apple’s portable music player, and broadcast, and was first suggested in 2004. Podcasts grew slowly at first, then boomed in the 2010s as smartphones made listening easy and hit shows attracted millions of fans. Today there are millions of podcasts covering every imaginable subject, from news and politics to comedy, true crime, education, religion, health and personal stories. Because they are cheap to make, podcasts allow independent creators as well as major media companies to reach audiences around the world.",
s:[["How podcasts work","Podcasts are published online with a special web feed, called RSS, that lists each new episode. Listeners subscribe using podcast apps, such as Apple Podcasts or Spotify, which automatically show new episodes. Many podcasts are also posted as videos on YouTube, where hosts are filmed while they talk."],["Popular formats","Some podcasts feature one or two hosts chatting about a topic, while others interview guests. Narrative podcasts tell a story over many episodes, like a radio documentary or drama. News podcasts summarize the day’s events, and educational podcasts teach languages, science or history."],["Making a podcast","A simple podcast needs a microphone, recording software and a place to publish episodes. Many creators start at home, recording with friends. Larger productions involve researchers, sound designers and producers. Podcasts earn money through advertisements read by the hosts, sponsorships, listener donations and paid subscriptions."],["Why people listen","People listen to podcasts while commuting, exercising, cooking, doing chores or working. Many say they enjoy the feeling of a personal conversation with hosts they trust, and the chance to explore subjects in more depth than short videos allow."]],
k:[["Name from","iPod and broadcast"],["Word first suggested","2004"],["Added to iTunes","2005"],["Boom began","Mid-2010s"],["Main platforms","Spotify, Apple Podcasts, YouTube"]],
d:[["2004","The word podcasting is suggested in a British newspaper"],["2005","Apple adds podcasts to its iTunes software"],["2005","New Oxford American Dictionary names podcast its word of the year"],["2014","The true crime podcast Serial becomes a global hit"],["2019","Spotify invests heavily in podcasts"],["2020s","Video podcasts on YouTube become one of the most popular ways to listen"]],
s2:[["Radio roots","Podcasts carry on a long tradition of radio storytelling, talk shows and drama. In the Philippines, radio dramas, comedy programs and commentary shows were hugely popular for decades, and many families gathered around the radio each evening. Some radio programs are now released as podcasts too."],["The Serial effect","In 2014 Serial, a podcast that reinvestigated a 1999 murder case in the United States over many episodes, became one of the first podcasts heard by millions. Its success inspired a wave of true crime and narrative podcasts and drew major media companies into podcasting."],["Podcasts in the Philippines","Filipino podcasts cover topics such as comedy, relationships, horror stories, business, faith, history and current affairs, often in Filipino, English or Taglish. Many are recorded on video and shared on YouTube and Facebook. Listening has grown with cheaper smartphones and mobile data."],["Video podcasts","Many podcasts are now filmed as well as recorded, with hosts and guests sitting around microphones. Clips from these shows spread widely on social media. This has blurred the line between podcasts, talk shows and online video."],["Influence and responsibility","Some podcast hosts have audiences larger than those of television networks and have interviewed leading politicians and celebrities. Supporters praise podcasts for allowing long, open conversations. Critics note that some podcasts spread misleading claims without fact-checking, and debates continue over whether platforms should remove harmful content."],["Learning by listening","Teachers and students use podcasts to learn languages, practice listening skills and explore subjects such as history and science. Some schools ask students to create their own podcasts as projects, building research, writing and speaking skills."]],
sp:["How the podcast got its name","In February 2004 the British journalist Ben Hammersley wrote an article in The Guardian newspaper about a new way of sharing online radio shows that people could download automatically to their computers and music players. Wondering what to call it, he listed several possibilities, including audioblogging, guerrilla media and podcasting. The last one stuck. Within a year, Apple added podcasts to iTunes, and in 2005 podcast was named word of the year by the New Oxford American Dictionary. Ironically, although the name came from the iPod, most people today listen to podcasts on smartphones."],
g:[["Podcast","A series of audio or video episodes available online"],["Episode","One part of a podcast series"],["RSS feed","A web feed that lists new episodes automatically"],["Host","The person who presents a podcast"]],
f:["The word podcast comes from iPod and broadcast.","Serial became a hit in 2014.","Podcasts can be audio or video."],
fun:"Although the word podcast comes from Apple’s iPod, the iPod itself was discontinued in 2022, while podcasts kept growing.",
tl:"Ang podcast ay serye ng mga episode na audio o video na maaaring pakinggan anumang oras. Galing ang pangalan sa iPod at broadcast. Maraming uri nito, mula balita at komedya hanggang kasaysayan at agham."});

;
/* data/space9.js */
// Space, more articles: Titan, Europa, light pollution, India's Chandrayaan missions and the Philippine Space Agency.
ADDART({id:"titan",t:"Titan",c:"Astronomy",i:"🟠",w:"Titan (moon)",r:["saturn","moons","astrobio","voyager","atmosphere"],
p:[["Saturn’s giant moon","Titan is the largest moon of Saturn and the second-largest moon in the solar system."],["A thick atmosphere","It is the only moon with a thick atmosphere."],["Lakes of methane","Titan has rivers, lakes and seas of liquid methane and ethane."]],
l:"Titan is the largest moon of Saturn and the second-largest moon in the solar system, after Jupiter’s Ganymede. It is bigger than the planet Mercury. Titan is the only moon known to have a thick atmosphere, made mostly of nitrogen, and the only world besides Earth known to have stable bodies of liquid on its surface. But Titan’s lakes and seas are filled with liquid methane and ethane rather than water, because its surface is about minus 180°C. Discovered in 1655 by Christiaan Huygens, Titan was explored by the Cassini spacecraft, which dropped the Huygens probe onto its surface in 2005. NASA’s Dragonfly mission plans to fly a drone-like lander there.",
s:[["Size and orbit","Titan is about 5,150 kilometers across, roughly 1.5 times the width of Earth’s Moon. It orbits Saturn once every 16 days, always keeping the same side facing the planet. It is made of a mixture of rock and water ice."],["A hazy atmosphere","Titan’s atmosphere is thicker than Earth’s, with a surface pressure about one and a half times as great. Sunlight breaks down methane high in the atmosphere, creating an orange smog of complex chemicals that hides the surface from ordinary telescopes."],["Methane weather","On Titan, methane plays the role that water plays on Earth. It forms clouds, falls as rain, carves river channels and collects in lakes and seas near the poles. The largest, Kraken Mare, is bigger than the Caspian Sea."],["A hidden ocean","Measurements by Cassini suggest that beneath Titan’s icy crust lies an ocean of salty liquid water. Scientists are interested in whether this ocean, or the chemistry on Titan’s surface, could support any form of life."]],
k:[["Planet","Saturn"],["Diameter","About 5,150 km"],["Discovered","1655, by Christiaan Huygens"],["Surface temperature","About −180°C"],["Atmosphere","Mostly nitrogen"]],
d:[["1655","Christiaan Huygens discovers Titan"],["1944","Gerard Kuiper detects methane in Titan’s atmosphere"],["1980","Voyager 1 flies past Titan"],["2004","The Cassini spacecraft arrives at Saturn"],["2005","The Huygens probe lands on Titan"],["2017","Cassini’s mission ends after many Titan flybys"]],
s2:[["The Huygens landing","On 14 January 2005, the European Huygens probe parachuted through Titan’s atmosphere for about two and a half hours and landed on a damp plain scattered with rounded pebbles of ice. It sent back pictures and measurements for over an hour. It was the first landing on a world in the outer solar system and remains the most distant landing ever made."],["Cassini’s discoveries","During 13 years orbiting Saturn, the Cassini spacecraft flew past Titan more than 120 times. Its radar mapped dunes, mountains, river valleys and the methane seas near the north pole. It also detected signs of the hidden underground ocean."],["Dunes and mountains","Wide belts of dunes, made not of sand but of grains of solid organic material, cover large areas near Titan’s equator. Mountains of water ice rise from the surface, and some scientists think features on Titan may be cryovolcanoes, which erupt icy slush instead of lava."],["Could Titan host life?","Titan has many of the ingredients that scientists think led to life on Earth, including complex carbon chemistry and liquid on the surface and below. But it is extremely cold. Some researchers have imagined strange life forms that could live in liquid methane, while others focus on the underground water ocean."],["The Dragonfly mission","NASA’s Dragonfly mission, planned for launch in 2028, will send a nuclear-powered rotorcraft, like a large drone, to Titan. Titan’s thick air and low gravity make flying easier than on Earth. Dragonfly is designed to hop between sites, studying the chemistry of the surface and searching for signs of the processes that may lead to life."],["Titan in imagination","Writers have long been fascinated by Titan. It has appeared in science fiction novels, films and games as a place for future human settlements. With its thick atmosphere, people there would not need pressure suits, only warm clothing and oxygen masks, though the extreme cold would be a great challenge."]],
sp:["Postcards from Titan","As the Huygens probe drifted down under its parachute in 2005, its cameras revealed a landscape that looked surprisingly familiar: dark channels branching like riverbeds down to what appeared to be a shoreline. After landing, its pictures showed rounded stones on the ground, smoothed by flowing liquid just like pebbles in an Earth stream. But the stones were made of water ice as hard as rock, and the liquid that shaped them was methane. Scientists described Titan as a strange mirror of Earth, with familiar landforms built from completely different materials."],
g:[["Moon","A natural satellite that orbits a planet"],["Methane","A gas, also called natural gas, which is liquid on Titan"],["Cryovolcano","A volcano that erupts icy materials instead of molten rock"],["Rotorcraft","An aircraft lifted by spinning blades, such as a helicopter"]],
f:["Titan is bigger than Mercury.","Its lakes are filled with liquid methane.","The Huygens probe landed there in 2005."],
fun:"Because Titan’s air is thick and its gravity is weak, a person there could fly by flapping wings strapped to their arms.",
tl:"Ang Titan ang pinakamalaking buwan ng Saturn at mas malaki pa kaysa sa planetang Mercury. Ito lamang ang buwan na may makapal na atmospera. May mga lawa at dagat ito ng likidong methane."});

ADDART({id:"europa",t:"Europa",c:"Astronomy",i:"🧊",w:"Europa (moon)",r:["jupiter","moons","astrobio","galileo","water"],
p:[["An icy moon","Europa is one of Jupiter’s four largest moons, covered by a shell of ice."],["A hidden ocean","Beneath the ice lies a vast ocean of salty water."],["Searching for life","Scientists consider it one of the best places to look for life beyond Earth."]],
l:"Europa is the fourth-largest moon of Jupiter and one of the four Galilean moons discovered by Galileo Galilei in 1610. Slightly smaller than Earth’s Moon, it has a bright, smooth surface of water ice crossed by long cracks and ridges. Strong evidence suggests that beneath this icy shell lies a global ocean of salty liquid water, possibly containing twice as much water as all of Earth’s oceans. Because life on Earth depends on liquid water, Europa is considered one of the most promising places in the solar system to search for life. NASA’s Europa Clipper spacecraft, launched in 2024, is on its way to study the moon in detail.",
s:[["Surface of ice","Europa’s surface is among the smoothest in the solar system, with few craters, which means it is geologically young. It is covered with a web of dark lines and ridges where the ice has cracked and shifted, and areas of jumbled ice blocks called chaos terrain, which may form where warmer water rises close to the surface."],["The ocean below","Measurements by the Galileo spacecraft showed that Europa disturbs Jupiter’s magnetic field in a way best explained by a layer of salty, electrically conducting water beneath the ice. The ice shell may be 15 to 25 kilometers thick, with an ocean perhaps 60 to 150 kilometers deep."],["Heated by tides","Europa is far from the Sun, but Jupiter’s strong gravity stretches and squeezes it as it orbits, heating its interior through friction. This tidal heating keeps the ocean from freezing and may power hydrothermal vents on the seafloor, like those that support life in Earth’s deep oceans."],["Ingredients for life","Life as we know it needs liquid water, a source of energy and certain chemical elements. Europa appears to have water and energy, and scientists hope to learn whether it also has the right chemistry. No evidence of life has been found, but Europa is a top target in the search."]],
k:[["Planet","Jupiter"],["Discovered","1610, by Galileo Galilei"],["Diameter","About 3,100 km"],["Surface","Water ice"],["Mission","Europa Clipper, launched 2024"]],
d:[["1610","Galileo Galilei discovers Europa and three other moons of Jupiter"],["1979","Voyager 1 and 2 photograph Europa’s cracked surface"],["1995","The Galileo spacecraft begins orbiting Jupiter"],["2000","Galileo data strongly suggest an ocean beneath the ice"],["2023","Europe launches the JUICE mission to Jupiter’s icy moons"],["2024","NASA launches Europa Clipper"]],
s2:[["Galileo’s moons","In January 1610 Galileo pointed his telescope at Jupiter and saw four small points of light moving around it. They were the first moons discovered orbiting another planet, and they showed that not everything in the sky circles Earth. The moons, Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto, are now called the Galilean moons."],["Water plumes?","Observations by the Hubble Space Telescope in 2012 and 2016 hinted that plumes of water vapor might sometimes burst from cracks in Europa’s ice. If so, a spacecraft flying through them could sample the ocean without drilling. The evidence remains uncertain, and Europa Clipper will search for plumes."],["Europa Clipper","Launched in October 2024, Europa Clipper is the largest spacecraft NASA has built for a planetary mission. Expected to reach Jupiter in 2030, it will fly past Europa dozens of times, using cameras, radar and other instruments to measure the ice shell, study the ocean and analyze the surface chemistry."],["JUICE","The European Space Agency’s Jupiter Icy Moons Explorer, called JUICE, launched in 2023. It will study Ganymede, Callisto and Europa and become the first spacecraft to orbit a moon of another planet, Ganymede. The two missions will complement each other."],["Protecting Europa","Because Europa might host life, space agencies take great care to avoid contaminating it with microbes from Earth. Spacecraft are carefully cleaned, and at the end of its mission the Galileo spacecraft was deliberately crashed into Jupiter in 2003 so that it could never hit Europa."],["A future lander","Scientists have proposed sending a lander to Europa to scoop up surface ice and test it for chemical signs of life. Further in the future, some have imagined robotic probes that could melt through the ice and explore the ocean below, though such missions would be extremely difficult."]],
sp:["An ocean bigger than Earth’s","Europa is smaller than our Moon, yet its hidden ocean may hold about twice as much water as all the oceans on Earth combined. On Earth, the deep ocean floor has hydrothermal vents, cracks where hot, mineral-rich water gushes out. There, in complete darkness, communities of tubeworms, clams, shrimp and bacteria thrive on chemical energy rather than sunlight. Scientists wonder whether similar vents might exist on Europa’s seafloor. If life could arise in such a place on Earth, perhaps it could also arise in the dark ocean beneath Europa’s ice."],
g:[["Galilean moons","The four largest moons of Jupiter, discovered by Galileo"],["Tidal heating","Heat produced inside a moon by a planet’s gravity squeezing it"],["Chaos terrain","Areas of broken, jumbled ice on Europa’s surface"],["Hydrothermal vent","A crack in the seafloor where heated water flows out"]],
f:["Europa was discovered in 1610.","It may have more water than Earth’s oceans.","Europa Clipper launched in 2024."],
fun:"Europa’s surface is so smooth that its tallest features rise only a few hundred meters.",
tl:"Ang Europa ay isa sa malalaking buwan ng Jupiter na nababalutan ng yelo. Sa ilalim ng yelo ay may malawak na karagatan ng maalat na tubig. Isa ito sa pinakamainam na lugar upang hanapin ang buhay sa labas ng Daigdig."});

ADDART({id:"lightpollution",t:"Light Pollution",c:"Astronomy",i:"🌃",w:"Light pollution",r:["stars","constellations","megacities","seaturtle","sleep"],
p:[["Too much light","Light pollution is excessive or badly aimed artificial light at night."],["Losing the stars","It hides the stars and the Milky Way from most people on Earth."],["Effects on life","It disturbs animals, plants and human sleep, and wastes energy."]],
l:"Light pollution is the excessive, misdirected or unnecessary use of artificial light at night. Streetlights, buildings, billboards and vehicles send light up into the sky, where it scatters and creates a glow over cities called skyglow. As a result, most people in the world can no longer see a truly dark night sky, and about a third of humanity cannot see the Milky Way from where they live. Besides hiding the stars, light pollution confuses migrating birds and sea turtle hatchlings, disrupts insects, wastes energy and may affect human sleep and health. Unlike many pollutants, it can be reduced quickly with better lighting design.",
s:[["Types of light pollution","Skyglow is the brightening of the night sky over populated areas. Glare is excessive brightness that causes discomfort and makes it harder to see. Light trespass is light falling where it is not wanted, such as into bedrooms. Clutter is a confusing mass of bright lights, often seen along busy roads."],["Losing the night sky","A 2016 world atlas of artificial sky brightness found that about 80 percent of the world’s people live under light-polluted skies. In cities, only the brightest stars and planets are visible. Studies using observations by volunteers suggest the night sky has been getting brighter by several percent each year."],["Wildlife in danger","Many animals rely on natural darkness. Newly hatched sea turtles head toward the brightest horizon, normally the moonlit sea, but bright beachfront lights can lead them inland. Migrating birds are attracted to lit buildings and may collide with them, while artificial light draws insects away from their normal activities."],["People and health","Human bodies follow a daily rhythm controlled by light and darkness. Bright light at night, especially blue-rich light, can reduce the hormone melatonin and disturb sleep. Researchers are studying possible links between long-term light at night and health problems."]],
k:[["Main effect","Skyglow"],["People under light-polluted skies","About 80 percent"],["Cannot see the Milky Way","About a third of humanity"],["Key solution","Shielded, dimmer, warmer lighting"],["Protected dark places","Dark sky parks and reserves"]],
d:[["1879","Thomas Edison demonstrates a practical light bulb"],["1958","Flagstaff in the United States passes an early law to limit outdoor lighting"],["1988","The International Dark-Sky Association is founded"],["2001","The first world atlas of night sky brightness is published"],["2016","A new atlas finds 80 percent of people live under light-polluted skies"],["2023","A study finds the night sky brightening by nearly 10 percent a year"]],
s2:[["Better lighting","Light pollution can be reduced by using fixtures that point light downward rather than into the sky, lighting only what needs to be lit, using timers and motion sensors, and choosing warmer, less blue light. Such changes save energy and money while keeping streets safe."],["LEDs: a mixed blessing","Many cities have replaced older streetlights with LEDs, which use much less electricity. However, bright white LEDs contain more blue light, which scatters more in the atmosphere, and lower costs sometimes lead to more lighting overall. Experts recommend warmer LEDs and careful design."],["Dark sky places","Around the world, parks, reserves and communities have been certified as dark sky places for protecting their natural night skies. They attract astronomers and tourists who want to see the Milky Way, meteor showers and countless stars."],["Satellites in the sky","A newer concern is the growing number of satellites in low orbit, such as those providing internet service. Sunlight reflecting off them creates moving streaks in telescope images. Astronomers are working with companies to make satellites less reflective, and the issue is debated as satellite networks grow."],["Light pollution in the Philippines","Metro Manila and other large cities glow brightly at night, and few residents can see more than a handful of stars. Astronomy groups organize stargazing trips to darker provinces and islands, and conservation groups ask beach resorts to limit lights during sea turtle nesting seasons to protect hatchlings."],["Seeing the stars again","During large blackouts, such as the one after an earthquake in Los Angeles in 1994, many city residents saw the Milky Way for the first time. Some reportedly called emergency services, alarmed by the strange glowing cloud in the sky. Such stories show how much of the night sky city dwellers have lost."]],
sp:["The turtles that follow the light","On beaches across the tropics, including those in the Philippines, sea turtles crawl ashore at night to lay their eggs in the sand. About two months later, the hatchlings dig their way out, usually after dark, and scramble toward the sea. They find it by heading for the brightest, lowest horizon, which on a natural beach is the open sea reflecting moonlight and starlight. Bright lights from hotels, roads and houses can confuse them, drawing them inland, where they may be eaten or die from exhaustion. Conservation volunteers turn off lights, use turtle-friendly amber lamps and guide lost hatchlings back toward the waves."],
g:[["Light pollution","Excessive or misdirected artificial light at night"],["Skyglow","The glow of the night sky over populated areas"],["Glare","Excessive brightness that causes discomfort"],["Melatonin","A hormone that helps regulate sleep"]],
f:["About 80 percent of people live under light-polluted skies.","Bright lights confuse sea turtle hatchlings.","Downward-facing lights reduce skyglow."],
fun:"From a truly dark place, the human eye can see a few thousand stars at once; from a big city, often only a few dozen.",
tl:"Ang light pollution ay labis o maling paggamit ng artipisyal na ilaw sa gabi. Itinatago nito ang mga bituin at ang Milky Way sa karamihan ng tao. Ginugulo rin nito ang mga hayop tulad ng mga pawikan at ang pagtulog ng tao."});

ADDART({id:"chandrayaan",t:"Chandrayaan Missions",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🚀",w:"Chandrayaan programme",r:["moon","indiacountry","spaceagencies","artemis","marsrovers"],
p:[["India to the Moon","Chandrayaan is the name of India’s missions to explore the Moon."],["Water discovered","Chandrayaan-1 helped discover water molecules on the Moon in 2009."],["A historic landing","In 2023 Chandrayaan-3 made India the first country to land near the Moon’s south pole."]],
l:"The Chandrayaan program is a series of lunar exploration missions by the Indian Space Research Organisation, ISRO. The name means moon craft in Sanskrit. Chandrayaan-1, launched in 2008, orbited the Moon and carried a NASA instrument that helped confirm the presence of water molecules on the lunar surface. Chandrayaan-2, launched in 2019, placed an orbiter around the Moon, but its lander crashed during its descent. On 23 August 2023, Chandrayaan-3 successfully landed near the Moon’s south pole, making India the fourth country to soft-land on the Moon and the first to land in that region. The missions were achieved at relatively low cost and became a source of great national pride.",
s:[["Chandrayaan-1","Launched on 22 October 2008, Chandrayaan-1 orbited the Moon for about ten months. It released an impact probe that crashed near the south pole, and its instruments mapped the surface. NASA’s Moon Mineralogy Mapper on board detected signs of water and hydroxyl molecules spread across the Moon, a major discovery announced in 2009."],["Chandrayaan-2","Chandrayaan-2 launched on 22 July 2019 with an orbiter, a lander named Vikram and a small rover. In September 2019, contact with Vikram was lost moments before touchdown and it crashed. The orbiter, however, continued to work successfully, mapping the Moon in high detail."],["Chandrayaan-3","Chandrayaan-3 launched on 14 July 2023 with an improved lander, again named Vikram, and a rover called Pragyan. On 23 August it touched down near the south pole. The six-wheeled rover drove about 100 meters, measuring the soil and confirming the presence of sulfur and other elements."],["Why the south pole","The Moon’s south pole has craters where sunlight never reaches, which may contain ice that has survived for billions of years. This ice could be studied for clues to the history of the solar system and might one day supply water and fuel for astronauts. Several countries are planning missions there."]],
k:[["Agency","Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO)"],["Name meaning","Moon craft"],["First mission","Chandrayaan-1, 2008"],["Historic landing","23 August 2023"],["Lander and rover","Vikram and Pragyan"]],
d:[["1969","The Indian Space Research Organisation is founded"],["2008","Chandrayaan-1 is launched"],["2009","Chandrayaan-1 data help confirm water molecules on the Moon"],["2019","Chandrayaan-2’s lander crashes, but its orbiter succeeds"],["2023","Chandrayaan-3 lands near the Moon’s south pole"],["2024","India celebrates its first National Space Day on 23 August"]],
s2:[["India’s space program","India began its space program in the 1960s, launching small rockets from a fishing village in Kerala. Its pioneers, including Vikram Sarabhai, after whom the landers are named, aimed to use space technology for national development, such as weather forecasting, communications and mapping. India launched its first satellite, Aryabhata, in 1975 with Soviet help."],["Low cost, high achievement","Chandrayaan-3 was reported to cost about 75 million US dollars, less than the budget of some Hollywood space films. ISRO is known for designing missions efficiently, reusing proven technology and using clever flight paths. Supporters see this as a model for developing countries, while critics debate whether space spending should take priority over other needs."],["A nation watches","The Chandrayaan-3 landing was broadcast live, and millions of people across India watched in homes, schools and public squares. When the lander touched down, celebrations broke out across the country. The landing site was named Shiv Shakti Point, and 23 August was declared National Space Day."],["Learning from failure","After Chandrayaan-2’s lander crashed, ISRO studied what had gone wrong and redesigned the lander for Chandrayaan-3 with stronger legs, more fuel, extra sensors and a larger landing area. The success four years later showed the value of learning from setbacks."],["Mars and beyond","In 2014 India’s Mars Orbiter Mission, known as Mangalyaan, reached Mars on its first attempt, making India the first Asian nation to do so. In 2023 India launched Aditya-L1 to study the Sun. India is also developing Gaganyaan, a program to send its own astronauts into space."],["Future Moon missions","India has announced plans for further lunar missions, including one to bring Moon samples back to Earth and a joint mission with Japan to explore the south pole. These missions form part of a wider international return to the Moon involving the United States, China, Japan and others."]],
sp:["Fifteen minutes of terror","Landing on the Moon is extremely difficult, because there is no air to slow a spacecraft with parachutes and every move must be controlled by rocket engines and onboard computers. ISRO engineers called the final descent the fifteen minutes of terror. On 23 August 2023, as Vikram slowed from thousands of kilometers per hour, scanned the ground for hazards and gently touched down, engineers in the control room in Bengaluru burst into cheers and hugs. Prime Minister Narendra Modi, watching from South Africa, congratulated the team live, saying India was now on the Moon."],
g:[["ISRO","Indian Space Research Organisation, India’s space agency"],["Lander","A spacecraft designed to touch down on a surface"],["Rover","A vehicle that moves across the surface of another world"],["Soft landing","A controlled landing without crashing"]],
f:["Chandrayaan means moon craft.","Chandrayaan-3 landed in 2023.","India was the first to land near the Moon’s south pole."],
fun:"The landers on Chandrayaan-2 and Chandrayaan-3 were both named Vikram, after Vikram Sarabhai, the father of India’s space program.",
tl:"Ang Chandrayaan ay mga misyon ng India upang galugarin ang Buwan. Noong 2023, ang Chandrayaan-3 ang unang lumapag malapit sa south pole ng Buwan. Tumulong ang Chandrayaan-1 na matuklasan ang tubig sa Buwan."});

ADDART({id:"philsa",t:"The Philippine Space Agency",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛰️",w:"Philippine Space Agency",r:["sat","spaceagencies","phtyph","gps","disasterprep"],
p:[["The country’s space agency","The Philippine Space Agency, or PhilSA, leads the country’s space program."],["Filipino-made satellites","Filipino engineers have built satellites such as Diwata-1 and Maya-1."],["Space for development","Satellites help with disaster response, farming, mapping and protecting seas."]],
l:"The Philippine Space Agency, known as PhilSA, is the government agency responsible for the country’s space science and technology programs. It was created by the Philippine Space Act, signed into law on 8 August 2019. Even before PhilSA was formed, Filipino scientists and engineers, working with universities in Japan through a government program, built the country’s first microsatellite, Diwata-1, launched in 2016, followed by Diwata-2 and the tiny cube satellites of the Maya series. PhilSA uses satellite data to help monitor typhoons, floods and crops, map land and seas, and support national security. It also trains young scientists and promotes public interest in space.",
s:[["Creating PhilSA","For decades the Philippines relied on foreign satellites for weather images, communication and mapping. Scientists argued that a national space agency would help the country make better use of space technology. The Philippine Space Act of 2019 created PhilSA and set out a national space policy covering security, disaster management, research, industry and education."],["Diwata-1","Diwata-1, whose name comes from the Filipino word for a fairy or spirit, was built by Filipino engineers with Tohoku and Hokkaido universities in Japan. About the size of a small suitcase, it was released into orbit from the International Space Station in April 2016. Its cameras photographed the Philippines to help monitor weather, vegetation and coasts."],["The Maya cubesats","Maya-1, a cube satellite only 10 centimeters on each side, was built by Filipino students in Japan and deployed in 2018. It was followed by Maya-2 and by Maya-5 and Maya-6, the first cube satellites built entirely in the Philippines, deployed in 2023. These small satellites give students and engineers hands-on experience in building spacecraft."],["Using satellite data","PhilSA processes images from Filipino and foreign satellites to map floods after typhoons, track oil spills, monitor ships in Philippine waters, measure crop growth and detect changes in forests and coastlines. This information is shared with disaster agencies, local governments and researchers."]],
k:[["Created by","Philippine Space Act, 2019"],["Headquarters set by law","New Clark City, Tarlac"],["First microsatellite","Diwata-1, 2016"],["First Philippine-built cubesats","Maya-5 and Maya-6, 2023"],["Major planned satellite","MULA"]],
d:[["1997","Agila-2, the first satellite owned by a Philippine company, is launched"],["2016","Diwata-1 is deployed from the International Space Station"],["2018","Diwata-2 is launched and Maya-1 is deployed"],["2019","The Philippine Space Act creates PhilSA"],["2021","Maya-2 is deployed"],["2023","Maya-5 and Maya-6, built in the Philippines, are deployed"]],
s2:[["Satellites before PhilSA","In 1997 a Philippine telecommunications company launched Agila-2, a large communications satellite, from China. It carried television and telephone signals across Asia. For the next two decades, however, the country had no satellites of its own for observing the Earth."],["MULA","PhilSA has been developing a larger Earth-observation satellite called MULA, short for Multispectral Unit for Land Assessment, built with a British company. It is designed to take detailed images in many colors of light to help monitor farms, forests, coasts and disasters. Its launch has been postponed several times and, as of 2026, was planned for 2027."],["Space and national security","Satellite images help the Philippines monitor its large maritime territory, including the West Philippine Sea. PhilSA has used satellite data to track ships and to document changes to reefs. Supporters say this improves the country’s awareness of its seas, while the issue is part of wider regional tensions."],["Training the next generation","PhilSA and partner universities run scholarships and training programs in space science and engineering. Filipino students have built satellites in Japan, joined international space competitions and worked with foreign space agencies. Programs in schools aim to inspire young Filipinos to pursue careers in science and technology."],["International cooperation","The Philippines works with space agencies and organizations in Japan, the United States, Europe, the United Kingdom and Southeast Asia. Cooperation includes sharing satellite data, training, joint satellite projects and participation in regional disaster-monitoring networks."],["Why space matters for an archipelago","As a country of thousands of islands in the path of typhoons, the Philippines benefits greatly from space technology. Satellites provide weather forecasts, communication to remote islands, navigation for ships and fishermen, and quick maps after disasters. Supporters argue that investing in space helps the country solve problems on the ground."]],
sp:["A satellite from the Space Station","On 27 April 2016, astronauts aboard the International Space Station released Diwata-1 into orbit using a special launcher attached to the station’s Japanese module. As it drifted away, Filipino engineers on the ground celebrated a milestone years in the making. The young team had spent many months in Japan learning to design and build every part of the satellite. Over its life in orbit, Diwata-1 took thousands of images of the Philippines before it re-entered the atmosphere in 2020. Many of its builders went on to lead the country’s growing space program."],
g:[["PhilSA","Philippine Space Agency"],["Microsatellite","A small satellite, usually weighing between 10 and 100 kg"],["Cubesat","A tiny satellite built from cubes about 10 cm on each side"],["Earth observation","Studying Earth from space using satellites"]],
f:["PhilSA was created in 2019.","Diwata-1 was the first Philippine microsatellite.","Maya-5 and Maya-6 were built in the Philippines."],
fun:"Diwata-1 was launched from the International Space Station, released by astronauts using a special launcher on the station’s Japanese module.",
tl:"Ang Philippine Space Agency o PhilSA ang namamahala sa programang pangkalawakan ng bansa. Itinatag ito noong 2019. Gumawa ang mga inhinyerong Pilipino ng mga satellite tulad ng Diwata-1 at Maya-1 na tumutulong sa pagbabantay ng sakuna, pagsasaka at karagatan."});

;
/* data/body6.js */
// The Human Body, more articles: touch, reflexes, sun safety, stroke and asthma.
ADDART({id:"touch",t:"The Sense of Touch",c:"Human Body",i:"✋",w:"Somatosensory system",r:["skin","nervous","brain","pain","smelltaste"],
p:[["Feeling the world","Touch lets us feel pressure, vibration, texture, heat, cold and pain."],["Tiny sensors","Millions of tiny sensors in the skin send signals to the brain."],["Fingertips and lips","Some body parts, such as the fingertips and lips, are especially sensitive."]],
l:"Touch is the sense that allows us to feel pressure, vibration, texture, temperature and pain through the skin. It is part of a larger system, called the somatosensory system, that also tells us where our body parts are and how they are moving. The skin contains millions of sensory receptors of different kinds, each responding to particular sensations. Their signals travel along nerves to the spinal cord and brain, which interpret them as a gentle stroke, a sharp pinch, a hot cup or a rough surface. Touch helps us explore and handle objects, protects us from injury and plays a vital role in emotional bonds between people.",
s:[["Receptors in the skin","Different receptors detect different things. Some respond to light touch and fine texture, others to deep pressure or vibration, and others to stretching of the skin. Separate receptors sense warmth and cold, and nociceptors detect potential harm, producing the feeling of pain."],["Sensitive places","Receptors are not spread evenly. The fingertips, lips and tongue have many receptors close together, so they can detect tiny details. The back has far fewer. A simple test shows this: two pencil points pressed close together feel like one point on the back but two separate points on a fingertip."],["From skin to brain","Touch signals travel along nerve fibers into the spinal cord and up to the brain. They arrive in a strip of the brain called the somatosensory cortex, where each part of the body has its own area. Body parts with more receptors, such as the hands and lips, have larger areas."],["Knowing where your body is","Besides touch on the skin, sensors in muscles, tendons and joints tell the brain where the arms and legs are and how they are moving, a sense called proprioception. It lets people touch their nose with their eyes closed or walk without looking at their feet."]],
k:[["Sense organ","Skin"],["Brain area","Somatosensory cortex"],["Most sensitive areas","Fingertips, lips, tongue"],["Pain sensors","Nociceptors"],["Nobel Prize for touch and temperature sensors","2021"]],
d:[["1824","Louis Braille creates his raised-dot reading system"],["1834","Ernst Weber begins measuring the sensitivity of the skin"],["1937","Wilder Penfield maps body areas in the brain"],["1950s","Studies by Harry Harlow show the importance of comforting touch for young monkeys"],["1997","The heat and chili sensor TRPV1 is identified"],["2021","David Julius and Ardem Patapoutian win the Nobel Prize for discovering touch and temperature receptors"]],
s2:[["Reading with fingers","Because fingertips are so sensitive, they can read. In 1824 a blind French teenager, Louis Braille, developed a system of raised dots that represent letters and numbers. Braille is now used around the world, including in Filipino and other Philippine languages, allowing blind people to read books, signs and elevator buttons."],["The map in the brain","In the 1930s the Canadian neurosurgeon Wilder Penfield gently stimulated the brains of awake patients during surgery and asked what they felt. He mapped which part of the somatosensory cortex corresponds to each body part. Drawings of a little person with huge hands and lips, called a homunculus, show how much brain space each part receives."],["Hot peppers and cool mint","In 1997 the scientist David Julius and his team found a receptor in nerve cells, now called TRPV1, that responds both to heat and to capsaicin, the chemical that makes chili peppers taste hot. A related receptor responds to cold and menthol, which is why mint feels cool. Ardem Patapoutian discovered the Piezo receptors that sense pressure. The two shared the 2021 Nobel Prize."],["The power of a hug","Gentle, caring touch, such as a hug, holding hands or a parent cradling a baby, can calm the body, lower stress and strengthen bonds. Special nerve fibers in hairy skin respond best to slow stroking. Hospitals encourage skin-to-skin contact between newborn babies and their parents, sometimes called kangaroo care."],["When touch goes wrong","Damage to nerves from diabetes, injury or disease can cause numbness, tingling or burning pain. People who lose feeling in their feet may not notice cuts or blisters, so doctors advise them to check their feet daily. Some people are born unable to feel pain, which is dangerous because they cannot sense injuries."],["Touch and technology","Engineers are building robots and prosthetic hands with artificial skin that can sense pressure, so that people using them can feel what they grip. Phones and game controllers use vibrations, called haptic feedback, to make touchscreens and games feel more realistic."]],
sp:["The two-point test","Try this with a friend: close your eyes while your friend gently touches your skin with either one or two pencil tips held close together. On your fingertip you can probably tell when there are two points even if they are only a few millimeters apart. On your forearm they need to be much farther apart, and on your back they may need to be several centimeters apart before you can feel two separate points. The test, first used by the German scientist Ernst Weber in the 1830s, shows how densely packed touch receptors are in different parts of the body."],
g:[["Somatosensory system","The body system that senses touch, temperature, pain and position"],["Receptor","A sensor that responds to a particular stimulus"],["Proprioception","The sense of the position and movement of body parts"],["Haptic","Relating to the sense of touch"]],
f:["Fingertips are among the most sensitive parts of the body.","Braille lets people read with their fingers.","A chili receptor also senses heat."],
fun:"Chili peppers feel hot because their chemical capsaicin triggers the same receptor that senses real heat.",
tl:"Ang pandama ay nagbibigay-daan upang maramdaman ang diin, tekstura, init, lamig at sakit sa pamamagitan ng balat. Milyon-milyong receptor sa balat ang nagpapadala ng signal sa utak. Lalong sensitibo ang dulo ng daliri at mga labi."});

ADDART({id:"reflexes",t:"Reflexes",c:"Human Body",i:"🦵",w:"Reflex",r:["nervous","spine","brain","muscles","eye"],
p:[["Automatic actions","A reflex is a fast, automatic response to something, without thinking."],["Protecting the body","Reflexes such as blinking and pulling away from heat protect us from harm."],["Baby reflexes","Newborn babies have special reflexes that fade as they grow."]],
l:"A reflex is an automatic, rapid response to a stimulus that happens without conscious thought. Examples include blinking when something flies toward the eyes, pulling a hand away from a hot stove, coughing when something irritates the throat and the knee-jerk that occurs when a doctor taps just below the kneecap. Many reflexes are controlled by the spinal cord or brainstem rather than the thinking parts of the brain, which makes them extremely fast. Reflexes protect the body from injury, help keep balance and posture, and control vital processes such as breathing and swallowing. Doctors test reflexes to check how well the nervous system is working.",
s:[["The reflex arc","The pathway of a simple reflex is called a reflex arc. A sensor detects a stimulus, such as heat on the skin, and sends a signal along a sensory nerve to the spinal cord. There, the signal passes, sometimes through a connecting nerve cell, to a motor nerve that makes a muscle contract. The message reaches the brain only afterward, which is why we often feel pain a moment after pulling away."],["Protective reflexes","Many reflexes protect us. Blinking shields the eyes, coughing and sneezing clear the airways, the gag reflex prevents choking, and the withdrawal reflex pulls a limb away from pain. The pupils of the eyes shrink automatically in bright light to protect the retina."],["The knee-jerk","When a doctor taps the tendon below the kneecap, the thigh muscle is stretched slightly. Sensors in the muscle send a signal to the spinal cord, which immediately tells the muscle to contract, so the lower leg kicks forward. This stretch reflex helps us keep our posture and balance without thinking."],["Reflexes and reactions","A reflex is different from a reaction. Catching a ball or braking a bicycle requires the brain to decide what to do, which takes longer. Reflexes are faster because they skip conscious thought, often taking only a few hundredths of a second."]],
k:[["Pathway","Reflex arc"],["Controlled mainly by","Spinal cord and brainstem"],["Famous example","Knee-jerk reflex"],["Nobel Prize for reflex research","Charles Sherrington, 1932"],["Learned reflexes studied by","Ivan Pavlov"]],
d:[["1664","René Descartes’s description of an automatic withdrawal from fire is published"],["1875","Doctors begin using the knee-jerk test to check the nervous system"],["1897","Ivan Pavlov begins studying digestive reflexes in dogs"],["1904","Pavlov receives the Nobel Prize for work on digestion"],["1906","Charles Sherrington publishes his landmark book on reflexes"],["1932","Sherrington shares the Nobel Prize for his work on nerve cells and reflexes"]],
s2:[["Descartes and the fire","The French philosopher René Descartes, in the 1600s, described how a person automatically pulls a foot away from fire, imagining that the heat tugged a thread running to the brain, which opened a valve to move the muscles. His explanation was wrong in its details, but the idea of an automatic pathway was an early version of the reflex."],["Sherrington’s discoveries","The British scientist Charles Sherrington studied reflexes in detail around 1900. He showed how muscles work in coordinated pairs during reflexes, one contracting while its partner relaxes, and introduced the word synapse for the junction between nerve cells. He shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1932."],["Pavlov’s dogs","The Russian scientist Ivan Pavlov noticed that dogs began to drool not only when food arrived but when they heard sounds that usually came before feeding. He showed that animals can learn new reflexes, called conditioned reflexes, by linking a signal with a reward. His work greatly influenced psychology and the study of learning."],["Newborn reflexes","Babies are born with reflexes that help them survive. The rooting reflex makes a baby turn toward a touch on the cheek and search for milk, and the grasping reflex makes it grip a finger tightly. The startle, or Moro, reflex makes a baby fling out its arms at a sudden noise. Most of these fade within months, and doctors check them to monitor development."],["Testing reflexes","Doctors use a small rubber hammer to test reflexes at the knee, ankle and elbow, and shine a light to test the pupils. Reflexes that are too weak, too strong or uneven between the two sides can be signs of nerve damage or other problems that need further investigation."],["Reflexes in sport","Athletes such as goalkeepers, badminton players and boxers seem to have lightning reflexes, but much of their speed comes from training their brains to recognize patterns and predict what will happen. Practice can make reactions faster and more automatic, though true reflexes stay largely the same."]],
sp:["The yawn and the sneeze","Some reflexes are surprising. Sneezing can be triggered not only by dust or pepper but, in some people, by suddenly looking at bright sunlight, an inherited trait called the photic sneeze reflex. Yawning is partly a reflex, too, and it is famously contagious: seeing, hearing or even reading about yawning can make people yawn. Scientists are still not sure why we yawn or why it spreads, though theories include cooling the brain and signaling tiredness to others. Did you yawn while reading this?"],
g:[["Reflex","A fast, automatic response to a stimulus"],["Reflex arc","The nerve pathway of a reflex"],["Stimulus","Something that causes a response"],["Conditioned reflex","A learned response to a signal"]],
f:["Many reflexes are controlled by the spinal cord.","Blinking is a protective reflex.","Babies grip fingers by reflex."],
fun:"Some people sneeze when they suddenly look at bright sunlight, an inherited reflex nicknamed the ACHOO syndrome.",
tl:"Ang reflex ay mabilis at kusang tugon ng katawan nang hindi nag-iisip, tulad ng pagkurap at paghila ng kamay mula sa mainit na bagay. Kadalasan itong kinokontrol ng spinal cord. Pinoprotektahan nito ang katawan mula sa pinsala."});

ADDART({id:"sunsafety",t:"Sun Safety",c:"Health",i:"🧴",w:"Sunscreen",r:["skin","cancer","extremeheat","light","eye"],
p:[["Sunlight and health","Sunlight helps the body make vitamin D, but too much can harm the skin and eyes."],["Ultraviolet rays","Invisible ultraviolet, or UV, rays cause sunburn and skin damage."],["Staying protected","Shade, clothing, hats, sunglasses and sunscreen protect against UV."]],
l:"Sun safety means protecting the skin and eyes from harmful ultraviolet, or UV, radiation from the Sun. UV rays cannot be seen or felt, but they can burn the skin within minutes on a sunny day, cause premature aging and wrinkles, damage the eyes and raise the risk of skin cancer. Some sunlight is helpful, because it lets the skin make vitamin D, which keeps bones healthy. In tropical countries such as the Philippines, UV levels are often very high or extreme for much of the year. Simple habits, such as seeking shade at midday, wearing hats and protective clothing, using sunglasses and applying sunscreen, greatly reduce the risks.",
s:[["Types of UV rays","Sunlight contains two main types of UV that reach the ground: UVA, which penetrates deep into the skin and causes aging, and UVB, which mainly causes sunburn. Both can damage the DNA in skin cells. A third type, UVC, is blocked by the ozone layer."],["The UV Index","The UV Index is a scale that shows how strong the Sun’s UV rays are on a given day. Values of 1 to 2 are low, while 11 or more are extreme. In the Philippines, the UV Index often reaches very high or extreme levels around midday, and weather services publish daily forecasts."],["Sunburn","Sunburn happens when UV rays damage skin cells, causing redness, pain and sometimes blisters. It can occur even on cloudy days, because most UV passes through clouds. Water, sand and concrete reflect UV and increase exposure, which is why people often burn at the beach or pool."],["Skin cancer","Repeated sun exposure and sunburns raise the risk of skin cancer, including melanoma, the most dangerous type. People with fair skin are at greater risk, but people with darker skin can also get skin cancer and suffer sun damage. Changes in moles or new spots on the skin should be checked by a doctor."]],
k:[["Harmful rays","Ultraviolet (UVA and UVB)"],["Extreme UV Index","11 or higher"],["Sunscreen strength measure","SPF"],["Strongest sun","About 10 a.m. to 4 p.m."],["Benefit of sunlight","Vitamin D"]],
d:[["1930s","Early sunscreens are developed"],["1974","The sun protection factor, or SPF, becomes widely used"],["1985","Scientists discover the ozone hole over Antarctica"],["1987","The Montreal Protocol is agreed to protect the ozone layer"],["1992","Canada introduces the UV Index"],["2002","The World Health Organization publishes a global guide to the UV Index"]],
s2:[["How sunscreen works","Sunscreens contain ingredients that absorb or reflect UV rays before they damage the skin. SPF measures protection against UVB; SPF 30 blocks about 97 percent of UVB rays when applied correctly. Broad-spectrum sunscreens also protect against UVA. Experts recommend applying enough, about a teaspoon for the face and neck, and reapplying every two hours and after swimming or sweating."],["The payong habit","In the Philippines, many people carry a payong, or umbrella, not only for rain but for shade from the Sun, a sensible habit in a tropical climate. Hats, long sleeves and shaded walkways also help. Health authorities recommend staying in the shade during the hours of strongest sunlight, roughly 10 in the morning to 4 in the afternoon."],["Protecting the eyes","UV rays can damage the eyes, contributing to cataracts, which cloud the lens, and growths on the eye’s surface called pterygium, common among people who work outdoors in sunny places such as fishermen and farmers. Sunglasses labeled as blocking UV and wide-brimmed hats protect the eyes."],["Vitamin D","Sunlight on the skin helps the body make vitamin D, needed for strong bones and teeth. Short periods of sun exposure are usually enough, especially in the tropics. Vitamin D is also found in fish, eggs and fortified foods. Health experts advise balancing the benefits of some sunlight with the need to avoid sunburn."],["Children and the sun","Children’s skin is more easily damaged by the Sun, and sunburns in childhood raise the risk of skin cancer later in life. Schools in some countries have no hat, no play rules for outdoor breaks, and many encourage shade structures in playgrounds. Babies under six months should be kept out of direct sun."],["The ozone layer","The ozone layer high in the atmosphere absorbs most harmful UV rays. In the 1980s scientists discovered that chemicals used in refrigerators and spray cans were thinning it. The Montreal Protocol of 1987 phased out these chemicals, and the ozone layer is slowly recovering, a success story often cited for international cooperation."]],
sp:["Slip, slop, slap","In 1981 Australia, which has one of the highest skin cancer rates in the world, launched a famous public health campaign featuring a cartoon seagull named Sid. Its catchy slogan urged people to slip on a shirt, slop on sunscreen and slap on a hat. Later versions added seek shade and slide on sunglasses. The campaign became part of everyday life in Australia, changing habits at beaches and schools. Studies later found that skin cancer rates among younger Australians began to fall, and other countries have adapted the idea for their own sun safety campaigns."],
g:[["Ultraviolet","Invisible rays from the Sun that can harm skin and eyes"],["UV Index","A scale showing the strength of UV rays"],["SPF","Sun protection factor, a measure of sunscreen strength against UVB"],["Melanoma","A dangerous type of skin cancer"]],
f:["UV rays pass through clouds.","SPF 30 blocks about 97 percent of UVB.","Sunlight helps the body make vitamin D."],
fun:"Snow, sand and water reflect UV rays, so you can get sunburned even while sitting in the shade at the beach.",
tl:"Ang sun safety ay pag-iingat sa balat at mata mula sa nakapipinsalang ultraviolet o UV na sinag ng Araw. Nagdudulot ang labis na UV ng sunburn at kanser sa balat. Nakatutulong ang lilim, payong, sumbrero, salaming pang-araw at sunscreen."});

ADDART({id:"stroke",t:"Stroke",c:"Disease and Medicine",i:"🧠",w:"Stroke",r:["brain","heartdisease","blood","exercise","firstaid"],
p:[["A brain attack","A stroke happens when blood flow to part of the brain is blocked or a blood vessel bursts."],["Act fast","Quick treatment can save brain cells and lives."],["Preventable","Controlling blood pressure and healthy habits greatly reduce the risk."]],
l:"A stroke occurs when the blood supply to part of the brain is suddenly cut off, either because a blood vessel is blocked by a clot, called an ischemic stroke, or because a blood vessel bursts and bleeds into or around the brain, called a hemorrhagic stroke. Without oxygen, brain cells begin to die within minutes. Strokes can cause sudden weakness or numbness on one side of the body, trouble speaking or seeing, confusion and loss of balance. Stroke is one of the leading causes of death and disability in the world and in the Philippines. Fast emergency treatment can greatly improve recovery, and most strokes can be prevented by controlling high blood pressure and living a healthy lifestyle.",
s:[["Types of stroke","Most strokes are ischemic, caused by a clot blocking an artery that supplies the brain. Hemorrhagic strokes, caused by bleeding, are less common but often more severe. A transient ischemic attack, or mini-stroke, causes brief symptoms that go away, but it is a serious warning sign that a major stroke may follow."],["Warning signs","The acronym BE FAST helps people recognize a stroke: Balance loss, Eyes losing vision, Face drooping on one side, Arm weakness, Speech difficulty, and Time to call emergency services. In the Philippines, campaigns also teach the signs in Filipino so families can act quickly."],["Why speed matters","During a stroke, millions of brain cells can die every minute. Doctors say time is brain. For ischemic strokes, clot-dissolving medicine given within a few hours, or a procedure to pull out the clot, can restore blood flow and reduce lasting damage. Patients should be taken to hospital immediately."],["Risk factors","The biggest risk factor is high blood pressure, or hypertension. Others include smoking, diabetes, high cholesterol, being overweight, heavy drinking, physical inactivity, an irregular heartbeat called atrial fibrillation, and older age. Many of these can be controlled."]],
k:[["Main types","Ischemic and hemorrhagic"],["Biggest risk factor","High blood pressure"],["Warning signs","BE FAST"],["Clot-dissolving drug approved","1996"],["World Stroke Day","29 October"]],
d:[["c. 400 BC","Hippocrates describes sudden paralysis, called apoplexy"],["1658","Johann Jakob Wepfer links apoplexy to bleeding in the brain"],["1996","A clot-dissolving drug is approved for stroke treatment in the United States"],["2006","The first World Stroke Day is held"],["2015","Trials show that removing clots mechanically improves recovery"],["2020s","Mobile stroke units and telemedicine spread to more hospitals"]],
s2:[["Stroke in the Philippines","Stroke and other diseases of the heart and blood vessels are among the leading causes of death in the Philippines. High blood pressure is very common and often goes undiagnosed or untreated. Health programs encourage people to have their blood pressure checked regularly, take prescribed medicines and reduce salt in their diet."],["Salt and blood pressure","Eating too much salt raises blood pressure in many people. Filipino diets often include salty foods such as dried fish, instant noodles, processed meats and condiments such as patis, bagoong and soy sauce. Health experts recommend tasting food before adding salt, cooking with herbs and spices and choosing fresh foods."],["Recovering from stroke","Many stroke survivors need rehabilitation to relearn skills such as walking, speaking, eating and dressing. Physical, occupational and speech therapists help them regain abilities. The brain can partly rewire itself, a process called neuroplasticity, especially with early and regular therapy. Family support is very important."],["Stroke in younger people","Although most strokes occur in older adults, they can happen at any age, even in children. Rising rates of high blood pressure, diabetes and obesity among younger adults worry doctors. Anyone with sudden stroke symptoms, whatever their age, should get emergency help."],["Modern treatments","A clot-dissolving medicine was approved for stroke in the United States in 1996, transforming treatment. In 2015 major trials showed that removing large clots with a thin tube threaded through the arteries greatly improves recovery. Some cities use ambulances with CT scanners, called mobile stroke units, to begin treatment before reaching hospital."],["Preventing stroke","Up to about 80 percent of strokes may be preventable. Measures include checking and controlling blood pressure, not smoking, being physically active, eating plenty of vegetables and fruit, limiting salt and alcohol, keeping a healthy weight and managing diabetes. Treating an irregular heartbeat with medicine can also greatly reduce stroke risk."]],
sp:["Time is brain","One morning, a woman notices that her father’s smile looks lopsided and he cannot lift his right arm. His words come out slurred. Remembering a television campaign about stroke, she calls for an ambulance instead of waiting to see whether he gets better. At the hospital, a scan shows a clot blocking an artery in his brain, and doctors quickly give him clot-dissolving medicine. Within hours his arm begins to move again. Doctors often share stories like this to show how recognizing the signs and acting fast can make the difference between recovery and lifelong disability."],
g:[["Stroke","Sudden loss of blood flow to part of the brain"],["Ischemic stroke","A stroke caused by a blocked blood vessel"],["Hemorrhagic stroke","A stroke caused by bleeding in the brain"],["Hypertension","High blood pressure"]],
f:["High blood pressure is the biggest stroke risk factor.","BE FAST helps people spot a stroke.","Many strokes can be prevented."],
fun:"The old name for stroke, apoplexy, comes from a Greek word meaning struck down suddenly.",
tl:"Ang stroke ay nangyayari kapag naharangan o pumutok ang ugat ng dugo sa utak. Mahalaga ang agarang paggamot upang mailigtas ang utak. Maiiwasan ito sa pagkontrol ng altapresyon at malusog na pamumuhay."});

ADDART({id:"asthma",t:"Asthma",c:"Disease and Medicine",i:"🫁",w:"Asthma",r:["lungs","allergy","immune","hygiene","exercise"],
p:[["Narrowed airways","Asthma is a long-term condition in which the airways in the lungs become swollen and narrow."],["Wheezing and coughing","It causes coughing, wheezing, chest tightness and shortness of breath."],["Can be controlled","With the right medicines and care, most people with asthma live full, active lives."]],
l:"Asthma is a common long-term disease of the lungs in which the airways become inflamed, swollen and narrowed, making it hard to breathe. Symptoms include coughing, wheezing, chest tightness and shortness of breath, which may come and go and can suddenly worsen in an asthma attack. Attacks are often triggered by things such as dust mites, smoke, air pollution, pollen, pet dander, colds, cold air or exercise. Asthma affects hundreds of millions of people worldwide, often beginning in childhood. There is no cure, but inhaled medicines can control symptoms and prevent attacks, allowing most people with asthma to study, work and play sports normally.",
s:[["What happens in the lungs","Air reaches the lungs through branching tubes called bronchi. In asthma, the lining of these tubes is inflamed and sensitive. When triggered, the muscles around them tighten, the lining swells and extra mucus is produced, so the airways narrow and air struggles to flow in and out."],["Common triggers","Triggers vary from person to person. They include house dust mites, cockroaches, mold, pollen, animal fur, tobacco and wood smoke, vehicle and factory pollution, strong smells, respiratory infections, cold or humid air, exercise and strong emotions. Learning and avoiding personal triggers helps prevent attacks."],["Inhalers","Most asthma medicines are breathed in using inhalers, which deliver medicine directly to the lungs. Reliever inhalers quickly relax the airway muscles during symptoms. Preventer inhalers, usually containing steroids, reduce inflammation over time and are taken regularly. Spacers attached to inhalers help children and others get more medicine into the lungs."],["An asthma attack","During a severe attack, a person may struggle to breathe or speak, and their lips may turn blue. They should use their reliever inhaler and seek emergency help if they do not improve quickly. Having a written asthma action plan helps families know what to do."]],
k:[["Affected organ","Lungs (airways)"],["Main symptoms","Cough, wheeze, chest tightness, breathlessness"],["Main treatment","Inhaled medicines"],["Metered-dose inhaler invented","1956"],["World Asthma Day","First Tuesday of May"]],
d:[["c. 400 BC","Greek physicians use the word asthma, meaning panting"],["1190","Maimonides writes a treatise on asthma"],["1956","The pressurized metered-dose inhaler is invented"],["1970s","Inhaled steroid medicines are introduced"],["1993","The Global Initiative for Asthma is launched"],["1998","The first World Asthma Day is held"]],
s2:[["Who gets asthma","Asthma often starts in childhood and is one of the most common chronic diseases in children. It tends to run in families and is more common in people with allergies or eczema. Some children grow out of it, while others have it throughout life. Adults can also develop asthma."],["Air pollution and asthma","Polluted air, from vehicles, factories, burning garbage and cooking with wood or charcoal indoors, can trigger asthma and may contribute to its development. In crowded cities such as Metro Manila, traffic fumes are a concern. Cleaner fuels, better ventilation and reducing smoke help protect people’s lungs."],["The inhaler’s invention","In 1955 a 13-year-old girl with asthma asked her father, who worked at a drug company in the United States, why her medicine could not come in a spray can like hairspray. The company developed the pressurized metered-dose inhaler, released in 1956, which transformed asthma treatment and is still widely used."],["Sport and asthma","Exercise can trigger asthma symptoms in some people, but with good control, people with asthma can play sports at the highest level. Many Olympic athletes and professional footballers have asthma. Warming up properly and using a reliever inhaler before exercise, if prescribed, can help."],["Asthma in the Philippines","Asthma is common among Filipino children and adults. Doctors encourage patients to use preventer inhalers regularly, avoid smoke and keep homes free of dust and mold. Health groups have worked to make inhalers more affordable and to train health workers to diagnose and manage asthma."],["Living well with asthma","With good treatment, most people with asthma have few symptoms. Key steps include taking preventer medicine as prescribed, knowing how to use inhalers correctly, avoiding triggers, getting vaccinated against flu and having regular check-ups. Schools and coaches should know which students have asthma and where their inhalers are kept."]],
sp:["A spray can for medicine","In the 1950s, people with asthma used bulky glass nebulizers with rubber bulbs to breathe in medicine. They were fragile and hard to carry. According to the company’s history, Susie Maison, the 13-year-old daughter of the president of Riker Laboratories in California, asked why her asthma medicine could not be put in a spray can like her mother’s hairspray. Engineers took up the challenge and developed a small canister that released an exact dose of medicine with each puff. Launched in 1956, the metered-dose inhaler became one of the most widely used medical devices in the world."],
g:[["Asthma","A long-term condition that narrows the airways"],["Bronchi","The main airways leading into the lungs"],["Inhaler","A device that delivers medicine into the lungs"],["Trigger","Something that sets off asthma symptoms"]],
f:["Asthma narrows the airways of the lungs.","Inhalers deliver medicine straight to the lungs.","The metered-dose inhaler was invented in 1956."],
fun:"The word asthma comes from an ancient Greek word meaning panting or short of breath.",
tl:"Ang hika ay pangmatagalang sakit kung saan namamaga at kumikitid ang daanan ng hangin sa baga. Nagdudulot ito ng ubo, paghuni sa paghinga at hirap huminga. Nakokontrol ito ng tamang gamot gaya ng inhaler."});

;
/* data/tech9.js */
// Technology, more articles: AI-generated art, voice assistants, motorcycles, open-source software and fiber optics.
ADDART({id:"aiart",t:"AI-Generated Images",c:"Artificial Intelligence",i:"🖼️",w:"Artificial intelligence art",r:["ai","neural","llm","modernart","aisafety"],
p:[["Pictures from words","AI image generators create pictures from written descriptions."],["Rapid growth","Tools such as DALL-E, Midjourney and Stable Diffusion became popular in 2022."],["Big debates","They raise questions about creativity, copyright, jobs and fake images."]],
l:"AI-generated images are pictures created by artificial intelligence systems, usually from a written description called a prompt. A person might type a cat astronaut painted in the style of a Filipino jeepney, and within seconds the system produces original-looking images. These tools are trained on huge collections of images and captions from the internet, learning patterns that link words to visual features. AI image generators became widely available in 2022 with tools such as DALL-E, Midjourney and Stable Diffusion, and later video generators followed. They are used for illustration, design, advertising, games and fun, but they have also sparked intense debate about artistic creativity, copyright, the livelihoods of artists and the spread of misleading fake images.",
s:[["How they work","Most modern image generators use a method called diffusion. During training, the system learns to remove random noise from images step by step. To create a new picture, it starts from pure noise and gradually shapes it into an image that matches the prompt, guided by a model that connects words and pictures."],["Early AI art","Computer-generated art has existed since the 1960s, and the program AARON, created by the artist Harold Cohen from the 1970s, drew original pictures. In 2014 researchers invented generative adversarial networks, or GANs, which could produce realistic faces and artworks. In 2018 a GAN-made portrait sold at auction for 432,500 US dollars."],["The 2022 boom","In 2022 several powerful image generators became available to the public. Millions of people experimented with them, sharing fantastical images online. Companies added image generation to design software, phones and chat assistants, and quality improved quickly, from blurry pictures to images that can be hard to tell from photographs."],["Uses","AI images are used to brainstorm ideas, create concept art, illustrate stories and presentations, design products and make advertising. Some artists use them as one tool in their creative process, combining AI output with their own drawing, painting or photography."]],
k:[["Input","A written prompt"],["Main method","Diffusion models"],["Public boom","2022"],["Well-known tools","DALL-E, Midjourney, Stable Diffusion"],["Main debates","Copyright, artists’ jobs, deepfakes"]],
d:[["1973","Harold Cohen begins developing AARON, an art-making program"],["2014","Generative adversarial networks are invented"],["2018","An AI-generated portrait sells at Christie’s auction house"],["2021","OpenAI introduces DALL-E"],["2022","Midjourney and Stable Diffusion are released to the public"],["2023","Artists and image companies file lawsuits over AI training"]],
s2:[["Is it art?","People disagree about whether AI-generated images are art and who should be considered the artist: the person writing the prompt, the developers or the system itself. In 2022 an AI-assisted image won a prize at a state fair art competition in the United States, causing a heated debate. Supporters compare AI to earlier tools such as cameras, while critics argue it lacks human intention and skill."],["Copyright questions","AI image systems are trained on billions of images, many created by artists and photographers who did not give permission. Artists and stock photo companies have filed lawsuits arguing that this infringes their rights, while AI companies argue that learning from public images is lawful. Courts in several countries are considering these cases. Authorities in the United States have said that images generated without meaningful human creative input cannot be copyrighted."],["Artists’ livelihoods","Some illustrators, designers and concept artists report losing work as companies turn to AI images. Others have found new opportunities. Artists’ groups call for consent, credit and payment when their work is used to train AI, and some tools now let artists opt out or use systems trained only on licensed images."],["Fake images","AI can create realistic images of events that never happened, such as fake photos of politicians, disasters or celebrities. These can spread misinformation and be used to harass people. Technology companies are developing watermarks and labels to identify AI-generated content, and many governments are considering rules."],["Bias in images","Because AI learns from internet images, it can repeat stereotypes, for example showing certain jobs mainly with one gender or ethnicity, or poorly representing people and places from some countries, including Southeast Asia. Developers are working to reduce such biases, and users are encouraged to look critically at results."],["Using AI images responsibly","Experts suggest being open about when images are AI-generated, respecting the rights of artists, not creating images that deceive or harm real people, and checking whether school, workplace or competition rules allow AI-made work."]],
sp:["The portrait that sold for a fortune","In October 2018 the auction house Christie’s in New York sold a blurry portrait of a man in a dark coat, titled Edmond de Belamy. It was created by a French art group called Obvious using a generative adversarial network trained on thousands of historical portraits. Instead of a painter’s signature, the corner bore a mathematical formula from the algorithm. Expected to sell for less than 10,000 dollars, it fetched 432,500 dollars, making headlines around the world. The sale also stirred controversy, as the group had used computer code written by another young programmer who received no share of the money."],
g:[["Prompt","A written instruction given to an AI system"],["Diffusion model","An AI that creates images by gradually removing noise"],["GAN","Generative adversarial network, two AIs that compete to make realistic images"],["Watermark","A hidden or visible mark identifying the source of an image"]],
f:["AI image generators create pictures from text prompts.","An AI portrait sold for 432,500 dollars in 2018.","AI images raise copyright debates."],
fun:"Early AI image generators were notorious for drawing hands with too many or too few fingers.",
tl:"Ang AI-generated images ay larawang nililikha ng artificial intelligence mula sa nakasulat na paglalarawan. Sumikat ang mga ito noong 2022. Pinagtatalunan ang epekto nito sa copyright, sa hanapbuhay ng mga artista at sa pagkalat ng pekeng larawan."});

ADDART({id:"voiceassistants",t:"Voice Assistants",c:"Artificial Intelligence",i:"🗣️",w:"Virtual assistant",r:["ai","llm","smartphone","smarthome","dataprivacy"],
p:[["Talking to computers","Voice assistants let people control devices and find information by speaking."],["Siri, Alexa and more","Well-known assistants include Siri, Alexa and Google Assistant."],["Smarter conversations","New AI models allow more natural spoken conversations."]],
l:"A voice assistant is a computer program that understands spoken commands and questions and responds by speaking, carrying out tasks or finding information. Voice assistants are built into smartphones, smart speakers, cars, televisions and other devices. They use speech recognition to turn spoken words into text, artificial intelligence to understand what the person wants, and speech synthesis to reply aloud. Apple’s Siri, launched in 2011, brought voice assistants to millions of people, followed by Amazon’s Alexa, Google Assistant and others. Since the 2020s, assistants powered by large language models can hold more natural conversations. They make technology easier to use, especially for people with disabilities, but raise concerns about privacy.",
s:[["How they work","When a person says a wake word, such as hey Siri or Alexa, the device starts listening. The recorded speech is converted into text by speech recognition software, often in the cloud. Language-understanding systems work out the request, such as setting a timer or checking the weather, and a synthetic voice gives the answer."],["Early speech recognition","In 1952 Bell Laboratories built Audrey, a machine that could recognize spoken digits from a single speaker. IBM’s Shoebox, shown in 1962, understood 16 words. For decades, speech recognition was slow and error-prone, but improvements in computing and statistical methods gradually made it practical for dictation software in the 1990s."],["The smartphone era","Apple introduced Siri on the iPhone 4S in 2011. Google launched Google Now and later Google Assistant, and Microsoft introduced Cortana. Amazon’s Echo smart speaker with Alexa, launched in 2014, put voice assistants in homes, where people use them to play music, control lights and ask questions."],["What they can do","Voice assistants can make calls, send messages, set alarms and reminders, answer questions, translate languages, give directions, play music and control smart home devices. Newer assistants can summarize information, help with homework, hold conversations and describe what a phone camera sees."]],
k:[["Early speech recognizer","Audrey, Bell Labs, 1952"],["Siri launched","2011"],["Amazon Echo launched","2014"],["Key technologies","Speech recognition and speech synthesis"],["Main concern","Privacy"]],
d:[["1952","Bell Labs builds Audrey, which recognizes spoken digits"],["1962","IBM demonstrates the Shoebox speech recognizer"],["1997","Dragon NaturallySpeaking software allows continuous dictation"],["2011","Apple launches Siri"],["2014","Amazon launches the Echo smart speaker with Alexa"],["2023","Assistants powered by large language models begin to spread"]],
s2:[["Helping people with disabilities","Voice assistants can be life-changing for people who are blind or have limited movement, allowing them to send messages, read news, control lights and call for help by voice. Older people who find screens difficult also benefit. Disability advocates have pushed companies to improve support for people with speech differences."],["Accents and languages","Voice assistants have worked best for speakers of major languages with standard accents and have struggled with regional accents and smaller languages. Filipinos who mix English and Filipino in Taglish have found some assistants confused by switching languages. Companies have been adding more languages and training systems on more diverse voices."],["Privacy concerns","Because voice assistants listen for wake words, many people worry that they may record private conversations. Reports have revealed that some companies had human workers listen to recordings to improve accuracy. Companies now offer settings to delete recordings and mute microphones, and privacy laws regulate how voice data is used."],["Large language model assistants","From 2023, companies began connecting voice assistants to large language models, the technology behind AI chatbots. These assistants can understand more complex requests, answer follow-up questions and speak with more natural voices and timing. Critics warn that they can still make mistakes or invent facts."],["Voice cloning","AI can now copy a person’s voice from short recordings. This allows people who have lost their voices to illness to speak again with a synthetic version of their own voice. However, cloned voices have also been used in scams, such as fake calls pretending to be relatives in trouble, so experts advise families to agree on secret code words."],["The future of talking to machines","Researchers expect voice to become a more common way to interact with computers, cars and appliances. Assistants may become more personal, remembering preferences and helping with daily planning. How to balance convenience with privacy and accuracy remains a central question."]],
sp:["The machine called Audrey","In 1952 engineers at Bell Laboratories in the United States unveiled a machine called Audrey, short for Automatic Digit Recognizer. It filled a large rack of electronics and could recognize the numbers zero to nine when spoken by its inventor, with high accuracy, though it worked poorly for other voices. Lights flashed to show which digit it had heard. Engineers hoped such machines could one day let people dial telephones by voice. It took nearly 60 years of progress in computing before people could simply ask their phones to call home."],
g:[["Voice assistant","A program that responds to spoken commands"],["Speech recognition","Technology that converts speech into text"],["Speech synthesis","Technology that produces spoken words from text"],["Wake word","A word that activates a voice assistant"]],
f:["Siri launched in 2011.","Audrey recognized spoken digits in 1952.","Voice assistants help people with disabilities."],
fun:"The name Siri was chosen by one of its creators and is a Norwegian name meaning beautiful woman who leads you to victory.",
tl:"Ang voice assistant ay programang nakauunawa ng sinasabing utos at sumasagot sa pamamagitan ng pagsasalita. Kabilang dito ang Siri, Alexa at Google Assistant. Nakatutulong ito sa mga may kapansanan ngunit may alalahanin sa privacy."});

ADDART({id:"motorcycles",t:"Motorcycles",c:"Transport",i:"🏍️",w:"Motorcycle",r:["cars","bicycle","jeepney","megacities","gigeconomy"],
p:[["Two wheels and an engine","A motorcycle is a two-wheeled vehicle powered by an engine."],["Asia’s workhorse","Motorcycles are the main family vehicle in much of Southeast Asia."],["Safety matters","Helmets and safe riding save many lives."]],
l:"A motorcycle is a two-wheeled motor vehicle, ranging from small scooters and underbones to powerful sports bikes and heavy touring machines. The first motorcycles appeared in Germany in the 1880s, and mass production began in the 1890s. In the 20th century motorcycles became popular for sport, travel and transport, and Japanese companies such as Honda made them affordable worldwide. Today hundreds of millions of motorcycles are used around the world, especially in Asia, where they are often the main vehicle for families, workers and delivery riders. In the Philippines, motorcycles are the most common type of registered vehicle. They are cheap and efficient but riders face high risks, making helmets and road safety vital.",
s:[["Early motorcycles","In 1885 the German engineers Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach fitted a small gasoline engine to a wooden frame with two wheels, creating the Reitwagen, often called the first motorcycle. In 1894 the Hildebrand and Wolfmüller became the first motorcycle produced in large numbers for sale."],["How a motorcycle works","Most motorcycles have a gasoline engine that turns the rear wheel through a chain, belt or shaft. Riders control speed with a twist grip on the handlebar, brake with hand and foot levers, and in many models change gears with a foot pedal. Scooters and many small bikes have automatic transmissions. Riders balance and steer by leaning into turns."],["Kinds of motorcycles","Scooters have small wheels and a platform for the feet. Underbones, popular in Southeast Asia, combine features of scooters and motorcycles. Standard and sport bikes are built for speed and handling, cruisers for relaxed riding, touring bikes for long trips and dirt bikes for rough tracks."],["The Super Cub","In 1958 Honda launched the Super Cub, a light, reliable and easy-to-ride step-through motorcycle. It became the best-selling motor vehicle in history, with more than 100 million built by 2017. Its success helped make motorcycles everyday transport across Asia, Africa and Latin America."]],
k:[["First motorcycle","Daimler Reitwagen, 1885"],["First mass-produced motorcycle","Hildebrand and Wolfmüller, 1894"],["Best-selling motor vehicle","Honda Super Cub"],["Philippine helmet law","Republic Act 10054 (2009)"],["Most registered vehicles in the Philippines","Motorcycles and tricycles"]],
d:[["1885","Daimler and Maybach build the Reitwagen"],["1894","The first mass-produced motorcycle goes on sale"],["1903","Harley-Davidson is founded in the United States"],["1958","Honda launches the Super Cub"],["2009","The Philippines passes the Motorcycle Helmet Act"],["2019","A pilot program for motorcycle taxis begins in Metro Manila"]],
s2:[["Motorcycles in the Philippines","Motorcycles are the most popular vehicles in the Philippines, used for commuting, carrying goods, farming and family trips. They are affordable, use little fuel and can weave through heavy traffic and travel on narrow rural roads. Many families buy them through installment plans."],["Tricycles and habal-habal","The tricycle, a motorcycle with a covered sidecar, is a common form of public transport in Philippine towns and cities. In rural and mountainous areas, habal-habal, motorcycles carrying several passengers or extended with wooden planks, transport people where other vehicles cannot go."],["Motorcycle taxis and delivery","App-based motorcycle taxi services have grown in Metro Manila and other cities since a government pilot program began in 2019. Food and parcel delivery riders, many working through apps, have become a familiar sight. Supporters praise the speed and jobs these services provide, while debates continue over safety, regulation and workers’ rights."],["Road safety","Motorcycle riders are much more likely than car occupants to be killed or seriously injured in crashes. In the Philippines, motorcyclists make up a large share of road deaths. The Motorcycle Helmet Act of 2009 requires riders and passengers to wear standard helmets, and rules limit carrying young children. Training, safe speeds and visible clothing also help."],["Electric motorcycles","Electric scooters and motorcycles are spreading quickly, especially in China, Vietnam, Taiwan, India and Indonesia. They are quiet and produce no exhaust fumes, helping reduce air pollution in cities. Battery-swapping stations allow riders to exchange a flat battery for a charged one in seconds."],["Motorcycle culture","Motorcycles have inspired clubs, races and films. Events such as the Isle of Man TT and MotoGP draw huge audiences. In the Philippines, riding clubs organize long rides to provinces, charity events and safety campaigns, and motorcycle shows attract many fans."]],
sp:["The first motorcycle ride","On a November day in 1885, 17-year-old Paul Daimler climbed onto a strange machine built by his father, Gottlieb Daimler, and his partner Wilhelm Maybach. It had a wooden frame, iron-banded wooden wheels, small side wheels to help it stay upright, and a tiny gasoline engine under the seat. He rode it about 12 kilometers between two towns near Stuttgart, Germany, reaching about 12 kilometers per hour. The leather seat reportedly caught fire from the hot engine. The Reitwagen, or riding car, was really a test of the engine Daimler would soon put into automobiles, but it is remembered as the ancestor of all motorcycles."],
g:[["Motorcycle","A two-wheeled motor vehicle"],["Scooter","A motorcycle with small wheels and a platform for the feet"],["Underbone","A light motorcycle with a step-through frame, common in Asia"],["Habal-habal","A motorcycle used to carry passengers in rural Philippines"]],
f:["The first motorcycle was built in 1885.","The Honda Super Cub is the best-selling motor vehicle ever.","Philippine law requires motorcycle helmets."],
fun:"The world’s first motorcycle had a leather seat that reportedly caught fire from the engine during its first ride.",
tl:"Ang motorsiklo ay sasakyang may dalawang gulong at makina. Ito ang pinakakaraniwang rehistradong sasakyan sa Pilipinas. Mahalaga ang helmet at ligtas na pagmamaneho upang makaiwas sa disgrasya."});

ADDART({id:"opensource",t:"Open-Source Software",c:"Computers",i:"🐧",w:"Open-source software",r:["coding","comp","internet","cybersecurity","cloud"],
p:[["Code anyone can see","Open-source software is software whose code is freely available to read, change and share."],["Built together","Volunteers and companies around the world collaborate to build it."],["Everywhere","Open-source software runs much of the internet, phones and supercomputers."]],
l:"Open-source software is computer software whose source code, the instructions written by programmers, is published so that anyone can read it, use it, change it and share it, usually under licenses that guarantee these freedoms. It is often developed collaboratively by communities of volunteers, students and companies around the world. Famous examples include the Linux operating system, the Firefox web browser, the Android phone platform and Wikipedia’s software. Open-source code now forms the foundation of much of the internet, most smartphones and nearly all of the world’s supercomputers. Supporters value its openness, security and shared benefits, while challenges include funding the volunteers who maintain critical code.",
s:[["Source code","Programs are written by people in programming languages, producing source code. Proprietary software companies usually keep their source code secret and sell only the finished program. Open-source projects publish their code, letting others learn from it, fix problems and build new features."],["Free software","In 1983 the American programmer Richard Stallman launched the GNU Project to create a completely free operating system, and in 1985 he founded the Free Software Foundation. He argued that users should have the freedom to run, study, change and share software, and created licenses such as the GNU General Public License to protect these freedoms."],["Linux","In 1991 Linus Torvalds, a Finnish student, released the first version of the Linux kernel, the core of an operating system, and invited others to help. Thousands of programmers joined in. Combined with GNU tools, Linux became a complete system that now runs most web servers, the world’s fastest supercomputers and, through Android, billions of phones."],["The term open source","In 1998 a group of software developers introduced the term open source to describe freely shared software in a way that appealed to businesses. That year the company Netscape released the code of its web browser, which later became the basis of Firefox. Today many large technology companies both use and contribute to open-source projects."]],
k:[["GNU Project founded","1983"],["Linux first released","1991"],["Term open source coined","1998"],["Famous examples","Linux, Firefox, Android, Python"],["Common license","GNU General Public License"]],
d:[["1983","Richard Stallman announces the GNU Project"],["1985","The Free Software Foundation is founded"],["1991","Linus Torvalds releases Linux"],["1998","The term open source is introduced"],["2004","Firefox 1.0 is released"],["2008","The code-sharing platform GitHub is launched"]],
s2:[["How projects work","Open-source projects usually have maintainers who review and accept changes. Contributors anywhere in the world can suggest improvements, report bugs or write documentation. Platforms such as GitHub and GitLab host millions of projects, allowing people to collaborate across time zones and countries."],["Why companies use it","Businesses use open-source software because it saves money, avoids dependence on a single supplier and lets them customize tools. Many companies pay employees to contribute to projects they rely on. Governments, schools and nonprofit groups, including some in the Philippines, use open-source tools to cut costs."],["Security","Supporters argue that open code is more secure because many people can inspect it and find flaws. However, problems can still go unnoticed. In 2021 a serious flaw called Log4Shell was found in widely used open-source code, and in 2024 a hidden backdoor was discovered in a compression tool called xz before it could spread widely. These events highlighted the need to support maintainers."],["Who pays the maintainers?","Many important projects are maintained by small groups of volunteers in their spare time. Critics warn that the digital world depends on code that may be underfunded. Foundations, company sponsorships and donation platforms now help pay maintainers, and some governments have started funding open-source security."],["Open source beyond software","The open-source idea has spread to other fields. Wikipedia lets anyone edit its encyclopedia articles, OpenStreetMap is a free world map made by volunteers, and open hardware projects share designs for electronics and 3D printers. Scientists increasingly share data and code openly."],["Learning to code","Open-source projects give students and beginners a chance to read real code, practice programming and contribute to software used by millions. Filipino developers contribute to international projects, and local communities hold meetups and events to encourage open-source participation."]],
sp:["Just a hobby","On 25 August 1991, Linus Torvalds, a 21-year-old computer science student at the University of Helsinki, posted a short message to an online discussion group. He wrote that he was making a free operating system, just a hobby, which he said would not be big and professional. He invited others to share ideas. Within a few years, programmers around the world were contributing code, and his hobby grew into Linux, one of the most important pieces of software ever created. Its mascot, a friendly penguin named Tux, was chosen because Torvalds said he had once been bitten by a penguin at a zoo."],
g:[["Source code","The human-readable instructions of a computer program"],["Open source","Software whose code anyone can read, change and share"],["License","Legal terms that say how software may be used"],["Maintainer","A person who manages and reviews changes to a project"]],
f:["Linux was released in 1991.","Android is built on Linux.","Most web servers run open-source software."],
fun:"Linux’s penguin mascot, Tux, was chosen partly because Linus Torvalds said he had once been nipped by a penguin at a zoo.",
tl:"Ang open-source software ay software na ang code ay malayang mababasa, mababago at maibabahagi ng kahit sino. Sama-sama itong binubuo ng mga boluntaryo at kompanya sa buong mundo. Kabilang dito ang Linux, Firefox at Android."});

ADDART({id:"fiberoptics",t:"Fiber Optics",c:"Communication",i:"💡",w:"Optical fiber",r:["internet","light","laser","phone","cloud"],
p:[["Light through glass","Fiber optics send information as pulses of light through thin strands of glass."],["The internet’s backbone","Undersea fiber cables carry most international internet traffic."],["Fast and clear","Fiber connections are much faster than copper wires."]],
l:"Fiber optics is the technology of sending light through long, thin strands of very pure glass or plastic called optical fibers. Each fiber is about as thin as a human hair. Light travels along it by bouncing off its walls, a process called total internal reflection, and can carry huge amounts of information as rapid flashes of laser light. Optical fibers form the backbone of the internet and modern telephone networks, including undersea cables that link continents and carry almost all international data. They are also used in medicine to see inside the body, in lighting and in sensors. The physicist Charles Kao, who showed in 1966 that glass fibers could carry signals over long distances, received the Nobel Prize in Physics in 2009.",
s:[["How light stays inside","An optical fiber has a glass core surrounded by a layer of glass called cladding that bends light less. When light hits the boundary at a shallow angle, it reflects back into the core instead of escaping. Bouncing again and again, it can travel many kilometers along a fiber, even around gentle bends."],["Carrying information","A laser at one end switches on and off billions of times a second, representing the ones and zeros of digital data. At the other end, a detector turns the light back into electrical signals. Many colors of light can travel through the same fiber at once, each carrying its own stream of data, so a single fiber can carry enormous amounts of information."],["Better than copper","Compared with copper wires, optical fibers carry far more data over much longer distances with less signal loss. They are not affected by electrical interference, are hard to tap secretly and are lighter. This is why internet providers install fiber to homes and businesses for fast broadband."],["Undersea cables","Hundreds of fiber-optic cables lie on the ocean floor, linking countries and continents. They carry the vast majority of international internet traffic, phone calls and financial transactions. Ships lay them across the seabed, and repair ships fix them when they are damaged by anchors, fishing or earthquakes."]],
k:[["Principle","Total internal reflection"],["Key paper","Charles Kao, 1966"],["Low-loss fiber made","Corning, 1970"],["First transatlantic fiber cable","TAT-8, 1988"],["Nobel Prize","Charles Kao, 2009"]],
d:[["1842","Daniel Colladon shows light guided along a stream of water"],["1960","The first working laser is built"],["1966","Charles Kao proposes glass fibers for communication"],["1970","Corning makes low-loss optical fiber"],["1988","TAT-8, the first transatlantic fiber-optic cable, begins service"],["2009","Charles Kao receives the Nobel Prize in Physics"]],
s2:[["Guiding light","In 1842 the Swiss physicist Daniel Colladon showed that light could follow a curved stream of water pouring from a tank, staying trapped inside it. This light fountain demonstrated the principle behind optical fibers. Later inventors used bundles of glass fibers to carry images, leading to medical instruments for looking inside the body."],["Charles Kao’s insight","In the 1960s glass fibers lost too much light to be useful for long-distance communication. Charles Kao, working in England, showed in 1966 that the losses came from impurities, and that very pure glass could carry signals for kilometers. His prediction inspired researchers at Corning, who in 1970 produced the first low-loss fiber. Kao is often called the father of fiber optics."],["Fiber in the Philippines","As an archipelago, the Philippines depends on undersea cables to connect its islands and link it to the world. Several international cables land at stations on Luzon and other islands, and domestic cables connect major islands. Internet providers have expanded fiber-to-the-home service in cities, while a national broadband plan aims to bring fast internet to more provinces."],["Vulnerable cables","Because so much traffic depends on undersea cables, damage can cut off communication. In 2022 a volcanic eruption near Tonga severed its only international cable, leaving the country largely offline for weeks. Countries worry about protecting cables from accidents, earthquakes and possible sabotage."],["Fiber in medicine","Endoscopes use bundles of optical fibers to carry light into the body and send images back, allowing doctors to examine the stomach, lungs or joints and perform keyhole surgery through small cuts. Fibers also deliver laser light for eye surgery and other treatments."],["Light for the future","Researchers are developing fibers that carry even more data, hollow-core fibers in which light travels through air for greater speed, and fibers that can sense earthquakes, temperature or vibration along their length. Optical connections are also used inside large data centers that power cloud computing and artificial intelligence."]],
sp:["Repairing a cable at the bottom of the sea","When an undersea cable breaks, perhaps snagged by a ship’s anchor or shaken by an undersea earthquake, a special repair ship sails to the area. Engineers send test signals to locate the break, sometimes thousands of meters deep. The crew lowers a grapnel, a hooked tool, or a remotely operated underwater robot to catch the cable and haul it to the surface. On board, technicians carefully splice the hair-thin glass fibers together in a clean room, test the connection and lower the repaired cable back to the seabed. Repairs can take days or weeks, depending on weather and depth."],
g:[["Optical fiber","A thin strand of glass that carries light"],["Total internal reflection","When light bounces back inside a material instead of escaping"],["Cladding","The outer glass layer that keeps light in the core"],["Broadband","A fast internet connection"]],
f:["Optical fibers are about as thin as a human hair.","Undersea cables carry most international internet traffic.","Charles Kao won the 2009 Nobel Prize."],
fun:"A single modern optical fiber can carry the equivalent of millions of video calls at the same time.",
tl:"Ang fiber optics ay teknolohiyang nagpapadala ng impormasyon bilang liwanag sa manipis na hibla ng salamin. Ang mga kable nito sa ilalim ng dagat ang nagdadala ng karamihan ng internet sa pagitan ng mga bansa. Mas mabilis ito kaysa sa kable na tanso."});

;
/* data/arts9.js */
// Arts and Culture, more articles: the kundiman, weaving and textiles, manga and ceramics.
ADDART({id:"kundiman",t:"Kundiman",c:"Music",i:"🎼",w:"Kundiman",r:["opm","zarzuela","phdance","guitar","phlit"],
p:[["The Filipino love song","The kundiman is a traditional Filipino song of love, longing and devotion."],["Love and country","Many kundiman expressed love for the nation in the language of romance."],["From folk to concert hall","Composers such as Francisco Santiago and Nicanor Abelardo turned it into a classical art song."]],
l:"The kundiman is a genre of traditional Filipino song, usually slow and lyrical, about love, longing, sorrow and devotion. It developed in the Tagalog region during the Spanish colonial period, and by the late 1800s it was sung in serenades, in which young men sang beneath the windows of the women they courted. During the Philippine Revolution and the American period, many kundiman used the language of romantic love to express love for the homeland, at times when open patriotic songs were dangerous. In the early 1900s classically trained composers such as Francisco Santiago and Nicanor Abelardo created the kundiman art song. The kundiman remains a treasured part of Filipino music and identity.",
s:[["Musical features","A typical kundiman is written in triple time, like a slow waltz, and often begins in a minor key, expressing sadness or longing, before shifting to a major key in its second part, suggesting hope or resolution. The melodies are smooth and expressive, and the lyrics are usually in Tagalog, rich in poetic imagery."],["Serenades","In the 1800s and early 1900s, the harana was a custom in which a young man, accompanied by friends playing guitars, sang kundiman and other love songs outside a young woman’s house at night. If she was interested, she might open her window or invite the singers in. The harana tradition has faded but is still remembered and sometimes revived."],["Songs of the nation","During the Revolution against Spain and later under American rule, songwriters disguised patriotic feelings as love songs. The beloved woman in the song often stood for the Philippines itself. Jocelynang Baliwag, a kundiman from about 1896, is known as the kundiman of the Revolution, and Bayan Ko, written in 1928, became one of the most famous patriotic songs."],["Art songs","Filipino composers trained in Western classical music raised the kundiman to a concert form. Francisco Santiago, called the father of the kundiman art song, composed Anak Dalita in 1917. Nicanor Abelardo wrote Nasaan Ka Irog and Mutya ng Pasig, which remain favorites of singers and audiences."]],
k:[["Origin","Tagalog region, Spanish colonial period"],["Time signature","Usually triple time"],["Typical key pattern","Minor, then major"],["Father of the kundiman art song","Francisco Santiago"],["Famous kundiman","Nasaan Ka Irog, Mutya ng Pasig, Bayan Ko"]],
d:[["1800s","The kundiman develops as a Tagalog love song"],["c. 1896","Jocelynang Baliwag is sung during the Revolution"],["1917","Francisco Santiago composes Anak Dalita"],["1924","Nicanor Abelardo composes Nasaan Ka Irog"],["1926","Abelardo composes Mutya ng Pasig"],["1928","Bayan Ko is written by José Corazón de Jesús and Constancio de Guzmán"]],
s2:[["Where the name comes from","The origin of the word kundiman is uncertain. One popular explanation is that it comes from the phrase kung hindi man, meaning if it were not so, which appeared in the lyrics of early songs. Others have suggested different origins, and scholars continue to discuss it."],["Jocelynang Baliwag","Jocelynang Baliwag is a kundiman said to have been written for a young woman from Baliwag, Bulacan. During the Revolution, revolutionaries sang it as a song about the motherland, with the woman representing the Philippines. It is often called the first kundiman of the Revolution."],["Bayan Ko","Bayan Ko, with words by the poet José Corazón de Jesús and music by Constancio de Guzmán, was written in 1928 for a play. Its lyrics compare the Philippines to a bird longing to be free. It became an anthem of protest against foreign rule and, during the martial law years and the 1986 People Power Revolution, a powerful song of resistance and hope."],["Great kundiman singers","The singer Atang de la Rama was celebrated as the Queen of the Kundiman, and later singers such as Sylvia La Torre and Ruben Tagalog helped popularize kundiman on radio and records. Opera singers and choirs continue to perform kundiman in concerts at home and abroad."],["Kundiman today","Although pop, rock and hip-hop dominate the airwaves, kundiman are still taught in schools, sung at cultural events and performed by classical singers and choirs. Contemporary artists have recorded new versions with modern arrangements, and some modern Filipino love songs, such as many in the OPM tradition, carry the kundiman’s spirit of heartfelt longing."],["Kundiman around the world","Filipino communities abroad perform kundiman to keep their heritage alive, and the songs have been featured in international concerts. Choirs from the Philippines, which have won many international competitions, often include kundiman arrangements in their programs."]],
sp:["A serenade by moonlight","Picture a quiet town in the early 1900s. Late in the evening, a group of young men gathers outside a house with capiz windows. One carries a guitar, another perhaps a banduria. The suitor clears his throat and begins to sing a kundiman, telling of a love so deep that he would give his life for it. Neighbors may peek out from their windows. Inside, the young woman listens, and if she approves, she slides open her window or her family invites the singers in for refreshments. If not, the window stays closed. The harana was a gentle and public way of courting, and kundiman were its finest songs."],
g:[["Kundiman","A traditional Filipino song of love and longing"],["Harana","A serenade sung outside a woman’s home"],["Art song","A classical song for voice and piano"],["Triple time","A rhythm with three beats in each measure"]],
f:["The kundiman is a Filipino love song.","Many kundiman expressed love for the country.","Bayan Ko was written in 1928."],
fun:"A popular explanation says the word kundiman comes from kung hindi man, meaning if it were not so, though no one is certain.",
tl:"Ang kundiman ay tradisyonal na awit ng pag-ibig, pananabik at pagmamahal. Maraming kundiman ang nagpahayag ng pag-ibig sa bayan sa wika ng romansa. Ginawa itong klasikong awit ng mga kompositor tulad nina Francisco Santiago at Nicanor Abelardo."});

ADDART({id:"weaving",t:"Weaving and Textiles",c:"Crafts",i:"🧵",w:"Weaving",r:["fastfashion","silk","indrev","comp","phfolk"],
p:[["Making cloth","Weaving is the craft of interlacing threads to make cloth."],["An ancient art","People have woven fabrics for thousands of years using looms."],["Philippine traditions","The Philippines has rich weaving traditions, from piña to t’nalak and inabel."]],
l:"Weaving is the craft of making cloth by interlacing two sets of threads at right angles: the warp, which runs lengthwise and is held tight on a frame called a loom, and the weft, which passes over and under the warp threads. People have woven textiles for thousands of years from fibers such as cotton, wool, silk, linen, abaca, pineapple and bamboo. Weaving traditions express the culture, history and beliefs of peoples around the world, from Chinese silk and Indian cotton to West African kente and Andean textiles. The Philippines has many distinctive weaving traditions, including piña cloth from Aklan, t’nalak of the T’boli, inabel of the Ilocos region and Yakan weaving of Basilan.",
s:[["How weaving works","On a loom, the warp threads are stretched side by side. The weaver lifts some warp threads and lowers others, creating a gap called a shed, then passes the weft thread through it, often on a shuttle. Changing which warp threads are lifted creates different patterns, from simple plain weaves to complex designs."],["Kinds of looms","The simplest looms include the backstrap loom, in which the weaver uses their own body to keep the warp tight; it is still used by many Philippine and other Indigenous weavers. Floor looms use foot pedals to lift threads, while modern factories use power looms run by computers."],["Fibers from nature","Cotton, linen, wool and silk are among the world’s most common natural fibers. In the Philippines, weavers also use abaca, a strong fiber from a relative of the banana plant, piña from pineapple leaves, and fibers from bamboo, banana and other plants. Natural dyes come from roots, bark, leaves and mud."],["Patterns with meaning","Many weaving traditions use patterns with special meanings, representing animals, plants, ancestors, stars or social status. Some designs are worn only for ceremonies or by certain people. Techniques such as ikat, in which threads are tie-dyed before weaving, create blurred, shimmering patterns."]],
k:[["Lengthwise threads","Warp"],["Crosswise threads","Weft"],["Weaving frame","Loom"],["Philippine fibers","Abaca, piña, cotton"],["Philippine fabric law","Philippine Tropical Fabrics Law (2004)"]],
d:[["c. 5000 BC","Linen is woven in ancient Egypt"],["c. 3000 BC","Silk weaving is practiced in China"],["1733","John Kay invents the flying shuttle"],["1804","Joseph Marie Jacquard introduces a loom controlled by punched cards"],["1998","T’boli weaver Lang Dulay receives the National Living Treasures Award"],["2004","The Philippines passes the Philippine Tropical Fabrics Law"]],
s2:[["Weaving and the Industrial Revolution","Weaving played a central part in the Industrial Revolution. John Kay’s flying shuttle of 1733 doubled the speed of weaving, and power looms driven by water and steam soon filled factories in Britain. This transformed cloth from a costly handmade product into a cheap one, but also put many hand weavers out of work."],["The loom that inspired computers","In 1804 the French weaver Joseph Marie Jacquard introduced a loom controlled by chains of punched cards, each card setting which threads would be lifted to create a pattern. This allowed complex designs to be woven automatically. The idea of storing instructions on punched cards later inspired early computer pioneers such as Charles Babbage and Herman Hollerith."],["Piña","Piña cloth is woven from fibers of pineapple leaves, mainly in Aklan province. It is fine, light and almost transparent, and is often hand-embroidered. Piña is used for the barong tagalog and Filipiniana dresses worn on formal occasions. In 2023 the traditional piña handloom weaving of Aklan was added to UNESCO’s list of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity."],["T’nalak, inabel and Yakan weaving","The T’boli of South Cotabato weave t’nalak from abaca fibers tie-dyed in deep red, black and white. Weavers are said to receive their designs in dreams, and the cloth is considered sacred. The Ilocano inabel is a sturdy cotton cloth with geometric patterns, while the Yakan of Basilan weave brightly colored, intricate textiles."],["Masters of the loom","The Philippines honors master weavers with the National Living Treasures Award, or Gawad sa Manlilikha ng Bayan. Recipients include Lang Dulay, a T’boli t’nalak weaver honored in 1998, and Magdalena Gamayo, an Ilocano inabel weaver honored in 2012. They have taught younger generations to keep their traditions alive."],["Weaving today","Handwoven textiles face competition from cheap machine-made fabrics, and fewer young people learn the craft. Designers, cooperatives and government programs promote Philippine textiles in fashion and home goods. The Philippine Tropical Fabrics Law of 2004 requires that uniforms of government officials and employees use locally made fabrics, supporting weavers."]],
sp:["The dream weavers of Lake Sebu","Around Lake Sebu in South Cotabato, T’boli women have woven t’nalak for generations. The process takes weeks or even months. Abaca fibers are stripped, dried and knotted together into long threads. Following a design held in memory, the weaver ties parts of the threads tightly so they resist dye, then soaks them in natural dyes made from roots and leaves. Finally she weaves the cloth on a backstrap loom, leaning back to keep the threads taut. Many T’boli weavers say the patterns come to them in dreams, gifts from the spirit of the abaca. This is why they are known as dream weavers."],
g:[["Warp","The lengthwise threads held tight on a loom"],["Weft","The crosswise threads woven over and under the warp"],["Loom","A frame for weaving cloth"],["Ikat","A technique of dyeing threads before weaving"]],
f:["Weaving uses warp and weft threads.","Jacquard’s punched-card loom inspired computers.","Piña cloth is woven from pineapple leaf fibers."],
fun:"Punched cards used to control weaving looms in 1804 later helped inspire the first computers.",
tl:"Ang paghahabi ay sining ng paggawa ng tela sa pamamagitan ng pagsasalabid ng mga sinulid. Mayaman ang Pilipinas sa tradisyon ng paghahabi tulad ng piña ng Aklan, t’nalak ng T’boli at inabel ng Ilocos. Ginagamit sa paghahabi ang habihan o loom."});

ADDART({id:"manga",t:"Manga",c:"Comics",i:"📚",w:"Manga",r:["anime","comics","japan","childlit","kdrama"],
p:[["Japanese comics","Manga are comics and graphic novels from Japan."],["Read right to left","Manga are traditionally read from right to left."],["A global phenomenon","Manga are read by millions of people around the world."]],
l:"Manga are comics and graphic novels created in Japan, or in a style that developed there. They cover almost every subject and audience, from adventure, sports and comedy to romance, horror, history, science fiction and everyday life, for children and adults alike. Manga are usually printed in black and white, published first in weekly or monthly magazines and later collected into books called tankōbon. Traditionally they are read from right to left. Modern manga were shaped after the Second World War by artists such as Osamu Tezuka. Many popular manga have been adapted into anime, films and games, and manga have become a global phenomenon, inspiring artists in the Philippines and around the world.",
s:[["Reading manga","Japanese is traditionally written from top to bottom and right to left, so manga books open from what Western readers think of as the back, and panels are read from the top right. Many translated editions keep this layout to preserve the artist’s original design."],["Styles and audiences","Manga are often grouped by their target audience. Shōnen manga are aimed at boys and teenagers and often feature action and friendship; shōjo manga for girls often focus on relationships and emotions; seinen and josei manga are written for adult men and women. Readers of all kinds enjoy every category."],["Art style","Manga often use expressive characters with large eyes, dramatic speed lines, close-ups and creative panel layouts that give a cinematic feel. Artists use symbols, such as a sweat drop for nervousness or a nosebleed for embarrassment, that readers learn to recognize."],["From magazine to book","Most manga first appear as chapters in magazines such as Weekly Shōnen Jump, which has published many famous series. Readers vote for their favorites, and popular series are collected into volumes. Some series run for decades and hundreds of chapters."]],
k:[["Country of origin","Japan"],["Reading direction","Right to left"],["God of manga","Osamu Tezuka"],["Collected volumes","Tankōbon"],["Best-selling series","One Piece"]],
d:[["1814","Hokusai publishes sketchbooks titled Hokusai Manga"],["1952","Osamu Tezuka begins Astro Boy"],["1968","Weekly Shōnen Jump magazine is launched"],["1984","Akira Toriyama begins Dragon Ball"],["1997","Eiichiro Oda begins One Piece"],["2010s","Digital manga apps and webcomics spread worldwide"]],
s2:[["Early roots","Japanese art has a long tradition of humorous and story-telling pictures, including picture scrolls from the 12th century showing animals behaving like people. The word manga, meaning whimsical pictures, became widely known through the sketchbooks of the woodblock artist Katsushika Hokusai, published from 1814."],["Osamu Tezuka","Osamu Tezuka, often called the god of manga, created hundreds of works after the Second World War, including Astro Boy, Kimba the White Lion and Black Jack. Inspired by films and animation, he used cinematic techniques that shaped modern manga and anime. He also founded an animation studio that produced the first Japanese television anime series."],["Famous series","Some of the world’s best-known manga include Dragon Ball by Akira Toriyama, One Piece by Eiichiro Oda, Naruto by Masashi Kishimoto, Sailor Moon by Naoko Takeuchi and Doraemon by Fujiko F. Fujio. One Piece has sold hundreds of millions of copies worldwide, more than any other comic series by a single author."],["Manga in the Philippines","Anime shows such as Voltes V, Dragon Ball and Slam Dunk, broadcast on Philippine television, made Japanese stories hugely popular, and manga followed through bookstores, fan translations and digital apps. Filipino artists have drawn on manga styles in their own comics, and fans gather at conventions dressed as their favorite characters in cosplay."],["Komiks and manga","The Philippines has its own comics tradition, called komiks, which flourished from the 1940s to the 1990s with characters such as Darna, created by Mars Ravelo. Some Filipino artists have worked for American and Japanese publishers, and new generations of Filipino creators mix local stories with influences from manga and Western comics."],["Manhwa, manhua and webtoons","Korea’s comics, called manhwa, and China’s, called manhua, have their own traditions. Korean webtoons, designed to be read by scrolling vertically on phones, have become globally popular, and several have been adapted into television dramas. These forms often share audiences with manga."]],
sp:["Drawing a weekly manga","Creating a weekly manga is demanding work. A manga artist, or mangaka, may produce around 20 pages every week, often for years. The artist plans the story, sketches rough layouts called name, and discusses them with an editor before drawing the final pages. Assistants help with backgrounds, shading and special effects. Deadlines are strict because the magazine prints every week, and some famous artists have described working through the night. Readers’ votes help decide whether a new series continues or ends, so artists feel great pressure to keep their stories exciting."],
g:[["Manga","Japanese comics and graphic novels"],["Mangaka","A manga artist"],["Tankōbon","A collected volume of manga chapters"],["Webtoon","A digital comic designed for vertical scrolling"]],
f:["Manga are read from right to left.","Osamu Tezuka is called the god of manga.","One Piece is one of the best-selling comics ever."],
fun:"The word manga can be translated as whimsical or random pictures.",
tl:"Ang manga ay komiks mula sa Japan na binabasa mula kanan pakaliwa. Saklaw nito ang halos lahat ng paksa at mambabasa. Kilala ito sa buong mundo, kabilang ang Pilipinas, at maraming manga ang ginawang anime."});

ADDART({id:"ceramics",t:"Pottery and Ceramics",c:"Crafts",i:"🏺",w:"Pottery",r:["sculpture","chineseart","archaeology","silk","islamicart"],
p:[["Art from clay","Pottery and ceramics are objects made from clay and hardened by heat."],["One of the oldest crafts","People have made pottery for more than 20,000 years."],["Philippine treasures","The Manunggul Jar is a famous ancient Philippine burial jar."]],
l:"Pottery and ceramics are objects made by shaping clay or similar materials and hardening them with heat in a fire or kiln. They include everyday cooking pots, plates, cups and storage jars, as well as decorative vases, sculptures, tiles and fine porcelain. Pottery is among the oldest human crafts: fragments found in a cave in China are about 20,000 years old. Different cultures developed distinctive styles and techniques, from Chinese porcelain and Japanese tea bowls to Islamic tiles and Greek painted vases. In the Philippines, ancient burial jars such as the Manunggul Jar and the Maitum jars show remarkable artistry, and traditions such as the burnay jars of Vigan continue today.",
s:[["From clay to pot","Clay is a fine earth that becomes soft and shapeable when wet. Potters shape it by hand, by coiling ropes of clay, by pressing it into molds or by throwing it on a spinning potter’s wheel. After drying, the object is fired at high temperatures, which changes the clay permanently into a hard material."],["Kinds of ceramics","Earthenware is fired at lower temperatures and remains slightly porous. Stoneware is fired hotter, making it dense and waterproof. Porcelain, made from a fine white clay called kaolin and fired at very high temperatures, is hard, white and can be thin enough to let light through."],["Glazes and decoration","Many ceramics are coated with a glaze, a glassy layer that makes them waterproof and adds color and shine. Potters decorate their work by painting, carving, stamping, adding colored slips or using special firing methods that create unique effects."],["Kilns","A kiln is an oven for firing pottery. Early pottery was fired in open bonfires or pits, but kilns allowed higher, more controlled temperatures. Chinese potters built long dragon kilns climbing up hillsides, which could fire thousands of pieces at once."]],
k:[["Oldest known pottery","About 20,000 years old, Xianrendong Cave, China"],["Main types","Earthenware, stoneware, porcelain"],["Porcelain clay","Kaolin"],["Philippine National Treasure","Manunggul Jar"],["Vigan tradition","Burnay jars"]],
d:[["c. 18,000 BC","Pottery is made at Xianrendong Cave in China"],["c. 14,000 BC","Jōmon pottery is made in Japan"],["c. 3500 BC","The potter’s wheel is used in Mesopotamia"],["c. 890–710 BC","The Manunggul Jar is made in Palawan"],["1300s","Blue-and-white porcelain is made in Jingdezhen, China"],["1964","The Manunggul Jar is discovered in Tabon Caves"]],
s2:[["Chinese porcelain","Chinese potters perfected porcelain over many centuries, and the city of Jingdezhen became famous for it. Blue-and-white porcelain, decorated with cobalt blue, was traded across Asia, the Middle East and Europe. Porcelain was so closely linked with China that in English it came to be called china."],["The Manunggul Jar","The Manunggul Jar, discovered in 1964 in a cave in the Tabon Caves complex in Palawan, is a burial jar about 2,800 years old. Its lid is topped by a boat carrying two figures, thought to represent a soul being ferried to the afterlife. It is a National Treasure of the Philippines and appears on the old thousand-peso banknote."],["The Maitum jars","In 1991 archaeologists found clay burial jars in a cave in Maitum, Sarangani, shaped like human figures with faces showing different expressions, such as joy, sadness and calm. They are about 2,000 years old and are among the most remarkable artworks of early Philippine history."],["Trade ceramics","For centuries before and during Spanish rule, Chinese, Vietnamese and Thai ceramics were traded in the Philippine islands. Archaeologists have found large amounts of these ceramics in burial sites and shipwrecks, such as the San Diego, a Spanish galleon that sank off Fortune Island, Batangas, in 1600."],["Burnay of Vigan","In Vigan, Ilocos Sur, potters make large earthenware jars called burnay, a tradition said to have been introduced by Chinese settlers. The jars are used to ferment and store basi, a sugarcane wine, and bagoong, fermented fish paste. Visitors can watch potters shaping burnay on wheels turned by a carabao or by foot."],["Ceramics today","Ceramics are used in many modern technologies, including tiles, toilets, spark plugs, electronics, heat shields on spacecraft and artificial joints. At the same time, studio potters around the world, including in the Philippines, create handmade pottery as art, and pottery classes have become popular hobbies."]],
sp:["The boat of souls","Sitting on top of the Manunggul Jar is one of the most moving sculptures of ancient Southeast Asia. A small boat carries two figures, one steering with a paddle and the other sitting with arms crossed over the chest, a position associated with burial. The boat’s prow is carved with a face. Archaeologists think the scene shows a soul traveling to the afterlife, guided by a ferryman, reflecting a belief that the spirits of the dead journeyed across water. The swirling red-painted designs on the jar’s body add to its beauty. Nearly three thousand years old, the jar connects modern Filipinos with the beliefs of their ancestors."],
g:[["Ceramics","Objects made from clay and hardened by heat"],["Kiln","An oven used to fire pottery"],["Glaze","A glassy coating on pottery"],["Porcelain","A hard, white, fine ceramic"]],
f:["Pottery is over 20,000 years old.","Porcelain is called china in English.","The Manunggul Jar is a Philippine National Treasure."],
fun:"In English, fine porcelain is called china because it was first made in China.",
tl:"Ang palayok at seramika ay mga bagay na yari sa luwad na pinatigas sa apoy. Isa ito sa pinakamatandang sining ng tao. Ang Manunggul Jar ng Palawan ay isang tanyag na sinaunang banga at Pambansang Kayamanan ng Pilipinas."});

;
