// Planet Earth, more articles: landslides, sinkholes, glaciers and plateaus.
ADDART({id:"landslides",t:"Landslides",c:"Earth Science",i:"⛰️",w:"Landslide",r:["quake","phtyph","deforestation","soil","disasterprep"],
p:[["Sliding ground","A landslide is the movement of rock, soil and debris down a slope."],["Rain and earthquakes","Heavy rain and earthquakes are the most common triggers."],["A deadly hazard","Landslides kill thousands of people worldwide every year, including in the Philippines."]],
l:"A landslide is the movement of a mass of rock, earth or debris down a slope under the pull of gravity. Landslides range from small falls of loose stones to huge flows that bury whole villages. They happen when the forces pulling material downhill become greater than the strength holding it in place, often after heavy rain soaks the ground, when earthquakes shake slopes, or when people cut into hillsides or remove trees. Landslides are a major hazard in mountainous, rainy and earthquake-prone countries such as the Philippines, where typhoons and monsoon rains frequently trigger them. Warning systems, careful land use, reforestation and evacuation plans can save lives.",
s:[["Why slopes fail","Every slope is held in place by friction and the strength of its soil and rock. Water adds weight and fills the spaces between soil particles, reducing friction and making the ground slippery. Earthquakes shake material loose, and erosion by rivers or waves can remove the support at the base of a slope."],["Kinds of landslides","Rockfalls are rocks dropping from cliffs. Slides occur when a mass of soil or rock moves along a surface. Mudflows and debris flows are fast-moving rivers of mud, rocks and water, often racing down valleys after heavy rain. Creep is very slow downhill movement, shown by tilted trees, fences and poles."],["Human causes","People can make landslides more likely by cutting roads into hillsides, building on steep slopes, mining, quarrying and clearing forests. Tree roots help bind soil, so deforested slopes are often more unstable. Leaking water pipes and poor drainage can also weaken slopes."],["Warning signs","Signs that a landslide may happen include new cracks in the ground or walls, tilting trees and poles, doors and windows that suddenly stick, bulging ground at the base of a slope, muddy water in streams and rumbling sounds. People who notice such signs, especially during heavy rain, should move to safety."]],
k:[["Main force","Gravity"],["Common triggers","Heavy rain, earthquakes"],["Fast, wet landslide","Debris flow or mudflow"],["Slowest movement","Creep"],["Philippine hazard maps","Mines and Geosciences Bureau"]],
d:[["1920","An earthquake triggers huge landslides in Haiyuan, China"],["1970","An earthquake-triggered avalanche buries the town of Yungay in Peru"],["1985","Mudflows from the Nevado del Ruiz volcano destroy Armero, Colombia"],["2006","A landslide buries the village of Guinsaugon in Southern Leyte"],["2018","Typhoon Mangkhut triggers deadly landslides in Itogon, Benguet"],["2024","Severe storm Kristine causes deadly landslides in Batangas and other provinces"]],
s2:[["The Guinsaugon tragedy","On 17 February 2006, after days of heavy rain, a mountainside collapsed above the village of Guinsaugon in Saint Bernard, Southern Leyte. Within minutes, mud and rock buried the village, including its elementary school, which was in session. More than 1,000 people were killed or went missing. The disaster led to greater attention to landslide hazard mapping in the Philippines."],["Typhoons and landslides","In the Philippines, typhoons and monsoon rains bring huge amounts of water in a short time, saturating mountain slopes. In 2018 Typhoon Mangkhut, known locally as Ompong, triggered a landslide that buried miners’ bunkhouses in Itogon, Benguet. In 2024 severe tropical storm Kristine caused landslides that killed many people in Talisay, Batangas, and other areas."],["Volcanic mudflows","Volcanic eruptions leave loose ash and rock on mountain slopes. When heavy rain falls, this material can form lahars, fast-flowing volcanic mudflows. After Mount Pinatubo erupted in 1991, lahars buried towns in Pampanga and Tarlac for years. Lahars from Mayon Volcano, triggered by typhoon rains in 2006, also caused many deaths."],["Hazard maps and warnings","The Mines and Geosciences Bureau and other agencies produce maps showing areas at risk of landslides, floods and other hazards. Local governments use them to plan where people can safely build and when to evacuate. Rain gauges, sensors and community monitoring help give early warnings."],["Making slopes safer","Engineers stabilize slopes with retaining walls, drainage channels, nets and anchors. Planting trees, grasses and bamboo helps bind soil. Avoiding construction on the steepest and most dangerous slopes, and relocating families from high-risk areas, are among the most effective ways to reduce deaths."],["Landslides around the world","Landslides occur on every continent. Earthquakes in China, Peru and Nepal have triggered huge slides, and volcanic mudflows have buried towns in Colombia. Scientists warn that heavier rainfall linked to climate change and the spread of settlements onto steep slopes may increase landslide risks in many places."]],
sp:["Listening to the mountain","In many upland communities in the Philippines, villagers have learned to watch the mountains during heavy rain. Some local governments train residents as landslide watchers, who check cracks in the ground, measure rainfall with simple gauges and listen for unusual sounds. When danger signs appear, they ring bells or use radios to warn neighbors to evacuate to safer places such as schools or chapels on stable ground. Such community-based warning systems, combined with hazard maps and government alerts, have helped save lives during storms."],
g:[["Landslide","The movement of rock, soil or debris down a slope"],["Debris flow","A fast-moving mix of mud, rocks and water"],["Lahar","A volcanic mudflow"],["Creep","Very slow downhill movement of soil"]],
f:["Heavy rain is a common landslide trigger.","Tree roots help hold soil in place.","Lahars are volcanic mudflows."],
fun:"Some landslides move so slowly that they shift only a few centimeters a year, slowly tilting trees and fences.",
tl:"Ang pagguho ng lupa ay ang pagbagsak ng bato, lupa at iba pang bagay pababa ng dalisdis. Karaniwan itong dulot ng malakas na ulan at lindol. Isa itong malaking panganib sa Pilipinas, lalo na tuwing may bagyo."});

ADDART({id:"sinkholes",t:"Sinkholes",c:"Earth Science",i:"🕳️",w:"Sinkhole",r:["caves","rocks","water","quake","geysers"],
p:[["Holes in the ground","A sinkhole is a hole or hollow in the ground caused by the collapse of the surface."],["Limestone lands","Most sinkholes form where water slowly dissolves rock such as limestone."],["Sudden or slow","Some form gradually, while others collapse suddenly."]],
l:"A sinkhole is a depression or hole in the ground formed when the surface layer collapses or sinks into an underground space. Most sinkholes occur in areas of karst, landscapes built on rocks such as limestone, dolomite or gypsum that slowly dissolve in slightly acidic water. Over thousands of years, water seeping underground creates caves and hollows; when their roofs become too thin, the ground above can sag or suddenly collapse. Sinkholes range from small dips to enormous pits hundreds of meters deep. Some are filled with water, forming beautiful pools. Human activities, such as pumping groundwater or leaking pipes, can also cause sinkholes, sometimes swallowing roads, cars and buildings.",
s:[["How sinkholes form","Rainwater absorbs carbon dioxide from the air and soil, becoming a weak acid. As it seeps through cracks in limestone, it slowly dissolves the rock, widening cracks into channels and caves. When the rock or soil above an empty space can no longer support its own weight, it collapses, creating a sinkhole."],["Kinds of sinkholes","Solution sinkholes form slowly as rock at the surface dissolves, creating bowl-shaped hollows. Cover-subsidence sinkholes form when sand gradually trickles into openings below. Cover-collapse sinkholes happen suddenly when a hidden cavity’s roof gives way, and they are the most dangerous."],["Karst landscapes","Karst areas often have caves, underground rivers, springs and few surface streams, because water drains underground. Famous karst landscapes include the towering limestone hills of Guilin in China, Halong Bay in Vietnam and the Chocolate Hills of Bohol in the Philippines."],["Human-caused sinkholes","Pumping large amounts of groundwater, mining, construction and broken water or sewer pipes can wash away soil and create sinkholes, even in places without limestone. Heavy rains from storms can trigger collapses in areas already weakened."]],
k:[["Common rock","Limestone"],["Landscape type","Karst"],["Cause","Dissolving rock or underground erosion"],["Deepest known sinkhole","Xiaozhai Tiankeng, China, about 660 m"],["Famous water-filled sinkhole","Great Blue Hole, Belize"]],
d:[["1971","Jacques Cousteau explores the Great Blue Hole of Belize"],["1981","A large sinkhole swallows homes and cars in Winter Park, Florida"],["1994","Explorers survey the giant Xiaozhai Tiankeng in China"],["2010","A sinkhole opens in Guatemala City after a tropical storm"],["2013","The Bohol earthquake opens many new sinkholes in the province"],["2022","Scientists explore a newly found giant sinkhole with a forest in Guangxi, China"]],
s2:[["Sinkholes in the Philippines","Large parts of the Philippines, including Bohol, Palawan, Cebu and Samar, are built on limestone. When a magnitude 7.2 earthquake struck Bohol in October 2013, it opened many new sinkholes, damaged roads and farmland and changed the landscape. Geologists mapped the sinkholes to help communities avoid dangerous areas."],["Underground rivers","Water that drains into karst can form long underground rivers. The Puerto Princesa Subterranean River in Palawan flows for about 8 kilometers through a limestone cave before reaching the sea. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and one of the most famous karst features in Asia."],["Giant sinkholes","In southern China, huge sinkholes called tiankeng, meaning heavenly pits, are so large that forests grow on their floors, with plants and animals isolated from the world above. The Xiaozhai Tiankeng is about 660 meters deep. Explorers continue to discover new giant sinkholes in the region."],["Blue holes and cenotes","Some sinkholes near the coast are filled with water. Blue holes, such as the Great Blue Hole off Belize, appear as dark blue circles in the sea. In Mexico’s Yucatán Peninsula, water-filled sinkholes called cenotes were sacred to the ancient Maya and are now popular for swimming and diving."],["Dangers and detection","Sudden sinkholes can damage roads, houses and pipelines and, rarely, injure people. Geologists use ground-penetrating radar, surveys and satellite measurements to find areas at risk. Signs include cracks in walls and floors, sagging ground, and pools of water forming where none existed before."],["The Dead Sea sinkholes","Around the shores of the Dead Sea, between Israel, Jordan and the West Bank, thousands of sinkholes have appeared since the 1980s. As the sea’s level drops because water is diverted from the Jordan River, fresh groundwater dissolves underground salt layers, causing the ground to collapse. The sinkholes have damaged roads, farms and resorts."]],
sp:["The forest at the bottom of the pit","In 2022 a team of Chinese cave explorers lowered themselves by rope into a newly found sinkhole in Guangxi, southern China. About 192 meters below the surface, they found a hidden forest of ancient trees, some reaching about 40 meters tall, growing toward the circle of sky far above. Thick undergrowth covered the floor. Scientists believe such isolated forests may hold plant and animal species that have never been described. The region has dozens of these giant sinkholes, and explorers continue to discover new ones, each a small lost world."],
g:[["Sinkhole","A hole formed when the ground collapses into an underground space"],["Karst","A landscape formed by dissolving rocks such as limestone"],["Limestone","A rock made mostly of calcium carbonate"],["Cenote","A natural water-filled sinkhole in Mexico"]],
f:["Most sinkholes form in limestone areas.","China has the deepest known sinkhole.","The Bohol earthquake of 2013 opened new sinkholes."],
fun:"Some giant sinkholes in China are so large that whole forests grow on their floors.",
tl:"Ang sinkhole ay butas sa lupa na nabubuo kapag gumuho ang ibabaw papunta sa espasyo sa ilalim. Karaniwan itong nangyayari sa mga lugar na may batong apog o limestone. Nagbukas ng maraming sinkhole ang lindol sa Bohol noong 2013."});

ADDART({id:"glaciers",t:"Glaciers",c:"Earth Science",i:"🧊",w:"Glacier",r:["iceage","climchange","antarc","alps","himal"],
p:[["Rivers of ice","Glaciers are huge masses of ice that slowly flow over land."],["Shaping the land","Glaciers carve valleys, lakes and mountain peaks."],["Melting fast","Most glaciers are shrinking as the climate warms."]],
l:"A glacier is a large, long-lasting mass of ice that forms on land from compacted snow and moves slowly under its own weight. Glaciers form where more snow falls in winter than melts in summer, building up layers that are squeezed into dense ice over many years. They are found in high mountains on every continent except Australia, and in the vast ice sheets covering Antarctica and Greenland. Glaciers hold about two-thirds of the world’s fresh water. As they move, they carve deep valleys, sharpen mountain peaks and leave behind lakes and piles of rock. Today most glaciers are shrinking rapidly because of rising temperatures, affecting water supplies and sea levels.",
s:[["How glaciers form","In cold places, snow that does not melt piles up year after year. The weight of new snow presses the older snow into grainy ice called firn, and over decades into solid glacier ice. Air is squeezed out, making old glacier ice look blue, because dense ice absorbs red light and lets blue light pass."],["How glaciers move","Glaciers flow like very thick, slow rivers. Ice deep inside deforms under pressure, and meltwater at the base can help the glacier slide over the rock below. Most glaciers move a few centimeters to a few meters a day, but some fast glaciers in Greenland can move more than 30 meters a day."],["Kinds of glaciers","Mountain or valley glaciers flow down valleys from high peaks. Ice sheets are enormous glaciers covering huge areas of land, found today only in Antarctica and Greenland. Ice caps are smaller dome-shaped glaciers, and tidewater glaciers flow into the sea, where chunks break off as icebergs."],["Sculpting the land","As glaciers move, they scrape and pluck rock, carving U-shaped valleys, sharp ridges and pointed peaks such as the Matterhorn in the Alps. When glaciers melt, they leave behind piles of rock called moraines and basins that fill with water to form lakes. Norway’s fjords are valleys carved by glaciers and flooded by the sea."]],
k:[["Share of world’s fresh water","About two-thirds"],["Ice sheets today","Antarctica and Greenland"],["Old snow turning to ice","Firn"],["Rock piles left behind","Moraines"],["UN year","2025, International Year of Glaciers’ Preservation"]],
d:[["c. 20,000 years ago","Ice sheets reach their greatest extent in the last ice age"],["1837","Louis Agassiz proposes that great ice sheets once covered Europe"],["1991","Ötzi the Iceman is found in a melting Alpine glacier"],["2019","Iceland holds a funeral for the lost Okjökull glacier"],["2024","Venezuela is reported to have lost its last glacier"],["2025","A glacier collapse buries most of the Swiss village of Blatten"]],
s2:[["Glaciers and water supplies","In dry seasons, meltwater from glaciers feeds rivers that supply drinking water, farms and hydroelectric dams. Glaciers of the Himalayas and Tibetan Plateau help feed rivers such as the Ganges, Indus and Yangtze, on which more than a billion people depend. Shrinking glaciers threaten these supplies in the long term."],["Melting glaciers","Scientists measure glaciers using satellites, photographs and field visits. Most glaciers around the world have been losing ice for decades, and the rate has increased. Melting glaciers and ice sheets are a major cause of sea level rise. The United Nations declared 2025 the International Year of Glaciers’ Preservation to draw attention to their loss."],["Lost glaciers","Some small glaciers have already disappeared. In 2019 people in Iceland held a memorial ceremony for Okjökull, which had melted so much that it was no longer a glacier, and placed a plaque addressed to the future. Venezuela was reported in 2024 to have lost its last glacier, and tropical glaciers on Mount Kilimanjaro and in Indonesia’s Papua are also shrinking quickly."],["Glacier hazards","Glaciers can cause disasters. Lakes dammed by glacial ice or rock may burst, sending floods down valleys. Chunks of glacier can break off and fall. In May 2025, part of the Birch Glacier in Switzerland collapsed, burying most of the village of Blatten, which had been evacuated days earlier after scientists noticed warning signs."],["Secrets in the ice","Glaciers preserve objects and information from the past. In 1991 hikers found Ötzi, a man who died in the Alps about 5,300 years ago, preserved in glacier ice with his clothes and tools. Ice cores drilled from Antarctica and Greenland contain air bubbles that reveal Earth’s climate over hundreds of thousands of years."],["Glaciers near the equator","Glaciers can exist even in the tropics on very high mountains, where the air is cold. Mount Kilimanjaro in Tanzania, the Andes of Peru and Ecuador and the peaks of Puncak Jaya in Indonesian Papua have had tropical glaciers. The Philippines has no glaciers, because its mountains are not high enough, but the country is affected by the sea level rise that melting ice causes."]],
sp:["A letter to the future","In August 2019, a group of scientists, officials and local people climbed the volcano Ok in western Iceland to mark the loss of Okjökull, once a glacier covering about 16 square kilometers. By 2014 it had shrunk so much that it no longer moved and was declared dead. They installed a bronze plaque titled A letter to the future, written by the Icelandic author Andri Snær Magnason. It says that Ok was the first Icelandic glacier to lose its status as a glacier and that in the next 200 years all of the country’s glaciers are expected to follow, ending with the level of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere at the time."],
g:[["Glacier","A large mass of ice that slowly flows over land"],["Ice sheet","A huge glacier covering a large area of land"],["Moraine","A pile of rock and soil left by a glacier"],["Fjord","A long, narrow sea inlet carved by a glacier"]],
f:["Glaciers hold about two-thirds of the world’s fresh water.","Old glacier ice looks blue.","Most glaciers are shrinking."],
fun:"Glacier ice can look bright blue because dense ice absorbs red light and lets blue light pass through.",
tl:"Ang glacier ay napakalaking masa ng yelo na mabagal na dumadaloy sa lupa. Inuukit nito ang mga lambak at bundok. Karamihan sa mga glacier ay lumiliit habang umiinit ang klima."});

ADDART({id:"plateaus",t:"Plateaus",c:"Geography",i:"🏞️",w:"Plateau",r:["himal","andes","tect","canyon","apo"],
p:[["High flat lands","A plateau is a large, high area of fairly flat land."],["Roof of the World","The Tibetan Plateau is the highest and largest plateau on Earth."],["Philippine plateaus","Bukidnon and Lanao in Mindanao have high plateaus."]],
l:"A plateau is a large area of high, relatively flat land that rises sharply above the surrounding area on at least one side. Plateaus are sometimes called tablelands. They form in several ways: when forces inside the Earth push up large blocks of land, when layers of lava spread out and harden, or when erosion wears away surrounding land. Plateaus cover a large part of Earth’s land surface and include some of its most dramatic landscapes, such as the Tibetan Plateau, called the Roof of the World, the Colorado Plateau with the Grand Canyon, and the Deccan Plateau of India. In the Philippines, the Bukidnon and Lanao plateaus of Mindanao are important farming regions.",
s:[["How plateaus form","Some plateaus are raised when tectonic plates collide, pushing up broad areas of land, as happened with the Tibetan Plateau. Volcanic plateaus form when huge amounts of runny lava pour out and spread over wide areas, as on the Deccan Plateau. Others are left standing when rivers and weather erode lower land around them."],["The Tibetan Plateau","The Tibetan Plateau, in western China and nearby regions, has an average height of about 4,500 meters and covers about 2.5 million square kilometers. It was raised by the collision of the Indian and Eurasian plates. Its glaciers and snowfields feed many of Asia’s great rivers, including the Yangtze, Yellow, Mekong, Indus and Brahmaputra."],["The Colorado Plateau","The Colorado Plateau in the southwestern United States is famous for its colorful layered rocks, cut into deep canyons by rivers. The Colorado River carved the Grand Canyon through it, revealing nearly two billion years of Earth’s history in its walls."],["Life on plateaus","High plateaus have thin air, strong sunlight and large temperature changes between day and night. People who have lived for generations on high plateaus, such as Tibetans and some peoples of the Andes and Ethiopia, have bodies adapted to the low oxygen. Plateaus are used for grazing animals, farming and, in some places, mining."]],
k:[["Also called","Tableland"],["Highest and largest","Tibetan Plateau"],["Average height of Tibetan Plateau","About 4,500 m"],["Volcanic plateau","Deccan Plateau, India"],["Philippine plateaus","Bukidnon and Lanao, Mindanao"]],
d:[["c. 66 million years ago","Vast lava flows build the Deccan Plateau"],["c. 50 million years ago","India begins colliding with Asia, raising the Tibetan Plateau"],["1869","John Wesley Powell leads an expedition through the Grand Canyon"],["1919","Grand Canyon National Park is established"],["2000","The Mount Kitanglad Range in Bukidnon is protected by law as a natural park"],["2006","A railway across the Tibetan Plateau to Lhasa opens"]],
s2:[["Plateaus of the Philippines","In northern Mindanao, the Bukidnon Plateau rises between mountain ranges, with a cool climate suitable for growing pineapples, corn, sugarcane, coffee and vegetables, and for raising cattle. To the west, the Lanao Plateau holds Lake Lanao, the country’s second-largest lake. Smaller upland plateaus and tablelands are found in Luzon and other islands."],["Bukidnon’s farms","Large pineapple and banana plantations, as well as small farms, cover parts of the Bukidnon Plateau. Its cooler temperatures and fertile volcanic soils make it one of the country’s food baskets. Indigenous peoples of Bukidnon, such as the Higaonon, Talaandig and Bukidnon, have lived in the region’s highlands and mountains for generations."],["Volcanic plateaus","Lava plateaus form when lava erupts from long cracks rather than cone-shaped volcanoes, spreading in layers over huge areas. The Deccan Traps in India formed about 66 million years ago, around the time the dinosaurs died out, and some scientists think the eruptions contributed to that mass extinction. The Columbia Plateau in the United States formed in a similar way."],["Tepuis","In Venezuela, Guyana and Brazil, flat-topped mountains called tepuis rise steeply from the rainforest. They are remnants of an ancient plateau worn away over millions of years. Their isolated tops have unique plants and animals and inspired Arthur Conan Doyle’s adventure novel The Lost World. Angel Falls, the world’s highest waterfall, plunges from a tepui."],["The Altiplano","The Altiplano, meaning high plain in Spanish, lies in the Andes of Peru, Bolivia, Chile and Argentina at an average height of over 3,500 meters. It holds Lake Titicaca and the Salar de Uyuni, the world’s largest salt flat. Indigenous peoples such as the Aymara and Quechua have farmed and herded llamas and alpacas there for thousands of years."],["Plateaus and climate","Large plateaus influence weather over wide regions. The Tibetan Plateau heats up in summer, helping to drive the South Asian monsoon, which brings vital rain to India and neighboring countries. Changes on the plateau, such as melting glaciers and thawing permafrost, are closely studied by climate scientists."]],
sp:["The railway to the roof of the world","In 2006 China opened the Qinghai–Tibet Railway, linking the city of Xining to Lhasa, the capital of Tibet. More than 960 kilometers of the line lie above 4,000 meters, and its highest point, the Tanggula Pass, is about 5,070 meters above sea level, making it the highest railway in the world. Engineers had to build over frozen ground called permafrost, using cooling pipes to keep it from thawing and shifting the tracks. Passenger carriages supply extra oxygen to help travelers cope with the thin air. The railway has boosted tourism and trade, while critics have raised concerns about its effects on Tibetan culture and the fragile environment."],
g:[["Plateau","A large area of high, fairly flat land"],["Tableland","Another name for a plateau"],["Tepui","A flat-topped mountain in South America"],["Permafrost","Ground that stays frozen all year"]],
f:["The Tibetan Plateau is called the Roof of the World.","The Grand Canyon cuts through the Colorado Plateau.","Lake Lanao lies on the Lanao Plateau."],
fun:"Angel Falls, the world’s highest waterfall, plunges from the top of a flat-topped mountain called a tepui.",
tl:"Ang talampas ay malawak at mataas na lupang medyo patag. Ang Tibetan Plateau ang pinakamataas at pinakamalaki sa mundo. Sa Pilipinas, kilala ang mga talampas ng Bukidnon at Lanao sa Mindanao."});

;
/* data/science9.js */
// Science and Numbers, more articles: quarks, polymers and germ theory.
ADDART({id:"quarks",t:"Quarks",c:"Physics",i:"🔴",w:"Quark",r:["particles","standardmodel","atom","higgs","accelerators"],
p:[["Inside protons","Quarks are tiny particles that make up protons and neutrons."],["Six kinds","There are six types, or flavors, of quark."],["Never alone","Quarks are always found bound together, never on their own."]],
l:"Quarks are elementary particles, among the smallest known building blocks of matter. They combine to form protons and neutrons, which make up the nuclei of atoms. A proton contains two up quarks and one down quark, while a neutron contains one up quark and two down quarks. There are six types, or flavors, of quark: up, down, strange, charm, bottom and top. Quarks are held together by the strong nuclear force, carried by particles called gluons, and are never found alone in nature. The idea of quarks was proposed in 1964 by Murray Gell-Mann and George Zweig, and experiments soon confirmed that protons have smaller particles inside. Quarks are a central part of the Standard Model of particle physics.",
s:[["Building protons and neutrons","Every atom has a nucleus made of protons and neutrons, and each of these is made of three quarks. Up quarks carry an electric charge of plus two-thirds and down quarks minus one-third, so two ups and a down give the proton a charge of plus one, while one up and two downs give the neutron zero charge."],["Six flavors","Up and down quarks are the lightest and make up ordinary matter. The other four, strange, charm, bottom and top, are heavier and unstable, quickly decaying into lighter particles. They are created in high-energy collisions in particle accelerators and in cosmic rays. The top quark, discovered in 1995, is the heaviest known elementary particle."],["Color charge and gluons","Quarks carry a property called color charge, which has nothing to do with ordinary color but comes in three types named red, green and blue. The strong force between colored quarks is carried by gluons, which act like a powerful glue. The theory describing this is called quantum chromodynamics."],["Confinement","The strong force behaves strangely: the farther apart two quarks are pulled, the stronger the force between them becomes. If enough energy is used to separate them, it creates new quarks, which pair up with the old ones. This is why quarks are always confined inside particles and have never been seen alone."]],
k:[["Proposed","1964"],["Proposed by","Murray Gell-Mann and George Zweig"],["Flavors","Up, down, strange, charm, bottom, top"],["Force carriers","Gluons"],["Heaviest quark","Top, discovered 1995"]],
d:[["1964","Gell-Mann and Zweig propose quarks"],["1968","Experiments at Stanford reveal point-like particles inside protons"],["1969","Murray Gell-Mann receives the Nobel Prize in Physics"],["1974","The charm quark is discovered"],["1977","The bottom quark is discovered"],["1995","The top quark is discovered at Fermilab"]],
s2:[["A name from a novel","Murray Gell-Mann took the word quark from a line in James Joyce’s novel Finnegans Wake: Three quarks for Muster Mark! He liked the sound of the word and the fact that it involved the number three, matching the three quarks inside a proton. George Zweig, who developed the idea independently, called them aces, but quark was the name that stuck."],["The particle zoo","In the 1950s and 1960s, particle accelerators produced dozens of new particles, a confusing collection physicists called the particle zoo. Gell-Mann and others noticed patterns among them, like a periodic table for particles. The quark model explained these patterns by showing that many particles were combinations of a few kinds of quarks."],["Seeing inside the proton","In 1968 experiments at the Stanford Linear Accelerator Center fired electrons at protons. Some electrons bounced back sharply, as if hitting small, hard objects inside, much as Rutherford had discovered the atomic nucleus decades earlier. These results provided strong evidence for quarks, and the leaders of the experiment received the 1990 Nobel Prize in Physics."],["Where mass comes from","Surprisingly, the quarks in a proton account for only about one percent of its mass. Most of the proton’s mass comes from the energy of the quarks’ motion and the gluons binding them, following Einstein’s famous equation linking energy and mass. In this sense, most of the mass of everyday objects comes from energy trapped inside protons and neutrons."],["Quark-gluon plasma","Just after the Big Bang, the universe was so hot that quarks and gluons moved freely in a soup called quark-gluon plasma. Scientists have recreated tiny drops of this plasma by smashing heavy atoms together at enormous energies at laboratories in the United States and at CERN. They found it behaves like an almost perfect liquid."],["Exotic combinations","Most particles made of quarks contain either three quarks or a quark and an antiquark. In the 2000s and 2010s, experiments found exotic particles with four or five quarks, called tetraquarks and pentaquarks. These discoveries help physicists test their understanding of the strong force."]],
sp:["Three quarks for Muster Mark","Physicists are not always serious when naming things. Murray Gell-Mann, a brilliant physicist with a love of languages, first imagined the sound of the word kwork for his new particles before finding the word quark in James Joyce’s playful, puzzling novel Finnegans Wake, in which a flock of seagulls cries three quarks for Muster Mark. Later physicists continued the playful tradition, naming quark flavors strange and charm and calling their color charges red, green and blue. For a time, the bottom and top quarks were also nicknamed beauty and truth."],
g:[["Quark","An elementary particle that makes up protons and neutrons"],["Flavor","A type of quark, such as up or down"],["Gluon","A particle that carries the strong force between quarks"],["Confinement","The fact that quarks are never found alone"]],
f:["A proton contains three quarks.","There are six flavors of quark.","The word quark comes from Finnegans Wake."],
fun:"The quarks in a proton make up only about one percent of its mass; the rest comes from the energy that binds them.",
tl:"Ang quark ay napakaliit na partikulong bumubuo sa proton at neutron. Anim ang uri nito: up, down, strange, charm, bottom at top. Hindi ito kailanman natatagpuang mag-isa sa kalikasan."});

ADDART({id:"polymers",t:"Polymers",c:"Chemistry",i:"🧶",w:"Polymer",r:["plastics","molecules","dna","carbonchem","reactions"],
p:[["Long chains","Polymers are huge molecules made of many small units linked in long chains."],["Natural and synthetic","They include natural materials such as silk and rubber, and synthetic ones such as nylon and plastics."],["Everywhere","Polymers are found in our bodies, our clothes, our homes and our technology."]],
l:"A polymer is a very large molecule made of many smaller repeating units, called monomers, joined together in long chains or networks, like beads on a necklace. Polymers occur naturally in living things: proteins, DNA, starch, cellulose, silk, wool and natural rubber are all polymers. Since the early 1900s chemists have also made synthetic polymers, including plastics such as polyethylene and PVC, fibers such as nylon and polyester, and materials such as synthetic rubber, Kevlar and silicone. Polymers can be soft or hard, stretchy or rigid, and are used in clothing, packaging, medicine, construction, electronics and vehicles. Their durability is useful but also causes problems, as plastic waste builds up in the environment.",
s:[["Monomers and chains","Polymers are made when small molecules, the monomers, link together in a chemical reaction called polymerization. Ethylene molecules join to form polyethylene, for example. A single polymer molecule may contain thousands or even millions of monomers. The length and arrangement of the chains help determine how a material behaves."],["Natural polymers","Life depends on polymers. Proteins are chains of amino acids, DNA is a chain of nucleotides carrying genetic information, and starch and cellulose are chains of sugar molecules. Cellulose makes up the cell walls of plants and is the main material of wood, paper and cotton. Abaca fiber from the Philippines is rich in cellulose."],["Plastics","Plastics are synthetic polymers that can be molded into shapes. Thermoplastics, such as polyethylene and PET used in bottles, soften when heated and can be reshaped and often recycled. Thermosets, such as epoxy resins, set permanently when heated and cannot be melted again."],["Fibers and rubbers","Synthetic fibers such as nylon and polyester are used in clothing, ropes and fishing nets. Elastomers, or rubbers, have chains that can stretch and spring back. Natural rubber comes from the sap of rubber trees, grown in parts of Mindanao, while synthetic rubbers are made from petroleum."]],
k:[["Building blocks","Monomers"],["Chain-forming reaction","Polymerization"],["Natural examples","Proteins, DNA, cellulose, silk, rubber"],["Synthetic examples","Nylon, polyethylene, PVC, polyester"],["Polymer pioneer","Hermann Staudinger, Nobel Prize 1953"]],
d:[["1839","Charles Goodyear discovers how to make rubber stronger by heating it with sulfur"],["1907","Leo Baekeland invents Bakelite, the first fully synthetic plastic"],["1920","Hermann Staudinger proposes that polymers are long chain molecules"],["1935","Wallace Carothers’s team at DuPont makes nylon"],["1965","Stephanie Kwolek invents Kevlar"],["2022","United Nations members agree to negotiate a treaty on plastic pollution"]],
s2:[["Staudinger’s big idea","In the early 1900s, many chemists believed that substances such as rubber and cellulose were clusters of small molecules held loosely together. In 1920 the German chemist Hermann Staudinger argued that they were actually giant molecules made of long chains held by strong chemical bonds. His idea was at first ridiculed but was later proved correct, and he received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1953."],["Nylon","In 1935 a team led by the American chemist Wallace Carothers at the DuPont company created nylon, a strong, silky synthetic fiber. Nylon stockings went on sale in 1939 and 1940 and were hugely popular. During the Second World War nylon was used for parachutes, ropes and tents. It is still used in clothing, toothbrushes and many other products."],["Kevlar","In 1965 the chemist Stephanie Kwolek, working at DuPont, discovered a polymer solution that could be spun into fibers five times stronger than steel by weight. Called Kevlar, it is used in bulletproof vests, helmets, ropes, tires and sports equipment. Kwolek became one of the few women inducted into the National Inventors Hall of Fame in the United States."],["Polymers in medicine","Polymers are used in contact lenses, artificial joints, heart valves, dissolving stitches, medicine capsules and blood bags. Some special polymers release medicines slowly inside the body, while others form scaffolds on which new tissue can grow."],["The plastic problem","Because most synthetic polymers do not break down easily, plastic waste can last for hundreds of years. Much of it ends up in landfills, rivers and oceans, harming wildlife and breaking into tiny pieces called microplastics. The Philippines is among the countries where large amounts of plastic waste reach the sea. Solutions include reducing single-use plastics, better recycling and new biodegradable materials."],["Polymers from plants","Scientists are developing polymers made from plants rather than petroleum, such as polylactic acid from corn or sugarcane, and materials made from seaweed, cassava and agricultural waste. Some are biodegradable under the right conditions. Researchers in the Philippines have explored materials from local sources such as abaca, coconut and seaweed."]],
sp:["The stockings that caused riots","When nylon stockings went on sale across the United States in May 1940, women lined up outside stores and millions of pairs sold within days. During the Second World War, nylon was needed for parachutes and other military uses, and stockings became scarce. Some women painted lines on the backs of their legs to look as if they were wearing stockings. When nylon stockings returned to stores after the war, huge crowds gathered, and scuffles broke out in several cities in what newspapers called the nylon riots. A new polymer had become a symbol of modern life."],
g:[["Polymer","A very large molecule made of many repeating units"],["Monomer","A small molecule that joins with others to form a polymer"],["Polymerization","The chemical process of joining monomers into polymers"],["Microplastics","Tiny pieces of plastic less than 5 millimeters long"]],
f:["DNA and proteins are natural polymers.","Nylon was invented in 1935.","Kevlar is stronger than steel by weight."],
fun:"A single long polymer molecule can contain millions of atoms linked in a chain.",
tl:"Ang polymer ay napakalaking molekulang binubuo ng maraming maliliit na bahaging magkakadugtong. Kabilang dito ang likas na sutla, goma at DNA, at ang gawang-taong nylon at plastik. Matatagpuan ito sa ating katawan, damit at tahanan."});

ADDART({id:"germtheory",t:"Germ Theory of Disease",c:"History of Science",i:"🔬",w:"Germ theory of disease",r:["pasteur","vacc","antibiotics","hygiene","sanitation"],
p:[["Germs cause disease","Germ theory states that many diseases are caused by tiny living things called microorganisms."],["A medical revolution","It replaced older ideas, such as bad air, as the explanation for disease."],["Saving lives","Germ theory led to handwashing, sterilization, vaccines and clean water."]],
l:"The germ theory of disease is the scientific understanding that many diseases are caused by microorganisms, or germs, such as bacteria, viruses, fungi and parasites, which invade the body and multiply. Before germ theory was accepted in the late 1800s, many people believed that diseases were caused by miasma, poisonous bad air from rotting matter, or by imbalances in the body. The work of scientists such as Ignaz Semmelweis, John Snow, Louis Pasteur, Joseph Lister and Robert Koch showed how germs spread illness and how they could be stopped. Germ theory transformed medicine and public health, leading to antiseptic surgery, sterilization, vaccines, clean water systems and antibiotics, and saving countless lives.",
s:[["Before germ theory","For centuries, many doctors followed the theory of the four humors, believing illness came from imbalances of bodily fluids. Others blamed miasma, foul-smelling air. Although these ideas were wrong, the miasma theory sometimes encouraged useful steps, such as cleaning streets and draining swamps."],["Seeing microbes","In the 1670s the Dutch scientist Antonie van Leeuwenhoek used tiny handmade microscopes to see living creatures in pond water and scrapings from his teeth, which he called animalcules. He was among the first people to see bacteria, but no one yet connected them with disease."],["Semmelweis and handwashing","In 1847 the Hungarian doctor Ignaz Semmelweis noticed that women giving birth in a Vienna hospital ward run by doctors died of fever far more often than in a ward run by midwives. Doctors often came straight from examining dead bodies. When he required them to wash their hands in a chlorine solution, deaths dropped sharply. Many doctors rejected his ideas at the time."],["John Snow and cholera","During a cholera outbreak in London in 1854, the doctor John Snow mapped the cases and found they clustered around a water pump on Broad Street. He persuaded officials to remove the pump’s handle, and the outbreak declined. His work showed that cholera spread through contaminated water, not bad air."]],
k:[["Main idea","Microorganisms cause many diseases"],["Older theory replaced","Miasma, or bad air"],["Key scientists","Pasteur, Koch, Lister, Semmelweis, Snow"],["Accepted","Late 1800s"],["Led to","Antiseptics, vaccines, clean water, antibiotics"]],
d:[["1546","Girolamo Fracastoro suggests that diseases spread by tiny seeds"],["1676","Antonie van Leeuwenhoek describes microscopic organisms"],["1847","Ignaz Semmelweis introduces handwashing in a Vienna hospital"],["1854","John Snow traces a London cholera outbreak to a water pump"],["1867","Joseph Lister publishes his work on antiseptic surgery"],["1882","Robert Koch identifies the bacterium that causes tuberculosis"]],
s2:[["Pasteur’s experiments","In the 1860s the French chemist Louis Pasteur showed that microorganisms cause fermentation and spoiling. Using flasks with long, curved necks that let in air but trapped dust, he showed that broth stayed fresh unless germs reached it, disproving the idea that life arises spontaneously. He developed pasteurization, heating liquids to kill germs, and later vaccines against anthrax and rabies."],["Lister’s antiseptic surgery","Inspired by Pasteur, the British surgeon Joseph Lister began in 1865 to clean wounds, instruments and dressings with carbolic acid. Infections and deaths after surgery fell dramatically. His methods led to modern sterile surgery, in which instruments are sterilized and surgeons wear gloves and masks."],["Koch’s postulates","The German doctor Robert Koch proved that specific bacteria cause specific diseases. He identified the germs behind anthrax in 1876, tuberculosis in 1882 and cholera in 1883. He developed a set of rules, known as Koch’s postulates, for proving that a particular microbe causes a particular disease. He received the Nobel Prize in 1905."],["Public health revolution","Germ theory changed how cities were built and run. Governments built sewer systems and water treatment plants, inspected food, and promoted handwashing and vaccination. Deaths from diseases such as cholera, typhoid and tuberculosis fell sharply in many countries in the late 1800s and early 1900s."],["Germ theory in the Philippines","In the late 1800s and early 1900s, cholera, smallpox and other epidemics struck the Philippines. Public health campaigns introduced vaccination, water purification, quarantine and sanitation in Manila and other towns. Filipino doctors and scientists trained in modern medicine, and the University of the Philippines College of Medicine and the Bureau of Science were founded in the early 1900s."],["Beyond germs","Germ theory explains infectious diseases, but not all illnesses. Many diseases, such as heart disease, diabetes and most cancers, are caused by a mix of genes, lifestyle and environment. Scientists now also know that many microbes living in and on the body are helpful, forming the microbiome that aids digestion and protects against harmful germs."]],
sp:["The pump on Broad Street","In late August 1854, cholera swept through the Soho district of London, killing hundreds of people in a few days. Most experts blamed foul air. Dr. John Snow suspected the water. He went door to door, recording where victims lived, and marked each death on a map. The marks clustered around a public water pump on Broad Street. Interestingly, workers at a nearby brewery, who drank beer instead of water, mostly escaped the disease. Snow presented his evidence to local officials, who removed the pump’s handle. His map is now famous as one of the founding works of epidemiology, the study of how diseases spread."],
g:[["Germ","A microorganism that can cause disease"],["Miasma","An old belief that disease was caused by bad air"],["Antiseptic","A substance that kills germs on skin or wounds"],["Epidemiology","The study of how diseases spread in populations"]],
f:["Germ theory replaced the idea of bad air.","Semmelweis promoted handwashing in 1847.","John Snow traced cholera to a water pump."],
fun:"During the 1854 London cholera outbreak, workers at a brewery near the infected pump mostly escaped because they drank beer instead of water.",
tl:"Ang germ theory ay ang kaalamang maraming sakit ang dulot ng mikrobyo tulad ng bakterya at virus. Pinalitan nito ang lumang paniniwala na masamang hangin ang sanhi ng sakit. Humantong ito sa paghuhugas ng kamay, malinis na tubig at bakuna."});

;
/* data/modern7.js */
// The Modern World, part 6: smart cities, overtourism, financial literacy, deepfakes, online scams and sea level rise.
ADDART({id:"smartcities",t:"Smart Cities",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🏙️",w:"Smart city",r:["megacities","smarthome","dataprivacy","powergrid","worldpop"],
p:[["Cities using data","A smart city uses digital technology and data to run services more efficiently."],["Sensors everywhere","Sensors, cameras and apps help manage traffic, energy, water and safety."],["Promise and concern","Smart cities promise convenience but raise questions about privacy and fairness."]],
l:"A smart city is an urban area that uses digital technology, sensors and data to improve public services and the quality of life of its residents. Smart city projects may use connected traffic lights to ease congestion, sensors to detect water leaks and air pollution, apps for paying fares and taxes, smart meters for electricity, and data platforms to help governments plan. Cities such as Singapore, Seoul, Barcelona and Amsterdam are often cited as leaders, and new planned cities, including New Clark City in the Philippines, are being designed with smart technology in mind. Supporters say smart cities can be cleaner, safer and more efficient, while critics warn about surveillance, data privacy, high costs and leaving poorer residents behind.",
s:[["What makes a city smart","Smart cities connect devices and systems through networks, often called the Internet of Things. Data from sensors, cameras, phones and payment systems are collected and analyzed to help officials and residents make decisions, from adjusting traffic signals to sending repair crews where they are needed."],["Smarter transport","Cities use real-time data to manage traffic lights, guide drivers to parking spaces, show when buses and trains will arrive and allow cashless fares. Some cities charge drivers to enter crowded areas at busy times, using cameras to read license plates, which reduces congestion."],["Energy and environment","Smart grids, efficient street lighting that dims when no one is around, and sensors that monitor air quality, noise and flooding help cities save energy and respond to environmental problems. Smart waste bins can signal when they are full, so collection trucks avoid unnecessary trips."],["Digital government","Many cities let residents apply for permits, pay taxes, report problems such as potholes and access public information online. Open data portals share information on budgets, transport and services, which supporters say improves transparency."]],
k:[["Key technology","Sensors and data networks"],["Often-cited leaders","Singapore, Seoul, Barcelona"],["Philippine planned city","New Clark City, Tarlac"],["Main benefits","Efficiency, convenience, sustainability"],["Main concerns","Privacy, cost, inequality"]],
d:[["2008","Technology companies begin promoting the idea of smarter cities"],["2014","Singapore launches its Smart Nation initiative"],["2015","India launches its Smart Cities Mission"],["2018","ASEAN launches the ASEAN Smart Cities Network"],["2020","A high-profile smart neighborhood plan in Toronto is canceled"],["2020s","Philippine cities expand digital services and traffic monitoring"]],
s2:[["Singapore’s Smart Nation","Singapore launched its Smart Nation initiative in 2014, using technology for transport, health care, government services and digital payments. It has sensors to monitor traffic and water, a national digital identity system and widespread cashless payments. Supporters see it as a model, while some critics raise concerns about government surveillance."],["Smart cities in the Philippines","Several Philippine cities have introduced digital services, traffic command centers, closed-circuit cameras and online payment systems. New Clark City in Tarlac, developed by the government, is planned as a green and smart city with resilient infrastructure. Cities such as Manila, Cebu and Davao have joined the ASEAN Smart Cities Network."],["Privacy and surveillance","Smart city systems collect large amounts of data about people’s movements and activities. Critics worry that cameras, facial recognition and tracking could be used for surveillance or that data could be misused or hacked. In 2020 a project by a technology company to build a smart neighborhood in Toronto was canceled after public concerns about privacy and cost."],["Who benefits?","Some critics argue that smart city projects focus on gadgets rather than basic needs such as affordable housing, clean water and public transport, and may benefit wealthier areas more than poorer ones. Supporters reply that technology can help deliver services more efficiently to everyone if planned inclusively."],["Resilient cities","For cities facing typhoons, floods and earthquakes, smart technology can provide early warnings, monitor rivers and drainage, and coordinate emergency response. In the Philippines, flood sensors, hazard maps and emergency apps help local governments prepare for disasters."],["People at the center","Many urban experts now say a truly smart city is not about technology alone but about people. They emphasize involving residents in decisions, protecting rights, designing walkable neighborhoods, providing green spaces and using technology only where it solves real problems."]],
sp:["A city built from scratch","On former farmland near Clark in Tarlac, the Philippine government has been developing New Clark City, a planned city meant to ease pressure on crowded Metro Manila. Its first major facilities were a sports complex, including an athletics stadium and aquatics center, built for the 2019 Southeast Asian Games. Planners describe a city with green spaces, flood-resilient design, renewable energy, efficient public transport and digital services, and land set aside for government offices and businesses. How quickly it grows into a full city, and whether it achieves its goals, will be watched closely by urban planners."],
g:[["Smart city","A city that uses digital technology and data to improve services"],["Internet of Things","Everyday devices connected to the internet"],["Open data","Public information shared freely online"],["Surveillance","Close monitoring of people’s activities"]],
f:["Singapore launched its Smart Nation initiative in 2014.","New Clark City is a planned city in Tarlac.","Smart cities raise privacy debates."],
fun:"Some smart street lights dim automatically when no one is around and brighten when a person or car approaches.",
tl:"Ang smart city ay lungsod na gumagamit ng teknolohiyang digital at datos upang mapabuti ang mga serbisyo. Ginagamit ang mga sensor at app sa trapiko, kuryente, tubig at kaligtasan. May mga alalahanin naman tungkol sa privacy at pagkakapantay-pantay."});

ADDART({id:"overtourism",t:"Overtourism",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🧳",w:"Overtourism",r:["tourism","megacities","reef","costliving","housing"],
p:[["Too many visitors","Overtourism happens when too many tourists crowd a place, harming it and its residents."],["Boracay’s closure","The Philippines closed Boracay for six months in 2018 to rehabilitate it."],["Finding balance","Places try limits, fees and better planning to manage visitors."]],
l:"Overtourism describes a situation in which a destination receives more visitors than it can handle, damaging the environment, overloading local services and harming residents’ quality of life. Crowded streets, rising rents, pollution, traffic and damaged natural sites are common complaints. The term became widely used in the late 2010s as cheap flights, cruise ships and social media drew huge crowds to popular cities, beaches and landmarks such as Venice, Barcelona, Kyoto and Maya Bay in Thailand. In the Philippines, the island of Boracay was closed to tourists for six months in 2018 to clean up pollution. Places now try visitor limits, entry fees, reservation systems and spreading tourists to less-visited areas, balancing tourism’s economic benefits with its costs.",
s:[["Causes","International tourism grew rapidly in recent decades as incomes rose, budget airlines spread and travel became easier to book online. Social media made certain spots famous overnight, drawing crowds seeking the same photographs. Cruise ships can bring thousands of visitors to a small town at once."],["Effects on places","Too many visitors can damage coral reefs, beaches, trails and historic buildings, and produce more garbage and sewage than local systems can handle. Traffic and crowds make daily life harder for residents. In some cities, homes are converted into short-term vacation rentals, raising rents and pushing local people out."],["Benefits of tourism","Tourism also brings jobs, income and investment, supporting hotels, restaurants, guides, transport and crafts. In the Philippines, tourism employs millions of people and is an important part of the economy. The challenge is to keep these benefits while limiting harm."],["Managing tourism","Destinations use many tools, including daily visitor limits, entry fees, tourist taxes, timed tickets, bans on new hotels, restrictions on short-term rentals and cruise ships, and campaigns encouraging visitors to travel off-season or to lesser-known places."]],
k:[["Term popular since","Late 2010s"],["Boracay closure","April to October 2018"],["Famous examples","Venice, Barcelona, Kyoto, Maya Bay"],["Common tools","Visitor limits, fees, reservations"],["Related idea","Sustainable tourism"]],
d:[["2017","Residents in Barcelona and Venice protest against mass tourism"],["2018","Boracay is closed to tourists for six months"],["2018","Thailand closes Maya Bay to let its ecosystem recover"],["2022","Maya Bay reopens with visitor limits"],["2024","Venice begins charging day-trippers an entry fee"],["2024","Residents in Spain hold large protests against mass tourism"]],
s2:[["Boracay’s rehabilitation","Boracay, famous for its white sand beaches, attracted millions of visitors a year. By 2018, problems with sewage, illegal construction and water pollution had become severe, and President Rodrigo Duterte described the island as a cesspool. The government closed it to tourists from 26 April to 26 October 2018. Sewage systems were repaired, illegal structures removed and rules introduced on visitor numbers and beach use. Supporters praised the cleanup, while some workers and businesses suffered from lost income during the closure."],["Maya Bay","Maya Bay in Thailand became world-famous after appearing in the 2000 film The Beach. Thousands of visitors a day damaged its coral reefs. Thailand closed the bay in 2018, and when it reopened in 2022, boats were banned from the bay itself, swimming was prohibited and visitor numbers were limited. Reef sharks returned to the waters."],["Venice","Venice, a city of canals in Italy, has seen its resident population shrink while millions of tourists visit each year. Large cruise ships were banned from the city center in 2021, and in 2024 Venice began testing an entry fee for day visitors on busy days. Some residents welcomed the measures, while others argued they were not enough."],["Protecting natural wonders","Many natural sites limit visitors to protect them. In the Philippines, places such as Tubbataha Reefs, the Puerto Princesa Underground River and some beaches and dive sites require permits or have daily limits. Bhutan charges international visitors a daily sustainable development fee to keep tourism low-impact."],["Residents versus tourists","In cities such as Barcelona, Amsterdam and Lisbon, residents have protested against crowds, noisy party tourism and rising rents linked to holiday rentals. Some cities have restricted short-term rentals or banned new tourist accommodation in central areas. Tourism businesses argue that such measures cost jobs, showing the competing interests involved."],["Being a responsible traveler","Travelers can help by visiting in less crowded seasons, exploring lesser-known places, staying in locally owned accommodation, following local rules, respecting residents’ privacy, avoiding littering, not touching coral or wildlife and supporting local businesses and crafts."]],
sp:["Six months without tourists","When Boracay closed in April 2018, the island fell quiet for the first time in decades. Bulldozers removed buildings that had been built too close to the shore, workers dug new drainage and sewage pipes, and the main road was widened. Some families who depended on tourism struggled without income, and the government offered aid. When the island reopened in October, visitors found cleaner water, a beach without crowds of vendors, and new rules banning smoking, drinking and parties on the beach. Officials set limits on the number of tourists and hotel rooms, aiming to keep the island from becoming overcrowded again."],
g:[["Overtourism","Too many tourists harming a place and its residents"],["Sustainable tourism","Tourism that protects the environment and local communities"],["Carrying capacity","The number of visitors a place can handle without damage"],["Short-term rental","A home rented to visitors for short stays"]],
f:["Boracay was closed for six months in 2018.","Venice charges some day visitors an entry fee.","Maya Bay reopened in 2022 with visitor limits."],
fun:"After Thailand closed Maya Bay to tourists, groups of blacktip reef sharks returned to swim in its shallow waters.",
tl:"Ang overtourism ay nangyayari kapag sobra ang dami ng turista sa isang lugar, na nakasisira sa kalikasan at buhay ng mga residente. Isinara ang Boracay nang anim na buwan noong 2018 upang linisin. Gumagamit ang mga lugar ng limitasyon, bayad at mas mahusay na pagpaplano."});

ADDART({id:"financialliteracy",t:"Financial Literacy",c:"Economics and Society",i:"💰",w:"Financial literacy",r:["money","costliving","digitalpay","onlinescams","inflation"],
p:[["Money skills","Financial literacy is the knowledge and skills needed to manage money wisely."],["Saving and budgeting","It includes budgeting, saving, borrowing carefully and avoiding scams."],["Starting young","Learning about money early helps people make better choices throughout life."]],
l:"Financial literacy is the knowledge, skills and confidence needed to make good decisions about money. It includes understanding how to earn, budget, save, borrow, invest and protect money, as well as concepts such as interest, inflation, risk and debt. Financially literate people are better able to handle emergencies, avoid costly loans and scams, plan for education, housing and retirement, and reach their goals. Surveys around the world, including in the Philippines, have found that many adults struggle with basic financial ideas. Governments, schools, banks and community groups now teach financial literacy, which has become especially important as digital banking, e-wallets and online lending grow.",
s:[["Budgeting","A budget is a plan for how to use money. It lists income and expenses, such as food, rent, transport, school costs and bills, and sets aside money for savings. Tracking spending helps people see where their money goes and avoid spending more than they earn. Many people use notebooks, envelopes or phone apps to budget."],["Saving and emergency funds","Saving means setting aside part of one’s income for future needs. Experts recommend building an emergency fund to cover several months of basic expenses, so that illness, job loss or disasters do not force people into debt. Savings kept in a bank or digital account are safer than cash at home."],["Interest and debt","When people borrow money, they usually pay interest, an extra charge for using the lender’s money. When they save in a bank, they may earn interest. Compound interest means interest is earned or charged on previous interest, so savings can grow over time but debts can also grow quickly if unpaid."],["Investing","Investing means putting money into assets such as stocks, bonds, mutual funds, businesses or property, hoping it will grow. Investments can earn more than savings accounts but carry risk, as their value can go down. Experts advise understanding any investment before putting money in and spreading money across different investments."]],
k:[["Core skills","Budgeting, saving, borrowing wisely, investing"],["Key concept","Compound interest"],["Safety net","Emergency fund"],["Philippine central bank","Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas"],["Major risk","Scams and high-interest debt"]],
d:[["1993","The Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas is established"],["2008","A global financial crisis highlights the dangers of risky borrowing"],["2012","The OECD tests financial literacy among students for the first time"],["2015","The Philippines adopts a National Strategy for Financial Inclusion"],["2020","The COVID-19 pandemic speeds up the use of e-wallets and digital banking"],["2024","The Philippines passes a law against financial account scams"]],
s2:[["Filipino savings traditions","Many Filipinos save through traditions such as the paluwagan, in which a group of friends or coworkers contributes a fixed amount regularly and each member takes turns receiving the whole pot. Others save in alkansya, coin banks often made from bamboo. Cooperatives also help members save and borrow at fair rates."],["The danger of high-interest loans","Informal lenders sometimes charge extremely high interest. The 5-6 lending system, in which a borrower receives 5 pesos and repays 6 within a short period, can trap people in debt. Online lending apps have also been criticized for high charges and for harassing borrowers. Authorities have cracked down on abusive lenders."],["Digital money","E-wallets, online banking and buy now, pay later services have made financial services easier to reach, especially for people without bank accounts. They also bring new risks, such as overspending, hidden fees and scams. Financial literacy includes understanding digital tools, protecting passwords and one-time PINs and reading terms carefully."],["Remittances and planning","Millions of Filipino families receive money from relatives working abroad. Financial advisers encourage families to budget remittances, save a portion, invest in education or small businesses and plan for when the overseas worker returns home, rather than spending everything on immediate needs."],["Teaching money skills","Financial literacy is increasingly taught in schools, through lessons on budgeting, saving and entrepreneurship. The Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas and other institutions run programs for students, teachers, overseas workers and communities. Experts say parents can teach children by giving allowances, encouraging saving and talking openly about money."],["Recognizing scams","Financially literate people are better able to spot investment scams that promise unrealistic returns, such as Ponzi and pyramid schemes, which pay early investors with money from new ones until they collapse. A common rule is that if an offer sounds too good to be true, it probably is. Authorities such as the Securities and Exchange Commission publish warnings about illegal schemes."]],
sp:["The magic of compound interest","Imagine two friends. One starts saving 1,000 pesos a month at age 20 and stops at 30. The other starts at 30 and saves the same amount every month until 60. If both earn a steady return, the friend who started earlier can end up with a similar or even larger amount, despite saving for only ten years, because their money had more time to grow. Each year, interest is earned not only on the money saved but on the interest already earned. This is the power of compound interest, and it is why financial advisers often say the best time to start saving is as early as possible."],
g:[["Budget","A plan for using money"],["Interest","A charge for borrowing money or a payment for saving it"],["Compound interest","Interest earned on both the original amount and past interest"],["Paluwagan","A Filipino rotating savings group"]],
f:["A budget helps people plan their spending.","Compound interest helps savings grow.","Paluwagan is a Filipino savings tradition."],
fun:"Albert Einstein is often quoted calling compound interest the eighth wonder of the world, though there is no evidence he actually said it.",
tl:"Ang financial literacy ay kaalaman at kasanayan sa wastong paghawak ng pera. Kabilang dito ang pagbabadyet, pag-iipon, maingat na pangungutang at pag-iwas sa scam. Mahalagang matuto tungkol sa pera habang bata pa."});

ADDART({id:"deepfakes",t:"Deepfakes",c:"Technology and Society",i:"🎭",w:"Deepfake",r:["misinformation","aiart","dataprivacy","socialmedia","onlinescams"],
p:[["Fake but realistic","Deepfakes are fake videos, images or audio made with artificial intelligence."],["Hard to spot","They can make it look as if real people said or did things they never did."],["Risks and responses","Deepfakes are used in scams, harassment and misinformation, and laws and tools are being developed in response."]],
l:"A deepfake is a video, image or audio recording that has been created or altered using artificial intelligence to make it appear that a real person said or did something they never did. The word combines deep learning, a type of AI, and fake. Deepfakes first spread online in 2017, and the technology has since become much easier to use and more realistic. They can be used for entertainment, satire, filmmaking and education, but also for scams, fraud, harassment, fake sexual images of real people and political misinformation. In the Philippines, a deepfake audio clip of President Ferdinand Marcos Jr. circulated in 2024. Governments, technology companies and researchers are developing laws, detection tools and labels to address the harms.",
s:[["How deepfakes are made","Deepfake systems learn from many images, videos or recordings of a person and then generate new content that mimics their face, voice and movements. Early methods used generative adversarial networks, in which two AI systems compete to make increasingly convincing fakes. Newer tools can produce realistic video or clone a voice from just a short sample."],["Harmful uses","Criminals use deepfake voices and videos to impersonate company bosses, relatives or celebrities to trick people into sending money. Deepfakes are also used to create fake intimate images of real people without consent, overwhelmingly targeting women, causing serious harm. Fake videos of politicians can spread false information, especially during elections."],["Positive uses","The same technology can be used in films to de-age actors or dub speech into other languages, in museums to bring historical figures to life, and in medicine to help people who have lost their voices speak with a synthetic version of their own voice. Satirists and artists also use it creatively, ideally with clear labels."],["Spotting deepfakes","Signs of a deepfake can include unnatural blinking, mismatched lighting or shadows, blurry edges around faces, odd mouth movements or voices with strange rhythm. However, deepfakes are improving, so experts advise checking the source, looking for reports from trusted news organizations and being cautious with shocking clips."]],
k:[["Word from","Deep learning plus fake"],["First spread","2017"],["Common harms","Scams, fake intimate images, misinformation"],["Philippine example","Fake audio of the President, 2024"],["Responses","Laws, detection tools, labels"]],
d:[["2014","Generative adversarial networks are invented"],["2017","The term deepfake appears online"],["2019","A deepfake voice is reportedly used to trick a company into transferring money"],["2024","A deepfake audio of President Marcos Jr. circulates and is flagged by the government"],["2024","The European Union’s AI Act requires labeling of deepfakes"],["2025","The United States passes a law against sharing nonconsensual intimate deepfakes"]],
s2:[["The Philippine deepfake audio","In April 2024, an audio clip circulated online in which a voice resembling President Ferdinand Marcos Jr. appeared to order military action. The Presidential Communications Office declared it a deepfake, stating that the President never made such remarks, and authorities ordered an investigation. The incident raised concern about how AI could be used to spread false information on sensitive issues."],["Elections and deepfakes","Election authorities in many countries worry that deepfakes could mislead voters, for example with fake videos of candidates or fake robocalls. Some countries require AI-generated political content to be labeled or ban deceptive deepfakes near elections. Others warn that genuine recordings might be dismissed as fake, a problem sometimes called the liar’s dividend."],["Protecting people from abuse","Many countries are passing laws against creating or sharing sexual deepfakes of real people without consent. In the Philippines, existing laws against voyeurism, cybercrime and violence against women may apply, and lawmakers have proposed specific deepfake legislation. Victims are encouraged to report abuse to platforms and authorities."],["Technology responses","Technology companies are developing systems to detect deepfakes and to add watermarks or tamper-proof labels to content showing whether it was made or edited with AI. Some cameras and apps record information about where and how a photo was taken. No method is perfect, so experts stress education and critical thinking."],["Voice cloning scams","Scammers have used cloned voices of family members claiming to be in trouble and needing money urgently. Police and banks advise people to hang up and call the person directly using a known number, and some families agree on a secret code word to confirm identity in emergencies."],["Free expression concerns","Some civil liberties groups caution that laws against deepfakes must be carefully written so they do not restrict legitimate satire, parody, art or journalism. Lawmakers try to balance protecting people from harm with protecting free speech."]],
sp:["Think before you share","Imagine scrolling through social media and seeing a video of a famous person saying something shocking. Before sharing it, fact-checkers suggest pausing to ask a few questions. Who posted it first? Is it reported by trusted news organizations? Does the person’s voice or face look slightly unnatural? Are there signs it was edited? Searching for the clip along with words like fake or fact check can reveal whether it has been debunked. Because false content often spreads faster than corrections, taking a moment to check before sharing can help stop the spread of deepfakes and other misinformation."],
g:[["Deepfake","Fake media created with artificial intelligence"],["Deep learning","A type of AI that learns patterns from large amounts of data"],["Voice cloning","Copying a person’s voice using AI"],["Liar’s dividend","Using the existence of deepfakes to deny real evidence"]],
f:["Deepfake combines deep learning and fake.","Deepfakes are used in scams and misinformation.","Check the source before sharing shocking videos."],
fun:"The word deepfake came from the username of an online account that shared AI-altered videos in 2017.",
tl:"Ang deepfake ay pekeng video, larawan o audio na ginawa gamit ang artificial intelligence. Maaari nitong ipakitang may sinabi o ginawa ang isang tao na hindi naman totoo. Ginagamit ito sa scam at maling impormasyon, kaya mahalagang suriin muna bago magbahagi."});

ADDART({id:"onlinescams",t:"Online Scams",c:"Technology and Society",i:"🎣",w:"Internet fraud",r:["cybersecurity","dataprivacy","digitalpay","financialliteracy","deepfakes"],
p:[["Tricks for money","Online scams are tricks that use the internet, texts or calls to steal money or personal information."],["Many forms","They include phishing messages, fake online sellers, investment fraud and romance scams."],["Staying safe","Never share passwords or one-time PINs, and verify before you click or pay."]],
l:"Online scams are dishonest schemes that use the internet, text messages, phone calls, social media or apps to trick people into giving away money or personal information. Common types include phishing messages that pretend to come from banks or delivery companies, fake online shops, investment schemes promising high returns, romance scams, fake job offers and impersonation of family members or officials. Scammers often create a sense of urgency, fear or excitement to make victims act without thinking. Online scams cost people billions of dollars every year worldwide and have grown with digital payments. In the Philippines, laws such as the SIM Registration Act and the Anti-Financial Account Scamming Act aim to fight fraud, while awareness remains the best protection.",
s:[["Phishing and smishing","Phishing messages are emails, texts or chats that pretend to come from trusted organizations, such as banks, e-wallets, government agencies or couriers, and ask people to click a link, log in or share details. When sent by text message, they are called smishing. The links often lead to fake websites that steal passwords and one-time PINs."],["Fake sellers and online shopping","Scammers set up fake online shops or social media pages offering popular products at very low prices. After buyers pay, the goods never arrive. Others pose as buyers and send fake proof of payment. Using trusted platforms, checking reviews and paying through secure methods reduces risk."],["Investment and romance scams","Investment scams promise guaranteed high returns, often involving cryptocurrency or trading, and use fake testimonials. In romance scams, criminals build online relationships over weeks or months, then ask for money for emergencies or investments. Some combine both, in schemes sometimes called pig butchering."],["Warning signs","Common red flags include messages creating urgency, such as your account will be locked; requests for passwords, PINs or OTPs; offers that seem too good to be true; pressure to pay by unusual methods; poor spelling; and links with strange web addresses. Legitimate banks never ask customers to share their one-time PINs."]],
k:[["Common type","Phishing"],["Text message phishing","Smishing"],["Philippine SIM law","SIM Registration Act, 2022"],["Philippine anti-scam law","Anti-Financial Account Scamming Act, 2024"],["Golden rule","Never share your OTP or password"]],
d:[["1990s","Email scams spread with the growth of the internet"],["2012","The Philippines passes the Cybercrime Prevention Act"],["2022","The Philippines passes the SIM Registration Act"],["2023","A United Nations report warns that hundreds of thousands of people are forced to work in scam centers in Southeast Asia"],["2024","The Philippines passes the Anti-Financial Account Scamming Act"],["2024","The Philippines announces a ban on offshore gaming operators linked to scams and crime"]],
s2:[["Scams in the Philippines","Filipinos have reported large numbers of fake text messages, often containing links or offers of prizes and jobs. In response, the SIM Registration Act of 2022 required all mobile phone SIM cards to be registered with users’ identities, aiming to make scammers easier to trace. Critics questioned its effectiveness and raised privacy concerns, and scammers shifted to other methods."],["The anti-scam law","The Anti-Financial Account Scamming Act, signed in 2024, made it a crime to act as a money mule, allowing one’s bank or e-wallet account to be used to receive stolen funds, and to engage in social engineering schemes to steal account information. It also gives banks and regulators more power to freeze suspicious accounts."],["Scam compounds","Investigations have revealed large scam centers in parts of Southeast Asia, where workers, some of them trafficking victims lured by fake job offers, are forced to run online scams targeting people around the world. In the Philippines, raids on some offshore gaming operations uncovered scam activities, and in 2024 the government announced a ban on offshore gaming operators."],["Protecting yourself","Experts recommend never sharing passwords or one-time PINs, enabling two-factor authentication, typing website addresses directly instead of clicking links, verifying requests by calling official numbers, keeping apps updated and being cautious about what personal information is shared online."],["Helping family members","Older people and those new to digital technology are often targeted. Family members can help by explaining common scams, setting up security features and encouraging relatives to ask before responding to unusual messages or calls. Young people are also targeted through gaming, social media and fake job offers."],["What to do if scammed","Victims should immediately contact their bank or e-wallet provider to try to freeze transactions, change passwords, report the scam to authorities such as the cybercrime units of the police and the National Bureau of Investigation, and warn others. Reporting helps authorities track scammers, even if money cannot always be recovered."]],
sp:["Congratulations, you have won!","A text message arrives: Congratulations! Your number has won 50,000 pesos in a raffle. Click this link to claim your prize. It looks exciting, but there was no raffle. Clicking the link leads to a page asking for bank or e-wallet details, or a small processing fee, and then the one-time PIN sent to the phone. Once the scammer has these, they can empty the account within minutes. Such messages are among the most common scams in the Philippines. The safest response is simple: do not click, do not reply, and delete or report the message."],
g:[["Phishing","Tricking people into giving information by pretending to be trustworthy"],["Smishing","Phishing through text messages"],["OTP","One-time PIN, a temporary code used to confirm transactions"],["Money mule","A person whose account is used to move stolen money"]],
f:["Banks never ask for your one-time PIN.","Smishing is phishing by text message.","The Philippines passed an anti-scam law in 2024."],
fun:"The word phishing comes from fishing, because scammers throw out bait hoping someone will bite.",
tl:"Ang online scam ay panlilinlang gamit ang internet, text o tawag upang magnakaw ng pera o personal na impormasyon. Kabilang dito ang phishing, pekeng tindahan at investment scam. Huwag kailanman ibahagi ang password o OTP at suriin muna bago mag-click o magbayad."});

ADDART({id:"sealevel",t:"Sea Level Rise",c:"Environment",i:"🌊",w:"Sea level rise",r:["climchange","glaciers","pacific","megacities","extremeheat"],
p:[["Rising oceans","Global sea levels are rising as the planet warms."],["Two main causes","Melting ice and warming seawater, which expands, both raise sea levels."],["Coasts at risk","Low-lying islands and coastal cities face flooding and erosion."]],
l:"Sea level rise is the increase in the average level of the world’s oceans. Since 1900, global sea level has risen by roughly 20 centimeters, and the rate of rise has been speeding up. The main causes are the melting of glaciers and the great ice sheets of Greenland and Antarctica, and the expansion of seawater as it warms. Rising seas increase coastal flooding, erosion, storm surges and the intrusion of salt water into farmland and drinking water. Low-lying island nations and coastal cities, including many in Southeast Asia, are especially at risk. In parts of the Philippines, such as around Manila Bay, local sea levels are rising faster than the global average, partly because the land is sinking.",
s:[["Why seas are rising","As Earth warms, glaciers and ice sheets on land melt and add water to the oceans. At the same time, seawater expands as it warms, a process called thermal expansion. Together these have caused most of the observed rise. Floating sea ice, by contrast, does not raise sea level much when it melts."],["Measuring sea level","Scientists measure sea level using tide gauges along coasts, some with records going back more than a century, and satellites that bounce radar off the ocean surface. Since satellite measurements began in 1993, the rate of global rise has roughly doubled."],["Future projections","How much seas rise this century depends mainly on how much greenhouse gas emissions are reduced. The Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change has projected a likely rise of roughly 30 centimeters to one meter by 2100, depending on emissions, with larger increases possible if ice sheets become unstable. Sea levels will continue rising for centuries."],["Effects on coasts","Higher seas make storm surges more dangerous, cause more frequent high-tide flooding, wear away beaches and push salt water into rivers, wells and rice fields. Mangroves and coral reefs, which protect coasts, can be harmed if seas rise faster than they can adapt."]],
k:[["Rise since 1900","Roughly 20 centimeters"],["Main causes","Melting ice and warming water"],["Measured by","Tide gauges and satellites"],["Projected rise by 2100","Roughly 0.3 to 1 meter, depending on emissions"],["Philippine hotspot","Manila Bay area"]],
d:[["1880","Reliable global sea level estimates begin"],["1993","Satellites begin precise measurements of sea level"],["2013","Typhoon Haiyan’s storm surge devastates Tacloban"],["2019","An IPCC report focuses on oceans and frozen regions"],["2021","An IPCC report projects continued sea level rise for centuries"],["2023","Tuvalu and Australia agree a treaty offering residency to Tuvaluans affected by climate change"]],
s2:[["Sinking land in the Philippines","In some coastal areas of Metro Manila, Bulacan and Pampanga, the land itself is sinking, a process called subsidence, mainly because large amounts of groundwater are pumped out. Combined with rising seas, this makes flooding worse. Some towns in Bulacan now flood regularly at high tide, and residents have raised floors and roads."],["Storm surges","Storm surges, walls of seawater pushed ashore by strong typhoon winds, become more dangerous as sea levels rise. In 2013 Typhoon Haiyan, known locally as Yolanda, drove a storm surge of several meters into Tacloban and other towns in Leyte and Samar, causing thousands of deaths. Higher seas mean future surges can reach farther inland."],["Island nations","Low-lying island nations such as Tuvalu, Kiribati, the Marshall Islands and the Maldives have much of their land only a few meters above sea level. Their leaders have warned that rising seas threaten their existence and have called for strong global action. In 2023 Australia and Tuvalu signed a treaty offering Tuvaluans a pathway to live in Australia."],["Big cities at risk","Many of the world’s largest cities lie on coasts or river deltas, including Manila, Jakarta, Bangkok, Ho Chi Minh City, Shanghai, Mumbai, Lagos and New York. Jakarta, which is both sinking and facing rising seas, is one reason Indonesia decided to build a new capital city on the island of Borneo."],["Adapting to rising seas","Coastal communities adapt by building sea walls and dikes, raising homes and roads, restoring mangroves and wetlands, improving drainage, limiting groundwater pumping and, in some cases, moving away from the most exposed areas. The Netherlands, much of which lies below sea level, is famous for its long history of managing water."],["Debates over solutions","Hard defenses such as sea walls can protect cities but are expensive and may damage natural coastlines. Some communities debate whether to defend, adapt or relocate. In Manila Bay, land reclamation projects have been supported for economic development and criticized by environmental groups concerned about flooding and ecosystems."]],
sp:["The village where the tide comes in","In some coastal villages of Bulacan, north of Manila, the sea now enters homes during high tides even on sunny days. Residents have raised their floors with concrete and rubble, built walkways of planks and moved belongings to upper levels. Churches and schools have been elevated, and some families travel by boat along streets that were once dry. The causes include both rising seas and land sinking because of groundwater pumping. Researchers study these communities to understand how people adapt to living with water, and what could happen in other coastal areas in the coming decades."],
g:[["Sea level rise","The increase in the average level of the oceans"],["Thermal expansion","The expansion of water as it warms"],["Subsidence","The gradual sinking of land"],["Storm surge","A rise of seawater pushed ashore by a storm"]],
f:["Global sea level has risen about 20 cm since 1900.","Warming water expands and raises sea level.","Parts of Manila Bay are sinking."],
fun:"If all the ice on Greenland melted, global sea level would rise by about 7 meters, though this would take many centuries.",
tl:"Ang pagtaas ng antas ng dagat ay dulot ng pagkatunaw ng yelo at paglawak ng tubig habang umiinit. Nanganganib ang mabababang isla at mga lungsod sa baybayin. Sa ilang bahagi ng Manila Bay, mas mabilis ang pagtaas dahil lumulubog din ang lupa."});

;
/* data/modern8.js */
// The Modern World, part 7: food waste, electronic waste, plant-based eating, the four-day week, universal basic income and digital IDs.
ADDART({id:"foodwaste",t:"Food Waste",c:"Environment",i:"🍲",w:"Food waste",r:["foodsecurity","recycling","climchange","plasticwaste","rice"],
p:[["Wasted food","Around a fifth of the food available to shoppers worldwide is thrown away."],["Lost along the way","More food is lost between farms and stores."],["Simple fixes","Planning meals, storing food well and sharing leftovers reduce waste."]],
l:"Food waste is food that is fit to eat but is thrown away, while food loss refers to food that spoils or is discarded between harvest and the shops. The United Nations estimated that in 2022 households, restaurants and shops wasted more than a billion tonnes of food, about a fifth of the food available to consumers, while millions of people went hungry. Wasted food also wastes the water, land, energy and labor used to produce it, and rotting food in landfills releases methane, a powerful greenhouse gas. Reducing food waste is part of the Sustainable Development Goals, which aim to halve it by 2030. Governments, businesses and families are trying many solutions, from better storage and donation programs to composting.",
s:[["Where food is wasted","Most food waste happens in homes, followed by restaurants, canteens and shops. Households throw away leftovers, spoiled fruit and vegetables and food past its date. Restaurants prepare more than they sell, and shops discard items that look imperfect or reach their sell-by dates."],["Food lost before it reaches us","In many developing countries, more food is lost on farms and in transport than in kitchens. Poor roads, lack of refrigeration, pests and weak storage cause harvests to rot. In the Philippines, a large share of fruits and vegetables can spoil between farm and market because of heat and delays."],["Why it matters","Wasted food means wasted money for families, wasted resources for farmers and more greenhouse gas emissions. Scientists estimate that food loss and waste together cause a significant share of global emissions. At the same time, hundreds of millions of people do not have enough to eat."],["Reducing waste at home","Families can reduce waste by planning meals, buying only what they need, storing food properly, understanding date labels, freezing extra portions and turning leftovers into new meals. Many Filipino dishes already use leftovers creatively, such as sinangag, garlic fried rice made from day-old rice."]],
k:[["Food wasted by consumers (2022)","More than 1 billion tonnes"],["Share of consumer food wasted","About one-fifth"],["Main source","Households"],["UN goal","Halve food waste by 2030"],["Greenhouse gas from rotting food","Methane"]],
d:[["2011","A UN report estimates that about a third of food is lost or wasted"],["2015","The Sustainable Development Goals include halving food waste"],["2016","France requires large supermarkets to donate unsold food"],["2019","The UN declares 29 September the International Day of Awareness of Food Loss and Waste"],["2021","The UN Food Waste Index is first published"],["2024","The UN reports more than a billion tonnes of food wasted in 2022"]],
s2:[["Date labels","Many people throw away food because they misunderstand date labels. A use-by date is about safety, especially for meat and dairy, while a best-before date is about quality; food is often still safe to eat after its best-before date if stored well. Clearer labels could reduce waste."],["Food banks and donation","Food banks collect surplus food from shops, farms and restaurants and give it to people in need. In the Philippines, groups and community pantries have shared food, especially during the COVID-19 pandemic, when community pantries spread across the country in 2021. Laws in some countries encourage or require donations."],["Composting","Food scraps can be composted, turning them into fertilizer for gardens and farms instead of producing methane in landfills. Some Philippine cities and barangays run composting programs, and the Ecological Solid Waste Management Act of 2000 requires waste to be separated into biodegradable and other types."],["Ugly produce","Fruits and vegetables that are misshapen, scarred or the wrong size are often rejected by shops, even though they taste the same. Some companies now sell such ugly produce at lower prices, and campaigns encourage shoppers to accept imperfect food."],["Restaurants and buffets","Restaurants and buffets can produce large amounts of waste. Some charge diners for uneaten food, offer smaller portions or donate leftovers. Technology such as smart scales helps kitchens measure what they throw away and adjust how much they cook."],["Rice and respect for food","Many cultures teach respect for food. Filipino parents often remind children to finish every grain of rice on their plates, sometimes warning that wasted rice will make the rice cry. Such traditions reflect the hard work of farmers and the value of food in a country where rice is the staple."]],
sp:["The community pantries of 2021","In April 2021, during the COVID-19 lockdowns, a young woman set up a small bamboo cart on Maginhawa Street in Quezon City, filled with vegetables, rice and canned goods. A sign invited people to give what they could and take only what they needed. The idea spread quickly on social media, and within days hundreds of community pantries appeared across the Philippines, in cities, villages and parishes. They helped families struggling with lost incomes and gave farmers and shops a way to share surplus food. The movement became a symbol of the Filipino spirit of bayanihan, or community cooperation."],
g:[["Food waste","Edible food thrown away by shops, restaurants or households"],["Food loss","Food lost between harvest and retail"],["Compost","Decayed organic matter used as fertilizer"],["Best-before date","A label showing when food is at its best quality"]],
f:["About a fifth of consumer food is wasted.","Households waste the most food.","Rotting food in landfills releases methane."],
fun:"If food waste were a country, its greenhouse gas emissions would rank among the highest in the world.",
tl:"Ang food waste ay pagkaing maaari pang kainin ngunit itinatapon. Halos ikalimang bahagi ng pagkain para sa mamimili ang nasasayang sa buong mundo. Nakatutulong ang pagpaplano ng pagkain, wastong pag-iimbak at pagbabahagi ng tira upang mabawasan ito."});

ADDART({id:"ewaste",t:"Electronic Waste",c:"Environment",i:"📱",w:"Electronic waste",r:["recycling","smartphone","plasticwaste","battery","comp"],
p:[["Old gadgets","Electronic waste, or e-waste, is discarded phones, computers, appliances and other electronics."],["Fastest-growing waste","E-waste is one of the fastest-growing kinds of waste in the world."],["Treasure and danger","It contains valuable metals but also toxic substances."]],
l:"Electronic waste, or e-waste, refers to discarded electrical and electronic devices, such as mobile phones, computers, televisions, refrigerators, batteries, chargers and toys. As people buy more devices and replace them more often, e-waste has become one of the fastest-growing types of waste in the world. A United Nations report estimated that 62 million tonnes were produced in 2022, and less than a quarter was properly collected and recycled. E-waste contains valuable materials such as gold, silver, copper and rare metals, but also hazardous substances such as lead, mercury and cadmium. When it is dumped or burned in the open, it can poison soil, water and people. Proper recycling, repair and longer-lasting products are key solutions.",
s:[["What is in e-waste","A smartphone contains dozens of elements, including copper wiring, gold and silver in circuits, cobalt and lithium in batteries and rare earth metals in speakers and motors. Older televisions and monitors contain lead in their glass, and some devices contain mercury, flame retardants and plastics."],["A growing mountain","The amount of e-waste is rising several times faster than the amount properly recycled. Reasons include falling prices of electronics, short product lifetimes, difficulty repairing devices and rapid changes in technology that make older gadgets obsolete."],["Health and environmental dangers","When e-waste is burned to recover copper or dumped in open sites, toxic chemicals can enter the air, soil and water. Informal recyclers, including children in some countries, may be exposed to lead, mercury and smoke, which can harm the brain, lungs and kidneys."],["Urban mining","Recovering metals from e-waste is sometimes called urban mining. A tonne of old phones can contain more gold than a tonne of gold ore. Proper recycling saves resources, reduces the need for new mining and creates jobs, but it requires safe facilities and good collection systems."]],
k:[["E-waste produced (2022)","About 62 million tonnes"],["Properly recycled","Less than a quarter"],["Valuable materials","Gold, silver, copper, rare metals"],["Hazardous materials","Lead, mercury, cadmium"],["Related idea","Right to repair"]],
d:[["1989","The Basel Convention on hazardous waste trade is adopted"],["2003","The European Union introduces rules on electronic waste"],["2014","The first Global E-waste Monitor is published"],["2021","Ghana clears the Agbogbloshie scrapyard, a major informal e-waste site"],["2022","The European Union agrees a common charger for phones and devices"],["2024","The UN reports 62 million tonnes of e-waste produced in 2022"]],
s2:[["Shipping e-waste abroad","For years, large amounts of used electronics from rich countries were shipped to developing countries in Africa and Asia, sometimes labeled as secondhand goods. Much of it ended up dumped or burned. The Basel Convention restricts trade in hazardous waste, and countries have tightened rules, though illegal shipments continue."],["E-waste in the Philippines","The Philippines generates growing amounts of e-waste as more people own phones, computers and appliances. Much is collected by informal junk shops, whose workers dismantle devices by hand. The government regulates hazardous waste under laws on toxic substances and has encouraged accredited recycling facilities and collection drives for old gadgets and batteries."],["The right to repair","Many devices are hard to repair because parts are glued together, spare parts are expensive or manufacturers restrict repair. The right to repair movement campaigns for laws requiring companies to provide parts, tools and manuals. The European Union and several US states have passed right-to-repair rules, while some manufacturers argue that such rules raise safety and security concerns."],["Batteries","Lithium-ion batteries in phones, laptops and electric bikes can catch fire if crushed or damaged in garbage trucks or recycling plants. They should be taken to special collection points. Recycling batteries also recovers valuable metals such as lithium, cobalt and nickel, which are needed for electric vehicles."],["Designing longer-lasting products","Experts encourage companies to design devices that last longer, can be easily repaired and upgraded, and can be recycled at the end of their life. The European Union’s decision to require a common USB-C charger aims to reduce the number of chargers thrown away."],["What you can do","People can reduce e-waste by keeping devices longer, repairing rather than replacing them, buying refurbished products, donating working devices, deleting personal data before passing them on and taking broken electronics and batteries to proper collection points instead of throwing them in household trash."]],
sp:["Medals from old phones","For the Tokyo 2020 Olympic and Paralympic Games, held in 2021, organizers made all of the gold, silver and bronze medals from metals recovered from old electronics. Over two years, people across Japan donated about 79,000 tonnes of used devices, including more than six million mobile phones, at collection boxes in shops and offices. Recyclers extracted gold, silver and copper from the circuits to produce about 5,000 medals. The project showed how valuable discarded electronics can be and encouraged people to recycle their old gadgets instead of leaving them in drawers or throwing them away."],
g:[["E-waste","Discarded electrical and electronic devices"],["Urban mining","Recovering valuable materials from waste such as electronics"],["Right to repair","The idea that people should be able to fix their own devices"],["Basel Convention","An international treaty controlling the movement of hazardous waste"]],
f:["About 62 million tonnes of e-waste were produced in 2022.","Old phones contain gold and silver.","Tokyo 2020 medals were made from old electronics."],
fun:"A tonne of old mobile phones can contain more gold than a tonne of gold ore from a mine.",
tl:"Ang e-waste ay mga itinapong elektronikong kagamitan tulad ng cellphone at kompyuter. Isa ito sa pinakamabilis lumaking uri ng basura sa mundo. May mahahalagang metal ito ngunit may nakalalasong sangkap din, kaya mahalaga ang wastong pag-recycle."});

ADDART({id:"plantbased",t:"Plant-Based Eating",c:"Food and Health",i:"🥗",w:"Plant-based diet",r:["nutri","climchange","foodsecurity","obesity","rice"],
p:[["Eating more plants","Plant-based eating means building meals mainly around vegetables, fruits, grains, beans and nuts."],["Vegetarian and vegan","Some people avoid meat entirely, while others simply eat less of it."],["Why people choose it","Reasons include health, the environment, animal welfare, religion and cost."]],
l:"Plant-based eating refers to diets that are made up mostly or entirely of foods from plants, such as vegetables, fruits, whole grains, legumes, nuts and seeds. It ranges from vegan diets, which exclude all animal products, and vegetarian diets, which exclude meat and fish but may include eggs and dairy, to flexitarian diets that simply include less meat. People choose plant-based eating for health, environmental, ethical, religious and economic reasons. In recent years, plant-based meat and milk alternatives have become widely available in supermarkets and restaurants. Nutrition experts generally agree that eating more plant foods is healthy, while debates continue over processed alternatives, nutrient needs and the role of meat in diets and economies.",
s:[["Kinds of plant-based diets","Vegans eat no animal products at all, including meat, fish, eggs, dairy and honey. Lacto-ovo vegetarians eat dairy and eggs but no meat or fish. Pescatarians eat fish but not other meat. Flexitarians eat mostly plant foods with occasional meat. Many traditional diets around the world are largely plant-based."],["Health","Diets rich in vegetables, fruits, whole grains and legumes are linked to lower risks of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and some cancers. People who avoid all animal products need to plan carefully to get enough protein, iron, calcium, vitamin B12 and other nutrients, sometimes using fortified foods or supplements."],["The environment","Producing meat, especially beef, generally uses more land and water and produces more greenhouse gases than producing plant foods. Scientists have suggested that eating more plants could reduce the environmental impact of food systems. Others note that grazing animals can use land unsuited to crops and support livelihoods."],["Plant-based alternatives","Companies now make burgers, sausages, chicken, milk and cheese from soy, peas, wheat, oats, coconut and other plants, designed to taste like animal products. Some consumers welcome them, while others prefer whole foods and question highly processed products. Scientists are also developing cultivated meat grown from animal cells."]],
k:[["No animal products","Vegan"],["No meat or fish","Vegetarian"],["Mostly plants, some meat","Flexitarian"],["Key nutrient for vegans","Vitamin B12"],["First cultivated meat approval","Singapore, 2020"]],
d:[["1847","The Vegetarian Society is founded in England"],["1944","The word vegan is coined by Donald Watson"],["2009","Beyond Meat is founded"],["2016","The Impossible Burger is launched"],["2019","A major scientific report proposes a planetary health diet rich in plants"],["2020","Singapore becomes the first country to approve the sale of cultivated meat"]],
s2:[["Plant foods in Filipino cuisine","Many Filipino dishes are built around vegetables, legumes and coconut, such as pinakbet, laing, ginataang gulay, monggo stew, tortang talong, ensaladang mangga and ginataang langka. Tofu, called tokwa or tahu, has long been part of the diet through Chinese influence. Some dishes include small amounts of meat or fish sauce for flavor, but can be adapted."],["Religion and tradition","Many religious traditions encourage plant-based eating. Hindus, Jains and many Buddhists follow vegetarian diets, and some Christian groups, such as Seventh-day Adventists, promote vegetarianism. Catholics traditionally avoid meat on certain days, such as Fridays during Lent, when many Filipino families eat fish and vegetable dishes."],["Cost and access","Beans, rice, vegetables and root crops are often cheaper than meat, making plant-based meals affordable. However, specialty products such as plant-based meat substitutes can be expensive, and fresh vegetables may be hard to find or costly in some places. Food prices shape what people can choose."],["The planetary health diet","In 2019 a group of scientists known as the EAT–Lancet Commission proposed a diet designed to be healthy for people and sustainable for the planet. It recommended mainly plant foods, with modest amounts of fish, poultry and dairy and small amounts of red meat. Supporters see it as a useful guide, while critics argue it may not fit all cultures, budgets or nutritional needs."],["Debates over meat","Livestock farming supports millions of farmers worldwide, including backyard hog and poultry raisers in the Philippines. Some people argue for reducing meat consumption for environmental reasons, while others defend meat as nutritious, culturally important and economically vital. Many experts suggest that the choice is personal and that balance and moderation are key."],["Getting enough nutrients","People eating fully plant-based diets can meet their needs with careful planning. Beans, lentils, tofu and nuts provide protein; leafy greens such as malunggay provide iron and calcium; and vitamin B12, found naturally mainly in animal foods, can come from fortified foods or supplements. Children, pregnant women and older people may need special advice."]],
sp:["The miracle tree in the backyard","In many Filipino backyards grows a slender tree with feathery leaves: malunggay, or moringa. Its small leaves are rich in vitamins A and C, calcium, iron and protein, and are added to tinola, monggo and many other dishes. Malunggay grows quickly, tolerates drought and needs little care, making it a valuable source of nutrition for families. It has been promoted in school feeding programs and is now sold worldwide as a powder in health food stores. For many Filipinos, though, it is simply a familiar leaf picked fresh from the yard for the day’s soup."],
g:[["Vegan","A diet without any animal products"],["Vegetarian","A diet without meat or fish"],["Flexitarian","A mainly plant-based diet that sometimes includes meat"],["Cultivated meat","Meat grown from animal cells in a laboratory"]],
f:["The word vegan was coined in 1944.","Many Filipino dishes are vegetable-based.","Malunggay is rich in nutrients."],
fun:"Singapore became the first country to allow cultivated chicken, grown from animal cells, to be sold to diners in 2020.",
tl:"Ang plant-based eating ay pagkain na karamihan ay mula sa halaman tulad ng gulay, prutas, butil at munggo. May mga hindi kumakain ng karne at may mga nagbabawas lamang nito. Pinipili ito dahil sa kalusugan, kapaligiran, relihiyon at gastos."});

ADDART({id:"fourdayweek",t:"The Four-Day Week",c:"Economics and Society",i:"📅",w:"Four-day week",r:["remotework","gigeconomy","automation","mentalyouth","labor"],
p:[["Working fewer days","A four-day week means working four days instead of five, usually for the same pay."],["Trials around the world","Companies and governments in several countries have tested it."],["Debated benefits","Supporters cite wellbeing and productivity; critics raise costs and practicality."]],
l:"The four-day week is a work arrangement in which employees work four days a week instead of the usual five. In the version most discussed today, workers keep their full pay while working fewer total hours, often described as 100 percent pay for 80 percent of the time in exchange for maintaining productivity. Another version, the compressed workweek, keeps the same total hours spread over four longer days. Trials in Iceland, the United Kingdom, Spain, Japan and elsewhere have reported benefits such as less stress and burnout with productivity maintained, and many participating companies kept the arrangement. Critics argue that it does not suit every job, may raise costs and that results from voluntary trials may not apply everywhere.",
s:[["Two kinds of four-day weeks","In a reduced-hours four-day week, staff work around 32 hours instead of 40 for the same pay. In a compressed workweek, they work the same 40 hours in four days of about ten hours each. Both give a three-day weekend, but they have different effects on workers and employers."],["How the five-day week began","For much of history people worked six or seven days a week. In the 20th century, labor movements and some employers, such as the car maker Henry Ford in 1926, adopted a five-day, 40-hour week, which became standard in many countries. Some argue that, with modern technology, it is time to shorten the week again."],["What trials found","In a large trial in the United Kingdom in 2022, involving about 60 companies, most firms reported that productivity held steady while employees reported less stress, burnout and sick leave, and the majority continued the four-day week afterward. Trials in Iceland between 2015 and 2019 involving public sector workers reported similar results."],["Concerns","Critics say a four-day week may be difficult in jobs requiring constant coverage, such as hospitals, shops, factories and emergency services, and may increase costs if extra staff are needed. Some workers find longer days in compressed weeks tiring. Economists note that voluntary trials may attract companies most likely to succeed."]],
k:[["Common formula","100% pay, 80% time, 100% output"],["Five-day week popularized","1920s and 1930s"],["Large UK trial","2022"],["Iceland trials","2015–2019"],["Alternative","Compressed workweek"]],
d:[["1926","Henry Ford adopts a five-day week in his factories"],["1938","The United States sets a 44-hour, later 40-hour, standard workweek"],["2015","Iceland begins trials of shorter working weeks"],["2019","Microsoft Japan reports productivity gains in a four-day week trial"],["2022","About 60 British companies take part in a large four-day week trial"],["2022","Belgium gives workers the right to request a compressed four-day week"]],
s2:[["Wellbeing and burnout","Supporters argue that a longer weekend gives workers more time to rest, exercise, spend time with family, care for children and older relatives, and pursue hobbies and learning. Trials have reported improvements in mental health, sleep and job satisfaction. Some companies say it helps them attract and keep staff."],["Productivity","Supporters argue that people often waste time in long meetings and on distractions, and that a shorter week encourages focus. Some trials reported that output stayed the same or improved. Skeptics say productivity gains can be hard to measure in many jobs and may fade over time."],["Work in the Philippines","The standard workweek in the Philippines is generally 48 hours over six days or 40 hours over five days, depending on the employer. The Department of Labor and Employment has allowed compressed workweeks by agreement between employers and workers, and the Civil Service Commission has allowed compressed schedules in some government offices, including during periods of high fuel prices. Long commutes in Metro Manila make shorter weeks attractive to many workers."],["Who benefits?","Office workers may find a four-day week easier to adopt than people in shift work, farming, retail or the informal economy, where most Filipinos and many workers worldwide earn their living. Some critics worry that the four-day week could widen gaps between different kinds of workers."],["Governments and the four-day week","Some governments have encouraged experiments. Belgium allows workers to request a compressed four-day week, and local governments in Japan, Spain and elsewhere have tested shorter weeks for public employees. Others remain cautious about economic effects."],["The future of work","The four-day week is part of wider changes in how people work, along with remote and hybrid work, flexible hours and automation. Economist John Maynard Keynes predicted in 1930 that his grandchildren’s generation might work only 15 hours a week. Whether shorter weeks become widespread is still an open question."]],
sp:["Keynes’s 15-hour week","In 1930, during the Great Depression, the British economist John Maynard Keynes wrote an essay imagining life a hundred years later. He predicted that technology and rising productivity would make people so much richer that by around 2030 they might need to work only about 15 hours a week, and that the main challenge would be how to use their free time well. Productivity did rise enormously, but working hours fell much less than he expected. Economists still debate why, pointing to rising consumption, inequality and changing kinds of work. The four-day week movement often cites Keynes’s prediction as an inspiration."],
g:[["Four-day week","A schedule of four working days a week"],["Compressed workweek","The usual hours worked in fewer, longer days"],["Productivity","The amount of work produced in a given time"],["Burnout","Exhaustion caused by long-term work stress"]],
f:["Henry Ford adopted a five-day week in 1926.","About 60 UK companies tested a four-day week in 2022.","Keynes predicted a 15-hour week."],
fun:"In 1930 economist John Maynard Keynes predicted that people would work only about 15 hours a week by 2030.",
tl:"Ang four-day week ay pagtatrabaho nang apat na araw sa halip na lima, kadalasan nang may parehong sahod. Sinubukan ito sa ilang bansa at iniulat na nabawasan ang stress habang napanatili ang produktibidad. May mga kritiko na nagsasabing hindi ito angkop sa lahat ng trabaho."});

ADDART({id:"ubi",t:"Universal Basic Income",c:"Economics and Society",i:"💵",w:"Universal basic income",r:["inequality","automation","costliving","money","sdgs"],
p:[["Money for everyone","Universal basic income is a regular cash payment given to every person, with no conditions."],["Experiments worldwide","Pilot programs have been tested in Finland, Kenya, the United States and elsewhere."],["A lively debate","Supporters see it as a safety net; critics worry about cost and work incentives."]],
l:"Universal basic income, or UBI, is the idea that a government should give every adult, or every person, a regular cash payment that is enough to cover basic needs, without conditions such as working or proving need. Supporters argue that UBI would reduce poverty, give people security as jobs change because of automation, simplify welfare systems and respect individual freedom. Critics argue that it would be extremely expensive, might discourage work, could raise taxes or prices and may be less effective than targeted help for the poorest. No country has introduced a full national UBI, but pilot programs and related cash transfer schemes, including conditional cash transfers such as the Philippines’ Pantawid Pamilyang Pilipino Program, have provided evidence for the debate.",
s:[["Key features","A true universal basic income has several features: it is paid to individuals rather than households, in cash, regularly, to everyone regardless of income, and without requirements to work or look for work. Related ideas include guaranteed income for people below a certain level and negative income taxes."],["History of the idea","Thinkers have proposed versions of basic income for centuries. In 1797 the writer Thomas Paine suggested giving every citizen a payment funded by land taxes. In the 20th century, economists from different political traditions, including Milton Friedman, supported versions such as a negative income tax."],["Why supporters back it","Supporters say a basic income would give everyone a floor below which they cannot fall, reduce poverty and stress, allow people to study, care for family, start businesses or find better jobs, and prepare societies for automation and artificial intelligence. Some argue it would reduce the cost and stigma of complicated welfare programs."],["Why critics oppose it","Critics say giving money to everyone, including the rich, is costly and inefficient, and that money would be better targeted at those in need. They worry it could reduce incentives to work, require high taxes or cuts to other services, and cause inflation. Some argue that work gives people dignity and purpose beyond income."]],
k:[["Short name","UBI"],["Main features","Universal, unconditional, regular cash"],["Early proposal","Thomas Paine, 1797"],["Long-running dividend","Alaska Permanent Fund, since 1982"],["Philippine cash transfer program","Pantawid Pamilyang Pilipino Program (4Ps)"]],
d:[["1797","Thomas Paine proposes payments funded by land taxes"],["1982","Alaska begins paying an annual dividend to residents from oil revenues"],["2008","The Philippines launches the Pantawid Pamilyang Pilipino Program"],["2016","Kenya-based long-term basic income experiments are announced"],["2017","Finland begins a two-year basic income experiment"],["2019","The 4Ps is made permanent by Philippine law"]],
s2:[["Finland’s experiment","From 2017 to 2018, Finland gave 2,000 unemployed people a monthly payment of 560 euros with no conditions, even if they found work. The results showed that recipients reported better wellbeing and less stress, while employment rose slightly compared with others. Researchers debated how much the findings would apply to a full national program."],["Kenya’s long-term study","The charity GiveDirectly began a large, long-term study in rural Kenya in which thousands of people receive regular cash payments, some for twelve years. Early results suggested people invested in businesses, food and homes rather than wasting the money, and that cash helped communities cope with shocks such as the COVID-19 pandemic."],["Alaska’s dividend","Since 1982, every resident of Alaska, including children, has received a yearly payment from a fund built from the state’s oil revenues. The amount varies each year, usually from several hundred to over a thousand US dollars. It is not enough to live on but is often cited as a real-world example of a universal payment."],["The 4Ps and conditional cash","The Philippines’ Pantawid Pamilyang Pilipino Program, launched in 2008 and made permanent by law in 2019, gives cash grants to poor families on condition that children attend school and receive health checkups. It is not universal or unconditional, but studies have found it improved school attendance and health care use. Debates continue over targeting, conditions and how families leave the program."],["Pandemic payments","During the COVID-19 pandemic, many governments, including the Philippines through its Social Amelioration Program, sent emergency cash to millions of households. These payments renewed interest in basic income and showed both the value and the challenges of delivering cash quickly to large numbers of people."],["Automation and the future","Some technology leaders and economists argue that artificial intelligence and robots may replace many jobs, making a basic income necessary. Others believe new kinds of jobs will appear, as in past technological revolutions, and that education and training are better responses. The debate is likely to continue as technology changes work."]],
sp:["Paine’s idea of a fair share","In 1797, the political writer Thomas Paine, famous for supporting the American and French revolutions, published a pamphlet called Agrarian Justice. He argued that the Earth was originally the common property of all people, and that landowners owed society a payment for the land they held. With this money, he proposed, every person reaching the age of 21 should receive a lump sum to help them start adult life, and every person over 50 should receive a yearly pension. More than two centuries later, supporters of universal basic income often point to Paine as one of the first to suggest that everyone should share in society’s wealth."],
g:[["Universal basic income","A regular cash payment to everyone with no conditions"],["Conditional cash transfer","Cash given to families who meet certain conditions"],["Pilot program","A small trial to test an idea"],["Safety net","Programs that protect people from falling into poverty"]],
f:["No country has a full national UBI.","Finland tested basic income in 2017 and 2018.","The Philippines’ 4Ps is a conditional cash transfer."],
fun:"Every resident of Alaska, including newborn babies, receives a yearly payment from the state’s oil fund.",
tl:"Ang universal basic income ay regular na halagang ibinibigay sa bawat tao nang walang kondisyon. Sinubukan ito sa Finland, Kenya at iba pang lugar. Ang 4Ps ng Pilipinas naman ay conditional cash transfer na may kondisyon tulad ng pagpasok ng mga bata sa paaralan."});

ADDART({id:"digitalid",t:"Digital and National IDs",c:"Technology and Society",i:"🪪",w:"Philippine Identification System",r:["dataprivacy","digitalpay","cybersecurity","onlinescams","digitaldivide"],
p:[["Proving who you are","National ID systems give people an official way to prove their identity."],["The Philippine system","The Philippine Identification System began registering Filipinos in 2018."],["Benefits and risks","Digital IDs make services easier but raise privacy and security concerns."]],
l:"A national identity system gives residents of a country an official way to prove who they are, often with an ID card and, increasingly, a digital identity that can be checked online. Having proof of identity helps people open bank accounts, receive government aid, register for school, vote, travel and use health services. Around the world, hundreds of millions of people still lack official identification. Countries such as India, Estonia and Singapore have built large digital ID systems. In the Philippines, the Philippine Identification System Act of 2018 created PhilSys, which has registered tens of millions of Filipinos and issues the PhilID card and a digital version. Digital IDs bring convenience and inclusion but also raise concerns about privacy, data security and exclusion.",
s:[["Why identity matters","Without proof of identity, people may be unable to open a bank account, get a SIM card, receive social benefits, enroll children in school or claim property. The World Bank has estimated that hundreds of millions of people worldwide lack official ID, most of them in poorer countries. The Sustainable Development Goals include providing legal identity for all, including birth registration."],["How digital IDs work","A digital ID system usually stores basic information, such as name, birth date and address, along with biometrics such as fingerprints, iris scans or a photograph. Each person receives a unique number. Services can verify identity by checking the number and biometrics, or by scanning a QR code on an ID card or phone."],["PhilSys","The Philippine Identification System Act, signed in 2018, established a single national ID for all citizens and resident foreigners, managed by the Philippine Statistics Authority. Registration began in 2018 and expanded nationwide from 2020. People receive a PhilSys number and a PhilID card, and a printable or mobile digital version, the ePhilID and later the digital National ID, was introduced."],["Using the national ID","The PhilID is meant to be accepted as valid proof of identity for government and private transactions, such as opening bank accounts, applying for benefits and registering SIM cards. It aims to replace the need for multiple IDs and help people without other documents, especially the poor, access services."]],
k:[["Philippine law","Philippine Identification System Act, 2018"],["Managing agency","Philippine Statistics Authority"],["Card","PhilID"],["Registered","More than 80 million Filipinos"],["Large digital ID example","India’s Aadhaar"]],
d:[["2002","Estonia introduces a national ID card with digital signatures"],["2009","India launches Aadhaar, which becomes the world’s largest biometric ID system"],["2018","The Philippine Identification System Act is signed"],["2020","PhilSys registration expands nationwide"],["2022","The printable ePhilID is introduced"],["2025","More than 80 million Filipinos are confirmed as registered after duplicate checks"]],
s2:[["India’s Aadhaar","India’s Aadhaar system, launched in 2009, has given unique identity numbers based on fingerprints and iris scans to well over a billion people. It is used for welfare payments, bank accounts and phone connections. Supporters say it reduced fraud and helped millions access services; critics raised concerns about privacy, data leaks and people excluded when authentication failed. India’s Supreme Court upheld the system in 2018 but limited some of its uses."],["Estonia’s e-government","Estonia, a small country in northern Europe, issues digital ID cards that let citizens sign documents, vote online, file taxes and access health records. Almost all government services can be used online. Estonia’s system is often cited as a model, though it also faced a security flaw in its ID cards in 2017 that required millions of cards to be updated."],["Privacy and security","Because national ID systems hold sensitive personal and biometric data for millions of people, they are attractive targets for hackers and could be misused. Privacy advocates call for strong legal protections, limits on who can access data and independent oversight. The Philippines’ Data Privacy Act of 2012 and the PhilSys law include rules on data protection and penalties for misuse."],["Challenges in rollout","PhilSys faced delays in printing and delivering physical cards, and some people had problems with registration or with banks and offices that did not yet accept the ID. The digital version was introduced partly to address delays. People in remote areas, older people and those without phones or internet may face difficulties using digital IDs."],["Inclusion","Supporters say national IDs especially help poor and marginalized people, including those without birth certificates, to prove their identity and receive aid, open bank accounts and access digital payments. Critics warn that if services require digital ID, people who cannot register or authenticate may be left out."],["Digital wallets for IDs","Governments are developing ways to store IDs in mobile phone apps, along with driver’s licenses, health records and certificates. These can be convenient but depend on secure phones and reliable internet. Experts recommend keeping alternative options so that no one is excluded."]],
sp:["An ID for the unbanked","Before PhilSys, many Filipinos, especially in poor and remote communities, struggled to open bank accounts because they lacked the two valid IDs banks often required. Some had no birth certificate or had never held a government ID. During PhilSys registration drives, the Philippine Statistics Authority partnered with a government bank so that registrants could open a basic bank account on the spot. Millions of accounts were opened this way, helping people receive government aid, save money and use digital payments. Supporters saw this as one of the system’s clearest benefits for financial inclusion."],
g:[["National ID","An official identity document issued by a government"],["Biometrics","Physical traits such as fingerprints used to identify people"],["PhilSys","The Philippine Identification System"],["Authentication","Checking that a person is who they claim to be"]],
f:["PhilSys was created by law in 2018.","More than 80 million Filipinos have registered.","India’s Aadhaar is the largest biometric ID system."],
fun:"In Estonia, citizens have been able to vote online using their digital ID cards since 2005.",
tl:"Ang national ID ay opisyal na paraan upang patunayan ang pagkakakilanlan ng isang tao. Sa Pilipinas, nilikha ng batas noong 2018 ang PhilSys at mahigit 80 milyong Pilipino na ang nakarehistro. Pinadadali nito ang mga serbisyo ngunit may alalahanin sa privacy at seguridad."});

;
/* data/space10.js */
// Space, more articles: binary stars, radio astronomy, star clusters, Sputnik and the Rosetta comet mission.
ADDART({id:"binarystars",t:"Binary Stars",c:"Astronomy",i:"✨",w:"Binary star",r:["stars","exoplanet","pulsar","supernova","gravwaves"],
p:[["Stars in pairs","A binary star is a pair of stars that orbit each other."],["Very common","Many stars, perhaps around half of Sun-like stars, have at least one companion."],["Measuring stars","Binary stars let astronomers measure the masses of stars."]],
l:"A binary star system consists of two stars bound together by gravity, orbiting a shared center of mass. Systems with three or more stars are called multiple stars. Binary and multiple stars are very common: many stars in our galaxy, including a large share of stars similar to the Sun, have at least one companion. Some binaries are far apart and take thousands of years to complete an orbit, while others are so close that they share gas or even touch. Binary stars are very important to astronomers, because watching how the two stars move lets scientists calculate their masses. Close binaries can produce dramatic events such as novae, some supernovae and the gravitational waves made when two dead stars merge.",
s:[["How binaries are found","Some binaries can be seen as two separate stars through a telescope; these are visual binaries. Others are too close to separate, but their light shows periodic shifts caused by their motion, revealing spectroscopic binaries. In eclipsing binaries, one star passes in front of the other as seen from Earth, causing regular dips in brightness."],["Measuring the masses of stars","Using the laws of gravity worked out by Johannes Kepler and Isaac Newton, astronomers can calculate the masses of the two stars from the size and period of their orbit. Binary stars provided the first reliable measurements of stellar masses, which are essential for understanding how stars live and die."],["Nearby examples","The nearest star system to the Sun, Alpha Centauri, is a triple system: two Sun-like stars, Alpha Centauri A and B, orbit each other, and a small red dwarf, Proxima Centauri, orbits farther away. Sirius, the brightest star in the night sky, has a faint white dwarf companion called Sirius B."],["Planets with two suns","Planets can orbit binary stars. In 2011 NASA’s Kepler telescope found Kepler-16b, a planet orbiting two stars, which reminded many people of the fictional desert world with two suns in the Star Wars films. Astronomers have since found other such circumbinary planets."]],
k:[["Definition","Two stars orbiting a common center"],["Nearest multiple system","Alpha Centauri (three stars)"],["Brightest star’s companion","Sirius B"],["Famous eclipsing binary","Algol"],["First planet found orbiting two stars","Kepler-16b, 2011"]],
d:[["1650","Giovanni Riccioli reports that the star Mizar is double"],["1782","John Goodricke explains Algol’s dimming as an eclipsing binary"],["1803","William Herschel shows that some double stars orbit each other"],["1862","Alvan Graham Clark sees Sirius B, the companion of Sirius"],["2011","Kepler-16b is found orbiting two stars"],["2015","Gravitational waves from two merging black holes are detected"]],
s2:[["The demon star","Algol, in the constellation Perseus, dims noticeably every few days. Its name comes from Arabic words meaning the demon’s head. In 1782 a young deaf English astronomer, John Goodricke, suggested that Algol dims because a darker companion star regularly passes in front of it. He was right; Algol was one of the first eclipsing binaries understood."],["Herschel’s discovery","In the late 1700s the astronomer William Herschel carefully measured the positions of many double stars, hoping to use them to measure distances. Instead, by 1803 he found that some pairs had changed their positions because they were orbiting each other, showing that Newton’s law of gravity applied beyond the solar system."],["Stars that share gas","In some close binaries, one star swells up as it ages and spills gas onto its companion. If the companion is a white dwarf, the gas can build up and ignite in a nova, a sudden brightening. If too much gas piles on, the white dwarf can explode completely as a type of supernova, which astronomers use to measure distances across the universe."],["Colliding dead stars","When both stars in a binary end their lives as neutron stars or black holes, they can slowly spiral together and merge, sending out ripples in space-time called gravitational waves. In 2017 detectors recorded two neutron stars colliding, and telescopes saw the explosion, which produced heavy elements such as gold."],["Albireo","Albireo, in the constellation Cygnus, is one of the most beautiful double stars in a small telescope: a golden-yellow star beside a blue companion. Amateur astronomers enjoy observing such colorful pairs, though recent measurements suggest Albireo’s two stars may not be orbiting each other but merely lined up by chance."],["Why binaries matter","Because so many stars are in binaries, understanding them is essential for understanding how stars form and evolve. Many stars are born in pairs or groups from the same collapsing cloud of gas, and interactions between companions can change how long they live and how they die."]],
sp:["Two sunsets","Imagine standing on a planet orbiting two stars. Each evening, two suns would set, one after the other, casting double shadows that change shape as the stars move. Such a scene appears in the Star Wars films on the planet Tatooine, and for years it was only science fiction. Then in 2011 astronomers studying data from NASA’s Kepler space telescope announced Kepler-16b, a cold, Saturn-sized planet orbiting a pair of stars about 245 light-years away. Since then, more planets with two suns have been discovered, showing that such worlds are real, though none yet known is like Earth."],
g:[["Binary star","Two stars orbiting each other"],["Eclipsing binary","A binary in which one star passes in front of the other"],["Center of mass","The balance point around which two objects orbit"],["Nova","A sudden brightening caused by gas igniting on a white dwarf"]],
f:["Alpha Centauri is a triple star system.","Sirius has a white dwarf companion.","Kepler-16b orbits two stars."],
fun:"Algol’s name comes from Arabic for the demon’s head, perhaps because ancient observers noticed it dimming like a winking eye.",
tl:"Ang binary star ay dalawang bituin na umiikot sa isa’t isa. Napakarami nito sa ating galaxy. Sa pamamagitan ng binary star, nasusukat ng mga astronomo ang masa ng mga bituin."});

ADDART({id:"radioastronomy",t:"Radio Astronomy",c:"Astronomy",i:"📡",w:"Radio astronomy",r:["scope","pulsar","cmb","bh","radio"],
p:[["Listening to space","Radio astronomy studies the universe by detecting radio waves from space."],["Giant dishes","Radio telescopes use huge dishes or arrays of antennas."],["Big discoveries","Radio astronomers discovered pulsars and the afterglow of the Big Bang."]],
l:"Radio astronomy is the study of objects in space through the radio waves they give off. Radio waves are a form of light with much longer wavelengths than visible light, and they can pass through dust and clouds that block ordinary telescopes. Radio astronomy began in the 1930s when Karl Jansky detected radio noise coming from the center of the Milky Way. Since then, radio telescopes, from single giant dishes to networks of antennas spread across continents, have revealed pulsars, quasars, the cosmic microwave background left over from the Big Bang, cold gas between stars and the first images of black holes. Major projects today include China’s FAST telescope and the Square Kilometre Array.",
s:[["Radio waves from space","Many objects in space give off radio waves, including the Sun, Jupiter, clouds of gas, exploding stars, pulsars and the regions around black holes. Radio waves can pass through dust that hides the center of our galaxy, so radio telescopes can see places that optical telescopes cannot."],["How radio telescopes work","A radio telescope usually has a large curved dish that collects faint radio waves and focuses them onto a receiver, like a giant satellite dish. The signals are amplified and recorded by computers. Because radio waves are so long, very large dishes are needed to see fine detail."],["Telescopes working together","Astronomers combine signals from many antennas spread over large distances, a technique called interferometry. The farther apart the antennas, the sharper the images. Arrays such as the Very Large Array in the United States and ALMA in Chile’s Atacama Desert use dozens of dishes working together."],["Imaging black holes","The Event Horizon Telescope links radio observatories around the world to act like a telescope the size of Earth. In 2019 it released the first image of a black hole, at the center of the galaxy M87, showing a bright ring around a dark shadow. In 2022 it revealed the black hole at the center of the Milky Way."]],
k:[["Founded by","Karl Jansky, 1930s"],["Largest single dish","FAST, China, 500 meters"],["First black hole image","Event Horizon Telescope, 2019"],["Pulsars discovered","1967"],["Future giant","Square Kilometre Array"]],
d:[["1932","Karl Jansky detects radio waves from the Milky Way"],["1937","Grote Reber builds the first dish-shaped radio telescope in his backyard"],["1965","Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson report the cosmic microwave background"],["1967","Jocelyn Bell Burnell discovers the first pulsar"],["2016","China completes the 500-meter FAST telescope"],["2019","The Event Horizon Telescope releases the first image of a black hole"]],
s2:[["Jansky’s static","In the early 1930s Karl Jansky, an engineer at Bell Telephone Laboratories, built a rotating antenna to track down sources of radio static that disturbed transatlantic telephone calls. He found a faint hiss that rose and fell once a day, and realized it came from the direction of the center of the Milky Way. It was the first detection of radio waves from beyond the solar system."],["The afterglow of the Big Bang","In 1964 and 1965 Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson, testing a radio antenna in New Jersey, found a faint background noise coming from every direction. After ruling out other causes, even cleaning pigeon droppings from the antenna, they learned it was the cosmic microwave background, leftover radiation from the early universe. They received the Nobel Prize in 1978."],["Pulsars","In 1967 Jocelyn Bell Burnell, a graduate student in Cambridge, noticed a strange, regular pulse in her radio telescope data, repeating every 1.3 seconds. The source was a pulsar, a rapidly spinning neutron star sweeping a beam of radio waves across space like a lighthouse. Her supervisor shared the 1974 Nobel Prize, an omission many have since criticized."],["Arecibo and FAST","For decades the largest single-dish radio telescope was the 305-meter Arecibo telescope in Puerto Rico, used to study pulsars, planets and asteroids and to send a famous radio message into space in 1974. It collapsed in 2020 after cable failures. China’s FAST telescope, 500 meters across, built in a natural limestone basin in Guizhou, is now the largest."],["Searching for signals","Radio telescopes are also used to search for signals from possible intelligent life elsewhere, an effort known as SETI. No confirmed signal has been found. In 1977 a telescope in Ohio recorded a strong, unexplained signal, nicknamed the Wow! signal after an astronomer wrote Wow! beside it on a printout; it was never detected again."],["Quiet places","Radio telescopes are so sensitive that phones, microwave ovens and other electronics can interfere with them. Many are built in remote areas, such as deserts and valleys, with radio quiet zones where certain devices are restricted. The Square Kilometre Array, under construction in South Africa and Australia, uses remote sites for this reason."]],
sp:["The pulse that seemed artificial","When Jocelyn Bell Burnell first noticed a regular pulse in her data in 1967, she and her supervisor, Antony Hewish, were puzzled. The signal ticked like a clock, every 1.33 seconds, more regularly than anything known in nature. Half-jokingly, they labeled it LGM-1, for little green men, wondering whether it could be a signal from aliens. When Bell Burnell found a second pulsing source elsewhere in the sky, it became clear that a natural explanation was more likely. The sources turned out to be pulsars, spinning neutron stars, one of the most important astronomical discoveries of the 20th century."],
g:[["Radio telescope","A telescope that detects radio waves from space"],["Interferometry","Combining signals from several telescopes to make sharper images"],["Pulsar","A spinning neutron star that sends out beams of radio waves"],["Cosmic microwave background","Faint radiation left over from the early universe"]],
f:["Radio astronomy began in the 1930s.","FAST in China is 500 meters across.","The first black hole image was made with radio telescopes."],
fun:"The first pulsar signal was jokingly nicknamed LGM-1, for little green men, because it ticked so regularly.",
tl:"Ang radio astronomy ay pag-aaral ng kalawakan sa pamamagitan ng radio waves mula rito. Gumagamit ito ng malalaking dish o network ng antena. Natuklasan sa pamamagitan nito ang mga pulsar at ang liwanag na naiwan ng Big Bang."});

ADDART({id:"starclusters",t:"Star Clusters",c:"Astronomy",i:"🌟",w:"Star cluster",r:["stars","milky","constellations","galaxies","nebula"],
p:[["Families of stars","A star cluster is a group of stars born together and held by gravity."],["Open and globular","Clusters come in two main types: loose open clusters and dense globular clusters."],["The Pleiades","The Pleiades, or Seven Sisters, is a famous open cluster visible without a telescope."]],
l:"A star cluster is a group of stars that formed together from the same cloud of gas and dust and are held together, at least for a time, by their mutual gravity. There are two main kinds. Open clusters, found in the disk of the Milky Way, contain dozens to a few thousand young stars loosely grouped together, such as the Pleiades. Globular clusters are dense, ball-shaped swarms of hundreds of thousands to millions of very old stars that orbit around the center of the galaxy. Because the stars in a cluster are about the same age and distance, clusters are natural laboratories that help astronomers understand how stars are born, age and die, and how old the universe is.",
s:[["Open clusters","Open clusters are loose groups of young stars, often only tens or hundreds of millions of years old. They lie in the spiral arms of the Milky Way, where new stars are forming. Over time, the gravity of passing clouds and stars pulls them apart, so their stars drift away to join the general population of the galaxy, as the Sun probably did long ago."],["Globular clusters","Globular clusters are tightly packed spheres of stars, some containing millions of members within a region about a hundred light-years across. Their stars are among the oldest in the galaxy, often more than ten billion years old. The Milky Way has about 150 known globular clusters, found mostly in a halo surrounding the galaxy."],["Clusters you can see","Several clusters are visible to the naked eye. The Pleiades, in Taurus, looks like a tiny dipper of stars. The Hyades form the V-shaped face of Taurus. Omega Centauri, the largest globular cluster of the Milky Way, appears as a fuzzy star in southern skies and can be seen from the Philippines."],["Clues to stellar life","Because cluster stars are born at the same time but have different masses, they show how stars of different sizes age. Massive stars burn out first, while smaller ones live longer. By seeing which stars have aged, astronomers can estimate a cluster’s age, which helped set limits on the age of the universe."]],
k:[["Main types","Open and globular"],["Famous open cluster","Pleiades (Seven Sisters)"],["Largest Milky Way globular cluster","Omega Centauri"],["Globular clusters in the Milky Way","About 150"],["Typical globular cluster age","Over 10 billion years"]],
d:[["c. 1000 BC","The Pleiades are mentioned in ancient texts and myths"],["1610","Galileo sees many more stars in the Pleiades through his telescope"],["1665","The globular cluster M22 is discovered"],["1764","Charles Messier begins cataloging clusters and nebulae"],["1918","Harlow Shapley uses globular clusters to locate the center of the Milky Way"],["2010s","The Gaia spacecraft maps cluster stars in unprecedented detail"]],
s2:[["The Seven Sisters","The Pleiades have been known since ancient times and appear in the myths of many cultures. In Greek mythology they were seven sisters, daughters of the titan Atlas. In Japan they are called Subaru, the name of a car company whose logo shows the cluster. Many Pacific and Southeast Asian peoples used the rising of the Pleiades to mark seasons for planting and fishing."],["Finding the galaxy’s center","In 1918 the American astronomer Harlow Shapley studied the distances of globular clusters and found that they formed a sphere centered on a point far from the Sun, in the direction of Sagittarius. He concluded that the Sun is not at the center of the Milky Way but far out in its disk, a major change in our view of our place in the galaxy."],["Messier’s catalog","In the 1700s the French astronomer Charles Messier, a comet hunter, made a list of fuzzy objects that could be confused with comets. His catalog of about 110 objects includes many star clusters, such as M13 in Hercules and M45, the Pleiades, as well as nebulae and galaxies. Amateur astronomers still use Messier numbers today."],["Omega Centauri","Omega Centauri contains millions of stars and is the largest and brightest globular cluster of the Milky Way. Some astronomers think it may be the core of a small galaxy that was swallowed by the Milky Way long ago. In 2024 researchers reported evidence of a medium-sized black hole at its center."],["Clusters and star birth","Most stars, including the Sun, are thought to be born in clusters within giant clouds of gas and dust. Young clusters, such as those in the Orion Nebula, can be seen still surrounded by the glowing gas from which they formed. Studying them helps scientists understand how planetary systems like ours begin."],["Sending messages to a cluster","In 1974 the Arecibo radio telescope beamed a message toward the globular cluster M13, about 25,000 light-years away, describing human beings, DNA and our solar system in a simple picture code. It was mainly a demonstration; the message will take about 25,000 years to arrive, and by then the cluster will have moved."]],
sp:["Stars that mark the seasons","For many peoples around the world, the appearance of the Pleiades in the sky was a calendar. In parts of the Philippines, farmers traditionally watched for the cluster before dawn to know when to clear fields and plant rice. Some Filipino peoples have names for it in their own languages, and similar traditions exist in Indonesia, Malaysia and the Pacific islands, where navigators and fishers watched its rising and setting. In New Zealand, the Māori celebrate Matariki, the rising of the Pleiades in midwinter, as their new year, now a national public holiday."],
g:[["Star cluster","A group of stars born together and bound by gravity"],["Open cluster","A loose group of young stars"],["Globular cluster","A dense ball of very old stars"],["Messier catalog","A list of about 110 clusters, nebulae and galaxies"]],
f:["The Pleiades are called the Seven Sisters.","Omega Centauri contains millions of stars.","Globular clusters are among the oldest things in the galaxy."],
fun:"The logo of the Subaru car company shows the stars of the Pleiades, which are called Subaru in Japanese.",
tl:"Ang star cluster ay grupo ng mga bituing sabay na isinilang at magkakasama dahil sa grabidad. May dalawang pangunahing uri: open cluster at globular cluster. Ang Pleiades ay tanyag na cluster na nakikita kahit walang teleskopyo."});

ADDART({id:"sputnik",t:"Sputnik",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛰️",w:"Sputnik 1",r:["space","sat","rocket","coldwar","apollo"],
p:[["The first satellite","Sputnik 1, launched by the Soviet Union in 1957, was the first artificial satellite."],["A beeping ball","It was a small metal sphere that sent out radio beeps heard around the world."],["The space race begins","Its launch shocked the United States and started the space race."]],
l:"Sputnik 1 was the first artificial satellite to orbit Earth. The Soviet Union launched it on 4 October 1957 from what is now the Baikonur Cosmodrome in Kazakhstan. Sputnik, meaning fellow traveler, was a polished metal sphere about 58 centimeters across, weighing about 84 kilograms, with four long antennas. It circled Earth every 96 minutes, broadcasting simple radio beeps that could be picked up by amateur radio operators around the world. Its launch, during the Cold War, shocked the United States and triggered the space race, leading to the creation of NASA, new investment in science education and, eventually, the Moon landings. Sputnik burned up in the atmosphere in January 1958.",
s:[["The launch","Sputnik was launched by an R-7 rocket, originally designed as an intercontinental missile. The project was led by the Soviet chief designer Sergei Korolev, whose identity was kept secret. The satellite was simple, but getting it into orbit required a rocket powerful enough to reach about 28,000 kilometers per hour."],["Beep, beep","Sputnik’s two radio transmitters sent out beeps on frequencies that ordinary radio receivers could pick up. People around the world listened to the signal, and newspapers printed the times it would pass overhead. Its batteries lasted about three weeks before the beeping stopped."],["Shock in America","The launch alarmed Americans, who feared that if the Soviet Union could put a satellite in orbit, it could also launch nuclear missiles across the world. Newspapers spoke of a Sputnik crisis, and politicians demanded action. The first American attempt, Vanguard, exploded on the launch pad in December 1957."],["Sputnik 2 and Laika","Just a month later, on 3 November 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 2, carrying a dog named Laika, the first animal to orbit Earth. Laika did not survive the flight, which was planned without a way to return. Her mission showed that a living creature could survive launch and weightlessness, paving the way for human spaceflight."]],
k:[["Launched","4 October 1957"],["Country","Soviet Union"],["Diameter","About 58 cm"],["Mass","About 84 kg"],["Orbit time","About 96 minutes"]],
d:[["1955","The United States and the Soviet Union announce plans to launch satellites"],["1957","Sputnik 1 is launched on 4 October"],["1957","Sputnik 2 carries the dog Laika into orbit"],["1958","Explorer 1 becomes the first American satellite"],["1958","Sputnik 1 burns up in the atmosphere, and NASA is founded"],["1961","Yuri Gagarin becomes the first human in space"]],
s2:[["The International Geophysical Year","Sputnik was launched during the International Geophysical Year, an 18-month period of worldwide scientific cooperation from 1957 to 1958. Both the United States and the Soviet Union had announced they would launch satellites to study Earth. The Soviet success came first, turning a scientific project into a symbol of Cold War rivalry."],["America responds","The United States launched its first satellite, Explorer 1, on 31 January 1958. It carried an instrument that discovered belts of radiation around Earth, now called the Van Allen belts. In July 1958 Congress created NASA to lead civilian space exploration, and the military began new rocket programs."],["Sputnik and schools","The Sputnik shock led the United States to invest heavily in science, mathematics and engineering education. The National Defense Education Act of 1958 funded schools, universities and student loans. Many scientists and engineers of the following decades were inspired by the space race."],["Watching from the Philippines","Sputnik and its rocket stage could be seen crossing the night sky from many parts of the world, including Asia. Newspapers in the Philippines and elsewhere reported on the satellite, and amateur radio operators tuned in to its beeps. The space race became a major news story of the late 1950s."],["From Sputnik to today","Since Sputnik, people have launched thousands of satellites for communication, weather forecasting, navigation, Earth observation and science. Today there are many thousands of active satellites in orbit, and the growing number raises concerns about crowding and space debris."],["A lasting symbol","The word sputnik entered languages around the world, and the phrase Sputnik moment is still used to describe a sudden realization that a rival has taken the lead, prompting a nation to respond. Replicas of Sputnik are displayed in museums in Moscow, Washington and elsewhere."]],
sp:["The sound heard around the world","On the night of 4 October 1957, radio operators began picking up a strange, repeating sound: beep, beep, beep. It came from a small shiny ball racing around Earth more than 200 kilometers up. Radio stations broadcast the beeps to millions of listeners, and people stepped outside to look for the tiny moving light, though what most saw was actually the much larger rocket stage that followed it. For many, it was thrilling and frightening at once: humanity had reached space, but the achievement belonged to one side of the Cold War. The beeps marked the beginning of the space age."],
g:[["Artificial satellite","A human-made object that orbits Earth or another body"],["Space race","The Cold War competition in space between the US and the Soviet Union"],["Orbit","The curved path of an object around another"],["Cold War","The rivalry between the United States and the Soviet Union, 1947–1991"]],
f:["Sputnik 1 was launched on 4 October 1957.","It orbited Earth every 96 minutes.","Sputnik 2 carried the dog Laika."],
fun:"The word sputnik means fellow traveler or companion in Russian.",
tl:"Ang Sputnik 1 ang unang artipisyal na satellite na inilunsad ng Unyong Sobyet noong 4 Oktubre 1957. Isa itong maliit na bolang metal na nagpapadala ng tunog na beep. Sinimulan nito ang paligsahan sa kalawakan sa pagitan ng Estados Unidos at Unyong Sobyet."});

ADDART({id:"rosetta",t:"The Rosetta Mission",c:"Space Exploration",i:"☄️",w:"Rosetta (spacecraft)",r:["comet","spaceagencies","solarform","astrobio","rocket"],
p:[["Chasing a comet","Rosetta was a European spacecraft that orbited a comet for two years."],["A landing on a comet","Its lander, Philae, made the first landing on a comet in 2014."],["Clues to our origins","Rosetta helped scientists learn about the early solar system."]],
l:"Rosetta was a space probe built by the European Space Agency that became the first spacecraft to orbit a comet and to send a lander to its surface. Launched in 2004, it spent ten years traveling through the solar system, using flybys of Earth and Mars to gain speed, before reaching comet 67P/Churyumov–Gerasimenko in August 2014. In November 2014 its small lander, Philae, touched down on the comet, the first landing ever on a comet’s nucleus, though it bounced and came to rest in a shadowed spot. Rosetta studied the comet for more than two years as it swung past the Sun, discovering organic molecules, unusual water and a surprisingly rugged, duck-shaped nucleus. The mission ended in 2016 with a controlled landing on the comet.",
s:[["The long journey","Rosetta was launched by an Ariane 5 rocket on 2 March 2004. To reach its fast-moving target, it flew past Earth three times and Mars once, using their gravity to change speed and direction. It also passed two asteroids, Steins and Lutetia. For about 31 months it was put into hibernation to save power before waking up in January 2014."],["A duck-shaped comet","When Rosetta arrived in August 2014, it found that comet 67P has two lobes joined by a narrow neck, resembling a rubber duck about four kilometers long. Its surface has cliffs, pits, boulders and smooth plains of dust. As the comet approached the Sun, jets of gas and dust burst from its surface."],["The Philae landing","On 12 November 2014 Rosetta released Philae, a lander about the size of a washing machine. Its harpoons failed to fire, and it bounced twice before settling in a shaded spot where its solar panels received little light. Philae sent back data for about 60 hours before its batteries ran down. Rosetta photographed its final resting place in 2016."],["Discoveries","Rosetta found that the comet contains organic molecules, including the amino acid glycine, a building block of proteins, and phosphorus, important for life. It also found that the comet’s water has a different chemical signature from Earth’s oceans, suggesting that comets like 67P were not the main source of Earth’s water."]],
k:[["Agency","European Space Agency"],["Launched","2 March 2004"],["Target","Comet 67P/Churyumov–Gerasimenko"],["Philae landing","12 November 2014"],["Mission ended","30 September 2016"]],
d:[["2004","Rosetta is launched"],["2007","Rosetta flies past Mars"],["2011","Rosetta enters hibernation"],["2014","Rosetta reaches comet 67P and Philae lands"],["2015","The comet makes its closest approach to the Sun"],["2016","Rosetta ends its mission with a controlled descent onto the comet"]],
s2:[["Names from Egypt","Rosetta was named after the Rosetta Stone, the ancient Egyptian slab whose inscriptions helped scholars decipher hieroglyphs. Scientists hoped the mission would similarly unlock the secrets of comets and the early solar system. The lander Philae was named after an island in the Nile where an obelisk was found that also helped with decipherment."],["Why study comets","Comets are icy bodies left over from the formation of the solar system about 4.6 billion years ago. They have changed little since then, so studying them is like opening a time capsule. Scientists wonder whether comets delivered water and the ingredients for life to the early Earth."],["A dramatic landing","The Philae landing captured worldwide attention. Mission controllers in Darmstadt, Germany, cheered when the first signals arrived, then learned that Philae had bounced more than a kilometer back into space before settling. Despite the difficult landing, it completed most of its planned experiments."],["Watching a comet wake up","Unlike earlier missions that flew quickly past comets, Rosetta stayed with 67P as it moved closer to the Sun and heated up. It watched the comet’s activity grow, with jets of gas and dust, outbursts and changes in its surface, and then die down as it moved away."],["The final descent","As the comet moved away from the Sun and power for Rosetta’s solar panels declined, the team decided to end the mission by slowly crashing Rosetta onto the comet on 30 September 2016. During the descent, it took close-up photographs and measurements until contact was lost on impact."],["Public engagement","Rosetta was followed closely by the public through social media, where the spacecraft and lander had cartoon versions that posted updates. A short science fiction film called Ambition was made to promote the mission. The excitement helped many people learn about comets and space science."]],
sp:["Ten years to catch a comet","Catching a comet is like trying to catch a speeding bullet with another bullet. Comet 67P travels around the Sun on a long, elliptical orbit, moving tens of thousands of kilometers per hour. To match its speed and path, Rosetta took a winding ten-year route, swinging past Earth and Mars to steal a little of their orbital energy each time. Engineers then put it to sleep for more than two and a half years to save power while it traveled far from the Sun. In January 2014 the world waited anxiously as Rosetta’s alarm clock went off. Hours later, its signal reached Earth: it had woken up, ready for its rendezvous."],
g:[["Comet","An icy body that releases gas and dust near the Sun"],["Nucleus","The solid core of a comet"],["Lander","A spacecraft that touches down on a surface"],["Gravity assist","Using a planet’s gravity to change a spacecraft’s speed"]],
f:["Rosetta was the first spacecraft to orbit a comet.","Philae made the first landing on a comet.","Comet 67P looks like a rubber duck."],
fun:"Rosetta’s journey to its comet took ten years and included three flybys of Earth and one of Mars.",
tl:"Ang Rosetta ay sasakyang pangkalawakan ng Europa na umikot sa isang kometa nang dalawang taon. Ang lander nitong Philae ang unang lumapag sa kometa noong 2014. Nakatulong ito upang maunawaan ang pinagmulan ng solar system."});

;
