// The Human Body, more articles: high blood pressure, myopia, leptospirosis, anemia and dyslexia.
ADDART({id:"hypertension",t:"High Blood Pressure",c:"Disease and Medicine",i:"🩺",w:"Hypertension",r:["heart","stroke","heartdisease","kidney","exercise"],
p:[["The silent killer","High blood pressure, or hypertension, often has no symptoms but damages the body over time."],["Very common","More than a billion adults worldwide have high blood pressure."],["Can be controlled","Lifestyle changes and medicines keep blood pressure in a healthy range."]],
l:"Blood pressure is the force of blood pushing against the walls of the arteries as the heart pumps. High blood pressure, or hypertension, occurs when this force stays too high over time. It usually causes no symptoms, which is why it is called the silent killer, but it strains the heart and blood vessels and is a leading cause of heart attacks, strokes, kidney disease and early death. The World Health Organization estimates that about 1.3 billion adults aged 30 to 79 have hypertension, and many do not know it. In the Philippines it is one of the most common health problems among adults. Regular checks, a healthy lifestyle and, when needed, medicines can control it and prevent serious complications.",
s:[["Reading blood pressure","Blood pressure is written as two numbers, such as 120 over 80, measured in millimeters of mercury. The top number, systolic pressure, is the pressure when the heart beats; the bottom number, diastolic pressure, is the pressure between beats. A reading below about 120 over 80 is considered normal, and readings repeatedly at or above 140 over 90 are generally considered hypertension."],["Why it is dangerous","High pressure makes the heart work harder and damages the inner walls of arteries, making them stiff and narrow. Over years this raises the risk of heart attacks, heart failure, strokes, kidney damage, vision loss and dementia. Because it usually causes no warning signs, many people learn they have it only after a serious event."],["Causes and risk factors","Risk rises with age and family history. Other factors include eating too much salt, being overweight, lack of physical activity, smoking, drinking too much alcohol, stress and conditions such as diabetes and kidney disease. In many cases there is no single cause."],["Checking regularly","The only way to know whether you have high blood pressure is to have it measured, at a clinic, health center, pharmacy or with a home monitor. Adults are advised to have their blood pressure checked regularly. In the Philippines, barangay health stations often offer free checks."]],
k:[["Also called","Hypertension"],["Normal reading","Below about 120/80 mmHg"],["Hypertension","Often defined as 140/90 mmHg or higher"],["Adults affected worldwide","About 1.3 billion (ages 30–79)"],["World Hypertension Day","17 May"]],
d:[["1733","Stephen Hales first measures blood pressure, in a horse"],["1896","Scipione Riva-Rocci invents the inflatable cuff for measuring blood pressure"],["1905","Nikolai Korotkov describes the sounds used to measure blood pressure"],["1950s","The first effective blood pressure medicines become available"],["2005","The first World Hypertension Day is held"],["2023","The WHO publishes its first global report on hypertension"]],
s2:[["Measuring pressure","In 1733 the English clergyman and scientist Stephen Hales inserted a tube into a horse’s artery and watched the blood rise high up the tube, the first measurement of blood pressure. In 1896 the Italian doctor Scipione Riva-Rocci invented the inflatable arm cuff, and in 1905 the Russian surgeon Nikolai Korotkov discovered the tapping sounds heard through a stethoscope that are still used to take readings."],["Salt and Filipino food","Many people eat far more salt than recommended. Common sources in Filipino diets include patis, toyo, bagoong, salted dried fish, instant noodles, canned goods, processed meats and salty snacks. Health experts suggest reducing added salt, choosing fresh foods, reading labels and using herbs, garlic, calamansi and spices for flavor."],["Treatment","Lifestyle changes, such as eating less salt, eating more vegetables and fruit, staying active, keeping a healthy weight, limiting alcohol and not smoking, can lower blood pressure. Many people also need medicines, which must usually be taken every day, even when they feel well. Stopping medicines without advice can be dangerous."],["Hypertension in the Philippines","Surveys have found that a large share of Filipino adults have high blood pressure, and many are unaware or not receiving treatment. Government programs provide some maintenance medicines for free at health centers, and campaigns encourage regular checks, especially during May, Hypertension Awareness Month."],["High blood pressure in pregnancy","Some women develop high blood pressure during pregnancy. A serious form called pre-eclampsia can harm both mother and baby, so blood pressure is checked at prenatal visits. With proper care, most women with high blood pressure in pregnancy have healthy babies."],["Low blood pressure","Blood pressure can also be too low, causing dizziness or fainting, especially when standing up quickly. Dehydration, heat, some medicines and certain illnesses can lower it. Mild low blood pressure without symptoms is usually not a concern."]],
sp:["The horse and the glass tube","In 1733 Stephen Hales, an English clergyman with a passion for experiments, wanted to know how strongly the heart pushes blood. He tied down a horse, inserted a brass pipe into an artery in its neck and connected it to a glass tube nearly three meters tall. When he released the blood, it shot up the tube to a height of about 2.5 meters and rose and fell slightly with each heartbeat. It was a crude and, by modern standards, cruel experiment, but it was the first measurement of blood pressure. Today the same measurement takes a minute with a soft cuff around the arm."],
g:[["Blood pressure","The force of blood against the walls of the arteries"],["Hypertension","Blood pressure that stays too high"],["Systolic pressure","The pressure when the heart beats"],["Diastolic pressure","The pressure between heartbeats"]],
f:["High blood pressure often has no symptoms.","Salt can raise blood pressure.","The blood pressure cuff was invented in 1896."],
fun:"The first blood pressure measurement, in 1733, used a horse and a glass tube nearly three meters tall.",
tl:"Ang altapresyon o hypertension ay kapag laging mataas ang presyon ng dugo sa mga ugat. Kadalasan itong walang sintomas ngunit maaaring humantong sa atake sa puso at stroke. Nakokontrol ito ng malusog na pamumuhay, pagbabawas ng asin at gamot."});

ADDART({id:"myopia",t:"Nearsightedness",c:"Health",i:"👓",w:"Near-sightedness",r:["eye","screentime","light","onlinelearning","exercise"],
p:[["Blurry far away","Nearsightedness, or myopia, makes distant objects look blurry."],["Rising worldwide","It is becoming much more common, especially among children in cities."],["Time outdoors helps","Spending time outdoors may help protect children’s eyesight."]],
l:"Nearsightedness, known medically as myopia, is a common eye condition in which nearby objects can be seen clearly but distant objects appear blurry. It happens when the eyeball is slightly too long, or the cornea too curved, so that light focuses in front of the retina instead of on it. Myopia usually begins in childhood and can worsen during the school years. It is easily corrected with eyeglasses, contact lenses or surgery, but severe myopia raises the risk of serious eye diseases later in life. Myopia has become much more common in recent decades, particularly in East and Southeast Asian cities, and researchers estimate that about half the world’s population could be nearsighted by 2050. Spending more time outdoors appears to help prevent it in children.",
s:[["How myopia works","For clear vision, the cornea and lens must focus light exactly on the retina at the back of the eye. In a nearsighted eye, which is usually a little too long, light from distant objects comes to a focus in front of the retina, so the image is blurred. Light from nearby objects focuses correctly, so reading and close work remain clear."],["Signs of myopia","Children with myopia may squint, sit close to the television or board, hold books very close, rub their eyes or complain of headaches. Many do not realize their vision is blurry because they have never seen clearly. Regular eye checks help catch it early."],["Correcting myopia","Glasses and contact lenses with concave lenses spread light out so that it focuses on the retina. Laser eye surgery can reshape the cornea in adults. Special lenses, eye drops and contact lenses worn overnight can slow the worsening of myopia in some children."],["A growing problem","Studies have found that in some East Asian cities, the large majority of young adults are nearsighted. A widely cited study estimated that about a third of the world’s population had myopia in 2020 and that about half may have it by 2050. Scientists think changes in how children spend their time are a major reason."]],
k:[["Medical name","Myopia"],["Cause","Eyeball too long or cornea too curved"],["Correction","Glasses, contact lenses or surgery"],["Protective habit","Time outdoors"],["Projected by 2050","About half of people worldwide"]],
d:[["c. 350 BC","Aristotle describes people who see better up close"],["c. 1286","Eyeglasses are invented in Italy"],["1450s","Concave lenses for nearsightedness come into use"],["1880s","Contact lenses are first made"],["1980s","Laser eye surgery for myopia is developed"],["2016","A study projects that half the world may be nearsighted by 2050"]],
s2:[["Why is myopia increasing?","Genes play a part, but they cannot explain such a rapid rise. Researchers point to children spending more time indoors, doing close work such as reading, studying and using screens, and less time outdoors. Bright outdoor light appears to help regulate eye growth in children."],["The power of sunlight","Studies in Asia, Australia and elsewhere have found that children who spend more time outdoors are less likely to become nearsighted. Some schools, such as in Taiwan, encouraged children to spend more time outside during breaks, and rates of new myopia among children fell. Experts often suggest at least two hours a day outdoors."],["Screens and close work","Long periods of close work, including reading and using phones and tablets, may contribute to myopia, though the evidence is still being studied. Eye doctors often recommend the 20-20-20 rule: every 20 minutes, look at something about 20 feet, or 6 meters, away for 20 seconds."],["Myopia and the pandemic","During the COVID-19 pandemic, children in many countries, including the Philippines, spent long periods at home learning online. Several studies reported an increase in new cases of myopia among children during this time, adding to concerns about screen time and time indoors."],["Eye care in the Philippines","Many Filipino children with vision problems do not receive eye checks or glasses, which can affect their learning. School vision screening programs, community eye missions and government health insurance help, and groups provide free or low-cost glasses to students."],["High myopia","Very strong nearsightedness, called high myopia, increases the risk of serious conditions later in life, such as retinal detachment, glaucoma and cataracts. People with high myopia are advised to have regular eye examinations and to seek urgent care if they notice sudden flashes of light, floaters or a shadow in their vision."]],
sp:["Glasses through the ages","Eyeglasses were invented in northern Italy around 1286, possibly in Pisa or Venice. The first glasses were convex lenses that helped older people read. Lenses for nearsightedness, which are concave, came later, in the 1400s. Early glasses were balanced on the nose or held by hand, and arms that hook over the ears became common only in the 1700s. For centuries glasses were expensive, but today they are among the most effective and affordable health tools in the world, allowing millions of children to see the board at school and millions of adults to keep working."],
g:[["Myopia","Nearsightedness, difficulty seeing distant objects clearly"],["Retina","The light-sensitive layer at the back of the eye"],["Concave lens","A lens thinner in the middle that spreads light out"],["Optometrist","A health professional who checks eyes and prescribes glasses"]],
f:["Nearsighted people see near objects clearly.","Time outdoors may protect children’s eyes.","Eyeglasses were invented around 1286."],
fun:"The 20-20-20 rule suggests looking at something 20 feet away for 20 seconds every 20 minutes of close work.",
tl:"Ang nearsightedness o myopia ay kondisyon kung saan malabo ang tingin sa malayo ngunit malinaw sa malapit. Dumarami ito sa buong mundo, lalo na sa mga batang nasa lungsod. Maaaring makatulong ang paglalaro sa labas upang maprotektahan ang mata ng mga bata."});

ADDART({id:"leptospirosis",t:"Leptospirosis",c:"Disease and Medicine",i:"🐀",w:"Leptospirosis",r:["phtyph","immune","antibiotics","sanitation","dengue"],
p:[["A flood-borne disease","Leptospirosis is a bacterial infection often caught by wading in floodwater."],["Spread by rats","The bacteria come from the urine of infected animals, especially rats."],["Treatable","Early treatment with antibiotics can save lives."]],
l:"Leptospirosis is an infection caused by bacteria called Leptospira, which are carried in the urine of infected animals, especially rats, but also dogs, pigs, cattle and other mammals. People can be infected when water or soil contaminated with this urine comes into contact with cuts or scratches on the skin or with the eyes, nose or mouth. In tropical countries such as the Philippines, outbreaks often follow typhoons and floods, when people wade through floodwater. Symptoms range from fever, headache and muscle pain to severe illness affecting the kidneys, liver and lungs. Leptospirosis can be treated with antibiotics, and avoiding floodwater, wearing boots and getting early care help prevent deaths.",
s:[["How it spreads","Rats and other animals can carry Leptospira bacteria in their kidneys and pass them out in their urine. The bacteria survive in water and moist soil for weeks. During floods, contaminated water spreads widely through streets and homes. The bacteria enter the body through cuts, scratches or soft skin softened by long exposure to water, or through the eyes, nose and mouth."],["Symptoms","Symptoms usually begin about 2 to 14 days after exposure and include high fever, headache, muscle pain especially in the calves, red eyes, chills and vomiting. Many cases are mild, but some people develop a severe form, sometimes called Weil’s disease, with yellowing of the skin, kidney failure, bleeding and lung problems."],["Treatment","Leptospirosis is treated with antibiotics such as doxycycline or penicillin. Early treatment works best, so anyone who develops fever after wading in floodwater should see a doctor and mention the exposure. Severe cases need hospital care, sometimes including dialysis for kidney failure."],["Prevention","The best protection is to avoid wading or swimming in floodwater. People who must enter it should wear rubber boots and gloves, cover wounds and wash with soap and clean water afterward. Controlling rats, keeping garbage covered and improving drainage also reduce risk. Doctors may give preventive antibiotics to people with high exposure."]],
k:[["Cause","Leptospira bacteria"],["Main carriers","Rats and other animals"],["Common source","Floodwater"],["Severe form","Weil’s disease"],["Treatment","Antibiotics"]],
d:[["1886","Adolf Weil describes a severe disease with jaundice and kidney failure"],["1907","Leptospira bacteria are first seen in a patient’s kidney tissue"],["1915","Scientists in Japan identify the bacteria as the cause"],["2009","Leptospirosis cases surge in Metro Manila after Typhoon Ondoy’s floods"],["2012","Monsoon floods in Luzon are followed by more leptospirosis cases"],["2020s","Health authorities expand flood-season warnings and preventive treatment"]],
s2:[["Floods and outbreaks","In the Philippines, leptospirosis cases usually rise during the rainy season, especially one to two weeks after major floods. After Typhoon Ondoy flooded much of Metro Manila in 2009, hospitals treated large numbers of patients, and many people died. Similar surges have followed other typhoons and monsoon floods."],["Who is at risk","People who wade through floodwater, farmers working in wet rice fields, sewer workers, animal handlers and people living in crowded areas with poor drainage and many rats face the highest risk. Children playing in floodwater are also at risk."],["Hospitals under strain","During major outbreaks, hospitals can be overwhelmed by patients with leptospirosis, many needing dialysis for kidney failure. Health authorities may open special wards and urge people to seek early treatment rather than wait until symptoms become severe."],["Rats in cities","Large rat populations in cities increase the risk of leptospirosis. Improper garbage disposal, clogged drains and crowded housing provide food and shelter for rats. Better waste collection, sanitation and drainage benefit public health in many ways."],["A global disease","Leptospirosis occurs worldwide but is most common in tropical and subtropical regions, including Southeast Asia, Latin America and the Pacific islands. Scientists estimate there are about a million severe cases and tens of thousands of deaths each year. It is considered a neglected disease because it receives relatively little attention."],["Animals and vaccines","Vaccines are used to protect dogs, pigs and cattle from leptospirosis, which can also make animals sick. Human vaccines exist in a few countries but are not widely used. Keeping pets vaccinated and away from rats helps protect families."]],
sp:["After the flood","When floodwater finally recedes after a typhoon, many families return to clean their homes, scrubbing mud from floors and throwing out ruined furniture. Health workers warn that this is also a dangerous time. The water that filled the streets may have carried rat urine, and small cuts on hands and feet can let Leptospira bacteria enter the body. A week or two later, some people develop fever and aching calves and think it is only the flu. Doctors urge anyone who has waded through floodwater and then falls ill to seek medical care quickly and to tell the doctor about the flood exposure, because early antibiotics can prevent severe illness."],
g:[["Leptospirosis","A bacterial disease spread by the urine of infected animals"],["Leptospira","The spiral-shaped bacteria that cause leptospirosis"],["Weil’s disease","A severe form of leptospirosis"],["Dialysis","A treatment that filters blood when the kidneys fail"]],
f:["Leptospirosis is often caught from floodwater.","Rats are the main carriers.","Antibiotics can treat it."],
fun:"Leptospira bacteria are shaped like tiny spirals or question marks and can wriggle through water.",
tl:"Ang leptospirosis ay impeksiyong dulot ng bakteryang nasa ihi ng daga at iba pang hayop. Karaniwan itong nakukuha sa paglusong sa baha. Nagagamot ito ng antibiotic, kaya magpatingin agad sa doktor kung nilagnat matapos lumusong sa baha."});

ADDART({id:"anemia",t:"Anemia",c:"Disease and Medicine",i:"🩸",w:"Anemia",r:["blood","nutri","pregnancy","heart","hygiene"],
p:[["Too few red blood cells","Anemia is a condition in which the blood cannot carry enough oxygen."],["Tired and pale","It can cause tiredness, weakness, pale skin and shortness of breath."],["Often preventable","Eating iron-rich foods and treating its causes can prevent and cure many cases."]],
l:"Anemia is a condition in which a person has too few healthy red blood cells or too little hemoglobin, the protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen. As a result, the body’s tissues do not receive enough oxygen, causing tiredness, weakness, pale skin, dizziness and shortness of breath. The most common cause worldwide is iron deficiency, often due to a diet low in iron, blood loss or the extra needs of pregnancy and growth. Other causes include lack of vitamin B12 or folate, infections such as malaria and intestinal worms, and inherited conditions such as thalassemia and sickle cell disease. Anemia affects a large share of young children and women worldwide, including in the Philippines. Many cases can be prevented with a good diet, fortified foods, supplements and treatment of infections.",
s:[["What anemia does","Red blood cells carry oxygen from the lungs to every part of the body using hemoglobin, which contains iron. When there is not enough hemoglobin, the heart and lungs must work harder. People may feel tired, weak and short of breath, have trouble concentrating, look pale or feel cold."],["Iron deficiency","Iron deficiency anemia is the most common type. It can result from not eating enough iron-rich foods, poor absorption of iron, heavy menstrual bleeding, blood loss from intestinal worms or ulcers, and the extra iron needs of babies, growing children and pregnant women."],["Other causes","Anemia can also be caused by a lack of vitamin B12 or folate, long-term diseases such as kidney disease, infections like malaria, and inherited blood disorders. In thalassemia, the body makes abnormal hemoglobin; in sickle cell disease, red blood cells become stiff and curved."],["Who is most at risk","Young children, adolescent girls, women of reproductive age and pregnant women are at greatest risk. Anemia in pregnancy can lead to premature birth and low birth weight, while in children it can affect growth, learning and energy for play and school."]],
k:[["Main problem","Too little hemoglobin"],["Most common cause","Iron deficiency"],["Common symptoms","Tiredness, weakness, pale skin"],["Groups most affected","Young children and women"],["Philippine fortification law","Food Fortification Law of 2000"]],
d:[["1660s","Doctors use iron to treat a condition called chlorosis, a form of anemia"],["1849","Thomas Addison describes a form of anemia later called pernicious anemia"],["1926","George Minot and William Murphy show that eating liver treats pernicious anemia"],["1934","Minot, Murphy and George Whipple receive the Nobel Prize for their work on anemia"],["1948","Vitamin B12 is isolated"],["2000","The Philippines passes a law requiring fortification of rice, flour and other foods"]],
s2:[["Iron-rich foods","Good sources of iron include red meat, liver, fish, chicken, eggs, beans, lentils and dark green leafy vegetables such as malunggay, kangkong and alugbati. Iron from meat and fish is absorbed more easily than iron from plants. Eating foods rich in vitamin C, such as calamansi, guava and tomatoes, with plant foods helps the body absorb iron."],["Fortified foods","Many countries add iron and other nutrients to staple foods to prevent deficiencies. The Philippine Food Fortification Law of 2000 requires the addition of iron to rice and wheat flour, vitamin A to cooking oil and sugar, and other nutrients. Iron-fortified rice has been distributed in some government programs and schools."],["Supplements","Health workers give iron and folic acid supplements to pregnant women and iron supplements or micronutrient powders to young children in many countries. Deworming programs for schoolchildren also reduce anemia caused by intestinal worms, which feed on blood."],["Liver for anemia","In the 1920s, American doctors George Minot and William Murphy found that patients with pernicious anemia, then usually fatal, recovered when they ate large amounts of raw or lightly cooked liver. The key ingredient was later identified as vitamin B12. Minot, Murphy and George Whipple received the Nobel Prize in 1934."],["Thalassemia in the Philippines","Thalassemia is an inherited blood disorder that is relatively common in Southeast Asia, including the Philippines. People with severe forms need regular blood transfusions. Genetic counseling and screening help families understand their risk, and awareness campaigns promote early diagnosis."],["Diagnosis","Anemia is diagnosed with a simple blood test that measures hemoglobin. Because tiredness has many causes, doctors look for the reason behind anemia before treating it. Treating the underlying cause, such as worms, heavy bleeding or poor diet, is important for lasting recovery."]],
sp:["Sinigang with malunggay","Many Filipino family recipes naturally help fight anemia. A bowl of tinola with chicken and malunggay leaves, monggo stew cooked with dried fish and leafy greens, or adobong atay, liver cooked in vinegar and soy sauce, are all good sources of iron. Squeezing calamansi over dishes or eating a guava afterward adds vitamin C, helping the body absorb iron from plant foods. Nutritionists in health centers and schools often teach parents about these everyday foods, showing that a healthy diet need not be expensive."],
g:[["Anemia","A lack of healthy red blood cells or hemoglobin"],["Hemoglobin","The iron-containing protein that carries oxygen in blood"],["Iron deficiency","Not having enough iron in the body"],["Food fortification","Adding vitamins and minerals to common foods"]],
f:["Iron deficiency is the most common cause of anemia.","Vitamin C helps the body absorb iron.","The Philippines requires fortified rice and flour."],
fun:"In the 1920s, patients with a deadly form of anemia were cured by eating large amounts of liver.",
tl:"Ang anemia ay kondisyon kung saan kulang ang malulusog na pulang selula ng dugo o hemoglobin. Nagdudulot ito ng pagkapagod, panghihina at pamumutla. Maiiwasan ito sa pagkain ng mayaman sa iron tulad ng malunggay, isda at atay."});

ADDART({id:"dyslexia",t:"Dyslexia",c:"Brain and Learning",i:"📖",w:"Dyslexia",r:["brain","memory","psychology","alphabet","onlinelearning"],
p:[["A learning difference","Dyslexia is a learning difference that makes reading and spelling difficult."],["Not about intelligence","People with dyslexia are just as intelligent as others."],["Help works","Early support and special teaching methods help people with dyslexia succeed."]],
l:"Dyslexia is a common learning difference that mainly affects reading, spelling and writing. People with dyslexia often find it hard to connect letters with the sounds they represent, to decode words and to read fluently, even though they have normal intelligence, vision and opportunities to learn. It is thought to affect a significant share of people, often estimated at 5 to 10 percent, and it tends to run in families. Dyslexia is not caused by laziness or poor teaching, and it is not a sign of low intelligence. With early identification and structured, multisensory teaching, people with dyslexia can learn to read well, and many become successful in every field. Many also have strengths in areas such as creative thinking, problem solving and visual reasoning.",
s:[["What dyslexia is","Reading requires the brain to link written symbols to the sounds of spoken language. In dyslexia, this process works differently, particularly the ability to break words into individual sounds, called phonological awareness. As a result, decoding new words, spelling and reading quickly can be difficult."],["Signs of dyslexia","Signs can include difficulty learning letter sounds, reading slowly or inaccurately, guessing words, spelling the same word in different ways, trouble remembering sequences such as the days of the week, and avoiding reading aloud. Signs differ from person to person, and dyslexia can range from mild to severe."],["Myths about dyslexia","A common myth is that people with dyslexia see letters backwards. While young children often reverse letters such as b and d, this is normal and not the main feature of dyslexia. Another myth is that dyslexia can be outgrown; it is lifelong, but people can learn effective strategies."],["Diagnosis","Dyslexia is usually identified through assessments by psychologists, specialist teachers or other professionals who test reading, spelling, sound skills and memory. Early identification, ideally in the first years of school, allows support to begin before children fall far behind or lose confidence."]],
k:[["Main area affected","Reading and spelling"],["Estimated share of people","Often 5 to 10 percent"],["Not related to","Intelligence"],["Effective teaching","Structured, multisensory phonics"],["First described","1880s and 1890s"]],
d:[["1877","Adolph Kussmaul describes word blindness"],["1887","Rudolf Berlin coins the term dyslexia"],["1896","W. Pringle Morgan describes a bright boy who cannot learn to read"],["1930s","Samuel Orton and Anna Gillingham develop teaching methods for dyslexia"],["1949","The International Dyslexia Association’s forerunner is founded"],["2022","The Philippines passes a law on inclusive education for learners with disabilities"]],
s2:[["Discovering dyslexia","In 1896 the British doctor W. Pringle Morgan described a 14-year-old boy, Percy, who was bright and quick in many subjects but could not learn to read despite years of teaching. Morgan called the condition congenital word blindness. The term dyslexia, from Greek words meaning difficulty with words, had been coined in 1887 by the German eye doctor Rudolf Berlin."],["The brain and reading","Brain imaging studies show that people with dyslexia often use different brain areas when reading, particularly in regions on the left side of the brain involved in linking sounds and letters. With effective teaching, brain activity can change, showing that the brain can adapt."],["Teaching methods","Structured literacy approaches teach the sounds of language and how they connect to letters step by step, often using several senses at once, such as seeing, saying, hearing and tracing letters. The Orton-Gillingham approach, developed in the 1930s, inspired many programs used today."],["Tools that help","Audiobooks, text-to-speech software, speech-to-text tools, colored overlays for some readers, extra time on tests and permission to type instead of handwrite can help students with dyslexia show what they know. Fonts designed for readability and clear layouts can also help."],["Dyslexia in the Philippines","Awareness of dyslexia has grown in the Philippines, where parents’ groups and organizations provide information and support. Reading in more than one language, such as Filipino, English and a regional language, may present additional challenges. A 2022 law promotes inclusive education and support services for learners with disabilities, including learning disabilities."],["Strengths and success","Many people with dyslexia report strengths such as creativity, big-picture thinking, storytelling, spatial reasoning and problem solving. People with dyslexia work successfully as scientists, entrepreneurs, artists, engineers, athletes and leaders. Experts emphasize that recognizing strengths, along with support for reading, helps people thrive."]],
sp:["Percy’s story","In 1896 a British doctor named W. Pringle Morgan wrote about a puzzling patient, a 14-year-old boy called Percy. Percy was intelligent and good at arithmetic, and his teacher said he would be the smartest boy in the school if instruction were entirely oral. Yet after seven years of school, he could barely read. He wrote his own name as Precy and spelled simple words in strange ways. Morgan concluded that Percy had a specific difficulty with written words, unrelated to his intelligence. His short article in the British Medical Journal is often cited as one of the first descriptions of what we now call dyslexia."],
g:[["Dyslexia","A learning difference that mainly affects reading and spelling"],["Phonological awareness","The ability to hear and work with the sounds in words"],["Multisensory teaching","Teaching that uses sight, hearing, speech and touch together"],["Learning difference","A difference in how a person learns certain skills"]],
f:["Dyslexia is not related to intelligence.","It mainly affects reading and spelling.","The word dyslexia was coined in 1887."],
fun:"The word dyslexia comes from Greek words meaning difficulty with words.",
tl:"Ang dyslexia ay pagkakaiba sa pagkatuto na nagpapahirap sa pagbasa at pagbaybay. Hindi ito kaugnay ng talino. Nakatutulong ang maagang pagtukoy at espesyal na paraan ng pagtuturo upang magtagumpay ang mga may dyslexia."});

;
/* data/science10.js */
// Science and Numbers, more articles: oxygen, negative numbers and the Nobel Prize.
ADDART({id:"oxygen",t:"Oxygen",c:"Chemistry",i:"🫧",w:"Oxygen",r:["periodictable","atmosphere","lungs","photo","reactions"],
p:[["The breath of life","Oxygen is the element that animals and people need to breathe."],["Everywhere","It makes up about a fifth of the air and is the most abundant element in Earth’s crust."],["Discovered in the 1770s","Scientists in Sweden, England and France discovered and named it."]],
l:"Oxygen is a chemical element with the symbol O and atomic number 8. At normal temperatures it is a colorless, odorless gas made of pairs of atoms, O2, and it makes up about 21 percent of Earth’s air. Oxygen is the most abundant element in Earth’s crust, found in rocks, sand and water, and by mass it is the most abundant element in the human body, mostly in the form of water. Nearly all living things depend on it: animals breathe it to release energy from food, and plants produce it through photosynthesis. Oxygen also allows fire to burn. It was discovered in the 1770s by Carl Wilhelm Scheele and Joseph Priestley, and named by Antoine Lavoisier, whose work on it helped found modern chemistry.",
s:[["Oxygen in the air","Air is about 78 percent nitrogen and 21 percent oxygen, with small amounts of argon, carbon dioxide and other gases. Each breath brings oxygen into the lungs, where it passes into the blood and is carried by red blood cells to every cell of the body."],["Oxygen and energy","Cells use oxygen to break down sugar and release energy in a process called cellular respiration, producing carbon dioxide and water as waste. This is why we breathe in oxygen and breathe out carbon dioxide. Without oxygen, brain cells begin to die within minutes."],["Where oxygen comes from","Most of the oxygen in the air was produced by photosynthesis, as plants, algae and certain bacteria use sunlight to turn carbon dioxide and water into sugar, releasing oxygen. Ocean plankton produce a large share of it. Early Earth had almost no free oxygen until photosynthetic microbes slowly filled the air over hundreds of millions of years."],["Oxygen and fire","Fire needs fuel, heat and oxygen. Burning is a fast reaction between a fuel and oxygen that releases heat and light. Rusting is a much slower reaction between iron and oxygen in the presence of water. Firefighters put out fires partly by cutting off their oxygen supply, for example with foam or blankets."]],
k:[["Symbol","O"],["Atomic number","8"],["Share of air","About 21 percent"],["Discovered","1770s, Scheele and Priestley"],["Named by","Antoine Lavoisier"]],
d:[["c. 2.4 billion years ago","Oxygen begins to build up in Earth’s atmosphere"],["1771","Carl Wilhelm Scheele produces oxygen in Sweden"],["1774","Joseph Priestley isolates oxygen in England"],["1777","Antoine Lavoisier names the element oxygen"],["1840","Christian Schönbein identifies ozone, a form of oxygen"],["1877","Oxygen is first liquefied"]],
s2:[["Who discovered oxygen?","The Swedish chemist Carl Wilhelm Scheele produced oxygen around 1771 by heating chemicals, calling it fire air, but his results were published only in 1777. The English minister and scientist Joseph Priestley independently made it in 1774 by heating mercury oxide and found that a candle burned brightly and a mouse lived longer in it. Both are usually credited with the discovery."],["Lavoisier’s revolution","The French chemist Antoine Lavoisier repeated these experiments and realized that burning and breathing involve combining with this gas, rather than releasing a mysterious substance called phlogiston, as many believed. He named it oxygen, from Greek words meaning acid-maker, because he wrongly thought all acids contained it. His work helped create modern chemistry."],["The Great Oxidation Event","For the first half of Earth’s history, the atmosphere had very little oxygen. About 2.4 billion years ago, oxygen produced by microbes called cyanobacteria began to accumulate in the air, an event known as the Great Oxidation Event. It transformed the planet’s chemistry and eventually made complex animal life possible."],["Ozone","Ozone is a form of oxygen with three atoms, O3. High in the atmosphere, the ozone layer absorbs most of the Sun’s harmful ultraviolet rays. Near the ground, however, ozone is a pollutant that forms from vehicle and factory emissions in sunlight and can harm the lungs."],["Medical oxygen","Hospitals give extra oxygen to patients who have trouble breathing because of conditions such as pneumonia, asthma attacks, heart failure or COVID-19. During the COVID-19 pandemic, shortages of medical oxygen in many countries, including parts of the Philippines, showed how vital it is."],["Oxygen in industry and space","Oxygen is used in steelmaking, welding and cutting metals, water treatment and chemical production. Liquid oxygen, which forms at about minus 183°C and is pale blue, is used with fuels to power rockets. Astronauts on the International Space Station get oxygen by splitting water with electricity."]],
sp:["The mouse in the jar","In 1774 Joseph Priestley focused sunlight through a large lens onto a red powder, mercury oxide, inside a sealed container. A gas was given off. When he placed a candle in it, the flame burned with remarkable brightness. He then put a mouse in a jar of the gas and found that it lived much longer than a mouse in the same amount of ordinary air. Curious, Priestley breathed the gas himself and wrote that his breath felt particularly light and easy, wondering whether this pure air might one day become a fashionable luxury. He had isolated oxygen."],
g:[["Element","A pure substance made of one kind of atom"],["Respiration","The process cells use to release energy from food using oxygen"],["Photosynthesis","The process by which plants make food and release oxygen"],["Ozone","A form of oxygen with three atoms"]],
f:["Oxygen makes up about 21 percent of air.","Oxygen is element number 8.","Plants release oxygen through photosynthesis."],
fun:"Liquid oxygen is pale blue and is attracted to magnets.",
tl:"Ang oxygen ay elementong kailangan ng tao at hayop upang huminga. Bumubuo ito ng humigit-kumulang 21 porsiyento ng hangin. Natuklasan ito noong dekada 1770 at pinangalanan ni Antoine Lavoisier."});

ADDART({id:"negativenumbers",t:"Negative Numbers",c:"Mathematics",i:"➖",w:"Negative number",r:["zero","alg","infin","graphs","percent"],
p:[["Numbers below zero","Negative numbers are numbers less than zero, written with a minus sign."],["Everyday uses","They describe debts, temperatures below freezing and places below sea level."],["A slow acceptance","Mathematicians took centuries to fully accept them."]],
l:"Negative numbers are numbers less than zero, such as minus 1, minus 5 or minus 3.7. Together with zero and positive numbers, they form the integers and the wider set of real numbers. On a number line, negative numbers lie to the left of zero. They describe quantities that can go in opposite directions: money owed versus money owned, temperatures below freezing, floors below ground, heights below sea level and losses compared with gains. Negative numbers were used in ancient China and were explained with clear rules by Indian mathematicians such as Brahmagupta in the 600s. In Europe, many mathematicians regarded them with suspicion until the 1600s and later. Today they are essential in mathematics, science, finance and computing.",
s:[["The number line","A number line shows zero in the middle, positive numbers to the right and negative numbers to the left. The farther left, the smaller the number, so minus 10 is less than minus 2. The distance of a number from zero, ignoring its sign, is called its absolute value."],["Rules for calculating","Adding a negative number is like subtracting: 5 plus minus 3 equals 2. Subtracting a negative number is like adding: 5 minus minus 3 equals 8. Multiplying or dividing two negative numbers gives a positive result, while multiplying a positive and a negative number gives a negative result."],["Everyday negative numbers","Temperatures below zero degrees Celsius are negative. A bank account that is overdrawn shows a negative balance. Places below sea level, such as the shores of the Dead Sea at about minus 430 meters, have negative elevations. In sports, goal difference can be negative, and golf scores below par are written as negative numbers."],["Why negative times negative is positive","One way to understand it: losing a debt is like gaining money. If removing a debt of 10 pesos is written as minus times minus 10, the result is a gain of 10. Mathematicians also show that the rule is needed for arithmetic to stay consistent."]],
k:[["Definition","Numbers less than zero"],["Symbol","Minus sign (−)"],["Early use","Ancient China, red and black counting rods"],["Clear rules","Brahmagupta, 628"],["Negative times negative","Positive"]],
d:[["c. 200 BC–AD 200","The Chinese Nine Chapters on the Mathematical Art uses negative numbers"],["628","Brahmagupta sets out rules for negative numbers and zero"],["c. 1200","Fibonacci interprets negative results as debts"],["1545","Gerolamo Cardano works with negative solutions but calls them fictitious"],["1637","René Descartes calls negative roots false roots"],["1700s","Negative numbers become widely accepted in European mathematics"]],
s2:[["Red and black rods","In ancient China, people calculated with counting rods laid out on a board. The Nine Chapters on the Mathematical Art, compiled between about 200 BC and AD 200, used red rods for positive numbers and black rods for negative numbers, with rules for adding and subtracting them to solve problems such as balancing accounts. This is the reverse of modern accounting, where red often means loss."],["Brahmagupta’s rules","In 628 the Indian mathematician Brahmagupta wrote rules for working with positive numbers, which he called fortunes, negative numbers, called debts, and zero. He stated, for example, that a debt minus zero is a debt and that the product of two debts is a fortune. His work was one of the first clear treatments of negative numbers as numbers in their own right."],["Suspicion in Europe","Many European mathematicians long distrusted negative numbers, asking how a quantity could be less than nothing. Some called negative solutions absurd, false or fictitious and ignored them. Only gradually, through their use in algebra, accounting and coordinate geometry, did they become fully accepted."],["Coordinates","In the 1600s, René Descartes and others developed coordinate geometry, in which points are located by numbers on two perpendicular axes. Negative numbers are needed to describe points to the left of or below the origin. Graphs, maps and computer screens all rely on such coordinates."],["Negative numbers in science","Scientists use negative numbers for electric charges, such as the negative charge of an electron, for temperatures below zero, for directions of motion and for changes such as falling prices or populations. Absolute zero, the coldest possible temperature, is about minus 273.15 degrees Celsius."],["Negative numbers in computers","Computers store negative numbers using clever binary codes, the most common being a method called two’s complement, which lets the same circuits add and subtract both positive and negative numbers. Very occasionally, errors in handling negative numbers have caused famous software bugs in games and systems."]],
sp:["Fortunes and debts","When Brahmagupta wrote his great astronomy and mathematics book in 628, he needed words for numbers greater and less than zero. He chose dhana, meaning fortune or wealth, for positive numbers and rina, meaning debt, for negative numbers. With these, his rules read almost like advice about money: a fortune plus a fortune is a fortune; a debt plus a debt is a debt; and a debt subtracted from zero becomes a fortune. Thinking of negative numbers as debts helped people understand them, and teachers around the world still use the idea of money owed to explain negative numbers to students."],
g:[["Negative number","A number less than zero"],["Integer","A whole number, positive, negative or zero"],["Number line","A line showing numbers in order"],["Absolute value","The distance of a number from zero"]],
f:["Negative numbers are less than zero.","Ancient Chinese mathematicians used negative numbers.","Negative times negative is positive."],
fun:"Ancient Chinese mathematicians used black rods for negative numbers and red rods for positive ones, the opposite of modern accounting colors.",
tl:"Ang negatibong numero ay numerong mas mababa sa sero, na isinusulat na may minus. Ginagamit ito sa utang, temperaturang mas mababa sa sero at lugar na mas mababa sa antas ng dagat. Matagal bago ito tinanggap ng mga matematiko sa Europa."});

ADDART({id:"nobel",t:"The Nobel Prize",c:"History of Science",i:"🏅",w:"Nobel Prize",r:["scimeth","curie","hrights","particles","novel"],
p:[["The world’s best-known prizes","The Nobel Prizes honor outstanding achievements in science, literature and peace."],["Alfred Nobel’s will","They were created by the will of Alfred Nobel, the inventor of dynamite."],["Awarded since 1901","The first prizes were awarded in 1901."]],
l:"The Nobel Prizes are international awards given each year for outstanding contributions in physics, chemistry, physiology or medicine, literature and peace, along with a related prize in economic sciences. They were established by the will of Alfred Nobel, a Swedish chemist, engineer and businessman who invented dynamite and left most of his fortune to fund prizes for those who have conferred the greatest benefit on humankind. The first prizes were awarded in 1901. The prizes are presented each December, in Stockholm, Sweden, except for the Peace Prize, which is awarded in Oslo, Norway. They are widely regarded as the most prestigious awards in their fields. In 2021 journalist Maria Ressa became the first Filipino Nobel laureate, sharing the Peace Prize.",
s:[["Alfred Nobel","Alfred Nobel was born in Stockholm in 1833. He invented dynamite in 1867, a safer way to handle the powerful explosive nitroglycerin, and built factories and companies in many countries, becoming very wealthy. He held more than 350 patents. He never married and, when he died in 1896, left most of his fortune to create the prizes."],["The prize categories","Nobel’s will named five prizes: physics, chemistry, physiology or medicine, literature and peace. In 1968 Sweden’s central bank established a prize in economic sciences in Nobel’s memory, first awarded in 1969. Each prize can be shared by up to three people, and the Peace Prize can also go to organizations."],["How winners are chosen","Different institutions choose the winners. The Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences selects physics, chemistry and economics; the Karolinska Institute selects physiology or medicine; the Swedish Academy selects literature; and a committee appointed by the Norwegian parliament selects peace. Nominations come from invited experts, and records are kept secret for 50 years."],["The ceremonies","The prizes are presented on 10 December, the anniversary of Nobel’s death. Laureates receive a gold medal, a diploma and a cash award, and give lectures about their work. The ceremony in Stockholm is followed by a grand banquet, while the Peace Prize ceremony takes place in Oslo City Hall."]],
k:[["Founder","Alfred Nobel"],["First awarded","1901"],["Ceremony date","10 December"],["Cities","Stockholm and Oslo"],["First Filipino laureate","Maria Ressa, Peace, 2021"]],
d:[["1867","Alfred Nobel patents dynamite"],["1895","Nobel signs his will establishing the prizes"],["1901","The first Nobel Prizes are awarded"],["1903","Marie Curie becomes the first woman to win a Nobel Prize"],["1969","The first prize in economic sciences is awarded"],["2021","Maria Ressa becomes the first Filipino Nobel laureate"]],
s2:[["The merchant of death","According to a well-known story, in 1888 a French newspaper mistakenly published Alfred Nobel’s obituary when it was actually his brother Ludvig who had died. The article reportedly called him the merchant of death because his inventions were used in war. Historians debate how much the story influenced him, but it is often said to have encouraged him to leave a more positive legacy."],["Remarkable laureates","Marie Curie is the only person to have won Nobel Prizes in two different sciences, physics in 1903 and chemistry in 1911. Linus Pauling won two unshared prizes, chemistry and peace. Malala Yousafzai became the youngest laureate at 17 when she shared the Peace Prize in 2014. Some organizations, such as the International Committee of the Red Cross, have won the Peace Prize several times."],["Maria Ressa","In 2021 the Filipino journalist Maria Ressa, co-founder of the news website Rappler, shared the Nobel Peace Prize with Russian journalist Dmitry Muratov for their efforts to safeguard freedom of expression. She became the first Filipino to receive a Nobel Prize. The award was praised by press freedom groups, while her reporting and legal cases have been the subject of debate in the Philippines."],["Asian laureates","The first Asian laureate was the Indian poet Rabindranath Tagore, who won the literature prize in 1913. Since then, scientists, writers and peacemakers from Japan, China, India, South Korea, Vietnam, Myanmar, East Timor, Bangladesh and other Asian countries have received prizes."],["Criticism and controversy","The Nobel Prizes have been criticized for choosing mostly men and people from Europe and North America, for overlooking important figures, and for limiting science prizes to three people when modern research involves large teams. Some Peace Prize choices have been controversial. The Nobel institutions say they are working to recognize a wider range of achievements."],["The Ig Nobel Prizes","Since 1991, a humorous award called the Ig Nobel Prize has honored real research that first makes people laugh and then makes them think, such as studies of why woodpeckers do not get headaches. Real Nobel laureates often help present the awards, celebrating the curiosity behind science."]],
sp:["The will that surprised everyone","When Alfred Nobel died in Italy in December 1896, his family expected to inherit his large fortune. Instead, his will, signed in Paris in November 1895, left most of his wealth to fund annual prizes for physics, chemistry, physiology or medicine, literature and peace, to be given to those who had conferred the greatest benefit on humankind regardless of nationality. Relatives challenged the will, and the institutions he named were unsure at first how to carry out his wishes. It took several years of legal work by his assistant Ragnar Sohlman before the Nobel Foundation was established and the first prizes were awarded in 1901."],
g:[["Laureate","A person who has won a major prize such as the Nobel"],["Nobel Foundation","The organization that manages Nobel’s fortune and the prizes"],["Dynamite","An explosive invented by Alfred Nobel"],["Nomination","A formal suggestion of a candidate for a prize"]],
f:["The first Nobel Prizes were awarded in 1901.","Alfred Nobel invented dynamite.","Maria Ressa was the first Filipino Nobel laureate."],
fun:"Marie Curie is the only person to have won Nobel Prizes in two different sciences.",
tl:"Ang Nobel Prize ay mga parangal para sa pisika, kemika, medisina, panitikan, kapayapaan at ekonomiya. Itinatag ito sa testamento ni Alfred Nobel, imbentor ng dinamita, at unang iginawad noong 1901. Si Maria Ressa ang unang Pilipinong nagwagi nito noong 2021."});

;
/* data/tech10.js */
// Technology, more articles: ocean energy, biofuels, barcodes and QR codes, and shipping containers.
ADDART({id:"oceanenergy",t:"Ocean Energy",c:"Energy",i:"🌊",w:"Marine energy",r:["renew","tides","hydropower","energytransition","pacific"],
p:[["Power from the sea","Ocean energy is electricity made from tides, waves, currents and the heat of seawater."],["Tidal power","Tidal power plants have run in France since 1966 and in South Korea since 2011."],["Promise for islands","Island nations such as the Philippines have strong tidal currents that could be harnessed."]],
l:"Ocean energy, also called marine energy, is renewable energy captured from the movement and temperature of the sea. Its main forms are tidal energy, from the rise and fall of tides and the strong currents they create; wave energy, from the up-and-down motion of waves; ocean current energy; and ocean thermal energy conversion, which uses the temperature difference between warm surface water and cold deep water. Tides are highly predictable, which is an advantage over wind and solar power. Ocean energy still produces only a tiny share of the world’s electricity, because the technology is expensive and must survive harsh sea conditions, but researchers and companies are developing new devices. Archipelagos such as the Philippines, with many narrow straits and strong currents, are considered promising locations.",
s:[["Tidal barrages","A tidal barrage is a dam built across an estuary or bay. As the tide rises and falls, water flows through turbines in the dam, generating electricity. The La Rance tidal power station in France, opened in 1966, was the first large one, and the Sihwa Lake station in South Korea, opened in 2011, is now the largest."],["Tidal stream turbines","Tidal stream turbines work like underwater wind turbines, placed in places where tidal currents flow fast, such as narrow channels between islands. Because water is much denser than air, slower currents can still turn turbines powerfully. Projects in Scotland have operated arrays of such turbines."],["Wave energy","Wave energy devices capture the motion of waves using floating buoys, hinged rafts, oscillating water columns or underwater pads. Waves carry enormous energy, especially along coasts facing large oceans, but devices must survive storms and saltwater corrosion, and few have operated commercially for long."],["Ocean thermal energy","In tropical seas, surface water can be over 20 degrees Celsius warmer than water a kilometer deep. Ocean thermal energy conversion uses this difference to run a heat engine that produces electricity. Small pilot plants have operated in Hawaii and Japan, and tropical islands are seen as possible sites."]],
k:[["Main forms","Tidal, wave, current, thermal"],["First large tidal plant","La Rance, France, 1966"],["Largest tidal plant","Sihwa Lake, South Korea"],["Main advantage","Predictable tides"],["Main challenges","Cost and harsh sea conditions"]],
d:[["c. 1100s","Tide mills grind grain along European coasts"],["1966","La Rance tidal power station opens in France"],["1984","A tidal plant opens at Annapolis Royal in Canada"],["2011","South Korea’s Sihwa Lake tidal station begins operating"],["2016","Tidal stream turbines begin supplying power in Scotland"],["2020s","Several countries test new wave and tidal devices"]],
s2:[["Tide mills","People have used the power of tides for centuries. Medieval tide mills in Europe trapped water in a pond at high tide and released it through a waterwheel as the tide fell, grinding grain into flour. Some old tide mills survive as museums."],["Ocean energy in the Philippines","Studies have identified many straits in the Philippines with strong tidal currents, such as the San Bernardino Strait between Luzon and Samar and channels in the Visayas, as potential sites for tidal stream energy. The country’s renewable energy law includes ocean energy, and developers have proposed pilot projects. Supporters see it as a way to power islands that now rely on expensive diesel."],["Environmental questions","Tidal barrages can change water levels, sediment and salinity in estuaries, affecting fish, birds and wetlands. Tidal stream turbines and wave devices have smaller footprints but may affect marine animals, noise and fishing. Scientists study their effects to design projects that protect ecosystems."],["Why so little ocean energy?","Despite its huge potential, ocean energy remains small because devices are costly to build, install and maintain in rough seas, saltwater corrodes equipment, and connecting offshore devices to power grids is expensive. Wind and solar costs have fallen much faster. Governments and researchers hope costs will drop as technology matures."],["Predictable power","Unlike wind and sunlight, tides follow the Moon’s orbit and can be predicted years in advance. This makes tidal power valuable for grids that need reliable supplies, as it can complement solar and wind. However, tidal power peaks and dips during each tidal cycle, so it still requires storage or other sources."],["Other ocean ideas","Researchers are also exploring salinity gradient power, which captures energy released when fresh river water mixes with salty seawater, and floating solar farms on the sea. Offshore wind turbines, while not strictly ocean energy, are the most successful way of generating electricity at sea so far."]],
sp:["The dam that runs on the Moon","Across the mouth of the Rance River in Brittany, France, stretches a 750-meter dam that also serves as a road. Inside it, 24 turbines turn as the tides rush in and out of the estuary, where the difference between high and low tide can reach 13 meters, among the largest in the world. Since 1966 the La Rance power station has generated electricity powered, ultimately, by the gravitational pull of the Moon and Sun on Earth’s oceans. For decades it was the largest tidal power plant in the world, and it remains a popular attraction, showing how the rhythm of the tides can be turned into electric power."],
g:[["Ocean energy","Renewable energy captured from the sea"],["Tidal barrage","A dam that generates electricity from tides"],["Tidal stream","A fast current created by the tides"],["Ocean thermal energy conversion","Making electricity from temperature differences in seawater"]],
f:["La Rance in France opened in 1966.","Tides can be predicted years ahead.","Water is about 800 times denser than air."],
fun:"Because seawater is about 800 times denser than air, a slow tidal current can push a turbine as strongly as a very strong wind.",
tl:"Ang enerhiya mula sa karagatan ay kuryenteng nalilikha mula sa tubig-alat, alon, agos at init ng dagat. May tidal power plant sa France mula 1966. Maraming makipot na daanang-dagat sa Pilipinas na may malalakas na agos na maaaring gamitin."});

ADDART({id:"biofuels",t:"Biofuels",c:"Energy",i:"🌽",w:"Biofuel",r:["energytransition","cars","foodsecurity","climchange","fossilfuels"],
p:[["Fuel from plants","Biofuels are fuels made from plants, algae or organic waste."],["Ethanol and biodiesel","The most common biofuels are ethanol, made from sugar or starch crops, and biodiesel, made from oils and fats."],["A debated solution","Biofuels can cut fossil fuel use, but they raise questions about food prices and land use."]],
l:"Biofuels are fuels produced from living things, such as crops, trees, algae and organic wastes, rather than from fossil fuels like oil and coal. The two most widely used are ethanol, an alcohol made by fermenting sugar from crops such as sugarcane and corn, and biodiesel, made from vegetable oils such as coconut, palm and soybean oil, or from used cooking oil and animal fats. Biofuels are usually blended with gasoline and diesel. Countries promote them to reduce oil imports, support farmers and cut greenhouse gas emissions. Brazil and the United States are the largest producers. The Philippines requires gasoline to contain ethanol and diesel to contain coconut-based biodiesel. Critics warn that growing fuel crops can compete with food, raise prices and drive deforestation.",
s:[["Ethanol","Ethanol is made by fermenting sugars with yeast, the same process used to make alcoholic drinks, and then distilling the alcohol. In Brazil it comes mainly from sugarcane, and in the United States mainly from corn. Most cars can run on gasoline with about 10 percent ethanol, called E10, and flex-fuel cars can use much higher blends."],["Biodiesel","Biodiesel is made by chemically treating vegetable oils or animal fats so they can be used in diesel engines. Rudolf Diesel himself demonstrated an engine running on peanut oil in 1900. In the Philippines, biodiesel is made from coconut oil and is known as coco-biodiesel or coconut methyl ester."],["Advanced biofuels","Newer biofuels are made from non-food materials, such as crop leftovers, grasses, wood chips, algae and used cooking oil. Sustainable aviation fuels made from such sources are being developed to reduce emissions from air travel, which is hard to power with batteries."],["How green are biofuels?","Plants absorb carbon dioxide as they grow, so burning fuels made from them can release less net carbon than fossil fuels. However, growing, harvesting, fertilizing and processing crops uses energy, and clearing forests or grasslands to plant fuel crops can release large amounts of carbon. Studies reach different conclusions depending on crops and methods."]],
k:[["Main types","Ethanol and biodiesel"],["Largest producers","United States and Brazil"],["Philippine law","Biofuels Act of 2006"],["Philippine biodiesel source","Coconut oil"],["Common blend","E10 (10 percent ethanol)"]],
d:[["1900","Rudolf Diesel shows an engine running on peanut oil"],["1908","Henry Ford’s Model T is designed to run on ethanol as well as gasoline"],["1975","Brazil launches its national ethanol program"],["2006","The Philippines passes the Biofuels Act"],["2007","Rising food prices spark a food versus fuel debate"],["2024","The Philippines raises the coconut biodiesel blend in diesel to 3 percent"]],
s2:[["Brazil’s ethanol","After the oil crisis of the 1970s, Brazil launched a national program in 1975 to produce ethanol from sugarcane. Today most new cars in Brazil are flex-fuel vehicles that can run on any mix of gasoline and ethanol, and all gasoline sold contains a large share of ethanol. Brazil’s sugarcane ethanol is considered one of the more efficient biofuels."],["Biofuels in the Philippines","The Biofuels Act of 2006 requires blending ethanol into gasoline and coconut biodiesel into diesel, aiming to reduce oil imports, cut emissions and support farmers. Gasoline contains about 10 percent ethanol, from sugarcane and imports. The biodiesel blend was raised to 3 percent in 2024, but a planned increase to 4 percent was suspended in 2025 because of high coconut oil prices and supply concerns, a decision debated by coconut farmers and the fuel industry."],["Food versus fuel","When food prices rose sharply in 2007 and 2008, some experts blamed the growing use of corn, sugar and vegetable oils for fuel. Others argued that oil prices, weather and other factors were more important. The debate continues, and many countries have limited the share of food crops used for biofuels."],["Palm oil and forests","Palm oil is used to make biodiesel in Indonesia, Malaysia and elsewhere. The expansion of oil palm plantations has been linked to the clearing of tropical rainforests and peatlands, threatening orangutans and other wildlife and releasing carbon. Certification schemes and rules aim to ensure sustainable production, though critics say they are not always effective."],["Used cooking oil","Used cooking oil from restaurants and food factories can be collected and turned into biodiesel or aviation fuel, reducing waste and avoiding the need for new crops. Demand for it has grown so much that authorities watch for fraud, such as fresh oil being sold as used."],["Biofuels and the future","Electric vehicles may reduce the need for biofuels in cars, but biofuels may remain important for ships, aircraft and heavy machinery. Researchers continue to explore algae and other sources that could produce fuel without competing for farmland."]],
sp:["The car that ran on alcohol","When Henry Ford designed the Model T, introduced in 1908, he made it able to run on gasoline, kerosene or ethanol. Ford was enthusiastic about fuel made from plants, imagining farmers growing their own fuel and saying that it could be made from anything that could be fermented, from fruit to weeds. Cheap gasoline soon won out, and ethanol was largely forgotten until the oil shocks of the 1970s. Today, more than a century later, the idea of running cars on fuel grown in fields is again part of the debate over how to reduce dependence on oil."],
g:[["Biofuel","A fuel made from plants, algae or organic waste"],["Ethanol","An alcohol fuel made by fermenting sugar"],["Biodiesel","A diesel fuel made from vegetable oils or fats"],["Flex-fuel vehicle","A car that can run on gasoline, ethanol or any mix"]],
f:["Philippine diesel contains coconut biodiesel.","Brazil makes ethanol from sugarcane.","Rudolf Diesel ran an engine on peanut oil."],
fun:"Rudolf Diesel demonstrated one of his engines running on peanut oil at the 1900 World’s Fair in Paris.",
tl:"Ang biofuel ay panggatong na gawa sa halaman, algae o organikong basura. Ang pinakakaraniwan ay ethanol at biodiesel. Sa Pilipinas, may ethanol ang gasolina at may biodiesel mula sa niyog ang diesel."});

ADDART({id:"qrcodes",t:"Barcodes and QR Codes",c:"Technology",i:"🔳",w:"QR code",r:["ecommerce","digitalpay","smartphone","binary","onlinescams"],
p:[["Codes machines can read","Barcodes and QR codes store information in patterns that machines can scan."],["From groceries to payments","They are used to price groceries, track parcels, pay bills and open websites."],["Invented in 1994","The QR code was invented in Japan to track car parts."]],
l:"Barcodes and QR codes are patterns that store information in a form that machines can read quickly. A traditional barcode is a row of black and white lines of different widths that represents numbers, scanned by a laser or camera. The first product sold using a barcode scan was a pack of chewing gum in the United States in 1974. A QR code, short for quick response code, is a square grid of black and white modules that can store much more information, such as web addresses and payment details. It was invented in 1994 by the Japanese company Denso Wave to track car parts. With the spread of smartphone cameras, QR codes became part of daily life, used for payments, menus, tickets and contact tracing during the COVID-19 pandemic.",
s:[["How barcodes work","A barcode represents numbers using the widths of bars and spaces. A scanner shines light across the code and measures the reflected pattern, turning it into digits that a computer looks up in a database to find the product, its price and stock levels. The most common retail barcodes are the UPC in North America and EAN codes used in many other countries."],["How QR codes work","A QR code stores data in two directions, horizontally and vertically, so it can hold far more information than a barcode. Three large squares in the corners help scanners find and align the code from any angle. Error correction allows a QR code to be read even if part of it is damaged or covered."],["Everyday uses","Barcodes help shops speed up checkout and track inventory, airlines handle luggage, hospitals identify patients and medicines, and libraries manage books. QR codes link to websites, store event tickets and boarding passes, share contact details, connect to Wi-Fi and allow quick payments from phones."],["QR payments","In many Asian countries, including the Philippines, China and India, paying by scanning QR codes with mobile wallets has become very common, even at small stores and market stalls. The Philippines introduced a national QR code standard, called QR Ph, in 2019 so that different banks and e-wallets can accept the same codes."]],
k:[["First barcode scan at checkout","1974"],["QR code invented","1994"],["Inventor of QR code","Masahiro Hara, Denso Wave"],["QR stands for","Quick response"],["Philippine QR standard","QR Ph, 2019"]],
d:[["1952","Norman Woodland and Bernard Silver patent a barcode design"],["1974","A pack of chewing gum becomes the first product sold by scanning a UPC barcode"],["1994","Denso Wave invents the QR code"],["2000","The QR code becomes an international standard"],["2019","The Philippines launches the QR Ph standard for payments"],["2020","QR codes are widely used for contact tracing and menus during the COVID-19 pandemic"]],
s2:[["A barcode drawn in sand","According to a famous story, the barcode was inspired on a beach in Florida in 1948, when the engineer Norman Joseph Woodland drew lines in the sand with his fingers while thinking about Morse code, stretching dots and dashes into thin and thick lines. He and Bernard Silver patented a barcode design in 1952, though it took two decades and the invention of lasers and cheap computers before barcodes became practical."],["The first scan","At 8:01 in the morning on 26 June 1974, at a supermarket in Troy, Ohio, a cashier scanned a 10-pack of Wrigley’s chewing gum, the first retail product sold using a Universal Product Code. The pack is now kept in a museum in Washington, DC. Barcodes soon spread worldwide, changing how shops operate."],["Inventing the QR code","In the early 1990s, workers in Japanese car factories had to scan many barcodes to track parts. Masahiro Hara, an engineer at Denso Wave, a company related to Toyota, set out to design a code that could hold more information and be read faster. Inspired partly by the black and white stones of the game Go, he created the QR code in 1994. Denso Wave chose not to enforce its patent, allowing anyone to use it freely, which helped it spread."],["The pandemic boost","During the COVID-19 pandemic, QR codes became common for contactless menus, payments and health check-ins. In the Philippines, many local governments used QR-based contact tracing systems for entering stores and offices. The pandemic made millions of people comfortable scanning codes with their phones."],["QR code scams","Criminals sometimes place fake QR codes over real ones on parking meters, posters or payment stands, directing people to fraudulent websites or payment accounts, a scam sometimes called quishing. Experts advise checking the website address after scanning, avoiding codes that look tampered with and being cautious with codes received in unexpected messages."],["Beyond codes","Newer technologies such as RFID tags, which use radio waves, and near-field communication, used in contactless cards and phones, can identify items and allow payments without a visible pattern. Even so, barcodes and QR codes remain popular because they are cheap, easy to print and readable by almost any camera."]],
sp:["Inspired by a board game","Masahiro Hara liked to play Go, an ancient board game in which players place black and white stones on a grid. One day, while thinking about how to make a better code for tracking car parts, he imagined information arranged like stones on a Go board, in a pattern that could be read in two directions. His team experimented with designs and added three distinctive squares in the corners, carefully chosen so that their pattern would rarely appear in ordinary printed material, allowing scanners to find the code instantly. The result was the QR code, which can be read in a fraction of a second, even upside down."],
g:[["Barcode","A pattern of lines representing numbers for machines to read"],["QR code","A square, two-dimensional code that stores information"],["Scanner","A device that reads codes using light"],["Error correction","A method that lets damaged codes still be read"]],
f:["The first barcode scan was a pack of chewing gum in 1974.","The QR code was invented in Japan in 1994.","QR Ph is the Philippine payment QR standard."],
fun:"The inventor of the QR code was partly inspired by the black and white stones of the board game Go.",
tl:"Ang barcode at QR code ay mga disenyo na nag-iimbak ng impormasyon na nababasa ng makina. Ang unang produktong na-scan sa barcode ay chewing gum noong 1974. Inimbento sa Japan ang QR code noong 1994 at ginagamit na ngayon sa pagbabayad at iba pa."});

ADDART({id:"containers",t:"Shipping Containers",c:"Transport",i:"🚢",w:"Containerization",r:["ships","ecommerce","canals","panamacountry","rail"],
p:[["Steel boxes","Shipping containers are standard steel boxes used to move goods around the world."],["A trade revolution","Containers made shipping faster and cheaper, transforming global trade."],["Giant ships","The largest container ships carry more than 20,000 containers."]],
l:"A shipping container is a large, standard-sized steel box used to transport goods by ship, train and truck without unloading the contents in between. Before containers, cargo was loaded piece by piece by teams of dockworkers, which was slow, costly and prone to theft and damage. In 1956 the American trucking businessman Malcom McLean sent the first commercially successful container ship from New Jersey to Texas. Containers were standardized internationally in the late 1960s, and container shipping grew rapidly, sharply reducing the cost of moving goods. Today millions of containers travel the world’s seas, and most manufactured goods, from clothes and electronics to food, spend part of their journey in one. Containerization is considered one of the most important forces behind globalization.",
s:[["Standard sizes","Most containers are 20 or 40 feet long, about 6 or 12 meters, and about 2.4 meters wide. Their size is measured in twenty-foot equivalent units, or TEU; a 40-foot container counts as two TEU. Standard corner fittings allow cranes, ships, trains and trucks around the world to lift and lock them in place."],["How containers changed shipping","Before containers, loading a ship could take days or weeks. With containers, giant cranes can load or unload a container in a couple of minutes, and ships spend far less time in port. Goods are sealed from factory to destination, reducing theft and damage. The cost of shipping fell dramatically."],["Container ships","Container ships have grown enormously, from a few hundred containers in the 1950s to more than 20,000 TEU today. The largest are about 400 meters long, longer than the Eiffel Tower is tall. They sail between major ports in Asia, Europe and the Americas along busy routes, many passing through the Suez and Panama canals."],["Ports","Container ports have huge cranes, stacking yards and connections to railways and highways. The busiest are in Asia, especially China, along with Singapore. In the Philippines, the Manila International Container Terminal is the largest, and other ports such as Batangas, Subic, Cebu and Davao handle container traffic."]],
k:[["First container ship voyage","Ideal X, 1956"],["Pioneer","Malcom McLean"],["Standard unit","TEU (twenty-foot equivalent unit)"],["Largest ships","More than 20,000 TEU"],["Largest Philippine container port","Manila International Container Terminal"]],
d:[["1956","Malcom McLean’s Ideal X carries containers from Newark to Houston"],["1966","The first transatlantic container service begins"],["1968","International standards for container sizes are agreed"],["1988","The Manila International Container Terminal begins private operation"],["2021","The container ship Ever Given blocks the Suez Canal for six days"],["2023","The largest container ships exceed 24,000 TEU"]],
s2:[["Malcom McLean’s idea","Malcom McLean, a trucking company owner in North Carolina, grew frustrated waiting at ports while his trucks’ cargo was unloaded by hand. He decided to load entire truck trailers, and then just their boxes, onto ships. On 26 April 1956 his converted oil tanker, the Ideal X, sailed from Newark, New Jersey, to Houston, Texas, carrying 58 containers. Loading costs fell dramatically compared with traditional methods."],["Globalization","By making shipping cheap and reliable, containers allowed companies to manufacture goods wherever costs were lowest and ship them around the world. Many economists consider containerization as important to globalization as trade agreements. It helped Asian economies, including China and Southeast Asian countries, become major exporters."],["Dockworkers and ports","Containers sharply reduced the number of dockworkers needed, causing job losses and strikes in many port cities. Old ports in city centers declined, while new container terminals were built on open land with deep water. Some former docklands, such as in London, were redeveloped as offices and homes."],["The Ever Given","In March 2021 the Ever Given, one of the world’s largest container ships, ran aground in the Suez Canal during a sandstorm, blocking it for six days. Hundreds of ships were stuck waiting, delaying global trade worth billions of dollars. The incident showed how much the world economy depends on container shipping routes."],["Supply chain shocks","During the COVID-19 pandemic, shortages of containers, port congestion and high demand caused shipping costs to soar and delayed deliveries worldwide. The Philippines and other import-dependent countries saw higher prices for goods. Businesses began rethinking long supply chains."],["Containers as buildings","Old shipping containers are often reused as homes, shops, offices, cafes, classrooms and emergency shelters. Container architecture can be quick and relatively cheap, though containers need insulation and ventilation, especially in hot climates. Container parks with food stalls have appeared in Philippine cities."]],
sp:["The box that changed the world","On 26 April 1956, a crowd gathered at a port in Newark, New Jersey, to watch an unusual sight. A crane lifted 58 aluminum truck bodies onto the deck of an old tanker named the Ideal X. Five days later, in Houston, trucks were waiting to collect the boxes and drive them straight to their destinations. Malcom McLean calculated that loading cost had fallen from several dollars per tonne to just a few cents. Dockworkers and shipping companies were skeptical at first, but within a few decades almost all general cargo traveled in containers, and the humble steel box had reshaped the world economy."],
g:[["Shipping container","A standard steel box for moving goods"],["TEU","Twenty-foot equivalent unit, a measure of container capacity"],["Containerization","The use of standard containers to move goods"],["Supply chain","The steps that bring a product from maker to buyer"]],
f:["The first container ship voyage was in 1956.","A 40-foot container equals two TEU.","The Ever Given blocked the Suez Canal in 2021."],
fun:"The largest container ships can carry more than 24,000 twenty-foot containers, enough to hold hundreds of millions of bananas.",
tl:"Ang shipping container ay kahong bakal na may pamantayang laki na ginagamit sa paglilipat ng kalakal sa barko, tren at trak. Pinabilis at pinamura nito ang pagpapadala ng produkto sa buong mundo. Inilunsad ito ni Malcom McLean noong 1956."});

;
/* data/arts10.js */
// Arts and Culture, more articles: stage magic, film festivals, kulintang and tattooing.
ADDART({id:"stagemagic",t:"Stage Magic",c:"Performing Arts",i:"🎩",w:"Magic (illusion)",r:["theatre","circus","illusions","psychology","tv"],
p:[["The art of illusion","Stage magic is the performing art of creating seemingly impossible effects."],["Tricks and secrets","Magicians use skill, misdirection and clever devices, not real magic."],["Famous magicians","Performers such as Robert-Houdin and Harry Houdini made magic world-famous."]],
l:"Stage magic is a performing art in which entertainers, called magicians or illusionists, create effects that seem impossible, such as making objects appear and vanish, reading minds, escaping from chains or sawing a person in half. Magicians do not use supernatural powers; they rely on sleight of hand, misdirection, psychology, hidden mechanisms and careful rehearsal. Magic has been performed for thousands of years, from street performers in ancient times to grand theater shows, television specials and close-up tricks at parties. The French magician Jean-Eugène Robert-Houdin is often called the father of modern magic, and Harry Houdini became famous worldwide for his daring escapes. Magicians traditionally keep their methods secret.",
s:[["Kinds of magic","Close-up magic is performed right in front of a small audience, often with cards, coins or everyday objects. Stage illusions use large props and assistants on a theater stage. Mentalism creates the impression of mind reading or prediction. Escape artistry involves breaking free from locks, ropes, handcuffs or tanks of water."],["Misdirection","A key skill in magic is misdirection: guiding the audience’s attention away from the secret move. A magician may look at one hand while the other does the work, tell a joke at the crucial moment or use a big gesture to draw eyes elsewhere. Scientists studying attention have learned from magicians."],["Sleight of hand","Sleight of hand, or legerdemain, is the skillful use of the hands to secretly move, hide or switch objects. Card and coin magicians practice moves for many hours to make them look natural and invisible. Some techniques have been passed down for centuries."],["The magician’s code","Magicians generally follow an informal code: never reveal the secret of a trick to non-magicians, never perform the same trick twice for the same audience and always practice. Magic societies such as The Magic Circle in London, founded in 1905, promote the art and share knowledge among members."]],
k:[["Father of modern magic","Jean-Eugène Robert-Houdin"],["Famous escape artist","Harry Houdini"],["Key techniques","Misdirection, sleight of hand"],["The Magic Circle founded","1905, London"],["Famous illusion","Sawing a person in half"]],
d:[["c. 2500 BC","Egyptian stories tell of magicians performing wonders"],["1584","Reginald Scot’s book explains how conjuring tricks are done"],["1845","Robert-Houdin opens his theater of magic in Paris"],["1905","The Magic Circle is founded in London"],["1921","The illusion of sawing a woman in half is first performed"],["1983","David Copperfield makes the Statue of Liberty appear to vanish on television"]],
s2:[["Robert-Houdin","Jean-Eugène Robert-Houdin, a French clockmaker who became a magician, opened a small theater in Paris in 1845. Instead of the traditional wizard’s robes, he performed in elegant evening clothes, presenting magic as refined entertainment. He used his mechanical skills to build ingenious automata and illusions. Later magicians honored him as the father of modern magic."],["Harry Houdini","Harry Houdini, born Erik Weisz in Hungary in 1874 and raised in the United States, became the most famous magician in history through his escapes from handcuffs, straitjackets, locked trunks and water tanks. He also spent his later years exposing fake mediums who claimed to contact the dead. He died in 1926."],["Magic and science","Magicians and scientists have long been connected. Some magicians, like Robert-Houdin, were skilled engineers. Others have helped scientists test people claiming psychic powers. Today, neuroscientists study magic tricks to understand attention, perception and memory, because magic reveals how easily the brain can be fooled."],["Exposing fake powers","Some people have claimed real supernatural powers, such as communicating with spirits or bending spoons with their minds. Magicians such as Houdini and later James Randi argued that such feats could be done with ordinary tricks and challenged performers to prove their powers under controlled conditions."],["Magic on screen","Television brought magic to huge audiences. Large illusions, such as making landmarks vanish or walking through walls, were staged as television specials, while street magic shows filmed reactions of passersby. Online videos have made magic tricks, and sometimes their secrets, widely accessible."],["Learning magic","Many magicians begin as children with simple tricks from magic sets and books. Learning magic builds hand skills, confidence and public speaking. Magic clubs, conventions and competitions bring together amateurs and professionals, including in the Philippines, where magicians perform at parties, events and on television."]],
sp:["Sawing a woman in half","In 1921 the British magician P. T. Selbit presented a shocking new illusion in London: a woman lay inside a wooden box, and he appeared to saw straight through it, and her. The trick caused a sensation, and theater managers reportedly placed nurses and ambulances outside to add to the drama. Soon many magicians were performing their own versions, and arguments broke out over who had invented it. A century later, the illusion remains one of the most famous in magic, and its secret, though widely guessed, still delights audiences when performed well."],
g:[["Magician","A performer who creates illusions"],["Misdirection","Guiding attention away from a secret move"],["Sleight of hand","Skillful secret movement of the hands"],["Mentalism","Magic that seems to show mind reading"]],
f:["Robert-Houdin is called the father of modern magic.","Houdini was famous for escapes.","The Magic Circle was founded in 1905."],
fun:"Harry Houdini took his stage name in honor of the French magician Jean-Eugène Robert-Houdin.",
tl:"Ang stage magic ay sining ng pagtatanghal ng tila imposibleng mahika. Gumagamit ang mga salamangkero ng husay ng kamay, panlilinlang sa atensiyon at mga lihim na kagamitan. Tanyag sina Robert-Houdin at Harry Houdini."});

ADDART({id:"filmfestivals",t:"Film Festivals",c:"Film",i:"🎞️",w:"Film festival",r:["cinema","phcinema","documentary","anim","festivals"],
p:[["Celebrating films","Film festivals are events where new films are shown, judged and celebrated."],["Famous festivals","Venice, Cannes and Berlin are among the oldest and most prestigious."],["Filipino films abroad","Filipino filmmakers and actors have won top awards at international festivals."]],
l:"A film festival is an organized event, usually held in one city over several days, where films are screened for audiences, critics and filmmakers, and often compete for awards. Festivals introduce new films, launch careers, help independent and foreign films find audiences and distributors, and celebrate cinema as an art. The oldest major festival, the Venice Film Festival, began in 1932, followed by Cannes in France and Berlin in Germany, which are often considered the three most prestigious. Thousands of festivals now take place worldwide, from huge events to small local ones. In the Philippines, the Metro Manila Film Festival, held every Christmas season since 1975, and independent festivals such as Cinemalaya are major events, and Filipino filmmakers have won top prizes abroad.",
s:[["What happens at a festival","Festivals screen new films, often for the first time, called premieres. Juries of filmmakers, actors and critics award prizes. Filmmakers attend to present their work, answer audience questions and meet distributors who buy rights to show films in cinemas, on television or on streaming services. Many festivals also hold film markets and workshops."],["The big three","The Venice Film Festival, founded in 1932, awards the Golden Lion. The Cannes Film Festival, first held in 1946, awards the Palme d’Or and is famous for its red carpet on the French Riviera. The Berlin International Film Festival, begun in 1951, awards the Golden Bear. Together they are often called the big three."],["Other major festivals","The Toronto International Film Festival in Canada is a major showcase and is often seen as an early indicator for awards season. The Sundance Film Festival in the United States champions independent films. The Busan International Film Festival in South Korea, founded in 1996, is one of Asia’s leading festivals."],["Independent cinema","Festivals are especially important for independent filmmakers who work outside big studios with small budgets. A festival award or strong reviews can help a small film find a distributor and an audience. Many celebrated directors first gained attention at festivals."]],
k:[["Oldest major festival","Venice, 1932"],["Cannes top prize","Palme d’Or"],["Berlin top prize","Golden Bear"],["Metro Manila Film Festival","Since 1975"],["Philippine independent festival","Cinemalaya, since 2005"]],
d:[["1932","The first Venice Film Festival is held"],["1946","The first Cannes Film Festival takes place"],["1951","The Berlin International Film Festival begins"],["1975","The first Metro Manila Film Festival is held"],["2005","The Cinemalaya Philippine Independent Film Festival is launched"],["2016","Lav Diaz wins the Golden Lion and Jaclyn Jose wins best actress at Cannes"]],
s2:[["Why Cannes began","The Cannes Film Festival was planned for 1939 partly in response to the Venice festival, which had come under the influence of Italy’s fascist government. The first edition was canceled when the Second World War began, and the festival finally opened in 1946. It has since become a glamorous event attended by film stars from around the world."],["Filipino triumphs abroad","Filipino filmmakers have won major international awards. Brillante Mendoza won best director at Cannes in 2009 for Kinatay. In 2016 Jaclyn Jose became the first Southeast Asian actor to win best actress at Cannes, for Ma’ Rosa, and Lav Diaz won the Golden Lion at Venice for The Woman Who Left, after winning a Silver Bear in Berlin earlier that year. Earlier, directors such as Lino Brocka brought Filipino films to Cannes in the 1970s and 1980s."],["The Metro Manila Film Festival","Every year from Christmas Day into early January, cinemas across Metro Manila, and later the whole country, show only Filipino films entered in the Metro Manila Film Festival. Families often watch films together during the holidays. The festival features a parade of floats and an awards night. Debates often arise over whether entries should favor commercial blockbusters or artistic films."],["Cinemalaya and independent film","Cinemalaya, launched in 2005 at the Cultural Center of the Philippines, gives grants to independent filmmakers and screens their works, helping launch many new directors and actors. Other festivals, such as QCinema in Quezon City and regional film festivals, have expanded opportunities for Filipino independent cinema."],["Festivals and streaming","Streaming services now buy many festival films, and some festivals debate whether films made mainly for streaming should compete alongside films made for cinemas. The COVID-19 pandemic led festivals to hold online editions, making films accessible to wider audiences."],["Small and local festivals","Beyond the famous events, thousands of festivals focus on particular kinds of films, such as short films, documentaries, animation, children’s films, environmental films or films from certain regions. Student film festivals in schools and universities help young filmmakers share their work."]],
sp:["A Filipino on the Croisette","In May 2016, at the Cannes Film Festival, the jury announced the best actress award, and the name called was Jaclyn Jose, for her role in Brillante Mendoza’s Ma’ Rosa, about a mother who runs a small store in a poor Manila neighborhood and is arrested in a drug raid. She became the first Filipino and first Southeast Asian actor to win the prize. Back home, the news was celebrated in newspapers and on social media, and the award brought international attention to Filipino cinema. Jose, a veteran of many films and television shows, died in 2024, remembered as one of the country’s finest actors."],
g:[["Film festival","An event where films are screened and often judged"],["Premiere","The first public showing of a film"],["Palme d’Or","The top prize at the Cannes Film Festival"],["Distributor","A company that brings films to cinemas or streaming services"]],
f:["Venice is the oldest major film festival.","The Metro Manila Film Festival began in 1975.","Jaclyn Jose won best actress at Cannes in 2016."],
fun:"The first Cannes Film Festival was planned for September 1939 but was canceled when the Second World War broke out.",
tl:"Ang film festival ay pagdiriwang kung saan ipinalalabas at hinuhusgahan ang mga bagong pelikula. Kabilang sa pinakatanyag ang Venice, Cannes at Berlin. Sa Pilipinas, tuwing Pasko idinaraos ang Metro Manila Film Festival mula pa noong 1975."});

ADDART({id:"kulintang",t:"Kulintang",c:"Music",i:"🔔",w:"Kulintang",r:["phdance","indianmusic","drums","orchestra","phfolk"],
p:[["A row of gongs","The kulintang is a row of small, bossed gongs played with sticks."],["Music of Mindanao","It is the heart of gong ensembles of the Maguindanao, Maranao and other peoples."],["Shared across islands","Related gong traditions are found in Indonesia, Malaysia and Brunei."]],
l:"The kulintang is a musical instrument made of a row of small, knobbed gongs laid horizontally on a wooden frame and played with two wooden beaters. It is also the name of the ensemble it leads, which includes larger hanging gongs and a drum. Kulintang music is a living tradition of peoples in the southern Philippines, especially the Maguindanao, Maranao and Tausug of Mindanao and the Sulu Archipelago, and related gong-chime traditions exist in Indonesia, Malaysia, Brunei and East Timor. Played at weddings, festivals, celebrations and gatherings, kulintang music is mostly learned by ear and features lively rhythms and improvised melodies. It is a proud symbol of the cultural heritage of the Bangsamoro and other Mindanao peoples.",
s:[["The instrument","A kulintang set usually has eight gongs of different sizes and pitches, arranged from lowest to highest in a row on a wooden frame called an antangan. Each gong has a raised knob, or boss, in the center, which the player strikes with soft wooden beaters to produce clear, ringing tones."],["The ensemble","The kulintang leads an ensemble that, among the Maguindanao, may include the agung, a pair of large hanging gongs with deep tones; the gandingan, four large hanging gongs; the babendil, a small gong played on its rim for rhythm; and the dabakan, a goblet-shaped drum. Each instrument has its own role in creating a rich, layered sound."],["How it is played","Kulintang music is based on rhythmic modes rather than fixed written compositions. Musicians learn by listening and imitating, and the kulintang player improvises melodies within the patterns of a chosen mode. Performances can be energetic and virtuosic, and good players are admired for their creativity."],["Where it is heard","Kulintang music is played at weddings, religious and community celebrations, welcoming guests and festivals. It once also played a role in courtship, as young people performed for one another. Today it is also performed in schools, cultural shows and concerts in the Philippines and abroad."]],
k:[["Instrument","Row of small knobbed gongs"],["Typical number of gongs","Eight"],["Main peoples","Maguindanao, Maranao, Tausug"],["Ensemble instruments","Agung, gandingan, babendil, dabakan"],["Region","Mindanao, Sulu and neighboring countries"]],
d:[["c. 1000s","Gong-chime instruments spread through maritime Southeast Asia"],["1500s","Gongs are traded across the Sulu and Celebes seas"],["1950s","Researchers begin recording kulintang music in Mindanao"],["1960s","Folk dance companies bring kulintang music to world stages"],["1993","Kutyapi master Samaon Sulaiman receives the National Living Treasures Award"],["2000s","University programs in the Philippines and abroad teach kulintang"]],
s2:[["Origins","Gongs came to Southeast Asia through trade, possibly from mainland Asia, many centuries ago. Local peoples developed their own instruments and music, including gong-chime instruments such as the kulintang in the southern Philippines, the bonang of Java and the kulintangan of Borneo. Bronze gongs were also valued as heirlooms, dowry gifts and symbols of wealth."],["Maguindanao and Maranao styles","Different peoples have distinct kulintang styles. Maguindanao music is often described as lively and rhythmically complex, while Maranao music is known for its flowing melodies. Players from each group recognize the modes and pieces of their own tradition."],["Women and kulintang","Traditionally, the kulintang has often been played by women, while men frequently play the agung and other instruments, though both may play all instruments. Skilled kulintang players are highly respected in their communities."],["Kulintang and dance","Kulintang music accompanies traditional dances of Mindanao, such as the singkil, a Maranao dance in which a princess steps gracefully between clapping bamboo poles. Philippine folk dance companies, such as the Bayanihan, have performed these dances with kulintang music around the world."],["Masters of the tradition","Master musicians have helped preserve kulintang music. Some, such as the Maguindanao musician Danongan Kalanduyan, taught in the United States, where kulintang ensembles formed in universities and Filipino American communities. The Philippines’ National Living Treasures Award has honored masters of Mindanao music, such as Samaon Sulaiman, a master of the kutyapi lute."],["Keeping the music alive","Changing lifestyles, conflict and the popularity of modern music have challenged traditional music in parts of Mindanao. Schools of living traditions, cultural centers, universities and community groups teach young people to play the kulintang and other instruments, helping keep the tradition alive."]],
sp:["The singkil and the gongs","In the Maranao dance called singkil, a princess wearing a tall headdress and holding fans steps calmly in and out of pairs of bamboo poles that are clapped together in faster and faster rhythms, while an attendant shades her with an umbrella. The dance is said to retell an episode from the Darangen, the great epic of the Maranao people. Behind the dancers, musicians play the kulintang, gongs and drum, their ringing patterns guiding the dancers’ steps. When performed by folk dance companies on stages around the world, the singkil and its gong music have become one of the best-known images of Philippine culture."],
g:[["Kulintang","A row of small, knobbed gongs, and the ensemble it leads"],["Boss","The raised knob in the center of a gong"],["Agung","A pair of large hanging gongs"],["Dabakan","A goblet-shaped drum used in kulintang ensembles"]],
f:["A kulintang usually has eight gongs.","It is played by peoples of Mindanao and Sulu.","Kulintang music is learned by ear."],
fun:"In some Mindanao communities, sets of bronze gongs were valued heirlooms and could be given as part of a marriage dowry.",
tl:"Ang kulintang ay hanay ng maliliit na gong na tinutugtog gamit ang dalawang patpat. Mahalagang tradisyon ito ng mga Maguindanao, Maranao at Tausug ng Mindanao at Sulu. Tinutugtog ito sa kasalan, pagdiriwang at pagtitipon."});

ADDART({id:"tattoo",t:"Tattooing",c:"Art",i:"🖋️",w:"Tattoo",r:["skin","phfolk","modernart","streetart","caveart"],
p:[["Art on skin","Tattooing is the art of putting permanent designs into the skin with ink."],["Ancient roots","People have been tattooed for more than 5,000 years."],["Philippine traditions","Peoples such as the Kalinga and Visayans have long tattoo traditions."]],
l:"A tattoo is a permanent design made by inserting ink or pigment into the dermis, the layer of skin beneath the surface. Tattooing is one of the oldest art forms: Ötzi the Iceman, who lived about 5,300 years ago, had dozens of tattoos. Across the world, tattoos have marked identity, status, bravery, religious devotion, healing, love and memory. In the Philippines, many peoples had rich tattoo traditions; Spanish chroniclers called Visayans pintados, the painted ones, and the Kalinga of the Cordillera still practice traditional hand-tapped tattooing, made famous by the master tattooist Apo Whang-od. Tattoos have had different meanings in different times and places, being honored in some cultures and stigmatized in others. Today they are widely popular as personal art.",
s:[["How tattoos are made","Traditional methods include tapping ink into the skin with a sharpened stick, thorn or comb, or pricking it with needles. Modern tattoo machines, invented in 1891, use electrically powered needles that move up and down rapidly. The ink settles in the dermis, where it stays for life, although it may fade over time."],["Meanings of tattoos","In many traditional cultures, tattoos showed a person’s family, community, achievements or passage into adulthood. Warriors might earn tattoos for bravery, and women might receive them at marriage or for beauty. Some tattoos were believed to protect or heal. Today people get tattoos for many personal reasons, such as remembering loved ones or expressing their identity."],["Tattoos around the world","Polynesian peoples of the Pacific developed elaborate tattoo traditions; the English word tattoo comes from the Samoan or Tahitian word tatau, brought to Europe after Captain James Cook’s voyages in the 1760s and 1770s. Japan has a long tradition of full-body tattoos called irezumi. The Māori of New Zealand practice tā moko, including facial designs."],["Health and safety","Tattooing breaks the skin, so it must be done with sterile, single-use needles, clean equipment and safe inks to prevent infections such as hepatitis. Licensed studios follow hygiene rules. Laws in many places, including the Philippines, set age limits for getting tattoos. Tattoos can be removed with lasers, but removal is costly and may not be complete."]],
k:[["Oldest known tattooed person","Ötzi the Iceman, about 5,300 years ago"],["Word origin","Polynesian tatau"],["Modern tattoo machine","1891"],["Spanish name for tattooed Visayans","Pintados"],["Famous Kalinga tattoo artist","Apo Whang-od"]],
d:[["c. 3300 BC","Ötzi the Iceman bears about 61 tattoos"],["c. 1590","The Boxer Codex illustrates tattooed Visayans"],["1769","Captain James Cook’s crew records the Tahitian word tatau"],["1891","Samuel O’Reilly patents an electric tattoo machine"],["1990s","Traditional tattooing begins a revival among Filipinos and Pacific peoples"],["2023","Apo Whang-od appears on the cover of Vogue Philippines at age 106"]],
s2:[["The pintados","When Spanish explorers arrived in the Visayas in the 1500s, they were struck by the extensive tattoos of many men and women, covering arms, chests, backs and faces. They called the islands the Islas de los Pintados, the islands of the painted ones. Tattoos showed bravery in battle and were considered marks of beauty. The Boxer Codex, an illustrated manuscript from around 1590, shows tattooed Visayans."],["Kalinga batok","Among the Kalinga of the Cordillera in northern Luzon, traditional tattooing, called batok or batek, is done by tapping a thorn attached to a stick with another stick, using ink made of soot and water. In the past, men earned tattoos as warriors, while women were tattooed for beauty and identity. The designs represent things such as centipedes, snakes, ferns, rice terraces and eagles."],["Apo Whang-od","Whang-od Oggay of Buscalan, Kalinga, born around 1917, became known as the last traditional mambabatok of her generation. Thousands of visitors have traveled to her mountain village to receive her tattoos, and she has trained younger relatives to carry on the tradition. In 2023 she appeared on the cover of Vogue Philippines, becoming its oldest cover model. Her fame has brought tourism and attention, along with debates about protecting the tradition’s cultural meaning."],["Tattoos and stigma","In many places, tattoos have at times been associated with sailors, soldiers, prisoners or gangs, and people with tattoos have faced discrimination in jobs or public places. In Japan, some baths and gyms still restrict people with visible tattoos. In the Philippines, attitudes have changed, though some employers and institutions have rules about visible tattoos."],["Revival of traditional tattoos","In recent decades, many Indigenous peoples, including Filipinos at home and abroad, Polynesians and the Māori, have revived traditional tattooing as a way to reconnect with ancestors and identity. Filipino tattoo artists and researchers have studied old records and elders’ memories to bring back traditional designs, with attention to their meanings."],["Tattoos today","Tattoos are now popular across many countries and age groups, with styles ranging from fine-line and watercolor designs to realistic portraits and traditional patterns. Tattoo conventions attract artists and fans, and tattoo artists are increasingly recognized as skilled artists."]],
sp:["The village in the clouds","Reaching Buscalan, a village high in the mountains of Kalinga, once meant hours of travel by road followed by a steep hike up rice terrace paths. Yet thousands of visitors have made the journey, hoping to receive a tattoo from Apo Whang-od, the legendary tattoo artist who learned her craft as a teenager. Using a thorn from a citrus tree fixed to a bamboo stick, she taps soot-based ink into the skin with a steady rhythm, creating designs passed down for generations. In her later years, her signature was often three simple dots. Her grandnieces and other young women now continue the tradition, ensuring that Kalinga tattooing lives on."],
g:[["Tattoo","A permanent design made by putting ink into the skin"],["Dermis","The layer of skin beneath the surface where tattoo ink stays"],["Batok","Traditional Kalinga hand-tapped tattooing"],["Pintados","The painted ones, a Spanish name for tattooed Visayans"]],
f:["Ötzi the Iceman had about 61 tattoos.","The word tattoo comes from Polynesia.","Apo Whang-od is a famous Kalinga tattoo artist."],
fun:"Spanish explorers named the Visayan islands the islands of the painted ones because so many people there were tattooed.",
tl:"Ang tattoo ay permanenteng disenyo sa balat gamit ang tinta. Mahigit 5,000 taon na itong ginagawa ng tao. Sa Pilipinas, kilala ang tradisyon ng mga Kalinga at ang tanyag na manunurat na si Apo Whang-od, gayundin ang mga Pintados ng Kabisayaan."});

